const NODE_DETAILS = {"root": "## At a Glance\n\nThe Artificial Intelligence Governance Professional (AIGP) is the IAPP's certification for professionals who ensure AI systems are developed, deployed, and managed responsibly and in line with law, policy, and emerging standards. It is deliberately cross-disciplinary: the Body of Knowledge spans foundational AI concepts, the global legal and regulatory landscape, and hands-on governance of the AI lifecycle from design through retirement.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nThe exam draws from four domains with fixed question-count ranges, so preparation effort should roughly mirror those weights: Domains III and IV (governing AI development, deployment, and use) together carry about half the exam, while Domain I (foundations) and Domain II (laws, standards, and frameworks) carry the rest. Scenario questions dominate — expect to apply governance judgment to realistic situations, not just recall definitions.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Four BOK domains: I Foundations of AI Governance, II Laws/Standards/Frameworks, III Governing AI Development, IV Governing AI Deployment & Use\n- The exam favors applied judgment: 'what should the governance professional do NEXT' scenarios over rote definitions\n- Core recurring themes across every domain: risk-based proportionality, human oversight, documentation, transparency, and accountability\n- The heavyweight reference texts (BOK v2.1) are the EU AI Act, GDPR, the NIST AI RMF and Playbook, ISO/IEC 22989, 42001 and 42005, and the OECD AI Principles — plus the South Korean AI Basic Act and US federal/state AI laws for II.C\n- When two answers both look right, prefer the one that is risk-based, documented, and multidisciplinary — that pattern resolves a large share of hard questions\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] AIGP Study Index\n\n# AIGP Study Index\n\nArtificial Intelligence Governance Professional (AIGP) certification — complete study vault.\n\n---\n\n## 📚 Knowledge Areas\n\n### Foundations of AI and Governance\n| Chapter | Topic | BOK Domain |\n|---------|-------|------------|\n| [[AI and AI Governance]] | AI types, risks, responsible AI principles | I.A |\n| [[Organizational Readiness]] | Roles, collaboration, training, tailoring governance | I.B |\n| [[Updating Policies for AI]] | Oversight, policy updates, third-party risk | I.C |\n\n### Legal and Regulatory Obligations\n| Chapter | Topic | BOK Domain |\n|---------|-------|------------|\n| [[Privacy and Data Protection Law]] | Notice, consent, data minimization, controller obligations | II.A |\n| [[Sectoral and Civil Laws]] | IP, non-discrimination, consumer protection, product liability | II.B |\n| [[The EU AI Act]] | AI-specific laws: EU risk classification, GPAI, enforcement, implementation | II.C |\n| [[AI Standards and Frameworks]] | OECD, NIST AI RMF + Playbook, ISO 22989/42001/42005 | II.D |\n\n### Governing the AI Lifecycle\n| Chapter | Topic | BOK Domain |\n|---------|-------|------------|\n| [[AI System Design]] | Lifecycle docs, impact assessments, design risks | III.A |\n| [[Data Governance and Model Training]] | Data governance, lineage, testing methodologies | III.B |\n| [[Deployment and Monitoring]] | Pre-release readiness, deployment, continuous monitoring | III.C, IV.A |\n| [[AI Risk Management and Assurance]] | Risk forecasting, audits, incident management | III.C, IV.B |\n| [[Ongoing AI Operations]] | Records, communications, system retirement | IV.C |\n\n---\n\n## 📋 Exam Resources\n\n| Resource | Description |\n|----------|-------------|\n| [[AIGP Body of Knowledge — Exam Blueprint]] | IAPP BOK v2.1 (effective 2 Feb 2026) — all domains, competencies, performance indicators, question counts, what changed from v2.0.1 |\n| [[AIGP FSG — Official Study Guide]] | IAPP official candidate guide — exam format, study tips, 3 sample questions |\n| [[Framework Recall Cards — AIGP Study Kit]] | 54 third-party recall cards (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0) by framework, incl. test-day tactics |\n\n---\n\n## ⚖️ Legislation & Legal References\n\n| Document | Type | Pages | Key For |\n|----------|------|-------|---------|\n| [[EU AI Act 2024 — Key Provisions]] | Regulation | 144p | Risk tiers, high-risk requirements, GPAI, penalties (€35M/7%) |\n| [[GDPR — AI-Relevant Provisions]] | Regulation | 88p | Art 22 (automated decisions), Art 9 (special categories), Art 35 (DPIAs) |\n| [[South Korea AI Basic Act — Key Provisions]] | Statute | — | High-impact AI, generative AI labeling, domestic representative, KRW 30M fines |\n| [[US AI Laws — Federal and State]] | Statutes | — | Colorado ADMT Act, TRAIGA, CA SB 53/SB 942/CCPA ADMT rules, NYC LL 144, IL HB 3773, RAISE Act |\n\n---\n\n## 🔬 Standards & Frameworks\n\n| Document | Type | Pages | Key For |\n|----------|------|-------|---------|\n| [[NIST AI RMF — Structured Reference]] | Framework | 48p | Govern → Map → Measure → Manage, trustworthiness |\n| [[NIST AI RMF Playbook — Structured Reference]] | Guidance | 72 subcats | Suggested actions and documentation prompts per subcategory |\n| [[NIST ARIA — Structured Reference]] | Evaluation | 36p | *Background only — removed from BOK v2.1* |\n| [[NIST AI 600-1 — Structured Reference]] | Guidance | 64p | RMF profile development, gap analysis |\n| [[OECD AI Principles — Structured Reference]] | Principles | 12p | 5 principles: growth, fairness, transparency, robustness, accountability |\n| [[ISO 42001 — Structured Reference]] | Standard | 227p | AI Management System, Annex SL, certification, auditing |\n| [[ISO 22989 — Key Definitions]] | Terminology | 147p | AI system, ML, bias, robustness, explainability — ISO definitions |\n| [[ISO 23894 — AI Risk Management]] | Standard | 12p | AI-specific risk management (SAMPLE — partial; not named in BOK v2.1) |\n| [[ISO 42005 — AI System Impact Assessment]] | Standard | 39p | AI system impact assessment — one of v2.1's three core ISO standards |\n| [[Agentic AI Governance]] | Guidance | — | Governing AI agents: autonomy, action-space, human checkpoints, agent red-teaming |\n| [[GPAI Code of Practice — Key Provisions]] | Guidance | 55p | GPAI obligations, systemic risk, transparency |\n\n---\n\n## 🗺️ Visual Tools\n\n| File | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| [[Canvas/AIGP Master Mind Map.canvas]] | Visual map: 25 nodes, 30 edges — chapters + key concepts |\n\n---\n\n## 🏷️ Tag Reference\n\n| Tag | Used On |\n|-----|---------|\n| `aigp/domain-I` | Domain I chapters and references |\n| `aigp/domain-II` | Domain II chapters, legal references |\n| `aigp/domain-III` | Domains III & IV design/training chapters |\n| `aigp/domain-IV` | Domains III & IV deployment/ops chapters |\n| `aigp/area` | Study guide area notes |\n| `aigp/exam` | Question banks |\n| `aigp/questions` | Question banks |\n| `aigp/reference` | Legislation, standards, frameworks |\n| `aigp/legal` | EU AI Act, GDPR |\n| `aigp/privacy` | GDPR, privacy law |\n| `aigp/standards` | NIST, ISO, OECD |\n| `aigp/nist` | NIST documents |\n| `aigp/iso` | ISO standards |\n| `aigp/oecd` | OECD principles |\n| `aigp/eu-ai-act` | EU AI Act |\n| `aigp/risk` | Risk management documents |\n| `aigp/index` | Index and hub notes |\n| `aigp/bok` | Body of Knowledge |\n| `aigp/master` | This master index |\n\n---\n\n## 🔗 Cross-Reference Map\n\n```\nAIGP BOK (exam blueprint)\n ├── Domain I → Domain I chapters + OECD Principles\n ├── Domain II → Domain II chapters + EU AI Act + GDPR + all standards\n ├── Domain III → Domains III & IV (AI System Design-11) + NIST RMF + NIST ARIA + ISO 42001\n └── Domain IV → Domains III & IV (Deployment and Monitoring-12) + NIST RMF + ISO 23894 + GPAI Code\n\nEU AI Act ← → GDPR (Art 22 ↔ AI Act high-risk provisions)\nNIST AI RMF ← → NIST ARIA (RMF core + ARIA evaluation)\nISO 42001 ← → NIST AI RMF (management system ↔ risk framework)\nISO 22989 ← → all (definitions used across all standards)\nGPAI Code ← → EU AI Act (GPAI provisions in both)\n```\n\n---\n\n> [!source] AIGP FSG — Official Study Guide\n\n# AIGP Fundamental Study Guide (IAPP Official)\n\n> Official IAPP candidate preparation guide — exam format, study recommendations, and sample questions.\n\n## Exam Format\n\n| Detail | Value |\n|--------|-------|\n| Duration | 3 hours |\n| Questions | 100 (90 scored + 10 unscored) |\n| Format | Multiple choice + case studies |\n| Passing | 300/500 (scaled score) |\n| Cost (member) | $649 |\n| Cost (nonmember) | $799 |\n| Delivery | Pearson VUE (in-person or remote OnVUE) |\n\n## IAPP Study Recommendations\n\n1. **Review the Body of Knowledge** — comprehensive outline with exam blueprint (min/max questions per domain)\n2. **Get certification training** — in-person, live online, or self-paced\n3. **Take the AIGP practice exam** — 100 questions matching actual exam depth and rigor\n4. **Purchase and schedule** — Pearson VUE testing center or remote proctoring\n\n> **Recommended study time:** minimum 30 hours\n\n## Continuing Education\n\n- **20 CPE credits** required annually to maintain certification\n- Membership: $295/year (includes maintenance fee)\n- Nonmember maintenance fee: $250/2 years\n\n## Official Sample Questions\n\n### Q1: Explainability\nAn AI system is explainable when the system's input, output, and functioning are?\n\n- **A.** Understandable to human beings. ✅\n- **B.** Disclosed to human beings.\n- **C.** Designed by human beings.\n- **D.** Documented by human beings.\n\n> **Answer: A** — Explainability means the system's workings are *understandable* to humans, not merely disclosed or documented.\n\n### Q2: AI Impact Assessment\nThe primary purpose of an AI impact assessment is to provide understanding of?\n\n- **A.** The return on investment of building and operating an AI system.\n- **B.** The potential benefits and harms from an AI system. ✅\n- **C.** The computing power necessary to run an AI system.\n- **D.** The design components of an AI system.\n\n> **Answer: B** — Impact assessments surface potential harms, benefits, and risks before deployment.\n\n### Q3: Production Monitoring Metrics (Multi-Select)\nWhich of the following metrics should be tracked when monitoring the performance of a model that has been deployed into production? *(Select 3 of 5)*\n\n- **A.** Data drift. ✅\n- **B.** Data quality. ✅\n- **C.** Historical training data.\n- **D.** Internal training processes.\n- **E.** Latency and computational metrics. ✅\n\n> **Answer: A, B, E** — Production monitoring focuses on live system behavior (drift, quality, latency), not historical/internal processes.\n\n## IAPP Certification Ecosystem\n\n| Cert | Focus |\n|------|-------|\n| **AIGP** | AI governance — risk, ethics, responsible AI management |\n| **CIPP** | Data privacy laws (5 jurisdictional concentrations: A, CN, C, E, US) |\n| **CIPM** | Privacy program management and operations |\n| **CIPT** | Privacy in technology — building privacy-friendly systems |\n\n---\n\n> [!source] AIGP Body of Knowledge — Exam Blueprint\n\n## Exam Structure\n\n| Domain | Title | Questions (min–max) | Share of scored items* |\n|--------|-------|---------------------|--------|\n| I | Understanding the foundations of AI governance | 16–20 | ~21% |\n| II | Understanding how laws, standards and frameworks apply to AI | 19–23 | ~25% |\n| III | Understanding how to govern AI development | 21–25 | ~27% |\n| IV | Understanding how to govern AI deployment and use | 21–25 | ~27% |\n| **Total** | | **100 questions (~90 scored + ~10 unscored)** | |\n\n\\* From the range midpoints. The app's Full Exam uses these weights.\n\n- **Format:** multiple choice, including multi-select items that tell you how many to choose (e.g. \"Select 3\") with no partial credit, and scenario sets where several questions share one case.\n- **Timing and scoring:** 2h45m plus an optional 15-minute break that splits the exam into halves; you cannot return to the first half after submitting it. Scaled score 100–500, and 300 passes.\n- **Cognitive level:** questions focus on **Remember/Understand** and **Apply/Analyze** (Bloom's taxonomy).\n\n---\n\n> [!source] AIGP Body of Knowledge — Exam Blueprint\n\n## Version 2.1 — What Changed\n\nCompared with v2.0.1 (effective 3 Feb 2025):\n\n- **II.C broadened from \"the EU AI Act\" to \"AI-specific laws\"** (now 6–8 questions, was 5–7).\n  - The Domain II description names the **EU AI Act**, the **South Korean AI Basic Law**, and **federal and state AI laws that apply to private-sector organizations**.\n  - v2.0.1 had explicitly *excluded* US state laws as too piecemeal.\n- **II.D slimmed** (now 3–5, was 4–6).\n  - **NIST ARIA removed.**\n  - The core ISO standards are now **22989, 42001 and 42005** (AI system impact assessment).\n  - OECD and the NIST AI RMF + Playbook are unchanged.\n- **II.A:** \"notice, choice, consent\" became \"**transparency, choice, lawful basis**\"; controller obligations now include **automated decision-making**.\n- **I.B:** **providers** added alongside developers, deployers and users.\n- **I.C:** the policy review now covers **data governance and intellectual property**; the third-party item covers policies, assessments **and contracts**, and adds **acceptable use**.\n- **III.A and IV.B:** the \"identify applicable laws\" indicators were removed, and \"model\" became \"system\" throughout.\n- **IV.B:** \"open source agreement\" became \"**licensing agreement**\"; the proprietary-model item is reframed as risks *and opportunities*.\n- **IV.A:** deployment options now include **agentic architectures**, alongside fine-tuning and RAG.\n- **New intro:**\n  - The certification explicitly covers **generative and agentic AI**.\n  - \"Developer\" and \"deployer\" are used as *task* labels, not legal categories.\n  - Laws are distilled to their most widely accepted elements rather than every niche.\n- **Unchanged:** the four domain ranges, the other competency ranges, and the Bloom's levels.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n> [!source] AIGP Body of Knowledge — Exam Blueprint\n\n## Key Standards Referenced in the BOK\n\n| Standard / law | What it covers | BOK |\n|----------|---------------|------------|\n| **OECD AI Principles** (+ classification framework, definition, recommended practices) | Trustworthy AI: inclusive growth, human-centred values, transparency, robustness, accountability | II.D |\n| **NIST AI RMF 1.0 + Playbook** | Govern → Map → Measure → Manage; suggested actions per subcategory | II.D |\n| **ISO/IEC 22989** | AI concepts and terminology | II.D |\n| **ISO/IEC 42001** | AI management system (certifiable) | II.D |\n| **ISO/IEC 42005** | AI system impact assessment (guidance) | II.D |\n| **EU AI Act** | Risk-based regulation: prohibited → high → limited → minimal; GPAI | II.C |\n| **South Korea AI Basic Act** | High-impact and generative AI duties, in force 22 Jan 2026 | II.C |\n| **US federal and state AI laws** | e.g. Colorado ADMT Act, Texas TRAIGA, California SB 53, NYC Local Law 144 | II.C |\n| **GDPR** (and other privacy laws) | Lawful basis, Art 22 automated decisions, Art 35 DPIAs, Art 9 special categories | II.A |\n\n> NIST ARIA appeared in v2.0.1 II.D and was **removed in v2.1**. [[NIST ARIA — Structured Reference]] is kept only as background.\n\n---\n\n> [!source] Framework Recall Cards — AIGP Study Kit\n\n## Blueprint calibration\n\n*Straight from the official BoK v2.1 — this changes where you spend time.*\n\n### How many questions come from each domain\n\nThe BoK publishes exact ranges. **Domain I: 16–20 · Domain II: 19–23 · Domain III: 21–25 · Domain IV: 21–25.** Midpoints total **85** of 100 items, which is where the 15 unscored pilot questions come from.\n\nShares of scored items: **I ≈ 21%, II ≈ 25%, III ≈ 27%, IV ≈ 27%**. Note Domain II is *larger* than the Wiley guide implies.\n\n### Which single competency is worth the most marks\n\n**IV.C — governing deployment and use: 9–11 items**, the largest block on the exam. Then **III.C** (release, monitoring, maintenance) at 8–10.\n\nThe smallest is **II.D at 3–5 items** — that is OECD, NIST RMF *and* ISO combined. Know them precisely, but do not spend a third of your time there; the marks live in Domains III and IV.\n\n### What cognitive level the questions sit at\n\nIAPP states questions focus on the **remember/understand** and **apply/analyze** levels of Bloom's Taxonomy — not evaluate or create. The verb opening each performance indicator signals the level.\n\nDomain II indicators nearly all begin with **'Understand'**, so that domain is more definitional than scenario-based. Domains III and IV use *govern, perform, apply, identify, document, conduct, forecast* — applied questions.\n\n### The question format the practice exams often miss\n\nThe official study guide includes a **MULTI-SELECT** example: 'select 3 of the 5 options — **no partial credit**.' Expect some. Read the required count, select exactly that many, and remember a near-miss scores the same as a blank.\n\nIAPP also recommends a minimum of **30 hours** of preparation, and sells its own 100-question practice exam.\n\n### BoK terminology to mirror on exam day\n\nUse the BoK's own words where they differ from common usage:\n• It says **'model or data drift'** — not 'concept drift'.\n• It names **'i.e., 22989, 42001 and 42005'** — a closed list, not examples.\n• It says **'AI system'** throughout, deliberately unified from 'AI model' in v2.1 — think system-level: infrastructure, human oversight, third parties, downstream impact.\n• It treats **developer/provider/deployer/user** as labels for *tasks*, noting many organizations fill several roles at once.\n\n---\n\n> [!source] Framework Recall Cards — AIGP Study Kit\n\n## Test-day tactics\n\n*Cheap marks and avoidable failures.*\n\n### How to work the question set\n\n~1.65 min per question (100 in ~165 min). **Answer everything** — no penalty for wrong answers, so never leave a blank. **Flag and return** is allowed; use it rather than stalling. Watch for **'BEST'**, **'MOST'**, **'FIRST'** — several options will be defensible and only one is best. Take the optional 15-minute break if offered; fatigue costs more than the time does.\n\n### How to break a two-option tie\n\nPrefer the answer that: is **proportionate** (neither ignores the risk nor bans the activity outright); **documents and assigns ownership**; acts **earliest in the lifecycle**; keeps a **human with real authority**; and treats accountability as **non-delegable**. Absolutes ('always', 'never', 'no need to') are usually wrong. Options that delete evidence, defer a live risk, or outsource responsibility are almost always wrong.\n\n### Logistics that can cost you the fee\n\nGovernment-issued **photo ID with a name matching your booking exactly**. For OnVUE remote: **check in 30 minutes early**, run the system test in advance, clear the room, and stay in webcam view. A **digital whiteboard** is provided — physical paper or pens are not permitted. In-person reschedules need **48 hours** notice; remote can be rescheduled up to 15 minutes after the start time. The exam must be sat within **one year of purchase** or the fee is forfeited.\n\n> [!info] **Retake rule — now verified.** The IAPP Certification Candidate Handbook v5.3.2 (effective 1 June 2026) settles it: you cannot reschedule sooner than **7 days** after sitting, and you must buy a new exam once results post. A no-show consumes the attempt. Source: [IAPP Certification Candidate Handbook](https://assets.contentstack.io/v3/assets/bltd4dd5b2d705252bc/blteb8b5d531fd78971/IAPP-Certification_Handbook.pdf). The earlier third-party \"14 days\" claims are wrong.", "d1": "## At a Glance\n\nDomain I establishes the conceptual bedrock: what AI is, the forms it takes, the risks and harms it creates, why those risks demand governance, and how an organization organizes people, policies and processes to respond. The BOK is explicit that the domain covers \"best practices regardless of industry, sector or size\", so nothing here is sector-specific. It runs from technology literacy (model types, learning paradigms) through organizational readiness (roles, collaboration, training) to policy (lifecycle oversight, legacy policy updates, third-party risk).\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nDomain I carries **16 to 20 of the 100 scored items**, split across three competencies. It is the smallest domain by question count and the one with the most downstream reach: the vocabulary and principle set it installs are the raw material every later domain reasons with.\n\n| Competency | What it covers | Questions |\n|---|---|---|\n| **I.A** Understand what AI is and why it needs governance | Definitions and types of AI; risks and harms; the characteristics that demand governance; responsible AI principles | 4 to 6 |\n| **I.B** Establish and communicate organizational expectations | Roles and responsibilities; cross-functional collaboration; training and awareness; tailoring by context; developers, providers, deployers and users | 5 to 7 |\n| **I.C** Establish policies and procedures across the life cycle | Oversight and accountability at every stage; updating existing privacy, security, data-governance and IP policies; third-party risk | 6 to 8 |\n\n### How the BOK frames it\n\nThe BOK describes Domain I as \"what AI governance is, including the common principles and pillars to build an AI governance program\", and repeats that this holds \"regardless of industry, sector or size.\" That framing is itself testable. An answer that assumes a bank, a hospital or a large enterprise is usually the wrong one, because Domain I is where the generic, transferable practice lives; sector specifics belong to Domain II's sectoral laws and to the lifecycle domains.\n\nThe BOK also states that exam items sit mostly at the **remember/understand** and **apply/analyze** levels of Bloom's taxonomy. Domain I is where the remember/understand share is highest, which is why definitional precision pays here more than anywhere else.\n\n### What changed in BOK v2.1\n\nThree v2.1 edits land inside Domain I:\n\n- **I.B now lists providers** alongside developers, deployers and users. The added role matters because a provider can be a small team that fine-tunes someone else's base model, so the BOK wants you to see the value chain rather than only the internal cast.\n- **I.C's policy-review item now names data governance and intellectual property** explicitly, alongside the data privacy and security policies that v2.0.1 already listed.\n- **I.C's third-party item now covers policies, assessments and contracts**, and adds **acceptable use** to the areas (procurement, supply chain, human resources) it names.\n\n### How to hold the domain\n\nTreat Domain I as two halves. The first (I.A) is descriptive: classify a system, name what can go wrong, and name the characteristic that makes the failure mode possible. The second (I.B and I.C) is organizational: say who is accountable, what has to be written down, and which legacy policy an AI use case has just broken. Nearly every Domain I item is one of those two moves.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nWorth roughly 16 to 20 questions, the smallest of the four domains, and the one where definitional precision is worth the most points. Expect narrow vs. general AI, supervised vs. unsupervised vs. reinforcement learning, and functionality ladders, then scenario items about assigning governance roles, updating pre-AI policies, and managing third-party AI risk.\n\nTwo patterns recur. First, a question gives you a system and asks you to classify it or name the harm it presents; the answer follows from vocabulary you either have cold or don't. Second, a question describes an organization adopting AI and asks what the governance professional should do first or next. Those answers reward the same instincts that pay across the whole exam: risk-based proportionality, documented decisions, and multidisciplinary involvement.\n\nBecause I.C carries the most items (6 to 8), do not under-weight policy work. The most common trap is treating Domain I as pure theory and spending all your revision time on the technical taxonomy.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Domain I is 16 to 20 questions across three competencies: I.A (4 to 6), I.B (5 to 7), I.C (6 to 8)\n- The BOK scopes it as best practice \"regardless of industry, sector or size\", so answers that depend on a specific sector are usually wrong\n- AI capability ladder: narrow or weak AI (exists) to AGI (theoretical) to ASI (theoretical); functionality ladder: reactive to limited memory to theory of mind to self-aware\n- The BOK's four I.A indicators are definitions and types, risks and harms, governance-demanding characteristics, and responsible AI principles\n- I.A's governance-demanding characteristics: complexity, opacity, autonomy, speed and scale, potential for harm or misuse, data dependency, probabilistic versus deterministic outputs\n- I.A's responsible AI principles: fairness, safety and reliability, privacy and security, transparency and explainability, accountability, human-centricity\n- I.B covers roles, cross-functional collaboration, training and awareness, tailoring to context, and the developer / provider / deployer / user distinction\n- I.C covers lifecycle oversight policy, updating existing privacy, security, data-governance and IP policies, and third-party risk across procurement, supply chain, HR and acceptable use\n- v2.1 changes: providers added to I.B, data governance and IP added to the policy-review item, contracts and acceptable use added to the third-party item\n- Later domains reuse this vocabulary constantly, so weak Domain I fluency shows up as lost points in Domains II, III and IV\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://assets.contentstack.io/v3/assets/bltd4dd5b2d705252bc/blt0d33152fd20bc134/AIGP_Cert%20BOK.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AIGP Body of Knowledge and Exam Blueprint v2.1 (PDF)</a> · the authoritative blueprint, effective 2 February 2026\n- <a href=\"https://iapp.org/certify/aigp/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IAPP AIGP certification page</a> · exam format, eligibility and scheduling\n- <a href=\"https://iapp.org/resources/article/key-terms-for-ai-governance/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IAPP Key Terms for AI Governance</a> · the glossary the BOK draws its terminology from\n- <a href=\"https://oecd.ai/en/wonk/definition\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OECD: what is an AI system?</a> · the 2023 revision of the definition that the EU AI Act mirrors\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI Risk Management Framework (AI 100-1)</a> · trustworthiness characteristics and the Govern function\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 concepts and terminology</a> · free from the IEC webstore\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Foundations Index\n\n# Foundations of AI and Governance\n\n**AIGP Domain I** — Understanding the Foundations of AI Governance\n\n| Chapter | Topic | Subdomain |\n|---------|-------|-----------|\n| [[AI and AI Governance]] | AI types, risks/harms, characteristics requiring governance, responsible AI principles | I.A |\n| [[Organizational Readiness]] | Roles/responsibilities, cross-functional collaboration, training, tailoring governance | I.B |\n| [[Updating Policies for AI]] | Autonomous decision-making oversight, policy updates for AI, third-party risk | I.C |\n\n## Key Themes\n- What AI is and why it needs governance\n- Who's responsible for AI governance\n- How existing policies must evolve for AI\n\n---\n\n> [!source] AIGP Body of Knowledge — Exam Blueprint\n\n## Domain I: Foundations of AI Governance (16–20 questions)\n\nWhat AI governance is, and the common principles and pillars of an AI governance program, regardless of industry, sector or size.\n\n### I.A — Understand what AI is and why it needs governance (4–6 q)\n- Know generally accepted **definitions and types of AI**\n- Identify **risks and harms** to individuals, groups, organizations and society (misalignment with objectives, ethics and bias, complexity and scalability)\n- Identify the **characteristics** that demand governance (complexity, opacity, autonomy, speed and scale, potential for harm or misuse, data dependency, probabilistic vs deterministic outputs)\n- Identify and apply **responsible AI principles** (fairness, safety and reliability, privacy and security, transparency and explainability, accountability, human-centricity)\n\n### I.B — Establish and communicate organizational expectations (5–7 q)\n- Define **roles and responsibilities** for governance stakeholders\n- Establish **cross-functional collaboration** (efficacy, diversity of expertise and perspective)\n- Create and deliver **training and awareness** on AI terminology, strategy and governance\n- Differentiate approaches by **size, maturity, industry, products and services, objectives and risk tolerance**\n- Identify differences among **developers, providers, deployers and users** (responsibilities, opportunities, needs)\n\n### I.C — Establish policies and procedures across the life cycle (6–8 q)\n- Policies for **oversight and accountability** at every stage (use-case assessment, risk management, ethics by design, data acquisition and use, model and system development, training and testing, deployment and monitoring, documentation and reporting, incident management)\n- Evaluate and update **existing policies** (data privacy, security, data governance, intellectual property) for AI\n- Policies, assessments and **contracts** for **third-party risk** (procurement, supply chain, human resources, acceptable use)\n\n### 📎 Cross-references\n- [[AI and AI Governance]] — I.A\n- [[Organizational Readiness]] — I.B\n- [[Updating Policies for AI]] — I.C\n- [[Agentic AI Governance]] — agentic AI concepts\n\n---\n\n---\n\n> [!source] Framework Recall Cards — AIGP Study Kit\n\n## Governance mechanics\n\n*The structural answers to 'what should they do next'.*\n\n### RACI — the rule that generates exam questions\n\n**R**esponsible (does the work — can be many) · **A**ccountable (**exactly one**, answerable for the outcome) · **C**onsulted (two-way input) · **I**nformed (one-way updates).\nDiffusing accountability across several people is always the flaw.\n\n### Documentation artifacts and what each is for\n\n**Model card** — intended use, training data, metrics, **limitations**, ethical considerations. Structured transparency for downstream deployers.\n**System card** — the same at whole-system level.\n**Datasheet for datasets** — provenance, collection method, composition, recommended uses.\n**AI impact assessment** — harms to people and society.\n**DPIA** — privacy risk. **Conformity assessment** — regulatory compliance. **Algorithmic audit** — independent evaluation.\n\n### Governance maturity progression\n\nAd hoc → repeatable → **defined** → managed → optimized. The move that matters most: from ad hoc to defined, via **mandatory lifecycle checkpoints with required artifacts and approval gates**. Templates and tooling help you execute a standard; they don't create one.\n\n**Proportionality:** governance must fit organizational size, sector, risk profile and culture. A structure too heavy to sustain produces skipped controls — worse than a lighter one that actually runs.\n\n### Third-party and vendor risk — the terms that carry governance weight\n\n**Audit rights** · documentation and technical information · **incident notification** duties · restrictions on the vendor **training on your data** · data provenance and licensing warranties · indemnities · IP ownership of outputs · exit and portability.\n\nWhen a vendor won't disclose something, the governed move is to convert the gap into **contractual terms plus a documented, authorized risk acceptance** — not to walk away and not to ignore it. **Outsourcing the model never outsources accountability.** (Weighted more heavily in BoK v2.1.)", "d2": "## At a Glance\n\nDomain II maps the external rulebook: the laws that already reach AI (GDPR, non-discrimination, IP, consumer protection, product liability), AI-specific laws - the EU AI Act as the first comprehensive AI statute, plus South Korea's AI Basic Act and US federal and state AI laws (added in BOK v2.1) - and the voluntary standards and frameworks (OECD, NIST, ISO) that organizations use to operationalize trustworthy AI. The skill being tested is knowing which instrument applies to which situation, and what it actually requires.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nDomain II is where the exam stops asking what AI governance **is** and starts asking what the law **requires**. It carries 19 to 23 of the 100 scored items, second only to Domains III and IV, and it splits into four competencies with different textures:\n\n| Competency | Subject | Questions |\n|---|---|---|\n| **II.A** | How existing data privacy laws apply to AI | 4 to 6 |\n| **II.B** | How other existing laws apply to AI (IP, non-discrimination, consumer protection, product liability) | 4 to 6 |\n| **II.C** | Main elements of AI-specific laws (EU AI Act, Korea AI Basic Act, US federal and state) | 6 to 8 |\n| **II.D** | Main industry standards and tools (OECD, NIST, ISO) | 3 to 5 |\n\n### The organizing idea: which instrument applies\n\nAlmost every Domain II item is a matching problem. A fact pattern is described and the task is to name the instrument that governs it, the obligation it triggers, or the role that carries it. The instruments fall into three families:\n\n- **Law that already reaches AI without naming it.** The GDPR, the US fair-lending and civil-rights statutes, the FTC Act, copyright and product liability all predate AI and apply to it anyway. II.A and II.B are about seeing the hook.\n- **Law written for AI.** The EU AI Act is the first comprehensive statute, with South Korea's AI Basic Act and a growing set of US state laws behind it. II.C is about knowing the mechanism.\n- **Voluntary standards and frameworks.** The OECD Principles, the NIST AI RMF and its Playbook, and ISO/IEC 22989, 42001 and 42005. None bind, but regulators, auditors and contracts increasingly treat them as the measure of due care. II.D is about knowing which instrument does what.\n\n### Currency: as enacted versus as amended\n\nDomain II is the domain where the calendar matters most, and the repo's convention is to state which legal state a fact describes. Two examples carry most of the weight:\n\n- **The EU AI Act.** As enacted (Regulation (EU) 2024/1689), Annex III high-risk rules applied from 2 August 2026 and Annex I product-embedded rules from 2 August 2027. As amended by the Digital Omnibus on AI (Regulation (EU) 2026/1744, in force 27 July 2026), those dates moved to 2 December 2027 and 2 August 2028. 2 August 2026 still starts Article 50 transparency and the Commission's GPAI enforcement, and 2 December 2026 brings two new Article 5 prohibitions.\n- **US non-discrimination.** Federal agencies pulled back from disparate-impact enforcement through 2025 and 2026 (EEOC guidance withdrawn, Executive Order 14281, the CFPB's Regulation B rule effective 21 July 2026), but the statutes and private suits are unaffected. An executive order cannot amend Title VII.\n\nBOK v2.1 was approved on 9 September 2025, before the Omnibus was agreed, so a live exam item may quote either timeline. Read each stem for its anchor (\"as originally enacted\" or \"as amended by the 2026 Digital Omnibus\"); if there is none, ask whether the fact being tested changed at all.\n\n### What changed in BOK v2.1\n\n- **II.C** was renamed from \"the main elements of the EU AI Act\" to \"the main elements of AI-specific laws\", its weight rose from 5 to 7 questions to 6 to 8, and the domain introduction now names South Korea's AI Basic Act and US federal and state AI laws.\n- **II.A**'s first indicator became \"transparency, choice, lawful basis\", widening the focus from consent to every Article 6 basis, and controller obligations now name automated decision-making explicitly.\n- **II.D** dropped NIST ARIA and added ISO/IEC 42005 to the core ISO list, so the core three are 22989, 42001 and 42005. Its weight fell from 4 to 6 questions to 3 to 5.\n\n### How to work a Domain II question\n\nName the instrument first, then the role, then the duty. A question about a hiring tool may be testing Title VII, the GDPR's Article 22, the EU AI Act's Annex III, or NYC Local Law 144, and the fact pattern usually contains the one detail that decides which. Precision about dates and status (adopted, in force, draft, proposed, withdrawn, on appeal) is worth as much as knowing the rules themselves.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nThe second-heaviest domain at roughly 19 to 23 questions. The EU AI Act is the single most-tested instrument - risk tiers, prohibited practices, high-risk obligations, GPAI rules, penalties and the implementation timeline all appear - and it is the only place the exam asks you to compare jurisdictions, so expect Korea and US state law items too. GDPR questions focus on its AI pressure points: Article 22 automated decision-making (with SCHUFA and Dun & Bradstreet Austria), Article 9 special categories, Article 35 DPIAs, Article 28 processor contracts, and the EDPB's 2024-2026 position on models and scraping. II.B is scenario matching (fact pattern to doctrine), and II.D is instrument matching (function to framework).\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Spans four knowledge areas: privacy/data protection law, other existing (sectoral and civil) laws, AI-specific laws (EU AI Act, Korea AI Basic Act, US federal and state AI laws), and AI standards and frameworks\n- EU AI Act risk ladder: unacceptable (banned), high-risk (heavy obligations), limited (transparency), minimal (largely unregulated)\n- Penalty anchors: EUR 35m/7% for prohibited practices, EUR 15m/3% for high-risk non-compliance, EUR 7.5m/1% for misleading information, all 'whichever is higher'\n- Existing law reaches AI even without 'AI' in its name: ECOA and Title VII for discrimination, FTC Act for deception, product liability for defects\n- Standards stack: OECD principles (values), NIST AI RMF (risk process), ISO/IEC 42001 (certifiable management system), ISO/IEC 23894 (risk guidance, background only)\n- State which legal state a fact describes: 'as enacted' (Regulation (EU) 2024/1689) versus 'as amended by the Digital Omnibus on AI' (Regulation (EU) 2026/1744, in force 27 July 2026)\n- BOK v2.1 widened II.C beyond the EU and moved the domain's centre of gravity toward AI-specific law; II.C now carries the most questions of the four competencies\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1689/oj\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 (EU AI Act)</a> · the anchor AI-specific statute for II.C, as enacted\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2026/1744/oj/eng\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Digital Omnibus on AI, Regulation (EU) 2026/1744</a> · the 2026 amendment that moved the high-risk dates and added two prohibitions\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679</a> · the privacy baseline for II.A\n- <a href=\"https://legalinstruments.oecd.org/en/instruments/OECD-LEGAL-0449\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OECD Recommendation on AI (OECD/LEGAL/0449)</a> · the intergovernmental values baseline for II.D\n- <a href=\"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI Risk Management Framework</a> · the voluntary risk process named in the BOK\n- <a href=\"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF Playbook</a> · per-subcategory suggested actions the BOK names directly\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989 (IEC webstore)</a> · the shared AI vocabulary, free from the IEC webstore\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Legal and Regulatory Index\n\n# Legal and Regulatory Obligations\n\n**AIGP Domain II** — Understanding Existing and Emerging AI Laws and Standards\n\n| Chapter | Topic | Subdomain |\n|---------|-------|-----------|\n| [[Privacy and Data Protection Law]] | Transparency, choice, lawful basis, purpose limitation, minimization, controller obligations incl. automated decisions | II.A |\n| [[Sectoral and Civil Laws]] | IP laws, non-discrimination, consumer protection, product liability | II.B |\n| [[The EU AI Act]] | AI-specific laws: EU risk classification, GPAI, enforcement, timeline (as amended by the 2026 Omnibus) | II.C |\n| [[South Korea AI Basic Act — Key Provisions]] | High-impact and generative AI duties, domestic representative, fines | II.C |\n| [[US AI Laws — Federal and State]] | Colorado, Texas, Utah, California, NYC, Illinois, New York; TAKE IT DOWN Act; EO 14365 | II.C |\n| [[AI Standards and Frameworks]] | OECD, NIST AI RMF + Playbook, ISO 22989/42001/42005 | II.D |\n\n## Key Themes\n- Privacy and data protection in AI contexts\n- How existing civil laws apply to AI\n- AI-specific laws: the EU AI Act, South Korea's AI Basic Act, and US federal/state AI laws (BOK v2.1 II.C)\n- International standards and frameworks for AI governance\n\n---\n\n> [!source] AIGP Body of Knowledge — Exam Blueprint\n\n## Domain II: Laws, Standards & Frameworks (19–23 questions)\n\nExisting laws that apply to AI, plus AI-specific laws, standards and frameworks: the major elements of current AI laws such as the EU AI Act, the South Korean AI Basic Law, and federal and state AI laws that apply to private-sector organizations.\n\n### II.A — How existing data privacy laws apply to AI (4–6 q)\n- **Transparency, choice, lawful basis and purpose limitation**\n- **Data minimization and privacy by design**\n- **Controller obligations** (privacy impact assessments, third-party processors, cross-border transfers, data subject rights, **automated decision-making**, incident management, breach notification, record keeping)\n- **Sensitive and special-category data** (e.g. biometrics)\n\n### II.B — How other existing laws apply to AI (4–6 q)\n- **Intellectual property** (e.g. limits on using data for training)\n- **Non-discrimination** (employment, credit, lending, housing, insurance)\n- **Consumer protection** (unfair and deceptive acts or practices)\n- **Product liability** (design and manufacturing defects)\n\n### II.C — Main elements of AI-specific laws (6–8 q)\n- **Risk classification** (e.g. prohibited / high / limited / minimal) and what falls in each category\n- **Risk management, data governance, technical documentation, conformity/impact assessments and record keeping**\n- **Human oversight, transparency and notification, quality management**\n- Distinct requirements for **general-purpose AI models**\n- **Enforcement framework and penalties**\n- Differences by **organizational context** (providers, deployers, importers, distributors)\n\n### II.D — Main industry standards and tools (3–5 q)\n- **OECD** principles, framework, policies and recommended practices for trustworthy AI\n- **NIST AI RMF and Playbook** (core functions, categories, subcategories)\n- **Core ISO AI standards: 22989, 42001 and 42005**\n\n### 📎 Cross-references\n- [[Privacy and Data Protection Law]] — II.A\n- [[Sectoral and Civil Laws]] — II.B\n- [[The EU AI Act]] — II.C\n- [[South Korea AI Basic Act — Key Provisions]] — II.C\n- [[US AI Laws — Federal and State]] — II.C\n- [[AI Standards and Frameworks]] — II.D\n- [[EU AI Act 2024 — Key Provisions]]\n- [[GDPR — AI-Relevant Provisions]]\n- [[NIST AI RMF — Structured Reference]]\n- [[NIST AI RMF Playbook — Structured Reference]]\n- [[ISO 42005 — AI System Impact Assessment]]\n- [[OECD AI Principles — Structured Reference]]\n\n---", "d3": "## At a Glance\n\nDomain III governs the build: planning and designing an AI system, assessing its impacts before committing, governing the data that feeds it, testing the resulting models, and taking the system through release into monitored operation. Its center of gravity is evidence — documented lifecycles, lineage, impact assessments, and test results that prove the system was built deliberately.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nRoughly 21–25 questions. Expect deep scenario testing on data governance (lawful sourcing, quality, representativeness), the testing taxonomy (unit, integration, validation, performance, security, bias, adversarial), and impact assessments. Questions often hinge on sequencing — what must happen before training, before testing, before release.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Spans BOK subdomains III.A (design & building), III.B (training & testing data), and III.C (release & monitoring)\n- Documentation artifacts to know: model cards, datasheets, system cards, impact assessments, risk registers\n- Data governance pillars: lawful authority to use data, quality, representativeness, lineage/provenance, minimization, security\n- Testing must cover more than accuracy: robustness (edge cases), fairness (across groups), security (poisoning, inversion, adversarial examples), explainability\n- Overfitting/underfitting, drift, and representativeness failures are the recurring technical failure modes governance must catch\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] AI Lifecycle Index\n\n# Governing the AI Lifecycle\n\n**AIGP Domains III & IV** — Governing AI Development, Deployment, and Use\n\n| Chapter | Topic | Subdomain |\n|---------|-------|-----------|\n| [[AI System Design]] | Lifecycle documentation, business context, impact assessments, design risks | III.A |\n| [[Data Governance and Model Training]] | Data governance, lineage/provenance, testing methodologies | III.B |\n| [[Deployment and Monitoring]] | Pre-release readiness, deployment, continuous monitoring | III.C |\n| [[AI Risk Management and Assurance]] | Risk forecasting, audits/testing, incident management/disclosures | III.C, IV.B |\n| [[Ongoing AI Operations]] | Business records, external communications, system retirement | IV.C |\n\n## Key Themes\n- The full AI system lifecycle from design to retirement\n- Data governance and model training best practices\n- Deployment readiness and continuous monitoring\n- Risk management, assurance, and incident response\n\n---\n\n> [!source] AIGP Body of Knowledge — Exam Blueprint\n\n## Domain III: Governing AI Development (21–25 questions)\n\nResponsibilities for designing, building, training, testing and maintaining AI systems.\n\n### III.A — Govern designing and building the AI system (6–8 q)\n- Define the **business context and use case**\n- Perform or review an **impact assessment**\n- Apply **policies, procedures, best practices and ethics** to design and build (purpose, requirements gathering, architecture and model selection, human oversight, data analysis, metric and threshold evaluation, stakeholder engagement and feedback, operational controls)\n- Identify and manage **internal and external design risks** (probability/severity harms matrix, risk mitigation hierarchy, stakeholder mapping, use-case evaluation, benchmarking, pre-deployment pilots and testing)\n- **Document** the design and build process\n\n### III.B — Govern data collection and use in training and testing (6–8 q)\n- **Data governance requirements** (lawful rights to collect and use; quality, quantity, integrity, fitness for purpose)\n- **Data lineage and provenance**\n- Plan and perform **training and testing** (unit, integration, validation, performance, security, bias, interpretability)\n- Manage **issues and risks** during training and testing\n- **Document** the training and testing process\n\n### III.C — Govern release, monitoring and maintenance (8–10 q)\n- **Release readiness** (model card, conformity requirements)\n- **Continuous monitoring** and a regular schedule for maintenance, updates and retraining\n- **Periodic assessments** of performance, reliability and safety (audits, red teaming, threat modeling, security testing)\n- Manage and document **incidents, issues and risks**\n- Cross-functional **incident analysis** (brittleness, lack of robustness, poor-quality data, insufficient testing, model or data drift)\n- **Public disclosures** (technical documentation, instructions for use to deployers, post-market monitoring plans)\n\n### 📎 Cross-references\n- [[AI System Design]] — III.A\n- [[Data Governance and Model Training]] — III.B\n- [[Deployment and Monitoring]] — III.C\n- [[AI Risk Management and Assurance]] — III.C\n- [[ISO 42005 — AI System Impact Assessment]] — impact assessments\n- [[NIST AI RMF — Structured Reference]]\n\n---\n\n---\n\n> [!source] Framework Recall Cards — AIGP Study Kit\n\n## Development & data\n\n*~27% of scored items per BoK v2.1 (the Wiley guide says 29%). Governance overlay on the ML lifecycle.*\n\n### Verification vs validation\n\n**Verification** — was the system built **right**, against specification?\n**Validation** — was the **right system** built for the real-world need and context?\nBoth sit inside **TEVV** (test, evaluation, verification, validation), before release.\n\n### Sources of bias, by origin\n\n**Historical** — the world (and the labels) were already biased; the model faithfully learns it. The recruitment-model-trained-on-past-hiring scenario.\n**Representation** — subgroups under-sampled.\n**Measurement** — the proxy chosen doesn't measure what you think.\n**Aggregation** — one model forced across distinct subgroups.\n**Evaluation** — benchmark unrepresentative.\n**Automation/confirmation bias** — humans over-trusting or cherry-picking model output.\n\n### Data lineage and provenance — what an auditor is actually asking for\n\n**Provenance** = where data came from and under what terms. **Lineage** = every transformation from origin to a specific model version. Together they underpin reproducibility, licensing defense, and audit.\nNot the same as minimization (how much you collect), access logs (who touched it), or retention (how long you keep it).\n\n### Testing types beyond accuracy\n\n**Subgroup/fairness testing** — aggregate accuracy routinely hides subgroup failure.\n**Adversarial robustness** and **red teaming**.\n**Explainability testing** — SHAP, LIME, counterfactuals.\nFairness metrics: demographic parity, equalized odds, disparate impact ratio — and they can conflict, so choosing one is a documented governance decision, not a technical default.\n\n### Threshold setting — why it's a governance decision\n\nWhere you set a decision threshold determines **who bears which type of error** (false positives vs false negatives). That's a value tradeoff about relative harm, not a tuning parameter — so it needs documented rationale, an owner, and review.\n\n### AI-specific security threats\n\n**Data poisoning** (corrupting training data) · **model inversion** (reconstructing training data) · **membership inference** (was this record in training?) · **evasion/adversarial examples** · **prompt injection** · **model theft/extraction**.\nThese are the additions AI makes to a conventional security policy that already covers access control, encryption and patching.", "d4": "## At a Glance\n\nDomain IV governs deployment and use: evaluating whether and how to deploy (use-case fit, model type, deployment environment), running the key pre-deployment assessments (impact assessment of the selected system, vendor and licensing terms, proprietary-model risks), and then governing the operating system — policies, monitoring, audits, incidents, records, communications, and eventual retirement. The unifying idea is that governance does not end at launch — it intensifies.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nRoughly 21–25 questions, and IV.C alone carries 9–11 of them. Scenarios dominate: choosing deployment options, vetting vendor terms, degraded accuracy discovered by monitoring, incidents requiring disclosure, systems needing rollback or retirement. The tested instinct is continuous verification — monitor, investigate root causes, document, remediate, communicate.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Spans BOK subdomains IV.A (deployment evaluation), IV.B (assessment activities), and IV.C (governing deployment & use)\n- IV.A weighs use-case context, model-type differences, and deployment options — including fine-tuning, RAG and agentic architectures — before anything ships\n- IV.B runs the due diligence: an impact assessment on the selected system, vendor and licensing terms, and the risks and opportunities of deploying a proprietary model\n- IV.C is the operating regime: policies, continuous monitoring, audits and red teaming, incident documentation, external communications, deactivation\n- The EU AI Act mandates post-market monitoring and serious-incident reporting for high-risk systems\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] AI Lifecycle Index\n\n# Governing the AI Lifecycle\n\n**AIGP Domains III & IV** — Governing AI Development, Deployment, and Use\n\n| Chapter | Topic | Subdomain |\n|---------|-------|-----------|\n| [[AI System Design]] | Lifecycle documentation, business context, impact assessments, design risks | III.A |\n| [[Data Governance and Model Training]] | Data governance, lineage/provenance, testing methodologies | III.B |\n| [[Deployment and Monitoring]] | Pre-release readiness, deployment, continuous monitoring | III.C |\n| [[AI Risk Management and Assurance]] | Risk forecasting, audits/testing, incident management/disclosures | III.C, IV.B |\n| [[Ongoing AI Operations]] | Business records, external communications, system retirement | IV.C |\n\n## Key Themes\n- The full AI system lifecycle from design to retirement\n- Data governance and model training best practices\n- Deployment readiness and continuous monitoring\n- Risk management, assurance, and incident response\n\n---\n\n> [!source] AIGP Body of Knowledge — Exam Blueprint\n\n## Domain IV: Governing AI Deployment & Use (21–25 questions)\n\nSelecting an AI model, then deploying and using it responsibly through ongoing monitoring, maintenance and other key obligations — in any deployment context, whether the company deploys its own proprietary model or a third party's.\n\n### IV.A — Evaluate key factors and risks in the decision to deploy (6–8 q)\n- **Context of the use case** (business objectives, performance requirements, data availability, ethical considerations, workforce readiness)\n- **AI model types** (classic vs generative, proprietary vs open source, small vs large, language vs multimodal)\n- **Deployment options** (cloud vs on-premise vs edge; as-is vs fine-tuning, retrieval-augmented generation, **agentic architectures** or other techniques)\n\n### IV.B — Perform key assessment activities (5–7 q)\n- Perform or review an **impact assessment** on the selected system\n- Identify and evaluate key terms and risks in the **vendor or licensing agreement**\n- Risks and opportunities unique to deploying a **proprietary model** (increased obligations, higher potential liability)\n\n### IV.C — Govern deployment and use (9–11 q)\n- Apply **policies, procedures, best practices and ethics** (data governance, risk management, issue management, user training)\n- **Continuous monitoring** and a regular maintenance, update and retraining schedule\n- **Periodic assessments** (audits, red teaming, threat modeling, security testing)\n- **Document** incidents, issues, risks and post-market monitoring plans\n- Forecast and reduce risks of **secondary or unintended uses and downstream harms**\n- **External communication plans**\n- Policy and controls to **deactivate or localize** a system (regulatory requirements, performance issues)\n\n### 📎 Cross-references\n- [[Deployment and Monitoring]] — IV.A, IV.C\n- [[AI Risk Management and Assurance]] — IV.B, IV.C\n- [[Ongoing AI Operations]] — IV.C\n- [[Agentic AI Governance]] — IV.A agentic architectures\n- [[GPAI Code of Practice — Key Provisions]]\n\n---\n\n---\n\n> [!source] Framework Recall Cards — AIGP Study Kit\n\n## Deployment, monitoring & retirement\n\n*~27% of scored items per BoK v2.1 (the Wiley guide says 28%). Scenario-heavy.*\n\n### Deployment environments and when each wins\n\n**Cloud** — scale and elasticity; less infrastructure control.\n**On-premises** — maximum control and data residency; capital cost.\n**Edge** — low latency, works offline, data never leaves the device. The answer whenever latency + outage tolerance + on-site sensitive data appear together.\n**Hybrid** — split; check *where inference actually happens* before choosing it.\n\n### Adaptation approaches — as-is, fine-tune, RAG, from scratch\n\n**As-is / prompting** — fastest, no training, limited by context window.\n**Fine-tuning** — adapts weights to domain; costly, and knowledge is frozen at training time.\n**RAG** — retrieves from an external knowledge base **at inference**. Updates take effect immediately and outputs can **cite sources**. The answer for frequently-changing corpora.\n**From scratch** — rarely proportionate.\n\n### Drift types\n\n**Data drift** — the **input distribution** shifts.\n**Concept drift** — the **relationship between inputs and outputs** shifts; the world changed, so the same inputs now imply different outcomes.\n**Model decay** — the general performance degradation over time.\nTell: 'input quality unchanged but behavior/patterns changed' → concept drift.\n\n### Human oversight — the three configurations, and what makes it meaningful\n\n**In the loop** — human approves each decision.\n**On the loop** — human monitors and can intervene.\n**Out of the loop** — fully autonomous.\n\nOversight is meaningful only when the human has **genuine authority and practical capacity to intervene** — time, information, training, and the power to override or halt. Bigger review samples and better dashboards improve visibility without conferring control.\n\n### Release patterns\n\n**Shadow** — runs on real traffic, outputs not served; compares a candidate against the incumbent with zero user risk.\n**Canary** — small share of live traffic.\n**Blue-green** — instant switch with rollback.\n**A/B** — controlled comparison.\n\n### Post-market monitoring vs a dashboard\n\nA **documented, forward-looking plan established before release**: what real-world data is collected, how it's reviewed, by whom, what thresholds trigger action, and how **serious incidents** get reported. Dashboards are one input. Metrics without thresholds and a defined escalation path produce reports nobody acts on.\n\n### Incident response order for an AI system\n\n**Contain → assess → investigate → remediate → notify → learn.**\nIf personal data leaked, **breach assessment and statutory notification clocks run independently of the technical fix**. Never retrain first (destroys evidence), never disable logging (destroys the audit trail).\n\n### Retirement done properly\n\n**Controlled deactivation** · **preserve model versions, decision logs and documentation** per retention obligations · notify affected external stakeholders · document lessons learned · transition or migrate dependent processes.\nAffected individuals may contest past decisions and regulators may examine them long after shutdown, so prompt deletion destroys exactly what you need.\n\n### Secondary use — the trap\n\nApproval attaches to a **use case in context**, not to a model in the abstract. Repurposing an approved model for a different decision, population, or consequence requires a **fresh impact assessment, risk review and approval**. Updating the model card documents the change; it doesn't authorize it.", "ch1": "## At a Glance\n\nThis knowledge area defines the technology being governed and makes the case for governing it. It walks the AI taxonomies (capability, functionality, technique, learning paradigm), catalogs the risks and harms AI creates for individuals, groups, organizations and society, identifies the characteristics that make AI uniquely hard to govern, and lands on the responsible AI principles that answer those characteristics. Four performance indicators, four sections, one vocabulary.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nI.A is the domain's vocabulary engine. Each of its four performance indicators maps onto one section of the knowledge area, and the exam tests them separately.\n\n### 1. Definitions and types of AI\n\nThree overlapping taxonomies get used, and questions mix them freely:\n\n| Taxonomy | Levels | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Capability or scope** | Narrow (weak) AI to artificial general intelligence to artificial superintelligence | Only narrow AI exists today, whatever a product page claims |\n| **Functionality** | Reactive machines, limited memory, theory of mind, self-aware | The last two are conceptual only |\n| **Learning paradigm** | Supervised, unsupervised, semi-supervised, reinforcement, self-supervised | This is how practitioners actually describe a system |\n\nThe legally operative definition is not in any of those tables. Both the OECD's 2023 revision and Article 3 of the EU AI Act define an AI system functionally: a machine-based system that, for explicit or implicit objectives, **infers** from the input it receives how to generate outputs such as predictions, content, recommendations or decisions that can influence physical or virtual environments. \"Infers\" is the load-bearing word, because it is what separates AI from ordinary rule-following software. ISO/IEC 22989 supplies the parallel formal vocabulary (AI system, AI model, AI component, and the role terms provider, producer, customer, user and AI subject) that the management-system standards lean on.\n\n### 2. Risks and harms\n\nThe BOK names three examples for this indicator: **misalignment with objectives**, **ethics and bias risk**, and **complexity and scalability**. Around them sits the standard four-way breakdown by who is harmed:\n\n| Who is harmed | Representative harms |\n|---|---|\n| **Individuals** | Privacy invasion, discriminatory decisions in employment or credit, safety failures, manipulation, loss of autonomy |\n| **Groups** | Bias amplification against protected classes, feedback loops that compound disadvantage, erosion of social cohesion |\n| **Organizations** | Regulatory penalties, litigation, reputational damage, IP contamination, security exposure, operational failure |\n| **Society** | Misinformation and deepfakes, displacement of work, concentration of power, environmental cost |\n\nGenerative systems add their own signature failures: confabulation (the formal term for hallucination), synthetic media abuse at scale, and the privacy exposure that comes from training on unfiltered web data.\n\n### 3. Characteristics that demand governance\n\nThe BOK's list is: **complexity, opacity, autonomy, speed and scale, potential for harm or misuse, data dependency, and probabilistic versus deterministic outputs.** The useful mental move is to pair each characteristic with the control it motivates, because exam items usually ask why a control exists:\n\n| Characteristic | Why it defeats ordinary software governance | Control it motivates |\n|---|---|---|\n| Complexity | Many interacting components and emergent behaviour resist review and root-cause analysis | Layered testing, documentation, auditability |\n| Opacity | Even builders cannot fully explain a deep model's output | Explainability work, model cards, disclosure duties |\n| Autonomy | Decisions execute without a human between inference and effect | Human oversight design, action limits, kill switches |\n| Speed and scale | One flaw replicates across millions of decisions before anyone notices | Pre-deployment testing, continuous monitoring, rollback |\n| Potential for harm or misuse | Surveillance, manipulation, weaponisation, fraud | Impact assessment, ethics review, security controls |\n| Data dependency | Quality, provenance and bias of training data drive outcomes | Data governance, lineage, bias testing |\n| Probabilistic outputs | The system is statistically confident, not correct | Thresholds, confidence handling, monitoring, user training |\n\n### 4. Responsible AI principles\n\nThe BOK lists **fairness, safety and reliability, privacy and security, transparency and explainability, accountability, and human-centricity.** These are the same substance as the OECD's five principles and NIST's seven trustworthiness characteristics, relabelled; the exam expects you to recognise the concept under whichever label the stem uses.\n\nOne distinction is worth committing to memory because it is a reliable distractor: **transparency** is about the organization disclosing that AI is in use and how, while **explainability** is about whether a specific output can be understood by the person affected by it. **Interpretability** sits underneath both, as the property of a model whose internal behaviour can be inspected at all.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nThe exam's vocabulary heartland, and the place where definitional questions cluster. Be able to classify an example system instantly: a recommender is narrow AI, a system that perceives its environment and acts on it is an agent, a model trained on labelled pairs is supervised learning, a rule-based medical advisor is an expert system. Then be able to match a harm or a characteristic to the principle or control that answers it.\n\nTwo traps repeat. First, options that treat a current product as general AI or as self-aware are wrong by definition; only narrow AI exists. Second, transparency, explainability and interpretability are routinely offered as interchangeable answers, and only one of them fits the stem. Read which party the question is asking about: the organization disclosing, or the affected individual understanding a decision.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- I.A has four performance indicators: definitions and types, risks and harms, characteristics requiring governance, and responsible AI principles\n- Capability ladder: narrow or weak AI (exists), AGI (theoretical), ASI (theoretical); functionality ladder: reactive, limited memory, theory of mind, self-aware\n- Learning paradigms: supervised (labelled pairs), unsupervised (structure in unlabelled data), semi-supervised, reinforcement (reward signals), self-supervised\n- The legally operative definition is functional and inference-based (OECD 2023, EU AI Act Article 3), not capability-based\n- The BOK's harm examples are misalignment with objectives, ethics and bias risk, and complexity and scalability\n- Harms are usually sorted by who is affected: individuals, groups, organizations, society\n- The BOK's governance-demanding characteristics: complexity, opacity, autonomy, speed and scale, potential for harm or misuse, data dependency, probabilistic versus deterministic outputs\n- The BOK's responsible AI principles: fairness, safety and reliability, privacy and security, transparency and explainability, accountability, human-centricity\n- Transparency (the organization discloses) is not explainability (an output can be understood); interpretability is the underlying model property\n- ISO/IEC 22989 is the source of the formal terminology; the OECD and the EU AI Act are the sources of the operative definition\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://oecd.ai/en/wonk/definition\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OECD: what is an AI system?</a> · the 2023 definitional revision\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/3/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 3 (definitions)</a> · the inference-based definition in binding form\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · AI system, AI model, AI component and the role terms\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1), trustworthiness characteristics</a> · seven characteristics: valid and reliable, safe, secure and resilient, accountable and transparent, explainable and interpretable, privacy-enhanced, fair\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 600-1, Generative AI Profile</a> · the twelve GAI-specific risks, including confabulation\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/SpecialPublications/NIST.SP.1270.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST SP 1270, Towards a Standard for Identifying and Managing Bias in AI</a> · systemic, computational and human-cognitive bias\n- <a href=\"https://oecd.ai/en/ai-principles\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OECD AI Principles</a> · the intergovernmental principle set the BOK's list parallels\n- <a href=\"https://aiindex.stanford.edu/report/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Stanford AI Index Report</a> · annual evidence base for capability, adoption and harm trends\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] AI and AI Governance\n\n## Performance Indicators\n\n- Know the generally accepted **definitions and types of AI**\n- Understand the **differences in AI model types**\n- Identify **types of risks and harms** posed by AI to individuals, groups, organizations, and society\n- Identify **unique characteristics of AI** requiring comprehensive governance\n- Identify and apply **common principles of responsible AI**\n\n---\n\n---\n\n> [!source] AI and AI Governance\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n1. **Types of AI systems:** Narrow/Weak AI, General AI (AGI), Superintelligence (ASI). AI functions: reactive machines, limited-memory AI, theory of mind AI, robotics.\n\n2. **Characteristics requiring governance:** Complexity, opacity, autonomy, speed/scale, potential for misuse and harm.\n\n3. **Principles of responsible AI:** Ethics, fairness, privacy, security, transparency, explainability, accountability.\n\n---", "t_ai_types": "## At a Glance\n\nThe classification system for everything the exam calls \"AI\": capability levels (narrow AI that exists, AGI and ASI that do not yet), functionality levels (reactive machines through the theoretical self-aware systems), the machine-learning family tree (supervised, unsupervised, semi-supervised, reinforcement, self-supervised), and the modern generative stack (neural networks, deep learning, transformers, large language models, foundation models).\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nFour taxonomies cover almost every classification question, and they answer different questions.\n\n### By capability\n\n| Level | Meaning | Exists today? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Narrow (weak) AI** | Performs a specific task or narrow task family | Yes. Every deployed system, however impressive |\n| **General AI (AGI)** | Human-level competence across essentially all cognitive domains | No |\n| **Superintelligence (ASI)** | Surpasses the best human performance in essentially every domain | No |\n\nThe trap here is breadth. A large language model answering questions about law, medicine and poetry is still narrow AI, because generality is about transfer across unrelated domains the way a person does it, not about the size of the training set.\n\n### By functionality\n\nReactive machines respond to input with no memory of it (the chess engine that evaluates only the current position). Limited memory systems retain recent context to inform the next decision (autonomous vehicles, and in practice most modern systems). Theory of mind and self-aware AI are conceptual categories, not deployed technology.\n\n### By learning paradigm\n\n| Paradigm | Data | Mechanism |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Supervised** | Labelled input-output pairs | Learns the mapping from labels |\n| **Unsupervised** | Unlabelled data | Finds structure, clusters, anomalies |\n| **Semi-supervised** | A little labelled plus a lot unlabelled | Uses structure to extend scarce labels |\n| **Reinforcement** | A reward signal from interaction | Learns a policy that maximises reward |\n| **Self-supervised** | Raw data, with the labels derived from the data itself | The paradigm behind modern language models |\n\n### The generative stack\n\nDeep learning stacks neural network layers; transformers made attention practical at scale and enabled large language models; foundation models are broad pretrained models adapted to downstream tasks by fine-tuning or prompting. Two governance-relevant properties come out of that lineage. Scale buys capability, sometimes abruptly: the literature on emergent abilities documents behaviours that appear only past a size threshold, which is why a model's capabilities cannot always be enumerated in advance. And pretrained models carry their training data's properties into every downstream use, including its biases.\n\n### Agents\n\nAn AI agent perceives its environment and takes action toward goals, rather than only returning a prediction when asked. Perception plus action is the tell. Agentic architectures matter to governance because they move AI from advising a human to acting on the world, which is where the oversight and action-limit questions begin.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nPure classification: given an example, name the type. A chess engine is a reactive machine; an autonomous vehicle uses limited memory; a chat assistant is narrow AI despite its breadth; a spam filter trained on labelled mail is supervised learning; a recommender finding clusters in unlabelled behaviour is unsupervised; a robot learning to navigate by reward is reinforcement learning. Get these to reflex speed, because they are free points and they underpin harder scenario items later.\n\nThe recurring trap is breadth versus generality, and the second is a system that acts. If a stem says the system takes actions in an environment, the answer is agent, not model.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Narrow or weak AI performs specific tasks and is the only kind that exists; AGI and ASI remain theoretical\n- Functionality ladder: reactive machines (no memory) to limited memory to theory of mind to self-aware, with the last two conceptual\n- Supervised learning uses labelled input-output pairs; unsupervised finds structure in unlabelled data; semi-supervised blends scarce labels with abundant unlabelled data\n- Reinforcement learning optimises a policy against a reward signal; self-supervised learning derives its own labels and underpins modern language models\n- Deep learning stacks neural network layers; transformers enabled large language models; foundation models are broad pretrained models adapted downstream\n- Scale can produce emergent abilities that appear only past a threshold, so a model's capability set is not always enumerable in advance\n- An AI agent perceives its environment and acts toward goals; perception plus action is the distinguishing feature\n- Breadth is not generality: an LLM covering many subjects is still narrow AI\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://oecd.ai/en/wonk/definition\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OECD: what is an AI system?</a> · the definitional baseline the BOK uses\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · formal definitions for the learning paradigms and system terms\n- <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.07682\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Emergent Abilities of Large Language Models</a> · capabilities that appear only past a scale threshold\n- <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.08774\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GPT-4 Technical Report</a> · how a frontier model's capabilities are documented and bounded\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · AI system and AI actor definitions, and the risk framing\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] AI and AI Governance\n\n## 1. Types of AI\n\n### Definition\n\n**Artificial Intelligence (AI)** — the simulation of human intelligence processes by machines (learning, reasoning, problem-solving, perception, language understanding).\n\n- **Narrow/specific AI**: Single-task systems (image recognition, speech processing)\n- **Broad AI**: Multi-domain systems (LLMs like Claude, Copilot)\n\n### AI Capabilities (by scope)\n\n| Level | Description | Exists? |\n|-------|-------------|---------|\n| **Narrow AI (Weak AI)** | Designed for a specific task; no general intelligence | Yes (ChatGPT, Deep Blue) |\n| **AI Agent** | Autonomous system perceiving environment, taking action toward goals | Yes |\n| **General AI (AGI)** | Human-level intelligence across all tasks | No |\n| **Artificial Superintelligence (ASI)** | Surpasses human intelligence in all domains | No |\n| **God-like AI** | Beyond ASI; incomprehensible to humans | No |\n\n> **Exam note:** AGI and ASI have not been publicly disclosed as of this writing.\n\n### AI Functionalities\n\n| Type | Description | Example |\n|------|-------------|---------|\n| **Reactive machines** | Respond to stimuli; no memory or learning | IBM Deep Blue |\n| **Limited memory AI** | Retain info short-term to improve decisions | Autonomous vehicles |\n| **Reasoning AI** | Analyze patterns, make predictions | Modern LLMs (bar exam) |\n| **Theory of mind AI** | Understand/simulate human emotions (theoretical) | HAL-9000 (fiction) |\n| **Self-aware AI** | Consciousness, self-recognition (theoretical) | Humanoid with introspection |\n| **Robotics** | Kinetic tasks (moving objects, cooking) | Factory robots |\n\n### AI Techniques\n\n| Technique | Description | Example |\n|-----------|-------------|---------|\n| **Machine Learning (ML)** | Learn from data without explicit programming | Spam filters |\n| **Deep Learning** | Multi-layer neural networks for unstructured data | Google Translate |\n| **Natural Language Processing (NLP)** | Understand/interpret/generate human language | ChatGPT |\n| **Expert Systems** | Simulate human expert decision-making via rules | Medical diagnosis |\n\n### Training Methods\n\n| Method | Data Type | Description |\n|--------|-----------|-------------|\n| **Supervised learning** | Labeled data | Spam filter trained on spam/legitimate examples |\n| **Unsupervised learning** | Unlabeled data | AI finds patterns on its own |\n| **Semi-supervised learning** | Small labeled + large unlabeled | Image recognition with limited training set |\n| **Reinforcement learning** | Rewards/penalties | Robot learning to navigate a maze |\n| **Zero-shot learning (ZSL)** | No prior examples | Recognize objects never seen before |\n\n---", "t_ai_risks": "## At a Glance\n\nThe harm catalog that motivates all of AI governance, organized by who gets hurt: individuals (privacy invasion, discriminatory decisions, safety), groups (bias amplification against protected classes), organizations (liability, reputational damage, IP leakage), and society (misinformation and deepfakes, displacement of work, eroded autonomy, environmental cost). The BOK names misalignment with objectives, ethics and bias risk, and complexity and scalability as its three examples.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\n### The four-way sort\n\n| Who is harmed | Representative harms |\n|---|---|\n| **Individuals** | Privacy violation, discriminatory decisions in hiring or credit, physical safety failure, manipulation, loss of autonomy |\n| **Groups** | Systematic bias against protected classes, amplification through feedback loops, erosion of shared factual ground |\n| **Organizations** | Regulatory penalty, litigation, reputational damage, IP contamination, security exposure, operational disruption |\n| **Society** | Misinformation and deepfakes, displacement of work, concentration of capability, environmental cost of training and inference |\n\nThe BOK's own three examples cut across the sort. **Misalignment with objectives** is the case where the system optimises the metric it was given rather than the outcome anyone wanted. **Ethics and bias risk** is unfair or harmful treatment that the data or the objective encoded. **Complexity and scalability** is the multiplier: a flaw in a manual process harms the people it touches, while the same flaw in a deployed model harms everyone it scores before anyone notices.\n\n### Generative-specific failures\n\nNIST's Generative AI Profile names twelve risks unique to or amplified by generative AI. The exam-relevant ones are **confabulation** (the formal term for hallucination: confidently stated, plausible, false output, which is a property of the paradigm rather than an occasional bug), **harmful bias and homogenization**, **information integrity** (the deepfake and disinformation problem), **data privacy**, **information security**, **intellectual property**, and **value-chain and component integration** risk, which is the risk you inherit from the models and data you did not build.\n\n### Why the sort matters operationally\n\nThe category determines who owns the response. An individual harm usually triggers a rights or recourse process; a group harm triggers fairness testing and remediation; an organizational harm runs through the risk register and the board; a societal harm may have no internal owner at all, which is exactly why impact assessments exist. A harm that is not assigned to a category tends not to get a control.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nQuestions ask you to identify the harm category in a scenario, or to pick the governance response proportionate to it. Distinguish individual from group from societal harm, and remember that a single incident usually lands in several categories at once: an employment screening model that penalises a protected class harms the applicants, the class, the employer and the labour market.\n\nHallucination or confabulation is the signature generative risk and a frequent correct answer. An option asserting that a well-tested model cannot be confidently wrong is wrong by construction.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- The BOK's three harm examples: misalignment with objectives, ethics and bias risk, complexity and scalability\n- Individual harms: privacy violation, unfair or discriminatory decisions, physical safety failure, manipulation, loss of autonomy\n- Group harms: systematic bias against protected classes, amplified by feedback loops and proxy variables\n- Organizational harms: regulatory penalties, litigation, reputational damage, security exposure, IP contamination\n- Societal harms: misinformation and deepfakes, displacement of work, concentration of capability, environmental cost\n- Confabulation (hallucination) is the signature generative risk: confidently stated, plausible, false output, structural to the paradigm\n- NIST AI 600-1's twelve GAI risks include confabulation, harmful bias and homogenization, information integrity, data privacy, information security, intellectual property and value-chain integration\n- Complexity and scalability turn a local flaw into a systemic one, which is why pre-deployment testing and monitoring exist\n- A single incident usually spans several harm categories, so assign each harm an owner or it gets no control\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 600-1, Generative AI Profile</a> · the twelve GAI risks and the actions mapped to each\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/SpecialPublications/NIST.SP.1270.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST SP 1270, bias in AI</a> · systemic, computational and human-cognitive bias\n- <a href=\"https://oecd.ai/en/incidents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OECD AI Incidents and Hazards Monitor</a> · real, catalogued AI incidents and near-miss hazards\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/5/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 5 (prohibited practices)</a> · the harms serious enough to be banned outright\n- <a href=\"https://aiindex.stanford.edu/report/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Stanford AI Index Report</a> · annual data on adoption, capability and documented harms\n- <a href=\"https://www.brookings.edu/articles/algorithmic-bias-detection-and-mitigation-best-practices-and-policies-to-reduce-consumer-harms/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Brookings: algorithmic bias detection and mitigation</a> · how bias harms arise and what mitigations exist\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] AI and AI Governance\n\n## 2. Risks and Harms Posed by AI\n\n### Risks to Individuals\n\n- **Privacy violations** — RAG systems, facial recognition without consent, training on non-anonymized PII\n- **Bias and discrimination** — Employment screening, loan applications, financial aid; training data reflects historical inequities\n- **Cybersecurity risks** — Identity theft, financial loss, exposed private data; orgs deploying AI without adequate security\n- **Loss of visibility, autonomy, control** — Humans taken out of the loop in healthcare, financial services\n\n### Risks to Groups\n\n- **Amplification of social inequality** — Loan qualification bias against communities\n- **Erosion of social cohesion** — Echo chambers, polarization, algorithmic content favoring sensationalism\n- **Cybersecurity risks** — Critical infrastructure AI vulnerabilities\n\n### Risks to Organizations\n\n- **Operational** — AI failure disrupting inventory, fraud detection, customer service\n- **Legal and regulatory** — Violating emerging AI/privacy regulations\n- **Financial** — AI trading losses, cost overruns\n- **Cybersecurity** — AI-powered security tools making mistakes; poisoning attacks\n- **Supply chain** — Vendor-compromise attacks (SolarWinds pattern)\n- **Misalignment** — Benign testing behavior leading to harmful deployment outcomes\n- **Reputation** — Public disclosure of any realized risk\n\n### Risks to Society\n\n- **Job displacement** — Automation in manufacturing, transport, IT, customer service\n- **Economic inequality** — Biased training data causing unequal treatment\n- **Erosion of human autonomy** — Over-reliance on algorithmic governance\n- **Ethical and moral challenges** — Life-or-death autonomous decisions (trolley problems)\n- **Cybersecurity and privacy** — Large-scale infrastructure attacks\n- **Manipulation of public opinion** — Algorithmic content control for political gain\n\n> **Key insight:** Risks rarely stay isolated to one level — an employment screening bias harms the applicant (individual), their community (group), the employer (organization), and regional fairness (society).\n\n---", "t_ai_char": "## At a Glance\n\nWhy AI cannot be governed like ordinary software: models are opaque (even builders cannot fully explain outputs), autonomous (acting without per-decision human review), probabilistic (confidently wrong), fast and scalable (one flaw replicated across millions of decisions), emergent (capabilities appearing unplanned), and data-hungry (creating pressure that breaks privacy assumptions). The BOK names seven: complexity, opacity, autonomy, speed and scale, potential for harm or misuse, data dependency, and probabilistic versus deterministic outputs.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe exam almost always asks this indicator indirectly: \"which characteristic of AI makes this control necessary?\" So learn each characteristic as a pair with the control it justifies.\n\n| Characteristic | What it defeats | Control it justifies |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Complexity** | Human intuition and simple review; many interacting parts and emergent behaviour | Layered testing, documentation, auditability, decomposition into components |\n| **Opacity** | The ability to explain a specific decision to the person affected | Explainability techniques, model cards, disclosure duties, contestability |\n| **Autonomy** | The assumption that a human is in the decision loop | Human oversight design, action limits, interruption and override, kill switches |\n| **Speed and scale** | The assumption that errors are caught locally and slowly | Pre-deployment testing, continuous monitoring, defined rollback criteria |\n| **Potential for harm or misuse** | The assumption that capability is used as intended | Impact assessment, ethics review, security controls, red teaming |\n| **Data dependency** | The assumption that behaviour is specified in code | Data governance, lineage and provenance, representativeness and bias testing |\n| **Probabilistic outputs** | Binary notions of correct and incorrect | Confidence thresholds, abstention, monitoring, user training against over-trust |\n\n### The two that surprise people\n\n**Probabilistic versus deterministic output** is easy to gloss over and heavily tested. Traditional software is deterministic: the same input yields the same output, and a defect is a deviation from specification. A model produces a statistical estimate, so the same input may yield different outputs, and being wrong is a property of the paradigm rather than a bug. That is why \"the model is accurate 97% of the time\" is a governance statement about residual risk, not a clean bill of health.\n\n**Data dependency** is what makes AI governance inseparable from data governance. The system's behaviour is learned from data whose provenance, representativeness and quality determine the outcome, which is why lineage, provenance and bias testing appear in every framework and, for high-risk systems, in law.\n\n### Emergence is the odd one out\n\nThe BOK's list is a set of characteristics, not a taxonomy, so it is worth adding the one that sits behind several of them: **emergence**. Capabilities and behaviours can appear after deployment through scale, fine-tuning, data drift or interaction with other systems. Emergence is the reason point-in-time approval is insufficient and why monitoring and re-assessment are continuous rather than one-off.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nExpect stems of the form \"which characteristic of AI makes X necessary.\" Opacity motivates explainability requirements; autonomy motivates human oversight; speed and scale motivate pre-deployment testing and rollback planning; probabilistic output motivates confidence handling, monitoring and user training; data dependency motivates lineage and bias testing.\n\nThe most reliable distractor is determinism. An option that assumes a model behaves like conventional software (same input, same output, defects trace to code) contradicts the probabilistic-output characteristic, so it is wrong whenever the stem involves a learned model.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- The BOK's seven characteristics: complexity, opacity, autonomy, speed and scale, potential for harm or misuse, data dependency, probabilistic versus deterministic outputs\n- Complexity motivates layered testing, documentation and decomposition into components\n- Opacity motivates explainability work, model cards, disclosure and contestability\n- Autonomy motivates oversight design, action limits, interruption and override\n- Speed and scale motivate pre-deployment testing, monitoring and defined rollback criteria\n- Potential for harm or misuse motivates impact assessment, ethics review and security controls\n- Data dependency is why AI governance is inseparable from data governance: lineage, provenance, representativeness, bias testing\n- Probabilistic output means the same input may produce different outputs and errors are a property of the paradigm, not a defect\n- Emergence (behaviour appearing after deployment) is why point-in-time approval is insufficient and monitoring is continuous\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · how each characteristic maps to a trustworthiness characteristic\n- <a href=\"https://www.iso.org/standard/77304.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 23894 AI risk management</a> · Annex B catalogs AI risk sources including data quality, explainability and degree of automation\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-2e2023.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 100-2, Adversarial Machine Learning taxonomy</a> · evasion, poisoning and privacy attacks against models\n- <a href=\"https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/artificial-intelligence/explaining-decisions-made-with-artificial-intelligence/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ICO: explaining decisions made with AI</a> · what a meaningful explanation of an automated decision looks like\n- <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.07682\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Emergent Abilities of Large Language Models</a> · capability appearing only past a scale threshold\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] AI and AI Governance\n\n## 3. Characteristics of AI Requiring Governance\n\n| Characteristic | Challenge | Governance Response |\n|----------------|-----------|---------------------|\n| **Complexity** | Deep learning interrelationships hard to trace | Explainability standards, documentation, auditability |\n| **Opacity** | \"Black box\" — unclear how inputs become outputs | Require explainability, transparency in design |\n| **Autonomy** | AI acts without human intervention; evolves over time | Define limits, safety measures, accountability, monitoring |\n| **Speed and scale** | Real-time decisions across millions of users | Real-time monitoring, response protocols, resource planning |\n| **Potential for harm/misuse** | Surveillance, autonomous weapons, malicious use | Ethical oversight, accountability, security controls |\n| **Data dependency** | Quality/volume/bias of training data drives outcomes | Data quality standards, security, bias detection |\n| **Privacy** | PII in training data; can't reliably \"untrain\" AI | Anonymize before training; comply with GDPR, privacy policies |\n| **Intellectual property** | Training on copyrighted content; no US legal precedent (2025) | Vet all training data for copyright; workforce policies on public AI use |\n| **Probabilistic vs. deterministic** | Outputs are probabilities, not certainties | Define acceptable thresholds, risk appetite, transparency |\n\n> **Notable:** Getty Images v. Stability AI (filed 2023) — decided by the UK High Court on 4 Nov 2025 ([2025] EWHC 2863 (Ch)). Getty dropped its training claim (no evidence training took place in the UK), and its secondary infringement claim failed because model weights that never stored the images are not an \"infringing copy\"; Getty won only limited trade mark findings over watermarks. Permission to appeal was granted in Dec 2025. See [[Sectoral and Civil Laws]].\n\n---", "t_rai": "## At a Glance\n\nThe principle set that answers AI's risk profile. The BOK's list is fairness, safety and reliability, privacy and security, transparency and explainability, accountability, and human-centricity. These recur in every major framework (the OECD's five principles, NIST's seven trustworthiness characteristics, the EU AI Act's requirements) with different labels but the same substance, which is why the exam can test them under any framework's name.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\n### The BOK's six, and what each demands\n\n| Principle | What it requires | How it is operationalized |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Fairness** | Equitable treatment and outcomes across groups | Bias testing across subgroups, fairness metrics chosen for context, mitigation, documentation of trade-offs |\n| **Safety and reliability** | Intended function under expected and unexpected conditions | Rigorous testing, edge-case and adversarial testing, fail-safes, interruptability, continuous monitoring |\n| **Privacy and security** | Personal data protected, systems resilient to attack | Minimization, de-identification, access control, AI-specific threat modelling, pipeline security |\n| **Transparency and explainability** | People know AI is in use and can understand outcomes affecting them | Disclosure, instructions for use, explanations of decisions, documentation, contestability |\n| **Accountability** | Identified humans answer for outcomes | Named owners, RACI, escalation paths, audit rights, records |\n| **Human-centricity** | AI augments human agency rather than overriding it | Human oversight for consequential decisions, impact assessment, design that preserves choice |\n\n### Where the labels come from\n\nThe same substance appears under three vocabularies, and the exam expects you to translate:\n\n- **OECD (2019, revised 2024)**: inclusive growth and well-being; human rights, rule of law and democratic values (including fairness and privacy); transparency and explainability; robustness, security and safety; accountability.\n- **NIST AI RMF**: valid and reliable; safe; secure and resilient; accountable and transparent; explainable and interpretable; privacy-enhanced; fair with harmful bias managed.\n- **EU AI Act**: risk management, data governance, technical documentation, record-keeping, transparency and instructions for use, human oversight, accuracy, robustness and cybersecurity for high-risk systems, plus the Article 50 transparency duties.\n\n### The distinctions worth memorizing\n\n**Transparency versus explainability.** Transparency is about the organization: that AI is in use, what it does, what its limits are. Explainability is about a specific output: whether the person affected can understand why it came out that way. Interpretability is the underlying model property that makes explanation possible at all.\n\n**Accountability versus responsibility.** Responsibility is who does the work; accountability is who answers for the outcome. Accountability cannot be delegated to a model or a vendor, which is the failure mode every accountability framework exists to prevent.\n\n**Fairness is plural.** Several fairness metrics cannot hold simultaneously except in degenerate cases, so \"make it fair\" is not a specification. The governance answer is to choose metrics appropriate to the context, document the trade-off, and test across subgroups.\n\n### Principles are the tiebreaker\n\nWhen two exam answers both look defensible, the one that honours more of these principles, and does so with documentation and proportionality, is almost always the intended answer.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nExpect matching questions (principle to definition, or principle to the framework that names it) and scenarios asking which principle a control serves. Transparency versus explainability is the classic trap pair, and so is accountability versus responsibility.\n\nA second pattern: a scenario describes a control (a bias audit, an oversight checkpoint, a disclosure notice, a model card) and asks which principle it advances. Bias audit is fairness; oversight checkpoint is human-centricity or accountability depending on whether the question is about the human or the owner; disclosure is transparency; a model card is usually transparency and explainability together. Where an option bundles the principles into a single-function answer (\"assign it to legal\"), it is wrong.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- The BOK's six principles: fairness, safety and reliability, privacy and security, transparency and explainability, accountability, human-centricity\n- Fairness requires context-appropriate metrics, subgroup testing and documented trade-offs, because fairness metrics mathematically conflict\n- Safety and reliability means intended function under expected and unexpected conditions, with fail-safes and graceful failure\n- Privacy and security spans data minimization, de-identification, access control and AI-specific threat modelling\n- Transparency is about the organization disclosing AI use; explainability is about an individual output being understandable; interpretability is the underlying model property\n- Accountability is non-delegable: named humans answer for outcomes, and responsibility is who does the work\n- Human-centricity means AI augments human agency, which is operationalized through oversight, impact assessment and preserved choice\n- The same substance appears as the OECD's five principles and NIST's seven trustworthiness characteristics, so translate between labels\n- These principles are the tiebreaker logic for exam scenarios: the answer honouring more of them usually wins\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://oecd.ai/en/ai-principles\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OECD AI Principles</a> · the intergovernmental baseline, adopted 2019 and revised 2024\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · the seven trustworthiness characteristics\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/5/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 5 (prohibited practices)</a> · where principle becomes prohibition\n- <a href=\"https://www.unesco.org/en/artificial-intelligence/recommendation-ethics\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">UNESCO Recommendation on the Ethics of AI</a> · the 193-state ethical framework\n- <a href=\"https://www.coe.int/en/web/artificial-intelligence/the-framework-convention-on-artificial-intelligence\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Council of Europe Framework Convention on AI</a> · the first binding international AI treaty\n- <a href=\"https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/artificial-intelligence/explaining-decisions-made-with-artificial-intelligence/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ICO: explaining decisions made with AI</a> · explainability in operational terms\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] AI and AI Governance\n\n## 4. Principles of Responsible AI\n\n### Ethics\n- Foundation of responsible AI; must align with human rights, dignity, social good\n- **Challenge:** Ethics can be subjective and manipulated; AI can conflict with moral values\n- **Application:** Ethical audits by objective external parties, impact assessments, stakeholder engagement\n\n### Fairness\n- AI systems must not discriminate based on race, gender, religion, ethnicity, disability, socioeconomic status\n- **Challenge:** Training data reflects historical inequities → amplified bias\n- **Application:** Diverse/representative training data, regular bias audits, bias mitigation techniques\n\n### Safety and Reliability\n- AI must function as intended even with unexpected inputs\n- **Challenge:** Malfunctions in safety-critical contexts (autonomous vehicles, healthcare)\n- **Application:** Rigorous testing, fail-safes, interruptability, fault tolerance, continuous monitoring\n\n### Privacy\n- Comply with all applicable privacy laws; minimize PII collection and use\n- **Challenge:** Organizations often exceed their own privacy policies with AI data use\n- **Application:** Data minimization, deidentification, security measures\n\n### Security\n- Protect sensitive data (PII, IP, copyrighted content) from unauthorized access\n- **Challenge:** Organizations prioritize innovation over security; AI subverts traditional trust boundaries\n- **Application:** Asset inventory → risk assessment → safeguards → due care\n\n### Transparency and Explainability\n\n> **Transparency** = understanding how AI functions. **Interpretability** = understanding how it reaches conclusions. **Explainability** = ability to provide understandable justifications.\n\n- **Challenge:** Neural networks operate as black boxes; can't explain loan/employment rejections\n- **Application:** Prioritize **Explainable AI (XAI)** for decision-making contexts\n\n### Accountability\n- Individuals and organizations are responsible for AI outcomes; can't blame the system or vendor\n- **Challenge:** Autonomous decisions make it tempting to deflect responsibility\n- **Application:** Clear policy assigning responsibility for design, deployment, monitoring; dispute resolution mechanisms\n\n> **Key:** Accountability should lie with **deployers** (not just developers). When deployers are accountable, security becomes a priority.\n\n### Human-centricity\n- AI should augment human capabilities, not replace or diminish them\n- **Challenge:** Financial efficiency prioritized over human values → worker displacement\n- **Application:** Human-centered design, impact assessments, **human in the loop** for consequential decisions\n\n---", "ch2": "## At a Glance\n\nThis knowledge area is about the humans: which roles carry AI governance (from board and C-suite through data scientists and privacy professionals), how cross-functional collaboration is structured, what training and awareness programs must cover, how governance is tailored to organizational context, and the developer / provider / deployer / user distinction that allocates responsibility along the AI value chain. Its underlying claim is that governance is a shared responsibility, not a department.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nI.B is the organizational half of Domain I, and the BOK gives it five performance indicators covering roles, collaboration, training, context and value-chain position.\n\n### Roles and responsibilities\n\nThe BOK asks you to \"define roles and responsibilities for AI governance stakeholders\" without prescribing an org chart, which is the point: governance is a shared responsibility spanning disciplines, and should not be centralized in a single team. In practice an effective program has three layers.\n\n| Layer | Who | What they own |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Direction** | Board, CEO, CRO, CISO, CDAO, CPO, CCO | Risk appetite, funding, tone, accountability for outcomes |\n| **Steering** | A cross-functional AI governance board or council | Framework ownership, high-risk use-case review, escalation as final reviewer, reporting upward |\n| **Operating** | Legal, privacy, security, data science, AI ops, ethics, compliance, product owners, frontline users | Building, assessing, monitoring, documenting, reporting anomalies |\n\nThe BOK's framing of governance as part of overall corporate **due care** is worth internalizing: organizations that face legal trouble over an AI system are judged worse when no governance program existed at all.\n\n### Cross-functional collaboration\n\nThe BOK states the purpose of collaboration directly: efficacy and **diversity of expertise and perspective**. A single function rarely has both business relevance and technical depth, so centralizing governance produces either an academic exercise or a rubber stamp. The practical instruments are a standing cross-functional working group, use-case reviews triggered by defined risk conditions (automated decisions affecting rights, sensitive or biometric data, employment or lending or medical impact, low explainability, rising regulatory scrutiny), and shared artifacts such as model cards, risk assessment forms and impact assessments.\n\n### Training and awareness\n\nThe BOK requires a program delivered \"to all stakeholders on AI terminology, strategy and governance.\" Two design rules follow from that wording. First, awareness for everyone, depth by role: executives need risk literacy and regulatory exposure, developers need governance by design and bias testing, users need to understand capabilities, limits and appropriate reliance. Second, the program has to be maintained, because a curriculum written before the current obligations existed does not satisfy them. Since 2 February 2025 the EU AI Act has made AI literacy an explicit expectation for provider and deployer staff, which turns training from a culture initiative into a compliance item.\n\n### Tailoring to organizational context\n\nThe BOK asks you to differentiate governance by **company size, maturity, industry, products and services, objectives and risk tolerance.** That is a proportionality instruction: a startup governs with a principles charter, a named champion and lightweight checklists, while a large enterprise builds a federated structure wired into enterprise risk management, with tooling and automation. Maturity models (COBIT-style, from nonexistent through initial, repeatable, defined, managed, optimized) give you the vocabulary for the second axis. The exam-relevant nuance: high maturity is not automatically the goal, and low risk tolerance should never mean no governance, only more conservative gates.\n\n### Developers, providers, deployers and users\n\nBOK v2.1 adds **providers** to the roles this indicator names. The BOK also states, in its introduction, that \"developer\" and \"deployer\" are used as **task labels, not legal categories**, which matters because the EU AI Act uses a partly different cast (provider, deployer, importer, distributor) with obligations attached. Keep the two vocabularies separate in your head: the BOK's developer builds and trains, the deployer operationalizes and monitors, the user operates within intended bounds, and the provider is the entity that places the system on the market under its own name.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nScenario questions ask who should be involved, who is accountable, or what a committee's composition should achieve. The dominant pattern is that an answer concentrating AI governance in one function (legal, IT, data science) is engineered to be wrong; the correct answer is multidisciplinary, and usually names the affected business function too.\n\nThe developer / provider / deployer / user split matters here and returns in the EU AI Act's provider / deployer / importer / distributor roles. A reliable trap: an entity that rebrands or substantially modifies a high-risk system becomes the provider and inherits provider duties, so read the stem for who is acting under whose name. Also expect a training question where the wrong answers are one-size-fits-all; role-tailored awareness plus depth where it counts is the answer the BOK is looking for.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- AI governance is a shared responsibility, not a single team's job; the BOK frames it as part of corporate due care\n- Three layers: direction (board and executives set risk appetite and fund the program), steering (a cross-functional governance board owns the framework and reviews high-risk use cases), operating (legal, privacy, security, data science, AI ops, ethics, product, users)\n- Cross-functional collaboration exists for efficacy and diversity of expertise and perspective; siloed AI decisions are how governance failures start\n- Use-case reviews should be triggered by defined conditions: rights-affecting automated decisions, sensitive or biometric data, employment, lending or medical impact, low explainability, rising regulatory scrutiny\n- Training is awareness for everyone plus depth by role: executives (risk and strategy), developers (responsible build), users (capabilities, limits, appropriate reliance)\n- EU AI Act Article 4 made AI literacy a legal expectation for provider and deployer staff from 2 February 2025\n- Governance is tailored by size, maturity, industry, products and services, objectives and risk tolerance; there is no single template\n- Maturity models (COBIT-style) give the vocabulary for the formality axis, and the right target depends on sector and risk appetite, not on maximizing the level\n- BOK v2.1 adds providers to the role list; developer and deployer are task labels in the BOK, not legal categories\n- The EU AI Act's cast is provider, deployer, importer and distributor; rebranding or substantially modifying a high-risk system makes you the provider\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/4/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 4 (AI literacy)</a> · training as a legal expectation from 2 February 2025\n- <a href=\"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/ai-act-explained\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act explained (European Commission)</a> · the provider / deployer / importer / distributor roles in context\n- <a href=\"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF Playbook, Govern function</a> · suggested actions for roles, accountability and culture (GV1 to GV6)\n- <a href=\"https://www.iso.org/standard/42001\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 42001 (AI management system)</a> · clause 5 on leadership and roles, and Annex A organizational controls\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · the Govern function as the cross-cutting foundation\n- <a href=\"https://iapp.org/resources/article/key-terms-for-ai-governance/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IAPP Key Terms for AI Governance</a> · role and stakeholder terminology\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Organizational Readiness\n\n## Performance Indicators\n\n- **Define roles and responsibilities** for AI governance stakeholders.\n- **Establish cross-functional collaboration** in the AI governance program (for efficacy and diversity of expertise and perspective).\n- **Create and deliver a training and awareness program** to all stakeholders on AI terminology, strategy, and governance.\n- **Differentiate approaches to AI governance** based upon company size, maturity, industry, products and services, objectives, and risk tolerance.\n- **Identify differences among AI developers, providers, deployers, and users** from a governance perspective (responsibilities, opportunities, needs).\n\n---\n\n---\n\n> [!source] Organizational Readiness\n\n## 4. Tailoring AI Governance to Organizational Context\n\nAI governance is **not one-size-fits-all**. To be effective, governance must be *proportionate, relevant, and adaptive*. Six critical factors shape governance design:\n\n### Factor 1: Company Size\n\n| Size | Traits | Governance Recommendations |\n|------|--------|--------------------------|\n| **Small / Startup** (1–200 employees) | Limited legal/compliance resources; few formal structures; flat org; high agility | Start with core principles/ethics charter; appoint a governance champion; use lightweight tools (spreadsheets, checklists); outsource when needed (legal reviews, red teaming) |\n| **Mid-sized** (200–5,000 employees) | Some formalized governance; documented policies; early compliance functions; expanding AI/ML | Establish AI governance council (IT, legal, product, data science); define risk-based review thresholds; begin lifecycle integration with documentation checkpoints |\n| **Large Enterprise** (5,000+ employees) | Extensive AI across departments; formal governance structures; complex regulatory profiles; dedicated legal/risk/data teams | Formalize multi-layer (federated) governance; integrate with ERM, cybersecurity, IT governance; invest in tooling and automation; report to executive leadership/board |\n\n### Factor 2: Organizational Maturity\n\nMaturity models (e.g., **COBIT**) define levels of process formality:\n\n| Level | Description | AI Governance Implication |\n|-------|-------------|--------------------------|\n| **0: Nonexistent** | Complete lack of recognizable processes | Start from scratch with basic documentation |\n| **1: Initial / Ad Hoc** | Unstructured, unpredictable; depends on individuals | Focus on education and risk awareness |\n| **2: Repeatable** | Similar procedures exist but informal, undocumented | Document decisions even if informal |\n| **3: Defined** | Processes standardized, documented, communicated | Standardize terminology and model documentation |\n| **4: Managed & Measurable** | Processes monitored and measured | Create centralized repositories; mandate vetting processes |\n| **5: Optimized** | Continuous improvement, automation, best practices | Embed governance gates into AI ops; regular third-party audits; continuous monitoring |\n\n> **Note:** Low maturity is not \"bad,\" and high maturity is not always \"good.\" Organizations should target a maturity level appropriate to their sector, risk appetite, and needs. Not all processes need to be at the same level.\n\n### Factor 3: Industry Sector\n\n| Sector | Characteristics | Governance Best Practices |\n|--------|----------------|--------------------------|\n| **Regulated** (finance, healthcare, insurance, energy, telecom) | High legal expectations (Basel III, HIPAA, GDPR); extensive documentation/audit; clear accountability | Use formal risk tiers (EU AI Act); model validation and stress testing; traceability from data to predictions; change control logs |\n| **Business/Consumer Tech** (e-commerce, social media, SaaS) | Rapid innovation cycles; high public visibility; algorithmic trust risks | Prioritize user feedback mechanisms; mandate transparency features; regular impact assessments on personalization/ranking |\n| **Industrial / Manufacturing** | Operational risks (physical safety, environmental); predictive maintenance, robotics | Partner AI with safety/reliability engineering; monitor model drift → equipment failure; require fail-safes and human override |\n| **Public Sector / Nonprofits** | Heightened public accountability; mission-oriented; procurement constraints | Publish open model impact statements; involve community stakeholders; document impacts on vulnerable populations |\n\n### Factor 4: Products and Services (Use Case Risk Tiers)\n\n| Risk Level | Examples | Governance Approach |\n|-----------|----------|-------------------|\n| **Low** (Internal Use) | Expense automation, HR chatbots, revenue forecasting, marketing | Simplified documentation; minimal review unless personal data is used |\n| **Moderate** (Decision Support) | Customer segmentation, predictive analytics, support chatbots | Validate assumptions and biases; disclose AI involvement; monitor accuracy over time |\n| **High** (Automated Decisions) | Job screening, loan applications, medical diagnosis, tenant screening | Full lifecycle oversight; bias audits and fairness testing; explainability and appeals processes |\n| **Very High** (Real-time / High-stakes) | Autonomous driving, AI surgery, fraud prevention, K-12 instruction | Red-team simulations; real-time monitoring with auto-shutoff; human-in-the-loop validation |\n\n### Factor 5: Risk Tolerance\n\n| Tolerance Level | Examples | Governance Traits |\n|----------------|----------|------------------|\n| **Low** | Banking, healthcare, energy | Pre-deployment risk quantification; conservative release gates; comprehensive documentation and signoffs |\n| **Medium** | Large tech, consumer products, retail | Tiered oversight by use case; partial automation with oversight; centralized ethics committee |\n| **High** | Startups, social media, R&D orgs | Faster iteration with post-hoc reviews; distributed risk ownership; ethical guardrails with fewer hard constraints |\n\n> **Note:** High risk tolerance should never mean *no governance*. Even risk-tolerant firms need escalation channels, monitoring, and accountability.\n\n### Factor 6: Business Alignment\n\n| Organization Type | Traits | Governance Approach |\n|-------------------|--------|-------------------|\n| **Innovation-driven** | Fast cycles, experimentation, higher risk appetite | Agile, feedback-driven processes; pilot phases; emphasize ethical foresight over strict compliance |\n| **Reputation-focused** | High public perception exposure, trust-sensitive domains | Public-facing ethics commitments; transparency dashboards; PR/legal in review boards |\n| **Efficiency-oriented** | Emphasis on automation and operational AI | Evaluate accuracy-fairness tradeoffs; accountability in automated decisions; document error tolerance thresholds |\n\n---\n\n---\n\n> [!source] Organizational Readiness\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n1. **Know the roles of AI developers, deployers, and users.** Developers design and train models (documentation, fairness testing, technical transparency). Deployers integrate models into production (infrastructure, monitoring, rollback). Users apply AI outputs operationally and are often first to notice failures, bias, or unintended consequences. Understanding these distinctions is critical for placing safeguards at every lifecycle phase.\n\n2. **Understand why cross-functional collaboration is critical.** AI governance requires legal, technical, ethical, operational, and business input. Each group identifies different risks. Collaboration takes the form of governance committees, risk/compliance reviews, and multi-role project teams. The exam may ask you to recognize when and how diverse roles should coordinate.\n\n3. **Know how governance strategies vary by company size, maturity, and risk tolerance.** Small/early-stage companies use lightweight governance (checklists, part-time champions, external audits). Large organizations need formalized structures, dedicated risk teams, and compliance reporting. Risk tolerance directly influences how strict or flexible controls should be. Be prepared to evaluate governance scaling by organizational context.\n\n4. **Identify role-specific responsibilities across the AI lifecycle.** Developers are accountable for data integrity, model documentation, and fairness testing. Deployers own post-deployment observability, rollback plans, and integration controls. Users must detect problems and escalate concerns. The exam may present lifecycle scenarios and ask who is responsible at key checkpoints.\n\n5. **Recognize components of an effective AI governance training program.** Training must be tailored to each stakeholder group's literacy level: executives need strategic alignment and risk awareness; developers need bias testing and transparency tools; users need to understand how to interpret and challenge AI outputs. Strong programs combine live training, e-learning, policy walkthroughs, and case-based exercises.\n\n---", "t_roles": "## At a Glance\n\nThe cast of AI governance: board and executive leadership setting risk appetite, a cross-functional governance committee steering policy and reviews, and operating roles (privacy officers, CISOs, data scientists, legal counsel, ethics leads, business owners, frontline users) each holding a defined piece. Stakeholder mapping and RACI matrices keep interests and duties visible, and integration into enterprise risk management keeps AI risk from living in a silo.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\n### Three layers\n\n| Layer | Roles | Owns |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Direction** | Board, CEO, COO, CRO, CISO, CDAO, CPO, CCO, CAIEO | Risk appetite, funding, tone, final accountability |\n| **Steering** | AI governance board or council: legal, privacy, security, data science, ethics, risk, compliance, business units | Framework, high-risk use-case review, escalation decisions, upward reporting |\n| **Operating** | Data scientists and AI engineers, data engineers, AI ops, product owners, risk officers, compliance, frontline users | Building, assessing, monitoring, documenting, flagging anomalies |\n\nA growing number of organizations add a **Chief AI Ethics Officer** to hold the ethical governance remit explicitly, which is a signal of maturity rather than a legal requirement.\n\n### RACI keeps it honest\n\nRACI (Responsible, Accountable, Consulted, Informed) is the instrument the BOK's \"define roles and responsibilities\" indicator points at. Applied per governance task rather than per department, it forces two things that org charts hide: exactly one accountable owner per task, and an explicit list of who must be consulted before the decision. Governance failures usually trace to a task with no accountable owner, or to a decision made without the function that would have caught the problem.\n\n### Stakeholder mapping is a control\n\nStakeholder mapping identifies everyone affected by or influencing a system, internal and external: the business sponsor, the affected employees, the customers scored, the communities implicated, the vendors in the chain, the regulators with jurisdiction. Mapping them is not documentation for its own sake. A stakeholder who is never identified is a risk nobody owns, and external stakeholders (civil society, affected communities) are the ones internal processes most reliably miss.\n\n### Integration into enterprise risk management\n\nAI risk that lives in a separate register gets reviewed separately and funded last. The mature pattern is to tag AI risks inside the existing ERM portfolio, with AI-specific fields (system, model version, affected population, fairness exposure) rather than a parallel process. That is also how AI governance gets a board audience: it arrives through the risk reporting that already has one.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nScenario questions test committee composition (multidisciplinary beats any single function), escalation paths, and stakeholder analysis. If an answer concentrates AI governance in one department (legal, IT, anyone), it is almost always wrong; the same is true of an answer that assigns a governance task without naming an accountable owner.\n\nA second reliable angle: a scenario where a stakeholder group was overlooked (contract data labelers, the affected community, frontline staff who will actually operate the system) and the question asks what went wrong. The answer names the missing perspective, not the missing control.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Boards and executives own oversight and risk appetite; committees operationalize the framework; operating roles execute\n- Effective governance committees are multidisciplinary by design: legal, privacy, security, data science, ethics, risk, business\n- RACI matrices make each governance task's Responsible, Accountable, Consulted and Informed roles explicit, with exactly one accountable owner\n- Stakeholder mapping covers everyone affected by or influencing a system, internal and external; unmapped stakeholders are unowned risks\n- AI risks should be tagged inside the existing enterprise risk management portfolio rather than kept in a parallel register\n- The Chief AI Ethics Officer role signals that ethical governance has an explicit owner\n- Cross-functional collaboration is a control in itself: siloed AI decisions are how governance failures start\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF Playbook, Govern function</a> · GV1 to GV6: roles, accountability, culture and third-party governance\n- <a href=\"https://www.iso.org/standard/42001\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 42001 (AI management system)</a> · clause 5 leadership and Annex A organizational controls\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · AI actors and the Govern function as the cross-cutting foundation\n- <a href=\"https://iapp.org/resources/article/key-terms-for-ai-governance/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IAPP Key Terms for AI Governance</a> · stakeholder and role terminology\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Organizational Readiness\n\n## 1. Roles and Responsibilities for AI Governance Stakeholders\n\nAI governance is a **shared responsibility** spanning disciplines — it should not be centralized in a single team. Defined roles are the backbone of governance; without clarity on \"who owns what,\" organizations face blurred accountability, regulatory pressures, and ineffective oversight.\n\n> **Note:** An effective AI governance function is part of overall corporate *due care*. Organizations facing legal issues related to AI systems will face greater consequences if they lack an AI governance program.\n\n### Stakeholder Categories and Governance Roles\n\n#### Executive Leadership\n\n| Role | Governance Responsibilities |\n|------|---------------------------|\n| CEO, COO, CRO, CDAO, CCO, CISO, CPO | Set tone from the top for responsible AI |\n| | Align strategic AI use with values and mission |\n| | Approve risk thresholds for high-stakes domains |\n| | Allocate funding for governance tools, training, staffing |\n| | Hold teams accountable for compliance with governance protocols |\n\n#### AI Governance Board or Council\n\nA cross-functional body including data science, cybersecurity, privacy, compliance, legal, ethics, risk, and business units.\n\n| Responsibility |\n|---------------|\n| Develop and maintain the organization's AI governance framework |\n| Conduct risk assessments and reviews for sensitive AI use cases |\n| Oversee implementation of audit and impact assessments (AIA, DPIA) |\n| Serve as final reviewer for algorithmic risk escalations |\n| Monitor and report responsible AI progress to the board of directors |\n\n#### Legal, Ethics, and Compliance Teams\n\n| Role | Governance Responsibilities |\n|------|---------------------------|\n| **Legal Counsel** | Identify/interpret regulations (GDPR, EU AI Act, CCPA); advise on liability, IP, third-party risk; draft AI-specific contracts and SLAs; participate in impact assessments and incident response |\n| **Ethics Officer / Responsible Innovation Lead** | Translate values into actionable AI principles; lead ethical impact assessments; host ethics roundtables for contentious use cases; develop moral narrative for complex decisions |\n| **Compliance Officer** | Embed governance controls into operational workflows; ensure adherence to policies and standards (e.g., ISO/IEC 42001); conduct periodic reviews and internal audits; train employees on AI compliance risks |\n\n> **Note:** A growing number of organizations are establishing **Chief AI Ethics Officer (CAIEO)** roles to oversee ethical governance — promoting public accountability, transparency strategies, and balancing profit with public interest.\n\n#### Technical and Engineering Teams\n\n| Role | Governance Responsibilities |\n|------|---------------------------|\n| **Data Scientists / AI Engineers** | Select, train, validate, and document AI models; proactively identify bias, drift, unintended behaviors; apply explainability techniques (SHAP, LIME); document all aspects of AI system and data design |\n| **Data Engineers** | Ensure data quality, lineage, and security; implement data minimization and privacy controls; tag and catalog datasets for training and evaluation |\n| **AI Ops** | Operationalize deployment through SDLC/AI lifecycle processes; implement monitoring for data drift, model performance, runtime anomalies; automate rollback/alert procedures |\n\n> **Note:** Consider \"model governance checkpoints\" in development lifecycles — gates that check for documentation, fairness assessments, and performance benchmarks before deployment.\n\n#### Cybersecurity and Risk Management\n\n| Responsibility |\n|---------------|\n| Integrate AI-related risks into the **Enterprise Risk Management (ERM)** portfolio |\n| Maintain a risk register specifically for AI systems (or AI-specific tags) |\n| Identify and evaluate financial, reputational, and legal exposures |\n| Conduct scenario planning and tabletop exercises for AI incidents |\n| Define mitigation strategies based on likelihood and severity matrices |\n| Include AI systems in all standard security processes (vulnerability management, threat intelligence, pen testing, etc.) |\n\n#### Product and Business Unit Leaders\n\n| Responsibility |\n|---------------|\n| Sponsor AI use cases meeting business objectives and customer needs |\n| Define success metrics aligned with values and risk appetite |\n| Provide domain-specific input on data relevance, operational impact, regulatory nuances |\n| Participate in model validation to ensure real-world alignment |\n| Serve as liaisons between technical teams and end users |\n\n#### End Users and Frontline Staff\n\n| Responsibility |\n|---------------|\n| Interact with AI systems according to corporate policies |\n| Monitor for inaccurate, unsafe, or biased outputs |\n| Provide feedback for model refinement and retraining |\n| Escalate anomalies or concerns through governance reporting channels |\n\n> **Note:** Design interfaces that allow users to flag AI recommendations, request human override, or obtain explanations — embedding governance into everyday workflows.\n\n#### External Parties\n\n| Party | Governance Role |\n|-------|----------------|\n| **Vendors & Suppliers** | Meet governance requirements in procurement/contracts; provide model documentation, risk assessments, performance metrics; support ongoing monitoring |\n| **Regulators & Auditors** | Review compliance with AI regulations and standards; evaluate fairness, accountability, societal impact |\n| **Civil Society / Communities** | Offer perspectives on rights, equity, inclusion; participate in design workshops, focus groups, community impact reviews |\n\n### Assigning Responsibilities Across the AI Lifecycle\n\n| Phase | Primary Roles | Key Responsibilities |\n|-------|--------------|---------------------|\n| **Ideation & Design** | Executives, Product Owners, Ethics Leads | Define acceptable use cases, assess ethical risk, allocate funding |\n| **Data Acquisition** | Data Engineers, Legal, Compliance | Ensure lawful collection, assess data bias, enforce privacy controls |\n| **Model Development** | Data Scientists, Risk Teams, Ethics Leads | Train and validate models, document assumptions, evaluate bias |\n| **Testing & Validation** | QA Engineers, Domain Experts, Compliance | Conduct robustness and security tests, validate accuracy, approve for deployment |\n| **Deployment** | AI Ops, IT, Business Leads | Embed into production, implement alerts, enforce SLAs |\n| **Monitoring** | Risk Officers, Users, Compliance | Audit for drift, gather user feedback, update documentation |\n| **Decommissioning** | Legal, IT, Governance Board | Retire outdated models, preserve data logs, update records |\n\n> **Key insight:** AI governance must be embedded \"left to right\" across the lifecycle. Governance is not an afterthought — it must be present in every stage.\n\n### RACI Matrix for Governance Tasks\n\n**RACI** = **R**esponsible, **A**ccountable, **C**onsulted, **I**nformed.\n\n| Task | Dev Lead | Risk Officer | Legal | Product Owner |\n|------|----------|-------------|-------|---------------|\n| Model training | **R** | C | I | C |\n| Fairness evaluation | C | **R** | C | C |\n| Regulatory disclosure | I | C | **R** | C |\n| Post-deployment monitoring | **R** | **A** | I | C |\n\nStandardized governance role documentation should define: mission statement, decision rights/veto power, escalation procedures, and performance metrics.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n> [!source] Organizational Readiness\n\n## 2. Cross-functional Collaboration in AI Governance\n\nAI governance is a **cross-functional endeavor** — no single team can govern AI effectively in isolation. Collaboration is the connective tissue ensuring AI systems remain aligned with corporate culture, human values, business goals, regulations, and societal expectations.\n\n### Why Cross-functional Collaboration Is Critical\n\nAI systems simultaneously influence:\n- **Legal/regulatory exposure** (discrimination, privacy violations)\n- **Reputational risk** (unethical uses, opaque decision-making)\n- **Technical robustness** (model bias, drift, adversarial attacks)\n- **Business operations** (automation efficiency, scalability)\n- **Human impact** (ethics, fairness, dignity, job displacement)\n\n> **Note:** When AI governance is centralized under a single function, it often lacks business relevance or technical depth. Shared ownership across the organization ensures higher compliance, more context-aware decisions, greater buy-in, and reduced post-implementation issues.\n\n### Principles of Effective Cross-functional Governance\n\n| Principle | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **Diverse expertise** | Include technical, legal/compliance, ethics, business, operational, and end-user representatives |\n| **Clear role definitions** | Define who owns decisions, who provides input, who is responsible for documentation, who monitors outcomes |\n| **Iterative integration** | Embed collaboration throughout the AI lifecycle — not just at risk reviews. Use checkpoints at design, data acquisition, validation, deployment, and incident response |\n| **Shared artifacts** | Model cards, risk assessment forms, impact assessments (DPIAs, AIAs), ethics checklists |\n\n> **Note:** Diverse perspectives improve risk detection. In one case, a linguist (not a data scientist) flagged bias in a language model by identifying sociolinguistic stereotypes during testing.\n\n### Building the Collaborative Structure\n\n| Structure | Composition & Purpose |\n|-----------|----------------------|\n| **AI Governance Working Group** | Cross-functional standing team: legal, risk, compliance, technical leads, product, CCO/ethics, cybersecurity, privacy. Reviews high-risk use cases, coordinates assessments, refines policies, escalates to executive board |\n| **Cross-functional Use Case Reviews** | Triggered when: automated decisions affect rights, data includes sensitive/biometric info, system impacts hiring/lending/medical, model complexity reduces explainability, regulations increase scrutiny |\n| **Collaboration Templates** | Model fact sheets, risk assessment forms, DPIAs, AIAs, ethics checklists — shared documents reduce misunderstandings and serve as a record |\n\n### Fostering a Collaborative Culture\n\n- **Bridge language gaps:** Joint workshops, shadowing programs, rotational governance roles\n- **Incentivize participation:** Include governance in performance reviews, recognition programs, shared KPIs (e.g., \"AI project risk mitigated before deployment\")\n- **Create psychological safety:** Rotate facilitators, anonymous input channels, empower junior staff to flag concerns\n\n> **Note:** People closest to the technology often see risks early. Encourage input from those with less institutional power but more valuable insights.\n\n### External and Cross-organizational Collaboration\n\n| Domain | Approach |\n|--------|----------|\n| **Third-party vendors** | Require participation in governance reviews, review model documentation and risk assessments, align on shared principles, establish joint incident response protocols |\n| **Regulators & industry** | Engage proactively with regulators (EU AI Act), industry consortia (IEEE, ISO, NIST, ENISA), academic institutions for standards and testing |\n| **Community stakeholders** | Conduct focus groups, invite civil society review, use participatory design workshops, publish impact statements |\n\n> **Note:** \"Cross-functional\" doesn't just mean across departments — it means listening to those affected by AI systems, especially in housing, policing, or credit decisions.\n\n### Sustaining Collaboration Over Time\n\n- **Governance charters** — codify roles, meeting cadence, escalation paths\n- **Joint training programs** — cross-functional training on AI risks, biases, legal updates\n- **Shared metrics** — track how collaboration improves risk mitigation, transparency, compliance\n- **Retrospectives** — postmortems on governance failures or near misses\n\n---", "t_training": "## At a Glance\n\nThe workforce layer of governance: building AI literacy across the organization and role-specific competence where it counts, with executives who can weigh AI risk, developers who know governance by design, users trained for appropriate reliance, and governance staff current on law and standards. Since 2 February 2025 the EU AI Act has made AI literacy an explicit expectation for provider and deployer staff, which turns training from a culture initiative into a compliance obligation.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe BOK's indicator is to \"create and deliver a training and awareness program to all stakeholders on AI terminology, strategy and governance.\" Three design consequences follow.\n\n### Awareness for everyone, depth by role\n\n| Audience | What they need |\n|---|---|\n| **Executives and board** | Strategic alignment, regulatory exposure, reputational and enterprise risk, how to read an AI risk register |\n| **Developers and data scientists** | Governance by design, documentation standards, bias and fairness testing, reproducibility, the approval workflow |\n| **Legal, risk and compliance** | Regulatory fluency, impact assessment practice, vendor and contract risk, audit readiness |\n| **Product and business owners** | Use-case vetting, success criteria, user impact, their accountability for outcomes |\n| **Frontline users** | What the system can and cannot do, when to override, how to report an anomaly, recourse options |\n| **IT and security** | Monitoring, incident response, control ownership, AI-specific attack surface |\n| **Everyone** | Basic literacy, the organization's principles, how to escalate a concern |\n\n### Training is a control against automation bias\n\nAutomation bias is the documented human tendency to over-trust machine output, and it is the reason oversight that exists on paper still fails. Training is the cheapest countermeasure: users who understand error modes, confidence and the system's limits are materially better overseers. Interface design carries the other half, by making disagreement and override easy.\n\n### Maintenance is part of the obligation\n\nA curriculum written before the current obligations existed does not satisfy them. Programs need a refresh cycle tied to regulatory change, and effectiveness measures rather than attendance counts: pre and post literacy assessment, the share of staff trained within a stated window, the number of AI projects with complete documentation, and the volume of risk escalations initiated by trained staff. The last one is the interesting metric, because it measures whether people believe reporting is safe.\n\n### Change management is the unglamorous half\n\nWorkforce readiness is not only knowledge. New AI systems change workflows, job content and required skills, and the organizations that handle this badly generate the resistance that later shows up as shadow AI: employees using unapproved tools because the approved path is too slow. Readiness work is therefore also governance work.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nLook for the role-tailoring principle: one-size-fits-all training is the wrong answer, and awareness for everyone plus depth by role is the right one. A scenario about over-reliance or automation bias usually resolves to user training plus interface safeguards, not to model retraining.\n\nThe date to know is 2 February 2025, when the EU AI Act's Article 4 AI literacy expectation took effect alongside the prohibitions. Questions sometimes test that second, less famous half of the date. And if an option treats training as a one-time onboarding event, it contradicts the maintenance requirement.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- EU AI Act Article 4 makes AI literacy an expectation for provider and deployer staff from 2 February 2025, so training is compliance as well as culture\n- Tailor by role: executives (risk and strategy), developers (responsible build practices), users (capabilities, limits, appropriate reliance), governance staff (law and standards)\n- Training is a primary countermeasure to automation bias, the human tendency to over-trust machine output\n- Programs need refresh cycles tied to regulatory and system change; a stale curriculum does not satisfy a current obligation\n- Measure effectiveness with pre and post literacy assessment, completion rates, documentation completeness and escalations initiated by staff\n- Workforce readiness includes change management: new systems change workflows, job content and skills, and mishandled change produces shadow AI\n- Interfaces that make disagreement, override and reporting easy carry the other half of the oversight burden\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/4/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 4 (AI literacy)</a> · the legal expectation, in force 2 February 2025\n- <a href=\"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF Playbook, Govern function</a> · GV3 on workforce and training actions\n- <a href=\"https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/artificial-intelligence/guidance-on-ai-and-data-protection/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ICO guidance on AI and data protection</a> · training and accountability expectations alongside the technical guidance\n- <a href=\"https://www.iso.org/standard/42001\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 42001 (AI management system)</a> · clause 7.2 competence and clause 7.3 awareness\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Organizational Readiness\n\n## 3. Training and Awareness Program\n\nTraining and awareness are the **linchpin** that transforms governance policy from a document into day-to-day organizational behavior. AI literacy varies widely — legal teams may not understand overfitting, developers may not grasp regulatory constraints, executives may not be fluent in governance requirements.\n\n> **Note:** Without awareness and accountability, policy becomes theater. An organization may have ethical guidelines, but if employees don't understand them — or don't know they exist — they won't follow them.\n\n### Core Training Objectives\n\nEvery role must understand AI sufficiently to make sound, ethical, and compliant decisions:\n\n| Objective | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **AI terminology** | Key concepts: ML, neural networks, algorithmic bias, model drift, explainability |\n| **AI strategy** | How AI supports the organization's mission, priorities, risk posture |\n| **Governance principles** | Policies, roles, review processes, documentation requirements |\n| **Risk awareness** | Ethical, legal, operational, reputational risks |\n| **Escalation channels** | How to raise concerns, flag anomalies, seek guidance |\n\n### Mapping Audiences to Training Needs\n\n| Audience | Needs | Example Topics |\n|----------|-------|---------------|\n| **Executives & Board** | Strategic alignment, legal exposure, enterprise risk | AI strategy, regulatory trends, reputational risks |\n| **Developers & Data Scientists** | Technical rigor, fairness tools, standards | Model documentation, bias mitigation, reproducibility |\n| **Legal, Risk & Compliance** | Regulatory fluency, audit readiness | AI-related laws, impact assessments, vendor risks |\n| **Business/Product Teams** | Value alignment, impact awareness | Use case vetting, success criteria, user impact |\n| **Frontline Users** | Operational understanding | System behavior, human-in-the-loop workflows, recourse options |\n| **IT & Cybersecurity** | Controls, daily operations, incident management | System monitoring, incident response, control ownership |\n| **All Staff** | Basic literacy, ethical culture | Definitions, examples, escalation paths |\n\n### Curriculum Structure\n\n| Training Topic | Subject Coverage |\n|---------------|-----------------|\n| **AI Fundamentals** (all) | What are AI/ML/automation; how AI differs from traditional software; common myths |\n| **Strategic Alignment** (managers, execs) | Why the organization invests in AI; governance's role in scaling AI safely; business value and reputational risks |\n| **Governance Framework** (all) | Overview of internal policies and roles; required documentation; how models are reviewed and approved |\n| **Ethics and Risk** (all) | Principles: fairness, accountability, transparency; ethical pitfalls and mitigations; incident response process |\n| **Role-based Application** (custom tracks) | Case studies per department; step-by-step guides (e.g., \"How to conduct a fairness review\") |\n\n### Delivery Methods\n\n| Format | Strengths | Examples |\n|--------|-----------|----------|\n| **Live workshops** | Interactive, team building | Cross-functional case simulations |\n| **e-Learning modules** | Scalable, asynchronous | Onboarding courses, policy refreshers |\n| **Job aids / playbooks** | Role-specific, accessible | Ethics checklists, escalation flowcharts |\n| **Peer learning** | Context-rich, social | Lunch-and-learns, communities of practice |\n| **Microlearning** | Time-efficient, engaging | Five-minute videos, quick quizzes |\n\n> **Note:** People learn more through practice. Pair live sessions with hands-on exercises (red teaming a use case, writing a model factsheet) to make abstract principles tangible.\n\n### Training Pitfalls to Avoid\n\n| Pitfall | Mitigation |\n|---------|------------|\n| One-size-fits-all content | Segment training by role and context |\n| Overly technical modules | Use plain language and visuals |\n| Lack of executive participation | Mandate C-level attendance in key sessions |\n| No follow-through | Integrate training outcomes into performance goals and audits |\n| Training as a one-time event | Create ongoing learning tracks and refresher cycles |\n\n> **Note:** The EU AI Act explicitly requires documentation of employee training in AI-related roles.\n\n### Measuring Success\n\nKey KPIs for training programs:\n- Percent of staff completing training within 90 days\n- Pre/post assessments of AI literacy\n- Percent of AI projects with completed model documentation\n- Risk escalations initiated by trained staff\n- User satisfaction with governance tools and channels\n\n---", "t_devdeploy": "## At a Glance\n\nThe value-chain role model that allocates governance duties: developers build and train models, curate data and test for fairness; deployers operationalize systems, monitor real-world behaviour and manage user-facing risk; users operate within intended bounds and report anomalies. BOK v2.1 adds providers to this list, and the BOK states that developer and deployer are task labels rather than legal categories. The EU AI Act formalizes a partly different cast (provider, deployer, importer, distributor) with obligations attached to each.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\n### The BOK's roles, as tasks\n\n| Role | Focus | Governance responsibilities |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Developer** | Data scientists, ML engineers, data engineers, research scientists | Data curation and rights, model selection and training, fairness and robustness evaluation, documentation, testing |\n| **Provider** (added in v2.1) | The entity that places the system on the market under its own name, including a team that fine-tunes someone else's base model | Conformity, technical documentation, post-market monitoring, incident reporting for high-risk systems |\n| **Deployer** | Software engineers, AI ops, product managers, infrastructure | Fit-for-context assessment, integration, human oversight in operation, monitoring, incident escalation, user instructions |\n| **User** | Internal staff, external customers, decision-makers relying on output | Operate within intended bounds, apply judgment, report anomalies, exercise recourse |\n\nThe BOK's introduction says explicitly that \"developer\" and \"deployer\" are used as **task labels, not legal categories**. That is a deliberate signal: the same organization can be a developer for one system and a deployer for another, and the labels describe what you are doing rather than what you legally are.\n\n### The EU AI Act's cast, as legal categories\n\n| Role | Definition in outline | Core duties |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Provider** | Develops an AI system (or has it developed) and places it on the EU market or puts it into service under its own name or trademark | Risk management, data governance, technical documentation, conformity assessment, CE marking, registration, post-market monitoring, incident reporting |\n| **Deployer** | Uses an AI system under its authority in a professional capacity | Use per instructions, assign competent human oversight, ensure input data relevance, monitor, retain logs, report serious incidents, and for some, run a fundamental rights impact assessment |\n| **Importer** | Places a third-country provider's system on the EU market | Verify the conformity assessment was performed, documentation exists and CE marking is affixed |\n| **Distributor** | Makes a system available on the EU market without modifying it | Verify CE marking and documentation, and do not supply systems it believes non-compliant |\n\n### The role-shift trap\n\nUnder the EU AI Act, a deployer that puts its name or trademark on a high-risk system, substantially modifies it, or changes its intended purpose becomes the **provider** and inherits the provider's obligations. That single rule generates a disproportionate share of exam items, because it converts what looks like a deployment question into a conformity question.\n\n### Why the split matters\n\nMost of the risk everyone downstream inherits was determined upstream: training data, architecture, thresholds. But most of the harm happens downstream, in context, where the deployer chooses how the output is used and who oversees it. The role model exists so that neither party can point at the other, and so the exam can ask you who owes what.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nQuestions give you an entity and ask its obligations, or an obligation and ask whose it is. Watch the seam: an entity that rebrands or substantially modifies a high-risk system becomes the provider, and an option that assigns provider duties (conformity assessment, CE marking) to a deployer is wrong.\n\nKeep the two vocabularies straight, because the exam mixes them. The BOK's developer / provider / deployer / user are task labels; the EU AI Act's provider / deployer / importer / distributor are legal categories with defined duties. A question that mentions CE marking or Annex IV documentation is asking about the legal cast; one about bias testing or data curation is asking about the task cast.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- BOK v2.1 adds providers to the roles list, and the BOK treats developer and deployer as task labels, not legal categories\n- Developer duties: data curation and rights, model selection and training, fairness and robustness evaluation, documentation, testing\n- Deployer duties: fit-for-context assessment, human oversight in operation, monitoring, incident escalation, user instructions\n- User duties: operate within intended bounds, apply judgment, report anomalies, exercise recourse\n- EU AI Act legal cast: provider, deployer, importer and distributor, each with distinct obligations\n- Provider duties for high-risk systems: risk management, data governance, technical documentation, conformity assessment, CE marking, registration, post-market monitoring, incident reporting\n- Importers and distributors verify conformity (CE marking and documentation) rather than performing conformity assessment\n- Role shift: rebranding, substantially modifying, or changing the intended purpose of a high-risk system makes you the provider\n- Upstream decisions (data, architecture, thresholds) determine most inherited risk, while downstream context determines most realized harm\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/3/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 3 (definitions of provider, deployer, importer, distributor)</a> · the legal role definitions\n- <a href=\"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/ai-act-explained\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act explained (European Commission)</a> · role-based obligations in plain language\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/25/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 25 (responsibilities along the AI value chain)</a> · the rule that turns deployers into providers\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · the parallel role taxonomy: provider, producer, customer, user, subject\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · AI actors and their risk-management roles\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Organizational Readiness\n\n## 5. Developers, Deployers, and Users in AI Governance\n\nThree distinct groups interact with AI systems, each with different responsibilities, risks, and leverage over AI behavior and outcomes.\n\n### Definitions\n\n| Role | Who They Are | Focus Areas |\n|------|-------------|-------------|\n| **AI Developer** | Data scientists, ML engineers, data engineers, research scientists, privacy/cybersecurity analysts | Data curation and preprocessing; model selection and training; performance evaluation and tuning; documentation and testing |\n| **AI Deployer** | Software engineers, AI ops, product managers, infrastructure teams | Model deployment and monitoring; infrastructure and scalability; integration into applications/services; post-deployment observability |\n| **AI User** | Internal users (HR using resume screening), external users (customers receiving recommendations), decision-makers (loan officers, clinicians) | Interpretation of AI outputs; operational reliance on AI; reporting errors, inconsistencies, incidents, harms; exercising rights to recourse or appeal |\n\n### Role Responsibilities Across the AI Lifecycle\n\n#### Developer Responsibilities\n\n| Phase | Developer Tasks | Governance Responsibilities |\n|-------|----------------|--------------------------|\n| **Data collection** | Curate and clean datasets | Check for representational bias, ensure consent/privacy |\n| **Model design** | Select algorithms, tune hyperparameters | Document assumptions, ensure explainability |\n| **Evaluation** | Measure accuracy, fairness, robustness | Perform bias testing, validate against benchmarks, assess security |\n| **Documentation** | Record the development process | Create model cards, data sheets, risk disclosures |\n\n#### Deployer Responsibilities\n\n| Phase | Deployer Tasks | Governance Responsibilities |\n|-------|---------------|--------------------------|\n| **Integration** | Embed models into apps/tools | Ensure UI/UX supports explainability and user control |\n| **Scaling** | Enable access and load management | Apply access controls and logging |\n| **Monitoring** | Track real-time performance | Detect drift, alert on anomalies or failures |\n| **Maintenance** | Manage model updates | Version control, rollback procedures, impact validation |\n\n#### User Responsibilities\n\n| Phase | User Tasks | Governance Responsibilities |\n|-------|-----------|--------------------------|\n| **Interaction** | Use AI system in operations | Understand AI's role in decision-making, avoid blind trust |\n| **Feedback** | Identify problems or inconsistencies | Report anomalies through proper channels |\n| **Oversight** | Validate AI-assisted decisions | Exercise discretion, request human review if needed |\n| **Rights** | Appeal decisions, seek transparency | Invoke recourse mechanisms when impacted |\n\n### Governance Opportunities by Role\n\n| Role | Key Opportunities |\n|------|------------------|\n| **Developers** | Bias mitigation at the source; transparency tooling (SHAP, LIME); ethical foresight during experimentation; reproducibility via consistent pipelines; security and privacy by design |\n| **Deployers** | Embedding monitoring for performance/fairness drift; automating governance checkpoints (no deployment without documentation); implementing rollback protocols |\n| **Users** | Spotting unintended consequences; validating outputs with domain knowledge; initiating recourse or appeals — empowered frontline users become effective risk sentinels |\n\n### Resource Needs by Role\n\n| Role | Needs | Expectations |\n|------|-------|-------------|\n| **Developer** | Bias detection/explainability/robustness tools; governance guidelines for dev workflows; feedback from deployers and users | Clear success criteria beyond accuracy; time for documentation and audits; safe space to flag ethical concerns |\n| **Deployer** | Validated, well-documented models; monitoring platforms (drift, latency, usage); guidance on human oversight integration | Shared responsibility with developers for post-deployment risk; resources for responsible model updates; participation in incident response |\n| **User** | Clear understanding of when/how AI is used; training on interpreting outputs and exercising discretion; simple reporting channels | Right to explanation; mechanisms for appeal, override, or human review; protection from retaliation when raising concerns |\n\n### Governance Conflicts and Solutions\n\n| Conflict | Tension | Governance Solution |\n|----------|---------|-------------------|\n| **Developers vs. Users** | Developers optimize for technical performance; users care about practical usability | Incorporate user feedback into dev cycles; mandate usability testing |\n| **Developers vs. Deployers** | Developers expect smooth handoff; deployers face incomplete documentation or opaque code | Establish shared AI ops pipelines with required metadata and risk summaries |\n| **Users vs. Deployers** | Users want more control/transparency; deployers restrict for security/scalability | Involve users in UI/UX testing; include human review and escalation options |\n\n### Role-based Governance Controls\n\n| Role | Controls |\n|------|----------|\n| **Developers** | Model cards; fairness testing protocols; peer code review for risk; gate reviews; ethics approval for novel use cases |\n| **Deployers** | Deployment checklists; access control logs; real-time drift alerts; gate reviews; audit trails |\n| **Users** | Transparency disclosures; override mechanisms; feedback channels; recourse procedures |\n\n### Role Coordination\n\n- **Joint retrospectives** after AI system launches\n- **Cross-functional ethics committees** for high-risk use cases\n- **Shared dashboards** showing model performance, risk metrics, feedback loops\n- **Integrated governance tools**: model registry with audit metadata, alert systems routing incidents to the right role, role-based access to governance documentation\n\n---", "ch3": "## At a Glance\n\nThis knowledge area confronts the fact that most organizations' policies predate AI. It covers oversight of autonomous decision-making, the specific ways AI breaks existing privacy, security, data-governance and intellectual-property policy assumptions (inference, model retention, re-identification, new attack surfaces), and the third-party risk problem: pretrained models, APIs and data suppliers that traditional vendor management was never built to probe. It carries the largest share of Domain I items.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nI.C is the largest competency in Domain I at 6 to 8 questions, and it is the most practical. The BOK assumes **IT governance already exists**: AI governance activities sit on top of it, not in place of it. From there the competency has three indicators.\n\n### 1. Oversight and accountability across every lifecycle stage\n\nThe BOK names nine stages that policies must reach: **use case assessment, risk management, ethics by design, data acquisition and use, model and system development, training and testing, deployment and monitoring, documentation and reporting, and incident management.** The governance move is not to write nine documents; it is to place a decision point and an owner at each stage, so that nothing consequential happens without a named accountable human.\n\nIn practice that means policies that are risk-calibrated (stricter for high-stakes uses), versioned and auditable, embedded in the technical workflow rather than parallel to it, and operationalized through gates, templates, checklists and records. Three artifacts do most of the work:\n\n- **A use-case assessment gate** before any build, asking whether AI is the right tool, what harm is possible, whether sufficient quality data exists, and whether a simpler rule-based solution would do.\n- **A go/no-go decision point** at the end of testing, where management reviews results and records the decision.\n- **An AI-specific incident playbook**, tested and rehearsed, because detection capability has to exist before you can declare an incident.\n\n### 2. Updating existing policies for AI\n\nThe BOK names four policy families to evaluate and update: **data privacy, security, data governance and intellectual property** (v2.1 added the last two explicitly). Each breaks in a characteristic way:\n\n| Legacy policy | How AI breaks it | What the update has to say |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Data privacy** | Data is repurposed for training, retained inside model weights, and sensitive traits are inferred from ordinary inputs | Purpose-limitation analysis for secondary use, retention rules covering derived data and models, a position on deletion requests against trained models |\n| **Security** | New attack surface: model inversion, membership inference, data poisoning, prompt injection, model theft | AI in threat modelling, monitoring for AI-specific telemetry, pipeline and model-registry controls |\n| **Data governance** | Provenance and quality were never recorded because no model depended on them | Lineage and provenance requirements, quality dimensions, dataset ownership, representativeness checks |\n| **Intellectual property** | Training on third-party works, unclear output ownership, leaked trade secrets into prompts | Training-data rights review, machine-readable opt-out handling, output-ownership position, acceptable-use rules for external tools |\n\nA prerequisite the BOK states plainly: you need a **data classification policy and a data inventory** first. Without them, managing and monitoring AI training data is severely disadvantaged, and no amount of AI-specific policy will compensate.\n\n### 3. Third-party risk\n\nThe BOK names four areas: **procurement, supply chain, human resources and acceptable use** (the last added in v2.1), and covers **policies, assessments and contracts**. The core observation is that traditional third-party risk management checks solvency, insurance and certification, while AI risk arrives as opaque training data, sub-tier labelling suppliers, silently changing model versions and unclear liability for harmful output.\n\nThe governance answer has four parts: flag AI at procurement intake so the deal gets supplemental review; run AI-specific due diligence (training-data origin and rights, fairness and robustness evidence, explainability support, model-change practices); write the contract terms that make the risk manageable (data-use limits, performance and fairness warranties, model-change and incident notification, audit rights, termination and portability); and monitor continuously, because a vendor model update can change behaviour mid-contract. An **AI system bill of materials** listing upstream inputs is the emerging instrument for the sub-tier problem.\n\nThe non-negotiable: accountability is not delegable. Outsourcing the AI does not outsource the regulatory or ethical responsibility.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nExpect scenarios about a company adopting AI under legacy policies: what gap exists, what to update first, what contract terms to demand from an AI vendor. The strongest answers name a concrete broken assumption (retention schedules that never contemplated model weights, incident response that has no playbook for prompt injection, a data inventory that does not exist) rather than saying \"update the policies.\"\n\nTwo other reliable angles. First, human oversight levels: human-in-the-loop versus human-on-the-loop, and the requirement that oversight be meaningful rather than a rubber stamp. Second, sequencing: use-case assessment and impact assessment come before build, the go/no-go decision comes at the end of testing, and policies must be calibrated to risk rather than uniform. If an option proposes treating a high-stakes system and an internal productivity tool identically, it is wrong.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- I.C is the largest Domain I competency at 6 to 8 questions; the BOK assumes IT governance already exists and AI governance sits on top of it\n- The nine lifecycle stages policies must reach: use case assessment, risk management, ethics by design, data acquisition and use, model and system development, training and testing, deployment and monitoring, documentation and reporting, incident management\n- Policies should be risk-calibrated, versioned and auditable, embedded in the technical workflow, and operationalized through gates, templates and records\n- Three high-value artifacts: a use-case assessment gate before build, a documented go/no-go decision at the end of testing, and a tested AI-specific incident playbook\n- Four policy families to update for AI: data privacy, security, data governance and intellectual property (the latter two added in v2.1)\n- AI breaks privacy policy by repurposing data for training, retaining data in model weights, and inferring sensitive traits from ordinary inputs\n- AI breaks security policy with model inversion, membership inference, data poisoning, prompt injection and model theft; these belong in threat modelling\n- A data classification policy and data inventory are prerequisites; without them AI training data cannot be managed or monitored\n- Third-party risk areas: procurement, supply chain, human resources and acceptable use (the last added in v2.1), covered by policies, assessments and contracts\n- AI vendor due diligence adds training-data origin and rights, fairness and robustness evidence, explainability support, and model-change practices to conventional checks\n- Contracts should secure data-use limits, performance and fairness warranties, model-change and incident notification, audit rights, and exit or portability terms\n- Accountability is non-delegable: outsourcing the AI does not outsource the responsibility\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF Playbook</a> · suggested actions and documentation prompts for all 72 subcategories\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · Govern, Map, Measure, Manage as the policy spine\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/10/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 10 (data and data governance)</a> · the statutory version of AI data-governance duties\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/13/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 13 (transparency and instructions for use)</a> · what has to travel with a high-risk system\n- <a href=\"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2024-12/edpb_opinion_202428_ai-models_en.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EDPB Opinion 28/2024 on AI models and personal data</a> · the DPAs' position on lawful basis, purpose limitation and legitimate interest in model training\n- <a href=\"https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/artificial-intelligence/guidance-on-ai-and-data-protection/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ICO guidance on AI and data protection</a> · the UK regulator's practical treatment of the same problems\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-2e2023.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 100-2, Adversarial Machine Learning taxonomy</a> · poisoning, evasion, privacy and abuse attacks in structured form\n- <a href=\"https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications</a> · prompt injection, insecure output handling and the rest of the LLM attack surface\n- <a href=\"https://www.cisa.gov/ai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CISA artificial intelligence resources</a> · supply-chain and secure-development guidance for AI\n- <a href=\"https://www.iso.org/standard/42001\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 42001 (AI management system)</a> · Annex A controls for third-party and lifecycle management\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Updating Policies for AI\n\n## Performance Indicators\n\n- **Create and implement policies to ensure oversight and accountability across all AI lifecycle stages** (use case assessment, risk management, ethics by design, data acquisition and use, model and system development, training and testing, deployment and monitoring, documentation and reporting, and incident management)\n- **Evaluate and update existing policies for AI** (data privacy, security, data governance, and intellectual property)\n- **Create, update, and implement policies, assessments, and contracts to manage third-party risk** (procurement, supply chain, human resources, and acceptable use)\n\n> **Assumption:** IT governance is assumed to already exist. AI governance activities take place *in addition to* IT governance activities, not in place of them.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n> [!source] Updating Policies for AI\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n1. **Lifecycle-spanning governance** — AI governance must embed oversight at every stage: use case vetting → data sourcing → model design → deployment → ongoing monitoring. Each phase has unique risks requiring distinct policy attention. Governance is not a one-time activity.\n\n2. **Privacy and security policies must address model-specific risks** — Unlike traditional systems, AI models can unintentionally leak personal data or be probed to reveal training records. Concepts like differential privacy, membership inference testing, and lifecycle data retention must be applied in AI contexts. Traditional controls alone are insufficient.\n\n3. **Third-party AI procurement requires AI-specific due diligence** — Exam scenarios may involve vendors providing AI-enabled services. Correct responses include requesting model transparency, understanding training data origins, and incorporating AI-specific contractual clauses on fairness, explainability, and auditability.\n\n4. **Traditional data governance principles must be reinterpreted for AI** — Purpose limitation and data minimization take on new meaning when models can infer, memorize, and repurpose data. Understand issues of model reuse, synthetic data, reidentification risks, and how foundational privacy concepts evolve with machine learning.\n\n5. **Contracts with AI vendors must cover bias, explainability, data usage, and auditability** — Many third-party risks can only be mitigated through strong contract terms. Know what should be included in service agreements and how to structure accountability when using external AI models or services.\n\n---", "t_oversight": "## At a Glance\n\nHow humans stay in charge of automated decisions: human-in-the-loop (a person approves each consequential decision), human-on-the-loop (a person supervises operation and can intervene), and human-in-command (humans set the bounds and can override or shut the system down). The right level is a risk decision, and GDPR Article 22 plus the EU AI Act's human-oversight requirement make it a legal one for high-stakes uses.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\n### The three levels\n\n| Level | What the human does | Suits |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Human-in-the-loop** | Reviews and approves each consequential decision before it takes effect | High-stakes, lower-volume decisions: medical triage, loan denial, custody or benefits determinations |\n| **Human-on-the-loop** | Supervises operation, monitors outputs, can intervene or halt | Higher-volume, moderate-risk operation where per-decision review is impractical |\n| **Human-in-command** | Sets objectives, bounds and escalation rules, and retains the ability to override or deactivate | Overall program governance, and the fallback for every deployed system |\n\n### The legal hooks\n\n**GDPR Article 22** gives individuals the right not to be subject to a decision based solely on automated processing that produces legal effects or similarly significantly affects them. The exceptions (contract necessity, law, explicit consent) come with mandatory safeguards: at minimum human intervention, the ability to express a view, and the ability to contest the decision. \"Solely automated\" is the hinge, which is why meaningful human review before the decision takes effect moves a process outside Article 22, and why cosmetic review does not.\n\n**EU AI Act Article 14** requires high-risk systems to be designed so that competent humans can effectively oversee them, including the ability to understand the system's outputs, to disregard or override them, and to interrupt the system through a stop button or similar procedure. It also obliges deployers to assign oversight to people with the necessary competence, training and authority.\n\n### What makes oversight real\n\nThe exam-relevant insight is that oversight is a design problem, not a staffing problem. A human who rubber-stamps every recommendation satisfies no obligation. Effective oversight needs four things at once:\n\n- **Competence**: the overseer understands the system's limits and failure modes.\n- **Information**: the interface presents what is needed to judge, including confidence and reasons, not just the recommendation.\n- **Authority**: the overseer can actually reverse the output without career cost.\n- **Time and volume**: the review load is small enough to permit real attention.\n\nTake any one away and you have **automation bias with a witness**: a human who provides legal cover for a decision the machine already made. That is the failure the frameworks are written against, and it is why Article 22 speaks of human intervention rather than human presence.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nA classic testable distinction: in-the-loop versus on-the-loop, plus scenarios asking which level a use case demands. The rule of thumb is risk and volume together: a high-stakes, low-volume decision supports per-decision review, while high-volume moderate-risk operation supports supervisory oversight with intervention.\n\nThe deeper point exam items reach for is that oversight must be meaningful. An option that satisfies an oversight obligation by adding a human who always accepts the model's output is wrong, as is one that treats oversight as a one-time approval rather than a continuing capability. If the stem mentions an interface that shows only the recommendation, the missing element is information.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Human-in-the-loop: per-decision human approval, suited to high-stakes, lower-volume decisions\n- Human-on-the-loop: supervisory monitoring with intervention power, suited to higher-volume moderate-risk operation\n- Human-in-command: humans set bounds and retain override and deactivation authority over the system\n- GDPR Article 22: the right not to be subject to solely automated decisions with legal or similarly significant effects, with exceptions carrying mandatory safeguards\n- EU AI Act Article 14: high-risk systems must be designed for effective human oversight, including override and interruption\n- Oversight requires competence, information, authority and time together; remove one and oversight becomes a rubber stamp\n- Automation bias is the tendency to over-trust machine output and is the failure mode oversight design must defeat\n- \"Solely automated\" is the hinge in Article 22: genuine human review before the decision takes effect moves the process outside it\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/14/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 14 (human oversight)</a> · the oversight design requirements for high-risk systems\n- <a href=\"https://gdpr-info.eu/art-22-gdpr/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GDPR Article 22 (automated individual decision-making)</a> · the right and its safeguards\n- <a href=\"https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/artificial-intelligence/explaining-decisions-made-with-artificial-intelligence/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ICO: explaining decisions made with AI</a> · what meaningful human involvement looks like in practice\n- <a href=\"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF Playbook</a> · Govern and Manage actions on human oversight and override\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · controllability and predictability as trustworthiness vocabulary\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Updating Policies for AI\n\n## 1. Oversight in the Age of Autonomous Decision-Making\n\nAI systems introduce new types of situations — autonomous decision-making, impacts on data privacy — that existing policies may not cover. Governance must be **lifecycle-aware**, introducing enforceable, reviewable policies from use case proposal to model retirement.\n\n### 1.1 The Lifecycle Policy Model\n\nA lifecycle-oriented policy framework should:\n\n- Define **responsibilities and escalation paths** for each lifecycle phase (cradle to grave)\n- Be **risk-calibrated** — more rigorous for high-risk systems (healthcare, justice, employment scoring)\n- Enable **auditability and versioning** of proceedings and decisions\n- Be **embedded into technical workflows**, not parallel to them\n- Include **continuous monitoring and feedback loops** for post-deployment performance, incident reporting, and model recalibration\n- Address **data governance explicitly** — provenance, consent, minimization, retention, regulatory compliance\n\n> **Key:** Policies must be operationalized through procedures, gates, templates, checklists, recordkeeping, and oversight roles.\n\n### 1.2 Use Case Assessment: Aligning Purpose and Risk\n\nBefore any code is written, teams must evaluate whether AI is appropriate:\n\n| Assessment Criteria | Questions to Ask |\n|---------------------|------------------|\n| **Societal context** | Criminal justice, healthcare, hospitality — what domain? |\n| **Potential harm** | Could the outcome amplify inequities or cause harm? |\n| **Data quality** | Is sufficient, quality data available? |\n| **Necessity of AI** | Would a simpler, rule-based solution suffice? |\n\n#### Risk Rating Frameworks\n\nProposed AI systems should be assigned a **classification level or risk rating** at the onset of development:\n\n| Risk Level | Criteria | Required Actions |\n|------------|----------|------------------|\n| **Low** | Minimal impact on individuals, low automation | Standard review |\n| **Medium** | Some individual impact, moderate automation | Enhanced documentation, review |\n| **High** | Significant impact on protected groups, legal exposure, full automation | Third-party audits, external stakeholder input, executive sign-off |\n\n> **Note:** The EU AI Act defines risk levels with prescribed activities. Absent a formal classification, use low/medium/high.\n\n### 1.3 Risk Management Tailored for AI\n\nAI systems introduce novel risks that traditional software risk management does not fully address:\n\n| Novel Risk | Description |\n|------------|-------------|\n| **Epistemic uncertainty** | AI may be confidently wrong due to data gaps or improper training |\n| **Automation bias** | Human operators defer to model outputs even when flawed |\n| **Emergent behavior** | Unexpected outcomes exceeding the system's intentions (especially multi-agent/generative systems) |\n\n**Scheduled and trigger-based risk reviews** should occur:\n- After major data or model updates\n- When monitoring flags drift or degraded performance\n- In response to public feedback or legal changes\n\n> **Exam note:** Combining AI and IT governance into a single integrated process is often more effective than maintaining separate processes.\n\n### 1.4 Ethics by Design: From Principle to Practice\n\nAbstract values (fairness, dignity) must be translated into **practical safeguards**:\n\n- **Ethical impact assessments** tied to each model's use case\n- **Moral crumple zones** — where responsibility is unfairly shifted to low-authority individuals\n- **Stakeholder engagement** (especially affected communities) in design choices\n\n#### Ethics Assessment Questions\n\n- Could this system exacerbate social inequities?\n- Are vulnerable populations affected disproportionately?\n- Can users contest or appeal decisions?\n\n#### Technical Ethics Tools\n\n| Tool Category | Examples |\n|---------------|----------|\n| **Fairness toolkits** | Aequitas, AI Fairness 360 |\n| **Explainability libraries** | SHAP, LIME |\n| **Bias detection dashboards** | Used during model validation |\n\nPolicies should **mandate the use** of such tools and require outputs to be documented in business records.\n\n### 1.5 Data Acquisition and Use\n\n#### Data Lineage and Documentation\n\nEvery AI project must maintain a **data provenance (lineage) document** capturing:\n\n- Source of each dataset\n- Licensing or usage terms\n- Known limitations (underrepresentation, class imbalance)\n\n> **Best practice:** Data lineage should be required for **all** IT system projects, not just AI.\n\n#### Consent, Privacy, and Synthetic Data\n\nPolicies should also:\n- Require informed consent where required by law\n- Address use of third-party data brokers\n- Mandate privacy-preserving techniques (e.g., **differential privacy**) in sensitive domains\n- Establish safeguards for **synthetic data** to ensure realism without compromising confidentiality\n\n### 1.6 Model Development: Guardrails for Creation\n\nDevelopment policies should require:\n\n| Policy Requirement | Description |\n|-------------------|-------------|\n| **Approved tools list** | Short list of approved AI tools, systems, and platforms |\n| **Version control** | Managed check-in/check-out rules (DVC, MLflow) |\n| **Peer review** | Reviewed/approved pull requests for model changes |\n| **Model cards** | Documenting purpose, limitations, and testing results |\n| **Traceability** | Training data sources and preprocessing pipelines |\n| **Automated testing** | Performance, bias, and robustness in CI/CD pipeline |\n| **Standard interfaces** | Approved protocols (e.g., Model Context Protocol — MCP) |\n\n> **Two-person rule:** Some firms require that no model advance to testing without review and sign-off by another qualified AI practitioner.\n\n#### Safe and Interpretable Model Architectures\n\n- Enforce **interpretable models** unless performance tradeoffs are documented and approved\n- Require **justification and formal approval** for complex architectures (deep neural networks)\n- A **rule-based system** may be more appropriate than a black box ensemble for eligibility decisions\n\n### 1.7 Training and Testing: Preparing for the Real World\n\n> **Governance focus:** Construct business rules defining requirements for model training and testing — not the engineering details.\n\n#### Training Principles\n- Training data is accurate and complete\n- Privacy rights of data subjects are preserved\n\n#### Testing Categories\n\n| Category | What Is Tested |\n|----------|----------------|\n| **Accuracy** | Does the model produce correct outputs? |\n| **Fairness** | Are outcomes equitable across groups? |\n| **Bias** | Is there disparate impact (gender, race, etc.)? |\n| **Explainability** | Can decisions be understood/justified? |\n| **Performance** | Does it meet speed/scale requirements? |\n| **Security** | Is it resilient against attacks? |\n\n#### Rigorous Validation Standards\n\nPolicies should define:\n- Performance benchmarks tailored to use case goals\n- **Subgroup analysis** to detect disparate impact\n- Controls for **data leakage** and **overfitting**\n- All test plans, results, and override approvals documented as permanent business records\n\n#### Red Teaming and Adversarial Testing\n\n| Test Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **Adversarial input testing** | Malformed inputs designed to break the model |\n| **Prompt injection scenarios** | Manipulating LLMs via crafted prompts |\n| **Simulations of user misuse** | How the system handles abuse |\n| **Conventional cyber attacks** | Standard security penetration testing |\n\nPolicy can designate a cybersecurity **\"red team\"** role for stress-testing with logged outcomes.\n\n> **Vital gate:** AI governance must include a checkpoint at the conclusion of testing for management to review results and make a **go/no-go decision**.\n\n### 1.8 Deployment and Monitoring: Guarding the Gate\n\n#### Pre-deployment Controls\n\nBefore deployment, policies should require:\n1. Ethics and compliance review checkpoint\n2. Risk assessment and mitigation review\n3. Complete documentation package submitted\n4. Monitoring plan approved by a relevant governance body\n\n> Rigor varies by risk level — healthcare AI must demonstrate patient communication readiness (human-in-the-loop protocols, privacy).\n\n#### Post-deployment Monitoring\n\n| Requirement | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| **Real-time performance tracking** | Against established baseline |\n| **Alerting** | Model drift, out-of-distribution data, fairness metrics |\n| **Human oversight** | Especially for consequential decisions |\n| **Accessible dashboards** | For non-technical stakeholders, compliance officers, regulators |\n\n> **Warning:** Organizations with low process maturity face significantly higher risks of problems with AI systems.\n\n### 1.9 Documentation and Reporting: Building Institutional Memory\n\n#### Living Documentation\n\nAI systems must be **auditable**. Policies should require:\n- Model documentation updated after each significant change\n- **Decision logs** for ethical and legal review points\n- **Explainability snapshots** for deployed models\n\n#### Internal and External Reporting\n\n| Report Type | When Required |\n|-------------|---------------|\n| **Internal reporting** | Regular reports to governance committees |\n| **External transparency reports** | Anonymized, depending on jurisdiction and risk |\n| **User/public disclosures** | Especially for systems affecting rights |\n\n### 1.10 Incident Management: Planning for Failure\n\n#### Defining and Escalating Incidents\n\nAI governance and incident response policies must clarify:\n\n| Element | Description |\n|---------|-------------|\n| **What counts as an AI incident** | Unauthorized use, biased decisions, hallucination, data leakage, drift |\n| **Reporting timelines and thresholds** | When and how to escalate |\n| **Communication templates** | For regulators, customers, or the public |\n\n> **Note:** Detection capability must be in place — you can't declare an incident if you can't detect it.\n\n#### AI Playbooks\n\nIn addition to the overall incident response plan, organizations need **detailed playbooks** for AI-specific incident types. Like other IR playbooks, these must be **tested** and personnel must be **trained**.\n\n#### Root Cause and Remediation\n\nAfter an incident:\n- Multidisciplinary team conducts a **postmortem**\n- Governance gaps are identified\n- Policies and training are updated accordingly\n\n> **Culture:** Emphasize **blameless retrospectives** that surface systemic issues, not scapegoating individuals.\n\n---", "t_policy": "## At a Glance\n\nRetrofitting privacy and security policies for AI. Legacy policies assume data sits in databases with defined purposes, but AI repurposes data, retains it inside model weights, infers what was never collected, and exposes new attack surfaces (model inversion, membership inference, prompt injection, data poisoning). Acceptable-use, retention, access and incident policies all need AI-aware updates, and a data classification policy plus a data inventory are prerequisites.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\n### Where legacy policy breaks\n\n| Policy area | The assumption AI breaks | The gap to close |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Purpose limitation** | Data collected for one purpose stays there | A compatibility analysis or new lawful basis before training on repurposed data; a use-case registry tagging each dataset with approved purposes |\n| **Retention** | Deleting the record deletes the data | Retention rules covering derived data, checkpoints, embeddings, caches and the trained model itself, plus a stated position on what a deletion request means for a model |\n| **Minimization** | Minimization applies to storage | Minimization applied to features and training sets; if fewer features perform equivalently, use fewer |\n| **De-identification** | Removing names anonymizes | A standard that survives quasi-identifier combinations and model-based re-identification, with re-anonymization required when datasets are combined |\n| **Access control** | Access is to databases | Access control over prompts, model endpoints, training environments, feature stores and model registries |\n| **Incident response** | Incidents mean a breached database | Playbooks for model inversion, membership inference, prompt injection, poisoned training data and unintended memorization |\n\n### The AI-specific threat list\n\n- **Model inversion**: reconstructing training inputs by probing the model's outputs.\n- **Membership inference**: determining whether a specific person's record was in the training set, which is itself a disclosure about that person.\n- **Data poisoning**: corrupting training data to shape model behaviour.\n- **Prompt injection**: crafting input that overrides an LLM's instructions or safety filters, including indirectly through retrieved content.\n- **Unintended memorization**: a model reproducing fragments of sensitive training data on request.\n- **Function creep**: expanding data use beyond the stated purpose through gradual AI reuse.\n\n### Shadow AI and acceptable use\n\nThe most common real-world AI policy failure is not a sophisticated attack; it is an employee pasting customer data into an unapproved tool. Acceptable-use policy has to say what data may enter a prompt, which tools are approved, and what happens when someone needs something that is not on the list. Banning without providing an approved path reliably produces shadow AI, which is worse than the exposure it was meant to prevent.\n\n### Prerequisites\n\nTwo things must exist before AI-specific policy can work: a **data classification policy** (so you know what is sensitive) and a **data inventory** (so you know where it is). The BOK states this directly. Without them, monitoring and managing AI training data is severely disadvantaged, and no amount of policy language compensates.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nGap-analysis scenarios: a company adopts AI under legacy policies, and the question asks what breaks first. Strong answers name a concrete gap, such as retention schedules that never contemplated model weights, an incident response plan with no prompt-injection playbook, or the absence of a data inventory, rather than saying \"update the policies.\"\n\nTwo other recurring angles. First, deletion requests against models: the tested insight is that a model retains data in learned form, so deletion has to address the model, not only the database, and the honest answer may be retraining. Second, security: model inversion and membership inference are offered as exotic options and are the correct answers more often than people expect, because they are precisely what conventional threat models omit.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Privacy governance is a prerequisite for AI governance; organizations weak on privacy fail at AI compliance too\n- Models retain training data in learned form, so deletion rights, retention schedules and minimization must address the model, not only the database\n- AI-specific privacy threats: model inversion (reconstructing inputs), membership inference (was this person in the training set), unintended memorization, function creep\n- AI-specific security threats: data poisoning, prompt injection (direct and indirect), model theft, adversarial examples\n- Purpose limitation is AI's chronic pain point; repurposing data for training needs a compatibility analysis or a new lawful basis\n- De-identification standards must survive quasi-identifier combinations and model-based re-identification, with re-anonymization when datasets are combined\n- Acceptable-use policy must cover what data may enter prompts and which tools are approved; banning without an approved path produces shadow AI\n- A data classification policy and data inventory are prerequisites for managing and monitoring AI training data\n- Incident response needs AI-specific playbooks, tested and rehearsed, because you cannot declare an incident you cannot detect\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-2e2023.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 100-2, Adversarial Machine Learning taxonomy</a> · the structured attack taxonomy: poisoning, evasion, privacy, abuse\n- <a href=\"https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications</a> · prompt injection, insecure output handling and the LLM attack surface\n- <a href=\"https://atlas.mitre.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MITRE ATLAS</a> · adversarial threat landscape for AI systems, MITRE ATT&CK style\n- <a href=\"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2024-12/edpb_opinion_202428_ai-models_en.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EDPB Opinion 28/2024 on AI models and personal data</a> · lawful basis, purpose limitation and the consequences of anonymity in model training\n- <a href=\"https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/artificial-intelligence/guidance-on-ai-and-data-protection/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ICO guidance on AI and data protection</a> · retention, minimization and rights in an AI context\n- <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.08232\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Secret Sharer: unintended memorization in neural networks</a> · the research behind the memorization and extraction risk\n- <a href=\"https://www.cisa.gov/ai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CISA artificial intelligence resources</a> · secure development and supply-chain guidance\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Updating Policies for AI\n\n## 2. Evaluate and Update Existing Data Privacy and Security Policies for AI\n\nTraditional data privacy and security frameworks were not designed with AI in mind. AI's ability to learn, generalize, and infer creates new challenges.\n\n### 2.1 Why AI Disrupts Traditional Data Governance\n\n#### AI Is Data-Hungry by Design\n\nAI/ML models require vast datasets, creating incentives to:\n- Aggregate data from disparate sources\n- Purchase or scrape data from third parties\n- Repurpose data collected for unrelated purposes\n\nTraditional privacy policies often lack controls for:\n- **Purpose limitation** in AI systems\n- Ongoing access to data post-training\n- Third-party training data governance\n- **Data retention** practices\n\n#### Inference and Reidentification Risks\n\nAI models can **infer sensitive traits** (race, political views, sexual orientation) even without explicit labels. Models trained on supposedly anonymized data have been shown to **leak identifying information**.\n\n> **Example:** In 2021, researchers demonstrated GPT-style models could memorize and regurgitate sensitive data (SSNs, emails) from training corpora, even when lightly obfuscated.\n\n### 2.2 Privacy Policy Gaps and AI-Specific Threats\n\n> **Prerequisite:** Privacy governance is a prerequisite for AI governance. Without it, organizations are more likely to run afoul of privacy laws.\n\n#### Common Policy Shortfalls\n\nMost organizations' existing policies:\n- Do not specify how long training data is retained post-model development\n- Lack controls for **model inversion** or **membership inference attacks**\n- Assume data minimization applies only to storage, not model retention\n- Do not include data subject rights in model outputs (right to explanation, opt-out, deletion)\n\n#### AI-Specific Privacy Threats\n\n| Threat | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| **Model inversion** | Reconstructing input data by probing the model |\n| **Membership inference** | Determining whether a specific record was in the training set |\n| **Model poisoning** | Maliciously injecting false data into training pipelines |\n| **Function creep** | Expanding data use beyond stated original purposes through AI reuse |\n| **Unintended memorization** | LLMs retaining and reproducing fragments of sensitive training data |\n\n### 2.3 Key Policy Areas to Evaluate and Update\n\n#### Purpose Limitation in Dynamic Pipelines\n\nPrivacy policies must:\n- Define permissible reuse conditions\n- Require new consent if original use cases change significantly\n- Establish logging and audit trails for each dataset's usage across model versions\n\n> **Policy tip:** Introduce a **\"use case registry\"** that tags each dataset with approved purposes and flags violations during pipeline construction.\n\n#### Enhanced Anonymization and Pseudonymization Standards\n\nUpdated policies should include:\n- **Quantifiable risk metrics** (k-anonymity, l-diversity)\n- **Differential privacy** for sensitive domains\n- Mandatory **re-anonymization** when combining datasets\n\n#### Lifecycle-Based Data Retention Controls\n\nPolicies must distinguish between:\n\n| Data Layer | Retention Considerations |\n|------------|-------------------------|\n| **Raw input data** | Delete after model convergence? |\n| **Transformed/feature data** | Same rules as raw data? |\n| **Labeled datasets** | Purpose-specific retention |\n| **Model artifacts** | How do deletion requests apply to learned weights? |\n\n> **\"Forgetting budget\":** A threshold on how much data a model can memorize, triggering retraining when exceeded. Disclose this practice in the Notice of Privacy Practices (NOPP).\n\n### 2.4 Data Subject Rights in AI Contexts\n\n#### Right to Explanation and Access\n\nUpdated privacy policies must clarify how data subjects can:\n- Request access to data used to train models affecting them\n- Receive a **meaningful explanation** of AI-driven decisions\n- Understand when an AI system is involved in decision-making\n\n> Aligns with GDPR (Articles 13–15, 22) and the EU AI Act.\n\n#### Right to Be Forgotten (Data Deletion)\n\nPrivacy policies should define whether:\n- Models must be **retrained** when personal data is deleted\n- Deletion applies to downstream systems, fine-tuned derivatives, or API-accessible models\n- A process exists for data subjects to **verify** that deletion has occurred\n\n> **Exam note:** This is especially important for foundation models trained on large, uncurated web datasets.\n\n### 2.5 Security Policy Updates for AI Systems\n\n#### Expanding the Threat Model\n\nAI systems are vulnerable to:\n\n| Threat Vector | Description |\n|---------------|-------------|\n| **Adversarial attacks** | Manipulating inputs to cause harmful outputs |\n| **Data poisoning** | Corrupting training data |\n| **Model theft** | Extracting model via API access |\n| **Prompt injection / jailbreaks** | Bypassing LLM safety filters |\n\nSecurity policies must be revised to:\n- Include AI in threat modeling exercises\n- Define monitoring for AI-specific logs, telemetry, and behaviors\n- Enforce rate-limiting, access control, and model segmentation\n\n#### Securing the AI Supply Chain\n\nUpdated security policies must address:\n- **Software Bill of Materials (SBOM)** for AI pipelines\n- Vetting and licensing review of third-party models\n- Continuous vulnerability scanning for AI components\n\n### 2.6 Practical Steps for Policy Revision\n\n1. **Conduct a gap assessment** — audit privacy and security policies for AI exposure\n2. **Build cross-functional review teams** — include data privacy officers, security architects, AI/ML engineers, legal and ethics advisors\n3. **Produce policy compliance artifacts** — tangible evidence (architecture review, configuration settings, worksheets/charts) that policies are being followed\n\n> **Prerequisite:** Organizations must already have a **data classification policy** and **data inventory**. Without these, management and monitoring of AI training data will be severely disadvantaged.\n\n---", "t_thirdparty": "## At a Glance\n\nVendor risk, AI edition. Organizations increasingly buy AI capability (pretrained models, APIs, labeling services, cloud platforms), importing risks that traditional third-party risk management never probed: opaque training data, sub-tier suppliers, silently changing model versions, and contractual gaps around fairness, audit and incidents. The BOK covers this under policies, assessments and contracts, across procurement, supply chain, human resources and acceptable use.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\n### Why AI slips past conventional TPRM\n\n| Conventional TPRM checks | What it misses about AI |\n|---|---|\n| Financial solvency, insurance, certification | Training data of unknown origin or rights status |\n| Standard security questionnaires | Model behaviour that changes with each vendor update |\n| Contract templates for services | Liability allocation for harmful or discriminatory output |\n| Named-subcontractor disclosure | Sub-tier data labelers, base-model suppliers and open-source dependencies |\n\nThe structural problem is that a vendor's model is a black box inside a black box: you cannot inspect the training data, you often cannot see the model version, and you may not learn that behaviour changed until your users report it. Point-in-time diligence therefore decays fast.\n\n### Four-part response\n\n**1. Flag AI at procurement intake.** Modify intake to ask whether the product includes AI, ML, LLMs or automated decision-making; whether your data will train their models; and whether personal, biometric or sensitive data is processed. Flagged deals get supplemental review by legal, risk and technical stakeholders. Not all vendors volunteer their AI use, so the question must be asked rather than inferred.\n\n**2. Run AI-specific due diligence.** Training-data origin and rights; fairness and robustness test evidence; explainability support; model-change practices; security of the ML supply chain; and named sub-tier dependencies (who labeled the data, whose base model is underneath). A standardized supplier questionnaire mapped to risk tiers keeps this repeatable.\n\n**3. Write the contract terms that make risk manageable.**\n\n| Clause | What it should secure |\n|---|---|\n| Data-use limits | No customer data used for retraining without explicit consent |\n| Fairness and performance | Warranties, plus evidence of testing and remediation of disparate impact |\n| Model-change notification | Advance notice of updates that change behaviour or intended use |\n| Incident notification | Time-bound reporting (for example within 24 hours) and defined responsibilities |\n| Audit rights | The right to audit or inspect high-risk vendor systems, and to receive documentation |\n| Termination and portability | Exit conditions, model deactivation rights, and data return or deletion |\n\n**4. Monitor continuously.** Continuous monitoring beats point-in-time diligence because vendor model updates change behaviour mid-contract. An **AI system bill of materials** listing upstream inputs is the emerging instrument for visibility into the sub-tier chain, and open-source components need the same licensing, vulnerability and bias review as commercial ones.\n\n### The line that does not move\n\nAccountability is non-delegable. Outsourcing the AI does not outsource the regulatory or ethical responsibility, and the EU AI Act reinforces this by attaching obligations to whoever places a system on the market or uses it, not to whoever built it furthest upstream.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nScenarios ask what due diligence or contract terms an AI procurement demands. Strong answers are AI-specific: training-data provenance, bias testing evidence, model-change notification, audit rights, incident SLAs. Answers that list only generic vendor checks (financials, insurance, SOC 2) are engineered to be incomplete.\n\nThe other reliable angle is the sub-tier question: a vendor that subcontracts data labeling, fine-tunes someone else's base model, or ships open-source dependencies has extended your exposure past your contractual counterparty. And a question that frames outsourcing as transferring responsibility is wrong: accountability stays with the deploying organization.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- AI vendor due diligence adds training-data origin and rights, fairness and robustness evidence, explainability support, security of the ML supply chain, and model-change practices\n- Flag AI at procurement intake: does the product use AI, will your data train their models, is sensitive data processed\n- Sub-tier risk extends your exposure: data labelers, base-model suppliers and open-source dependencies are your risk too\n- Contracts should secure data-use limits, fairness and performance warranties, model-change and incident notification, audit rights, and exit or portability terms\n- Continuous monitoring beats point-in-time diligence because vendor model updates change behaviour mid-contract\n- An AI system bill of materials listing upstream inputs is the emerging instrument for supply-chain visibility\n- Open-source AI components need licensing, vulnerability and bias review, not just a security scan\n- Accountability is non-delegable: outsourcing the AI does not outsource the regulatory or ethical responsibility\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1), third-party and value-chain risk</a> · GOVERN 6 and the Map function on dependencies\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/25/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 25 (responsibilities along the AI value chain)</a> · how obligations follow the value chain\n- <a href=\"https://www.iso.org/standard/42001\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 42001 (AI management system)</a> · Annex A controls for third-party relationships\n- <a href=\"https://www.cisa.gov/ai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CISA artificial intelligence resources</a> · supply-chain and secure-development guidance\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 600-1, value-chain and component integration risk</a> · the generative-AI version of third-party risk\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Updating Policies for AI\n\n## 3. Policies to Manage Third-Party Risk\n\nAs organizations outsource AI components (pretrained models, cloud infrastructure, data labeling, algorithmic APIs), third-party risk management must evolve beyond traditional vendor checklists.\n\n### 3.1 Why AI Multiplies Third-Party Risk\n\n#### Beyond the Traditional Vendor Model\n\nAI introduces risks that slip past standard procurement controls:\n\n| Traditional TPRM Focus | AI-Specific Gaps |\n|------------------------|------------------|\n| Legal, financial, cyber exposure | Black box algorithms lacking explainability |\n| Vendor solvency, insurance, compliance | Datasets of unknown origin or labeling quality |\n| Standard contract terms | LLM APIs with unpredictable outputs or safety filters |\n\n#### Hidden Dependencies and Sub-Tier Risks\n\nA procurement team may contract with one vendor, unaware that:\n- The vendor relies on subcontracted data labeling teams overseas\n- The model was trained on scraped or copyrighted data without authorization\n- Code libraries include outdated or vulnerable dependencies\n- The vendor's own third parties may use AI capabilities affecting the organization\n\n> **Key challenge:** How many levels down should the TPRM program probe?\n\n### 3.2 Core Policy Objectives for Third-Party AI Risk\n\nAn effective governance framework should:\n1. **Identify and categorize** third-party touchpoints in the AI lifecycle\n2. **Impose due diligence** requirements including AI-specific risks\n3. **Define contractual obligations** around data use, fairness, security, accountability, and recourse\n4. **Establish monitoring and review mechanisms** for ongoing compliance\n\n### 3.3 Procurement Policy Controls for AI-Enabled Solutions\n\n#### AI Risk Flagging at Procurement Intake\n\nModify procurement intake to flag when:\n- A product includes AI, ML, LLMs, or automated decision-making\n- Data from the organization will be used to train models\n- The vendor processes personal, biometric, or sensitive information\n\n> Flagged items trigger supplemental review by legal, risk, and technical stakeholders.\n\n#### AI-Specific Due Diligence\n\nProcurement policies should require vendors to provide:\n\n| Requirement | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| **Model documentation** | Training data sources and licensing |\n| **Explainability** | Model logic/decision criteria (or justification for lack thereof) |\n| **Audit results** | Internal or third-party audits for bias, security, robustness |\n| **Regulatory compliance** | Evidence of compliance with CPRA, GDPR, EU AI Act |\n\n> Use standardized **AI supplier questionnaires** mapping responses to risk levels and approval requirements.\n\n#### Vendor Risk Tiers\n\n| Risk Tier | Criteria | Required Controls |\n|-----------|----------|-------------------|\n| **Low** | Minimal automation, non-sensitive data | Standard review |\n| **Medium** | Some automation, limited PII | Enhanced review, contractual safeguards |\n| **High** | Full autonomy, PII/health data, financial decisions | Fairness testing, incident response clauses, continuous monitoring |\n\n> **Important:** Not all third parties disclose AI capabilities. It's vital to understand **how** AI is used, not just **whether**.\n\n### 3.4 Contracting and Legal Safeguards\n\n#### AI-Specific Contractual Clauses\n\n| Clause | Requirement |\n|--------|-------------|\n| **Disclosure** | Vendors must disclose all uses of AI in their organization |\n| **Data use limits** | No customer data for retraining without explicit consent |\n| **Fairness and bias** | Test for and remediate disparate impact |\n| **Security** | Model access control and incident reporting requirements |\n| **Continuous monitoring** | Vendor must detect problems in its AI system |\n| **Audit rights** | Customer may audit or inspect high-risk vendor systems |\n| **Termination** | AI-related conditions for agreement termination |\n\n#### Liability and Incident Handling\n\n- **Clear definitions of responsibility** for AI errors or harms\n- **Time-bound incident notifications** (e.g., within 24 hours)\n- **Model deactivation rights** if risks exceed acceptable thresholds\n\n### 3.5 AI Supply Chain Governance\n\n#### Mapping the AI Supply Chain\n\nOrganizations must create visibility into:\n- Models built in-house using third-party data or tools\n- Open-source libraries used in AI workflows\n- Pretrained foundation models fine-tuned for internal use\n\n> Require vendors to submit an **AI System Bill of Materials (AI-SBOM)** listing all upstream inputs.\n\n#### Open-Source AI Risk Policies\n\nPolicies must address:\n- **Licensing clarity** — ensure proper licensing of all open-source assets\n- **Security vetting** — scan packages for known vulnerabilities\n- **Bias review** — validate data quality and representation in open datasets\n\n> **Note:** A 2022 paper (\"Tragedy of the Digital Commons\" by Chinmayi Sharma) refuted the long-held belief that open-source software is inherently more secure.\n\n### 3.6 Other Third-Party AI Issues\n\n#### Contracting with Human Annotators and AI Workers\n\nPolicies should require:\n- Vetting of vendor practices for **ethical labor standards**\n- **Data confidentiality agreements** with all annotators\n- Clear instructions and **QA protocols** for human labelers\n\n#### Employee Use of Generative AI Tools\n\nOrganizations should amend **Acceptable Use Policies (AUPs)** to define:\n\n| Policy Element | Description |\n|----------------|-------------|\n| **Approved tools** | Which AI tools and use cases are permitted |\n| **Prohibited data types** | What data must not be input (per data classification policy) |\n| **Logging and review** | Mechanisms for AI usage monitoring |\n\n> Conduct training programs to inform workers of policy intent and enforcement.\n\n### 3.7 Monitoring and Reviewing Third-Party AI Risk\n\n#### Ongoing Vendor Oversight\n\nTPRM policies should mandate periodic reviews of:\n- Model performance (drift, bias)\n- Compliance with contract terms\n- Changes to vendor business practices or ownership\n\n> High-risk vendors → **continuous monitoring program** with periodic check-ins and annual recertification.\n\n#### Triggers for Reassessment\n\n| Trigger Event | Response |\n|---------------|----------|\n| Additions or changes in vendor services | Immediate reassessment |\n| Security incidents and breaches | Immediate reassessment |\n| Public controversy (model misuse) | Immediate reassessment |\n| Technical incidents (hallucinations, outages) | Immediate reassessment |\n| Regulatory changes affecting the model's domain | Immediate reassessment |\n\n> Without continuous monitoring, organizations will likely miss events that would trigger reassessments.\n\n---", "ch4": "## At a Glance\n\nThis knowledge area applies privacy and data protection law to AI. Core doctrines - transparency, choice, lawful basis, purpose limitation, data minimization, privacy by design - collide with AI's appetite for repurposed data and its ability to infer what was never collected. It also covers controller obligations (DPIAs, processor contracts, cross-border transfers, data subject rights, automated decision-making, breach notification, records) and the extra rules for sensitive or special-category data such as biometrics.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nII.A carries 4 to 6 questions and is four indicators, each mapping onto a section of the knowledge area.\n\n### 1. Transparency, choice, lawful basis and purpose limitation\n\nA privacy notice must disclose purposes, data categories, legal basis, retention, recipients and whether decisions are made solely by automated means. AI strains each: models are opaque, they **infer** sensitive facts from ordinary inputs, they change behaviour after collection, they pull from many sources, and controller/processor lines blur across a supply chain.\n\n**Consent** must be freely given, specific, informed, unambiguous, given by a clear affirmative act and as easy to withdraw as to give, with the burden of proof on the controller. Pre-ticked boxes, bundling and dark patterns all fail. Consent is one of six Article 6 bases, not the default, and there is no hierarchy among them.\n\n**Purpose limitation** (Article 5(1)(b)) is AI's chronic problem: data collected to deliver a service cannot be silently repurposed to train a model, and a secondary use needs a compatibility assessment or a fresh basis. **Lawful basis** for training is usually contested on legitimate interest (Article 6(1)(f)), because consent does not scale. EDPB Opinion 28/2024 applies a three-step test: a real and present interest, necessity (no less intrusive route, proportionate data), and a balancing test in which data subjects' reasonable expectations carry weight. The EDPB's draft Guidelines 03/2026 on web scraping say consent \"would most probably not be\" workable at that scale and that legitimate interest is the usual basis, and they repeat that special-category data needs an Article 9(2) exception **as well as** an Article 6 basis. The Commission's proposed AI-specific legitimate-interest article (Article 88c) is still a proposal, not law.\n\n### 2. Data minimization and privacy by design\n\nArticle 5(1)(c) requires data that is adequate, relevant and limited to what is necessary. AI's pull toward large datasets and overcollection runs against it, so minimization is applied stage by stage: limit scope at collection, drop non-contributing features and mask sensitive fields at preprocessing, train with privacy-preserving methods (federated learning, differential privacy), and limit inputs at inference.\n\n**Privacy by design** (given legal force by Article 25) embeds protection from the outset: proactive not remedial, privacy as the default, embedded into design, full functionality, end-to-end security, visibility and transparency, and respect for the user. In practice: DPIAs at problem framing, privacy-preserving training and fairness validation during development, then retention limits, contestability and drift monitoring in operation.\n\n**Anonymisation is a high bar.** Under EDPB Opinion 28/2024 a model trained on personal data is anonymous only if it is insignificantly likely that anyone could extract a training subject's data from it, directly or through queries, judged case by case. The draft Guidelines 02/2026 propose three tests: no record isolation, no linkage, no inference. Removing names is pseudonymisation, not anonymisation.\n\n### 3. Controller obligations\n\nThe controller determines the purposes and means of processing (Article 4(7)) and owes the accountability duty to **demonstrate** compliance (Article 5(2)). A **DPIA** (Article 35) is required for processing likely to result in high risk, which AI triggers through automated decisions with legal or similarly significant effects, large-scale special-category data, systematic profiling, monitoring of public areas, or vulnerable populations.\n\nThird-party **processors** (cloud, training, foundation models, labelling, AI-as-a-Service) need an Article 28 data processing agreement covering subject matter, data types, security, sub-processing, rights and DPIA assistance, deletion and audit rights. Watch for **processor drift**, where a vendor's continuous training quietly widens processing beyond instructions.\n\n**Cross-border transfers** need an adequacy decision, standard contractual clauses, binding corporate rules or a derogation, and remote **access** from outside the EEA counts even if no data is stored there. After Schrems II, SCCs and BCRs require a transfer impact assessment.\n\n**Data subject rights** meet their hardest cases here: access (Article 15) must explain black-box outputs, erasure (Article 17) struggles with weights, and objection (Article 21) collides with profiling. **Article 22** prohibits decisions based **solely** on automated processing with legal or similarly significant effects unless a contract, law or explicit consent allows it, and even then the controller owes safeguards including meaningful human intervention. Two CJEU rulings define the doctrine: **SCHUFA** (C-634/21, 2023) holds a credit bureau's score is itself an Article 22 decision when the lender draws strongly on it, so upstream score and model providers can owe the duties; **Dun & Bradstreet Austria** (C-203/22, 2025) holds Article 15(1)(h) means a concise, intelligible account of the procedure and principles actually applied, not a formula dump, with claimed trade secrets weighed by the DPA or court. **Breach notification** (Article 33) requires telling the supervisory authority within 72 hours and affected individuals without undue delay where risk is high, with AI-specific vectors (training-data leakage, inversion and membership inference, output regurgitation, model poisoning) that standard DLP misses.\n\n### 4. Sensitive and special-category data\n\nArticle 9 forbids processing special-category data (biometrics for identification, health, genetic, racial or ethnic origin, religion, political opinions, union membership, sex life or orientation) unless a narrow exception applies, with explicit consent the usual one. AI makes this harder three ways: deliberate use (health records), incidental collection (a CV tool harvesting political affiliation), and **inference**, where a model deduces a protected characteristic from inputs that never contained it, and the inference still triggers Article 9. Heightened protection for biometrics attaches when the data is used to **uniquely identify** a person, so categorisation without identity linkage may fall outside Article 9 but still carries bias risk. As amended by the 2026 Digital Omnibus on AI, a new AI Act Article 4a adds an AI Act basis for special-category data used strictly to detect and correct bias, on top of the GDPR.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nGDPR is the reference statute: anchor articles by number - 5 (principles), 6 (lawful bases), 9 (special categories), 22 (automated decisions), 25 (privacy by design), 28 (processors), 33 (breach notice), 35 (DPIAs). Scenario questions test whether a processing operation needs consent or another lawful basis, when a DPIA is mandatory, what secondary use of training data requires, and what safeguards an automated decision needs. Expect the two CJEU rulings to appear: SCHUFA (a decisive score is itself an Article 22 decision) and Dun & Bradstreet Austria (access means an intelligible explanation of the logic actually applied). EDPB Opinion 28/2024 supplies the three-step legitimate-interest test and the rule that models are not automatically anonymous; the 2026 anonymisation and web-scraping guidelines are drafts, so an answer that treats them as settled law is wrong.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Purpose limitation is AI's chronic pain point: data collected for service delivery cannot be silently repurposed for model training without a compatibility assessment or a fresh basis\n- Data minimization applies to models too: if 15 features perform like 50, use 15, and train with federated learning or differential privacy where raw data is not needed\n- GDPR Article 22 grants the right not to be subject to solely automated decisions with legal or significant effects, with narrow exceptions and required safeguards including meaningful human intervention\n- DPIAs are required for high-risk processing - systematic profiling, large-scale special categories, automated decisions with significant effects - and AI routinely triggers them\n- Special categories (health, biometrics, race and more) need an Article 9 condition; AI can infer them from ordinary inputs, and the inference triggers Article 9 even though nothing was collected\n- Controllers must bind processors with Article 28 DPAs covering instructions, security, sub-processors, rights assistance, deletion and audit, and must watch for processor drift\n- SCHUFA (2023) makes a decisive third-party score an Article 22 decision; Dun & Bradstreet Austria (2025) requires an intelligible explanation of the logic actually applied, with trade secrets weighed by the regulator or court\n- Models are not automatically anonymous: EDPB Opinion 28/2024 sets the extraction test, and removing names is pseudonymisation, not anonymisation\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679</a> · Articles 5, 6, 9, 22, 25, 28, 33 and 35 are the ones AI governance leans on\n- <a href=\"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/documents/opinion-of-the-board-art-64/opinion-282024-on-certain-data-protection-aspects-related-to_en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EDPB Opinion 28/2024 on AI models and personal data</a> · the three-step legitimate-interest test, and the position that trained models are not automatically anonymous\n- <a href=\"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/news/edpb-sheds-light-on-anonymisation-and-web-scraping-for-generative-ai-and-adopts-final-version_en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EDPB draft Guidelines 02/2026 (anonymisation) and 03/2026 (web scraping)</a> · drafts, consultation to 30 October 2026: how DPAs will judge anonymisation and scraping, not yet final\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/C/2024/913/oj/eng\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CJEU SCHUFA, C-634/21 (7 December 2023)</a> · a score the lender draws on strongly is itself an Article 22 automated decision\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=celex:62022CJ0203\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CJEU Dun & Bradstreet Austria, C-203/22 (27 February 2025)</a> · Article 15(1)(h) means an intelligible account of the logic actually applied, not an algorithm dump\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2026/1744/oj/eng\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Digital Omnibus on AI, Regulation (EU) 2026/1744</a> · new AI Act Article 4a: a basis for special-category data used strictly to detect and correct bias\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Privacy and Data Protection Law\n\n## Performance Indicators\n\n*BOK v2.1, II.A, paraphrased. The subdomain carries 4–6 exam questions.*\n\n- Apply the rules on **transparency, choice, lawful basis and purpose limitation** to AI processing\n- Apply **data minimization** and **privacy by design** to AI systems\n- Explain how **controller obligations** work for AI: privacy impact assessments, third-party processors, cross-border transfers, data subject rights, **automated decision-making**, incident management, breach notification and record keeping\n- Know the extra rules for **sensitive or special categories of data**, such as biometrics\n\n> [!note] What changed from BOK v2.0.1\n> - **Indicator 1:** \"notice, choice, consent\" became \"**transparency, choice, lawful basis**\". The focus widens from consent to every Art. 6 basis, so legitimate interest for AI training is now squarely testable, and from notices to transparency generally.\n> - **Indicator 3:** the controller-obligations list now names **automated decision-making** (GDPR Art. 22), alongside DPIAs, processors, transfers, rights, incidents, breach notification and records.\n> - **Weight:** unchanged at 4–6 questions.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n> [!source] Privacy and Data Protection Law\n\n## 3. Practical Implications and Governance\n\n> **GDPR Articles:** 5(2) accountability obligation, Art. 24 controller responsibilities\n\n### Governance Touchpoints Across the AI Lifecycle\n\n| AI Lifecycle Stage | Privacy Activities |\n|--------------------|--------------------|\n| **Problem definition** | Conduct DPIAs, define legitimate purpose, assess necessity |\n| **Data acquisition** | Validate lawful basis, apply data minimization, assess third-party sources |\n| **Model development** | Bias/fairness audits, privacy-preserving ML techniques, pseudonymization/anonymization |\n| **Model evaluation** | Confirm outputs align with original purpose, validate explainability and transparency |\n| **Deployment** | Enable opt-outs, provide contestability/notices, apply access controls, encrypt data |\n| **Monitoring & updates** | Monitor drift, ensure purposes remain consistent, reevaluate risks |\n\n### Privacy Roles and Responsibilities\n\n| Role | Responsibility |\n|------|----------------|\n| **Data Protection Officer (DPO)** | Legal compliance, DPIA advice, risk management, subject rights |\n| **Chief AI Officer / AI governance lead** | Ethical AI development and use; alignment with policies and regulations |\n| **MLOps & engineering teams** | Privacy-respecting infrastructure, logging, monitoring, model lifecycle management |\n| **Product & UX designers** | Intuitive, fair consent mechanisms; free of dark patterns |\n| **Security teams** | Encryption, pseudonymization, intrusion detection, incident response |\n| **Legal & compliance teams** | Translate legal requirements into controls; audits; breach response |\n\n### Documentation and Auditability\n\nKey accountability artifacts:\n- **Data flow diagrams**\n- **Model cards and system cards**\n- **Records of processing activities** (GDPR Art. 30)\n- **Logs of consent and purpose linkage**\n- **Risk assessment outputs** (DPIAs, LIA balancing tests)\n- **Validation and testing reports**\n\n> Maintain a centralized **AI risk register** or documentation hub for audit readiness.\n\n### AI Governance Playbook: Key Policies\n\n| Policy | Purpose |\n|--------|---------|\n| **AI use policy** | Defines acceptable and prohibited AI uses |\n| **Privacy impact policy** | Requires DPIAs for high-risk AI systems |\n| **Data governance policy** | Covers lineage, quality, classification, minimization |\n| **Model governance policy** | Versioning, retraining, validation |\n| **Third-party risk policy** | Governs vendor AI tools and data sources |\n| **Incident response policy (AI)** | AI-specific breach, model failure, misuse scenarios |\n| **Change management policy (AI)** | Review, approval, rollback procedures for model updates |\n\n### Scaling Governance\n\n- **AI risk committees** or review boards\n- **Tiered governance** based on AI risk levels (high-risk vs. low-impact)\n- **Tooling integration** with data catalogs, MLOps, governance platforms\n- **Training programs** for AI privacy risk awareness\n- **AI centers of excellence (COE)** for cross-team collaboration\n\n---\n\n---\n\n> [!source] Privacy and Data Protection Law\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n1. **Processing of sensitive data is prohibited by default under GDPR** unless a specific legal exception applies (explicit consent or substantial public interest). Special category data — biometrics, health, religion, political beliefs — cannot be processed without a narrow Art. 9(2) exception. Explicit consent must be freely given, specific, informed, and documented.\n\n2. **Data controllers are accountable for the entire AI lifecycle** — DPIAs, lawful basis, data subject rights, vendor oversight, and cross-border transfer compliance. Controllers must demonstrate compliance, not just ensure it. Responsibilities cannot be delegated.\n\n3. **Purpose limitation, data minimization, and privacy by design are foundational** to trustworthy AI governance. Data should only be collected for defined, lawful purposes and minimized to what is necessary. PbD requires integrating privacy controls throughout development — feature selection, model explainability, user opt-outs.\n\n4. **AI systems can infer sensitive attributes** (race, religion, health) even without explicit collection — still triggering legal obligations. Inferred sensitive data is subject to the same protections as explicitly collected data. Controllers must assess whether systems generate protected characteristics and apply appropriate legal bases and safeguards.\n\n5. **Automated decisions involving special category data may be restricted or require meaningful human involvement** and additional safeguards. Under GDPR Art. 22, individuals have the right not to be subject to solely automated decisions with legal/similar effects. Where permitted, systems must provide explainability, human review, and redress mechanisms.\n\n6. **EU regulators and courts have filled in the AI detail since 2023.** EDPB Opinion 28/2024 says trained models are not automatically anonymous and sets out a three-step legitimate-interest test. *SCHUFA* (2023) makes a decisive third-party score an Art. 22 decision. *Dun & Bradstreet Austria* (2025) requires an intelligible account of the logic actually applied, with trade secrets weighed by the regulator or court. The EDPB's 2026 anonymisation and web-scraping guidelines are still drafts, and the GDPR changes in the Digital Omnibus are still proposals.\n\n---", "t_consent": "## At a Glance\n\nThe front door of data protection applied to AI: people must know (notice) and often agree (consent, choice) to how their data is used, and every use must honour the purpose it was collected for (purpose limitation). AI strains all three, because models are opaque, they infer what was never collected, and training is a tempting second use for data gathered for something else.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThis topic is the first II.A indicator, and it is three doctrines that lean on each other.\n\n### Notice\n\nNotice means telling people how their data will be collected, used, stored and shared. For AI it must also disclose whether decisions are made solely by automated means. The required content: purposes, data categories, legal basis, retention periods, recipients, and the automated-decision fact.\n\nAI makes notice hard in six ways: opaque black-box logic, **inference** (creating sensitive facts from ordinary inputs), dynamic behaviour (a model that keeps learning uses data differently after collection), many data sources with unclear provenance, automated decision-making that Article 22 does not fully reach, and accountability gaps where controller and processor roles blur across a supply chain. A good AI notice is plain-language and layered, explains the AI's role in the decision, says whether a human is in the loop, and tells people how to access, contest or opt out. Burying \"we train models on your data\" on page 40 of a privacy policy is not notice, and it draws both regulator and FTC attention.\n\n### Choice and consent\n\nValid consent is freely given, specific, informed, unambiguous, given by a clear affirmative act, revocable, and recorded, with the burden of proof on the controller. Explicit consent is the higher bar required for special-category data and high-risk uses; implicit consent is generally insufficient for AI; opt-in is the GDPR default and opt-out is available only in limited jurisdictions. Four obstacles recur: power imbalance (people cannot refuse an essential service), lack of understanding (nobody comprehends what a deep model will infer), opacity, and **dark patterns** (pre-ticked boxes, hidden opt-outs, biased defaults, the illusion of choice). Consent is one of six Article 6 bases, not the default, and there is no hierarchy among them.\n\n### Purpose limitation\n\nArticle 5(1)(b) requires data collected for specified, explicit and legitimate purposes and not further processed incompatibly. **Purpose creep** is the gradual widening of use beyond the original purpose, and it is endemic in AI where data is reused across product features, R&D, monetisation and unrelated prediction. Training is a separate purpose from inference, third-party models arrive with undisclosed training data, and users are rarely told what downstream purposes a model serves. The cure is a **compatibility assessment** for each secondary use, separate datasets for distinct tasks, dataset documentation aligned to intended use, and updated notices at every material change.\n\n### How the three fit\n\nNotice tells; choice empowers; purpose limitation constrains. An exam scenario usually breaks one of the three: a repurposed dataset (purpose), a coerced or bundled consent (choice), or an undisclosed automated decision (notice).\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nScenarios probe whether repurposing data for AI training needs fresh notice and consent or a compatibility analysis, and what valid consent requires (freely given, specific, informed, unambiguous, and as easy to withdraw as to give). The recurring trap is treating consent as the default basis: it is one of six, and legitimate interest is often the real basis for training. A second trap is bundling consent with a service or hiding it behind a dark pattern, which fails the free and specific tests.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Purpose limitation: data collected to deliver a service cannot be silently diverted to train models - a compatibility assessment or a new lawful basis is required\n- GDPR-valid consent is freely given, specific, informed, unambiguous and as easy to withdraw as to give; bundled or coerced consent fails\n- Notice must actually inform: burying AI training in page 40 of a privacy policy invites regulator and FTC attention\n- Consent is one lawful basis among several (contract, legitimate interests, legal obligation) - choose deliberately and document the choice\n- Secondary use is the recurring AI sin: assess compatibility, or obtain fresh consent, before repurposing\n- AI inference creates sensitive data from ordinary inputs, so notice and consent must cover what the model deduces, not only what was collected\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679</a> · Articles 5, 6, 12 to 14 and 21 are the notice, consent and purpose-limitation rules\n- <a href=\"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/documents/opinion-of-the-board-art-64/opinion-282024-on-certain-data-protection-aspects-related-to_en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EDPB Opinion 28/2024 on AI models and personal data</a> · the three-step legitimate-interest test, where reasonable expectations carry weight\n- <a href=\"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/news/edpb-sheds-light-on-anonymisation-and-web-scraping-for-generative-ai-and-adopts-final-version_en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EDPB draft Guidelines 03/2026 on web scraping</a> · draft: consent does not scale for scraping, so legitimate interest is the usual basis\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Privacy and Data Protection Law\n\n## 1. Transparency, Choice, Lawful Basis, and Purpose Limitation in AI\n\n> **Foundational GDPR Articles:** 5, 6, 12–14, 21. BOK v2.1 calls this indicator \"transparency, choice, lawful basis and purpose limitation\"; the older \"notice, choice, consent\" framing survives in the subsections below.\n\n### Notice\n\n**Notice** — informing individuals about how their personal data will be collected, used, stored, and shared. In AI, notice must also disclose how **automated decision-making** may affect individuals, especially when profiling or inferencing is involved.\n\n| Required Disclosure | Description |\n|---------------------|-------------|\n| Purposes of processing | Why data is collected and used |\n| Categories of personal data | What types of data |\n| Legal basis for processing | GDPR Art. 6 / Art. 9 grounds |\n| Data retention periods | How long data is kept |\n| Recipients of the data | Who receives or accesses it |\n| Automated decision-making | Whether decisions are made solely by automated means |\n\n#### Unique Challenges in AI\n\n| Challenge | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **Opacity of algorithms** | \"Black box\" systems — hard to explain processing or decisions |\n| **Inference** | AI creates sensitive data (political views, health) from non-sensitive inputs |\n| **Dynamic behavior** | ML models evolve, changing how data is used post-collection |\n| **Multiple data sources** | Third-party, sensors, open web — hard to trace provenance |\n| **Automated decision-making** | GDPR Art. 22 is narrow; doesn't fully address bias/fairness/discrimination |\n| **Accountability gaps** | Unclear controller/processor boundaries in AI supply chains |\n\n> **Example:** A city deploys facial recognition in public areas without notice — direct failure of notice requirement. (Prohibited in EU — see [[AI and Intellectual Property]])\n\n### Key Elements of an Effective AI Privacy Notice\n\n- Plain language and **layered formats**\n- Explanation of AI's role in decision-making\n- Presence of **human oversight** in AI decisions\n- Ability to **access or contest** decisions\n- Ability to **opt out** of automated decision-making\n- Clarification of **data subject rights**\n- Justification of **algorithmic fairness and necessity**\n\n### Choice and Consent\n\n**Consent** — individual's voluntary agreement to processing. Under GDPR, valid consent must be: freely given, specific, informed, unambiguous, revocable, and recorded.\n\n| Consent Type | When Required |\n|--------------|---------------|\n| **Explicit consent** | Sensitive data, high-risk AI use (e.g., health-related AI) |\n| **Implicit consent** | Generally **insufficient** for most AI applications |\n| **Opt-in** | GDPR default requirement |\n| **Opt-out** | Permitted only in limited jurisdictions |\n\n#### Obstacles to Meaningful Consent in AI\n\n| Obstacle | Impact |\n|----------|--------|\n| **Power imbalance** | Users lack bargaining power with essential AI services |\n| **Lack of understanding** | Complex AI reduces comprehension of what's being consented to |\n| **Opacity** | Undocumented or intentionally hidden data uses |\n| **Dark patterns** | UI designs that trick/coerce users into consent |\n\n> **Example:** Wellness app with pre-checked consent box, no granular control — fails informed/specific consent under GDPR.\n\n### Dark Patterns and AI: A Compliance Risk\n\n**Dark patterns** — deceptive interface designs manipulating users into unintended choices. Manifestations in AI:\n\n- Forced consent through **bundled services**\n- Obscured or **hidden opt-out buttons**\n- **Biased default settings**\n- **Illusion of choice** (all options lead to the same outcome)\n\n> EU and US regulators are increasingly cracking down on these practices.\n\n### Purpose Limitation\n\n**Purpose limitation** — personal data must be collected for **specified, explicit, and legitimate purposes** and not further processed in a way incompatible with those purposes (GDPR Art. 5(1)(b)).\n\n#### Purpose Creep and Reuse\n\n**Purpose creep** — gradual expansion of data use beyond its original intended purpose. Common in AI/ML where data is reused across:\n\n- Product features\n- Research and development\n- Monetization strategies\n- Unrelated predictive tasks\n\n> **Example:** Credit card company collects transaction data for fraud detection, later uses it to infer customer income for marketing — undisclosed secondary use.\n\n#### AI-specific Purpose Challenges\n\n| Challenge | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **Training vs. inference** | Training data reused indefinitely across model updates |\n| **Third-party models** | Licensed models trained on unknown/undisclosed datasets |\n| **Transparency gaps** | Users rarely informed of downstream purposes served by a model |\n\n#### Supporting Purpose Limitation\n\n- Align dataset documentation with intended use\n- **Separate datasets** for distinct model tasks\n- Conduct **compatibility assessments** for secondary uses\n- Update privacy notices with each **material change**\n\n### Lawful Basis for AI Training and Deployment\n\nEvery processing operation needs one of the six GDPR Art. 6 bases, and there is no hierarchy among them. For AI, the live question is usually **legitimate interest** (Art. 6(1)(f)), because consent is hard to obtain at scale.\n\n- **EDPB Opinion 28/2024 (17 Dec 2024)** applies the **three-step test** to AI models: (1) a lawful, clearly articulated, real and present interest (e.g. building a conversational agent, improving threat detection); (2) necessity, meaning no less intrusive way and a proportionate amount of data; (3) a **balancing test** in which data subjects' **reasonable expectations** carry weight. Relevant factors include whether the data was public, the relationship with the controller, the context and source of collection, possible further uses of the model, and whether people know their data is online. **Mitigating measures** beyond what the GDPR already requires can rescue a close balance.\n- **Draft EDPB Guidelines 03/2026 on web scraping** (consultation until 30 Oct 2026): consent \"would most probably not be\" workable for large-scale scraping, and legitimate interest is the basis usually relied on. Sensitive data needs an Art. 9(2) exception **as well as** an Art. 6 basis.\n- **Not yet law:** the Commission's Digital Omnibus would add an AI-specific legitimate-interest article (proposed Art. 88c). As of September 2026 it is still a proposal.\n\n---", "t_minimization": "## At a Glance\n\nTwo design principles that fight AI's instincts: collect only what the purpose needs (data minimization, GDPR Article 5(1)(c)), and build protection in from the start rather than bolting it on (privacy by design, Article 25). Both push toward fewer features, narrower collection, privacy-preserving training methods, and demonstrable limits on identifiability.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\n### Data minimization\n\nArticle 5(1)(c) requires personal data that is adequate, relevant and limited to what is necessary for the stated purpose. AI runs against it: model development wants large datasets, exploration is open-ended, and teams overcollect against a hypothetical future use. The practical answer is to minimize at each stage:\n\n| Stage | Minimization move |\n|---|---|\n| Collection | Limit scope; avoid sensitive data unless strictly necessary; do not collect on speculation |\n| Preprocessing | Drop non-contributing features; mask or redact sensitive fields |\n| Training | Use federated learning or differential privacy so raw personal data is not centralised |\n| Inference | Limit model input to the minimal feature set the task needs |\n\nThe framing to remember is \"smart data, not big data\": collection discipline (reduce scope and volume) plus technical safeguards (reduce identifiability). If 15 features perform like 50, use 15.\n\n### Privacy by design\n\nPrivacy by design, formulated by Ann Cavoukian and given legal force by Article 25, has seven principles: proactive not reactive; privacy as the default; embedded into design; full functionality (positive-sum, not privacy versus utility); end-to-end security; visibility and transparency; and respect for the user. Applied to AI it maps onto the lifecycle: DPIAs and necessity tests at problem framing; privacy-preserving training, bias detection and interpretability during development; retention limits, contestability and drift monitoring in operation. In MLOps it means privacy checks in CI/CD, privacy linting, model cards that record privacy characteristics, and drift detection.\n\n### Techniques\n\nData masking and tokenisation, pseudonymization, on-device processing, homomorphic encryption (compute on encrypted data), federated learning (data stays local, only model updates are shared), differential privacy (no single record can be reconstructed from outputs), and synthetic data. Synthetic data is not privacy-preserving by default: overfitted generators can leak, and small populations stay vulnerable.\n\n### Anonymisation is a high bar\n\nRemoving names is **pseudonymisation**, not anonymisation. Under EDPB Opinion 28/2024 a model trained on personal data is anonymous only if it is insignificantly likely that anyone could extract a training subject's data, directly or through queries, assessed case by case against the controller's documentation. The draft Guidelines 02/2026 propose three tests: no record isolation, no linkage, no inference, and note that anonymity can differ from one holder to another.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nThe minimization questions are usually the easiest points in II.A if you resist the bait: 'more data always improves the model' is wrong, and 'collect now, decide the purpose later' is wrong. Know the stage-by-stage moves and the technique-to-problem mapping (federated learning and differential privacy for training without centralising raw data; homomorphic encryption for computing on encrypted data). For privacy by design, remember it is a legal requirement under Article 25, not just good practice, and that pseudonymisation is not anonymisation.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Data minimization (Article 5(1)(c)): adequate, relevant and limited to what is necessary - the '50 features but 15 perform equally, use 15' scenario\n- Minimize stage by stage: scope at collection, features at preprocessing, raw data at training, inputs at inference\n- Privacy by design (Article 25): proactive, default-on, embedded, full-functionality, end-to-end secure, visible and transparent, user-centric\n- Privacy-preserving techniques map to problems: federated learning and differential privacy for training, homomorphic encryption for computing on encrypted data, masking and tokenisation for stored data\n- Synthetic data is not automatically privacy-preserving: overfitting can leak, and small populations remain identifiable\n- Removing names is pseudonymisation, not anonymisation; EDPB Opinion 28/2024 sets an extraction test before a model counts as anonymous\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679</a> · Article 5(1)(c) is data minimization; Article 25 is privacy by design and by default\n- <a href=\"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/documents/opinion-of-the-board-art-64/opinion-282024-on-certain-data-protection-aspects-related-to_en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EDPB Opinion 28/2024 on AI models and personal data</a> · the extraction test for anonymous models and the minimization measures it lists\n- <a href=\"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/news/edpb-sheds-light-on-anonymisation-and-web-scraping-for-generative-ai-and-adopts-final-version_en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EDPB draft Guidelines 02/2026 on anonymisation</a> · draft three tests: no record isolation, no linkage, no inference\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Privacy and Data Protection Law\n\n## 2. Data Minimization and Privacy by Design in AI\n\n> **Foundational GDPR Articles:** 5(1)(c) and 25\n\n### Data Minimization\n\nCollect only data that is **\"adequate, relevant, and limited to what is necessary\"** for stated purposes (GDPR Art. 5(1)(c)). Linked to necessity, proportionality, and accountability.\n\nAI complexities:\n- Perceived need for **large training datasets**\n- **Exploratory/experimental** nature of model development\n- **Overcollection** in anticipation of future use\n\n#### Data Minimization Across the AI Lifecycle\n\n| Stage | Minimization Strategy |\n|-------|----------------------|\n| **Data collection** | Limit scope; avoid sensitive data unless strictly necessary; don't collect on speculation |\n| **Preprocessing / feature selection** | Drop non-contributing features; mask or redact sensitive fields |\n| **Model training** | Use **federated learning**, **differential privacy** to train without raw personal data |\n| **Inference / deployment** | Limit model input to minimal feature set required |\n\n> **Example:** Predictive policing system redesigned to use only anonymized crime rates and timestamps (neighborhood level), removing direct identifiers and reducing profiling risks.\n\n### Synthetic Data and Minimization\n\n**Synthetic data** — artificially generated data reflecting statistical properties of real datasets without exposing actual PII.\n\n| Benefit | Risk |\n|---------|------|\n| Reduces reliance on sensitive/PII data | Not privacy-preserving by default |\n| Supports model training and testing | Overfitted models may risk re-identification |\n| Improves privacy and compliance posture | Small populations still vulnerable |\n\n#### Balancing Utility and Minimization\n\n- **\"Smart data, not big data\"** — Privacy-preserving ML (PPML) challenges the \"more is better\" mindset\n- Two approaches: **Collection discipline** (reduce scope/volume) and **Technical safeguards** (reduce identifiability)\n\n| Technique | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| Data masking and tokenization | Obscure sensitive values |\n| Pseudonymization | Replace identifiers with pseudonyms |\n| On-device processing | Keep data local |\n| **Homomorphic encryption** | Perform computations on encrypted data without decrypting |\n\n### Anonymisation and AI Models: the EDPB Position (2024–2026)\n\n- **Models are not automatically anonymous.** Under EDPB Opinion 28/2024, a model trained on personal data is anonymous only if it is insignificantly likely that anyone could extract training subjects' data from it, directly or through queries. Supervisory authorities assess this case by case against the controller's documentation.\n- **Draft Guidelines 02/2026 on Anonymisation** (consultation until 30 Oct 2026) propose three tests: no record isolation, no linkage, no inference. Anonymity can differ from one holder to another, following the CJEU's *EDPS v SRB* (C-413/23 P, 2025).\n- **Minimisation payoff:** removing names is pseudonymisation, not anonymisation. The Opinion's evidence list covers four areas: limiting personal data at collection, reducing identifiability in training (regularisation against overfitting, privacy-preserving techniques such as differential privacy), output-side measures, and testing against attacks such as membership inference, extraction and regurgitation.\n\n### Privacy by Design (PbD)\n\n**Privacy by design** — architectural approach embedding privacy protections from the outset. Originally formulated by Dr. Ann Cavoukian (1990s); gained legal force in GDPR Art. 25.\n\n#### Seven Foundational Principles\n\n1. **Proactive, not reactive** — preventive, not remedial\n2. **Privacy as the default setting**\n3. **Privacy embedded into design**\n4. **Full functionality** — positive-sum, not zero-sum\n5. **End-to-end security** — full lifecycle protection\n6. **Visibility and transparency**\n7. **Respect for user privacy** — keep it user-centric\n\n#### Implementing PbD in AI Development\n\n| Stage | Activities |\n|-------|------------|\n| **Problem framing** | Conduct DPIA; assess necessity/proportionality; determine if less intrusive methods work |\n| **Data & model development** | Privacy-preserving training methods; bias detection; model interpretability tools; validate fairness and explainability |\n| **Deployment & maintenance** | Limit retention of raw inputs; enable user contestability and feedback; continuously monitor drift and compliance degradation |\n\n> **Example:** NLP legal document review — on-premise processing, deidentified data, minimized logging, encrypted audit trails.\n\n#### Embedding PbD in MLOps Pipelines\n\n| Integration Point | Action |\n|-------------------|--------|\n| **CI/CD integration** | Embed privacy scans in automated builds |\n| **Privacy linting** | Check for dangerous data uses or code paths |\n| **Model cards** | Document privacy characteristics and risk assessments |\n| **Drift detection** | Monitor divergence from training data creating new risks |\n\n#### Aligning PbD with Organizational Roles\n\n| Role | Responsibility |\n|------|----------------|\n| **Privacy teams** | Work with data scientists and MLOps engineers |\n| **Product managers** | Champion privacy-respecting features |\n| **Legal & compliance** | Oversee risk assessments and documentation |\n| **Security teams** | Implement access controls, encryption, monitoring |\n| **Executives** | Foster culture of responsible AI development |\n\n#### Practical Tools and Frameworks\n\n| Framework | Focus |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **ISO/IEC 23894** | AI risk management |\n| **NIST AI RMF** | Map, measure, manage, govern |\n| **ENISA Guidelines** | AI-specific attack surfaces and mitigations |\n| **AI-specific DPIAs** | Templates from ICO (UK), CNIL (France), others |\n\n---", "t_controller": "## At a Glance\n\nThe controller determines the purposes and means of processing (Article 4(7)) and carries the accountability duty to demonstrate compliance (Article 5(2)). For AI that means DPIAs for high-risk processing, Article 28 contracts with processors, transfer safeguards for cross-border flows, answers to data subject rights, safeguards for automated decisions, breach notification and records.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\n### Who is the controller\n\nThe controller is the entity that determines the purposes and means of processing; a processor acts on the controller's behalf. In AI supply chains the line blurs: a cloud or model vendor that keeps training on customer data, or a black-box AI-as-a-Service platform with little configuration, can become a **joint controller**. The accountability principle (Article 5(2)) means controllers must not only comply but be able to **demonstrate** it.\n\n### DPIAs (Article 35)\n\nA Data Protection Impact Assessment is required where processing is likely to result in high risk. AI triggers it through automated decision-making with legal or similarly significant effects, large-scale special-category data, systematic profiling, monitoring of publicly accessible areas, and vulnerable populations. A DPIA describes the processing and its purpose, the training and operational data at field level, the applicable laws, necessity and proportionality, the risks from the data subject's perspective, the mitigations, and any consultation. The common AI pitfalls are unclear system boundaries (model versus business logic), framing technical risk instead of human impact, and never revisiting the DPIA once a model keeps learning.\n\n### Processors (Article 28)\n\nEvery processor relationship needs a binding data processing agreement: subject matter, duration, nature and purpose; data types and data subjects; security and confidentiality; sub-processing rules; assistance with rights requests and DPIAs; return or deletion at the end; and audit rights. Watch for **processor drift**, where a vendor's continuous training or new features widen processing beyond instructions, and for clauses that let a vendor train on the controller's data.\n\n### Cross-border transfers\n\nTransfers outside the EEA need an adequacy decision, standard contractual clauses, binding corporate rules or a derogation, and remote **access** from outside the EEA counts even if nothing is stored there. After Schrems II, SCCs and BCRs require a transfer impact assessment weighing the destination's surveillance law and any supplementary measures.\n\n### Data subject rights\n\n| Right | Article | AI challenge |\n|---|---|---|\n| Access | 15 | explaining black-box outputs |\n| Rectification | 16 | correcting data baked into weights |\n| Erasure | 17 | deletion from trained models |\n| Restriction | 18 | halting use in continuous learning |\n| Object | 21 | profiling is intrinsic to many systems |\n| Automated decisions | 22 | opt-out and genuine human review |\n\n**Article 22** prohibits decisions based **solely** on automated processing with legal or similarly significant effects unless a contract, law or explicit consent permits it, and even then the controller owes safeguards including meaningful human intervention, not rubber-stamping. Two CJEU rulings define the doctrine: **SCHUFA** (C-634/21, 2023) holds that a credit bureau's score is itself an Article 22 decision when the lender draws strongly on it, so upstream providers owe the duties; **Dun & Bradstreet Austria** (C-203/22, 2025) holds that Article 15(1)(h) means a concise, intelligible account of the logic actually applied, not an algorithm dump, with claimed trade secrets weighed by the DPA or court.\n\n### Breach notification and records\n\nArticle 33 requires notifying the supervisory authority within 72 hours of awareness and Article 34 requires telling affected people without undue delay where risk is high. AI adds vectors that standard DLP misses: training-data leakage through inversion or membership inference, output regurgitation, model poisoning, and silent degradation. Article 30 records of processing and the Article 5(2) accountability duty supply the paper trail: data flow diagrams, model and system cards, consent and purpose logs, DPIAs and transfer assessments.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nAnchor the articles: 4(7) controller, 5(2) accountability, 28 processors, 30 records, 33 and 34 breach, 35 DPIAs, 44 to 49 transfers. Scenario questions test when a DPIA is mandatory, what an Article 28 contract must contain, and whether a transfer safeguard is adequate. Article 22 items pair with the two rulings: a decisive third-party score is itself an automated decision (SCHUFA), and an access request about it needs an intelligible explanation of the logic applied, not a formula (Dun & Bradstreet Austria).\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Controller determines purposes and means (Article 4(7)); accountability (Article 5(2)) means being able to demonstrate compliance, not just assert it\n- DPIA (Article 35) is required for high-risk processing: automated decisions with significant effects, large-scale special categories, systematic profiling, public-area monitoring, vulnerable people\n- Article 28 processor contracts must cover instructions, security, sub-processing, rights and DPIA assistance, deletion and audit rights\n- Cross-border transfers need adequacy, SCCs, BCRs or a derogation; remote access counts as a transfer, and Schrems II requires a transfer impact assessment\n- Article 22 bans solely automated decisions with legal or significant effects absent a contract, law or explicit consent, and even then requires meaningful human intervention\n- SCHUFA (2023): a decisive credit score is itself an Article 22 decision; Dun & Bradstreet Austria (2025): access means an intelligible account of the logic actually applied\n- Breach notification: supervisory authority within 72 hours (Article 33), affected people without undue delay if high risk (Article 34)\n- AI-specific breach vectors include training-data leakage, membership inference, output regurgitation and model poisoning, which standard DLP misses\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679</a> · Articles 4, 5, 24 to 30, 33 to 35 and 44 to 49 are the controller obligations\n- <a href=\"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/documents/opinion-of-the-board-art-64/opinion-282024-on-certain-data-protection-aspects-related-to_en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EDPB Opinion 28/2024 on AI models and personal data</a> · a deployer must check the model was not built on unlawfully processed data unless it was genuinely anonymised\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/C/2024/913/oj/eng\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CJEU SCHUFA, C-634/21 (7 December 2023)</a> · an upstream score the lender draws on strongly is itself an Article 22 decision\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=celex:62022CJ0203\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CJEU Dun & Bradstreet Austria, C-203/22 (27 February 2025)</a> · access to automated-decision logic means an intelligible explanation, with trade secrets weighed by the DPA or court\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Privacy and Data Protection Law\n\n## 4. Data Controller Obligations in the AI Context\n\n> **GDPR Articles:** 5, 24–30, 44–49\n\nA **data controller** (GDPR Art. 4(7)) is the entity that \"determines the purposes and means of processing.\" Controllers bear primary responsibility for compliance with data protection laws.\n\n### Privacy Impact Assessments and Risk Management\n\n#### The Role of DPIAs in AI Development\n\nA **Data Protection Impact Assessment (DPIA)** is required under GDPR Art. 35 when processing \"is likely to result in a high risk to the rights and freedoms of natural persons.\" AI almost always triggers this threshold when involving:\n\n- Automated decision-making with legal or similarly significant effects\n- Large-scale processing of **sensitive categories of data**\n- Monitoring of **publicly accessible areas**\n- Systematic **profiling**\n\n#### DPIA Lifecycle in AI Projects\n\nA DPIA should include:\n1. Description of processing activity and its purpose\n2. Description of **training data** (field level)\n3. Description of **operational data** (field level)\n4. Applicable cybersecurity, privacy, and AI-related laws\n5. Evaluation of **necessity and proportionality**\n6. Risk analysis from the **data subject's perspective**\n7. Measures to address or mitigate risks\n8. Consultation with stakeholders (DPOs, regulators if required)\n\n#### When Must a DPIA Be Conducted? (GDPR Triggers for AI)\n\n| Trigger | Example |\n|---------|---------|\n| Automated decision-making with legal/similar effect | Job applications, credit scoring |\n| Profiling or predictive analytics at scale | Behavioral advertising |\n| Sensitive data (biometrics, health, religion) | Facial recognition, health AI |\n| Vulnerable populations | Children, elderly |\n| Systematic monitoring of behavior | Wearables, apps |\n\n#### Common Pitfalls in AI DPIAs\n\n- Lack of clarity on **system boundaries** (model vs. business logic)\n- **Inadequate risk framing** (technical risks vs. human impacts)\n- **Failure to revisit** DPIAs post-deployment (especially for continuously learning models)\n\n> Best practice: Use AI-specific DPIA templates (CNIL, ICO), involve interdisciplinary teams, document model logic and residual risks.\n\n#### Aligning with Emerging Frameworks\n\n| Framework | Relevance |\n|-----------|-----------|\n| **NIST AI RMF** | Map risks early and iteratively |\n| **OECD AI Principles** | Stakeholder consultation, transparency |\n| **EU AI Act** | Mandatory conformity assessments for high-risk AI |\n\n### Using Third-party Processors in AI Projects\n\n#### Controller vs. Processor (GDPR Art. 4(8))\n\nA **processor** processes personal data *on behalf of* the controller. In AI, third-party processors are engaged for:\n\n- Cloud storage/compute environments\n- Model training or MLOps services\n- Pretrained or foundation models\n- Data classification, labeling, annotation\n- AI-as-a-Service platforms\n\n> ⚠️ AI supply chain opacity can blur controller-processor boundaries. A \"black box\" AI vendor with limited configuration may function as a **joint controller**.\n\n#### Contractual Requirements (GDPR Art. 28)\n\nEvery processor relationship requires a binding **Data Processing Agreement (DPA)** specifying:\n\n- Subject matter, duration, nature, and purpose\n- Types of personal data and data subjects\n- Processor obligations (security, confidentiality, subprocessing)\n- Assistance with rights requests and DPIAs\n- Return or deletion of data after processing ends\n- **Audit and inspection rights**\n\n> **Example:** Financial institution DPA with ML vendor includes clause allowing vendor to use data to improve its own models — undermines controller's instructions, potentially violates purpose limitation.\n\n### Due Diligence Checklist for AI Vendors\n\n- Does the vendor use **subcontractors** for model development?\n- Can the vendor articulate **training data provenance and composition**?\n- Is the model **explainable or auditable**?\n- What **logging and security mechanisms** are in place?\n- Can the vendor **support data subject requests**?\n- Does the vendor agree to **process only on instruction** (no data reuse)?\n- Does the vendor exercise **due care** in cybersecurity and privacy?\n- Will the vendor agree to an **audit** in the event of a breach?\n\n#### Processor Drift and Compliance Gaps\n\n**Processor drift** — delegated tasks become uncontrolled and unmanaged processing. Dynamic AI systems risk:\n\n- Continuous training with customer data\n- New feature rollouts altering data processing\n- Model updates introducing profiling\n\nControllers should require: notification of subprocessor changes, change procedures, security/functionality audit logs, access to performance and fairness metrics.\n\n### Cross-border Data Transfers\n\n#### Global Data Flows in AI Systems\n\nUnder GDPR, personal data may only be transferred outside the EEA with appropriate safeguards:\n\n| Safeguard | Example |\n|-----------|---------|\n| **Adequacy decisions** | Japan, South Korea, UK |\n| **Standard Contractual Clauses (SCCs)** | Pre-approved contractual terms |\n| **Binding Corporate Rules (BCRs)** | Intra-group rules for multinationals |\n| **Derogations** | Explicit consent, legal claims (specific situations) |\n\n> ⚠️ **Access** from outside the EEA (even if no data is stored) counts as a transfer.\n\n#### AI-specific Data Transfer Challenges\n\n| Challenge | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **Ongoing access vs. one-time transfers** | Models may pull training data periodically/continuously from global sources |\n| **Inference risks** | Inference outputs can reveal sensitive information even if raw data stays local |\n| **Data localization laws** | China, India enforcing AI-relevant localization laws conflicting with GDPR |\n\n#### AI Inference as a Transfer?\n\n- **Remote access** to data = transfer\n- **Real-time model inference** from foreign servers = potential transfer\n- **Use of third-party AI APIs** = transfer if personal data is involved\n\n#### Transfer Impact Assessments (TIAs)\n\nSince **Schrems II** invalidated EU–US Privacy Shield, TIAs are required when relying on SCCs or BCRs. A TIA must consider:\n\n- Legal environment of the destination country\n- Applicable privacy laws and government surveillance/access\n- Nature of data and processing\n- **Supplementary measures** (encryption, pseudonymization)\n\nFor AI: remote model training with EEA data, vendor telemetry access, pretrained models built on non-EEA data, cross-border API calls.\n\n### Data Subject Rights and AI\n\n| Right | GDPR Article | AI Challenge |\n|-------|-------------|--------------|\n| **Right of access** | Art. 15 | Hard to explain black box model outputs |\n| **Right to rectification** | Art. 16 | How to correct data embedded in model weights? |\n| **Right to erasure** | Art. 17 | Deletion from trained models extremely difficult |\n| **Right to restriction** | Art. 18 | Halting use in active/continuous learning systems |\n| **Right to data portability** | Art. 20 | AI outputs are model-dependent, not easily portable |\n| **Right to object** | Art. 21 | Profiling is intrinsic to many AI systems |\n| **Right re: automated decisions** | Art. 22 | Requires robust opt-out mechanisms |\n\n> **Example:** Ride-sharing app deactivates drivers based on AI fraud prediction — cites \"proprietary algorithms\" without meaningful explanation. Violates GDPR Arts. 15 and 22.\n\n### GDPR Article 22: Human-in-the-loop or Window Dressing?\n\nArt. 22 prohibits decisions **\"based solely on automated processing\"** that significantly affect individuals, unless:\n- Necessary for a contract\n- Authorized by law\n- Based on explicit consent\n\n> Even with human review, regulators require **genuine oversight** — not rubber-stamping. Humans must be able to override AI decisions.\n\n#### Automated Decision-Making in the Courts (2023–2025)\n\nBOK v2.1 now lists automated decision-making among controller obligations. Two CJEU rulings define it for AI:\n\n| Ruling | Holding | Governance consequence |\n|---|---|---|\n| **SCHUFA** (C-634/21, 7 Dec 2023) | A credit bureau's automated **score** is itself an Art. 22 \"decision\" when the lender that receives it **draws strongly** on it to grant, run or end a contract | Upstream score and model providers can owe Art. 22 duties (an exception basis, safeguards, explanations), not just the business that acts on the output |\n| **Dun & Bradstreet Austria** (C-203/22, 27 Feb 2025) | Art. 15(1)(h) \"meaningful information about the logic involved\" means a concise, intelligible account of the **procedure and principles actually applied** to that person's data. A formula or algorithm dump is not enough; showing how different inputs would have changed the result may be. Claimed **trade secrets** go to the DPA or court to balance, and blanket national exclusions are not allowed | Build explanation capability for real decisions; \"proprietary\" is not a complete refusal |\n\n#### Practical Solutions for Data Subject Rights\n\n- **Design for rights** — build explainability and logging from the outset\n- **Model documentation** — maintain metadata about inputs, versions, logic, impacts\n- **Layered explanations** — combine high-level summaries with technical details\n- **Anonymization/pseudonymization** — train with anonymized data whenever possible\n- **Deletion strategies** — retraining pipelines that exclude deleted records\n\n### Incident Management and Breach Notification\n\n#### AI-Specific Breach Types\n\n| Breach Type | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| **Training data leakage** | Inversion attacks, membership inference, gradient extraction |\n| **Output leakage** | LLMs reproducing training data verbatim; prompt injection revealing sensitive content |\n| **Bias amplification** | Harmful/unfair decisions at scale |\n| **Model drift or poisoning** | Adversarial inputs undermining decision quality |\n\n#### GDPR Breach Notification (Art. 33)\n\n- Notify supervisory authority within **72 hours** of becoming aware\n- Inform data subjects **without undue delay** if high risk to rights/freedoms\n- Include: nature/scope, categories/number affected, likely consequences, mitigation measures\n- Failure: fines up to **€10 million or 2% of global turnover**\n\n#### AI Incident Playbook Essentials\n\n- AI model inventory, data provenance, and data lineage\n- Breach impact assessment criteria for **inference outputs**\n- Contact points for vendors and subprocessors\n- Legal escalation routes for DPIAs and Art. 33 reviews\n- Training for response teams on **AI-specific vectors**\n\n#### AI Incident Detection Challenges\n\n- **Silent failures** — model outputs degrade without obvious signs\n- **Lack of observability** — poor logging or opaque pipelines\n- **Unstructured data** — breaches affecting documents, media, embeddings not monitored by standard DLP\n- **Third-party dependency** — upstream provider incidents may leak data without internal alerts\n\n### Recordkeeping and Accountability\n\nGDPR's accountability principle (Art. 5(2)): controllers must not only comply but **demonstrate** compliance.\n\n#### What to Document in AI Projects\n\n| Artifact | Content |\n|----------|---------|\n| **Purpose definition** | Why AI was developed, what decisions it supports |\n| **Training data provenance** | Source, cleaning, legal basis |\n| **Model architecture and logic** | Type of model and how it functions |\n| **Evaluation results** | Accuracy, fairness, bias, robustness, explainability |\n| **Risk assessments** | DPIAs, TIAs, residual risk justifications |\n| **Version history and change logs** | Model updates, policy shifts |\n| **Reviews and approvals** | Gate reviews, who approved progression |\n\n#### AI Compliance Binder Contents\n\n- Model cards and system cards\n- Records of processing activities (GDPR Art. 30)\n- Records of events, incidents, and breaches\n- Consent logs, opt-outs, objection handling\n- DPIAs, TIAs, consultation records\n- Documentation of human review for Art. 22 decisions\n\n#### Tools and Frameworks for Governance\n\n- **Governance platforms:** OneTrust, Navex, Collibra, BigID\n- **MLOps integrations:** Privacy logging in CI/CD pipelines\n- **Open documentation tools:** Model Cards++, datasheets for datasets\n- **AI risk registers:** Track known risks, mitigations, review cycles\n\n### EU Developments 2024–2026 at a Glance\n\n| Development | Status (Sept 2026) | What it means for a controller |\n|---|---|---|\n| **EDPB Opinion 28/2024** (17 Dec 2024) | Adopted | Test anonymity claims for models; run and document the three-step legitimate-interest test; a deployer must check the model was not built on unlawfully processed data (unless it was genuinely anonymised) |\n| **CJEU SCHUFA** (C-634/21, 2023) | Final | Scores that drive decisions are Art. 22 decisions; assign Art. 22 duties across the vendor chain |\n| **CJEU Dun & Bradstreet Austria** (C-203/22, 2025) | Final | Access requests about automated decisions need an intelligible explanation of the logic actually applied; trade secrets are weighed by the DPA or court |\n| **EDPB Guidelines 02/2026** (anonymisation) and **03/2026** (web scraping for generative AI) | **Drafts**, consultation until 30 Oct 2026 | Signal how DPAs will judge anonymisation and scraping; not final |\n| **Digital Omnibus GDPR amendments** (COM(2025) 837) | **Proposals** under negotiation | Narrower personal-data definition and AI legitimate-interest article (Art. 88c) are *not* law; apply the current GDPR |\n| **EU AI Act Art. 4a** (via the 2026 AI Omnibus) | In force | An AI Act basis for special-category data used strictly for bias detection and correction, on top of the GDPR |\n\nDetails and article citations: [[GDPR — AI-Relevant Provisions]].\n\n---", "t_sensitive": "## At a Glance\n\nGDPR Article 9 forbids processing special-category data (biometrics for identification, health, genetic, racial or ethnic origin, religion, political opinions, union membership, sex life or orientation) unless a narrow exception applies. AI makes this harder because it can infer these traits from inputs that never contained them, and the inference triggers the same protection.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\n### The default prohibition\n\nArticle 9 starts from a ban, not a permission: special-category data may be processed only under a narrow exception, with **explicit consent** the usual one (freely given, specific, informed, unambiguous, affirmative, documented). Others include employment and social protection law, vital interests, substantial public interest, public health, archiving and research with safeguards, and legal claims. Consent and contract are not interchangeable: sensitive data under Article 9(2)(a) must be explicit and revocable, whereas contract necessity is a demanding test that nice-to-have features fail.\n\n### What counts as sensitive\n\nBiometrics used for identification, health and genetic data, racial or ethnic origin, religious or philosophical beliefs, political opinions, trade union membership, and sex life or sexual orientation. Jurisdictions differ at the edges: the CPRA adds precise geolocation; Brazil's LGPD, Canada's PIPEDA and India's DPDP Act track similar lists. Financial data, government identifiers and children's data carry extra protection by context.\n\n### AI's risk profile\n\nThree ways AI meets sensitive data: **deliberate** (a health tool using medical records), **incidental** (a CV sorter harvesting political affiliation from past activism), and **inferential**, where a model predicts ethnicity, health or orientation without ever being told it. The risk set is lack of a valid basis, discrimination and bias, opacity, surveillance and chilling effects.\n\n### Biometrics and the identification line\n\nBiometric data is especially sensitive because it cannot be changed if compromised. The GDPR's heightened protection attaches only where biometric data is used to **uniquely identify** a person, so categorisation alone (say, age estimation without identity linkage) may fall outside Article 9, though it still carries ethical and discrimination risk. Facial recognition for retail surveillance is the classic trigger.\n\n### Inference is the trap\n\nInferential privacy violations arise when models deduce religious affiliation from language, sexual orientation from social-graph connections, health conditions from wearable data, or political leaning from browsing history. The important rule: these inferences can trigger Article 9 even though the attribute was never explicitly collected. Controllers are responsible for downstream inferences, and sensitive-by-proxy features (postal code, credit history, education) can smuggle protected traits back in.\n\n### The AI Act overlay\n\nAs amended by the 2026 Digital Omnibus on AI, a new AI Act Article 4a adds an AI Act basis for special-category data used strictly to detect and correct bias, on top of the GDPR, with strict-necessity safeguards (no effective alternative data, pseudonymisation, access controls, no onward transfer, deletion and records).\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nExpect a scenario where a system infers a protected trait and the question is whether Article 9 applies. The answer is usually yes: inference counts. Know the exceptions, that explicit consent is the high bar for sensitive data, and the biometrics distinction between identification (Article 9) and categorisation (may fall outside it, but still risky). Article 9 data also needs an Article 6 basis as well as the Article 9(2) exception, a point the EDPB's web-scraping draft repeats.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Article 9 starts from prohibition: special-category data needs a narrow exception, with explicit consent the usual one\n- Sensitive means biometrics for identification, health, genetic, race and ethnicity, religion, political opinions, union membership, sex life and orientation\n- AI meets sensitive data deliberately, incidentally and inferentially, and the inference of a protected trait triggers Article 9 even without collection\n- Biometric protection attaches to identification, not mere categorisation, but categorisation still carries bias and ethical risk\n- Sensitive data needs an Article 9(2) exception as well as an Article 6 basis for the processing\n- New AI Act Article 4a (via the 2026 Omnibus) adds an AI Act basis for special-category data used strictly to detect and correct bias, with strict-necessity safeguards\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679</a> · Article 9 is the special-category prohibition and its exceptions\n- <a href=\"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/documents/opinion-of-the-board-art-64/opinion-282024-on-certain-data-protection-aspects-related-to_en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EDPB Opinion 28/2024 on AI models and personal data</a> · sensitive data needs an Article 9(2) exception as well as an Article 6 basis\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2026/1744/oj/eng\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Digital Omnibus on AI, Regulation (EU) 2026/1744</a> · new Article 4a: special-category data used strictly for bias detection and correction\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Privacy and Data Protection Law\n\n## 5. Sensitive or Special Categories of Data\n\n> **GDPR Article 9** — default prohibition on processing special category data\n\n### Legal and Regulatory Foundations\n\n#### GDPR Article 9: Prohibition Baseline\n\n**Default position:** Processing of special category data is **forbidden** unless a narrow exception applies:\n\n| Exception | Example |\n|-----------|---------|\n| **Explicit consent** | Freely given, specific, informed, documented |\n| Employment and social protection law | Statutory obligations |\n| **Vital interests** | Medical emergencies |\n| **Public interest in public health** | Public health monitoring |\n| Archiving, research, or statistical purposes | With safeguards |\n| Establishment/defense of legal claims | Litigation |\n\n#### Key International Equivalents\n\n| Jurisdiction | Law | Sensitive Data Categories |\n|-------------|-----|--------------------------|\n| **California** | CPRA | Biometric, health, race, religion, union, precise geolocation |\n| **Brazil** | LGPD | Biometric, health, racial/ethnic, religious, political, union |\n| **Canada** | PIPEDA (OPC) | Health, biometrics, ethnicity, religious beliefs |\n| **India** | DPDP Act (2023) | Health, biometric, financial, genetic, sex life, political |\n\n### What Qualifies as \"Sensitive\" Data?\n\n- **Biometrics** (for identification purposes)\n- **Health and genetic data**\n- **Racial or ethnic origin**\n- **Religious or philosophical beliefs**\n- **Political opinions and affiliations**\n- **Trade union membership**\n- **Sex life or sexual orientation**\n- **Precise geolocation** (in some jurisdictions)\n- Financial, government ID, or children's data (depending on context)\n\n#### AI's Unique Risk Profile for Sensitive Data\n\n| Processing Type | Description |\n|-----------------|-------------|\n| **Deliberate** | Health AI uses electronic medical records |\n| **Incidental** | CV-sorting tool collects political affiliations from past activism |\n| **Inferential** | Facial analysis model predicts ethnicity or emotion without being told |\n\nRisks include: lack of valid legal basis, discrimination/bias, lack of transparency, surveillance and **chilling effects**.\n\n### Biometrics\n\n**Biometric data** — personal data from technical processing of physical, physiological, or behavioral characteristics allowing unique identification.\n\n| Common Types | Common Uses |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| Facial features, fingerprints, iris/retina, voiceprints, gait, typing rhythm | Authentication, identification, surveillance/analytics |\n\n> Biometric data is especially sensitive because it **cannot easily be changed if compromised** (unlike a credit card number).\n\n> **Key distinction:** GDPR heightened protection applies only to biometric data **used to uniquely identify a person**. Categorization alone (e.g., age estimation without identity linkage) may not trigger Art. 9, but still carries ethical/legal risk.\n\n### Healthcare and Genetic Data\n\nKey concerns:\n- Medical and **employment discrimination**\n- **Inference of conditions** without consent\n- **Reidentification** from anonymized datasets\n- Use of patient data for **unrelated research or training**\n\n> Genetic data adds complexity due to **familial and hereditary implications** — may affect individuals who never gave consent.\n\n### Religious and Philosophical Beliefs\n\nAI may encounter belief data via: surveys, social media, textual analysis, behavioral inference (holiday travel, food purchases).\n\n> Risks: profiling/stereotyping, bias/discrimination, chilling effects on free expression, targeted advertising.\n\n### Political Opinions and Affiliations\n\nAI systems may infer political data from: party membership, voting history, stated views, engagement with political content.\n\nMisuse risks: opinion manipulation (microtargeting), dissent suppression, electoral influence, profiling-based discrimination.\n\n> Automated decisions based on political views (ad eligibility, financial services, employment screening) are likely **prohibited** under GDPR Art. 22 unless explicit consent + Art. 9 safeguards are met.\n\n### Race, Ethnicity, and Union Membership\n\nAI processes these attributes directly (demographic forms) or inferentially (names, language, location). Problematic in résumé screening, loan applications, housing, policing.\n\n> **Example:** Automated résumé filter deprioritizes HBCU candidates due to historic hiring patterns — disparate impact = indirect discrimination.\n\n### Hidden Bias in AI Training Sets\n\nMany datasets contain biased historical decisions even if sensitive attributes were excluded. Mitigation techniques:\n- **Fairness-aware learning**\n- **Counterfactual evaluation**\n- **Sensitive attribute testing**\n\n### Legal Basis and Consent for Sensitive Data\n\n#### Explicit Consent: A High Bar\n\nExplicit consent must be: freely given, specific, informed, unambiguous, affirmative (no pre-checked boxes), clearly documented.\n\nAI challenges:\n- Indirect or inferential data collection\n- Changes in model purpose over time\n- Complexity of explanations required for meaningful consent\n\n#### Consent vs. Contract\n\n| | Consent (Art. 9(2)(a)) | Contract (Art. 6(1)(b)) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Must be explicit and revocable | ✅ | ❌ |\n| Sensitive data strictly necessary | — | Required |\n| \"Nice-to-have\" features qualify | — | ❌ |\n\n#### When Consent Is Not Enough\n\nProcessing sensitive data may still be illegal/unethical if:\n- Power-imbalanced relationship (employer-employee)\n- Consent not freely given (gating core services behind consent)\n- AI system creates significant effects triggering Art. 22 protections\n\n### Technical and Organizational Safeguards\n\n#### Core Technical Measures\n\n- **Encryption** at rest and in transit (especially biometric/health data)\n- **Access controls** and role-based permissions\n- **Anonymization/pseudonymization** where feasible\n- **Data minimization** and field-level masking\n- **Secure model training** environments with audit logging\n- **Model explainability tools** to detect discrimination\n\n#### Federated Learning for Health Data\n\n**Federated learning** — decentralized model training; data remains on users' devices, only anonymized model updates shared.\n\n| Benefit | Caveat |\n|---------|--------|\n| Minimizes data centralization risks | Requires secure aggregation |\n| Helps meet data localization requirements | Requires validation safeguards |\n| Supports privacy in sensitive environments | Requires rollback safeguards |\n\n#### Organizational Safeguards\n\n- DPIAs for **all** sensitive data processing\n- Clear **retention and deletion policies**\n- Training for engineers and data scientists on sensitivity/risk\n- **Review boards or ethics committees** for high-risk AI\n- **Supplier vetting** (third-party biometric vendors meeting GDPR)\n- Formal **incident and breach response** procedures\n- Formal **risk management** and periodic assessments\n\n#### Designing for Data Minimization\n\n- Use **synthetic data** for early testing\n- **Feature reduction** to exclude unnecessary sensitive attributes\n- **Proportionality assessments** — does model purpose justify sensitivity level of inputs?\n- **Periodic dataset audits** to detect and remove unnecessary/sensitive data\n\n### Special Considerations in AI Applications\n\n#### Bias and Fairness in Sensitive Attributes\n\nCommon pitfalls:\n- **Training data bias** (historical exclusion patterns)\n- **Proxy discrimination** (postal codes standing in for race)\n- **Overfitting** to sensitive features skewing predictions\n- Unjustified **exclusion** of sensitive groups\n\n#### Audit Questions for Sensitive Attribute Fairness\n\n- Is any sensitive attribute explicitly used?\n- Can non-sensitive features serve as proxies?\n- How does accuracy vary across protected groups?\n- Is any demographic group underrepresented in training data?\n- Are fairness constraints applied and documented?\n- Does fairness hold over time with new data?\n\n#### Inference of Sensitive Data from Non-sensitive Inputs\n\n**Inferential privacy violations** — models deduce sensitive characteristics from unrelated data:\n\n| Inferred Attribute | From |\n|--------------------|------|\n| Religious affiliation | Language patterns |\n| Sexual orientation | Social graph connections |\n| Health conditions | Wearable sensor data |\n| Political leaning | Browsing history |\n\n> ⚠️ These inferences can trigger GDPR Art. 9 protections even if the attribute was **not explicitly collected**.\n\n#### Sensitive by Proxy: What to Watch For\n\n- High correlation between features and protected categories\n- Models trained on unbalanced/biased open datasets\n- Unlabeled sensitive attributes inferred during testing/deployment\n- Vendors offering \"demographic enrichment\" services\n- Post-deployment drift changing model fairness across groups\n\n> Controllers are responsible for **downstream inferences** — even when not explicitly designed.\n\n#### Purpose Creep and Secondary Use\n\nSensitive data collected for one purpose **must not** be reused for a new incompatible purpose unless:\n- Data subject **explicitly consents**\n- Reuse falls under a **clear legal exception**\n- New purpose is **demonstrably compatible** with original\n\n### DPIAs for Sensitive Data AI\n\nTriggers of high risk:\n- Systematic monitoring of sensitive data\n- Automated decision-making with legal/similar effects\n- Processing of **vulnerable populations**\n- Use of **biometric or health data at scale**\n\n#### What to Include in a DPIA for Sensitive AI\n\n- Description of AI system (inputs, logic, outputs)\n- Purpose and justification for data sensitivity\n- Risk assessment of potential harm to individuals\n- Evaluation of likelihood and severity\n- Safeguards, technical measures, compliance controls\n- Stakeholder input (DPO, legal, affected communities)\n\n#### Red-flag Questions for Sensitive AI Projects\n\n- Does the system **infer** sensitive attributes?\n- Are individuals **aware** their sensitive data is used?\n- Can users **opt out** without losing core services?\n- Are **downstream partners/vendors** using the same data?\n- Have risks been reviewed by **legal, ethics, and security teams**?\n\n> If any answer is unclear or negative → DPIA (and potentially regulator consultation) required.\n\n### Data Subject Rights and Sensitive Data\n\n#### Enhanced Rights Expectations\n\n| Right | Sensitive Data Consideration |\n|-------|------------------------------|\n| **Right to be informed** | Why is sensitive data processed? How was it obtained? |\n| **Right of access** | What sensitive data is held and how used? |\n| **Right to rectification** | Can users correct inferred or misclassified sensitive attributes? |\n| **Right to erasure** | Can users demand deletion of biometric/health records? |\n| **Right to object** | Can users opt out of profiling based on sensitive data? |\n| **Right re: automated decisions** | Art. 22 protections for legal/similar significant effects |\n\n#### How to Respond to a DSR Involving Sensitive Data\n\n1. Confirm identity with **heightened caution** (especially biometric data)\n2. Provide clear explanation: was data **collected or inferred**?\n3. Identify decisions or consequences linked to the data\n4. Explain **legal basis** for processing and third parties involved\n5. Log all actions taken and timeframes met\n\n#### Opt-out Rights and Limitations\n\nUnder GDPR and CPRA, individuals have the right to opt out of profiling, targeted advertising, or automated decision-making.\n\nA compliant opt-out mechanism must be:\n- **Clearly presented** and easy to exercise\n- **Granular** (decline specific AI uses)\n- **Effective** (confirm suppression of processing)\n\n> **Opt-out is absolute** for direct marketing under GDPR. Otherwise, may be overridden by contract necessity, legal obligation, or compelling legitimate interest.\n\n#### Rights in High-risk Automated Decision-making\n\nUnder GDPR Art. 22, when AI processes sensitive data and significantly affects individuals:\n\n- Right **not to be subject** to solely automated decisions\n- Exceptions require: meaningful human involvement, right to contest, transparent explanation of logic and consequences\n\n---", "ref_gdpr": "## At a Glance\n\nThe General Data Protection Regulation (EU 2016/679): the world's most influential data protection law and, until the AI Act, the EU's main lever over AI. For AI governance its pressure points are Article 22 automated decisions, Article 9 special categories, Article 5 purpose limitation and minimization, Article 25 privacy by design, Article 28 processors, Article 35 DPIAs and Article 33 breach notice.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\n### What it is and why it matters for AI\n\nThe GDPR governs the processing of personal data of people in the EU. It has extraterritorial reach (Article 3): it applies to controllers and processors outside the EU that offer goods or services to EU people or monitor their behaviour. For AI that means a model trained or deployed anywhere can fall inside it if EU people are affected, and it was the EU's principal AI regulator until the AI Act arrived.\n\n### The articles AI governance leans on\n\n| Article | Rule |\n|---|---|\n| 5 | principles: lawfulness, fairness, transparency, purpose limitation, minimization, accuracy, storage limitation, integrity, accountability |\n| 6 | six lawful bases, no hierarchy |\n| 9 | special-category prohibition and exceptions |\n| 13 to 15 | transparency and access, including automated-decision logic |\n| 17 and 21 | erasure and objection, which reach profiling |\n| 22 | solely automated decisions with legal or significant effects |\n| 25 | privacy by design and by default |\n| 28 | processor contracts |\n| 33 and 34 | breach notification (72 hours; individuals if high risk) |\n| 35 | DPIAs for high-risk processing |\n| 44 to 49 | cross-border transfers |\n\n### The AI pressure points\n\nThree doctrines carry most AI questions. **Article 22** bans solely automated decisions with legal or similarly significant effects unless a contract, law or explicit consent allows them, and even then requires safeguards including meaningful human intervention; the CJEU's **SCHUFA** (2023) and **Dun & Bradstreet Austria** (2025) rulings extend the duty up the vendor chain and define what an explanation must contain. **Article 9** protects special-category data, and AI's ability to infer such traits from ordinary inputs brings the inference inside the ban. **Purpose limitation and minimization** collide with training's appetite for repurposed and over-collected data.\n\n### Enforcement and penalties\n\nTwo fine tiers: up to EUR 10m or 2% of global annual turnover, and up to EUR 20m or 4%, whichever is higher, depending on the breach. National supervisory authorities enforce, coordinated by the EDPB, which also issues the guidance that shapes AI practice: Opinion 28/2024 on AI models and the 2026 drafts on anonymisation and web scraping.\n\n### Status discipline\n\nThe EDPB's 2026 anonymisation and web-scraping guidelines are **drafts** (consultation to 30 October 2026), not final. The Commission's Digital Omnibus GDPR amendments, including a narrower personal-data definition and an AI-specific legitimate-interest article (Article 88c), are **proposals**, not law. Apply the GDPR as it stands, and say so when a fact depends on a draft or a proposal.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nThe second-most-tested law after the AI Act. Anchor articles by number and expect the two fine tiers (up to EUR 20m or 4% for the top tier). The AI-specific items cluster on Article 22 (with SCHUFA and Dun & Bradstreet Austria), Article 9 inference, Article 35 DPIAs and Article 28 processors. Status traps are common: an answer that treats the EDPB's 2026 anonymisation or web-scraping guidelines as final law, or the Digital Omnibus GDPR changes as in force, is wrong.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Article 5 principles: lawfulness, fairness and transparency; purpose limitation; data minimization; accuracy; storage limitation; integrity and confidentiality; accountability\n- Article 22: right not to be subject to solely automated decisions with legal or similarly significant effects, with narrow exceptions and safeguards including human intervention\n- Article 35 DPIAs for likely-high-risk processing; consult the supervisory authority if risk remains high (Article 36)\n- Data subject rights versus AI: access includes meaningful information about automated decision-making logic; erasure and objection extend to profiling\n- Breach regime: notify the DPA within 72 hours (Article 33); notify individuals when risk is high (Article 34)\n- Penalties: two tiers, up to EUR 10m/2% and up to EUR 20m/4% of global annual turnover, whichever is higher\n- Extraterritorial scope (Article 3): applies to processing of EU people's data by controllers anywhere when offering goods or services or monitoring behaviour\n- The EDPB's 2026 anonymisation and web-scraping guidelines are drafts and the Digital Omnibus GDPR changes are proposals: apply the GDPR as it stands\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GDPR, Regulation (EU) 2016/679</a> · the full text; Articles 3, 5, 6, 9, 22, 25, 28, 33 and 35 are the AI pressure points\n- <a href=\"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/documents/opinion-of-the-board-art-64/opinion-282024-on-certain-data-protection-aspects-related-to_en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EDPB Opinion 28/2024 on AI models and personal data</a> · the three-step legitimate-interest test and the anonymity test for models\n- <a href=\"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/news/edpb-sheds-light-on-anonymisation-and-web-scraping-for-generative-ai-and-adopts-final-version_en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EDPB draft Guidelines 02/2026 and 03/2026</a> · drafts on anonymisation and web scraping, consultation to 30 October 2026\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/C/2024/913/oj/eng\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CJEU SCHUFA, C-634/21</a> · a decisive third-party score is itself an Article 22 automated decision\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=celex:62022CJ0203\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CJEU Dun & Bradstreet Austria, C-203/22</a> · Article 15(1)(h) requires an intelligible account of the logic actually applied\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] GDPR — AI-Relevant Provisions\n\n# GDPR — AI-Relevant Provisions\n\n> **Regulation (EU) 2016/679** — the EU's comprehensive data protection framework. Many of its core provisions directly govern AI systems that process personal data.\n\n---\n\n## Overview of AI-Relevant Articles\n\n| Article | Topic | AI Relevance |\n|:--------|:------|:-------------|\n| [[#Art 5 — Principles]] | Data protection principles | Foundation for all AI data processing |\n| [[#Art 6 — Lawfulness of Processing]] | Legal bases | Validating the legal basis for AI training/inference |\n| [[#Art 9 — Special Categories]] | Sensitive data (biometrics, health) | **Critical** — AI often processes biometrics, inferred health data |\n| [[#Art 13 — Transparency (Direct Collection)]] | Notice obligations | Explainability of AI to data subjects |\n| [[#Art 14 — Transparency (Indirect Collection)]] | Notice when data not from subject | LLM training data, web-scraped datasets |\n| [[#Art 15 — Right of Access]] | Data subject access | Disclosure of AI logic and profiling |\n| [[#Art 17 — Right to Erasure]] | Right to be forgotten | Machine unlearning, model deletion challenges |\n| [[#Art 22 — Automated Decision-Making]] | **Most important for AI** | Prohibition on solely automated decisions |\n| [[#Art 25 — Data Protection by Design/Default]] | Privacy by design | Embedding privacy into AI system architecture |\n| [[#Art 28 — Processor Obligations]] | Processor contracts | AI-as-a-service, cloud AI providers |\n| [[#Art 35 — DPIA]] | Data Protection Impact Assessment | Mandatory for high-risk AI systems |\n| [[#Arts 44–49 — Cross-Border Transfers]] | International data transfers | AI cloud infrastructure, global model deployment |\n| [[#Anonymous AI Models and Unlawful Training (EDPB Opinion 28/2024)]] | When a model holds personal data | Whether GDPR still applies to a trained model |\n| [[#Pending — GDPR Changes Proposed in the Digital Omnibus]] | Proposals only (not law) | Narrower personal-data definition, AI legitimate interest |\n| [[#EDPB and CJEU Developments 2023–2026 — Quick Reference]] | Cases and guidance | Opinion 28/2024, SCHUFA, Dun & Bradstreet Austria, 2026 drafts |\n\n> [!note] BOK v2.1 (II.A) framing\n> The first II.A indicator now reads \"transparency, choice, **lawful basis** and purpose limitation\" (v2.0.1 said \"notice, choice, consent\"). The controller-obligations indicator now names **automated decision-making** explicitly. Art 6 lawful bases and Art 22 are therefore squarely in scope.\n\n---\n\n## Art 5 — Principles Relating to Processing of Personal Data\n\n### Key Provisions\n\n1. **Lawfulness, fairness, and transparency** (Art 5(1)(a)): Personal data must be processed lawfully, fairly, and in a transparent manner.\n2. **Purpose limitation** (Art 5(1)(b)): Data collected for specified, explicit, and legitimate purposes; no incompatible further processing.\n3. **Data minimisation** (Art 5(1)(c)): Adequate, relevant, and limited to what is necessary.\n4. **Accuracy** (Art 5(1)(d)): Data must be accurate and kept up to date; inaccurate data erased or rectified without delay.\n5. **Storage limitation** (Art 5(1)(e)): Kept no longer than necessary; exceptions for archiving, research, statistics (Art 89(1)).\n6. **Integrity and confidentiality** (Art 5(1)(f)): Appropriate security measures against unauthorised/unlawful processing, loss, destruction, damage.\n7. **Accountability** (Art 5(2)): The controller is responsible for and must demonstrate compliance.\n\n### AI Implications\n\n> [!warning] Data Minimisation vs. AI\n> AI systems — especially deep learning — are data-hungry by design. The principle of **data minimisation** creates inherent tension with model training that benefits from more data. Organisations must document why the volume and variety of data is *necessary* for the specific purpose.\n\n- **Purpose limitation** constrains repurposing training data for new AI models without a compatibility assessment (Art 6(4)).\n- **Accuracy** is especially challenging for AI outputs: if an AI generates inaccurate inferences about a data subject (e.g., predicted credit score, inferred health status), these must be correctable.\n- **Accountability** means AI developers must maintain auditable records of data provenance, training methodology, and compliance measures.\n\n---\n\n## Art 6 — Lawfulness of Processing\n\n### Key Provisions\n\nProcessing is lawful only if **at least one** of the following applies:\n\n| Basis | Description |\n|:------|:------------|\n| (a) Consent | Data subject has given consent for specific purposes |\n| (b) Contract | Necessary for performance of a contract |\n| (c) Legal obligation | Necessary for compliance with a legal obligation |\n| (d) Vital interests | Necessary to protect vital interests |\n| (e) Public interest | Necessary for a task in the public interest / official authority |\n| (f) Legitimate interests | Necessary for legitimate interests pursued by controller or third party (except where overridden by data subject's rights) |\n\nArt 6(4): Further processing for a new purpose requires a **compatibility assessment** considering: links between purposes, context, nature of data, consequences for data subjects, and safeguards.\n\n### AI Implications\n\n> [!important] Legitimate Interests & AI Training\n> Many AI developers rely on **legitimate interests** (Art 6(1)(f)) as the legal basis for training models on personal data. This requires a **Legitimate Interests Assessment (LIA)** balancing the developer's commercial interest against the privacy rights of data subjects. The EDPB has increasingly scrutinised this basis for large-scale AI training.\n\n- **Consent** must be freely given, specific, informed, and unambiguous (Art 7) — difficult to obtain at scale for AI training.\n- **Contract necessity** is narrowly interpreted; AI profiling for marketing purposes generally does not qualify.\n- **Repurposing data** (Art 6(4)): Training a new AI model on data originally collected for a different purpose requires careful compatibility analysis.\n\n### EDPB Opinion 28/2024 — Legitimate Interest for AI Models\n\nAdopted **17 December 2024** under Art 64(2), at the request of the Irish supervisory authority. It answers four questions: (1) when a model is anonymous, (2)–(3) how to rely on legitimate interest in **development** and in **deployment**, and (4) what unlawful development means for later use. There is **no hierarchy** among the Art 6 bases; the controller must pick and justify one.\n\n| Step | Test | AI-specific points from the Opinion |\n|:-----|:-----|:------------------------------------|\n| **1. Legitimate interest** | The interest must be lawful, clearly and precisely articulated, and **real and present** (not speculative) | Examples: developing a conversational agent to assist users (development); improving threat detection in an information system (deployment) |\n| **2. Necessity** | The processing must actually achieve the interest, and no less intrusive way must exist | Supervisory authorities look at the *amount* of personal data against the purpose (data minimisation) |\n| **3. Balancing** | The interest must not be overridden by the data subjects' interests, rights and freedoms | Risks differ between development and deployment. **Reasonable expectations** weigh heavily: whether the data was public, the relationship with the controller, the nature of the service, the collection context and source (including the site's privacy settings), possible further uses of the model, and whether people even know their data is online |\n\n- **Mitigating measures** can tip a failing balance, but only measures that go *beyond* what the GDPR already requires. Examples cover the development phase (including web scraping) and the deployment phase: technical measures, easier exercise of rights, extra transparency.\n- The Opinion is guidance for supervisory authorities' **case-by-case** assessments; it does not pre-approve any model.\n\n### Draft EDPB Guidelines 03/2026 — Web Scraping for Generative AI (status: consultation draft)\n\nAdopted **7 July 2026** as version 1.0 for **public consultation until 30 October 2026**. It covers private entities scraping internet sources, directly or through contractors, to train generative AI.\n\n- The GDPR applies whenever scraping involves personal data (collection, storage, organisation, retrieval). Roles (controller, joint controller, processor) are assessed case by case.\n- **Consent** \"would most probably not be\" an applicable basis for large-scale indirect collection. **Legitimate interest** is the basis \"often used\", and it must pass the three cumulative conditions above. Other Art 6 bases are generally less likely to fit.\n- **Transparency:** individual notices may be unnecessary where impossible or disproportionate (Art 14(5)(b)), depending on design.\n- **Minimisation:** consider synthetic data first, set precise collection criteria, filter out categories of data, exclude sites that structurally hold sensitive data or clearly object to scraping, and pseudonymise, anonymise or delete early.\n- **Accuracy:** scrape from reliable sources, timestamp, and validate before training.\n- **Special categories:** see Art 9 below.\n\n---\n\n## Art 9 — Processing of Special Categories of Personal Data\n\n### Key Provisions\n\n**General prohibition** (Art 9(1)): Processing of the following is **prohibited**:\n\n- Racial or ethnic origin\n- Political opinions\n- Religious or philosophical beliefs\n- Trade union membership\n- **Genetic data**\n- **Biometric data** for uniquely identifying a natural person\n- **Data concerning health**\n- Data concerning sex life or sexual orientation\n\n**Exceptions** (Art 9(2)) — processing permitted where:\n\n| Exception | Description |\n|:----------|:------------|\n| (a) | **Explicit consent** |\n| (b) | Employment / social security / social protection law |\n| (c) | Vital interests (data subject incapable of consent) |\n| (d) | Not-for-profit bodies (political, philosophical, religious, trade union) |\n| (e) | Data **manifestly made public** by the data subject |\n| (f) | Legal claims / judicial capacity |\n| (g) | **Substantial public interest** (Union/Member State law basis) |\n| (h) | Preventive/occupational medicine, medical diagnosis, health/social care |\n| (i) | **Public health** (cross-border threats, quality/safety of healthcare) |\n| (j) | Archiving, scientific/historical research, statistics (Art 89(1)) |\n\nArt 9(4): Member States may introduce further conditions on genetic, biometric, and health data.\n\n### AI Implications\n\n> [!danger] Biometric & Health Inferences\n> This is arguably the **single most important GDPR article for AI governance**. AI systems routinely:\n> - **Infer** special category data from non-special category inputs (e.g., inferring health conditions from shopping patterns, inferring ethnicity from facial images).\n> - Process **biometric data** for facial recognition, emotion detection, voice analysis.\n> - Generate **health-related predictions** (diagnostic AI, wellness apps).\n>\n> The EDPB and supervisory authorities increasingly treat **inferred** special category data as falling within Art 9(1), even if the raw input data is not special category.\n\n- **Facial recognition systems** process biometric data → Art 9 prohibition applies unless explicit consent or substantial public interest (with Member State law basis).\n- **AI-driven health apps and diagnostic tools** must satisfy Art 9(2)(h) conditions (health professional involvement, professional secrecy).\n- **Emotion recognition AI** that infers mental/emotional state → likely triggers Art 9 concerns.\n- **Web-scraped training data** may inadvertently contain special category data; reliance on \"manifestly made public\" (Art 9(2)(e)) is risky and increasingly challenged.\n\n### Special Categories in AI Training — 2026 Developments\n\n- **Two locks, not one:** under draft EDPB Guidelines 03/2026, scraping that processes special category data needs **both** an Art 6 lawful basis **and** an Art 9(2) exception.\n- **Incidental data:** the EDPB suggests the CJEU's *GC and Others* (C-136/17) reasoning may cover *incidental and residual* collection, provided the controller acts within its responsibilities, powers and capabilities and has measures to prevent collection and dissemination. There is no general exemption from Art 9, and each case needs its own analysis.\n- **EU AI Act Art 4a (new, via the 2026 AI Omnibus):** gives an AI Act legal basis to process special category data *strictly* for bias detection and correction. It covers high-risk providers and, as amended, providers and deployers of other AI systems and models. The GDPR conditions apply **in addition**. The six safeguards are: no effective alternative (e.g. synthetic or anonymised data), re-use limits and pseudonymisation, strict access controls, no transfer to other parties, deletion once the bias is fixed, and an entry in the record of processing explaining why it was necessary. See [[EU AI Act 2024 — Key Provisions]] §13.\n- **Proposed, not law:** the data Omnibus would add a GDPR exception for incidental and residual special-category data in AI training. See [[#Pending — GDPR Changes Proposed in the Digital Omnibus]].\n\n---\n\n## Art 13 — Information to Be Provided Where Personal Data Are Collected from the Data Subject\n\n### Key Provisions\n\nControllers must provide at the time of collection:\n\n1. **Basic information** (Art 13(1)): Identity/contact of controller and DPO; purposes and legal basis; legitimate interests (if Art 6(1)(f)); recipients; international transfer details.\n\n2. **Additional transparency** (Art 13(2)) — **critical for AI**:\n - Storage period or criteria\n - Data subject rights (access, rectification, erasure, restriction, portability, objection)\n - Right to withdraw consent\n - Right to lodge a complaint\n - Whether provision is statutory/contractual and consequences of failure to provide\n - **(f) The existence of automated decision-making, including profiling, referred to in Article 22(1) and (4) and, at least in those cases, meaningful information about the logic involved, as well as the significance and the envisaged consequences of such processing for the data subject.**\n\n### AI Implications\n\n> [!note] Meaningful Information About the Logic\n> Art 13(2)(f) is the GDPR's core **explainability** requirement for AI. When AI systems make automated decisions or profile data subjects, the controller must provide:\n> - **Meaningful information** about the logic involved (not the full algorithm, but a comprehensible explanation of how the system works and how it reaches decisions)\n> - **Significance and envisaged consequences** — what the decision means for the data subject in practical terms\n>\n> This is not a right to full model disclosure, but a right to *functional explainability*. The CJEU confirmed the same approach for the parallel access right in *Dun & Bradstreet Austria* (C-203/22, 2025): explain the procedure and principles actually applied, not the formula (see Art 15 below).\n\n- Privacy notices for AI products must explain profiling/automated processing in **clear, plain language**.\n- This intersects with the **EU AI Act** transparency requirements for AI systems.\n\n---\n\n## Art 14 — Information to Be Provided Where Personal Data Have Not Been Obtained from the Data Subject\n\n### Key Provisions\n\nWhere data is **not** collected directly from the data subject, the controller must provide:\n\n- Same identity/contact information as Art 13\n- **Categories of personal data concerned**\n- Source of data (including publicly accessible sources)\n- The same Art 13(2) additional information — including **automated decision-making disclosures**\n- Must be provided: (a) within a reasonable period (max 1 month), (b) at first communication, or (c) before first disclosure to another recipient\n\n**Exceptions** (Art 14(5)): where data subject already has information; impossible or disproportionate effort (with safeguards); legal obligation; professional secrecy.\n\n### AI Implications\n\n> [!warning] LLM Training & Web-Scraped Data\n> Large language models and generative AI trained on **web-scraped data** typically process personal data not obtained from the data subject. Art 14 requires notifying individuals — but developers often invoke the **\"disproportionate effort\"** exception (Art 14(5)(b)). This exception is under intense regulatory scrutiny, particularly from EU DPAs investigating AI training practices.\n\n- **Foundation model training** that scrapes public data must assess whether providing Art 14 notices to billions of data subjects is feasible.\n- Even if invoking \"disproportionate effort,\" controllers must implement **safeguards** including making information publicly available.\n- **Synthetic data generation** derived from personal data may still trigger indirect collection obligations.\n\n---\n\n## Art 15 — Right of Access by the Data Subject\n\n### Key Provisions\n\nData subjects have the right to:\n\n1. **Confirmation** whether their personal data is being processed\n2. **Access** to the personal data and:\n - Purposes, categories, recipients, storage period\n - Rights to rectification, erasure, restriction, objection\n - Right to lodge a complaint\n - Source of data (if not collected from subject)\n - **(h) The existence of automated decision-making, including profiling, referred to in Article 22(1) and (4) and meaningful information about the logic involved, as well as the significance and envisaged consequences**\n - Safeguards for international transfers (Art 46)\n\n### AI Implications\n\n> [!important] Access Requests & AI Models\n> Art 15 presents unique challenges for AI systems:\n> - Can a data subject access their **training data** embedded in model weights?\n> - **Model inversion attacks** and **membership inference** complicate the line between personal data and model parameters.\n> - The controller must provide **meaningful information about the logic** — this goes beyond what Art 13 requires at collection time; it obligates responsive disclosure on request.\n\n- Data subjects can request the **logic of automated decisions** that affect them, including model type, input features, and relative weighting.\n- This right works alongside **Art 22** to ensure accountability for AI-driven outcomes.\n\n### CJEU: Dun & Bradstreet Austria (C-203/22, 27 February 2025)\n\n**Facts:** a mobile operator refused a €10-a-month contract after a credit assessment by Dun & Bradstreet Austria, and the customer sought to understand the score.\n\n**Holdings (Art 15(1)(h)):**\n1. For automated decision-making within Art 22(1), \"meaningful information about the logic involved\" means an explanation of **the procedure and principles actually applied** to the person's data to reach the result (e.g. a credit profile). It must be given concisely, transparently, intelligibly and in an easily accessible form.\n2. Handing over a **complex mathematical formula or algorithm**, or a step-by-step description of the whole process, does **not** satisfy the right, because neither is intelligible to the person (para 59). The complexity of the operations does not excuse the controller from explaining.\n3. It may be sufficiently transparent to tell the person **how far a variation in their data would have changed the result** (para 62), which is effectively a counterfactual explanation.\n4. **Trade secrets and third-party data:** the controller must give the allegedly protected information to the **supervisory authority or court**, which balances the interests and decides how much the person gets. A national rule that excludes access **as a rule** whenever trade secrets are at stake is precluded (para 75).\n\n**Governance takeaway:** design explanation capability for the actual decision path, meaning the inputs used and how they drove the outcome. \"It's proprietary\" is not a complete answer to an access request.\n\n---\n\n## Anonymous AI Models and Unlawful Training (EDPB Opinion 28/2024)\n\n### Is a trained model personal data? (Opinion Question 1)\n\n- AI models trained on personal data **cannot, in all cases, be considered anonymous**. Supervisory authorities assess anonymity claims **case by case**.\n- A model is anonymous only if **both** likelihoods are **insignificant**, taking account of all the means reasonably likely to be used by the controller or anyone else:\n 1. **direct extraction**, including probabilistic extraction, of personal data about the people whose data trained it; and\n 2. obtaining such data, intentionally or not, **through queries**.\n- **Evidence:** controllers document how they limited the collection of personal data, reduced identifiability, prevented extraction, and tested resistance to state-of-the-art attacks. The Opinion's list of methods is non-prescriptive.\n- **Consequence:** if the model is **not** anonymous, it still processes personal data, so lawful basis, rights (access, erasure, objection) and security duties reach the model itself, not just the source records.\n\n### What does unlawful development mean for deployment? (Opinion Question 4)\n\n| Scenario | Facts | EDPB position |\n|:---|:---|:---|\n| **1** | Personal data retained in the model; the **same controller** deploys it | Case by case: are development and deployment separate purposes, and how far does the missing legal basis taint the later processing? |\n| **2** | Personal data retained in the model; **another controller** deploys it | The deployer must carry out an appropriate assessment, as part of its accountability, that the model was not developed unlawfully. It looks at the data's source and any finding of infringement by an authority or court, in more or less depth depending on deployment risk |\n| **3** | Developed unlawfully, **then genuinely anonymised** | If later operation involves no personal data, the GDPR does not apply to it and the original unlawfulness does not taint it. New personal data processed in deployment is assessed on its own terms |\n\n> **Press-release shorthand:** unlawfully processed training data \"could have an impact on the lawfulness of its deployment, unless the model has been duly anonymised\".\n\n### Draft EDPB Guidelines 02/2026 on Anonymisation (status: consultation draft)\n\nAdopted at the **July 2026** plenary; **public consultation until 30 October 2026**.\n\n- Data is anonymous if it does not relate to an identified or identifiable person, and **whether it is anonymous can differ from one entity to another**. This reflects the CJEU's *EDPS v SRB* ruling (C-413/23 P, 4 September 2025).\n- **Three tests:** no **record isolation**, no **linkage**, no **inference**. If all three are met, the data can safely be treated as anonymous; otherwise further analysis is needed.\n- **Two ways to apply them:** a *contextual* approach that weighs who might re-identify and with what capabilities (the full legal standard), or a stricter *simplified* approach that ignores those differences for convenience and extra confidence.\n\n---\n\n## Art 17 — Right to Erasure (\"Right to Be Forgotten\")\n\n### Key Provisions\n\nData subjects can request erasure without undue delay where:\n\n| Ground | Description |\n|:-------|:------------|\n| (a) | Data no longer necessary for original purpose |\n| (b) | Consent withdrawn, no other legal ground |\n| (c) | Objection under Art 21(1) — no overriding grounds |\n| (d) | Unlawful processing |\n| (e) | Legal obligation to erase |\n| (f) | Data collected from child (information society services) |\n\n**Exceptions** (Art 17(3)): freedom of expression, legal obligation, public health, archiving/research/statistics, legal claims.\n\nArt 17(2): Where data made public, controller must take **reasonable steps** (including technical measures) to notify other processors.\n\n### AI Implications\n\n> [!danger] Machine Unlearning\n> This is one of the **hardest technical challenges** at the intersection of GDPR and AI. Deleting an individual's data from a trained model is not straightforward:\n> - **Full retraining** is computationally expensive and may be impractical for large foundation models.\n> - **Approximate unlearning** techniques (e.g., SISA training, gradient-based unlearning) are developing but unproven for regulatory compliance.\n> - **Differential privacy** at training time can reduce the influence of any single data point, making unlearning more feasible.\n>\n> Regulators are increasingly aware of this tension. The UK ICO and French CNIL have issued guidance acknowledging the difficulty but maintaining the obligation.\n\n- **Is the model in scope?** Run the EDPB Opinion 28/2024 anonymity test first. If extracting a person's data from the model, directly or by querying, is more than insignificantly likely, the model is not anonymous and the erasure request can reach the model, not just the training database. Do not assume model weights are never personal data.\n- **LLMs and generative AI** that memorize training data face particular challenges — erasure may require model editing or retraining.\n- **Vector databases** storing embeddings derived from personal data may also fall within scope of erasure obligations.\n- **Federated learning** architectures complicate the practicalities of erasure across distributed model updates.\n\n---\n\n## Art 22 — Automated Individual Decision-Making, Including Profiling\n\n### Key Provisions\n\n> **This is the most AI-relevant article in the GDPR.**\n\n**Art 22(1)** — The Prohibition:\n> \"The data subject shall have the right **not to be subject to a decision based solely on automated processing**, including profiling, which produces **legal effects** concerning him or her or **similarly significantly affects** him or her.\"\n\n**Art 22(2)** — Exceptions (the prohibition does not apply):\n\n| Exception | Description |\n|:----------|:------------|\n| (a) | **Necessary** for entering into or performance of a **contract** |\n| (b) | **Authorised by Union or Member State law** with suitable safeguards |\n| (c) | Based on the data subject's **explicit consent** |\n\n**Art 22(3)** — Safeguards: For exceptions (a) and (c), the controller must implement:\n- Right to obtain **human intervention**\n- Right to **express point of view**\n- Right to **contest the decision**\n\n**Art 22(4)** — Special Category Data: Automated decisions must **not** be based on Art 9(1) special categories unless Art 9(2)(a) (explicit consent) or (g) (substantial public interest) applies **and** suitable safeguards are in place.\n\n### AI Implications\n\n> [!danger] Solely Automated Decisions\n> The key word is **\"solely.\"** If there is **meaningful human involvement** in the decision, Art 22 may not apply. However, the WP29 Guidelines (now EDPB-endorsed) clarify:\n> - Human intervention must be **meaningful** — not a rubber stamp\n> - The human reviewer must have **authority and competence** to change the decision\n> - The human must actually **consider all relevant data**\n>\n> Simply having a person click \"approve\" on an AI recommendation does **not** satisfy the human intervention requirement.\n\n**Examples of Art 22-triggering AI use cases:**\n\n| Use Case | Triggers Art 22? | Basis |\n|:---------|:-----------------|:------|\n| AI denial of credit with no human review | ✅ Yes | Produces legal effects |\n| Credit bureau's automated score that a lender draws on strongly to refuse a loan | ✅ Yes, for the **bureau's scoring itself** | CJEU *SCHUFA* (C-634/21) |\n| AI recruitment screening that auto-rejects candidates | ✅ Yes | Similarly significantly affects |\n| AI recommendation with human decision-maker | ⚠️ Maybe | Depends on meaningfulness of human review |\n| AI content recommendation (Netflix, Spotify) | ❌ Likely not | May not \"significantly affect\" |\n| AI-generated targeted advertising | ⚠️ Debatable | Depends on significance of effects |\n\n- **Credit scoring AI**, **recruitment AI**, **insurance underwriting AI** → almost certainly within Art 22 scope.\n- The **EU AI Act** builds on Art 22 by classifying certain AI systems as high-risk and imposing additional requirements (human oversight, accuracy, robustness).\n- Organisations must implement **human review processes** that are genuine, documented, and effective.\n\n### CJEU: SCHUFA (C-634/21, 7 December 2023)\n\n**Holding:** a credit information agency's automated calculation of a **probability value** (a score) about a person's ability to meet future payments is itself \"automated individual decision-making\" under Art 22(1), where a third party to which the score is sent **draws strongly** on it to establish, implement or terminate a contract with that person.\n\n**Why it matters:**\n- **Upstream providers can be caught.** The score supplier, not only the lender, can be the one making the Art 22 \"decision\". It then needs an Art 22(2) exception (contract, EU or Member State law, or explicit consent) and must provide Art 22(3) safeguards and the Art 15(1)(h) information.\n- **It closes a gap.** Otherwise the bureau could say it made no decision, and the lender could say it lacks the logic, leaving the person with no one who must explain.\n- **Vendors and AI supply chains:** any model-as-a-service whose output effectively determines a customer's decision should be assessed under Art 22, and the contract should allocate who gives notices, explanations and human review.\n- **Follow-on:** *Dun & Bradstreet Austria* (C-203/22, 2025) sets out what the explanation must contain (see Art 15 above).\n\n---\n\n## Art 25 — Data Protection by Design and by Default\n\n### Key Provisions\n\n**Art 25(1)** — Data Protection by Design:\n- Controller must implement **appropriate technical and organisational measures** (e.g., pseudonymisation)\n- Designed to implement data protection principles (particularly data minimisation)\n- Take into account: state of the art, cost of implementation, nature/scope/context/purposes of processing, risks to rights and freedoms\n- Applied **both at the time of determining the means** and **at the time of processing itself**\n\n**Art 25(2)** — Data Protection by Default:\n- By default, only personal data **necessary for each specific purpose** is processed\n- Applies to: amount collected, extent of processing, storage period, accessibility\n- Default settings must not make personal data accessible to an indefinite number of persons without the individual's intervention\n\n### AI Implications\n\n> [!tip] AI System Architecture\n> Art 25 requires privacy to be embedded into AI system design from the **earliest stages**:\n> - **Federated learning**: train models without centralising raw personal data\n> - **Differential privacy**: add calibrated noise to protect individual contributions\n> - **On-device processing**: process data locally rather than uploading to cloud\n> - **Synthetic data generation**: train on privacy-preserving synthetic datasets\n> - **Pseudonymisation** at ingestion: separate identifiers from attributes\n> - **Data minimisation in feature engineering**: only collect/process features necessary for the defined purpose\n\n- This aligns with the **EU AI Act's** requirement for risk management systems and data governance.\n- Certification mechanisms (Art 42) can be used to demonstrate compliance.\n\n---\n\n## Art 28 — Processor\n\n### Key Provisions\n\nWhere processing is carried out **on behalf of** a controller:\n\n1. Controller must use only processors providing **sufficient guarantees** of GDPR-compliant technical/organisational measures (Art 28(1)).\n2. Processor cannot engage **sub-processors** without prior written authorisation (Art 28(2)).\n3. Processing must be governed by a **binding contract** specifying (Art 28(3)):\n - Subject matter, duration, nature, purpose, data types, categories of data subjects\n - Processor obligations: documented instructions only; confidentiality; security (Art 32); sub-processor conditions; **assist controller** with data subject rights and DPIA; delete/return data at end; audit rights; immediate notification if instructions infringe GDPR.\n4. **Cascading liability**: if sub-processor fails, the initial processor remains fully liable (Art 28(4)).\n5. If processor determines purposes/means of processing → becomes a **controller** (Art 28(10)).\n\n### AI Implications\n\n> [!important] AI-as-a-Service Providers\n> Common AI deployment patterns trigger Art 28 obligations:\n> - **Cloud AI APIs** (OpenAI, Anthropic, Google Vertex AI): The provider is typically a processor; the customer is the controller. A DPA (Data Processing Agreement) is required.\n> - **Fine-tuning services**: If the provider trains/tunes a model on the customer's data, this is processing under Art 28.\n> - **MLOps platforms**: Data pipeline, training infrastructure, and model serving may involve multiple processors and sub-processors.\n> - **Open-weight models run locally**: May avoid processor relationships if the customer controls all processing.\n\n- **Processor due diligence** must include evaluating the AI provider's security practices, data handling, and sub-processor ecosystem.\n- If a cloud AI provider uses customer data to **improve its own models**, it may cross the line from processor to controller (Art 28(10)).\n- **Standard Contractual Clauses (SCCs)** may need to address AI-specific processing activities.\n\n---\n\n## Art 35 — Data Protection Impact Assessment (DPIA)\n\n### Key Provisions\n\n**Art 35(1)**: DPIA required where processing — **\"in particular using new technologies\"** — is likely to result in a **high risk** to rights and freedoms of natural persons.\n\n**Art 35(3)** — DPIA **mandatory** for:\n\n| Trigger | Description |\n|:--------|:------------|\n| (a) | **Systematic and extensive evaluation** of personal aspects based on **automated processing, including profiling**, on which **decisions are based that produce legal effects** or similarly significantly affect the data subject |\n| (b) | Processing on a **large scale** of **special categories of data** (Art 9(1)) or criminal conviction data (Art 10) |\n| (c) | **Systematic monitoring** of a publicly accessible area on a **large scale** |\n\n**Art 35(7)** — DPIA must contain:\n- Systematic description of processing and purposes (including legitimate interests)\n- Assessment of **necessity and proportionality**\n- Assessment of **risks to rights and freedoms**\n- **Measures to address risks** (safeguards, security, mechanisms to demonstrate compliance)\n\n### AI Implications\n\n> [!warning] Most AI Systems Trigger DPIA\n> Almost any AI system that processes personal data and makes or informs decisions about individuals will fall within Art 35(3)(a) — especially:\n> - **AI recruitment/hiring tools**: systematic evaluation of candidates → legal effects (hiring decisions)\n> - **AI credit scoring/insurance underwriting**: profiling with significant effects\n> - **AI medical diagnosis**: large-scale special category data (Art 35(3)(b))\n> - **AI fraud detection**: systematic profiling with potential legal effects\n> - **Facial recognition in public spaces**: systematic monitoring (Art 35(3)(c))\n\n**DPIA content for AI systems should address:**\n\n| DPIA Element | AI-Specific Considerations |\n|:-------------|:---------------------------|\n| Necessity & proportionality | Why AI is needed vs. less intrusive alternatives; data minimisation in model design |\n| Risk assessment | Discrimination/bias risk; model drift; adversarial attacks; explainability gaps; over-reliance |\n| Safeguards | Human oversight; accuracy monitoring; bias testing; fallback procedures; data quality controls |\n| Data subject rights | How to handle access, rectification, erasure, and objection requests in AI context |\n\n- Art 36 **prior consultation** with supervisory authority if residual high risk remains after mitigation.\n- The **EU AI Act** requires a \"fundamental rights impact assessment\" for high-risk AI systems, which overlaps significantly with the DPIA.\n\n---\n\n## Arts 44–49 — Cross-Border Transfers of Personal Data\n\n### Art 44 — General Principle for Transfers\n\nTransfers to third countries or international organisations only where conditions of Chapter V are met — ensuring the level of protection guaranteed by the GDPR is **not undermined**. Applies to **onward transfers** as well.\n\n### Art 45 — Transfers on the Basis of an Adequacy Decision\n\nTransfers allowed where the **Commission has decided** the third country ensures an **adequate level of protection**. Assessed on:\n- Rule of law, human rights, data protection legislation\n- Existence of independent supervisory authority\n- International commitments\n\nNo specific authorisation required. Adequacy decisions are subject to periodic review (at least every 4 years).\n\n### Art 46 — Transfers Subject to Appropriate Safeguards\n\nIn the absence of an adequacy decision, transfers permitted with **appropriate safeguards** and **enforceable data subject rights and effective legal remedies**:\n\n| Safeguard | Description |\n|:-----------|:------------|\n| (a) | Legally binding instrument between public authorities |\n| (b) | **Binding Corporate Rules (BCRs)** (Art 47) |\n| (c) | **Standard Contractual Clauses (SCCs)** adopted by Commission |\n| (d) | SCCs adopted by supervisory authority and approved by Commission |\n| (e) | Approved code of conduct + binding commitments |\n| (f) | Approved certification mechanism + binding commitments |\n\nAdditionally, with supervisory authority authorisation: ad hoc contractual clauses or administrative arrangements.\n\n### Art 47 — Binding Corporate Rules\n\nBCRs must be approved by the competent supervisory authority and must:\n- Be legally binding and enforced across the corporate group\n- Confer enforceable rights on data subjects\n- Specify: structure, transfers, data protection principles, data subject rights (including Art 22 rights), liability, training, complaint mechanisms, audit procedures\n\n### Art 48 — Transfers Not Authorised by Union Law\n\nThird-country court/administrative orders requiring disclosure are enforceable only if based on an **international agreement** (e.g., MLAT). This is a direct response to concerns about foreign government access (e.g., US CLOUD Act, FISA 702).\n\n### Art 49 — Derogations for Specific Situations\n\nIn the **absence of adequacy decision OR Art 46 safeguards**, transfers possible only on **derogations**:\n\n| Derogation | Description |\n|:-----------|:------------|\n| (a) | **Explicit consent** after being informed of risks |\n| (b) | Necessary for **contract performance** with data subject |\n| (c) | Necessary for contract in data subject's interest |\n| (d) | **Important reasons of public interest** |\n| (e) | Legal claims |\n| (f) | Vital interests |\n| (g) | Public register consultation |\n\n**Last resort** (Art 49(1) subpara 2): Transfer possible where **not repetitive**, **limited number** of data subjects, **compelling legitimate interests** not overridden, **suitable safeguards** assessed, and controller **informs supervisory authority** and data subject.\n\n### AI Implications\n\n> [!important] Global AI Infrastructure\n> Cross-border transfer rules directly affect AI deployment:\n> - **Cloud AI infrastructure**: If your AI model runs on US-based cloud (AWS, GCP, Azure) and processes EU personal data, you need a transfer mechanism (typically SCCs + Transfer Impact Assessment).\n> - **AI model training offshore**: Training models in third countries with EU data requires Art 46 safeguards.\n> - **Global AI services**: Providing AI services to EU data subjects from a third country triggers GDPR territorial scope (Art 3).\n> - **LLM APIs**: When using US-based AI APIs (OpenAI, Anthropic), data flows to the US — SCCs or adequacy required.\n> - **Schrems II / EU-US Data Privacy Framework**: The evolving adequacy landscape directly impacts where AI processing infrastructure can be located.\n\n---\n\n## Pending — GDPR Changes Proposed in the Digital Omnibus\n\n> [!warning] Proposals, not law (as of September 2026)\n> The Commission's **Digital Omnibus** proposal, COM(2025) 837 of 19 November 2025, would amend the GDPR, ePrivacy Directive, Data Act, NIS2 and other acts. It is **separate** from the **AI Omnibus**, which amended the AI Act and is already law (Regulation (EU) 2026/1744). As of September 2026 the GDPR changes remain under negotiation. The Council is still working on compromise texts, there is no Council general approach or Parliament committee position, and trilogues have not started. **Answer exam questions on the current GDPR text.**\n\n| Proposed change (per EDPB-EDPS Joint Opinion 2/2026, February 2026) | EDPB-EDPS view |\n|:---|:---|\n| New sentence in **Art 4(1)**: information is not personal data *for an entity* that cannot identify the person (entity-relative definition) | Strongly urge co-legislators **not** to adopt it; it would narrow the concept of personal data |\n| Defining when pseudonymised data stops being personal data by **implementing act** | Oppose; scope of the law should not be set by implementing act |\n| New **Art 88c**: processing for developing and operating AI \"may\" rely on legitimate interest | Unnecessary, since Opinion 28/2024 already confirms it; suggestions offered if kept |\n| New exception for **incidental and residual** special-category data in AI training, testing and validation | Accept the need; want tighter wording and lifecycle safeguards |\n| Limits on **abusive access requests** | Welcome clarity, but should not be tied to non-data-protection motives |\n| **Breach notification:** higher threshold, deadline extended from 72 to 96 hours, common templates; **DPIA** common lists and templates | Broadly supportive; want the EDPB to prepare and approve the templates |\n| Scientific-research definition; exception for **biometric authentication** under the user's sole control | Supportive, with improvements |\n\nSecondary reporting (Privacy Next, 1 September 2026) says the Council's June 2026 compromise dropped the operative AI legitimate-interest article in favour of a recital. Treat details of the Council text as unconfirmed until it is published.\n\n---\n\n## EDPB and CJEU Developments 2023–2026 — Quick Reference\n\n| Instrument | Date | Status | Core point for AI governance |\n|:---|:---|:---|:---|\n| CJEU *SCHUFA* (C-634/21) | 7 Dec 2023 | Final judgment | A third-party score that a decision-maker draws on strongly is itself an Art 22 decision |\n| EDPB Opinion 28/2024 | 17 Dec 2024 | Adopted (Art 64(2)) | Model anonymity test; three-step legitimate interest; effect of unlawful training on deployment |\n| CJEU *Dun & Bradstreet Austria* (C-203/22) | 27 Feb 2025 | Final judgment | Art 15(1)(h) needs an intelligible account of the procedure and principles actually applied; trade secrets go to the DPA or court |\n| CJEU *EDPS v SRB* (C-413/23 P) | 4 Sep 2025 | Final judgment | Cited by the EDPB for the entity-relative view of identifiability |\n| EDPB-EDPS Joint Opinion 1/2026 (AI Omnibus) | Jan 2026 | Adopted | Opinion on the AI Act simplification proposal |\n| EDPB-EDPS Joint Opinion 2/2026 (Digital Omnibus) | Feb 2026 | Adopted | Opposes the narrower personal-data definition |\n| EDPB Guidelines 02/2026 on Anonymisation | Jul 2026 | **Draft**, consultation to 30 Oct 2026 | No record isolation, no linkage, no inference |\n| EDPB Guidelines 03/2026 on Web Scraping | 7 Jul 2026 | **Draft**, consultation to 30 Oct 2026 | Legitimate interest is the usual basis; consent rarely workable; Art 6 **and** Art 9(2) for sensitive data |\n| Digital Omnibus GDPR amendments | Proposed 19 Nov 2025 | **Proposal only** | Art 4(1) and Art 88c changes are not law |\n\n---\n\n## Key Terms\n\n- [[EDPB Opinion 28/2024]]\n- [[EDPB Guidelines 03/2026 on Web Scraping]]\n- [[SCHUFA Ruling (C-634/21)]]\n- [[Dun & Bradstreet Austria Ruling (C-203/22)]]\n\n---\n\n## Article Interdependencies for AI\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n A[Art 5 Principles] --> B[Art 6 Lawful Basis]\n A --> C[Art 9 Special Categories]\n B --> D[Art 13/14 Transparency]\n C --> D\n D --> E[Art 22 Automated Decisions]\n E --> F[Art 35 DPIA]\n F --> G[Art 44-49 Transfers]\n A --> H[Art 25 Design/Default]\n H --> F\n```\n\n---\n\n## Links\n\n- [[Privacy and Data Protection Law]] — Full AIGP study note on privacy law\n- [[The EU AI Act]] — The AI Act's relationship with GDPR\n- [[AIGP Body of Knowledge — Exam Blueprint]] — See Domain II: Legal and Regulatory\n- [[EU AI Act 2024 — Key Provisions]]\n\n---\n\n## Sources\n\n- Regulation (EU) 2016/679 (GDPR): http://data.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj\n- EDPB Opinion 28/2024 on AI models (PDF): https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2024-12/edpb_opinion_202428_ai-models_en.pdf\n- EDPB press release on Opinion 28/2024 (18 Dec 2024): https://www.edpb.europa.eu/news/news/2024/edpb-opinion-ai-models-gdpr-principles-support-responsible-ai_en\n- EDPB news, anonymisation and web-scraping guidelines (8 Jul 2026): https://www.edpb.europa.eu/news/edpb-sheds-light-on-anonymisation-and-web-scraping-for-generative-ai-and-adopts-final-version_en\n- EDPB Guidelines 03/2026 consultation: https://www.edpb.europa.eu/public-consultations/guidelines-032026-on-web-scraping-in-the-context-of-generative-ai_en\n- EDPB Guidelines 02/2026 consultation: https://www.edpb.europa.eu/public-consultations/guidelines-on-anonymisation_en\n- CJEU C-634/21 *SCHUFA Holding (Scoring)*, judgment of 7 Dec 2023: http://publications.europa.eu/resource/celex/62021CJ0634\n- CJEU C-203/22 *Dun & Bradstreet Austria*, judgment of 27 Feb 2025: http://publications.europa.eu/resource/celex/62022CJ0203 ; press release: https://curia.europa.eu/site/upload/docs/application/pdf/2025-02/cp250022en.pdf\n- EDPB-EDPS Joint Opinion 2/2026 on the Digital Omnibus: https://www.edpb.europa.eu/documents/legislative-opinion/edpb-edps-joint-opinion-22026-on-the-proposal-for-a-regulation-as_en\n- Regulation (EU) 2026/1744 (AI Omnibus; AI Act Art 4a): http://data.europa.eu/eli/reg/2026/1744/oj\n\n*Extracted from Regulation (EU) 2016/679 (General Data Protection Regulation). Official Journal L 119, 4.5.2016. Updated 2026-09-23 with EDPB and CJEU developments.*\n\n---\n\n> [!source] Framework Recall Cards — AIGP Study Kit\n\n## GDPR applied to AI\n\n*Your strong area — but know the AI-specific hooks cold.*\n\n### Article 22 — what it covers and what rights attach\n\nApplies to decisions based **solely on automated processing** (including profiling) producing **legal or similarly significant effects**. Rights: **human intervention**, to **express a point of view**, and to **contest** the decision, plus meaningful information about the logic involved. Exceptions: necessary for contract, authorized by law, or explicit consent — and safeguards still apply.\n\n### When a DPIA is required, and how it differs from a conformity assessment\n\n**DPIA** (Art 35) — required where processing is likely to result in **high risk to individuals' rights and freedoms**: large-scale profiling, special-category data, systematic monitoring. A **GDPR** instrument protecting data subjects.\n**Conformity assessment** — an **EU AI Act** instrument demonstrating a high-risk system meets Act requirements before CE marking. Different laws, different purposes. Don't swap them.\n\n### Special-category data and inferences\n\nArt 9 categories: racial/ethnic origin, political opinions, religious/philosophical beliefs, trade union membership, genetic, biometric (for unique ID), health, sex life/orientation. Processing is **prohibited unless an exception applies**.\n**Key AI point:** an **inferred** attribute is still personal data, and if it reveals a special category it attracts Art 9 protection — even if the person never supplied it.\n\n### Cross-border transfer requirements\n\nNeed a lawful transfer route: an **adequacy decision** (including the EU–US Data Privacy Framework for certified US recipients), or an Art 46 safeguard such as **SCCs** or **BCRs**. When relying on SCCs or BCRs, add a documented **transfer impact assessment** of destination-country law; no TIA is needed where adequacy applies. Art 49 derogations are a narrow last resort. Encryption and pseudonymization are **supplementary measures**, not substitutes. Notice alone never legitimizes a transfer.\n\n### The Art 5 principles most often breached by AI projects\n\n**Purpose limitation** — reusing data collected for one purpose to train a model for another is the single most common failure. Needs a compatibility assessment, fresh consent, or another lawful ground.\n**Data minimization** — 'more data is better' collides directly with this.\n**Accuracy**, **storage limitation**, **transparency**, and **accountability** round it out. Also know **Art 25** data protection by design and by default.", "ch5": "## At a Glance\n\nThis knowledge area surveys the sectoral and civil laws that reach AI without naming it: intellectual property (training-data copyright, output ownership, trade secrets), non-discrimination law (disparate impact through proxies), consumer protection (deception and unfairness), and product liability (defective AI as a product). The lesson is that 'no AI law here' never means 'no law here'.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nII.B carries 4 to 6 questions and is the domain's scenario-matching exercise: read the fact pattern, name the doctrine. It is substantively unchanged from BOK v2.0.1.\n\n### 1. Intellectual property\n\nIP meets AI at three points: inputs (training on protected works), the model itself (whether weights contain copies), and outputs (whether generated content infringes or can be owned).\n\nIn the **US**, training is tested under the four fair-use factors (purpose and character, nature of the work, amount used, effect on the market). District courts split in 2025: training was fair use on the records in **Bartz v. Anthropic** and **Kadrey v. Meta** (both June 2025), but not in **Thomson Reuters v. Ross** (February 2025), where the use built a competing market substitute. No US appellate court had ruled as of September 2026. Two details decide cases: **how the data was acquired** (Bartz refused to excuse a permanent library of pirated books, and the case settled for USD 1.5 billion, final approval 20 July 2026) and **whether market harm was proven** (Kadrey won on a thin record, not a safe harbour). Outputs matter too: **GEMA v. OpenAI** (Munich, November 2025) held memorised lyrics were reproductions in the model and in outputs, outside the TDM exception, and pinned responsibility on the operator. **Authorship is human**: *Thaler v. Perlmutter* (D.C. Cir., March 2025; cert. denied 2 March 2026) requires a work authored in the first instance by a human being, and prompts alone are not enough, though human selection, arrangement and modification of AI output can be protected (Copyright Office Part 2).\n\nIn the **EU** there is no fair use, only the DSM Directive's exceptions: Article 3 for research organisations and heritage institutions with no opt-out, and Article 4 for anyone with lawful access, subject to machine-readable rights reservations under Article 4(3). GPAI providers must honour those reservations and publish a training-content summary (AI Act Article 53(1)(c)-(d)), wherever training happened and even for open-source models. The UK treated model weights as not an infringing copy (*Getty v. Stability AI*, November 2025, on appeal) while Munich treated memorised works as reproduced in the parameters (*GEMA*): the split is live. **Trade secrets** protect datasets, architecture, hyperparameters and prompts only while genuinely secret.\n\n### 2. Non-discrimination\n\nTwo theories: **disparate treatment** (intentional, including deliberate use of a prohibited-basis proxy) and **disparate impact** (a neutral practice that disproportionately harms a protected group, no intent required). Title VII section 703(k) writes the disparate-impact standard into the statute: the plaintiff shows the practice causes the impact, the employer must show it is job-related and consistent with business necessity, and the plaintiff can still win by showing a less discriminatory alternative. Sector hooks: **Title VII, ADA and ADEA** in employment; **ECOA** (with Regulation B adverse-action reasons) and **FCRA** in credit; the **Fair Housing Act** in housing and mortgage lending; state insurance law for insurance. **Proxy variables** (postal code for race, credit history for race) are the recurring mechanism.\n\nFederal enforcement retreated in 2025 and 2026: the EEOC withdrew its AI technical assistance, Executive Order 14281 directed agencies to minimise disparate-impact liability, the CFPB withdrew its AI circulars, and a final Regulation B rule effective **21 July 2026** states that ECOA does not provide for the effects test. But an executive order cannot amend a statute, HUD's removal of its FHA effects rule is proposed not final, and private plaintiffs, states and the FHA route (via *Inclusive Communities*, 2015) remain.\n\n### 3. Consumer protection\n\n**FTC Act section 5** prohibits unfair or deceptive acts or practices, without proof of intent. AI enforcement has targeted deception about what AI can do: Operation AI Comply (September 2024, including DoNotPay) and Workado (April 2025) for unsubstantiated accuracy claims. The FTC set aside its Rytr order in December 2025 because the theory condemned a tool for possible misuse, but deception remains squarely actionable. Every state has a \"mini-FTC Act\" (California UCL, Massachusetts 93A, New York GBL 349). AI-specific risks: undisclosed AI interaction, **dark patterns** that personalise manipulation at scale, deepfake impersonation and fabricated endorsements, and AI-generated fake reviews, which the FTC's Consumer Reviews and Testimonials Rule (August 2024) bans.\n\n### 4. Product liability\n\nThree theories: **design defect** (manufactured as intended but unreasonably dangerous), **manufacturing defect** (deviates from the intended design), and **failure to warn**. They run through strict liability (defective and caused harm, no negligence needed), negligence, or breach of warranty. AI complicates defect analysis because behaviour is dynamic, models drift, and outputs are probabilistic. The EU settled the \"is software a product\" debate: under the revised **Product Liability Directive (EU) 2024/2853**, software including AI is a product however supplied, liability is no-fault, disclosure can be ordered once a claim is plausible, and defect and causation can be **presumed** where technical or scientific complexity makes proof excessively difficult, with machine learning a named example. Member States must transpose by **9 December 2026**, and it applies to products placed on the market after that date. The fault-based **AI Liability Directive was withdrawn** (OJ C/2025/5423, 6 October 2025), so an answer that relies on its presumption of causality is wrong.\n\n### The through-line\n\nIdentify the doctrine, then the actor, then the specific duty. Acquisition source, market-dilution evidence, competing substitute, training location and memorisation are the one-fact deciders in IP; proxy review, business necessity and specific adverse-action reasons are the ones in discrimination.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nScenario-heavy: an AI credit model with disparate impact (ECOA and fair lending), a chatbot making deceptive claims (FTC Act), a generative model trained on copyrighted works (the fair-use fight), an autonomous product causing injury (strict liability versus negligence). Match the fact pattern to the doctrine, and then to the actor. Watch the case-status traps: Thaler is final (cert. denied 2 March 2026), Bartz is settled (USD 1.5 billion, approved 20 July 2026), and Ross, Getty and GEMA are on appeal; NYT v. OpenAI is pending. Remember that withdrawn guidance or an executive order does not repeal a statute, and that the AI Liability Directive was withdrawn, so answers resting on its presumption of causality are wrong.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Copyright: training on protected works turns on fair use in the US (courts split in Bartz, Kadrey and Ross) and on the DSM Article 3 and Article 4 exceptions in the EU; purely AI-generated output lacks copyright without human authorship\n- Acquisition matters: pirated or shadow-library sources drive exposure, and Bartz settled for USD 1.5 billion after the court refused to excuse a permanent library of pirated books\n- Disparate impact doctrine catches facially neutral models that harm protected classes through proxy variables (zip code to race), and Title VII section 703(k) writes the standard into the statute\n- Key US anti-discrimination hooks: Title VII and the ADA in employment, ECOA with Regulation B in credit, the Fair Housing Act in housing, state law in insurance\n- FTC Act section 5 reaches AI: deceptive claims about AI capabilities (Workado, DoNotPay), unfair algorithmic practices and dark patterns, with no intent required\n- Product liability theories against AI are manufacturing defect, design defect and failure to warn; strict liability does not require proving negligence\n- EU Product Liability Directive (EU) 2024/2853 treats software including AI as a product, adds rebuttable presumptions of defect and causation, and must be transposed by 9 December 2026\n- The AI Liability Directive was withdrawn (OJ C/2025/5423, 6 October 2025): there is no EU fault-based AI liability regime\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2024/2853/oj/eng\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Directive (EU) 2024/2853 (revised Product Liability Directive)</a> · software and AI are products; rebuttable presumptions; transpose by 9 December 2026\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2019/790/oj\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Directive (EU) 2019/790 (DSM Directive)</a> · the Article 3 and Article 4 text and data mining exceptions and the Article 4(3) opt-out\n- <a href=\"https://www.copyright.gov/ai/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">US Copyright Office, Copyright and Artificial Intelligence</a> · Parts 1 to 3: digital replicas, copyrightability, and generative AI training\n- <a href=\"https://media.cadc.uscourts.gov/opinions/docs/2025/03/23-5233.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Thaler v. Perlmutter, D.C. Cir. opinion (18 March 2025)</a> · the human authorship requirement; the Supreme Court denied certiorari on 2 March 2026\n- <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/2000e-2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Title VII section 703(k), 42 U.S.C. 2000e-2</a> · the statutory disparate-impact standard that an executive order cannot remove\n- <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/15/45\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">FTC Act section 5, 15 U.S.C. 45</a> · unfair or deceptive acts or practices, the basis for AI deception enforcement\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/C/2025/5423/oj\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AI Liability Directive withdrawal, OJ C/2025/5423</a> · the fault-based proposal was formally withdrawn on 6 October 2025\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Sectoral and Civil Laws\n\n## Performance Indicators\n\nBOK v2.1 (approved 9 Sep 2025, effective 2 Feb 2026) lists four indicators under competency II.B, *understand how other types of existing laws apply to AI*:\n\n- Understand how **intellectual property laws** apply to AI (e.g., prohibiting or limiting use of data for AI training)\n- Understand how **nondiscrimination laws** apply to AI (e.g., in the employment, credit, lending, housing and insurance contexts)\n- Understand how **consumer protection laws** apply to AI (e.g., prohibiting unfair and deceptive acts or practices)\n- Understand how **product liability laws** apply to AI (e.g., prohibiting design and manufacturing defects)\n\n> **Version note:** II.B is substantively unchanged from v2.0.1; v2.1 only tidies the wording (\"nondiscrimination\", \"design and manufacturing defects\"). The task *identify laws that apply to the AI model* was never a II.B indicator: v2.0.1 listed it under III.A and IV.B, and v2.1 dropped it.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n> [!source] Sectoral and Civil Laws\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n1. **Regulations have not caught up to AI's paradigms.** Existing regulations and case law are challenged by AI capabilities. Organizations should err on the side of caution and conduct rigorous diligence.\n\n2. **Understand how IP laws affect AI training and outputs.** AI systems trained on copyrighted content may infringe IP rights. In the US, fair use is decided case by case and courts have split (*Bartz*, *Kadrey*, *Ross*); in the EU, commercial training relies on the DSM Art. 4 exception and must honour machine-readable opt-outs, and GPAI providers owe a copyright policy and training summary (AI Act Art. 53). Secure licenses, document provenance, and never rely on pirated sources. Only humans can be authors (*Thaler*, cert. denied 2026).\n\n3. **Recognize how non-discrimination laws apply to AI in regulated sectors.** Title VII, ECOA, and FHA prohibit intentional and unintentional discrimination with disparate impact. Federal agencies pulled back in 2025-2026 (EEOC guidance removed, EO 14281, the CFPB's Reg B rule effective 21 Jul 2026, HUD's proposal), but Title VII §703(k), FHA disparate impact and private suits remain. Audit for bias and proxy variables tied to protected traits, and keep adverse action reasons specific.\n\n4. **Identify consumer protection risks posed by AI deception or opacity.** Fake reviews, undisclosed AI, misleading claims, and dark patterns violate FTC Act and state UDAP laws. Be transparent, substantiate claims, provide clear information. The FTC set aside the Rytr order in Dec 2025 but still pursues deceptive AI claims (Workado, DoNotPay).\n\n5. **Apply product liability principles to AI-driven physical systems.** Design defect, manufacturing defect, and failure to warn apply. AI's adaptive behavior complicates causation — requiring rigorous testing, version tracking, and post-market monitoring. In the EU, software and AI are products under Directive (EU) 2024/2853 (transpose by 9 Dec 2026), with presumptions that ease proof; the AI Liability Directive was withdrawn.\n\n6. **Integrate legal compliance into the AI lifecycle.** Embed legal review, bias mitigation, documentation, and human oversight throughout development, especially for systems affecting rights, safety, or access to critical services.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n> [!source] Framework Recall Cards — AIGP Study Kit\n\n## US and sectoral law\n\n*Outside the usual privacy lane — a likely gap.*\n\n### The anti-discrimination statutes that bite on AI, and the doctrine that matters\n\n**Title VII** (employment), **ECOA/Reg B** (credit, plus adverse action notices), **Fair Housing Act** (housing), **ADA** (disability), **FCRA** (consumer reports, accuracy, adverse action).\n\n**Disparate treatment** = intentional. **Disparate impact** = facially neutral practice, discriminatory effect, **no intent required**. Proxy variables (postal code for race) are the classic AI fact pattern — excluding the protected attribute does not cure it.\n\n### How the FTC reaches AI\n\n**FTC Act §5** — unfair or deceptive acts or practices. Applied to overstated AI capability claims, undisclosed automated decision-making, and biased outcomes. Remedy to know: **algorithmic disgorgement** — ordering deletion of models and algorithms built on improperly obtained data.\n\n### Product liability theories applied to AI\n\n**Design defect** — the design itself is unreasonably dangerous.\n**Manufacturing defect** — the product deviates from its intended design (e.g. a botched update or corrupted config); strict liability can attach even though the design was sound.\n**Failure to warn** — inadequate instructions or risk disclosure.\nModernized regimes increasingly bring software and updates within scope.\n\n### Copyright and the four fair-use factors\n\n1. Purpose and character (is it **transformative**? commercial?)\n2. Nature of the work\n3. Amount and substantiality used\n4. **Effect on the market** for the original\n\nFactors 1 and 4 carry the most weight in AI disputes. **Public availability confers no licence.** Outputs substantially similar to protected expression undercut the defense. Also: US patent law requires a **human inventor**.\n\n### State and city AI laws worth recognizing by name\n\n**NYC Local Law 144** — the one the study guide teaches directly: **annual independent bias audits** of automated employment decision tools, **publication of audit results**, and **notice to candidates**. First of its kind in the US.\n**Illinois BIPA** (biometric consent) and the AI Video Interview Act.\n**California CCPA/CPRA**, including automated decision-making technology rules.\n**Colorado SB21-169** — insurance; requires insurers to show models do not produce unfairly discriminatory outcomes.\nSeveral states (California, Texas, New York) target malicious **deepfakes** in political and pornographic content.\n\n> [!warning] **Don't rely on the Colorado AI Act (SB 24-205).** It was repealed before ever taking effect — Governor Polis signed SB 26-189, the Automated Decision-Making Technology Act, on 14 May 2026, replacing the duty of care, impact assessments and high-risk classification with a narrower disclosure regime effective 1 January 2027. The study guide never covered SB 24-205 anyway, so it is unlikely to be tested; know NYC Local Law 144 instead.", "t_ip": "## At a Glance\n\nIP law meets AI at three points: inputs (training on copyrighted works, tested under US fair use and the EU text and data mining exceptions), the model itself (whether weights contain copies of the works), and outputs (whether generated content infringes, and whether it can be owned). Trade secrets protect the model only while it is genuinely secret.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\n### The three touchpoints\n\nInputs, the model, and outputs. Each raises a different question: was copying the works to train lawful; does a trained model contain copies; does an output infringe or attract copyright of its own.\n\n### Fair use in the US\n\nTraining is tested under the four fair-use factors: purpose and character (transformative or duplicative), nature of the work, amount used, and effect on the market (including dilution by competing output). District courts split in 2025: training was fair use on the records in **Bartz v. Anthropic** and **Kadrey v. Meta** (both June 2025), and not fair use in **Thomson Reuters v. Ross** (February 2025), where the model was trained on a competitor's headnotes to build a market substitute. No US appellate court had ruled as of September 2026; the Ross appeal was argued in the Third Circuit on 11 June 2026.\n\nTwo facts decide cases. **Acquisition**: Bartz refused to excuse a permanent central library of pirated books, and the case settled for USD 1.5 billion (final approval 20 July 2026). **Market harm**: Kadrey won only because the plaintiffs offered no dilution evidence, so the ruling is not a safe harbour.\n\n### Human authorship\n\n*Thaler v. Perlmutter* (D.C. Cir., 18 March 2025; cert. denied 2 March 2026) holds the Copyright Act requires a work authored in the first instance by a human being, so an AI system cannot be the author. Prompts alone are not enough, but human selection, arrangement and modification of AI output can be protected (Copyright Office Part 2, January 2025). Applicants must disclose AI-generated content and exclude more-than-de-minimis AI material.\n\n### The EU: TDM, not fair use\n\nThere is no fair use in the EU. The DSM Directive gives two text and data mining exceptions: **Article 3** for research organisations and cultural heritage institutions, with no opt-out (contract terms overriding it are unenforceable), and **Article 4** for anyone with lawful access, for any purpose including commercial training, but only if the rightholder has not reserved the right \"in an appropriate manner, such as machine-readable means\" (Article 4(3)). GPAI providers must honour those reservations and publish a public, sufficiently detailed training-content summary (AI Act Article 53(1)(c)-(d)), and recital 106 makes that apply regardless of where training happened; open-source models are not exempt from those two points.\n\n### Does the model contain copies?\n\nThe courts disagree, and both leading decisions are on appeal. *Getty v. Stability AI* (UK High Court, [2025] EWHC 2863 (Ch), 4 November 2025) held that a model which does not store or reproduce the works is not an \"infringing copy\", and Getty dropped its training and outputs claims. *GEMA v. OpenAI* (Regional Court Munich I, 11 November 2025) held that lyrics memorised in the model were reproductions in the model and in outputs, that the TDM exception covers building the training corpus and not memorisation, and that the operator (not the user) is responsible for outputs from simple prompts. Copyright is territorial, so where training happened matters.\n\n### Trade secrets\n\nTrade secret law protects proprietary datasets, architecture, hyperparameters, fine-tuning methods and prompts, but only while they are genuinely secret. Transparency duties collide with secrecy: the AI Act's training-content summary is meant to be comprehensive in scope rather than technically detailed, taking trade secrets into account.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nExpect scenario items that turn on one fact: how the data was acquired (Bartz), whether the plaintiff proved market harm (Kadrey), whether the use built a competing substitute (Ross), where training happened (Getty), or whether the model memorised (GEMA). Watch the status traps: Thaler is final, Bartz is settled, and Ross, Getty and GEMA are on appeal. Remember that fair use and the EU's TDM exceptions are different regimes: a US developer that places a GPAI model on the EU market must still honour Article 4(3) opt-outs.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- US fair use is decided case by case: training was held fair on the records in Bartz and Kadrey (June 2025) and rejected in Thomson Reuters v. Ross (February 2025); the Ross appeal, argued in the Third Circuit on 11 June 2026, is undecided\n- Acquisition matters: Bartz refused to excuse a central library of pirated books, and the case settled for USD 1.5 billion (final approval 20 July 2026)\n- Human authorship is required: Thaler v. Perlmutter (D.C. Cir. 2025, cert. denied 2 March 2026); prompts alone are not enough, but human selection, arrangement and edits are protectable (Copyright Office Part 2)\n- EU: DSM Article 3 (research organisations and heritage institutions, no opt-out) versus Article 4 (anyone, subject to machine-readable opt-outs for online content)\n- AI Act Article 53(1)(c)-(d): GPAI providers need a copyright policy honouring Article 4(3) opt-outs and a public training-content summary, wherever training happened and even for open-source models\n- Models as copies: Getty v. Stability AI (UK, November 2025) held weights that store no works are not an infringing copy; GEMA v. OpenAI (Munich, November 2025) held memorised lyrics were reproductions outside the TDM exception; both are on appeal\n- Trade secrets protect models, weights and prompts only while they are genuinely kept secret\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2019/790/oj\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Directive (EU) 2019/790 (DSM Directive)</a> · the Article 3 and Article 4 text and data mining exceptions and the Article 4(3) opt-out\n- <a href=\"https://www.copyright.gov/ai/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">US Copyright Office, Copyright and Artificial Intelligence</a> · Parts 1 to 3 on digital replicas, copyrightability and generative AI training\n- <a href=\"https://media.cadc.uscourts.gov/opinions/docs/2025/03/23-5233.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Thaler v. Perlmutter, D.C. Cir. opinion (18 March 2025)</a> · the human authorship requirement; certiorari denied 2 March 2026\n- <a href=\"https://www.judiciary.uk/judgments/getty-images-v-stability-ai/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Getty Images v. Stability AI [2025] EWHC 2863 (Ch)</a> · model weights that store no works are not an infringing copy; on appeal\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1689/oj\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 (EU AI Act), Article 53</a> · the GPAI copyright policy and training-content summary duties\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Sectoral and Civil Laws\n\n## 1. Intellectual Property Laws and AI\n\n### 1.1 Copyright Law and AI Training\n\n**Copyright** protects \"original works of authorship\" fixed in a tangible medium (text, music, images, video, software code).\n\n| Key Question | Relevance |\n|-------------|-----------|\n| Is training on copyrighted works a violation of the reproduction right? | Core legal debate |\n| Does training constitute \"transformative\" use under fair use? | Primary developer defense |\n| Are AI-generated outputs infringing if they resemble copyrighted works? | Emerging litigation focus |\n| Does a trained model itself \"contain\" copies of the works? | Split between UK and German courts (2025) |\n\n> **Where the cases stand:** see the dated tracker in §1.2. As of Sep 2026 no US appellate court has ruled on training and fair use.\n\n#### Fair Use Doctrine (U.S.)\n\nCourts weigh four factors (17 U.S.C. §107):\n\n| Factor | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| Purpose and character of use | Commercial vs. non-profit; transformative vs. duplicative |\n| Nature of the copyrighted work | Factual vs. creative |\n| Amount and substantiality used | How much of the original was used |\n| Effect on the market | Does the use compete with the original? Includes market dilution by AI output of the same kind |\n\n> **Exam note:** Developers argue training is transformative because models learn patterns rather than keep copies. As of 2026 district courts have split: training was fair use on the records in *Bartz* and *Kadrey*, but not in *Thomson Reuters v. Ross*. Factor one (transformativeness) and factor four (market harm) decide the cases, and how the data was acquired can decide the exposure.\n\n#### Training Data Dilemma\n\n- AI systems scrape massive datasets from websites, forums, art repositories, codebases\n- **GitHub Copilot litigation (2022):** Class-action alleged Copilot reproduced open-source code without proper attribution, violating licenses like GPL\n\n#### Licensing Models for Training Data\n\n| Approach | Example |\n|----------|---------|\n| Public domain content | U.S. government data, Project Gutenberg |\n| Creative Commons (CC) licenses | Reuse with conditions |\n| Paid licensing agreements | Shutterstock licensing images for training |\n| Synthetic data generation | Reduces dependency on real-world datasets |\n\n### 1.2 AI Copyright Case Tracker (as of 23 Sep 2026)\n\n| Case | Court and date | Holding or status | Governance lesson |\n|------|----------------|-------------------|-------------------|\n| **Thaler v. Perlmutter** | D.C. Cir., 18 Mar 2025; Supreme Court denied cert. 2 Mar 2026 (No. 25-449) | The Copyright Act of 1976 requires a work to be authored \"in the first instance by a human being\"; an AI system cannot be the author. The work-made-for-hire argument failed: that provision transfers authorship of a human-created work, it does not make machines authors | Record the human contribution to AI-assisted works; never list the AI as author |\n| **Thomson Reuters v. Ross Intelligence** | D. Del. (Bibas J., sitting by designation), 11 Feb 2025; 3d Cir. argued 11 Jun 2026, **pending** | Copying Westlaw headnotes to train a competing, **non-generative** legal search tool was not fair use: factors one and four favoured Thomson Reuters. First federal appeal on AI training and fair use | Training a direct market substitute on a competitor's content is the highest-risk pattern; license it |\n| **Bartz v. Anthropic** | N.D. Cal. (Alsup J.), 23 Jun 2025; settlement final approval 20 Jul 2026 (Martínez-Olguín J.) | Training on the books and scanning purchased print books into digital copies were fair use; downloading pirated copies to build a permanent central library was not (on the summary judgment record). **$1.5 billion** non-reversionary settlement covering about 482,000 works | Provenance and acquisition drive exposure: audit sources and purge pirated or shadow-library data |\n| **Kadrey v. Meta** | N.D. Cal. (Chhabria J.), 25 Jun 2025 | Summary judgment for Meta on training, but only because the 13 plaintiffs offered no evidence of market dilution; the ruling \"does not stand for the proposition\" that Meta's training was lawful. Torrent-distribution claims continue (leave to add a contributory infringement claim granted 25 Mar 2026) | A win on a thin record is not a safe harbour; assess market-dilution risk before training |\n| **NYT v. Microsoft & OpenAI** | S.D.N.Y. (Stein J.), filed Dec 2023; centralised in *In re OpenAI, Inc. Copyright Infringement Litigation*, MDL No. 3143 (Apr 2025) | **Pending**: summary judgment briefing under way in Sep 2026. Covers both training and regurgitated outputs | Output controls against memorisation and regurgitation matter as much as input licensing |\n| **Getty Images v. Stability AI** | UK High Court (Joanna Smith J.), 4 Nov 2025, **[2025] EWHC 2863 (Ch)** | Getty dropped its training claim (no evidence training occurred in the UK) and its outputs claim. The secondary infringement claim failed: an \"article\" can be intangible, but a model that does not store or reproduce the works, and never has, is not an \"infringing copy\". Limited, historic trade mark wins over Getty/iStock watermarks in older model versions. On 16 Dec 2025 ([2025] EWHC 3343 (Ch)) the judge gave Getty permission to appeal the secondary infringement ruling and refused Stability permission on the trade mark findings | Copyright is territorial: where training happens matters. Watermark regurgitation is a trade mark risk |\n| **GEMA v. OpenAI** | Regional Court Munich I, 11 Nov 2025, 42 O 14139/24 | Lyrics of nine German songs memorised in GPT-4 and GPT-4o were reproductions **in the model** and in outputs. The TDM exception covers copies made to build the training corpus, not memorisation. OpenAI, not its users, is responsible for outputs from simple prompts. Not final: OpenAI has appealed to the Higher Regional Court of Munich | Test for memorisation; in the EU a model that can regurgitate works may itself hold copies |\n\n> **Getty vs GEMA:** the UK court treated model weights as not containing the works; the Munich court treated memorised works as reproduced in the parameters. Both are on appeal as of 2026. A first CJEU reference on generative AI and copyright, *Like Company v. Google Ireland* (C-250/25), was heard on 10 Mar 2026; judgment pending.\n\n### 1.3 US Copyright Office AI Reports\n\n| Part | Date | Key conclusions |\n|------|------|-----------------|\n| **Part 1: Digital Replicas** | 31 Jul 2024 | Recommends a federal law against unauthorised digital replicas that realistically but falsely depict a person |\n| **Part 2: Copyrightability** | 29 Jan 2025 | Existing law is flexible enough. Outputs are protected only where a human author determined sufficient expressive elements: human work perceptible in the output, or creative selection, arrangement or modification. **Prompts alone are not enough.** AI assistance does not bar protection of the human contribution. No new rights for AI output are needed |\n| **Part 3: Generative AI Training** | 9 May 2025 (pre-publication version; final version to follow with no substantive changes expected) | Several stages of training implicate exclusive rights, so the key question is fair use, decided case by case. Many training uses are likely transformative, but commercial use of vast troves of works to produce expressive content that competes with them, especially through illegal access, \"goes beyond established fair use boundaries\". Factor four should not be read narrowly: market dilution counts. Voluntary licensing should develop; government intervention is premature |\n\n> **Registration guidance (16 Mar 2023, 88 FR 16190):** applicants have a duty to disclose AI-generated content and briefly explain the human author's contribution; more-than-de-minimis AI material must be excluded from the claim. Applications that omitted it should be corrected so the registration remains effective.\n\n### 1.4 EU Text and Data Mining Exception and the AI Act\n\nThe DSM Directive (EU) 2019/790 (transposition deadline 7 Jun 2021) defines text and data mining as any automated technique that analyses digital text and data to generate information such as patterns, trends and correlations (Art. 2(2)).\n\n| Provision | Who benefits or is bound | Opt-out? | Notes |\n|-----------|--------------------------|----------|-------|\n| **DSM Art. 3** | Research organisations and cultural heritage institutions, mining for scientific research, with lawful access | **No.** Contract terms overriding it are unenforceable (Art. 7(1)) | Copies stored with appropriate security; may be retained for research, including verification |\n| **DSM Art. 4** | Anyone with lawful access, for any purpose including commercial training | **Yes.** Applies only if the use has not been \"expressly reserved by their rightholders in an appropriate manner, such as machine-readable means in the case of content made publicly available online\" (Art. 4(3)) | Copies kept as long as necessary for TDM. Recital 18: machine-readable means include metadata and a website's terms and conditions |\n| **AI Act Art. 53(1)(c)** | Providers of general-purpose AI models | Must honour Art. 4(3) reservations | A policy to comply with EU copyright law, identifying and complying with reservations \"including through state-of-the-art technologies\". Recital 106: applies regardless of where the training took place |\n| **AI Act Art. 53(1)(d)** | Providers of general-purpose AI models | — | A public, sufficiently detailed summary of training content, using the AI Office template (published 24 Jul 2025) |\n\n- GPAI obligations (Chapter V) have applied since 2 Aug 2025; the Commission's fining powers under Art. 101 apply from 2 Aug 2026.\n- **Open-source models are not exempt** from (c) and (d): Art. 53(2) lifts only points (a) and (b), and not at all for models with systemic risk.\n- The **GPAI Code of Practice** copyright chapter commits signatories to respect robots.txt and other appropriate machine-readable protocols. The Commission consulted on opt-out protocols from 1 Dec 2025 to 23 Jan 2026 and will publish a list of generally agreed machine-readable solutions.\n- The 2026 Digital Omnibus on AI (Regulation (EU) 2026/1744) did not change Art. 53(1)(c)-(d).\n\n> **Fair use vs TDM:** fair use is a US defence weighed case by case; the EU has no fair use, only the Art. 3 and Art. 4 exceptions. A US developer that places a GPAI model on the EU market must still honour EU opt-outs.\n\n### 1.5 Patent Law and AI-Generated Inventions\n\n**Patent law** protects new, useful, and non-obvious inventions.\n\n| Issue | Status |\n|-------|--------|\n| Use of patented data/algorithms in training | Requires license consideration |\n| Patentability of AI-generated inventions | **Must have human inventor** |\n\n> **Key Case: Thaler v. Vidal (2022)** — U.S. Federal Circuit held the Patent Act requires a **human inventor**, rejecting DABUS AI as named inventor. Most patent offices (USPTO, EPO) agree; only South Africa granted a DABUS patent.\n\n> **[[Can AI Be an Inventor?]]** — Globally, courts and patent offices reject AI as inventor. Only natural persons qualify under current law.\n\n### 1.6 Trade Secrets and AI Models\n\n**Trade secret** — confidential business information deriving economic value from not being generally known.\n\n**AI-related trade secrets include:**\n- Proprietary training datasets\n- Model architecture and hyperparameters\n- Fine-tuning methodologies\n- Inference optimization algorithms\n- Data preprocessing and augmentation techniques\n- Deployment infrastructure and scaling methods\n\n| Challenge | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| Secrecy in collaborative environments | Multiple parties accessing datasets |\n| Reverse-engineering from outputs | Outputs may reveal training data/algorithms |\n| Transparency vs. secrecy | Ethical review requires visibility |\n| Supply chain exposure | Third-party vendors handling sensitive data |\n\n> **Example:** OpenAI shifted from publishing GPT models openly to proprietary GPT-4, citing safety and competitiveness — also shielding against IP infringement claims.\n\n> **Transparency duties vs secrecy:** the AI Act's training-content summary is meant to be \"generally comprehensive in its scope instead of technically detailed\", taking trade secrets into account (recital 107).\n\n### 1.7 Database Rights (EU)\n\nUnder the **EU Database Directive (96/9/EC)**, *sui generis* database rights protect substantial investment in compiling data.\n\n- Rights holders can **prohibit extraction or reuse** of substantial parts\n- AI developers may need licenses even for non-copyrightable content\n- Relevant for structured datasets: medical records, scientific databases, financial archives\n- The DSM Art. 3 and Art. 4 TDM exceptions also cover extraction from protected databases\n\n### 1.8 AI Outputs and Infringement\n\nTwo factors determine if output infringes:\n1. **Similarity** to protected work (and extent)\n2. **Access** — did the AI train on the original?\n\n| Liability Question | Potential Answer |\n|--------------------|-----------------|\n| Who is liable for infringing output? | Developer, trainer, end user, or model itself |\n| Prevailing theories | Vicarious infringement, contributory liability |\n\n> **Andersen v. Stability AI (filed 2023):** Artists allege Stable Diffusion creates derivative works mimicking their style. \"In the style of [artist]\" prompts may produce infringing content.\n\n> **Output responsibility:** in *GEMA v. OpenAI* (2025) the Munich court held the model operator, not the user, responsible for outputs that reproduced lyrics in response to simple prompts. *NYT v. OpenAI* tests regurgitation in the US (pending).\n\n### 1.9 Ownership of AI-Generated Content\n\n**U.S. position (as of 2026):**\n- Only works with **human authorship** are protected: *Thaler v. Perlmutter* (D.C. Cir. 2025; cert. denied 2 Mar 2026)\n- The human authorship requirement does not bar works made **with the assistance of** AI; it denies authorship to the machine (D.C. Cir.)\n- Prompts alone do not give enough control; human selection, arrangement and modification can be protected (Copyright Office, Part 2, Jan 2025)\n- Applicants **must disclose** AI-generated content and exclude more-than-de-minimis AI material (registration guidance, Mar 2023)\n\n| Use Type | Copyright Eligibility |\n|----------|----------------------|\n| **Tool-based use** — human guides and edits output | Eligible |\n| **Autonomous generation** — minimal human input | Not eligible |\n\n> **Zarya of the Dawn (2023):** Copyright Office revoked protection for Midjourney-generated images; human-written text retained protection.\n\n### 1.10 Cross-border IP Complexities\n\nIP laws are **territorial** — granted and enforced by individual jurisdictions.\n\n| Jurisdiction | Key Distinction |\n|-------------|-----------------|\n| **United States** | Fair use doctrine, decided case by case; no copyright for purely AI-generated works |\n| **European Union** | DSM Art. 3 (research, no opt-out) and Art. 4 (commercial, subject to machine-readable opt-outs); database rights; AI Act Art. 53 copyright policy and training summary for GPAI providers wherever training occurred |\n| **United Kingdom** | *Getty v. Stability AI* (2025): training claim failed for lack of UK training; model weights held not to be an \"infringing copy\" (on appeal) |\n| **Germany** | *GEMA v. OpenAI* (2025): memorisation in a model is reproduction; TDM exception covers corpus-building only (on appeal) |\n| **China** | Recognizes copyright in AI-generated content (under human guidance) |\n| **Japan** | Broad TDM for any purpose including commercial |\n| **Canada/Australia** | Case-by-case; some openness to broader training exceptions |\n\n> **Forum shopping** — plaintiffs seek favorable jurisdictions; organizations face multi-jurisdictional claims, conflicts of law, and extraterritorial enforcement. The AI Act's recital 106 deliberately removes the \"train abroad\" escape for GPAI models sold in the EU.\n\n### 1.11 IP Risk Mitigation Strategies\n\n**IP-Aware AI Governance Framework:**\n- Due diligence for training data sources, including how each dataset was acquired (purchased, licensed, scraped, pirated)\n- Review of licensing obligations (open-source, CC)\n- Honour machine-readable TDM opt-outs (robots.txt and other protocols) for any model offered in the EU\n- Memorisation and regurgitation testing before release\n- Controls over model output usage\n- Recordkeeping for data provenance and model lineage\n- Codified policies\n\n> **Best Practice: Data Provenance Logs** — Track origin, usage rights, and attribution requirements for each dataset. (See [[AI System Lifecycle and Governance]])\n\n**Contractual Protections (AIaaS):**\n- Clear allocation of liability between vendor and customer\n- Indemnification clauses for third-party IP claims\n- Usage restrictions\n- Representations and warranties regarding training data\n\n**Sample AI IP Risk Review Checklist:**\n- Were all training datasets legally acquired or licensed? Are any from shadow libraries or pirate sites?\n- Were open-source licenses respected (GPL, MIT, CC-BY)?\n- Were EU rights reservations (Art. 4(3) opt-outs) identified and honoured?\n- Does the model memorise or reproduce content resembling known copyrighted works?\n- If we provide a GPAI model in the EU, is the Art. 53(1)(d) training summary published?\n- Are users warned against improper use of outputs?\n- Is the organization prepared to respond to takedown or litigation?\n\n### 1.12 Where AI IP Regulation Is Heading\n\n**United States:**\n- No federal statute specifically governs AI training data (as of Sep 2026); the question is being settled in court\n- The Copyright Office favours voluntary licensing and calls government intervention premature (Part 3), and recommends a federal digital-replica law (Part 1)\n- Proposals seen in Congress: require developers to document data sources, restrict unauthorized use of copyrighted content for training, establish registries or licensing frameworks\n\n**European Union:**\n- AI Act Art. 53(1)(c)-(d) copyright policy and training summary, applying since 2 Aug 2025\n- Commission work on generally agreed machine-readable opt-out protocols\n- EU DSA and Copyright Directive — takedown mechanisms, platform liability\n\n> **Key cases to watch:** *Thomson Reuters v. Ross* (3d Cir.), *In re OpenAI* including *NYT* (S.D.N.Y.), *Getty v. Stability AI* (Court of Appeal), *GEMA v. OpenAI* (OLG Munich), *Like Company v. Google Ireland* (CJEU). Also filed and not re-verified here: Disney & Universal v. Midjourney, Andersen v. Stability AI, GitHub Copilot litigation.\n\n---", "t_nondiscrim": "## At a Glance\n\nAnti-discrimination law reaches AI through two theories: disparate treatment (intentional, including deliberate use of a proxy for a protected trait) and disparate impact (a neutral practice that disproportionately harms a protected group, with no intent required). The sector hooks are Title VII and the ADA in employment, ECOA and FCRA in credit, the Fair Housing Act in housing, and state law in insurance.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\n### Two theories\n\n**Disparate treatment** is intentional discrimination, including the deliberate use of a variable that stands in for a protected class. **Disparate impact** needs no intent: a facially neutral practice that disproportionately harms a protected group is unlawful unless it is justified. AI produces disparate impact readily, through biased training data, proxy variables, feedback loops and sampling bias.\n\n### The Title VII standard\n\nTitle VII section 703(k) (42 U.S.C. 2000e-2(k)) writes the disparate-impact standard into the statute: the plaintiff shows a particular practice causes the impact, the employer must then show the practice is job-related and consistent with business necessity, and the plaintiff can still win by showing a less discriminatory alternative the employer refuses to adopt. Because the standard is in the statute, an executive order or withdrawn agency guidance cannot remove it. Employers are responsible for the selection procedures they use even if a third-party vendor built the tool, and vendor disclaimers do not shield liability.\n\n### Credit and lending\n\nThe **Equal Credit Opportunity Act** prohibits discrimination in credit on protected bases. Two AI-facing duties: no intentional discrimination or deliberate proxy use, and specific **principal reasons for adverse action** (12 CFR 1002.9), which means explainability. Saying an applicant \"failed to achieve a qualifying score\" is not enough; the reasons must relate to and accurately describe the factors actually considered. The **Fair Credit Reporting Act** governs third-party consumer data: a communication bearing on creditworthiness used for credit, insurance or employment can be a consumer report, and adverse action based on one triggers notice, the credit score, the reporting agency's details and dispute rights. A CFPB final rule effective **21 July 2026** states that ECOA does not provide for the effects test, so federal ECOA disparate-impact liability is gone; intentional discrimination and deliberate proxies remain unlawful.\n\n### Housing\n\nThe **Fair Housing Act** prohibits discrimination in sale, rental and financing. It reaches discriminatory ad targeting (differential exposure), discriminatory impact, and failure to monitor third-party tools. The Supreme Court recognised FHA disparate impact in *Texas Dept. of Housing and Community Affairs v. Inclusive Communities Project* (2015). HUD proposed on 14 January 2026 to remove its discriminatory-effects regulations and supplemented that on 10 August 2026, but neither is final, and the Act-based theory does not depend on HUD's rule. Mortgage lending is covered by both ECOA and the FHA, so the FHA route survives the 2026 Regulation B change.\n\n### Insurance\n\nNo single federal statute; states regulate. Colorado's SB21-169 was the first to require insurers to test AI for unfair discrimination and document governance. Proxy variables (postal code, education, occupation, credit history) are the recurring mechanism.\n\n### The 2025 to 2026 federal pullback\n\nThe EEOC removed its AI technical assistance on 27 January 2025; Executive Order 14281 (23 April 2025) directed agencies to minimise disparate-impact liability; the CFPB withdrew its AI circulars on 12 May 2025; and the Regulation B rule followed. The governance takeaway: federal agencies stepped back, but the statutes, private plaintiffs, states and the FHA route remain, so keep testing for disparate impact and documenting proxy review.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nScenario items name a sector and ask which statute applies: Title VII and the ADA in hiring, ECOA and Reg B in credit, FCRA for third-party scores, the FHA in housing and mortgage lending, state law in insurance. Know the three-step Title VII disparate-impact test and that it is statutory. A recurring trap is the 2025 to 2026 federal pullback: withdrawn guidance and an executive order do not repeal a statute, and ECOA's effects test being removed from Regulation B does not remove FHA disparate impact.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Disparate treatment is intentional (including deliberate proxy use); disparate impact needs no intent and is the theory AI most often triggers\n- Title VII section 703(k): plaintiff shows the impact, employer shows job-relatedness and business necessity, plaintiff can still win with a less discriminatory alternative\n- ECOA requires specific principal reasons for adverse action (12 CFR 1002.9), which forces explainability; a 'qualifying score' statement is insufficient\n- FCRA treats third-party algorithmic scores as consumer reports when used for credit, insurance or employment, triggering notices and dispute rights\n- Fair Housing Act disparate impact (Inclusive Communities, 2015) survives the 2026 Regulation B change and still covers mortgage lending\n- Proxy variables (postal code, credit history, education, occupation) are the recurring mechanism and must be tested and justified\n- 2025 to 2026 pullback: EEOC guidance removed, Executive Order 14281, CFPB circulars withdrawn, Regulation B effects test removed - but the statutes, states and private plaintiffs remain\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/2000e-2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Title VII section 703(k), 42 U.S.C. 2000e-2</a> · the statutory disparate-impact standard that an executive order cannot remove\n- <a href=\"https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/04/28/2025-07378/restoring-equality-of-opportunity-and-meritocracy\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Executive Order 14281 (90 FR 17537)</a> · the April 2025 order to deprioritise disparate-impact enforcement\n- <a href=\"https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/04/22/2026-07804/equal-credit-opportunity-act-regulation-b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CFPB Regulation B final rule (91 FR 21620)</a> · effective 21 July 2026: ECOA does not provide for the effects test\n- <a href=\"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/compliance/guidance/withdrawn-guidance/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CFPB withdrawn guidance</a> · the AI adverse-action and algorithmic-score circulars withdrawn on 12 May 2025\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Sectoral and Civil Laws\n\n## 2. Non-Discrimination Laws and AI\n\n### 2.1 Legal Foundations\n\n**Protected classes** in U.S. civil rights laws: race, color, national origin, sex (including gender identity/sexual orientation), pregnancy, religion, disability, age, veteran status, genetic traits, familial status.\n\n| Theory | Definition |\n|--------|------------|\n| **Disparate treatment** | Intentional discrimination based on protected class |\n| **Disparate impact** | Neutral policies that disproportionately harm protected groups; **no intent required** |\n\n> **Disparate Impact 101 (Title VII §703(k), 42 U.S.C. 2000e-2(k)):** the plaintiff shows that a particular practice causes a disparate impact; the employer must then show the practice is job related and consistent with business necessity; the plaintiff can still win by showing a less discriminatory alternative the employer refuses to adopt. Central to algorithmic bias cases. Because the standard is written into the statute, agency guidance or an executive order cannot remove it (§2.7).\n\n#### Sources of Algorithmic Bias\n\n| Source | Example |\n|--------|---------|\n| Biased training data | Historical decisions reflecting systemic discrimination |\n| Proxy variables | Postal codes correlating with race |\n| Opaque models | Black-box models masking bias |\n| Feedback loops | Reinforcing existing inequality |\n| Sampling bias | Under/overrepresentation of certain groups |\n\n### 2.2 Employment: AI in Hiring, Promotion, and Termination\n\n**Key laws:** Title VII of Civil Rights Act, ADA, ADEA\n\n**Title VII rule:** Selection procedures causing disparate impact are unlawful unless:\n- Job-related and consistent with business necessity, AND\n- No less discriminatory alternative is available\n\n> **Amazon résumé screening tool (2018):** Abandoned after penalizing women's college graduates; trained on predominantly male résumés — classic **proxy discrimination**.\n\n#### EEOC Technical Assistance (2022-2023), Removed January 2025\n\nThe EEOC's May 2022 ADA and May 2023 Title VII technical assistance on AI was **removed from its website on 27 Jan 2025**. Guidance never created the law; the points below rest on the statutes and still apply:\n\n| Key Takeaway (still good law) |\n|-------------|\n| Employers are responsible for the selection procedures they use, even if **third-party vendors** develop the tools |\n| Vendor disclaimers do **not shield liability** |\n| The ADA bars selection criteria that screen out qualified people with disabilities unless job related and consistent with business necessity (42 U.S.C. 12112(b)(6)); reasonable accommodations still apply to algorithmic assessments |\n| Employers should audit and document algorithmic decision-making |\n\n> **NYC Law 144:** Requires bias audits of automated employment decision tools, annual publication of audit results, and disclosures to candidates. First of its kind in U.S. See [[US AI Laws — Federal and State]] for state and local AI hiring rules.\n\n### 2.3 Credit and Lending: ECOA and FCRA\n\n#### Equal Credit Opportunity Act (ECOA, 1974)\n\nProhibits discrimination in credit transactions based on race, sex, marital status, religion, national origin, age, or receipt of public assistance.\n\n| AI Requirement |\n|---------------|\n| No intentional discrimination (disparate treatment), including deliberate use of prohibited-basis proxies |\n| Specific principal reasons for adverse action (AI **explainability**), 12 CFR 1002.9 |\n| Fair access across protected groups |\n| **Disparate impact:** since **21 Jul 2026** Regulation B states that ECOA does not provide for the \"effects test\"; for housing-related credit the Fair Housing Act still supports disparate-impact claims (§2.4) |\n\n**Adverse action notice (Reg B §1002.9):** notice generally within 30 days of a completed application; the statement of reasons must be specific and give the principal reasons. Citing internal standards, or saying the applicant \"failed to achieve a qualifying score\" on the creditor's scoring system, is insufficient. The official commentary requires reasons that relate to and accurately describe the factors actually considered or scored, and notes that more than four reasons is unlikely to help.\n\n> **CFPB circulars, withdrawn 12 May 2025:** Circular 2022-03 (adverse action and complex algorithms) said \"black box\" AI does not excuse lenders from giving specific reasons; Circular 2023-03 covered adverse action notices and the proper use of sample forms. The withdrawal removed guidance, not the rule: §1002.9(b)(2) is unchanged.\n\n#### Fair Credit Reporting Act (FCRA, 1970)\n\nGoverns use of credit scores and third-party data. AI systems ingesting such data may qualify as **consumer reporting agencies (CRAs)** .\n\n| Requirement |\n|-------------|\n| Accuracy and integrity of consumer data |\n| Disclosure to consumers of decisions and data sources |\n| Mechanisms to dispute errors |\n\n- A **consumer report** is any communication by a CRA bearing on creditworthiness, character, reputation, personal characteristics or mode of living, used for credit, insurance, employment or another permissible purpose (15 U.S.C. 1681a(d)). Third-party algorithmic scores can fit.\n- **Adverse action based on a consumer report** (15 U.S.C. 1681m): notice, the credit score used, the CRA's contact details, a statement that the CRA did not make the decision, and the right to a free report and to dispute.\n- CFPB Circular 2024-06 (background dossiers and algorithmic scores for employment decisions) was withdrawn on 12 May 2025; the statutory definitions are unchanged.\n\n| | FCRA | ECOA |\n|---|------|------|\n| **Focus** | Consumer data and reports | Discrimination in lending |\n| **Triggers** | Use of third-party consumer data | Protected class status |\n| **Key Requirement** | Accurate, disputable reports | Equal access and justifiable criteria |\n| **AI Implication** | Responsible data sourcing | Bias detection and justification logic |\n\n> **Apple Card/Goldman Sachs (2019):** Multiple customers (including Steve Wozniak) reported receiving significantly lower credit limits than spouses with similar profiles. NYDFS investigation into algorithmic fairness — no intent found, but highlighted opacity risks.\n\n### 2.4 Housing: Fair Housing Act (FHA, 1968)\n\n**Protected characteristics:** race, color, religion, sex, national origin, disability, familial status.\n\n#### Algorithmic Advertising and Targeting\n\n**Differential exposure** — AI causes users/groups to receive different information, even when decision logic treats all equally.\n\n> **HUD v. Facebook (2019):** Facebook enabled housing advertisers to exclude users by race, religion, gender, and postal code. Settled; new rules implemented for ad targeting.\n\n**FHA violations include:**\n- Discriminatory targeting (excluding users of specific ethnicity)\n- Discriminatory impact (algorithm limits access to specific groups)\n- Failure to monitor third-party tools\n\n#### Tenant Screening\n\nAI-powered tools using credit scores, criminal history, or eviction records may reflect systemic bias. Must meet accuracy, relevance, and non-discrimination standards under both FHA and FCRA.\n\n> **Disparate impact under the FHA:** recognised by the Supreme Court in *Texas Dept. of Housing and Community Affairs v. Inclusive Communities Project* (2015). HUD proposed on **14 Jan 2026** to remove its discriminatory-effects regulations and leave the standard to the courts, and on **10 Aug 2026** issued a supplemental proposal that also targets its Title VI disparate-impact provisions (comments due 9 Oct 2026). **Neither is final** as of Sep 2026, and the Act-based theory does not depend on HUD's rule. Mortgage lending is covered by both ECOA and the FHA, so the FHA route survives the 2026 Regulation B change.\n\n### 2.5 Insurance: State-level Regulation\n\nNo single federal anti-discrimination statute for insurance. Regulation primarily at **U.S. state level**.\n\nState laws typically prohibit unfair discrimination based on: race, religion, gender, disability, national/ethnic origin, veteran status, genetic information. Some states add sexual orientation, gender identity, occupation.\n\n| State | Action |\n|-------|--------|\n| California, Hawaii, Massachusetts | Ban/restrict credit scores in auto insurance |\n| Colorado SB21-169 | First state law requiring insurers to evaluate AI for unfair discrimination, submit bias testing documentation, implement governance policies |\n\n#### Proxy Variables in Insurance\n\n| Variable | Potential Proxy For |\n|----------|--------------------|\n| Postal code | Race, socioeconomic status, religion |\n| Education level | Income or nationality |\n| Occupation | Gender or class status |\n| Credit history | Race (via systemic inequality) |\n\n### 2.6 Non-Discrimination Risk Mitigation\n\n**Bias Audits — Key Elements:**\n- Statistical evaluation of training/test datasets\n- Performance assessment across demographic subgroups\n- Review of feature relevance and proxy risk\n- Documentation of audit methodologies and results\n- Ongoing post-deployment monitoring for bias drift\n\n**AI Model Bias Audit Checklist:**\n- Disparate impact metrics tested\n- Protected class proxies removed or justified\n- Business necessity for impactful features documented\n- Model outputs are explainable and reviewable\n\n**Vendor Contract Requirements:**\n- Conduct and share bias audit results\n- Disclose data sources and model documentation\n- Agree to indemnify for regulatory violations\n- Assist with explainability and adverse action notices\n\n> **Red flags that trigger legal scrutiny:** opaque models, unjustified use of proxies (credit history, postal code, education), inability to provide specific adverse action reasons, no disparate impact testing, denial of reasonable accommodations.\n\n### 2.7 Federal Enforcement Shifts, 2025-2026: What Changed and What Did Not\n\n| Date | Action | What changed | What still applies |\n|------|--------|--------------|--------------------|\n| 27 Jan 2025 | EEOC removes its AI technical assistance (May 2022 ADA; May 2023 Title VII) | No current EEOC guidance on AI hiring tools | Title VII §703(k), ADA, ADEA; employer liability for vendor tools; private suits |\n| 23 Apr 2025 | **Executive Order 14281**, *Restoring Equality of Opportunity and Meritocracy* (90 FR 17537) | Policy \"to eliminate the use of disparate-impact liability in all contexts to the maximum degree possible\"; agencies deprioritise enforcement, naming 42 U.S.C. 2000e-2 (sec. 4); AG and EEOC Chair review pending matters (sec. 6); Title VI effects rules revoked or targeted for repeal (secs. 3, 5) | An executive order cannot amend a statute; courts, states and private plaintiffs are not bound |\n| 12 May 2025 | CFPB withdraws dozens of guidance documents, including Circulars 2022-03, 2023-03 and 2024-06 | AI adverse action and algorithmic-score guidance gone | Reg B §1002.9 specific-reasons rule; FCRA §1681m notices and §1681a(d) definition |\n| Published 22 Apr 2026; effective **21 Jul 2026** | CFPB final rule amending Regulation B (91 FR 21620) | §1002.6(a) now states that ECOA does not provide for the \"effects test\", i.e. disparate-impact liability; the CFPB acted \"consistent with E.O. 14281\" | Disparate treatment and intentional proxy use remain unlawful; FHA disparate impact for housing credit; state fair-lending laws |\n| 14 Jan 2026; supplement 10 Aug 2026 | HUD proposes removing its FHA discriminatory-effects regulations (and Title VI disparate-impact provisions) | **Proposed only**, not final as of Sep 2026 | FHA disparate impact under *Inclusive Communities* (2015) |\n\n> **Governance takeaway:** federal agencies have pulled back from disparate impact, but the statutes and private suits still apply, and state and local AI rules keep growing. Continue disparate-impact testing, proxy review and documentation; the legal exposure has shifted toward private plaintiffs and states rather than disappeared.\n\n---", "t_consumer": "## At a Glance\n\nConsumer protection law reaches AI mainly through the FTC Act's ban on unfair or deceptive acts or practices (section 5) and the state 'mini-FTC Acts'. The AI-specific risks are deceptive claims about what AI can do, undisclosed AI interaction, dark patterns that personalise manipulation, deepfake impersonation, and AI-generated fake reviews.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\n### FTC Act section 5\n\nSection 5(a) declares unfair or deceptive acts or practices in or affecting commerce unlawful (15 U.S.C. 45(a)(1)). **Unfair** acts cause or are likely to cause substantial injury that is not reasonably avoidable and not outweighed by benefits (section 45(n)); **deceptive** acts mislead or are likely to mislead a reasonable consumer through material representations or omissions. No intent is required, which is what makes section 5 broader than fraud.\n\n### State UDAP laws\n\nEvery state has a mini-FTC Act: California's Unfair Competition Law (unlawful, unfair or fraudulent acts), Massachusetts 93A (triple damages for deceptive conduct), New York GBL 349. State attorneys general enforce alongside the FTC.\n\n### AI enforcement\n\nThe FTC's AI actions have targeted deception about capability. **Operation AI Comply** (September 2024) brought five actions, including DoNotPay's \"robot lawyer\" claims, and the FTC's line was that there is no AI exemption from the laws on the books. **Workado** (April 2025) was ordered to substantiate its claim that its AI content detector was 98% accurate. The FTC set aside its **Rytr** order on 22 December 2025, holding that the complaint did not support a section 5 violation and that the order burdened AI innovation, but it said it will still pursue AI used to deceive consumers. The 2026 posture targets fraud and tangible harm rather than tools that could be misused, while deception about AI remains actionable.\n\n### Dark patterns\n\nDark patterns are interface designs that trick, coerce or confuse: hidden opt-outs, confirm-shaming, deceptively labelled toggles, and AI-powered real-time A/B testing that reduces clarity. AI makes it worse by personalising manipulation at scale, adapting content to behavioural predictions.\n\n### Deepfakes and fake reviews\n\nUndisclosed AI interaction in legal, health, spiritual or financial contexts can be a material omission. Deepfake risks include fabricated endorsements, false evidence, impersonation scams and non-consensual intimate images; the US **Take It Down Act** (2025) criminalises distributing non-consensual intimate images including AI deepfakes. The FTC's **Consumer Reviews and Testimonials Rule** (final, August 2024) bans creating, selling or buying reviews that misrepresent that they come from someone who does not exist, such as AI-generated fake reviews, and lets the FTC seek civil penalties against knowing violators.\n\n### Compliance habits\n\nDisclose that a user is interacting with AI, prominently and in plain language, at the time of use; substantiate every performance claim; provide accessible explanations and a route to a human; and monitor for hallucinations, false positives and manipulative behaviour.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nThe consumer items are usually claim-substantiation and disclosure scenarios. If a company says its AI is 98% accurate without evidence, that is Workado: a deceptive claim. If a chatbot gives legal or medical advice without disclosure, that is a material omission. Remember that section 5 needs no intent, that the Rytr order was set aside on a theory point rather than because AI deception became lawful, and that the fake-reviews rule reaches AI-generated reviews.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- FTC Act section 5 bans unfair or deceptive acts or practices with no intent requirement; unfairness turns on substantial, unavoidable injury not outweighed by benefits\n- State UDAP laws (California UCL, Massachusetts 93A, New York GBL 349) parallel the FTC and add remedies such as triple damages\n- Substantiate AI performance claims: Workado (2025) and DoNotPay show the FTC will act on unproven accuracy and capability claims\n- The Rytr order was set aside in December 2025 on the theory that it condemned a tool for possible misuse, not because AI deception became lawful\n- Dark patterns and undisclosed AI interaction are material omissions; AI personalises manipulation at scale\n- The FTC Consumer Reviews and Testimonials Rule (2024) bans AI-generated fake reviews; the Take It Down Act (2025) criminalises non-consensual intimate deepfakes\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/15/45\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">FTC Act section 5, 15 U.S.C. 45</a> · the unfair-or-deceptive standard, with the unfairness test at section 45(n)\n- <a href=\"https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2024/09/ftc-announces-crackdown-deceptive-ai-claims-schemes\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">FTC, Operation AI Comply (25 September 2024)</a> · the crackdown on deceptive AI claims and schemes, including DoNotPay\n- <a href=\"https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2025/04/ftc-order-requires-workado-back-artificial-intelligence-detection-claims\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">FTC, Workado order (28 April 2025)</a> · unsubstantiated AI accuracy claims must be backed by evidence\n- <a href=\"https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2025/12/ftc-reopens-sets-aside-rytr-final-order-response-trump-administrations-ai-action-plan\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">FTC, Rytr order set aside (22 December 2025)</a> · the theory was set aside; deception about AI remains actionable\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Sectoral and Civil Laws\n\n## 3. Consumer Protection Laws and AI\n\n### 3.1 FTC Act Section 5\n\nSection 5(a) declares \"unfair or deceptive acts or practices in or affecting commerce\" unlawful (15 U.S.C. §45(a)(1)). The FTC may take action against:\n\n| Type | Definition |\n|------|------------|\n| **Unfair acts** | Cause or likely to cause substantial injury; not reasonably avoidable; not outweighed by benefits (§45(n)) |\n| **Deceptive acts** | Mislead or likely to mislead a reasonable consumer; material representations or omissions |\n\n| | Unfair or Deceptive Act | Fraud |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Liability** | Civil | Often criminal |\n| **Intent** | Not required | Required |\n| **Scope** | Broad application | Narrow, specific conduct |\n| **Enforcement** | FTC, states | DOJ, AGs, plaintiffs |\n\n### 3.2 State-Level UDAP Laws\n\nEvery U.S. state has \"mini-FTC Acts\":\n\n| State | Law |\n|-------|-----|\n| **California** | Unfair Competition Law (UCL) — prohibits unlawful, unfair, or fraudulent acts |\n| **Massachusetts** | Consumer Protection Law 93A — triple damages for deceptive conduct |\n| **New York** | GBL §349 — focuses on deceptive conduct |\n\n### 3.3 FTC's AI Enforcement Focus\n\n**FTC's Five AI Governance Questions:**\n1. Are AI datasets biased or incomplete?\n2. Do AI-powered decisions discriminate against protected groups?\n3. Can consumers opt out of automated decisions?\n4. Is the organization transparent about when and how AI is used?\n5. Can the organization explain and justify AI-powered decisions?\n\n**Key enforcement actions:**\n\n| Case | Issue | Outcome |\n|------|-------|---------|\n| **Everalbum (final May 2021)** | Facial recognition switched on by default despite promises; kept photos of deactivated users | Delete the photos and the models and algorithms built from them (algorithmic disgorgement); express consent for face recognition |\n| **Amazon Alexa (May 2023)** | Kept children's voice recordings and undermined parents' deletion requests (COPPA) | $25 million; deletion and privacy safeguards |\n| **Rite Aid (Dec 2023)** | AI facial recognition used 2012-2020 without accuracy testing, false-positive monitoring or staff training; false matches fell disproportionately on people of color | 5-year ban on facial recognition for surveillance; delete images and algorithms built from them; safeguards programme |\n| **Operation AI Comply (Sep 2024)** | Five actions: DoNotPay's \"robot lawyer\" claims, Rytr's review generator, and three \"AI-powered\" online-storefront income schemes (Ascend Ecom, Ecommerce Empire Builders, FBA Machine) | DoNotPay paid $193,000 and must substantiate claims; storefront schemes halted by court orders |\n| **Workado (Apr 2025)** | Claimed its AI content detector was 98% accurate without evidence | Order requiring it to back up accuracy claims |\n| **Rytr order set aside (22 Dec 2025)** | FTC found the complaint did not support a Section 5 violation and the order unduly burdened AI innovation (AI Action Plan) | Order vacated; the FTC says it will still pursue AI used to deceive consumers |\n\n> **2026 posture:** the FTC now targets fraud and tangible consumer harm rather than tools that could be misused (the Rytr theory), but deception about what AI can do remains squarely actionable (Workado, DoNotPay). Section 5 itself has not changed. \"There is no AI exemption from the laws on the books\" (FTC, Operation AI Comply, 2024).\n\n### 3.4 Dark Patterns and Manipulative Interfaces\n\n**Dark patterns** — UI designs that trick, coerce, or confuse consumers:\n- Hidden/unclear opt-out and unsubscribe buttons\n- \"Confirm-shaming\" (e.g., \"No thanks, I like paying full price\")\n- Deceptively labeled toggles\n- AI-powered real-time A/B testing that reduces clarity\n\n> AI exacerbates these risks by **personalizing manipulation at scale** — adapting content in real time based on behavioral predictions.\n\n### 3.5 Chatbots, Deepfakes, and AI Impersonation\n\n**Undisclosed AI interactions** in legal, health, spiritual, or financial contexts may constitute a material omission under UDAP.\n\n**Deepfake risks:**\n- Fabricated celebrity endorsements\n- False evidence of events or statements\n- Impersonation scams\n- Non-consensual intimate images\n\n> **Take It Down Act (2025, U.S.):** Criminalizes distribution of non-consensual intimate images including AI deepfakes. UK's Online Safety Act similarly criminalizes.\n\n**Deepfake Red Flags:**\n- Unrealistically high-quality endorsements/confessions\n- Videos without metadata or original source\n- Synthetic voice in customer service without notice\n- Out-of-character statements\n\n### 3.6 Fake Reviews and Endorsements\n\n- **FTC Endorsement Guides (2023 update):** prohibit **fake or unverified reviews**; businesses are accountable for deceptive reviews even if generated by **affiliates or third-party vendors**\n- **Consumer Reviews and Testimonials Rule (final, announced 14 Aug 2024):** bans creating, selling or buying reviews or testimonials that misrepresent that they come from someone who does not exist, **such as AI-generated fake reviews**, or from someone with no actual experience of the product; lets the FTC seek civil penalties against knowing violators\n\n### 3.7 Consumer Protection Risk Mitigation\n\n**Transparency and disclosure requirements:**\n- Clear disclosure that users are interacting with AI\n- Accessible explanations of how decisions are made\n- Contextual disclaimers that AI is not professional advice\n- Model limitations statements\n- Easy escalation paths to human representatives\n\n> Disclosures must be **prominent** (not buried in ToS), **in plain language**, and provided **at time of AI use**.\n\n**AI Marketing Compliance Checklist:**\n- All AI performance claims supported by quantitative/qualitative evidence\n- Scope of AI's role clearly stated (human-assisted vs. fully automated)\n- Disclaimers visible where needed\n- Third-party tool limitations disclosed\n- Testimonials authentic and appropriately labeled\n\n**Ongoing monitoring:**\n- Routine performance and fairness audits\n- Red teaming for abusive/manipulative behavior\n- Logging and reviewing user complaints\n- Monitoring for hallucinations, false positives, unsafe responses\n- Security monitoring for prompt injection, data poisoning\n\n| Red Flag | Why Risky |\n|----------|-----------|\n| \"100% accurate\" AI claim | Deceptive unless provable |\n| AI chatbot gives medical/legal advice | Could mislead into unsafe decisions |\n| No disclosure of AI interaction | Violates material disclosure expectations |\n| Optimizing UI for engagement over clarity | May trigger dark pattern enforcement |\n| AI-generated reviews/endorsements | Likely deceptive unless disclosed |\n| Claims based on outdated/biased data | May cause unfair outcomes without recourse |\n\n---", "t_liability": "## At a Glance\n\nProduct liability reaches AI through design defect, manufacturing defect and failure to warn, under strict liability or negligence. The hard questions are whether software and AI are 'products', how to pin a defect in an adaptive system, and who in the supply chain is liable. The EU answered the first with the revised Product Liability Directive (EU) 2024/2853.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\n### Three theories and three standards\n\n**Design defect**: the product was made as intended but its design is unreasonably dangerous. **Manufacturing defect**: the product deviates from the intended design. **Failure to warn** (marketing defect): the product lacks adequate instructions or warnings. These run through **strict liability** (prove the product was defective and caused harm, no negligence needed), **negligence** (prove a failure of reasonable care), or **breach of warranty**. Defect is often judged by a **risk-utility test**: was the risk outweighed by the utility, and could a reasonable alternative design have reduced it?\n\n### Defect in an adaptive system\n\nAI complicates the analysis because behaviour is dynamic and context-sensitive, models drift after deployment, and outputs are probabilistic rather than deterministic. The Uber autonomous-vehicle fatality (2018) illustrates the problem: was the defect in the algorithm, the sensor configuration, or the testing protocol? The EU PLD answers by expressly weighing \"the effect on the product of any ability to continue to learn or acquire new features after it is placed on the market\" in the defect test.\n\n### Is AI a product?\n\nUnder the traditional US view, software is a service and licensing agreements govern. The emerging view treats embedded software that controls hardware, or that replaces human judgment, as part of a product system. The EU ends the debate: under the PLD, software including AI is a product \"irrespective of the mode of its supply or usage\" (on a device, over a network, or as software-as-a-service), and AI system providers under the AI Act are treated as manufacturers.\n\n### The revised EU Product Liability Directive (EU) 2024/2853\n\nAdopted 23 October 2024, in force 8 December 2024, Member States must transpose by **9 December 2026**, and it applies to products placed on the market or put into service after that date. Liability is no-fault: the claimant proves defect, damage and causation. It covers death and personal injury including medically recognised psychological harm, property damage, and the destruction or corruption of non-professional data. Once a claim is plausible, a court can order disclosure of evidence. **Presumptions** ease proof: defect is presumed if the defendant fails to disclose, the product breaches mandatory safety requirements, or there is an obvious malfunction; causation is presumed if the damage is typical of the defect; and either or both are presumed where technical or scientific complexity makes proof excessively difficult, with machine learning a named example. All are rebuttable. Non-commercial free and open-source software is excluded; a manufacturer that integrates FOSS is liable for it. Liability to the injured person cannot be contracted out.\n\n### The AI Liability Directive was withdrawn\n\nThe Commission proposed a fault-based AI Liability Directive in 2022; it was withdrawn, published in OJ C/2025/5423 on 6 October 2025. There is therefore no EU-level fault-based AI liability regime: victims rely on the revised PLD for defective products and national tort law for fault. Any answer that relies on an \"AI Liability Directive presumption\" is wrong as of 2026.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nTwo traps dominate. First, the AI Liability Directive: it was withdrawn (6 October 2025), so any item that assumes its presumption of causality exists is wrong. Second, the EU PLD: it is adopted, not proposed, software including AI is a product however supplied, its presumptions are rebuttable, and it applies to products placed on the market after 9 December 2026. For US items, know the three theories and that strict liability does not require proving negligence.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Three theories: design defect, manufacturing defect, failure to warn; three standards: strict liability, negligence, breach of warranty\n- Strict liability needs only a defective product and harm, not proof of negligence; the risk-utility test asks whether a reasonable alternative design existed\n- AI complicates defect analysis: dynamic behaviour, drift, probabilistic outputs, and supply chains where each actor blames the others\n- EU Product Liability Directive (EU) 2024/2853: software including AI is a product however supplied; no-fault liability; transpose by 9 December 2026\n- PLD aids: disclosure once a claim is plausible, and rebuttable presumptions of defect and causation where technical or scientific complexity makes proof excessively difficult (machine learning is a named example)\n- PLD scope: psychological harm counts, non-professional data loss counts, non-commercial FOSS is excluded, and liability cannot be contracted out\n- The AI Liability Directive was withdrawn (OJ C/2025/5423, 6 October 2025): no EU fault-based AI liability regime exists\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2024/2853/oj/eng\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Directive (EU) 2024/2853 (revised Product Liability Directive)</a> · software and AI are products; no-fault liability; presumptions; transpose by 9 December 2026\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/C/2025/5423/oj\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AI Liability Directive withdrawal, OJ C/2025/5423</a> · the fault-based proposal was formally withdrawn on 6 October 2025\n- <a href=\"https://www.europarl.europa.eu/legislative-train/theme-a-europe-fit-for-the-digital-age/file-ai-liability-directive\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">European Parliament Legislative Train, AI liability directive</a> · the procedural history of the withdrawal\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Sectoral and Civil Laws\n\n## 4. Product Liability Laws and AI\n\n### 4.1 Theories of Product Liability\n\n| Theory | Definition |\n|--------|------------|\n| **Design defect** | Product manufactured as intended but design is unreasonably dangerous |\n| **Manufacturing defect** | Product deviates from intended design due to manufacturing error |\n| **Failure to warn (marketing defect)** | Product lacks adequate instructions or warnings about risks |\n\n**Applied through:**\n\n| Standard | Requirement |\n|----------|-------------|\n| **Strict liability** | No need to prove negligence — only that product was defective and caused harm |\n| **Negligence** | Must show failure to act with reasonable care |\n| **Breach of warranty** | Based on contractual promises about safety/performance |\n\n| | Strict Liability | Negligence |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Standard** | Defective product causes harm | Failure to act with reasonable care |\n| **Intent** | Not required | Required (failure to prevent foreseeable harm) |\n| **Common Use** | Design/manufacturing defects | Inadequate testing, supervision, warning |\n| **AI Example** | Autonomous vacuum explodes due to flaw | AI decision causes harm due to poor oversight |\n\n### 4.2 What Is a \"Defect\" in AI?\n\nAI complicates traditional defect analysis:\n- Behavior is **dynamic and context-sensitive**\n- Models may **drift** after deployment\n- Outputs are **probabilistic, not deterministic**\n\n> **Uber autonomous vehicle (2018):** Software failed to classify pedestrian as hazard. Was the defect in algorithm, sensor configuration, or testing protocol? Ambiguity illustrates difficulty pinpointing defects in adaptive systems.\n\n> **EU answer:** the PLD's defect test expressly weighs \"the effect on the product of any ability to continue to learn or acquire new features after it is placed on the market\" (Art. 7(2)(c)).\n\n### 4.3 AI as a \"Product\"\n\n| Traditional view | Emerging view |\n|-----------------|---------------|\n| Software = service (not tangible good) | Embedded software controlling hardware = product |\n| Licensing agreements govern (not sales) | Courts treat AI as part of product system when it controls physical hardware or replaces human judgment |\n\n> **Restatement (Third) of Torts:** Defines product as \"tangible personal property distributed commercially.\" Embedded software operating physical products (autopilot, insulin pumps, drones) may qualify.\n\n> **EU contrast:** the PLD ends the debate for the EU. Software is a product \"irrespective of the mode of its supply or usage\": stored on a device, accessed through a network or cloud, or supplied as software-as-a-service; AI system providers under the AI Act are treated as manufacturers (recital 13). Information, such as the content of e-books or the mere source code, is not a product.\n\n### 4.4 Risk-Utility Balancing\n\nCourts apply the **risk-utility test**: \"Was the risk of harm outweighed by the product's utility, and could a **reasonable alternative design** have reduced the risk?\"\n\n**Plaintiffs may allege:**\n- Insufficient or improper training data used\n- Safer model architectures available but ignored\n- Design failed to include human oversight\n- Safer default behavior was feasible\n\n### 4.5 Manufacturing Defects in AI\n\n| AI Manufacturing Defects |\n|--------------------------|\n| Faulty sensors/cameras in autonomous vehicles |\n| Corrupted firmware or software deployment |\n| Hardware failures impairing AI perception |\n| Integration errors between AI and mechanical components |\n| Third-party component compatibility issues |\n\n> AI manufacturers are often **integrators** assembling components from multiple sources.\n\n### 4.6 Failure to Warn\n\nMust warn consumers about **non-obvious dangers**. Duty is **ongoing** — extends post-deployment as risks evolve.\n\n| Warning Triggers |\n|-----------------|\n| User instructions omit known AI accuracy limitations |\n| Disclaimers buried or not in plain language |\n| Users unaware AI is controlling outcomes |\n| Edge cases and risk conditions not disclosed |\n\n> **Tesla Autopilot:** \"Autopilot\" and \"Full Self-Driving\" branding led to consumer misunderstanding. Mid-2025: federal jury awarded **>$240 million** in damages for 2019 Autopilot-related death.\n\n**Continuing duty to warn** may include software update notices, safety alerts, recall announcements, and public disclosures.\n\n### 4.7 Preemption\n\n| Claim | Potential Preemption |\n|-------|---------------------|\n| Design defect in AI medical device | May be preempted by FDA approval |\n| Failure to warn about AI in drones | Unlikely to be preempted |\n| Post-sale updates causing harm | Less likely to be protected |\n\n### 4.8 AI Supply Chain Complexity\n\nAI supply chain actors: hardware vendors, cloud service providers, model developers, data brokers, system integrators. Each may **blame others** in litigation.\n\n> Legal ambiguity about **joint and several liability** in AI contexts complicates litigation. The EU PLD answers part of this: operators liable for the same damage are jointly and severally liable (Art. 12).\n\n### 4.9 EU Product Liability Directive (EU) 2024/2853 (Adopted)\n\n> **Status:** Adopted 23 Oct 2024; published OJ 18 Nov 2024; in force 8 Dec 2024. Member States must transpose by **9 Dec 2026**, and it applies to products placed on the market or put into service **after 9 Dec 2026**. It repeals Directive 85/374/EEC from 9 Dec 2026, but the 1985 Directive still governs products placed on the market before then. **Maximum harmonisation** (Art. 3): no stricter or looser national rules. Liability is **no-fault**: the claimant proves defect, damage and causation (Art. 10(1)).\n\n| Topic | Rule | Where |\n|-------|------|-------|\n| **Product** | All movables, including electricity, digital manufacturing files, raw materials and **software**; software, including AI systems, is a product however supplied (device, cloud, SaaS) | Art. 4(1); recital 13 |\n| **FOSS carve-out** | Free and open-source software developed or supplied **outside the course of a commercial activity** is excluded; supply for a price, or for personal data used beyond security and interoperability, is commercial. A manufacturer that integrates non-commercial FOSS into its product is liable for it; the FOSS developer is not | Art. 2(2); recitals 14-15 |\n| **Who is liable** | Manufacturer; manufacturer of a defective component (including software) integrated under its control; for non-EU makers, the importer, authorised representative, then fulfilment service provider; distributors and some online platforms if they fail to identify an EU operator within one month; anyone who **substantially modifies** a product outside the manufacturer's control | Art. 8 |\n| **Damage** | Death or personal injury, **including medically recognised damage to psychological health**; damage to property (not the product itself, not property used exclusively for professional purposes); **destruction or corruption of data not used for professional purposes** | Art. 6; recital 21 |\n| **Defectiveness** | Safety a person is entitled to expect or that law requires, considering foreseeable use, continued learning after placement, interconnected products, when the product left the manufacturer's control, cybersecurity requirements, and recalls | Art. 7 |\n| **Disclosure** | Once the claimant shows plausibility, the court can order the defendant to disclose relevant evidence; limited to what is necessary and proportionate, with trade-secret protection | Art. 9 |\n| **Presumptions** | **Defect** presumed if the defendant fails to disclose, the product breaches mandatory safety requirements, or there is an obvious malfunction; **causation** presumed if the damage is typical of the defect; either or both presumed where **technical or scientific complexity** makes proof excessively difficult and the claimant shows likelihood (machine learning is a named example). All **rebuttable** | Art. 10; recital 48 |\n| **Defences** | Not placing on the market; defect arose later; compliance with legal requirements; development risk (state of scientific and technical knowledge); component and modifier defences. **No later-defect defence** where the defect is due to a related service, software including updates, a lack of updates needed for safety, or a substantial modification, if within the manufacturer's control | Art. 11 |\n| **Development risk** | Judged at placing on the market or while the product is within the manufacturer's control; Member States may keep or introduce liability despite it, subject to notification | Arts. 11(1)(e), 18 |\n| **No contracting out** | Liability to the injured person cannot be limited or excluded by contract or national law | Art. 15 |\n| **Time limits** | 3-year limitation from awareness; claims expire 10 years after placing on the market (or after a substantial modification), 25 years for latent personal injury | Arts. 16-17 |\n| **Software supply chain** | A manufacturer that integrates software cannot recover from a micro or small software-component maker if it contractually waived that right | Art. 12(2) |\n\n> **Continuous learning and updates:** a substantial modification made through a software update, or through the continuous learning of an AI system, counts as placing the modified product on the market at that time (recital 40).\n\n### 4.10 AI Liability Directive (Withdrawn)\n\nThe Commission proposed a fault-based **AI Liability Directive** on 28 Sep 2022 (COM(2022) 496), alongside the PLD proposal. It would have given national courts power to order disclosure of evidence about high-risk AI systems suspected of causing damage and created a rebuttable **presumption of causality** to ease victims' burden of proof.\n\n- Work was suspended until the AI Act was adopted and resumed in 2024; the Parliament's internal market committee (IMCO) then called the proposal premature and unnecessary (May 2025)\n- The Commission announced withdrawal in its **2025 work programme** (COM(2025) 45, 11 Feb 2025) and approved it on 16 Jul 2025\n- Withdrawal published in **OJ C/2025/5423 on 6 Oct 2025**\n- The 2026 Digital Omnibus on AI added no liability rules\n\n| | PLD (EU) 2024/2853 | AI Liability Directive |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Status** | Adopted; transpose by 9 Dec 2026 | **Withdrawn** (OJ C/2025/5423, 6 Oct 2025) |\n| **Basis** | No-fault (defective product) | Fault-based (national tort claims) |\n| **Scope** | Defective products, including software and AI systems | Harm caused by AI systems |\n| **Claimant aids** | Disclosure; presumptions of defect and causation | Would have added disclosure for high-risk AI and a presumption of causality |\n\n> **Consequence:** there is no EU-level fault-based AI liability regime. Victims rely on the revised PLD for defective products and on national tort law for fault. Any exam answer that relies on an \"AI Liability Directive presumption\" is wrong as of 2026.\n\n### 4.11 Product Liability Risk Mitigation\n\n**AI-Specific Safety Testing:**\n- Edge-case behavior in non-deterministic environments\n- Bias and fairness audits for outputs affecting physical outcomes\n- Simulation testing for rare but catastrophic events\n- Human-AI interaction risks (overreliance, confusion)\n- Vulnerability testing: adversarial patch, prompt injection, data poisoning\n- Digital twins / sandboxed environments\n- Stress testing with unpredictable inputs (fuzzing)\n\n**AI System Bill of Materials (AI-SBOM):**\n\n| Element | Purpose |\n|---------|---------|\n| Model version and ID | Identify which version caused harm |\n| Dataset sources | Reveal bias or licensing issues |\n| Preprocessing steps | Explain unexpected model behavior |\n| Firmware/software logs | Validate configuration at time of error |\n| Update timestamps | Determine if recent changes were causal |\n\n**Post-Deployment Governance:**\n- Real-world performance monitoring\n- Event/incident escalation procedures\n- Contingency plans: **model rollback**, **human override**\n- Automated alerting for performance changes, unusual patterns, claims of harm\n- For EU products placed on the market after 9 Dec 2026: a security-update policy, update logs, and a substantial-modification check before each release (the PLD keeps the manufacturer liable for updates within its control)\n- Evidence readiness: documentation that can be disclosed under PLD Art. 9 without exposing trade secrets unnecessarily\n\n---", "ch6": "## At a Glance\n\nBOK v2.1 II.C covers the main elements of AI-specific laws. The EU AI Act is the anchor: risk tiers (prohibited, high, limited, minimal), a heavy compliance chain for high-risk systems, transparency duties, a separate GPAI regime, role-based obligations and GDPR-scale penalties. Under v2.1 the subdomain also covers South Korea's AI Basic Act and US federal and state AI laws that apply to private-sector organizations. The EU AI Act was amended by the Digital Omnibus on AI (Regulation (EU) 2026/1744, in force 27 July 2026), which moved the high-risk dates and simplified several duties.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nII.C carries 6 to 8 questions, the most of the four competencies, and BOK v2.1 widened it from \"the EU AI Act\" to \"AI-specific laws\". The EU AI Act is the anchor; Korea and the US states are the comparison.\n\n### Risk classification\n\nThe EU AI Act regulates by risk, not by technology:\n\n| Tier | Test | Consequence |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Prohibited** | Unacceptable risk to rights or safety | Banned outright (Article 5) |\n| **High-risk** | Annex I safety components, or Annex III use cases | Full Chapter III obligations chain |\n| **Limited** | Interaction or synthetic content | Transparency duties (Article 50) |\n| **Minimal** | Negligible risk | No new obligations; voluntary codes |\n\nAs enacted, Article 5(1) lists eight prohibitions: subliminal or purposefully manipulative techniques, exploitation of vulnerabilities, social scoring, individual criminal-offence risk prediction, untargeted facial-image scraping, emotion recognition in workplaces and education, biometric categorisation by sensitive traits, and real-time remote biometric identification in public spaces (with narrow law-enforcement exceptions). As amended by the 2026 Omnibus it lists ten: from 2 December 2026, AI that generates or manipulates realistic intimate or sexual imagery of an identifiable person without explicit consent (\"nudification\") and AI that generates child sexual abuse material (Article 5(1)(ba)-(bb)). High-risk arrives by two routes: a safety component of a product under EU harmonisation law (Annex I), or an Annex III use case (biometrics, critical infrastructure, education, employment, essential services including credit, law enforcement, migration, justice). The tier is contextual, and a deployer that substantially modifies a system becomes its provider.\n\n### The high-risk obligations chain\n\nA high-risk provider owes a documented risk-management system across the lifecycle; data governance (relevant, representative training, validation and test data, as free from bias as possible); technical documentation; automatic logging; instructions for use; human oversight designed for detection, override and shutdown; accuracy, robustness and cybersecurity; a quality management system; a conformity assessment (internal control or a notified body) with CE marking; EU database registration; and post-market monitoring with serious-incident reporting. Deployers owe human oversight in use, input-data quality, log retention and incident reporting; importers and distributors verify CE marking and documentation and cooperate with authorities. Article 50 transparency applies from **2 August 2026**: chatbots must disclose that they are AI and synthetic output must be marked in machine-readable form, and deployers must disclose deepfakes, emotion-recognition and biometric-categorisation use, and AI-generated text on matters of public interest unless human-reviewed. Generative systems already on the market before 2 August 2026 have until **2 December 2026** for marking.\n\n### General-purpose AI\n\nAll GPAI providers owe technical documentation and downstream information (Article 53(1)(a)-(b)), a copyright policy honouring Article 4(3) opt-outs, a public training-content summary (Article 53(1)(c)-(d)), and, for non-EU providers, an EU authorised representative (Article 54). Open-source models are exempt only from points (a) and (b), and not at all if they have systemic risk. A model is **presumed** to have systemic risk above 10^25 FLOP of training compute (Article 51(2)), or may be designated by the Commission using the Annex XIII criteria; the provider must then notify the Commission, run documented adversarial evaluation, assess and mitigate Union-level risks, report serious incidents and maintain adequate cybersecurity (Article 55). The Act requires adversarial testing to be conducted and documented, not published; \"external audits\" and public red-team reports are Code of Practice extras, not Article 55 duties.\n\n### Enforcement and penalties\n\nThe **European AI Office** (in the Commission) supervises GPAI and holds exclusive competence under Article 88; as amended it is also exclusively competent for AI systems built on a GPAI model by the same provider or group (with carve-outs) and for AI in DSA very large online platforms and search engines. **National competent authorities** run market surveillance for everything else, with inspection, corrective-order, withdrawal and recall powers. Penalty ceilings: **EUR 35m or 7%** for prohibited practices; **EUR 15m or 3%** for most other breaches, including Commission GPAI fines under Article 101; **EUR 7.5m or 1%** for incorrect or misleading information, all \"whichever is higher\". SMEs get the lower of the two figures on every tier, and as amended small mid-caps get the lower cap on the two lower tiers only.\n\n### Timeline: as enacted versus as amended\n\n| Date | As enacted (2024/1689) | As amended (2026/1744) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2 Feb 2025 | Prohibitions (eight) and AI literacy | Same (softened literacy wording from 27 Jul 2026) |\n| 2 Aug 2025 | GPAI obligations, governance, penalty regime except Article 101 | Same |\n| 2 Aug 2026 | Annex III high-risk, Article 50, Commission GPAI enforcement | Article 50 and GPAI enforcement only |\n| 2 Dec 2026 | - | Two new prohibitions; Article 50(2) marking deadline for legacy generative systems |\n| 2 Dec 2027 | - | Annex III high-risk rules |\n| 2 Aug 2028 | - | Annex I product-embedded high-risk rules |\n\nBOK v2.1 predates the Omnibus, so an item may quote the original dates. Read the stem for its anchor; prohibitions, GPAI dates and penalty ceilings did not change.\n\n### Beyond the EU\n\n**South Korea's AI Basic Act** (in force 22 January 2026) is lighter and promotion-first: no prohibited practices, duties on \"high-impact AI\" operators to run a risk-management plan, provide explanations, protect users and keep records for five years, a **best-efforts** impact assessment, transparency and labelling for generative AI, and fines capped at KRW 30 million for only three failures. **US law** is a patchwork: no comprehensive federal statute, but state and local laws that reach private organizations, including Colorado's SB 26-189 ADMT regime (effective 1 January 2027), Texas TRAIGA, California's SB 53 and SB 942, Utah's SB 149 and SB 226, Illinois HB 3773, New York's RAISE Act, and NYC Local Law 144's bias audits.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\n6 to 8 questions, up from 5 to 7 under BOK v2.0.1. Expect tier classification (spot the prohibited practice, place a use in Annex III), the high-risk obligations chain, GPAI versus systemic-risk GPAI duties, the provider/deployer/importer/distributor split, and the penalty ladder. Date items may follow the original AI Act text, so learn both columns: as enacted, Annex III high-risk applied 2 August 2026 and Annex I 2 August 2027; as amended, 2 December 2027 and 2 August 2028. 2 August 2026 still starts Article 50 and Commission GPAI enforcement, and 2 December 2026 brings the two new prohibitions. Since II.C now covers AI-specific laws generally, expect at least one comparative item on Korea's lighter, best-efforts regime or a US state law.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Risk tiers: prohibited (Article 5), high-risk (Annex I products and Annex III uses), limited-risk transparency (Article 50), minimal risk\n- Prohibitions: eight as enacted, from 2 February 2025; the 2026 Omnibus adds intimate-imagery and CSAM bans from 2 December 2026, for ten in total\n- High-risk provider chain: risk management, data governance, documentation, logging, instructions, human oversight, accuracy and security, QMS, conformity assessment, CE mark, registration, post-market monitoring\n- GPAI: all providers owe documentation, downstream information, a copyright policy and a training-content summary; systemic-risk models (presumed above 10^25 FLOP) add notification, adversarial evaluation, risk mitigation, incident reporting and cybersecurity\n- Penalties: EUR 35m/7% for prohibitions; EUR 15m/3% for most obligations and Commission GPAI fines; EUR 7.5m/1% for misleading information; lower-figure caps for SMEs on every tier and for small mid-caps on the two lower tiers\n- Timeline as amended: Article 50 and GPAI enforcement 2 August 2026; Annex III high-risk 2 December 2027; Annex I high-risk 2 August 2028\n- Roles decide obligations: deployers that substantially modify a system become providers, and importers are liable for placing non-compliant systems on the market\n- Beyond the EU: South Korea's AI Basic Act (best-efforts, KRW 30m fines, no prohibitions) and a US patchwork (Colorado, Texas, California, Utah, Illinois, New York, NYC) invite comparison of tiers, roles and enforcement\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1689/oj\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 (EU AI Act)</a> · the anchor AI-specific statute, as enacted\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2026/1744/oj/eng\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Digital Omnibus on AI, Regulation (EU) 2026/1744</a> · moved the high-risk dates and added the two new prohibitions\n- <a href=\"https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/timeline/timeline-implementation-eu-ai-act\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AI Act implementation timeline (AI Act Service Desk)</a> · the official milestone dates the two-timeline table tracks\n- <a href=\"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/contents-code-gpai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GPAI Code of Practice (European Commission)</a> · voluntary code; 21 full signatories as of 31 July 2026\n- <a href=\"https://elaw.klri.re.kr/eng_service/lawView.do?hseq=71019&lang=ENG\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">South Korea AI Basic Act (KLRI English translation)</a> · the authoritative government-funded English text of the Act\n- <a href=\"https://leg.colorado.gov/bills/sb26-189\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Colorado SB 26-189 (ADMT)</a> · the ADMT regime replacing SB 24-205, effective 1 January 2027\n- <a href=\"https://capitol.texas.gov/tlodocs/89R/billtext/html/HB00149F.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Texas TRAIGA, HB 149 (89R) chaptered text</a> · the Texas responsible-AI statute for developers and deployers\n- <a href=\"https://legislation.nysenate.gov/pdf/bills/2025/S6953B\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">New York RAISE Act, S6953B</a> · frontier-model safety framework signed 19 December 2025\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] The EU AI Act\n\n## Performance Indicators\n\n*BOK v2.1, II.C, paraphrased. The subdomain carries 6–8 exam questions.*\n\n- Explain how AI-specific laws sort AI into **risk tiers** (for example prohibited, high, limited and minimal risk), and place a given system or use in the right tier\n- Know the core requirements for **risk management, data governance, technical documentation, conformity and impact assessments, and record keeping**\n- Know the core requirements for **human oversight, transparency and notice to affected people, and quality management**\n- Distinguish the separate obligations for **general-purpose AI models**\n- Describe how these laws are **enforced** and the **penalties** for breaching them\n- Explain how obligations differ by **role**, for example provider, deployer, importer or distributor\n\n> [!note] What changed from BOK v2.0.1\n> - **Title:** II.C was renamed from \"the main elements of the EU AI Act\" to \"the main elements of AI-specific laws\".\n> - **Weight:** 6–8 questions, up from 5–7.\n> - **Tiers:** the tier list is now given as examples (\"e.g.\") rather than as the EU's four tiers, so other laws' classification schemes count too.\n> - **Requirements:** the old single requirements indicator is split in two, and impact assessments now sit alongside conformity assessments.\n> - **Scope:** the Domain II introduction now names the EU AI Act, South Korea's AI Basic Act, and US federal and state AI laws that apply to private-sector organisations.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n> [!source] The EU AI Act\n\n## 5. Organizational Context\n\nCompliance obligations depend on the role an organization plays in the AI supply chain.\n\n### 5.1 Role Definitions and Obligations\n\n| Role | Definition | Key Obligations |\n|------|------------|-----------------|\n| **Provider** | Develops an AI system/GPAI model and places it on the EU market under its own name/brand | Risk management, conformity assessments, technical documentation, CE marking, EU database registration, post-market monitoring, incident reporting |\n| **Deployer** | Uses AI systems in professional activities (not private use) | Human oversight during use, monitor performance and report incidents, ensure input data quality, maintain logs, use per provider instructions |\n| **Importer** | Places AI systems from non-EU countries onto the EU market | Verify CE marking and conformity assessment, ensure documentation is complete, register in EU database, cooperate with authorities |\n| **Distributor** | Makes AI systems available in EU market without modifying them (e.g., app stores, marketplaces) | Verify CE marking is present, confirm provider/importer contact info visible, suspend distribution if non-compliance suspected, cooperate with authorities |\n\n---\n\n### 5.2 Important Role Dynamics\n\n- **Deployers become providers** if they substantially modify an AI system or develop their own downstream application using GPAI — they inherit full provider obligations.\n- **Importers are legally liable** if they place a non-compliant AI system on the market. Contracts should clarify responsibility boundaries.\n- **Cloud-based AI platforms** (AI-as-a-Service) can blur roles; contractual designation of responsibilities is recommended.\n- **An organization can hold multiple roles** (e.g., both importer and distributor).\n\n---\n\n### 5.3 Shared Horizontal Duties\n\nAcross all roles, organizations must:\n\n- Cooperate with national competent authorities and regulators\n- Respond to incident reports\n- Maintain records (logs, updates, notices)\n\n> **Best practice:** Conduct an \"AI role assessment\" for each system to determine your role and resulting compliance obligations.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n> [!source] The EU AI Act\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n1. **AI systems are classified into four risk tiers: prohibited, high-risk, limited-risk, and minimal-risk.** This classification determines the level of regulatory control. Prohibited systems are banned outright. High-risk systems face the most stringent obligations.\n\n2. **High-risk AI systems must meet detailed compliance obligations before market placement.** Requirements include conformity assessments, technical documentation, risk management procedures, human oversight, post-market monitoring, and business records.\n\n3. **General-purpose AI (GPAI) models face their own requirements, scaled by systemic impact.** All GPAI providers owe technical documentation, downstream information, a copyright policy and a public training-content summary; non-EU providers also need an EU authorised representative. Systemic-risk models (presumed above 10²⁵ FLOP) add notification, model evaluation with adversarial testing, risk mitigation, serious-incident reporting and cybersecurity.\n\n4. **The role an organization plays (provider, deployer, importer, or distributor) determines its legal responsibilities.** Providers carry the heaviest burden. Deployers ensure safe implementation and oversight. Importers verify compliance for non-EU systems. Distributors must act upon credible non-compliance alerts.\n\n5. **Penalties under the EU AI Act can reach up to €35 million or 7% of global turnover.** The tiered enforcement system calibrates penalties to violation severity. Prohibited practices incur the highest fines; even documentation failures can result in substantial penalties.\n\n6. **Dates depend on the legal state.** As enacted, Annex III high-risk rules applied from 2 August 2026 and Annex I product rules from 2 August 2027. As amended by the Digital Omnibus on AI (Regulation (EU) 2026/1744, in force 27 July 2026), they apply from 2 December 2027 and 2 August 2028. 2 August 2026 still starts Art. 50 transparency and the Commission's GPAI enforcement, and 2 December 2026 brings the two new prohibitions. The exam blueprint predates the Omnibus, so know both columns.\n\n7. **II.C is broader than the EU.** Under BOK v2.1, \"AI-specific laws\" also means South Korea's AI Basic Act and US federal and state AI laws. Compare their risk tiers, role definitions and enforcement with the EU model ([[South Korea AI Basic Act — Key Provisions]], [[US AI Laws — Federal and State]]).\n\n---\n\n---\n\n> [!source] EU AI Act 2024 — Key Provisions\n\n# EU AI Act 2024 — Key Provisions\n\n> **Regulation (EU) 2024/1689** of the European Parliament and of the Council of 13 June 2024 — *The Artificial Intelligence Act*\n> Published in OJ: 12 July 2024 | Entry into force: 1 August 2024 | General date of application: 2 August 2026 (Art. 113)\n> **Amended by Regulation (EU) 2026/1744 (Digital Omnibus on AI)**, signed 8 July 2026, published 24 July 2026, **in force 27 July 2026**. The Omnibus moved the high-risk dates: Annex III systems from **2 December 2027**, Annex I product systems from **2 August 2028**. See §12 (timeline, as enacted vs as amended) and §13 (what else changed).\n\n> [!warning] Exam currency\n> BOK v2.1 was approved on 9 September 2025, before the Omnibus existed. Exam items written against the original text may still treat **2 August 2026** as the high-risk date and **2 August 2027** as the Annex I date. Learn both columns in §12 and read each question for its anchor (\"as enacted\" vs \"as amended\").\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview & Scope (Arts. 1–3)\n\n| Element | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| **Purpose** | Improve internal market functioning; promote human-centric, trustworthy AI; ensure high level of protection for health, safety, and fundamental rights (Art. 1) |\n| **Scope** | Applies to providers, deployers, importers, distributors, product manufacturers, authorised reps, and affected persons in the Union (Art. 2); extra-territorial reach for outputs used in the EU |\n| **Excluded** | Military/defence/national security; scientific R&D; personal non-professional use; free & open-source (unless high-risk or Art. 5/50) (Art. 2) |\n| **AI System Definition** | Machine-based system with varying autonomy, exhibiting adaptiveness, that infers from input how to generate outputs (predictions, content, recommendations, decisions) influencing physical/virtual environments (Art. 3(1)) |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Risk Classification — The 4-Tier Pyramid\n\n| Tier | Category | Approach | Key Articles |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **🔴 Unacceptable Risk** | Prohibited AI Practices | **Banned entirely** | Art. 5 |\n| **🟠 High Risk** | High-Risk AI Systems | **Strict pre-market & post-market requirements** | Arts. 6–7, 8–15, 16–27, Annex III |\n| **🟡 Limited Risk** | Transparency Obligations | **Transparency/disclosure duties only** | Art. 50 |\n| **🟢 Minimal / No Risk** | All other AI | **No obligations** (voluntary codes of conduct encouraged) | Art. 95 |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Prohibited AI Practices (Art. 5) — Unacceptable Risk 🔴\n\n| # | Prohibited Practice | Key Detail |\n|---|---|---|\n| (a) | **Subliminal / manipulative / deceptive techniques** | Materially distorting behaviour, causing or likely to cause **significant harm** |\n| (b) | **Exploitation of vulnerabilities** | Targeting age, disability, or social/economic situation to materially distort behaviour causing significant harm |\n| (c) | **Social scoring** | Evaluation/classification of persons over time based on social behaviour/personality, leading to detrimental treatment in unrelated contexts or unjustified/disproportionate treatment |\n| (d) | **Individual criminal risk assessment** | Predicting risk of criminal offence based *solely* on profiling or personality traits (human assessment based on objective facts still permitted) |\n| (e) | **Untargeted facial recognition database scraping** | Creating/expanding facial recognition databases via untargeted scraping from internet or CCTV |\n| (f) | **Emotion recognition** | Inferring emotions in **workplace and education** settings (medical/safety exceptions apply) |\n| (g) | **Biometric categorisation** | Categorising individuals based on biometric data to deduce race, political opinions, trade union membership, religious beliefs, sex life, or sexual orientation |\n| (h) | **Real-time remote biometric identification** in publicly accessible spaces for law enforcement | **Prohibited except** for three strictly defined purposes (Art. 5(1)(h)(i)–(iii)), requiring prior judicial authorisation |\n| (ba) *(added by the 2026 Omnibus)* | **Non-consensual intimate imagery** | AI that generates or manipulates realistic images, video, audio or similar material of an identifiable person's intimate parts or of that person in sexually explicit activity, without their explicit consent. **Applies from 2 December 2026** |\n| (bb) *(added by the 2026 Omnibus)* | **Child sexual abuse material** | AI that generates or manipulates material within Art. 2(c) and (e) of Directive 2011/93/EU. **Applies from 2 December 2026** |\n\n> **Count:** as enacted, Art. 5(1) lists **eight** practices, (a)–(h), all applicable from 2 February 2025. As amended by the 2026 Omnibus it lists **ten**; the two new points apply from 2 December 2026 (Art. 113(a) as amended).\n> **Scope of (ba)/(bb) (new Art. 5(1a)–(1b)):** *placing on the market* is caught only where that output is the system's intended purpose, or where it is a reasonably foreseeable, reproducible outcome that the system lacks reasonable safeguards to prevent. *Use* is caught only where the deployer uses the system for that purpose. Editing that does not increase exposure or alter the sexual nature of existing material is not \"manipulation\".\n\n> **Exception to Art. 5(1)(h):** Real-time RBI permitted only for: (i) targeted search for victims/missing persons, (ii) prevention of imminent threat to life/terrorist attack, (iii) localisation of suspects for crimes in Annex II (punishable by ≥4 years). Requires **prior judicial authorisation** (Art. 5(3)) and fundamental rights impact assessment (Art. 27).\n\n---\n\n## 4. High-Risk AI Systems 🟠\n\n### 4.1 Classification Rules (Arts. 6–7)\n\nA system is **high-risk** if it meets **either**:\n\n| Pathway | Condition | Article |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Pathway 1 — Product Safety** | AI is a safety component of, or is itself a product covered by Annex I harmonisation legislation, AND the product requires third-party conformity assessment | Art. 6(1) |\n| **Pathway 2 — Annex III Use Case** | AI system falls within one of the 8 areas listed in Annex III | Art. 6(2) |\n\n> **Derogation (Art. 6(3)):** An Annex III system is **NOT high-risk** if it does NOT pose significant risk to health/safety/fundamental rights — e.g., performs a narrow procedural task, improves previously completed human activity, detects decision-making patterns without replacing human assessment, or performs preparatory tasks. However, **if the system performs profiling, it is ALWAYS high-risk** (Art. 6(3) para 3).\n>\n> **Paper trail:** a provider relying on Art. 6(3) must document its assessment before placing the system on the market and register it in the EU database (Arts. 6(4), 49(2)). As amended by the 2026 Omnibus, registration continues but asks for less: the summary of grounds and the list of Member States (Annex VIII §B points 7 and 9) were deleted.\n\n### 4.2 Annex III — 8 High-Risk Areas\n\n| # | Area | Examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Biometrics** (where permitted) | Remote biometric identification, biometric categorisation by sensitive attributes, emotion recognition |\n| 2 | **Critical Infrastructure** | Safety components in management of digital infrastructure, road traffic, water/gas/heating/electricity supply |\n| 3 | **Education & Vocational Training** | Admission/access determination, learning outcome evaluation, education level assessment, monitoring prohibited student behaviour |\n| 4 | **Employment & Workers' Management** | Recruitment/selection, decisions affecting work relationships (promotion, termination), task allocation, performance monitoring/evaluation |\n| 5 | **Essential Services & Benefits** | Eligibility for public benefits/healthcare, creditworthiness/credit scoring, life/health insurance risk assessment & pricing, emergency call triage/dispatch |\n| 6 | **Law Enforcement** (where permitted) | Victim risk assessment, polygraphs, evidence reliability evaluation, offender/re-offender risk assessment, profiling during criminal investigations |\n| 7 | **Migration, Asylum & Border Control** | Polygraphs, risk assessment for migration/security/health, asylum/visa application examination, detection/identification of persons |\n| 8 | **Administration of Justice & Democratic Processes** | Judicial research/interpretation of facts and law, influencing election/referendum outcomes or voting behaviour |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Requirements for High-Risk AI Systems (Arts. 8–15)\n\n| Requirement | Article | Key Provisions |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Risk Management System** | Art. 9 | Continuous iterative process throughout lifecycle: identify, estimate, evaluate, and mitigate risks to health/safety/fundamental rights. Residual risk must be acceptable. Testing prior to market placement. |\n| **Data & Data Governance** | Art. 10 | Training/validation/testing datasets must meet quality criteria: relevant, representative, free of errors, complete. Governance for design choices, collection, preparation, bias examination, and gap identification. Special category data processing for bias detection permitted under strict safeguards (as enacted: Art. 10(5); as amended by the 2026 Omnibus: Art. 10(5) is deleted and the rule moves to the new **Art. 4a**, see §13). |\n| **Technical Documentation** | Art. 11 | Drawn up before market placement, kept up to date. Must demonstrate compliance per Annex IV (general description, development process, monitoring/functioning details). SMEs may use simplified form. |\n| **Record-Keeping (Logging)** | Art. 12 | Automatic logging over system lifetime. Must record events relevant for risk identification, post-market monitoring facilitation, and operation monitoring. Specific requirements for biometric systems. |\n| **Transparency & Information to Deployers** | Art. 13 | Operation must be sufficiently transparent for deployers to interpret output. Instructions for use must include: provider identity, capabilities/limitations, accuracy/robustness metrics, known risks, input specifications, human oversight measures, etc. |\n| **Human Oversight** | Art. 14 | Systems designed so natural persons can effectively oversee them. Oversight measures built-in or identified for deployer implementation. Humans must be able to understand, monitor, interpret, override/reverse, and intervene/stop the system. Biometric identification requires **2-person verification** (except LE/migration where disproportionate). |\n| **Accuracy, Robustness & Cybersecurity** | Art. 15 | Appropriate levels of accuracy, robustness, cybersecurity throughout lifecycle. Resilience to errors/faults/inconsistencies. Feedback loop mitigation. Protection against unauthorised third-party exploitation of vulnerabilities. |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Operator Obligations\n\n### 6.1 Provider Obligations (Art. 16)\n\n- Ensure compliance with Section 2 requirements\n- Indicate name/address on system\n- Maintain Quality Management System (Art. 17)\n- Keep technical documentation (Art. 18) — **10 years**\n- Keep automatically generated logs (Art. 19) — **≥6 months**\n- Undergo conformity assessment before market placement (Art. 43)\n- Draw up EU Declaration of Conformity (Art. 47)\n- Affix CE marking (Art. 48)\n- Register in EU database (Art. 49)\n- Take corrective actions and notify authorities (Art. 20)\n- Cooperate with competent authorities (Art. 21)\n\n### 6.2 Deployer Obligations (Art. 26)\n\n- Use systems per instructions for use\n- Assign human oversight to competent natural persons\n- Ensure input data is relevant and representative\n- Monitor operation and inform providers of risks\n- Keep logs (≥6 months)\n- Inform workers' representatives before workplace deployment\n- Public authorities must register in EU database (Art. 49)\n- Conduct fundamental rights impact assessment (Art. 27 — for bodies governed by public law, private entities providing public services, and credit/insurance use cases)\n- Inform natural persons they are subject to high-risk AI decisions\n\n### 6.3 Importer Obligations (Art. 23)\n\n- Verify conformity assessment completed, technical documentation drawn up, CE marking affixed, authorised representative appointed\n- Not place non-conforming systems on market\n- Indicate name/address on system\n- Keep documentation for **10 years**\n- Cooperate with authorities\n\n### 6.4 Distributor Obligations (Art. 24)\n\n- Verify CE marking, EU declaration, instructions for use present\n- Verify provider/importer compliance with identification obligations\n- Not make non-conforming systems available\n- Take corrective actions / cooperate with authorities\n\n### 6.5 Quality Management System (Art. 17)\n\nMust include documented policies/procedures for:\n- Regulatory compliance strategy & conformity assessment\n- Design, design control, design verification\n- Development, quality control, quality assurance\n- Examination, test, validation procedures\n- Technical specifications & standards\n- Data management systems\n- Risk management system (per Art. 9)\n- Post-market monitoring (per Art. 72)\n- Serious incident reporting (per Art. 73)\n- Communication with authorities, notified bodies, operators\n- Record-keeping systems\n- Resource management\n- Accountability framework\n\n---\n\n## 7. Transparency Obligations (Art. 50) — Limited Risk 🟡\n\n| Obligation | Applies To | Requirement |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Interaction disclosure** | Providers of AI systems interacting directly with natural persons | Inform persons they are interacting with an AI system (unless obvious) |\n| **Synthetic content marking** | Providers of AI systems generating synthetic audio, image, video, or text | Outputs must be marked in machine-readable format and detectable as AI-generated |\n| **Emotion/biometric categorisation notice** | Deployers of emotion recognition or biometric categorisation systems | Inform exposed natural persons of the system's operation |\n| **Deep fake disclosure** | Deployers of AI generating/manipulating image, audio, video content constituting a deep fake | Disclose artificial generation/manipulation (artistic/satirical works: limited disclosure) |\n| **AI-generated text disclosure** | Deployers publishing AI-generated text to inform the public on matters of public interest | Disclose artificial generation (unless human review/editorial control) |\n\n> Information must be provided in a **clear and distinguishable manner at the latest at the time of first interaction or exposure** (Art. 50(5)).\n\n> **When:** Art. 50 applies from **2 August 2026** under both the original and the amended text. As amended by the 2026 Omnibus, generative systems already on the market before that date have until **2 December 2026** to add Art. 50(2) marking (Art. 111(4)). The voluntary **Code of Practice on Marking and Labelling** (final 10 June 2026) and the Commission's Art. 50 guidelines (20 July 2026) support compliance; see §14.\n\n---\n\n## 8. General-Purpose AI (GPAI) Models (Arts. 51–56)\n\n### 8.1 Classification (Arts. 51–52)\n\n| Category | Criteria |\n|---|---|\n| **GPAI Model** | AI model displaying significant generality, capable of competently performing a wide range of distinct tasks (Art. 3(63)) |\n| **GPAI Model with Systemic Risk** | (a) High-impact capabilities based on technical tools/benchmarks; OR (b) Commission designation ex officio or via scientific panel alert. **Presumed** when cumulative training computation > **10²⁵ FLOPS** (Art. 51(2)). |\n\n### 8.2 Obligations — All GPAI Model Providers (Art. 53)\n\n| Obligation | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| **Technical documentation** | Draw up and keep up to date (Annex XI) — for AI Office and national competent authorities upon request |\n| **Information for downstream providers** | Provide information enabling understanding of capabilities/limitations and compliance (Annex XII), without prejudice to IP/trade secrets |\n| **Copyright policy** | Put in place policy to comply with EU copyright law, including respecting opt-outs under Art. 4(3) of Directive (EU) 2019/790 |\n| **Training content summary** | Draw up and make publicly available a sufficiently detailed summary of content used for training |\n| **Authorised representative** | Providers from third countries must appoint an authorised rep in the Union (Art. 54) |\n| **Compliance demonstration** | May rely on codes of practice (Art. 56) or harmonised standards; alternatively demonstrate alternative adequate means |\n\n> **Open-source exception** (Art. 53(2)): Obligations (a) and (b) above do NOT apply to free and open-source models whose parameters/weights/architecture/usage info are publicly available — **unless** the model presents systemic risk.\n\n### 8.3 Additional Obligations — GPAI with Systemic Risk (Art. 55)\n\n| Obligation | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| **Model evaluation** | Conduct and document adversarial testing (red teaming) using standardised protocols; identify and mitigate systemic risks |\n| **Risk assessment & mitigation** | Assess and mitigate possible systemic risks at Union level, including their sources |\n| **Serious incident tracking** | Track, document, and report serious incidents and corrective measures without undue delay to the AI Office |\n| **Cybersecurity** | Ensure adequate level of cybersecurity protection for the model and its physical infrastructure |\n\n### 8.4 Codes of Practice (Art. 56)\n\n- AI Office to facilitate drawing up of codes of practice covering Arts. 53 and 55\n- Codes to be ready by **2 May 2025**\n- If not finalised by **2 August 2025**, Commission may provide common rules via implementing acts\n- **Legal effect:** a code is a way to *demonstrate* compliance until a harmonised standard is published. Only a harmonised standard (once its reference is in the OJ, Art. 40(1)) gives a **presumption of conformity** (Arts. 53(4), 55(2)). Non-adherents must show \"alternative adequate means\".\n- **Status:** the GPAI Code of Practice was published 10 July 2025; the Commission and the AI Board confirmed it adequate on 1 August 2025. See [[GPAI Code of Practice — Key Provisions]].\n- **As amended by the 2026 Omnibus**, Art. 56(6) has the Commission (taking utmost account of the Board's opinion) assess the codes' adequacy and publish that assessment. The replaced paragraph no longer contains the old sentence letting the Commission give a code \"general validity\" by implementing act.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Conformity Assessment, CE Marking & Registration\n\n| Element | Article | Key Detail |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Conformity Assessment** | Art. 43 | Based on internal control (Annex VI) or QMS + technical documentation assessment by notified body (Annex VII). Notified body assessment required for Annex III, point 1 (biometrics) unless harmonised standards applied. |\n| **EU Declaration of Conformity** | Art. 47 | Issued by provider; contents per Annex V |\n| **CE Marking** | Art. 48 | Affixed to system/packaging/documentation; subject to general principles of Regulation (EC) 765/2008 |\n| **EU Database Registration** | Art. 49 | Providers register high-risk AI systems before market placement. Deployers who are public authorities also register. Information per Annex VIII. |\n| **Post-Market Monitoring** | Art. 72 | Providers establish and document post-market monitoring system proportionate to nature and risks |\n| **Serious Incident Reporting** | Art. 73 | Providers report serious incidents to market surveillance authorities within **15 days** (or immediately for death); deployers report to provider |\n\n---\n\n## 10. Governance (Arts. 64–70)\n\n| Body | Role |\n|---|---|\n| **AI Office** (within Commission) | Central enforcement of GPAI rules (Chapter V); coordinates governance; facilitates codes of practice. **As amended by the 2026 Omnibus (Art. 75(1))**, it is also *exclusively* competent for (a) AI systems built on a GPAI model where the model and system come from the same provider or group, with carve-outs (Annex I products, Annex III point 2 critical infrastructure, certain law-enforcement, border and financial-institution systems, justice uses), and (b) AI systems that are or sit inside a DSA-designated very large online platform or search engine. As enacted, Art. 75(1) already let it supervise systems built on a provider's own GPAI model. The new Arts. 75a–75d spell out its investigation, inspection, commitment, non-compliance and fining procedure. |\n| **European Artificial Intelligence Board** | Composed of Member State representatives; advises Commission; coordinates national authorities |\n| **Scientific Panel** | Independent experts providing qualified alerts on systemic risks from GPAI models (Art. 90). **60 experts appointed 1 June 2026**, two-year terms |\n| **National Competent Authorities** | Market surveillance and notification at Member State level; enforce against all AI systems not reserved to the AI Office |\n| **Advisory Forum** | Multi-stakeholder body providing technical expertise. **Appointed 1 June 2026** (two-year terms); the EU Fundamental Rights Agency, ENISA and the standardisation bodies hold permanent seats |\n| **European Data Protection Supervisor** | Supervises Union institutions' compliance |\n\n---\n\n## 11. Enforcement & Penalties 🚨\n\n### 11.1 Fine Tiers for Operators (Art. 99)\n\n| Tier | Violation | Max Fine | Article |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Tier 1** 🔴 | Non-compliance with **prohibited AI practices** (Art. 5) | **€35 million** or **7%** of worldwide annual turnover (whichever higher) | Art. 99(3) |\n| **Tier 2** 🟠 | Non-compliance with operator/notified body obligations (Arts. 16, 22, 23, 24, 26, 31, 33, 34, 50) | **€15 million** or **3%** of worldwide annual turnover (whichever higher) | Art. 99(4) |\n| **Tier 3** 🟡 | Supplying **incorrect, incomplete, or misleading information** to notified bodies/competent authorities | **€7.5 million** or **1%** of worldwide annual turnover (whichever higher) | Art. 99(5) |\n\n> **SME/Startup provision (Art. 99(6)):** For SMEs including start-ups, each fine is capped at the **lower** of the percentage or fixed amount.\n> **Small mid-caps (new Art. 99(6a), 2026 Omnibus):** for SMCs, the Tier 2 and Tier 3 fines (Art. 99(4) and (5)) are also capped at the lower figure. The SMC cap does **not** cover Tier 1 prohibited-practice fines, whereas the SME cap covers all three tiers.\n\n### 11.2 GPAI Model Provider Fines (Art. 101)\n\nArt. 101 was carved out of the 2 August 2025 start date, so these fines apply only from **2 August 2026**, when the Commission's GPAI enforcement powers began. Models placed on the market before 2 August 2025 have until 2 August 2027 to comply (Art. 111(3)).\n\nCommission may impose fines on GPAI model providers of up to **€15 million or 3%** of worldwide annual turnover for:\n- Infringement of Regulation provisions\n- Failure to comply with document/information requests\n- Failure to comply with requested measures\n- Failure to provide model access for evaluation\n\n### 11.3 Union Institutions (Art. 100)\n\n- Prohibited practice violations: up to **€1,500,000**\n- Other violations: up to **€750,000**\n- Enforced by European Data Protection Supervisor\n\n---\n\n## 12. Implementation Timeline 📅 (Art. 113, as enacted and as amended)\n\n> [!warning] Two legal states\n> The **\"as enacted\"** column is Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 as published in July 2024, which the BOK v2.1 exam (approved September 2025) was written against. The **\"as amended\"** column is the law after the Digital Omnibus on AI, Regulation (EU) 2026/1744, in force 27 July 2026. Where an exam item gives no anchor, the original dates may be the intended answer.\n\n| Milestone | As enacted (Reg. 2024/1689) | As amended by the 2026 Digital Omnibus (Reg. 2026/1744) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Publication / entry into force | 12 Jul 2024 / 1 Aug 2024 | Unchanged |\n| Chapters I–II: definitions, AI literacy (Art. 4), prohibitions (a)–(h) | 2 Feb 2025 | Unchanged. The softened Art. 4 wording applies from 27 Jul 2026 |\n| Codes of practice ready (Art. 56(9)) | 2 May 2025 | Unchanged (GPAI Code published 10 Jul 2025) |\n| Chapter V (GPAI), Ch. III §4 (notified bodies), Ch. VII (governance), Ch. XII (penalties, except Art. 101), Art. 78 | 2 Aug 2025 | Unchanged |\n| Commission guidelines on Art. 6 high-risk classification (Art. 6(5)) | By 2 Feb 2026 | Deadline missed; draft published 19 May 2026; final expected by end of 2026 |\n| **General date of application** | 2 Aug 2026 | **Still 2 Aug 2026** for Art. 50 transparency duties and for the Commission's GPAI enforcement powers, including **Art. 101 fines** |\n| Omnibus in force; Arts. 102–110 (sectoral amendments) apply | — | 27 Jul 2026 |\n| New prohibitions: intimate imagery and CSAM (Art. 5(1)(ba)–(bb)) | — | **2 Dec 2026** |\n| Art. 50(2) machine-readable marking for generative systems **already on the market before 2 Aug 2026** | 2 Aug 2026 (no separate grace period) | **2 Dec 2026** (new Art. 111(4)) |\n| At least one national AI regulatory sandbox operational (Art. 57(1)) | 2 Aug 2026 | **2 Aug 2027** |\n| GPAI models placed on the market before 2 Aug 2025 (Art. 111(3)) | 2 Aug 2027 | Unchanged |\n| **Annex III high-risk systems** (Ch. III §§1–3, incl. Art. 26 deployer duties and Art. 27 FRIA) | 2 Aug 2026 | **2 Dec 2027** (Art. 113(c)(i)) |\n| **Annex I / Art. 6(1) product-embedded high-risk systems** | 2 Aug 2027 | **2 Aug 2028** (Art. 113(c)(ii)) |\n| Legacy high-risk systems intended for public authorities (Art. 111(2)) | 2 Aug 2030 | Unchanged |\n| AI components of large-scale EU IT systems, Annex X (Art. 111(1)) | 31 Dec 2030 | Unchanged |\n\n**Other deadlines the Omnibus added:** Commission delegated acts on limiting duplicated requirements for Annex I Section A products (Art. 2(13)), by 2 Aug 2027. Guidelines on how the Act fits with Annex I sectoral law (Art. 96(1)(g)), by 1 Aug 2027. Post-market monitoring plan guidance and template (Art. 72(3)), by 2 Sep 2027. Existing Annex I notified bodies must apply for AI Act designation by 28 Jan 2028 (Art. 43(3)).\n\n**Legacy high-risk systems.** Art. 111(2) now keys the transition to \"the date of application of Chapter III\" rather than to 2 August 2026. A high-risk system already on the market before its category's high-risk date is caught only if its design later changes significantly. Recital 177 treats \"significant change\" as equivalent in substance to \"substantial modification\".\n\n> **Summary, as enacted:** prohibitions Feb 2025 → GPAI, governance, penalties Aug 2025 → general application and Annex III high-risk Aug 2026 → Annex I high-risk Aug 2027.\n> **Summary, as amended:** prohibitions Feb 2025 → GPAI Aug 2025 → transparency and Commission GPAI enforcement Aug 2026 → new bans and legacy marking Dec 2026 → sandboxes Aug 2027 → Annex III high-risk Dec 2027 → Annex I high-risk Aug 2028.\n\n---\n\n## 13. The Digital Omnibus on AI (Regulation (EU) 2026/1744): What Changed\n\n**Procedure:** Commission proposal 19 November 2025 (part of the digital omnibus package); provisional political agreement 7 May 2026; Parliament position 16 June 2026; Council decision 29 June 2026; signed 8 July 2026; published in the OJ 24 July 2026; in force 27 July 2026. The Omnibus also amends Regulation (EU) 2018/1139 (civil aviation) and Regulation (EU) 2023/1230 (machinery). The final text uses **fixed dates**, not a trigger tied to standards being ready.\n\n| Topic | As enacted | As amended by the 2026 Omnibus | Provision |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **AI literacy** | Providers and deployers must \"take measures to ensure, to their best extent, a sufficient level of AI literacy\" of staff | Providers and deployers must **take measures to support** AI literacy of staff and others acting for them. No guaranteed level for any individual. The Commission and Member States must support compliance (especially for SMEs); the Commission publishes practical examples; the Board adopts recommendations | Art. 4 (replaced) |\n| **Special-category data for bias detection** | High-risk providers only, \"strictly necessary\", with safeguards | New **Art. 4a**. Para 1 keeps the high-risk provider rule (conditions (a)–(f): no effective alternative such as synthetic or anonymised data; re-use limits and pseudonymisation; access controls; no transfer to other parties; deletion once the bias is corrected; ROPA records why it was necessary). Para 2 extends it to providers and deployers of **other AI systems and models** and to deployers of high-risk systems, under the same conditions. It creates no duty to do bias testing, and GDPR still applies on top | Art. 4a (new); Art. 10(5) deleted; Art. 2(7) |\n| **Prohibitions** | Eight practices, from 2 Feb 2025 | Adds AI generating **non-consensual intimate imagery** of identifiable people and **CSAM**, from **2 Dec 2026** | Art. 5(1)(ba)–(bb), 5(1a)–(1b); Art. 113(a) |\n| **High-risk dates** | Annex III 2 Aug 2026; Annex I 2 Aug 2027 | Annex III **2 Dec 2027**; Annex I **2 Aug 2028** | Art. 113(c) |\n| **Legacy generative systems** | Art. 50(2) from 2 Aug 2026 | Generative systems placed on the market before 2 Aug 2026 have until **2 Dec 2026** to add machine-readable marking | Art. 111(4) |\n| **Registration of Art. 6(3) \"not high-risk\" systems** | Provider registers in the EU database with the Annex VIII §B data, incl. a summary of grounds and the Member States concerned | **Still registers** (Art. 49(2) unchanged), but Annex VIII §B points 7 (summary of grounds) and 9 (Member States) are deleted | Annex VIII §B |\n| **Small mid-caps (SMCs)** | SME-only simplifications | Several SME measures extended to SMCs (Recommendation (EU) 2025/1099): simplified technical documentation form (Art. 11(1)), proportionate QMS (Art. 17(2)), priority sandbox access, and fine caps for Art. 99(4)–(5) fines (Art. 99(6a)). The simplified QMS route in Art. 63 extends from microenterprises to all SMEs | Arts. 1(2)(g), 3(14a)–(14b), 11, 17, 63, 99 |\n| **Sandboxes** | National sandbox operational by 2 Aug 2026 | By **2 Aug 2027**. The AI Office may run a **Union-level sandbox** for systems under its competence, with priority access for SMEs and SMCs | Art. 57(1), 57(3a) |\n| **AI Office competence** | Exclusive powers over GPAI *models* (Art. 88); systems built on a provider's own GPAI model were monitored under Art. 75(1) | **Exclusive competence** over (a) AI systems built on a GPAI model by the same provider or group (carve-outs: Annex I products, Annex III point 2, certain law-enforcement, border and financial-institution systems, justice), and (b) AI in DSA-designated very large online platforms and search engines. New powers include investigations, on-site inspections, binding commitments, non-compliance decisions, fines under Art. 99(3)–(7), and periodic penalty payments of up to 5% of average daily turnover | Art. 75(1)–(2a), Arts. 75a–75d |\n| **FRIA and DPIA** | FRIA may be complemented by an existing DPIA | Deployer may cross-reference or reuse relevant DPIA sections; the AI Office must provide a questionnaire template, including an automated tool | Art. 27(4)–(5) |\n| **Codes of practice** | AI Office and Board assess adequacy; Commission may give a code general validity by implementing act | The Commission, taking utmost account of the Board, assesses and publishes adequacy (Art. 56(6)). For Art. 50 codes it assesses adequacy and may adopt common rules by implementing act if a code is inadequate (Art. 50(7)) | Arts. 50(7), 56(6) |\n| **Products and machinery** | Machinery Directive listed in Annex I Section A (full AI Act high-risk regime) | Machinery moved to Annex I **Section B** (Machinery Regulation 2023/1230), where AI requirements flow through sectoral delegated acts applying by 2 Aug 2028. Non-safety \"user assistance, optimisation, convenience\" functions are not safety components (Art. 6(1a)–(1c)). CRA-compliant systems are deemed to meet Art. 15 cybersecurity (Art. 42(3)) | Annex I; Arts. 2(2), 6, 42 |\n| **Notified-body codes** | — | New **Annex XIV** lists the codes that define a notified body's scope, including a code for emerging technologies that names \"Agentic AI\" (a scoping label, not new agent obligations) | Art. 30(2); Annex XIV |\n\n> **What did not change:** the four-tier structure, the penalty ceilings (35/7, 15/3, 7.5/1), the GPAI obligations and their dates, the 10²⁵ FLOP systemic-risk presumption, and the general application date of 2 August 2026.\n\n---\n\n## 14. Implementation Status: Guidance, Codes, Standards and Enforcement (as of September 2026)\n\n| Instrument | Date | Status and key content |\n|---|---|---|\n| Guidelines on prohibited AI practices | 4 Feb 2025 | Non-binding Commission interpretation of Art. 5; authoritative interpretation rests with the CJEU |\n| Guidelines on the AI system definition | 6 Feb 2025 | Non-binding; explain Art. 3(1) |\n| GPAI Code of Practice | Published 10 Jul 2025; adequacy confirmed 1 Aug 2025 | Voluntary; three chapters (Transparency, Copyright, Safety & Security); 21 signatories plus xAI for Safety & Security only. See [[GPAI Code of Practice — Key Provisions]] |\n| GPAI guidelines (scope of obligations) | 18 Jul 2025 | **Indicative GPAI criterion:** training compute above 10²³ FLOP and able to generate language, text-to-image or text-to-video. **Systemic risk presumed** above 10²⁵ FLOP (Art. 51(2)). A downstream **modifier becomes the provider** if the modification uses more than **one third** of the original model's training compute. Legacy models need not be retrained or unlearned where that is impossible or disproportionate |\n| Template for the public summary of training content (Art. 53(1)(d)) | 24 Jul 2025 | Mandatory template for the GPAI training-data summary |\n| Draft guidance and template on serious incidents for high-risk systems (Art. 73) | 26 Sep 2025 (consultation to 7 Nov 2025) | Draft; aligned with the OECD AI Incidents Monitor |\n| Serious-incident reporting template for systemic-risk GPAI (Art. 55(1)(c)) | 4 Nov 2025 | Final template; supports Commitment 9 of the GPAI Code |\n| Art. 112 review of Art. 5 and Annex III | May 2026 | Flagged the intimate-imagery and CSAM gap (closed by the Omnibus); otherwise too early for changes |\n| Draft guidelines on high-risk classification (Art. 6) | 19 May 2026 (consultation to 23 Jul 2026) | **Still a draft**; final expected by end of 2026. The original 2 Feb 2026 deadline was missed |\n| Scientific Panel (60 experts) and Advisory Forum | Appointed 1 Jun 2026 | Two-year terms; the panel focuses on GPAI, systemic risk, model classification and evaluation methods |\n| Code of Practice on Marking and Labelling (Code of Practice on Transparency of AI-generated Content) | Final 10 Jun 2026 | Voluntary; Section 1 covers providers (marking and detection), Section 2 covers deployers (labelling deepfakes and public-interest text). Judged adequate by the Commission and AI Board; about 190 signatories by end of July 2026; EU icons available for labels |\n| Guidelines on Art. 50 transparency obligations | 20 Jul 2026 | Commission guidance on chatbots, marking, deepfake and text disclosure, applicable from 2 Aug 2026 |\n| **EN 18286:2026** (QMS for EU AI Act purposes) | Published July 2026 | First CEN-CENELEC standard supporting the Act, aimed at Art. 17. A presumption of conformity arises only once its reference is published in the OJ (Art. 40(1)) |\n| Enforcement | From 2 Aug 2026 | The AI Office enforces GPAI obligations with fines of up to 3% or €15m. Its first tool is \"technical compliance dialogues\". It runs a complaint tool, a whistleblower tool (Art. 87 applies Directive (EU) 2019/1937), and a downstream-provider complaints channel. **No AI Act fine has been publicly announced**; online reports of \"first fines\" are not supported by any Commission source |\n\n---\n\n## 15. Key Roles & Definitions (Art. 3)\n\n| Role | Definition | Art. 3 |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Provider** | Develops an AI system/GPAI model, or has one developed and places it on the market / puts into service under own name/trademark | (3) |\n| **Deployer** | Uses an AI system under its authority (except personal non-professional activity) | (4) |\n| **Importer** | Established in Union; places on market a system bearing name/trademark of third-country entity | (6) |\n| **Distributor** | In supply chain (not provider/importer) making AI system available on Union market | (7) |\n| **Authorised Representative** | Established in Union; mandated by third-country provider to perform obligations under the Regulation | (5) |\n| **Operator** | Umbrella term: provider, product manufacturer, deployer, authorised representative, importer, or distributor | (8) |\n\n---\n\n## 16. Relationship to GDPR & Other Union Law\n\n- The Act is **complementary to and does not affect** GDPR (Regulation 2016/679), LED (Directive 2016/680), DSA (Regulation 2022/2065), and consumer/product safety law (Art. 2(5)–(9))\n- As amended by the 2026 Omnibus, Art. 2(7) says the Act does not affect the GDPR, EUDPR, ePrivacy Directive or LED \"without prejudice to Articles 4a and 59\", the two provisions that themselves supply a legal basis for certain processing\n- Data subjects retain all GDPR rights, including rights related to **solely automated individual decision-making, including profiling** (Recital 10)\n- High-risk AI systems involving personal data must comply with both the AI Act **and** GDPR\n- Special category data processing for bias detection: as enacted under strict Art. 10(5) safeguards; as amended under **Art. 4a**, which also reaches non-high-risk systems and models. GDPR conditions apply in addition (see [[GDPR — AI-Relevant Provisions]])\n- FRIA and DPIA: a deployer may reuse or cross-reference its GDPR Art. 35 DPIA in the Art. 27 FRIA (Art. 27(4) as amended)\n\n---\n\n## 17. Article Quick-Reference Matrix\n\n| Topic | Key Articles |\n|---|---|\n| Definitions | Art. 3 |\n| AI Literacy | Art. 4 (replaced by the 2026 Omnibus) |\n| Special-category data for bias detection | Art. 4a (new, 2026 Omnibus; replaces Art. 10(5)) |\n| **Prohibited Practices** | Art. 5 (points (ba)–(bb) added by the 2026 Omnibus) |\n| High-Risk Classification | Arts. 6–7, Annex III |\n| Risk Management | Art. 9 |\n| Data Governance | Art. 10 |\n| Technical Documentation | Art. 11 (+ Annex IV) |\n| Record-Keeping / Logging | Art. 12 |\n| Transparency (High-Risk) | Art. 13 |\n| Human Oversight | Art. 14 |\n| Accuracy, Robustness, Cybersecurity | Art. 15 |\n| Provider Obligations | Art. 16 |\n| Quality Management System | Art. 17 |\n| Importer Obligations | Art. 23 |\n| Distributor Obligations | Art. 24 |\n| Value Chain Responsibilities | Art. 25 |\n| Deployer Obligations | Art. 26 |\n| Fundamental Rights Impact Assessment | Art. 27 |\n| **Transparency (Limited Risk)** | Art. 50 |\n| GPAI Classification | Arts. 51–52 |\n| GPAI Obligations | Art. 53 |\n| GPAI Systemic Risk Obligations | Art. 55 |\n| Codes of Practice (GPAI) | Art. 56 |\n| AI Regulatory Sandboxes | Arts. 57–58 |\n| Conformity Assessment | Art. 43 |\n| CE Marking | Art. 48 |\n| EU Database Registration | Art. 49 |\n| Post-Market Monitoring | Art. 72 |\n| Serious Incident Reporting | Art. 73 |\n| Governance (AI Office, Board) | Arts. 64–70 |\n| AI Office supervision of certain AI systems | Arts. 75(1), 75a–75d (2026 Omnibus) |\n| Whistleblowing | Art. 87 (applies Directive (EU) 2019/1937) |\n| **Penalties** | Arts. 99–101 (Art. 99(6a) SMCs added by the 2026 Omnibus) |\n| Legacy systems / transitional rules | Art. 111 (para 4 added by the 2026 Omnibus) |\n| **Entry into Force / Timeline** | Art. 113 (points (a) and (c) replaced, (d) added by the 2026 Omnibus) |\n\n---\n\n## 18. Related Notes\n\n- [[The EU AI Act]] — Main study chapter\n- [[GPAI Code of Practice — Key Provisions]] — Code status and signatories\n- [[Privacy and Data Protection Law]] — GDPR/LED intersection\n- [[GDPR — AI-Relevant Provisions]] — Article-level GDPR detail, EDPB and CJEU developments\n- [[AIGP Body of Knowledge — Exam Blueprint]] — Exam coverage mapping\n- [[EU AI Act — Annex III Deep Dive]] — Detailed high-risk use case analysis *(to be created)*\n- [[GPAI Model Obligations under the EU AI Act]] — GPAI deep dive *(to be created)*\n\n---\n\n## Sources\n\n- Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, OJ L, 12.7.2024: http://data.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1689/oj\n- Regulation (EU) 2026/1744 (Digital Omnibus on AI), OJ L, 24.7.2026: http://data.europa.eu/eli/reg/2026/1744/oj\n- Commission, \"AI Omnibus enters into force\": https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/ai-omnibus-enters-force\n- AI Act Service Desk, implementation timeline: https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/timeline/timeline-implementation-eu-ai-act\n- Commission, enforcement of the AI Act: https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/enforcement-ai-act\n- Commission GPAI guidelines (C(2025) 5045 approval; C(2025) 7719): https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/library/guidelines-scope-obligations-providers-general-purpose-ai-models-under-ai-act\n- Code of Practice on Transparency of AI-generated Content: https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/code-practice-ai-generated-content\n- Art. 50 guidelines: https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/library/guidelines-transparency-obligations-providers-and-deployers-ai-systems\n- Draft Art. 6 guidelines: https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/library/draft-commission-guidelines-classification-high-risk-ai-systems\n- CEN-CENELEC on EN 18286:2026: https://www.cencenelec.eu/news-events/news/2026/en-in-the-spotlight/2026-07-30-ai-quality-management/\n\n*Last updated: 2026-09-23 | Based on Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 as published in OJ L, 12.7.2024, and as amended by Regulation (EU) 2026/1744 (OJ L, 24.7.2026)*\n\n---\n\n> [!source] Framework Recall Cards — AIGP Study Kit\n\n## EU AI Act\n\n*The single heaviest topic. Know tiers, roles, obligations, dates.*\n\n### The four risk tiers, with examples of each\n\n**Prohibited (unacceptable):** social scoring (by public *or* private actors), subliminal/manipulative techniques, exploiting vulnerabilities, untargeted facial-image scraping, emotion recognition in workplace/education, real-time remote biometric ID in public for law enforcement (narrow exceptions), biometric categorisation inferring sensitive traits, predictive policing based solely on profiling. *Added by the 2026 omnibus (not on the exam): AI-generated child sexual abuse material and non-consensual intimate deepfakes ('nudifiers').*\n**High-risk:** Annex I (safety component of regulated products) + Annex III (employment, education, credit/essential services, law enforcement, migration, justice, biometrics, critical infrastructure).\n**Limited:** chatbots, deepfakes → **transparency obligations** only.\n**Minimal:** spam filters, AI in games → largely unregulated.\n\n### The four actor roles, and what flips a deployer into a provider\n\n**Provider** — develops and places on the market under its own name.\n**Deployer** — uses the system under its own authority.\n**Importer** — places a third-country system on the EU market.\n**Distributor** — makes it available in the supply chain without being provider or importer.\n\nA deployer **becomes a provider** if it: puts its own name/brand on a high-risk system, makes a **substantial modification**, or **changes the intended purpose**. Very common exam scenario.\n\n### The high-risk obligation checklist\n\nRisk management system · data governance (quality, bias examination) · technical documentation · automatic logging/record-keeping · transparency and instructions for use · **human oversight** · accuracy, robustness, cybersecurity · quality management system · **conformity assessment** → **CE marking** → EU database registration · post-market monitoring · serious incident reporting.\n\n### What a non-EU provider must do, and does the Act reach them at all\n\nYes — the Act is **extraterritorial**. A non-EU provider of a **high-risk AI system** (Art 22) or of a **general-purpose AI model** (Art 54) must appoint an **authorized representative established in the Union**. Providers of other systems have no such duty, though the Act can still reach them, including where a system's output is used in the EU. 'The developer is outside the EU so nobody is covered' is always a wrong answer.\n\n### GPAI obligations, and what triggers the systemic-risk tier\n\nAll GPAI: technical documentation, information for downstream providers, **copyright policy**, and a **sufficiently detailed summary of training content**.\nGPAI with **systemic risk** (presumed at **10^25 FLOPs** training compute) adds: model evaluations, adversarial testing, systemic risk assessment and mitigation, serious incident reporting, cybersecurity protection.\n\n### The implementation timeline — and the part that changed after the book\n\n**Give these on the exam:** 1 Aug 2024 entry into force · 2 Feb 2025 prohibitions + AI literacy · 2 Aug 2025 GPAI rules, governance, penalties, notified bodies · **2 Aug 2026 Annex III high-risk requirements** + Art 50 transparency · 2 Aug 2027 Annex I (product-embedded) high-risk obligations + grace deadline for GPAI models already on the market before Aug 2025.\n\n> [!warning] **Current law differs.** Regulation (EU) 2026/1744 (Digital Omnibus on AI) entered into force 27 July 2026 and deferred high-risk obligations: **Annex III → 2 December 2027**, **Annex I → 2 August 2028**. Prohibitions, GPAI rules and the 2027 GPAI grace period are unchanged, and Art 50 transparency still applies from 2 Aug 2026. The omnibus also softened **AI literacy** (Art 4): providers and deployers must now 'take measures to support' it rather than ensure it. It also added two new prohibitions (AI-generated CSAM and non-consensual intimate deepfakes). Your exam is written from BoK v2.1, which predates this — answer 2 Aug 2026 on test day, but know the real dates for work.\n\n### The penalty tiers\n\nUp to **€35M or 7%** of global annual turnover — prohibited practices.\nUp to **€15M or 3%** — most other obligations (including high-risk and GPAI).\nUp to **€7.5M or 1%** — supplying incorrect or misleading information to authorities.\nWhichever is higher, except for SMEs and startups, where the cap is whichever is **lower** (Art 99(6)).", "t_risktiers": "## At a Glance\n\nThe EU AI Act's organizing logic: regulate by risk, not by technology. Four tiers - unacceptable risk (prohibited outright), high risk (permitted under heavy obligations, defined by Annex I products and Annex III uses), limited risk (transparency duties under Article 50), and minimal risk (largely unregulated). The tier is contextual: the same model can sit in different tiers depending on its use.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\n### The four tiers\n\n| Tier | Test | Consequence |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Prohibited** | Unacceptable risk to rights or safety | Banned outright (Article 5) |\n| **High-risk** | Annex I safety components, or Annex III use cases | Full Chapter III obligations chain |\n| **Limited** | Interaction with users, or synthetic content | Transparency duties (Article 50) |\n| **Minimal** | Negligible risk | No new obligations; voluntary codes |\n\n### Prohibited: eight as enacted, ten as amended\n\nAs enacted, Article 5(1) lists eight practices: subliminal or purposefully manipulative techniques that distort behaviour; exploitation of vulnerabilities from age, disability or social or economic situation; **social scoring** by public or private actors; individual criminal-offence risk prediction based solely on profiling; untargeted scraping of facial images to build recognition databases; **emotion recognition** in workplaces and educational institutions; biometric categorisation by sensitive traits; and **real-time remote biometric identification** in publicly accessible spaces, with narrow law-enforcement exceptions under judicial or independent authorisation. As amended by the Digital Omnibus on AI (Regulation (EU) 2026/1744), two more apply from **2 December 2026**: AI that generates or manipulates realistic intimate or sexual imagery of an identifiable person without explicit consent (\"nudification\"), and AI that generates child sexual abuse material (Article 5(1)(ba)-(bb)). A provider is caught by the two new bans only if that is the system's intended purpose or a foreseeable outcome it lacks reasonable safeguards against.\n\n### High-risk: two routes\n\nRoute one (Annex I): an AI system that is a safety component of a product under EU harmonisation law, or is itself such a product - machinery, medical devices, vehicles, and so on. Route two (Annex III): biometrics; critical infrastructure (energy, water, transport); education and vocational training; employment, worker management and access to self-employment; access to essential private and public services including credit scoring and welfare; law enforcement; migration, asylum and border control; and the administration of justice and democratic processes.\n\n### Limited risk: Article 50\n\nProviders must make chatbots disclose that they are AI, and mark synthetic output in machine-readable form. Deployers must disclose deepfakes, emotion-recognition and biometric-categorisation use, and AI-generated text published on matters of public interest unless it was human-reviewed under editorial responsibility. These duties apply from **2 August 2026**; generative systems already on the market before then have until **2 December 2026** for marking (Article 111(4)). An opt-out or human alternative is good practice, not an Article 50 duty.\n\n### Minimal risk and reclassification\n\nSpam filters, recommendation engines and grammar checkers carry no new obligations, though voluntary codes are encouraged. Because the tier is contextual, a system classified as minimal or limited can become high-risk when its use changes, so continuous monitoring and reassessment are advised. A deployer that substantially modifies a high-risk system becomes its provider and inherits the full chain.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nThe most reliable EU AI Act question: classify a described system into its tier, or spot the prohibited practice in a lineup. Memorize the prohibited list (and the two 2026 additions) and representative Annex III categories. Remember that high-risk classification triggers the full Chapter III obligations chain, and that the tier is contextual rather than intrinsic to the technology.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Prohibited as enacted: subliminal manipulation, exploiting vulnerabilities, social scoring, criminal-offence prediction, untargeted facial scraping, workplace and school emotion recognition, biometric categorisation by sensitive traits, real-time public biometric ID\n- Two new prohibitions from 2 December 2026: non-consensual intimate imagery (nudification) and CSAM, for ten in total\n- High-risk route 1: a safety component of a product under EU harmonisation law (Annex I); route 2: Annex III use cases (biometrics, critical infrastructure, education, employment, essential services including credit, law enforcement, migration, justice)\n- Limited risk: Article 50 transparency, applying from 2 August 2026, with a 2 December 2026 marking deadline for legacy generative systems\n- Minimal risk: no new obligations; voluntary codes encouraged, and documentation now reduces cost if reclassification comes later\n- The tier is contextual: the same model can be minimal in one use and high-risk in another, and a deployer that substantially modifies a system becomes its provider\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1689/oj\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 (EU AI Act)</a> · Article 5 prohibitions, Article 6 and the Annexes for high-risk, Article 50 for transparency\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2026/1744/oj/eng\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Digital Omnibus on AI, Regulation (EU) 2026/1744</a> · the two new Article 5(1)(ba)-(bb) prohibitions from 2 December 2026\n- <a href=\"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/code-practice-ai-generated-content\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Art. 50 transparency guidelines and Code of Practice (European Commission)</a> · how the marking and labelling duties work in practice\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] The EU AI Act\n\n## 1. Risk Classification\n\nThe EU AI Act introduces a four-tier, risk-based classification framework. Each tier dictates the obligations developers, providers, and deployers must follow. Organizations outside the EU are adopting this scheme as a baseline.\n\n### Risk Classification Overview\n\n| Risk Category | Definition | Examples | Regulatory Requirements |\n|---------------|------------|----------|------------------------|\n| **Prohibited AI** | Poses unacceptable risk to rights/safety | Social scoring, subliminal manipulation | **Completely banned** |\n| **High-risk AI** | Impacts fundamental rights or safety | Credit scoring, AI hiring tools | Conformity assessments, logging, oversight, CE marking |\n| **Limited-risk AI** | Requires transparency but limited impact | Chatbots, synthetic media (deepfakes) | Disclosure, marking and labelling (Art. 50); opt-outs are good practice, not an Art. 50 duty |\n| **Minimal-risk AI** | Poses negligible risk | Spam filters, streaming recommendations, grammar checkers | No requirements (voluntary best practices) |\n\n---\n\n### 1.1 Prohibited AI Systems\n\n**Prohibited AI** refers to use cases that pose an unacceptable risk to safety, human dignity, or fundamental rights and are **banned outright**. These systems are not allowed to be placed on the market, put into service, or used anywhere in the EU.\n\n**Examples of prohibited AI:**\n\n| Prohibited Practice | Description |\n|---------------------|-------------|\n| **Subliminal manipulation** | AI that manipulates behavior by bypassing consciousness (e.g., neuro-influencing technologies) |\n| **Social scoring** | Ranking individuals over time on social behaviour or personal traits, leading to unjustified or out-of-context detrimental treatment. The final text covers public **and** private actors |\n| **Exploiting vulnerable groups** | Targeting children, elderly, or disabled individuals to distort behavior |\n| **Real-time remote biometric identification in public spaces** | Banned with limited exceptions for serious crimes under judicial oversight |\n| **Individual criminal offense risk assessment or prediction** | Predictive policing based solely on profiling |\n| **Emotional recognition in workplaces and educational institutions** | Banned due to manipulation, discrimination, and pseudoscience risks |\n| **Non-consensual intimate imagery and CSAM** *(added by the 2026 Digital Omnibus; applies from 2 Dec 2026)* | AI that generates or manipulates sexual imagery of identifiable people without their explicit consent (\"nudification\" apps), or child sexual abuse material (Art. 5(1)(ba)–(bb)) |\n\n> **Count check:** the table above is illustrative. As enacted, Art. 5(1) lists eight practices, (a)–(h), including untargeted facial-image scraping and biometric categorisation by sensitive traits. As amended by the 2026 Omnibus it lists ten.\n\n> **Exception:** Real-time biometric identification may be permitted under strict conditions (national security threats, ongoing criminal investigations) but must be individually authorized by a legal authority.\n\n> **Exam note:** Developers (vendor organizations) must ensure their system design, training data, and use cases do not cross into prohibited territory.\n\n---\n\n### 1.2 High-Risk AI Systems\n\n**High-risk AI** systems have the potential to significantly affect people's rights, lives, or safety and are subject to the most stringent regulatory controls.\n\n**Categories of High-risk AI (Annex III):**\n\n- Biometric identification and categorization\n- Critical infrastructure management (energy grids, wastewater, traffic control)\n- Education and vocational training (AI grading tools, lesson planning)\n- Employment, worker management, and access to self-employment (AI interviews, performance evaluations)\n- Access to and enjoyment of essential private and public services (credit scoring, welfare benefits)\n- Law enforcement (crime prediction tools)\n- Migration, asylum, and border control\n- Administration of justice and democratic processes\n\n**Core Compliance Requirements:**\n\n- **Conformity assessments** — demonstrated before market placement\n- **Risk management and data governance** — data quality, bias minimization, operational risk management\n- **Human oversight** — meaningful human intervention and escalation capability\n- **Technical documentation and logging** — lifecycle documentation and automatic logging\n\n> **CE Marking:** High-risk AI systems must bear the CE mark to indicate conformity with EU standards — similar to other product safety marks.\n\n---\n\n### 1.3 Limited-Risk AI Systems\n\n**Limited-risk AI** refers to AI systems that interact with users but do not significantly impact safety or rights.\n\n| Examples | Transparency Obligations |\n|----------|-------------------------|\n| Chatbots and virtual assistants | **Disclosure of AI use** — users must be informed they are interacting with AI |\n| AI-generated media (deepfakes, synthetic voices) | **Synthetic media disclosure** — generated content must be labeled as such |\n| Educational tools (AI learning assistants) | **User autonomy** — offering an opt-out or a human alternative is good practice; Art. 50 itself requires disclosure, not an opt-out |\n| Social media filters/avatars | |\n\n> **Art. 50 in one line:** providers must make chatbots disclose that they are AI and mark synthetic output in machine-readable form. Deployers must disclose deepfakes, emotion-recognition and biometric-categorisation use, and AI-generated text published on matters of public interest (unless it was human-reviewed under editorial responsibility). These duties apply from **2 August 2026**. As amended by the 2026 Omnibus, generative systems already on the market before then have until **2 December 2026** for marking.\n\n> **Dark patterns note:** Even limited-risk systems using dark patterns or manipulative UI may trigger scrutiny under consumer protection laws (separate from the EU AI Act and GDPR). See [[Consumer Protection and AI|Privacy and Data Protection Law]].\n\n---\n\n### 1.4 Minimal-Risk AI Systems\n\n**Minimal-risk AI** systems pose negligible or no risk to users or society. They represent the majority of AI systems and are largely unregulated.\n\n**Examples:** Spam filters, recommendation engines (Netflix, YouTube, Amazon Prime), AI-enabled grammar checkers.\n\n**Encouraged practices:** Voluntary codes of conduct, ethical guidelines, transparency practices. Documenting design and training now may reduce compliance costs later if reclassification occurs.\n\n---\n\n### 1.5 Risk Reclassification and Monitoring\n\nSystems initially categorized as minimal- or limited-risk may become high-risk depending on their use context. **Continuous monitoring and reassessment** are advised.\n\n---", "t_highrisk_req": "## At a Glance\n\nWhat a high-risk classification triggers: a documented risk-management system, data governance, technical documentation, automatic logging, instructions for use, human oversight, accuracy and cybersecurity, a quality management system, a conformity assessment with CE marking, EU database registration and post-market monitoring. Deployers, importers and distributors carry their own duties, and a deployer that substantially modifies a system becomes a provider.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\n### The obligations chain\n\nA high-risk **provider** owes, before placing the system on the market and then across its life:\n\n- A documented, ongoing **risk-management system**: identify known and foreseeable risks (including reasonably foreseeable misuse), evaluate and monitor them across the lifecycle, and apply control measures.\n- **Data governance**: training, validation and testing datasets that are relevant, sufficiently representative, and as free from errors and bias as possible, with documented lineage and version control, and compliance with privacy law.\n- **Technical documentation**: architecture, algorithms, training methodologies, risk-management and human-oversight protocols.\n- **Automatic logging**: records that support post-market monitoring, incident investigation and audit.\n- **Instructions for use**: intended purpose and limitations, human-oversight responsibilities, performance metrics and conditions of use, and contact points.\n- **Human oversight**: design for anomaly detection, effective override or shut-off, and human-in-the-loop review of consequential decisions. Oversight must be genuine, not rubber-stamping.\n- **Accuracy, robustness and cybersecurity**, declared in the instructions.\n- A **quality management system**: documented policies, corrective and preventive action, documentation control, monitoring and reporting, and resource and training management.\n- A **conformity assessment** (internal control in some cases, a notified body in others) and **CE marking** once conformity is confirmed, plus registration in the EU database.\n- **Post-market monitoring** with serious-incident reporting.\n\n### The roles\n\n| Role | Core duties |\n|---|---|\n| **Provider** | The full chain above; places the system on the market under its own name |\n| **Deployer** | Uses the system per instructions; human oversight in use, input-data quality, log retention, incident reporting |\n| **Importer** | Places non-EU systems on the market: verify CE marking and documentation, register, cooperate with authorities |\n| **Distributor** | Makes systems available without modifying them: verify CE marking and contacts, suspend if non-compliance is suspected |\n\nRole dynamics matter: a deployer that substantially modifies a system, or builds its own downstream application on a GPAI model, becomes a provider. An importer is liable if it places a non-compliant system on the market. An organization can hold several roles at once, and cloud or AI-as-a-Service arrangements blur them, so an \"AI role assessment\" per system is best practice.\n\n### Standards and the spirit\n\nThe risk-management and QMS requirements are not satisfied by citing a framework: ISO 31000, ISO 23894 or ISO 27001 are useful guidance but not a substitute. EN 18286:2026 is the QMS standard for Article 17, and gives a presumption of conformity only once it is cited in the Official Journal.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nExpect a list question on the high-risk obligations chain, or a role question asking who owes what. The trap is confusing provider and deployer duties: the provider builds and documents, the deployer uses and monitors, and only a substantial modification turns a deployer into a provider. Remember that a conformity assessment can be internal or by a notified body, and that CE marking follows conformity.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Provider chain: risk management, data governance, technical documentation, logging, instructions, human oversight, accuracy and security, QMS, conformity assessment, CE marking, registration, post-market monitoring\n- Data governance means relevant, representative training, validation and test data, as free from errors and bias as possible, with documented lineage\n- Human oversight must be genuine: detection, override or shut-off, and human review of consequential decisions, not rubber-stamping\n- Deployers owe oversight in use, input-data quality, log retention and incident reporting; importers and distributors verify CE marking and documentation\n- A deployer that substantially modifies a high-risk system becomes its provider and inherits the full chain\n- Conformity assessment is internal control or a notified body depending on the system; CE marking follows, then EU database registration\n- Referencing ISO 31000, 23894 or 27001 is guidance, not compliance; EN 18286:2026 gives a presumption of conformity only once cited in the Official Journal\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1689/oj\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 (EU AI Act), Chapter III</a> · Articles 8 to 27: the high-risk requirements and the deployer duties\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2026/1744/oj/eng\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Digital Omnibus on AI, Regulation (EU) 2026/1744</a> · moved the high-risk application dates and adjusted documentation and QMS for smaller firms\n- <a href=\"https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/timeline/timeline-implementation-eu-ai-act\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AI Act implementation timeline (AI Act Service Desk)</a> · when the Chapter III duties start for Annex I and Annex III systems\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] The EU AI Act\n\n## 2. Classification Requirements\n\n> **Note:** There are no requirements for *prohibited AI* — these systems are not allowed at all. Requirements apply to the three permitted tiers.\n\n### 2.1 High-Risk AI: Full Compliance Framework\n\nHigh-risk AI carries the most stringent requirements. These mirror requirements found in mature systems development lifecycle frameworks.\n\n#### Risk Management System\n\nMust be documented and ongoing:\n\n- Proactively identify known and foreseeable risks (intended use + reasonably foreseeable misuse)\n- Evaluate and monitor risks throughout the system lifecycle\n- Implement risk control measures to eliminate or reduce identified risks\n\n**Reference frameworks:** NIST SP 800-37, ISO/IEC 27001, ISO/IEC 31000, ISO/IEC 23894:2023\n\n> **Exam note:** These frameworks are helpful guidance but not a substitute for the EU AI Act's explicit requirements.\n\n#### Data and Data Governance\n\nTraining, validation, and testing datasets must be **high-quality, relevant, representative, and as free from errors and bias as possible**.\n\n- Bias mitigation strategies\n- Data relevance to intended purpose\n- Data lineage documentation and version control\n- Compliance with all applicable privacy laws (including GDPR)\n\n**Data governance frameworks:** DMBOK, COBIT, DGI Framework. See also [[Data Governance and Management|Data Governance and Model Training]].\n\n#### Technical Documentation\n\nProvider must maintain detailed, formally managed documentation:\n\n- System architecture and specifications\n- Algorithms and training methodologies\n- Risk management and human oversight protocols\n\n#### Record Keeping and Logging\n\nLogs must be maintained throughout the AI system lifecycle to support:\n\n- Post-market monitoring\n- Event and incident investigation\n- Audit readiness and accountability\n- Change management at business and system levels\n\n#### Conformity Assessment\n\nBefore market placement:\n\n- Internal control (self-assessment) in some cases\n- Third-party assessment by a **Notified Body** in other cases\n- **CE marking** once conformity is confirmed\n\n#### Human Oversight\n\nAI systems must be designed for:\n\n- **Detection** of anomalies or signs of malfunction (disaster recovery: take offline and fix)\n- **Effective override or shut-off mechanisms** — pause AI use without disrupting critical business processes\n- **Human-in-the-loop decision review** — for loan approvals, employment screening, medical diagnosis, etc.\n\n#### Transparency and User Instructions\n\nUsers must receive clear instructions including:\n\n- Intended purpose and limitations\n- Human oversight responsibilities\n- Performance metrics and conditions of use (accuracy levels, expected error rates, environmental/data quality requirements)\n- Contact points for reporting incidents or questions\n\n#### Quality Management System (QMS)\n\nMust include:\n\n- Policies and procedures for design, development, testing, and control\n- Corrective and preventive action procedures\n- Documentation control\n- Processes for monitoring, reporting, and logging (including post-market surveillance)\n- Resource and training management\n\n**Reference standard:** ISO 9000.\n\n> **Lifecycle documentation:** Required not only pre-market but throughout the system's use — including updates, retraining events, and downstream adaptations by deployers.\n\n---\n\n### 2.2 Limited-Risk AI: Targeted Transparency Obligations\n\n#### Disclosure Requirements\n\n- Inform users when interacting with AI systems (chatbots, virtual assistants)\n- Inform users when viewing synthetic or manipulated content (deepfakes, images, text)\n\n#### Notification and User Autonomy\n\n- Conspicuous notifications that a system is AI-driven\n- Easily found opt-out mechanisms or human interaction alternatives (good practice; not required by Art. 50)\n- Transparent instructions on system use and limitations\n\n#### Documentation and Accountability\n\n- Maintain records of how transparency mechanisms are implemented\n- Monitor for misleading outputs that could impair user autonomy\n\n> **Regulatory intersection:** Limited-risk systems may still fall under broader EU privacy and/or consumer protection laws.\n\n---\n\n### 2.3 Minimal-Risk AI: Voluntary Best Practices\n\nNo mandatory requirements. Encouraged:\n\n- Simple notices where AI functionality may affect user decisions\n- Avoid misleading or manipulative design patterns\n- Maintain internal logs and quality checks\n- Consider accessibility accommodations and avoid reinforcing harmful stereotypes\n\n> **Forward-looking:** Minimal-risk systems deployed at scale or adapted to new contexts (education, public policy) could face reclassification. Voluntary documentation now reduces compliance costs later.\n\n---\n\n### 2.4 Avoid Checkbox Compliance\n\nOrganizations should pursue the **spirit and intention** of requirements, not just minimum \"checkbox compliance.\" Align compliance measures with mission, objectives, and culture.\n\n---", "t_gpai": "## At a Glance\n\nGeneral-purpose AI models - models that serve many purposes and can be integrated into a variety of downstream systems - carry their own obligations, scaled by whether they pose systemic risk. All providers owe documentation, downstream information, a copyright policy and a training-content summary. Systemic-risk models (presumed above 10^25 FLOP of training compute) add notification, adversarial evaluation, risk mitigation, incident reporting and cybersecurity.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\n### What GPAI is\n\nA GPAI model can serve multiple intended purposes and be integrated into a variety of downstream systems or applications. A foundation model is the base technical artifact; GPAI is about usage and availability across applications. Examples: large language models, vision foundation models such as CLIP or SAM, and multimodal models. The Commission's July 2025 guidelines treat a model trained with more than 10^23 FLOP that can generate language, images or video as indicatively general-purpose.\n\n### Standard obligations (Article 53)\n\nEvery GPAI provider owes:\n\n- Technical documentation of the model, its training and testing process, and its capabilities (Article 53(1)(a), Annex XI).\n- Information for downstream providers so they can understand capabilities and limits and comply (Article 53(1)(b), Annex XII).\n- A **policy to comply with EU copyright law**, honouring Article 4(3) text and data mining reservations, \"including through state-of-the-art technologies\" (Article 53(1)(c)).\n- A **public, sufficiently detailed summary of training content** using the AI Office template (Article 53(1)(d)).\n- For non-EU providers, an **EU authorised representative** (Article 54).\n\nThe copyright policy and training summary apply regardless of where training happened (recital 106), and open-source models are **not** exempt from them: Article 53(2) lifts only points (a) and (b), and not at all where the model has systemic risk.\n\n### Systemic risk (Articles 51 and 55)\n\nA model is **presumed** to have high-impact capabilities, and therefore systemic risk, when cumulative training compute exceeds **10^25 FLOP** (Article 51(2)), a rebuttable presumption. The Commission can also designate a model using the Annex XIII criteria (parameters, data size, compute, modalities, benchmarks, autonomy and tool access, and reach, presumed at 10,000 or more registered EU business users). The provider must notify the Commission within two weeks of meeting or knowing it will meet the threshold (Article 52(1)), and then:\n\n- Conduct and document model evaluation, including adversarial testing (Article 55(1)(a)).\n- Assess and mitigate systemic risks at Union level (Article 55(1)(b)).\n- Track and report serious incidents to the AI Office (Article 55(1)(c)).\n- Maintain adequate cybersecurity for the model and its infrastructure (Article 55(1)(d)).\n\nThe Act requires adversarial testing to be conducted and documented, not published. External audits, public red-team reports and researcher APIs are Code of Practice extras, not Article 55 duties, though the AI Office can demand information and access.\n\n### Timing\n\nGPAI obligations have applied since **2 August 2025**; the Commission's enforcement powers, including Article 101 fines of up to EUR 15m or 3%, apply from **2 August 2026**; and models placed on the market before 2 August 2025 have until **2 August 2027**. The 2026 Omnibus did not change these dates.\n\n### Downstream duties\n\nOrganizations that integrate GPAI into downstream systems must verify the model's conformity with published documentation, assess whether their use raises the risk classification, and ensure transparency toward end users. Deployers remain responsible for their own compliance even when the model is open-source.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nSeparate the two layers cleanly: standard GPAI duties (documentation, downstream information, copyright policy, training summary, EU representative) versus systemic-risk additions (notification, adversarial evaluation, risk mitigation, incident reporting, cybersecurity). Memorize 10^25 FLOP as the systemic-risk presumption and 10^23 FLOP as the indicative GPAI threshold. A favourite trap: claiming open-source models are exempt from the copyright policy and training summary, which they are not.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- All GPAI providers: technical documentation, downstream information, an EU copyright policy honouring Article 4(3) opt-outs, a public training-content summary, and an EU authorised representative for non-EU providers\n- Open-source models are exempt only from documentation and downstream information, and not at all when they have systemic risk\n- Systemic risk is presumed above 10^25 FLOP of training compute, or designated by the Commission using the Annex XIII criteria\n- Systemic-risk providers must notify the Commission, run documented adversarial evaluation, assess and mitigate Union-level risks, report serious incidents and maintain cybersecurity\n- The Act requires adversarial testing to be conducted and documented, not published; public red-team reports are Code of Practice extras\n- Timing: obligations from 2 August 2025, Commission enforcement and Article 101 fines from 2 August 2026, legacy models until 2 August 2027\n- The indicative GPAI threshold is 10^23 FLOP for a model that generates language, images or video; a modifier becomes the provider above one third of the original training compute\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1689/oj\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 (EU AI Act), Chapter V</a> · Articles 51 to 56 and 101: GPAI obligations, systemic risk and fines\n- <a href=\"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/contents-code-gpai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GPAI Code of Practice (European Commission)</a> · the voluntary route to demonstrate compliance with Articles 53 and 55\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2026/1744/oj/eng\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Digital Omnibus on AI, Regulation (EU) 2026/1744</a> · confirmed the GPAI dates and widened the AI Office's competence\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] The EU AI Act\n\n## 3. Requirements for General-Purpose AI (GPAI)\n\n### 3.1 Defining GPAI\n\n**General-purpose AI (GPAI)** systems are models that can serve multiple intended purposes and be integrated into a variety of downstream systems or applications.\n\n| Concept | Definition |\n|---------|------------|\n| **Foundation model** | The base technical artifact |\n| **General-purpose AI** | Usage and availability across diverse applications |\n\n**Examples:** LLMs (GPT-family, Llama, Claude), foundation models for vision (CLIP, SAM), multimodal models (text, images, video, audio).\n\n---\n\n### 3.2 General Obligations for GPAI Providers\n\n#### Technical Documentation and Transparency\n\n- Model architecture, training data sources, and capabilities\n- Intended and foreseeable uses\n- Known limitations, biases, and failure modes\n- Disclosure of fine-tuning on sensitive data\n- Risk identification and mitigation mechanisms\n\n#### Model Evaluation and Testing\n\nRisk assessments must evaluate:\n\n- Accuracy and robustness across various tasks\n- Bias and fairness across demographics\n- Environmental impact (energy consumption)\n\n> Where GPAI is expected to be integrated into high-risk applications, additional testing and safeguards are required.\n\n#### Data Governance\n\nDocument training dataset composition and provenance; mitigate copyright risks, personal data inclusion, and representational bias. See also [[Data Governance and Management|Data Governance and Model Training]].\n\n> **Transparency Summary Sheet:** The EU AI Act encourages standardized GPAI model cards or factsheets summarizing key characteristics and risks for downstream users.\n\n---\n\n### 3.3 GPAI with Systemic Risk — Enhanced Obligations\n\n#### What Qualifies as Systemic Risk?\n\n- **Compute presumption:** cumulative training compute above 10²⁵ FLOP creates a rebuttable presumption of high-impact capabilities (Art. 51(2)). The provider must notify the Commission within two weeks of meeting, or knowing it will meet, the threshold (Art. 52(1)).\n- **Commission designation:** on its own initiative or after a qualified alert from the Scientific Panel (Art. 90), using the Annex XIII criteria. These include parameters, data size, compute, modalities, benchmarks, autonomy and tool access, and reach, which is presumed at 10,000 or more registered EU business users.\n- **Indicative GPAI threshold:** the Commission's July 2025 guidelines treat a model trained with more than 10²³ FLOP that can generate language, images or video as indicatively general-purpose.\n\n#### Enhanced Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Standard GPAI | GPAI with Systemic Risk |\n|-------------|:---:|:---:|\n| Technical documentation and downstream information (Art. 53(1)(a)–(b), Annexes XI–XII) | Yes, unless an exempt open-source model | Yes (no open-source exemption) |\n| Copyright policy and public training-content summary (Art. 53(1)(c)–(d)) | Yes | Yes |\n| EU authorised representative for non-EU providers (Art. 54) | Yes, unless an exempt open-source model | Yes (no open-source exemption) |\n| Notify the Commission of the model (Art. 52(1)) | No | **Yes** |\n| Model evaluation incl. documented adversarial testing (Art. 55(1)(a)) | No | **Yes** |\n| Assess and mitigate Union-level systemic risks (Art. 55(1)(b)) | No | **Yes** |\n| Track and report serious incidents to the AI Office (Art. 55(1)(c)) | No | **Yes** |\n| Adequate cybersecurity for the model and its infrastructure (Art. 55(1)(d)) | No | **Yes** |\n\n> **Statute vs. extras:** the Act requires adversarial testing to be *conducted and documented*, not published. \"External audits\", public red-team reports or researcher APIs are not Art. 55 obligations. The AI Office can, however, demand information and access to evaluate a model (Arts. 91–92). Where extra measures appear in study materials, check whether they come from the voluntary GPAI Code of Practice rather than the Act (see [[GPAI Code of Practice — Key Provisions]]).\n\n**Enforcement timing:** GPAI obligations apply from 2 August 2025. The Commission's enforcement powers, including Art. 101 fines of up to 3% or €15m, apply from **2 August 2026**. Models placed on the market before 2 August 2025 have until **2 August 2027**. The 2026 Omnibus did not change these dates.\n\n---\n\n### 3.4 Obligations for Distributors and Deployers of GPAI\n\nOrganizations integrating GPAI into downstream systems (especially high-risk) must:\n\n- Verify model's conformity with published documentation\n- Assess their own use case for potential elevation in risk classification\n- Ensure transparency toward end users\n\n---\n\n### 3.5 Open-Source GPAI Considerations\n\nOpen-source GPAI developers may be **exempt from some obligations** if they do not derive commercial benefit or maintain operational control. However, **deployers remain responsible** for downstream compliance.\n\n### 3.6 Encouraged Practices Across All GPAI Models\n\n- Voluntary codes of conduct addressing accessibility, inclusiveness, environmental sustainability, and misuse prevention (election manipulation, malware generation, fraud)\n- Collaborate with academia and civil society\n- Support open benchmarks and transparency initiatives\n\n---", "t_enforcement": "## At a Glance\n\nWho enforces the EU AI Act and with what: the European AI Office (in the Commission) supervises GPAI and coordinates; national market surveillance authorities investigate, inspect, order corrective action and can withdraw or recall systems; and a three-tier penalty ladder reaches EUR 35m or 7% for prohibited practices. Individuals can complain, and civil liability runs through national law.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\n### The authorities\n\n| Authority | Role |\n|---|---|\n| **European AI Office** (in the Commission) | Supervises and enforces GPAI obligations (exclusive power under Article 88); issues guidance and runs codes of practice |\n| **National competent authorities** (one or more per member state) | Market surveillance for all AI systems not reserved to the AI Office: investigate, inspect, order, penalise |\n| **European Data Protection Supervisor** | Supervises AI systems used by EU institutions, bodies and agencies |\n\nAs amended by the 2026 Digital Omnibus on AI, the AI Office is also **exclusively** competent for AI systems built on a GPAI model by the same provider or group (with carve-outs for Annex I products, critical infrastructure, certain law-enforcement, border and financial systems, and justice) and for AI in DSA-designated very large online platforms and search engines. It gained investigation, inspection, commitment and fining procedures (Articles 75a to 75d) and can run a Union-level sandbox.\n\n### Powers and remedies\n\nAuthorities can require access to technical documentation, perform unannounced inspections including digital audits, mandate and verify corrective actions, suspend or prohibit marketing, require withdrawal or recall, direct updates or relabelling, and require notification of affected users. Individuals affected by non-compliant AI can complain to a market surveillance authority, and civil liability runs through national law and the revised product liability regime. Article 87 applies the EU Whistleblower Directive to AI Act reports, and the AI Office runs a whistleblower tool.\n\n### The penalty ladder\n\n| Violation | Ceiling |\n|---|---|\n| Prohibited practices | EUR 35m or 7% of global annual turnover, whichever is higher |\n| Most other obligations, including Commission GPAI fines (Article 101) | EUR 15m or 3%, whichever is higher |\n| Incorrect, incomplete or misleading information to authorities | EUR 7.5m or 1%, whichever is higher |\n\nSMEs and start-ups get the **lower** of the two figures on every tier. As amended, small mid-caps get the same lower-figure cap for the EUR 15m/3% and EUR 7.5m/1% tiers only, not for prohibited-practice fines. When setting a fine, authorities weigh the nature and significance of the breach, intent or negligence, history, and cooperation; voluntary disclosure and cooperation can mitigate. No AI Act fine had been publicly announced as of September 2026, and the AI Office says its first tool is \"technical compliance dialogues\".\n\n### Extraterritorial reach\n\nThe Act applies to non-EU entities that place AI systems on the EU market, make them available to EU users, deploy systems outside the EU that affect EU persons, or develop GPAI models integrated into EU-facing applications. Non-EU providers must appoint an EU-based representative and meet the same conformity, transparency and documentation duties.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nPenalty tiers are pure memorization: 35/7 for prohibitions, 15/3 for most other obligations and Commission GPAI fines, 7.5/1 for misleading information, always whichever is higher, with SMEs getting the lower figure. Know the AI Office versus national authorities split, and the 2026 widening of the AI Office's exclusive competence. Expect at least one item on extraterritoriality: placing a system on the EU market or affecting EU persons is enough.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- European AI Office: GPAI supervision, guidance, codes of practice, coordination, and (as amended in 2026) exclusive competence over AI built on a provider's own GPAI model and AI in very large online platforms\n- National competent and market surveillance authorities: investigations, documentation access, corrective orders, withdrawal and recall powers, and fines for everything else\n- Penalty ladder: EUR 35m/7% prohibited practices; EUR 15m/3% most other breaches and Commission GPAI fines; EUR 7.5m/1% incorrect or misleading information, all whichever is higher\n- SMEs and start-ups get the lower of the two figures on every tier; small mid-caps get the lower cap on the two lower tiers only\n- No AI Act fine had been announced as of September 2026; the AI Office starts with technical compliance dialogues\n- Individuals can complain to market surveillance authorities, and civil liability runs through national law and the revised product liability regime\n- Extraterritoriality: placing AI on the EU market, offering it to EU users, or affecting EU persons brings a non-EU provider inside the Act, with an EU representative required\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1689/oj\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 (EU AI Act), Chapter VII and Article 99</a> · governance, market surveillance and the penalty ladder\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2026/1744/oj/eng\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Digital Omnibus on AI, Regulation (EU) 2026/1744</a> · widened AI Office competence and adjusted the SME and small-mid-cap fine caps\n- <a href=\"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/enforcement-ai-act\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">European Commission, enforcement of the AI Act</a> · how the Commission describes the enforcement architecture\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] The EU AI Act\n\n## 4. Enforcement and Penalties\n\n### 4.1 Oversight and Supervisory Authorities\n\n| Authority | Role |\n|-----------|------|\n| **European AI Office** (housed within European Commission) | Supervises and enforces GPAI model obligations (the Commission has exclusive powers under Art. 88), with fines from 2 Aug 2026; issues guidance and facilitates codes of practice. **As amended by the 2026 Omnibus (Art. 75(1))** it is also *exclusively* competent for AI systems built on a GPAI model by the same provider or group (with carve-outs) and for AI in DSA-designated very large online platforms and search engines |\n| **National competent authorities** (one or more per member state) | Market surveillance; handle complaints; investigate breaches; impose penalties within jurisdiction, for all AI systems not reserved to the AI Office |\n| **European Data Protection Supervisor** | Supervises AI systems used by EU institutions, bodies and agencies |\n\n### 4.2 Inspection Powers and Remedies\n\nAuthorities may:\n\n- Require access to technical documentation\n- Perform unannounced inspections (including digital audits)\n- Mandate and verify corrective actions\n- Suspend or prohibit marketing of AI systems\n- Require withdrawal or recall of non-compliant systems\n- Direct updates, retraining, or labeling changes\n- Require notification of affected users\n\n> Individuals affected by non-compliant AI may seek remedies under national civil liability frameworks or file complaints through data protection/consumer protection channels.\n\n### 4.3 Whistleblower Protections\n\nArt. 87 applies the EU Whistleblower Directive (EU) 2019/1937 to reports of AI Act infringements and to the protection of the people who make them. The AI Office runs an online whistleblower tool for people connected to providers of AI systems or GPAI models. It also runs a complaint tool and a complaints channel for downstream providers that integrate another provider's GPAI model.\n\n---\n\n### 4.4 Penalty Framework\n\n| Violation Type | Maximum Fine |\n|----------------|-------------|\n| **Prohibited AI practices** | Up to **€35 million or 7%** of global annual turnover (whichever higher) |\n| **Non-compliance with high-risk obligations** | Up to **€15 million or 3%** of turnover (whichever higher) |\n| **Incorrect, incomplete, or misleading information** | Up to **€7.5 million or 1%** of turnover (whichever higher) |\n| **GPAI model providers** (Art. 101, imposed by the Commission, from 2 Aug 2026) | Up to **€15 million or 3%** of worldwide turnover (whichever higher) |\n\n> **Smaller firms:** for SMEs and start-ups, every fine is capped at whichever figure is *lower* (Art. 99(6)). As amended by the 2026 Omnibus, small mid-caps get the same lower-figure cap for the €15m/3% and €7.5m/1% tiers only, not for prohibited-practice fines (Art. 99(6a)).\n>\n> **No fines yet:** as of September 2026 no AI Act fine has been publicly announced. The AI Office says its first tool is \"technical compliance dialogues\".\n\n> **Penalties compared to GDPR:** The EU AI Act's penalties resemble GDPR's but may exceed them for the most egregious violations.\n\n---\n\n### 4.5 Factors in Assessing Penalties\n\nAuthorities consider:\n\n- Nature and significance of the breach\n- Whether non-compliance was **intentional or negligent**\n- Previous history of violations\n- Degree of cooperation with authorities\n\n> **Voluntary disclosure benefit:** Organizations that voluntarily disclose incidents or deficiencies and cooperate with authorities may receive reduced penalties.\n\n**Post-enforcement:** Organizations may be required to submit to third-party audits, implement compliance remediation plans, and report on remediation progress periodically.\n\n---\n\n### 4.6 Cross-Border Enforcement and Extraterritoriality\n\n#### Mutual Assistance Framework\n\nNational authorities cooperate through:\n- Sharing information on investigations\n- Supporting cross-border enforcement actions\n- Coordinating activities through the European AI Office\n\n#### Extraterritorial Application\n\nThe EU AI Act applies to **non-EU entities** if they:\n- Place AI systems on the EU market\n- Make AI systems available to users located in the EU\n- Deploy AI outside the EU if the system affects EU persons\n- Develop GPAI models that are integrated into EU-facing applications\n\n> **Non-EU providers must:** Appoint an EU-based legal representative, ensure conformity assessments are in place, and meet all transparency and documentation requirements.\n\n---", "t_timeline": "## At a Glance\n\nThe EU AI Act's start dates depend on which version of the law you are reading. As enacted (Regulation (EU) 2024/1689), Annex III high-risk rules applied from 2 August 2026 and Annex I product rules from 2 August 2027. As amended by the Digital Omnibus on AI (Regulation (EU) 2026/1744, in force 27 July 2026), those moved to 2 December 2027 and 2 August 2028. 2 August 2026 still starts Article 50 transparency and Commission GPAI enforcement, and 2 December 2026 brings two new prohibitions.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\n### The two timelines side by side\n\n| Date | As enacted (2024/1689) | As amended (2026/1744) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 12 Jul 2024 | Published in the Official Journal | Same |\n| 1 Aug 2024 | Entry into force; no obligations yet | Same |\n| 2 Feb 2025 | Prohibitions (eight) and AI literacy apply | Same; softened literacy wording from 27 Jul 2026 |\n| 2 May 2025 | GPAI codes of practice due | GPAI Code published 10 Jul 2025, adequacy confirmed 1 Aug 2025 |\n| 2 Aug 2025 | GPAI obligations, governance bodies, penalty regime (except Article 101), notified bodies | Same |\n| 2 Aug 2026 | Annex III high-risk, Article 50 transparency, Commission GPAI enforcement including Article 101 | Article 50 and GPAI enforcement only; high-risk and sandboxes no longer |\n| 27 Jul 2026 | - | Omnibus enters into force |\n| 2 Dec 2026 | - | Two new Article 5 prohibitions; Article 50(2) marking deadline for legacy generative systems |\n| 2 Aug 2027 | Annex I high-risk; legacy GPAI deadline | Legacy GPAI deadline; national sandboxes operational; Annex I moves to 2028 |\n| 2 Dec 2027 | - | Annex III high-risk rules (Chapter III sections 1 to 3, including deployer duties and FRIAs) |\n| 2 Aug 2028 | - | Annex I product-embedded high-risk rules |\n| 2 Aug 2030 | Legacy high-risk systems for public authorities | Same |\n\n### Grace periods and transitional provisions\n\n- GPAI models placed on the market before 2 August 2025: until 2 August 2027 (Article 111(3)), unchanged. The Commission's guidelines say legacy providers need not retrain or \"unlearn\" where that is impossible or disproportionate, if they disclose and justify it.\n- High-risk systems already on the market before their category's high-risk date: caught only if their design changes significantly afterwards (Article 111(2)). Systems intended for public authorities must comply by 2 August 2030 regardless.\n- Generative systems already on the market before 2 August 2026: Article 50(2) marking by 2 December 2026 (Article 111(4), added by the Omnibus).\n\n### What the Omnibus changed beyond dates\n\nAI literacy (Article 4) became a duty to take measures to support literacy rather than a guaranteed level; Article 5 gained the two prohibitions; a new Article 4a replaced Article 10(5) with a wider legal basis for special-category data used for bias detection; the AI Office gained exclusive competence over certain systems; national sandboxes moved to 2 August 2027; small mid-caps gained simplifications; and FRIAs can reuse DPIA content.\n\n### Which dates the exam uses\n\nBOK v2.1 was approved on 9 September 2025, before the Omnibus was agreed, so a live item may quote the original dates. Read each stem for its anchor (\"as originally enacted\" or \"as amended by the 2026 Digital Omnibus\"); if there is none, ask whether the fact being tested changed at all. Prohibitions, GPAI dates and penalty ceilings did not.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nDate questions are common and the only safe method is to learn both columns. The unchanged anchors are 2 February 2025 (prohibitions and literacy), 2 August 2025 (GPAI), 2 August 2026 (Article 50 and Commission GPAI enforcement) and 2 August 2030 (legacy public-authority systems). The moved anchors are Annex III high-risk (2 August 2026 to 2 December 2027) and Annex I (2 August 2027 to 2 August 2028). New in 2026: the two prohibitions and the marking deadline on 2 December 2026.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- 2 February 2025: prohibitions (eight) and AI literacy; 2 August 2025: GPAI obligations, governance and the penalty regime except Article 101\n- 2 August 2026: Article 50 transparency and Commission GPAI enforcement including Article 101 fines, unchanged by the Omnibus\n- As enacted, Annex III high-risk applied 2 August 2026 and Annex I 2 August 2027; as amended, 2 December 2027 and 2 August 2028\n- 2 December 2026: two new Article 5 prohibitions and the Article 50(2) marking deadline for legacy generative systems\n- Legacy GPAI models (before 2 August 2025) have until 2 August 2027; legacy public-authority high-risk systems until 2 August 2030\n- BOK v2.1 predates the Omnibus, so read each stem for its anchor; prohibitions, GPAI dates and penalty ceilings did not change\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1689/oj\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 (EU AI Act), Article 113</a> · the original application dates, as enacted\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2026/1744/oj/eng\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Digital Omnibus on AI, Regulation (EU) 2026/1744</a> · the amended dates, the two prohibitions and the Article 111(4) marking deadline\n- <a href=\"https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/timeline/timeline-implementation-eu-ai-act\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AI Act implementation timeline (AI Act Service Desk)</a> · the official milestone dates, useful for both columns\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] The EU AI Act\n\n## 6. EU AI Act Implementation Timeline (as enacted vs. as amended by the 2026 Digital Omnibus)\n\n> [!warning] Which dates will the exam use?\n> BOK v2.1 was approved on 9 September 2025 and took effect on 2 February 2026, before the Digital Omnibus on AI (Regulation (EU) 2026/1744) was agreed. Live exam items may still use the **original** dates, such as \"high-risk rules apply 2 August 2026\" and \"Annex I systems 2 August 2027\". Read each stem for its anchor (\"as originally enacted\" or \"as amended by the 2026 Digital Omnibus\"). If there is none, ask whether the fact being tested changed at all. Prohibitions, GPAI dates and penalty ceilings did not.\n\n### 6.1 The two timelines side by side\n\n| Date | As enacted (Regulation (EU) 2024/1689) | As amended by the 2026 Digital Omnibus (Regulation (EU) 2026/1744) |\n|------|----------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| **12 Jul 2024** | Published in the Official Journal | Same |\n| **1 Aug 2024** | Entry into force; no obligations yet | Same |\n| **2 Feb 2025** | Prohibited practices (eight, Art. 5(1)(a)–(h)) and AI literacy (Art. 4) apply | Same. The softened Art. 4 wording applies from 27 Jul 2026 |\n| **2 May 2025** | GPAI codes of practice due | GPAI Code published 10 Jul 2025; adequacy confirmed 1 Aug 2025 |\n| **2 Aug 2025** | GPAI obligations, governance bodies, penalty regime (except Art. 101), notified bodies; Member States designate authorities | Same |\n| **2 Aug 2026** | General date of application: Annex III high-risk rules, Art. 50 transparency, Commission GPAI enforcement including Art. 101 fines, national sandboxes due | **Still applies** for Art. 50 transparency and Commission GPAI enforcement including **Art. 101 fines**. It **no longer** applies to high-risk rules or sandboxes |\n| **27 Jul 2026** | — | Omnibus enters into force |\n| **2 Dec 2026** | — | **Two new Art. 5 prohibitions** (non-consensual intimate imagery; CSAM). **Art. 50(2) marking deadline** for generative systems already on the market before 2 Aug 2026 (Art. 111(4)) |\n| **2 Aug 2027** | Annex I (Art. 6(1)) product-embedded high-risk rules; GPAI models placed before 2 Aug 2025 must comply | GPAI legacy deadline unchanged. **National sandboxes operational** (Art. 57(1)). Annex I moves to 2028 |\n| **2 Dec 2027** | — | **Annex III high-risk rules** (Ch. III §§1–3, incl. Art. 26 deployer duties and Art. 27 FRIA) |\n| **2 Aug 2028** | — | **Annex I product-embedded high-risk rules** |\n| **2 Aug 2030** | Legacy high-risk systems intended for public authorities must comply | Same |\n\n### 6.2 Grace periods and transitional provisions\n\n- **GPAI models placed on the market before 2 Aug 2025:** until **2 Aug 2027** (Art. 111(3)), unchanged by the Omnibus. The Commission's guidelines say legacy providers need not retrain or \"unlearn\" a model where that is impossible or disproportionate, provided they disclose and justify it.\n- **High-risk systems already on the market before their category's high-risk date:** caught only if their design changes significantly afterwards (Art. 111(2), now keyed to the Chapter III application date). Systems intended for public authorities must comply by 2 Aug 2030 in any case.\n- **Generative systems already on the market before 2 Aug 2026:** Art. 50(2) marking by **2 Dec 2026** (Art. 111(4), added by the Omnibus).\n- **Codes of practice:** voluntary. Adhering to a code the Commission has assessed as adequate is a straightforward way to *demonstrate* compliance with Arts. 53 and 55, and the Commission focuses its enforcement on monitoring adherence. Adherence is **not a presumption of conformity**: under Arts. 53(4) and 55(2) only harmonised standards give that. Non-signatories must demonstrate \"alternative adequate means\", for example through a gap analysis, and can expect more information requests. Commitments kept under an adequate code can count as a mitigating factor when fines are set (Art. 101(1); Commission GPAI guidelines, paras 91–97).\n\n### 6.3 What else the 2026 Digital Omnibus changed\n\n- **AI literacy (Art. 4):** now a duty to *take measures to support* AI literacy, with no guaranteed level for any individual. The Commission and Member States must actively promote literacy and publish practical examples.\n- **Two new prohibitions (Art. 5(1)(ba)–(bb)), from 2 Dec 2026:** AI that generates or manipulates realistic intimate or sexual imagery of an identifiable person without explicit consent, and AI that generates child sexual abuse material. Placing a system on the market is caught only if that is its intended purpose, or a foreseeable outcome it lacks reasonable safeguards against.\n- **Special-category data for bias detection (new Art. 4a):** replaces Art. 10(5) and extends the legal basis beyond high-risk providers to other AI systems and models. Strict necessity and six safeguards apply, including no effective alternative data, pseudonymisation, access controls, no onward transfer, deletion, and records. GDPR applies on top.\n- **AI Office:** exclusive competence over AI systems built on a GPAI model by the same provider or group (carve-outs: Annex I products, critical infrastructure, certain law-enforcement, border and financial systems, justice) and over AI in DSA very large platforms and search engines. It gains investigation, inspection, commitment and fining procedures (Arts. 75a–75d), and can run a Union-level sandbox.\n- **National sandboxes:** operational by **2 Aug 2027** instead of 2 Aug 2026.\n- **Small mid-caps:** simplified technical documentation, proportionate QMS, priority sandbox access and lower-figure fine caps (for the 15/3 and 7.5/1 tiers) extend beyond SMEs to SMCs.\n- **Registration of Art. 6(3) \"not high-risk\" systems:** still required, with less information (no summary of grounds, no list of Member States).\n- **Also:** FRIAs can reuse DPIA content; machinery moves to the Annex I sectoral route; CRA-compliant systems are deemed to meet Art. 15 cybersecurity. Article-level detail is in [[EU AI Act 2024 — Key Provisions]] §13.\n\n### 6.4 Commission guidance and implementation status (September 2026)\n\n| Instrument | Date | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| Prohibited-practices guidelines; AI-system-definition guidelines | 4 and 6 Feb 2025 | Final, non-binding |\n| GPAI guidelines | 18 Jul 2025 | Final. Indicative GPAI criterion 10²³ FLOP; systemic risk presumed above 10²⁵ FLOP; a modifier becomes the provider above **one third** of the original training compute |\n| Training-content summary template (Art. 53(1)(d)) | 24 Jul 2025 | Final; mandatory for GPAI providers |\n| GPAI serious-incident reporting template (Art. 55) | 4 Nov 2025 | Final |\n| High-risk serious-incident guidance and template (Art. 73) | 26 Sep 2025 | Draft (consultation closed 7 Nov 2025) |\n| Code of Practice on Marking and Labelling (Code of Practice on Transparency of AI-generated Content) | 10 Jun 2026 | Final and assessed adequate; about 190 signatories by end of July 2026 |\n| Art. 50 transparency guidelines | 20 Jul 2026 | Final |\n| Art. 6 high-risk classification guidelines | Draft 19 May 2026 | **Still draft**; the 2 Feb 2026 statutory deadline was missed; final expected by end of 2026 |\n| Scientific Panel (60 experts) and Advisory Forum | 1 Jun 2026 | Appointed for two-year terms |\n| EN 18286:2026, QMS standard for Art. 17 | Jul 2026 | Published by CEN-CENELEC; gives a presumption of conformity only once cited in the OJ |\n| Fines | — | None publicly announced as of Sept 2026 |\n\n### 6.5 GPAI Code of Practice: who has signed\n\nAs of the Commission's list (updated 31 July 2026), **21 providers** have signed the full Code. They include Amazon, Anthropic, Google, IBM, Microsoft, Mistral AI and OpenAI, as well as several EU firms such as Aleph Alpha. **xAI signed only the Safety & Security chapter**, so it must show Transparency and Copyright compliance by alternative adequate means. **Meta does not appear on the list.** Signatories run a Signatory Taskforce chaired by the AI Office. Details: [[GPAI Code of Practice — Key Provisions]].\n\n---", "ref_gpai_cop": "## At a Glance\n\nThe GPAI Code of Practice is a voluntary instrument under Article 56 through which general-purpose AI providers can demonstrate compliance with Articles 53 and 55. It has three chapters (Transparency, Copyright, Safety and Security), the Commission assessed it as adequate, and as of 31 July 2026 21 providers had signed the full Code. Adherence is not a presumption of conformity; it is evidence.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\n### What it is\n\nArticle 56 lets the Commission facilitate codes of practice for GPAI obligations. The GPAI Code of Practice was published on 10 July 2025 and its adequacy was confirmed on 1 August 2025. It is **voluntary**: a provider that adheres to a code the Commission has assessed as adequate has a straightforward way to **demonstrate** compliance with Articles 53 and 55, and the Commission focuses enforcement on monitoring adherence.\n\n### The three chapters\n\n- **Transparency**: documentation and information for downstream providers, so integrators can understand a model's capabilities and limits.\n- **Copyright**: commitments to respect robots.txt and other appropriate machine-readable protocols, and to honour Article 4(3) reservations, supporting the Article 53(1)(c) copyright policy.\n- **Safety and Security**: commitments aimed at systemic-risk models, supporting the Article 55 duties (evaluation, risk mitigation, incident reporting, cybersecurity).\n\nThe chapters map onto the Act's duties, and some study materials import extra measures (external audits, public red-team reports, researcher access) that live in the Code rather than the Act. Check whether a claimed obligation comes from Article 55 or from the Code.\n\n### Who has signed\n\nAs of the Commission's list (updated 31 July 2026), **21 providers** had signed the full Code, including Amazon, Anthropic, Google, IBM, Microsoft, Mistral AI and OpenAI, plus EU firms such as Aleph Alpha. **xAI signed only the Safety and Security chapter**, so it must demonstrate Transparency and Copyright compliance by alternative adequate means. **Meta does not appear on the list.** Signatories run a Signatory Taskforce chaired by the AI Office.\n\n### Legal effect\n\nAdherence is **not** a presumption of conformity: under Articles 53(4) and 55(2) only harmonised standards give that. Non-signatories must demonstrate \"alternative adequate means\", for example through a gap analysis, and can expect more information requests. Commitments kept under an adequate code can count as a mitigating factor when fines are set. The 2026 Digital Omnibus on AI did not change Article 53(1)(c)-(d) or the Code's role.\n\n### Related codes\n\nA separate Code of Practice on Marking and Labelling (on transparency of AI-generated content) was final and assessed adequate on 10 June 2026, with about 190 signatories by the end of July 2026, and the Commission published Article 50 transparency guidelines on 20 July 2026.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nThe Code is a status and effect question more than a content question. Know that it is voluntary, that it has three chapters, that adherence demonstrates but does not presume conformity (only harmonised standards do), and that non-signatories must show alternative adequate means. Signature facts are quotable: 21 full signatories as of 31 July 2026, xAI on the Safety and Security chapter only, Meta absent. A trap is treating Code commitments (external audits, public red-teaming) as Article 55 obligations.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- The GPAI Code of Practice is a voluntary Article 56 instrument, published 10 July 2025 and assessed adequate on 1 August 2025\n- Three chapters: Transparency, Copyright, Safety and Security, mapping onto Articles 53 and 55\n- 21 providers had signed the full Code as of 31 July 2026; xAI signed only the Safety and Security chapter; Meta is absent\n- Adherence demonstrates compliance with Articles 53 and 55 but is not a presumption of conformity, which only harmonised standards give\n- Non-signatories must demonstrate alternative adequate means, such as a gap analysis, and can expect more information requests\n- A separate Code of Practice on Marking and Labelling (final 10 June 2026) covers Article 50 transparency of AI-generated content\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/contents-code-gpai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GPAI Code of Practice (European Commission)</a> · the Code, its chapters and the list of signatories\n- <a href=\"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/code-practice-ai-generated-content\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Code of Practice on Transparency of AI-generated Content</a> · the Article 50 marking and labelling code, final 10 June 2026\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1689/oj\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 (EU AI Act), Article 56</a> · the legal basis for codes of practice and their effect\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] GPAI Code of Practice — Key Provisions\n\n# GPAI Code of Practice — Key Provisions\n\n> **Source:** Code of Practice for General-Purpose AI Models (2025), published by the European AI Office under Article 56 of the EU AI Act.\n>\n> **Status (September 2026):** published 10 July 2025. The Commission and the AI Board confirmed it an **adequate** voluntary tool on 1 August 2025. **21 providers** have signed the full Code, and **xAI** has signed the Safety & Security chapter only; **Meta has not signed**. The Commission's enforcement powers, including fines, have applied since **2 August 2026**. See §15.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview and Purpose\n\nThe **GPAI Code of Practice** is a compliance-facilitation instrument issued under **Article 56 of the EU AI Act**. It provides detailed Measures that signatory GPAI providers commit to implementing in order to demonstrate compliance with:\n\n| Article | Obligation | Applies To |\n|---------|-----------|------------|\n| **Art 53(1)(a)** | Technical documentation (Annex XI) | All GPAI providers |\n| **Art 53(1)(b)** | Information to downstream providers (Annex XII) | All GPAI providers |\n| **Art 53(1)(c)** | Copyright policy compliance | All GPAI providers |\n| **Art 53(1)(d)** | Public training-content summary | All GPAI providers |\n| **Art 55(1)** | Systemic risk assessment & mitigation | **GPAI with systemic risk only** |\n\n> **Critical nuance:** Adherence to the Code does **NOT** constitute conclusive evidence of compliance with EU AI Act obligations. It is a demonstration aid, not a safe harbor, and **not a presumption of conformity**: under Arts. 53(4) and 55(2), only harmonised standards give that.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Structure of the Code — Three Chapters\n\n| Chapter | Working Groups | Target Providers | Key Focus |\n|---------|---------------|------------------|-----------|\n| **Transparency** | WG 1 (Oliver, Bommasani) | All GPAI providers | Model documentation, downstream info, Annex XI/XII |\n| **Copyright** | WG 1 (Peukert, Castets-Renard) | All GPAI providers | Copyright policy, opt-out compliance (Art 4(3) CDSM), anti-infringement |\n| **Safety & Security** | WG 2–4 (Samwald, Bengio, Schaake) | Systemic-risk GPAI only | SSF, risk identification/analysis, mitigations, incident reporting, cybersecurity |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Transparency Chapter (All GPAI Providers)\n\n### 3.1 Commitment 1: Documentation\n\n**Legal basis:** Articles 53(1)(a), 53(1)(b), 53(2), 53(7); Annexes XI and XII AI Act\n\n| Measure | Requirement | Details |\n|---------|------------|---------|\n| **1.1** | Draw up & maintain Model Documentation | Use the **Model Documentation Form**; update for model changes; retain for **10 years** after market placement |\n| **1.2** | Provide information to downstream providers & AI Office | Publicly disclose contact info; respond to downstream providers within **14 days** (save exceptional circumstances); provide to AI Office upon request under Art 91/75(3) |\n| **1.3** | Quality, integrity & security of information | Control documented information; follow established protocols and technical standards |\n\n> **Open-source exception (Art 53(2)):** Transparency obligations do not apply to free and open-source GPAI models unless the model is classified as **systemic risk**. A downstream modifier becomes the provider of the modified model only if the modification significantly changes its generality, capabilities or systemic risk. Indicatively, that means the modification used more than one third of the original training compute (Commission GPAI guidelines, paras 59–60). Its obligations are then limited to the modification or fine-tuning (recital 109).\n\n### 3.2 Model Documentation Form\n\nThe Form captures information for two audiences:\n\n| Audience | Access Condition |\n|----------|-----------------|\n| **Downstream providers** | Provided upon request; enables integration & downstream compliance |\n| **AI Office / national competent authorities** | Only upon formal request with stated legal basis and purpose |\n\n**Confidentiality:** All recipients are bound by Art 78 AI Act — must protect IP, trade secrets, and confidential business information.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Copyright Chapter (All GPAI Providers)\n\n**Legal basis:** Article 53(1)(c) AI Act; Directive (EU) 2019/790 (CDSM Directive), particularly Art 4(3)\n\n### 4.1 Key Commitments\n\n| Measure | Commitment | Key Details |\n|---------|-----------|-------------|\n| **1.1** | Copyright policy | Draw up, maintain, and implement a single-document copyright policy; assign organizational responsibility |\n| **1.2** | Lawful access only | Do not circumvent paywalls/TPMs; exclude websites persistently flagged as infringing by EU/EEA courts |\n| **1.3** | Respect rights reservations | Comply with **robots.txt** (IETF RFC 9309); comply with other machine-readable opt-out protocols; make crawler info publicly available; notify rightsholders of updates |\n| **1.4** | Mitigate infringing outputs | Technical safeguards against reproducing training content; prohibit copyright-infringing uses in ToS/acceptable use policy |\n| **1.5** | Point of contact & complaints | Designate electronic contact for rightsholders; implement complaint mechanism; act on complaints diligently |\n\n> **Important:** The Copyright Chapter is **complementary** to the Art 53(1)(d) obligation to publish a training-content summary (template to be provided by the AI Office). Adherence to this Chapter does **not** constitute compliance with substantive copyright law — that remains for Member State courts and the CJEU.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Safety & Security Chapter (Systemic-Risk GPAI Only)\n\n**Legal basis:** Articles 55(1) and 56(5) AI Act\n\n**Ten Commitments** structure the entire systemic risk management lifecycle:\n\n### 5.1 Commitments Overview\n\n| # | Commitment | Purpose |\n|---|-----------|---------|\n| **1** | Safety & Security Framework (SSF) | Adopt a state-of-the-art SSF; create → implement → update; notify AI Office |\n| **2** | Systemic risk identification | Structured process to identify risks; develop systemic risk scenarios |\n| **3** | Systemic risk analysis | Gather model-independent info; model evaluations; risk modelling; risk estimation; post-market monitoring |\n| **4** | Risk acceptance determination | Define acceptance criteria (risk tiers); determine if risks are acceptable; decision to proceed/not proceed |\n| **5** | Safety mitigations | Implement safety mitigations across model lifecycle (filtering, monitoring, staging access, etc.) |\n| **6** | Security mitigations | Cybersecurity against unauthorized access, model theft, (self-)exfiltration; apply Appendices 4.1–4.5 |\n| **7** | Model Reports | Create and maintain Safety & Security Model Reports; notify AI Office |\n| **8** | Responsibility allocation | Define oversight, ownership, support, and assurance roles across organizational levels |\n| **9** | Serious incident reporting | Track, document, and report serious incidents to AI Office and national authorities |\n| **10** | Additional documentation & transparency | Detailed technical docs on request (retain 10 years); publish summarized Framework and Model Reports |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Systemic Risk — Qualification Thresholds\n\n### 6.1 Definition (Art 3(65) AI Act)\n\n> *\"Systemic risk\" = a risk specific to the **high-impact capabilities** of GPAI models, having a **significant impact on the Union market** due to their reach, or due to actual/reasonably foreseeable negative effects on public health, safety, public security, fundamental rights, or society as a whole, that can be **propagated at scale across the value chain**.*\n\n### 6.2 What Triggers Systemic Risk Classification\n\n| Factor | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| **Compute presumption** | Cumulative training compute above **10²⁵ FLOP** presumes high-impact capabilities (Art 51(2)). The provider must notify the Commission within two weeks (Art 52(1)) and may argue the model is exceptionally not systemic-risk (Art 52(2)) |\n| **High-impact capabilities** | Capabilities matching/exceeding the most advanced GPAI models (Art 3(64)) |\n| **Reach** | Annex XIII presumes high impact on the internal market when the model is available to at least 10,000 registered business users in the EU |\n| **Other Annex XIII criteria** | Parameters, data size, compute, modalities, benchmarks, autonomy and scalability, tool access, number of end-users |\n| **Commission designation** | Ex officio or after a Scientific Panel qualified alert (Art 90), using the Annex XIII criteria (Art 51(1)(b)) |\n\n> **Indicative GPAI threshold (Commission guidelines, July 2025):** a model trained with more than **10²³ FLOP** that can generate language, text-to-image or text-to-video is indicatively a GPAI model. A downstream modifier becomes the provider of the modified model if the modification uses more than **one third** of the original training compute.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Systemic Risk Types (Appendix 1.1 & 1.4)\n\n### 7.1 Five Risk Categories\n\n| Type | Examples |\n|------|---------|\n| **Public health** | Biosecurity risks, pandemic-enabling capabilities |\n| **Safety** | Major accidents, critical infrastructure disruption |\n| **Public security** | Cyber-attacks, CBRN weapon proliferation |\n| **Fundamental rights** | Discrimination, privacy violations, surveillance |\n| **Society as a whole** | Democratic process undermining, concentration of power, CSAM, NCII |\n\n### 7.2 Specified Systemic Risks (Appendix 1.4) — Must Be Assessed\n\n1. **CBRN:** Enabling chemical, biological, radiological, nuclear attacks or accidents\n2. **Loss of control:** Humans losing ability to direct, modify, or shut down a model (misalignment, self-replication, power-seeking)\n3. **Cyber offence:** Large-scale sophisticated cyber-attacks on critical systems\n4. **Harmful manipulation:** Strategic distortion of human behaviour/beliefs targeting large populations or high-stakes decision-makers\n\n---\n\n## 8. Obligations Comparison: Standard vs. Systemic-Risk GPAI\n\n| Obligation | Standard GPAI | GPAI with Systemic Risk |\n|-----------|:---:|:---:|\n| Technical documentation (Art 53(1)(a)) | ✅ Required | ✅ Required |\n| Downstream provider info (Art 53(1)(b)) | ✅ Required | ✅ Required |\n| Copyright policy (Art 53(1)(c)) | ✅ Required | ✅ Required |\n| Public training-content summary (Art 53(1)(d)) | ✅ Required | ✅ Required |\n| Safety & Security Framework (SSF) | ❌ | ✅ **Required** |\n| Systemic risk identification & analysis | ❌ | ✅ **Required** |\n| Model evaluations (state-of-the-art) | Encouraged | ✅ **Required** |\n| Safety mitigations | ❌ | ✅ **Required** |\n| Cybersecurity (Appendix 4 standard) | Best practice | ✅ **Required** |\n| Safety & Security Model Report | ❌ | ✅ **Required** (every 6 months for most capable) |\n| Independent external evaluations | ❌ | ✅ **Required** (unless safe-ref model or unavailable) |\n| Serious incident reporting to AI Office | ❌ | ✅ **Required** |\n| Systemic risk responsibility allocation | ❌ | ✅ **Required** |\n| Post-market monitoring | ❌ | ✅ **Required** |\n| EU authorised representative for non-EU providers (Art 54) | ✅ Required (open-source models exempt, Art 54(6)) | ✅ **Required** (no open-source exemption) |\n| Open-source exemption (Art 53(2)) | ✅ Available | ❌ Not available |\n\n> The two ✅/❌ columns above mix **statutory duties** (Arts. 53–55) with **Code commitments** such as the Safety & Security Framework, Model Reports and external evaluations. The Code commitments bind only signatories that adhere to that chapter.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Safety & Security Framework (SSF) — Commitment 1\n\nThe SSF is the central governance instrument for systemic-risk GPAI.\n\n### 9.1 Framework Contents\n\n| Element | Description |\n|---------|-------------|\n| **Trigger points** | When lighter-touch evaluations are conducted along the lifecycle |\n| **Risk acceptance criteria** | Systemic risk tiers and how they map to acceptance decisions |\n| **Mitigation descriptions** | What safety/security mitigations are needed per risk tier |\n| **Timeline estimates** | When models are expected to exceed current risk tiers |\n| **External input process** | How governments/others can influence go/no-go decisions |\n| **Responsibility allocation** | How systemic risk duties are distributed across the org |\n| **Update process** | How the Framework itself is reviewed and updated |\n\n### 9.2 Framework Lifecycle\n\n```\nCREATE → IMPLEMENT → UPDATE (continuously)\n ↓\nNOTIFY AI Office (within 5 business days of confirmation)\n ↓\nFRAMEWORK ASSESSMENT (every 12 months or when materially undermined)\n ├── Adequacy assessment\n └── Adherence assessment (with remediation plans if needed)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 10. Model Evaluations (Commitment 3, Appendix 3)\n\n### 10.1 Core Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Detail |\n|-------------|--------|\n| **Scientific rigour** | High internal validity, external validity, reproducibility |\n| **Model elicitation** | State-of-the-art techniques; minimize under-elicitation; prevent sandbagging/deception |\n| **Mitigation effectiveness** | Assess under adversarial pressure (jailbreaking, fine-tuning attacks) |\n| **Independent external evaluators** | Qualified external teams must conduct evaluations (unless exemptions apply) |\n| **Adequate resources** | 20+ business days; adequate compute, staffing, access to model internals |\n\n### 10.2 Evaluation Methods (Examples)\n\n- Q&A sets, task-based evaluations, benchmarks\n- Red-teaming & adversarial testing\n- Human uplift studies\n- Model organisms, simulations\n- Proxy evaluations for classified materials\n\n---\n\n## 11. Cybersecurity Requirements (Commitment 6, Appendix 4)\n\n### 11.1 Security Goal\n\nProviders must define a **Security Goal** specifying which threat actors to protect against:\n- **Non-state external threats:** ~10 experienced cybersecurity professionals, EUR 1M budget, multi-month operation\n- **Insider threats:** Hostile employees, industrial espionage, model self-exfiltration\n- **Other expected threat actors**\n\n### 11.2 Five Security Domains\n\n| Appendix | Domain | Key Mitigations |\n|----------|--------|----------------|\n| **4.1** | General security | MFA, zero trust, phishing filtering, patch management, removable media policies |\n| **4.2** | Unreleased model weights | Encryption (256-bit + TPM), confidential computing, physical access control, secure registry |\n| **4.3** | Interface hardening | Output rate limiting, security reviews of interfaces, limited personnel with non-hardened access |\n| **4.4** | Insider threats | Background checks, insider threat training, sandboxed execution, training data tamper checks |\n| **4.5** | Security assurance | Independent external reviews, red-teaming, bug bounties, EDR/IDS, personnel integrity testing |\n\n> **Exemption:** Models whose capabilities are inferior to at least one model whose parameters are publicly available for download are exempt from Commitment 6.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Serious Incident Reporting (Commitment 9)\n\n### 12.1 Information Required (Measure 9.2)\n\n1. Start/end dates of the incident\n2. Resulting harm and affected victims/groups\n3. Chain of events leading to the incident\n4. Model involved\n5. Evidence of model involvement\n6. Signatory's response actions\n7. Recommendations for authorities\n8. Root cause analysis (model outputs, inputs, mitigation failures)\n9. Patterns from post-market monitoring (including near misses)\n\n### 12.2 Reporting Deadlines\n\n| Severity | Deadline After Awareness |\n|----------|-------------------------|\n| **Critical infrastructure disruption** (serious & irreversible) | **2 days** |\n| **Serious cybersecurity breach** (incl. model weight exfiltration) | **5 days** |\n| **Death of a person** | **10 days** |\n| **Serious harm** (health, fundamental rights, property, environment) | **15 days** |\n| **Unresolved incidents** (interim update) | Every **4 weeks** after initial report |\n| **Final report** (after resolution) | Within **60 days** of resolution |\n\n### 12.3 Retention\n\nDocumentation retained for at least **5 years** from date of documentation or incident resolution (whichever later).\n\n---\n\n## 13. Governance & Responsibility (Commitment 8)\n\n### 13.1 Four Responsibility Types\n\n| Responsibility | Role | Organizational Level |\n|---------------|------|---------------------|\n| **Oversight** | Supervise systemic risk management | Supervisory board / risk committee / independent body |\n| **Ownership** | Manage systemic risks day-to-day | Executive management (Head of Research, Head of Product) |\n| **Support & Monitoring** | Conduct risk assessments; monitor processes | Independent executive (CRO, VP Safety & Security) — must be **separate** from core business |\n| **Assurance** | Internal/external audit of adequacy | Chief Audit Executive / Internal Audit / sub-committee |\n\n### 13.2 Healthy Risk Culture Indicators (Measure 8.3)\n\n- Tone from the top communicating the SSF\n- Clear communication and challenge of risk decisions\n- Incentives aligned against excessive risk-taking\n- Anonymous surveys confirming staff comfort raising concerns\n- Active internal reporting channels\n- Whistleblower protection policy (published, communicated annually)\n- No retaliation against reporters\n\n---\n\n## 14. How the Code Relates to the EU AI Act\n\n| Aspect | EU AI Act | GPAI Code of Practice |\n|--------|----------|----------------------|\n| **Legal force** | Binding regulation | Voluntary (compliance demonstration aid) |\n| **Issuing authority** | EU co-legislators | European AI Office (Art 56 mandate) |\n| **Scope** | All AI systems + GPAI models | GPAI models only |\n| **Obligations** | Prescribed in Arts 53 & 55 | Detailed Measures operationalizing Arts 53 & 55 |\n| **Enforcement** | National competent authorities; AI Office | Relied upon by providers to demonstrate compliance |\n| **Updates** | Amendment via legislative process | Continuous evolution; Framework updates every 12 months |\n| **Presumption of conformity** | Only harmonised standards give it, once cited in the OJ (Arts. 40(1), 53(4), 55(2)) | None. Adherence is a way to *demonstrate* compliance, not a presumption |\n\n> **Art 56(6) AI Act, as enacted:** the AI Office and Board assess the Code's adequacy; the Commission could also approve a code by implementing act and give it \"general validity\" within the Union.\n> **As amended by the 2026 Digital Omnibus (Regulation (EU) 2026/1744):** the Commission, taking utmost account of the Board's opinion, assesses whether codes cover Arts. 53 and 55 and publishes that assessment. The replaced paragraph no longer contains the implementing-act approval sentence. If a code is inadequate, the Commission can still adopt common rules by implementing act (Art. 56(9)).\n> The Commission's adequacy assessment of this Code was published on **1 August 2025**.\n\n---\n\n## 15. Status, Signatories and Enforcement (as of September 2026)\n\n### 15.1 Timeline\n\n| Date | Event |\n|---|---|\n| 10 Jul 2025 | Final Code published (three chapters: Transparency, Copyright, Safety & Security) |\n| 18 Jul 2025 | Commission GPAI guidelines approved; they complement the Code |\n| 24 Jul 2025 | Commission template for the public summary of training content (Art. 53(1)(d)) |\n| 1 Aug 2025 | Commission and AI Board confirm the Code is an adequate voluntary tool |\n| 2 Aug 2025 | GPAI obligations apply; in the first year the AI Office treats signatories still implementing commitments as acting in good faith |\n| 4 Nov 2025 | Commission serious-incident reporting template for systemic-risk models (supports Commitment 9) |\n| 2 Aug 2026 | Commission enforcement powers apply: information requests, model evaluations, risk-mitigation measures, restriction, withdrawal or recall, and fines up to 3% or €15m (Art. 101) |\n| 2 Aug 2027 | Models placed on the market before 2 Aug 2025 must comply (Art. 111(3)) |\n\nThe 2026 Digital Omnibus did **not** change any GPAI obligation or date.\n\n### 15.2 Signatories (Commission list, updated 31 July 2026)\n\n- **Full Code (21):** AI Studio Delta, Aleph Alpha, Almawave, Amazon, Anthropic, Black Forest Labs, Bria AI, Cohere, Domyn, Dweve, Fastweb, Google, IBM, LINAGORA, Microsoft, Mistral AI, Open Hippo, OpenAI, Pleias, ServiceNow, WRITER.\n- **Safety & Security chapter only:** **xAI**. It must show compliance with the Transparency and Copyright obligations (Art. 53) by alternative adequate means.\n- **Not signed:** **Meta** does not appear on the list.\n- Signatories run a **Signatory Taskforce**, chaired by the AI Office, for consistent application.\n\n### 15.3 What signing buys, and what not signing costs\n\n| | Signatory (adheres to the adequate Code) | Non-signatory |\n|---|---|---|\n| How compliance is shown | Adherence to the Code is a straightforward way to demonstrate compliance with Arts. 53 and 55 | \"Alternative adequate means\", reported to the AI Office, e.g. through a gap analysis against the Code |\n| Supervision | Commission focuses enforcement on monitoring adherence | Expect more information requests and requests for model access across the lifecycle |\n| Fines | Commitments kept can be a **mitigating factor** (Art. 101(1)) | No such credit |\n| Legal status | Not a safe harbor and **not a presumption of conformity** | Same statutory duties |\n\nOpting out of a chapter loses the benefit for that chapter only, which is xAI's position. *(Commission GPAI guidelines, paras 91–97.)*\n\n### 15.4 Do not confuse with the Code of Practice on Marking and Labelling\n\nA separate voluntary code, the **Code of Practice on Transparency of AI-generated Content**, was finalised on 10 June 2026 under **Art. 50(7)**. It covers *AI systems*, not GPAI models. Section 1 is for providers (machine-readable marking and detection) and Section 2 for deployers (labelling deepfakes and AI-generated public-interest text). The Commission and AI Board judged it adequate, and it had about 190 signatories by the end of July 2026. The GPAI Code deals with model documentation, copyright and systemic risk; the labelling code deals with the outputs people see.\n\n---\n\n## 16. Key Definitions (Glossary)\n\n| Term | Definition (Summarized) |\n|------|------------------------|\n| **State of the art** | Forefront of research, governance, and technology, beyond best practice |\n| **Best practice** | Accepted among systemic-risk GPAI providers as the best processes for risk assessment/mitigation |\n| **Appropriate** | Suitable and necessary — best practice, state of the art, or more innovative techniques |\n| **Model elicitation** | Technical work to systematically enhance a model's capabilities for accurate measurement |\n| **Near miss** | A situation in which a serious incident could have, but did not, materialize |\n| **Systemic risk scenario** | A scenario in which a systemic risk might materialize |\n| **Systemic risk tiers** | Tiers in the Framework corresponding to a certain level of systemic risk |\n| **Safety margin** | Buffer in acceptance criteria accounting for uncertainties in assessments, mitigations, and capability changes |\n| **Safe reference model** | A previously assessed model serving as benchmark for evaluating newer models (Appendix 2.1) |\n| **Similarly safe or safer model** | Model with no materially different risk scenarios and ≤ benchmark scores compared to safe reference (Appendix 2.2) |\n\n---\n\n## 17. Exam Relevance\n\nFor the **AIGP exam**, focus on:\n\n1. **Three chapters** and which GPAI providers they apply to\n2. **Articles 53 vs. 55** — transparency/copyright (all) vs. safety/security (systemic risk only)\n3. **Systemic risk triggers** — compute thresholds, high-impact capabilities, Commission designation\n4. **Safety & Security Framework** as the central governance instrument\n5. **Serious incident reporting deadlines** — 2/5/10/15 day tiers\n6. **Independent external evaluations** requirement for systemic-risk models\n7. **Governance structure** — four responsibility types (oversight, ownership, support, assurance)\n8. **Code is NOT conclusive evidence** of compliance — a frequent exam trap\n9. **Open-source exemption** (Art 53(2)) — applies to transparency/copyright, NOT to systemic risk models\n10. **Copyright** — Art 4(3) CDSM Directive opt-out; robots.txt compliance\n11. **Presumption of conformity** comes only from harmonised standards, never from the Code (Arts. 53(4), 55(2)); a common distractor\n12. **Signatories vs. non-signatories:** same duties; the difference is how compliance is shown and how closely the AI Office supervises\n13. **Dates:** GPAI obligations 2 Aug 2025; Commission fines from 2 Aug 2026; legacy models by 2 Aug 2027. The 2026 Omnibus left all three unchanged\n14. **Two different codes:** the GPAI Code (Art. 56, models) vs. the Code of Practice on Marking and Labelling (Art. 50(7), AI-generated content)\n\n---\n\n> **Related notes:** [[The EU AI Act]] covers the statutory GPAI framework; [[AI Standards and Frameworks]] covers OECD, NIST, and ISO standards referenced throughout; [[AI Risk Management and Assurance]] connects to systemic risk management practices.\n\n## Sources\n\n- Commission, GPAI Code of Practice, chapters and signatory list (updated 31 July 2026): https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/contents-code-gpai\n- Commission opinion on the assessment of the Code (1 August 2025): https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/library/commission-opinion-assessment-general-purpose-ai-code-practice\n- Commission Q&A on the Code: https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/faqs/questions-and-answers-code-practice-general-purpose-ai\n- Commission GPAI guidelines, section 5.1 (effects of adhering to a code): https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/library/guidelines-scope-obligations-providers-general-purpose-ai-models-under-ai-act\n- AI Act Service Desk FAQ on the Commission's GPAI enforcement powers: https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/faq/commissions-enforcement-powers-related-ai-act-obligations-providers-most-advanced-models\n- Serious-incident template for systemic-risk GPAI (4 November 2025): https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/library/ai-act-commission-publishes-reporting-template-serious-incidents-involving-general-purpose-ai\n- Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, Arts. 53–56, 101, 111: http://data.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1689/oj ; Regulation (EU) 2026/1744 (Art. 56(6) replaced): http://data.europa.eu/eli/reg/2026/1744/oj", "t_korea": "## At a Glance\n\nSouth Korea's Framework Act on the Development of Artificial Intelligence and Establishment of Trust (the AI Basic Act) has been in force since 22 January 2026 and is the country's overarching AI law. It pairs state promotion of the AI industry with a light set of trust duties enforced by the Ministry of Science and ICT (MSIT). Those duties are advance notice and labeling for generative and high-impact AI, six risk-management measures for high-impact AI, safety duties for frontier-compute systems, and a domestic representative for large foreign operators. The Enforcement Decree (Presidential Decree No. 36053) supplies the thresholds and procedures.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nExpect EU contrasts. Korea has no prohibited practices, and its fines are capped at KRW 30 million for only three failures. Its impact assessment is best-efforts rather than mandatory, MSIT is the single regulator, and MSIT's grace period of at least a year is policy, not statute. Know the high-impact domain list, the 10^26 FLOP threshold versus the EU's 10^25, and the domestic-representative thresholds.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Promulgated 21 Jan 2025 (Act No. 20676) and in force 22 Jan 2026; the Enforcement Decree (No. 36053) sets the numbers, and MSIT enforces\n- High-impact AI = a listed domain (energy, water, health, medical devices, nuclear, policing biometrics, hiring and lending judgments, transport, public decisions, K-12 assessment) plus significant risk to life, safety or rights\n- High-impact measures: risk management plan, explanation plan, user protection, human oversight and documents kept 5 years; the impact assessment is best efforts only\n- Transparency: advance notice for high-impact and generative AI, labels on generative outputs (human- or machine-readable), and a clear notice for realistic synthetic media, with a softer method allowed for artistic works\n- Article 32 safety duties cover systems of at least 10^26 FLOPs that are state of the art and pose wide-ranging risk; results go to MSIT (the EU presumes systemic risk at 10^25)\n- A foreign operator meeting any threshold (KRW 1 trillion revenue, KRW 10 billion AI revenue, 1 million daily Korean users) must appoint a domestic representative\n- Fines of up to KRW 30 million apply only to missing notice, a missing representative or an ignored order; other breaches go to fact-finding and corrective orders, with an MSIT grace period of at least one year\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] South Korea AI Basic Act — Key Provisions\n\n# South Korea AI Basic Act — Key Provisions\n\n> **Status:** In force (as of September 2026). Current text is Act No. 20676 as amended by Act No. 21311 (20 Jan 2026). **Effective:** 22 January 2026 (the digital medical device item followed on 24 Jan 2026; a few 2026 amendments on 21 Jul 2026). **Regulator:** Ministry of Science and ICT (MSIT). **Grace period:** MSIT policy, not statute: at least one year before fact-finding and fines, except in serious cases.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n- This is South Korea's overarching AI statute. Its Korean title (인공지능 발전과 신뢰 기반 조성 등에 관한 기본법) is usually translated as the **Framework Act on the Development of Artificial Intelligence and Establishment of Trust**. The word 기본법 literally means \"basic act\", which is why it is also called the **AI Basic Act** or **AI Framework Act**. The BOK v2.1 calls it \"the South Korean AI Basic Law\".\n- As a framework act, it does two jobs:\n  1. It builds the state's AI promotion machinery: a national plan, a presidential committee, institutes, and support for data, SMEs, startups and clusters.\n  2. It adds a thin layer of trust obligations in Chapter 4: transparency, safety duties for very large systems, duties for high-impact AI, and a domestic representative for large foreign operators.\n- **Purpose (Art. 1):** to protect human rights and dignity and improve quality of life, while strengthening national competitiveness.\n- **The rules sit in three layers:**\n  1. The **Act** sets principles and duties.\n  2. The **Enforcement Decree** (a Presidential Decree) supplies the thresholds and procedures.\n  3. **MSIT notices and guidelines** fill in details, such as how to count training compute.\n- **Compared with the EU AI Act**, it puts promotion first and regulates lightly. It has no banned practices and no conformity assessment. Its impact assessment is best-efforts only, and its administrative fines are capped at KRW 30 million.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Timeline\n\n| Date | Event |\n|---|---|\n| 26 Dec 2024 | The National Assembly passes a consolidated bill merging 19 AI bills |\n| **21 Jan 2025** | Promulgated as **Act No. 20676** |\n| 26 Feb – 31 Dec 2025 | Public consultation on the subordinate legislation |\n| 1 Oct 2025 | Act No. 21065: a consequential amendment that only renames an agency in Art. 38 |\n| 20 Jan 2026 | **Act No. 21311**, a partial amendment. It reorganizes the presidential committee, adds measures for \"AI-vulnerable groups\" and adds a public-procurement preference for AI products |\n| 21 Jan 2026 | Enforcement Decree issued (**Presidential Decree No. 36053**) |\n| **22 Jan 2026** | **The Act and the Decree take effect**, one year after promulgation (Supplementary Provisions Art. 1) |\n| 24 Jan 2026 | The digital medical device item of the high-impact definition, Art. 2(4)(d), takes effect |\n| 21 Jul 2026 | The deferred parts of Act No. 21311 take effect, including the AI-vulnerable-groups clause in the impact assessment (Art. 35(1)). The Decree is amended to match (Presidential Decree No. 36506) |\n| For at least one year from 22 Jan 2026 | MSIT's grace period. Fact-finding investigations (including corrective orders) and fines are generally deferred, except in serious cases such as loss of life or human-rights violations |\n\n> **Trap:** the grace period is an MSIT enforcement policy, not a clause of the Act. The obligations themselves have applied since 22 January 2026.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Scope and structure\n\n| Element | Rule | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Extraterritorial reach** | Applies to conduct abroad that affects the Korean market or users in Korea | Art. 4(1) |\n| **Exclusion** | AI developed and used *solely* for national defense or national security, as specified by decree. The decree covers functions designated by the Minister of National Defense, the Director of the National Intelligence Service or the Commissioner General of the National Police Agency | Art. 4(2); Decree Art. 2 |\n| **Relationship with other laws** | The Act applies unless another law has special AI provisions, and future AI laws must fit its purposes | Art. 5 |\n| **Who is regulated** | \"AI business operators\": corporations, organizations, individuals **and government bodies** engaged in the AI industry | Art. 2(7) |\n| **Who is protected** | **Users**, meaning anyone who receives AI products or services, and **affected persons**, meaning anyone whose life, physical safety or fundamental rights are significantly affected | Art. 2(8)-(9) |\n| **Explanation principle** | Affected persons should receive a clear, meaningful explanation of the key criteria and principles behind AI outcomes, as far as is technically and reasonably possible | Art. 3(2) |\n\n**Structure of the Act**\n\n| Chapter | Articles | Covers |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. General provisions | Arts. 1-5 | Purpose, definitions, scope |\n| 2. Governance and promotion | Arts. 6-12 | Basic Plan, presidential committee, AI Policy Center, AI Safety Institute |\n| 3. Technology and industry development | Arts. 13-26 | Industry support measures |\n| 4. Ethics and trustworthiness | Arts. 27-36 | **The regulatory core** |\n| 5. Supplementary provisions | Arts. 37-41 | Including fact-finding (Art. 40) |\n| 6. Penalties | Arts. 42-43 | Criminal penalty and administrative fines |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Key definitions (Art. 2)\n\n| Term | Meaning (paraphrased) |\n|---|---|\n| **Artificial intelligence** | An electronic implementation of human intellectual abilities such as learning, reasoning, perception, judgment and language understanding |\n| **AI system** | An AI-based system that, with varying autonomy and adaptability, infers outputs such as predictions, recommendations and decisions that affect real or virtual environments for given objectives. This is close to the OECD and EU wording |\n| **High-impact AI** | An AI system that may significantly affect, or pose risks to, life, physical safety or fundamental rights **and** is used in one of the listed domains (Section 5.1) |\n| **Generative AI** | An AI system that produces text, sound, images, video or other outputs by imitating the structure and characteristics of its input data |\n| **AI business operator** | The umbrella term, with two types: an **AI developer** develops and provides AI, and an **AI-using business operator** provides AI products or services using AI supplied by a developer. The English explorer translation calls the second type \"AI service provider\" |\n| **User** | Anyone who receives AI products or services |\n| **Affected person** | Anyone whose life, physical safety or fundamental rights are significantly affected by AI products or services |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Risk classification: which categories trigger which duties\n\nKorea has no four-tier pyramid. Duties attach to **categories** of AI, and one system can fall into several. A generative model used to screen loan applicants, for example, is both generative and high-impact.\n\n| Category | How it is defined | Main duties |\n|---|---|---|\n| Any AI product or service | — | Review in advance whether it is high-impact (Art. 33(1)) |\n| **Generative AI** | Art. 2(5) | Advance notice to users (Art. 31(1)); label the outputs (Art. 31(2)); give clear notice for realistic synthetic sound, images or video (Art. 31(3)) |\n| **High-impact AI** | Art. 2(4): listed domain + significant risk | Advance notice (Art. 31(1)); the six Art. 34 measures; a best-efforts impact assessment (Art. 35); strive for voluntary verification or certification (Art. 30(3)) |\n| **\"High-performance\" AI** (a commentators' label) | Art. 32 + Decree Art. 24: at least 10^26 FLOPs **and** state-of-the-art **and** wide-ranging, significant risk | Identify, assess and mitigate risks across the lifecycle; run an incident monitoring and response system; submit the results to MSIT |\n| **Prohibited AI** | **None**: the Act has no list of banned practices | — |\n| **Excluded AI** | AI used solely for defense or national security | Outside the Act |\n\n### 5.1 High-impact domains (Art. 2(4)(a)-(k))\n\n| Item | Domain |\n|---|---|\n| (a) | Energy supply |\n| (b) | Drinking water production processes |\n| (c) | Provision and operation of the health care system |\n| (d) | Development and use of medical devices and digital medical devices |\n| (e) | Safe management and operation of nuclear materials and facilities |\n| (f) | Analysis and use of biometric information (face, fingerprints, iris, palm veins) for criminal investigation or arrest |\n| (g) | Judgments or evaluations that significantly affect a person's rights or obligations, such as **hiring and loan screening** |\n| (h) | Core operation and management of transport vehicles, facilities and systems |\n| (i) | Decisions by the state, local governments or public institutions that affect the public, such as eligibility checks or charges for public services |\n| (j) | Student assessment in **early childhood, elementary and secondary** education |\n| (k) | Other areas added by Presidential Decree. The current decree adds none |\n\n- **Two-part test:** being in a listed domain is necessary but not enough. The system must also be capable of significantly affecting life, safety or fundamental rights.\n- **Compared with EU Annex III:** the lists overlap heavily: critical infrastructure, biometrics in policing, employment, credit, public services and education. But Korea's education item covers only student assessment up to secondary school, and its list sits in the statute itself, with additions by decree.\n\n### 5.2 Confirming high-impact status (Art. 33; Decree Arts. 25-26)\n\n- The operator **must review status in advance** and **may ask MSIT to confirm** it.\n- A confirmation request includes:\n  - a summary of the product or service;\n  - an overview of the training data;\n  - material showing how the system is used and what results it produces;\n  - any other supporting documents.\n- MSIT weighs:\n  - whether the system is used in a listed domain;\n  - the impact, severity, frequency and domain-specific features of its risks;\n  - the operator's own review;\n  - any advice from the expert committee.\n- **Timing:**\n  - MSIT answers **within 30 days** and may extend once by up to 30 days, giving written reasons.\n  - An operator that disagrees can ask for reconfirmation within 10 days.\n  - MSIT answers a reconfirmation request within 30 days, after consulting the expert committee.\n- The expert committee has 50 or more members appointed by MSIT, serving two-year terms renewable once.\n- MSIT may publish guidelines with criteria and examples of high-impact AI (Art. 33(3)).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Transparency and notification (Art. 31; Decree Art. 23)\n\n| Duty | Applies to | How (Decree Art. 23) | Enforcement |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Advance notice** (Art. 31(1)) | Operators providing products or services that use high-impact **or** generative AI | Stated on the product or in contracts, manuals or terms; shown on the user's screen; posted where the product is provided; or another method MSIT approves | **Directly finable** (Art. 43(1)1) |\n| **Output labeling** (Art. 31(2)) | Operators providing generative AI, or products or services that use it | A method humans can recognize, **or** a machine-readable method such as a watermark. With a machine-readable method, users must also be told at least once, by text or voice, that the output is AI-generated | Fact-finding, then a corrective order (Art. 40) |\n| **Realistic synthetic content** (Art. 31(3)) | Sound, images, video and similar outputs that are hard to tell apart from real ones | A notice users can clearly recognize, by sight, by sound or through software, adapted to the age and the physical or social conditions of the main users. For artistic or creative works, the notice may be given in a way that does not hinder display or enjoyment | Fact-finding, then a corrective order (Art. 40) |\n\n- **Exemptions (Decree Art. 23(4)):** all or part of these duties can be waived in three cases:\n  - where AI use is obvious from the product name, the screen text or the outputs;\n  - where the AI is used only for the operator's internal business;\n  - in other cases MSIT designates by notice.\n- **Compared with EU Art. 50:** the EU splits transparency duties between two roles, and they apply from 2 Aug 2026.\n  - Providers must disclose AI interaction and mark synthetic outputs in machine-readable form.\n  - Deployers must disclose emotion recognition, deepfakes and certain AI-generated public-interest text.\n  - Korea instead puts all three duties on \"AI business operators\", and they have applied since 22 Jan 2026.\n\n---\n\n## 7. High-impact AI duties (Arts. 30, 34-35; Decree Arts. 27-28)\n\n### 7.1 The six Art. 34(1) measures, mapped to AIGP themes\n\n| # | Measure | AIGP theme | Decree detail |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Set up and run a **risk management plan** | Risk management | Post the key contents, such as the risk policy and organizational structure |\n| 2 | Set up and carry out an **explanation plan**, covering final outputs, the key criteria used to reach them and an overview of the training data, as far as is technically feasible | Transparency, explainability, data governance | Post the key contents |\n| 3 | Set up and run **user protection measures** | Consumer protection | Post the measures |\n| 4 | **Human management and supervision** of the high-impact AI | Human oversight | Post the name and contact details of the person responsible |\n| 5 | Prepare and keep **documents** showing the safety and trust measures taken | Documentation, record keeping | Keep them for **5 years**; electronic storage is allowed (Decree Art. 27(2)) |\n| 6 | Any other measures the presidential committee resolves on | Catch-all | — |\n\n- **Posting:** items 1-4 must be posted at the operator's premises or on its website, but trade secrets may be left out (Decree Art. 27(1)).\n- **Guidance:** MSIT may publish detailed measures and recommend that operators follow them (Art. 34(2)).\n- **Equivalence:** equivalent measures taken under other laws count as compliance (Art. 34(3); Decree Attached Table 1).\n- **Voluntary certification:** operators \"shall strive\" to obtain verification and certification before providing high-impact AI, and public bodies should give priority to certified AI (Art. 30(3)-(4)).\n- **SME support:** MSIT may help SMEs carry out the Art. 34 measures and Art. 35 assessments (Art. 17(3)).\n- **What is missing compared with the EU:** Korea has none of the following:\n  - a quality management system;\n  - conformity assessment or CE marking;\n  - a registration database;\n  - data-quality requirements for training data (only an overview, in the explanation plan);\n  - an automatic logging duty;\n  - explicit post-market monitoring or serious-incident reporting for high-impact AI.\n\n### 7.2 Impact assessment (Art. 35; Decree Art. 28)\n\n- **Best efforts only:** operators providing high-impact AI \"shall make efforts\" to assess its impact on fundamental rights in advance. Skipping it carries no fine, and it is not a trigger for fact-finding under Art. 40.\n- **Procurement incentive:** national institutions (the state, local governments and public institutions) that use high-impact AI must give priority to products or services that have been assessed (Art. 35(2)).\n- **Contents (Decree Art. 28(1)):**\n  - the individuals or groups affected;\n  - the fundamental rights at stake;\n  - social and economic impacts;\n  - usage patterns;\n  - evaluation indicators and calculation methods;\n  - measures to prevent and mitigate risks and to recover losses;\n  - an improvement plan where one is needed.\n- **Who performs it:** the operator itself, or a third party it commissions (Decree Art. 28(2)).\n- **Since 21 Jul 2026:** the assessment must reflect the characteristics of AI-vulnerable groups, in light of the product or service (Act No. 21311). Decree Art. 1-2 defines these groups; they include people with disabilities and people aged 65 or over. Decree Art. 28(1)1 was amended so that the identification of affected groups takes them into account.\n- **Compared with the EU FRIA (Art. 27):** the EU assessment is mandatory, but only for certain deployers: bodies governed by public law, private entities providing public services, and deployers of credit-scoring or life and health insurance pricing systems.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Role differences: developers and AI-using operators\n\n| Issue | Korea | EU AI Act |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Role labels** | Two types of AI business operator: developer and AI-using business operator | Provider, deployer, importer, distributor, authorized representative, product manufacturer |\n| **Duty sets** | One shared set, which attaches to whoever *provides* the high-impact or generative AI product or service | Separate duty sets (e.g., providers under Art. 16, deployers under Art. 26) |\n| **Downstream relief** | **Deemed compliance** (Decree Art. 27(3)): an AI-using operator takes a system from an operator that carried out measures 1-3 (risk management, explanation, user protection). If it makes no significant functional change, such as substantially altering the original purpose or use, it is treated as having carried out those measures | A deployer that substantially modifies a high-risk system, or changes its intended purpose, becomes its provider (Art. 25) |\n| **What stays with the downstream operator** | The deeming rule does **not** cover measure 4 (human oversight) or measure 5 (documentation), and the advance-notice duty also remains | Deployer duties such as human oversight, monitoring, keeping logs and informing workers |\n| **Information flow** | The AI-using operator may ask the developer for the materials it needs, and the developer must try to cooperate (Decree Art. 27(4)) | Providers must give deployers instructions for use (Art. 13) |\n| **Public sector** | Government bodies are AI business operators too | Public authorities act as deployers, and the FRIA is mandatory for them |\n\n---\n\n## 9. \"High-performance\" AI safety duties (Art. 32; Decree Art. 24)\n\n- **Scope test:** a system must meet **all three** criteria in Decree Art. 24(1):\n  1. cumulative training compute of at least **10^26 floating-point operations**;\n  2. it is built and run with the most advanced AI technology now in use (state of the art);\n  3. its risks could have wide-ranging, significant effects on life, physical safety and fundamental rights.\n- **Duties (Art. 32(1)-(2)):**\n  - identify, assess and mitigate risks across the whole AI lifecycle;\n  - build a risk management system that monitors and responds to AI safety incidents;\n  - submit the results to MSIT.\n- **Details:** MSIT sets the method for counting compute and issues detailed guidelines (Art. 32(3); Decree Art. 24(2)). The criteria are reviewed every three years (Decree Art. 33).\n- **Compared with the EU:**\n  - All EU GPAI models carry baseline duties: technical documentation, information for downstream providers, a copyright policy and a training-content summary.\n  - A model is *presumed* to have systemic risk above **10^25 FLOPs** (Art. 51(2)). That adds evaluations, adversarial testing, incident reporting and cybersecurity duties.\n  - Korea has no general GPAI tier. Its compute bar is higher, it adds two qualitative tests, and the duty attaches to AI *systems* rather than models.\n- **Terminology:** \"high-performance AI\" is a label commentators use (Cooley, for example). The Act itself just refers to systems whose training compute meets the decree threshold.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Domestic representative (Art. 36; Decree Art. 29)\n\n- **Who must appoint one:** an AI business operator with no address or place of business in Korea that meets **any one** of these thresholds:\n  - prior-year total revenue of **KRW 1 trillion** or more;\n  - prior-year AI-service revenue of **KRW 10 billion** or more;\n  - an average of **1 million daily users in Korea** over the three months before the end of the prior year;\n  - a past fine for ignoring an MSIT suspension or corrective order.\n  - Revenue is converted to won at the prior year's average exchange rate.\n- **How:** designate the representative in writing and report it to MSIT. The representative must have a Korean address or place of business.\n- **Tasks:**\n  - submit the Art. 32 safety results;\n  - request high-impact confirmation under Art. 33;\n  - support the Art. 34 measures, including checking that the required documents are current and accurate.\n- **Liability:** violations by the representative are treated as the appointing operator's own (Art. 36(3)).\n- **Penalty:** failing to appoint one is directly finable (Art. 43(1)2).\n- **Compared with the EU:** the EU requires an authorized representative for *every* non-EU provider of high-risk systems (Art. 22) or GPAI models (Art. 54), with no revenue or user thresholds.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Enforcement and penalties (Arts. 40-43)\n\n### 11.1 Fact-finding and orders (Art. 40)\n\n- **Triggers:** MSIT finds or suspects a breach, or receives a report or complaint about one, of:\n  - Art. 31(2)-(3) (labeling);\n  - Art. 32(1)-(2) (high-performance safety);\n  - Art. 34(1) (high-impact measures).\n- **Powers:** MSIT can require data, and its officials can enter offices and inspect books, documents and other materials. The procedure follows the Framework Act on Administrative Investigations.\n- **Outcome:** an order to stop or correct the violation.\n\n### 11.2 Administrative fines (Art. 43)\n\n| Finable failure | Maximum |\n|---|---|\n| No advance notice of high-impact or generative AI (Art. 31(1)) | KRW 30 million |\n| No domestic representative (Art. 36(1)) | KRW 30 million |\n| Ignoring a suspension or corrective order (Art. 40(3)) | KRW 30 million |\n\n- MSIT imposes and collects the fines. Per-violation amounts are set in the Decree's Attached Table 2.\n- KRW 30 million is roughly US$21,000-22,000.\n- **Not directly finable:**\n  - output labeling, the realistic-content notice, the high-performance safety duties and the high-impact measures. These all go through an Art. 40 order first;\n  - the high-impact review (Art. 33) and the best-efforts impact assessment (Art. 35), which fall under neither Art. 40 nor Art. 43.\n- **Criminal penalty (Art. 42):** up to 3 years' imprisonment or a KRW 30 million fine for committee members who leak or misuse confidential information. It does not target AI operators.\n- **No private right of action:** the Act creates none, although a complaint can prompt MSIT fact-finding.\n\n### 11.3 Grace period (policy, not statute)\n\n- MSIT announced a grace period of **at least one year** from 22 Jan 2026. During it, fact-finding investigations (including corrective orders) and fines are generally deferred.\n- The exception is serious cases, such as loss of life or human-rights violations.\n\n### 11.4 The EU contrast\n\n- **EU AI Act fines (Art. 99):**\n  - up to **€35M or 7%** of worldwide annual turnover for prohibited practices;\n  - up to **€15M or 3%** for most other obligations, including Art. 50 transparency;\n  - up to **€7.5M or 1%** for supplying misleading information;\n  - for SMEs, the lower of the two figures applies.\n- **Enforcers:** the Commission fines GPAI providers separately. Enforcement is split between national market surveillance authorities and the AI Office (for GPAI), against Korea's single ministry.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Governance and promotion bodies\n\n| Body or tool | Role | Article |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Presidential Council on National AI Strategy** (originally the \"National AI Committee\") | Chaired by the President. Deliberates on the Basic Plan, AI policy and the regulation of high-impact AI. Up to 60 members (45 before the January 2026 amendment). Exists for 5 years from 22 Jan 2026. It has no investigation or fining powers | Arts. 7-8 |\n| **AI Basic Plan** | A national plan MSIT sets every three years, after the committee deliberates | Art. 6 |\n| **AI Policy Center** | A policy support body designated by MSIT | Art. 11 |\n| **AI Safety Institute** (CSET translation: \"AI Safety Research Institute\") | Defines risks, researches safety evaluation, works on standards and supports Art. 32 compliance | Art. 12 |\n| **AI ethics principles** | The government may issue them, covering safety, accessibility and human well-being | Art. 27 |\n| **Private autonomous AI ethics committees** | Voluntary committees at universities, research bodies and companies. They must not be single-gender and must include outside members | Art. 28 |\n| **Industry promotion** | Training data, standardization, SME and startup support, AI clusters, data centers, talent, and support for verification and certification | Arts. 13-26, 30 |\n\n---\n\n## 13. Comparison with the EU AI Act\n\n| Dimension | Korea AI Basic Act | EU AI Act |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Character** | Framework act: promotion plus light trust duties | Product-safety-style, risk-based regulation |\n| **Risk structure** | Categories: high-impact, generative, high-compute (\"high-performance\") | Prohibited, high-risk, transparency (limited) risk, minimal risk, plus a GPAI tier |\n| **Prohibited practices** | **None** | Art. 5 list, applying since 2 Feb 2025 |\n| **How classification works** | The operator reviews in advance; MSIT confirms on request within 30 days | The provider classifies against Art. 6 and Annexes I/III, with the Art. 6(3) derogation documented |\n| **High-risk / high-impact duties** | Six measures: risk plan, explanation plan, user protection, human oversight, documents, other | Arts. 9-15 requirements plus a QMS (Art. 17), conformity assessment, CE marking, registration and post-market monitoring |\n| **Conformity assessment** | None; voluntary verification and certification is encouraged | Required before a system is placed on the market |\n| **Impact assessment** | **Best efforts**, for operators providing high-impact AI; public buyers must prefer assessed products | FRIA **mandatory** for certain deployers (Art. 27) |\n| **Documentation retention** | 5 years (Decree) | 10 years (Art. 18) |\n| **Transparency** | Advance notice (high-impact and generative), output labeling, clear notice for realistic content; all on \"AI business operators\" | Art. 50: interaction disclosure and machine-readable marking (providers); deepfake disclosure (deployers) |\n| **General-purpose / frontier** | Systems with at least 10^26 FLOPs that are also state-of-the-art and pose wide-ranging risk: lifecycle risk management, results to MSIT | All GPAI: documentation, copyright policy, training summary. Systemic risk presumed above 10^25 FLOPs: evaluations, incident reporting, cybersecurity |\n| **Roles** | Developer and AI-using operator share one duty set; deemed-compliance rule | Separate provider, deployer, importer and distributor duties; the Art. 25 role switch |\n| **Foreign operators** | Domestic representative only above the size thresholds | Authorized representative for all non-EU high-risk and GPAI providers |\n| **Regulator** | **MSIT** (a single ministry) | National market surveillance authorities, the AI Office (GPAI) and the AI Board |\n| **Maximum penalties** | **KRW 30 million**, and only for three failures | €35M/7%, €15M/3%, €7.5M/1% |\n| **Territorial reach** | Conduct abroad that affects the Korean market or users | Providers placing systems on the EU market, and non-EU providers or deployers whose output is used in the EU |\n| **Exclusions** | AI used solely for defense or national security | Military, defense and national security; research; personal use; some open-source systems |\n| **Timing** | All at once on 22 Jan 2026, plus a policy grace period of at least one year | Staggered from 2025 to 2028. The Digital Omnibus (Reg. (EU) 2026/1744) moved Annex III high-risk duties from 2 Aug 2026 to 2 Dec 2027 and Annex I duties to 2 Aug 2028 |\n| **Promotion** | Extensive: Basic Plan, institutes, data, clusters, SMEs, startups | Limited: regulatory sandboxes and SME measures |\n\n---\n\n## 14. Applying it: a compliance sequence for a multinational\n\n1. **Scope:** list the AI products and services that reach Korean users or the Korean market (Art. 4(1)). Screen out only systems used *solely* for defense or national security.\n2. **Classify each system:**\n   - Is it generative?\n   - Is it in a listed high-impact domain, and does it pose significant risk?\n   - Was it trained with at least 10^26 FLOPs, and does it meet the two qualitative tests?\n   - If high-impact status is unclear, ask MSIT to confirm.\n3. **Representative:** test the three size thresholds. If any one is met, appoint a domestic representative in writing and report it to MSIT.\n4. **Transparency:** give advance notice for high-impact and generative services, label generative outputs, and add clear notices for realistic synthetic media.\n5. **High-impact systems:**\n   - put the six measures in place;\n   - post the summaries and the name of the person responsible for oversight;\n   - keep records for 5 years;\n   - run the best-efforts impact assessment (it helps in public procurement).\n6. **Downstream operators:** document that no significant functional change was made, so you can rely on the deeming rule. Ask the developer for the materials you need. Keep your own oversight and records.\n7. **Monitor:** watch MSIT notices and guidelines, decree amendments and the end of the grace period.\n\n---\n\n## Exam Angles\n\n- **No prohibited practices.** Any option saying Korea \"bans\" a practice, such as social scoring or real-time biometric identification, is wrong. Korea regulates through notice, labeling and high-impact duties.\n- **Fines are low and narrow:** a KRW 30 million cap, for only three failures. These are missing advance notice, missing domestic representative, and ignoring an MSIT order. Labeling and high-impact breaches go through investigation and a corrective order first. Contrast the EU's €35M/7% ceiling.\n- **The impact assessment is best efforts.** It is not mandatory and not finable, and public procurement provides the incentive. The EU FRIA is mandatory, but only for certain deployers.\n- **High-impact AI needs a listed domain plus significant risk.** The operator reviews it first, and MSIT confirms on request within 30 days. Watch for K-12-only student assessment, and remember that hiring, lending, policing biometrics, critical infrastructure, health and public-sector decisions are all covered.\n- **\"High-performance\" AI means 10^26 FLOPs plus state of the art plus wide-ranging risk,** with results reported to MSIT. The EU presumes systemic risk for GPAI models at 10^25. Don't swap the numbers.\n- **MSIT is the regulator.** The presidential council deliberates on policy but does not enforce. The privacy regulator (PIPC) is a distractor.\n- **The grace period is at least one year and is MSIT policy, not statute,** with exceptions for serious harm. The obligations have applied since 22 Jan 2026.\n- **Domestic representative:** any one threshold is enough (KRW 1 trillion revenue, KRW 10 billion AI-service revenue, 1 million daily Korean users, or a past fine for ignoring an order). The foreign operator answers for the representative's violations.\n- **Roles:** both operator types share the duties. The deeming rule covers only measures 1-3, and only if the downstream operator makes no significant functional change. Human oversight and documentation stay with it.\n- **Dates and reach:** promulgated 21 Jan 2025, in force 22 Jan 2026 (one year later), Decree No. 36053. It reaches conduct abroad that affects Korea, and excludes AI used solely for defense or national security.\n\n---\n\n## Key Terms\n\n- [[High-Impact AI (Korea)]]\n- [[AI Business Operator (Korea)]]\n- [[Domestic Representative (Korea AI Basic Act)]]\n- [[Generative AI Labeling (Korea)]]\n- [[Korea AI Basic Act Enforcement Decree]]\n- [[Best-Efforts Impact Assessment (Korea)]]\n- [[High-Performance AI (Korea)]]\n\n---\n\n## Sources\n\n- **Official English translation of the Act, Korea Legislation Research Institute (KLRI)** — government-funded, and the most authoritative English text available: https://elaw.klri.re.kr/eng_service/lawView.do?hseq=71019&lang=ENG\n- CSET English translation of the Act as enacted (translated 9 Jul 2025): https://cset.georgetown.edu/wp-content/uploads/t0625_south_korea_ai_law_EN.pdf\n- Official Korean text of the Act, current version (Act No. 21311, in force 21 Jul 2026), Korean Law Information Center: https://www.law.go.kr/LSW/lsInfoP.do?lsiSeq=282791&chrClsCd=010202&urlMode=lsInfoP&efYd=20260721&ancYnChk=0\n- Official Korean text of the Enforcement Decree, current version: https://www.law.go.kr/LSW/lsInfoP.do?lsiSeq=288781&chrClsCd=010202&urlMode=lsInfoP&efYd=20260820&ancYnChk=0\n- AI Basic Act explorer (Korea AI Safety Institute and Future of Life Institute): https://aibasicact.kr/explorer/\n- Enforcement Decree explorer: https://aibasicact.kr/decree-explorer/\n- Legislative timeline: https://aibasicact.kr/blog/historic-timeline\n- Cooley, \"South Korea's AI Basic Act: Overview and Key Takeaways\" (27 Jan 2026): https://www.cooley.com/news/insight/2026/2026-01-27-south-koreas-ai-basic-act-overview-and-key-takeaways\n- US International Trade Administration, \"South Korea AI Basic Act\": https://www.trade.gov/market-intelligence/south-korea-ai-basic-act\n- Shin & Kim newsletter (11 Feb 2026): https://www.shinkim.com/eng/media/newsletter/3117\n- Future of Privacy Forum, \"South Korea's New AI Framework Act\": https://fpf.org/blog/south-koreas-new-ai-framework-act-a-balancing-act-between-innovation-and-regulation/\n\n---\n\n> [!source] Framework Recall Cards — AIGP Study Kit\n\n## South Korean AI Basic Act\n\n*Named in the BoK beside the EU AI Act. The Wiley book does not cover it.*\n\n### What it is, and why the BoK names it\n\nFormally the **Act on the Development of Artificial Intelligence and the Establishment of a Foundation for Trust** — the AI Basic Act or AI Framework Act. Promulgated 21 January 2025, **took effect 22 January 2026** with its Enforcement Decree. It makes Korea the **second jurisdiction after the EU** with a comprehensive AI law, and the first in Asia-Pacific.\n\nIts defining feature is pairing **industrial promotion** (funding, talent, clustering) with **binding obligations** — a 'minimum regulation' philosophy, deliberately less prescriptive than the EU model.\n\n### Its core obligations and structure\n\n**High-impact AI** — systems significantly affecting human life, safety or fundamental rights, in sectors including healthcare, energy, employment, finance, public services and education. Obligations: risk management, **impact assessments**, human oversight, documentation and explanation.\n**Generative AI** — transparency duties including **labelling AI-generated content** and notifying users they are dealing with AI.\n**Extraterritorial**, with a **domestic representative** required for foreign operators without a Korean establishment — the direct analogue of the EU's authorized representative.\n**Enforcement:** administrative fines up to **KRW 30 million** (roughly USD 21,000) — dramatically lower than the EU's, and subject to a grace period of at least a year from January 2026.\n\n### Korea vs the EU AI Act — the contrasts worth holding\n\n**Shared:** risk/impact tiering driving proportionate duties; extraterritorial reach; a domestic-representative requirement; impact assessments; transparency for synthetic content.\n**Different:** Korea has **no prohibited-practices tier** equivalent to the EU's; penalties are orders of magnitude smaller; the approach is framework legislation with detail delegated to enforcement decrees and MSIT guidance; and its high-performance compute threshold is **10^26 FLOPs** — about ten times the EU's 10^25 GPAI trigger.", "t_us_ai": "## At a Glance\n\nThe US has no horizontal AI statute. Private-sector organizations instead face a patchwork of state and local laws. Decision laws cover Colorado's ADMT Act, California's CPPA ADMT rules, NYC Local Law 144 and Illinois HB 3773. Frontier-model safety laws are California SB 53, New York's RAISE Act and Illinois SB 315. Transparency and content laws include Utah's, California SB 942 and AB 2013, and the California and Colorado chatbot laws. Illinois BIPA covers biometrics, and Texas TRAIGA bans intentional misuse. Federally, the TAKE IT DOWN Act targets intimate deepfakes, while EO 14365 and a DOJ task force push back on state laws without preempting them.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nThis is new in BOK v2.1 Domain II. Expect scenario questions that turn on one distinguishing detail: which state's law applies, whether the standard is intent (Texas) or effect (Illinois), who enforces, and whether individuals can sue (among these laws, only BIPA and California SB 243 allow it). The numbers matter too: 10 business days' notice and yearly audits in NYC, 30-day explanations and a 60-day cure in Colorado, 15 days versus 72 hours for frontier incidents, and 48-hour takedowns. The biggest currency trap is Colorado: SB 24-205 never took effect.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Colorado SB 24-205 was repealed before taking effect. SB 26-189 governs consequential decisions from 1 Jan 2027 through notice, a 30-day adverse-outcome explanation, data correction and human review, enforced only by the AG with a 60-day cure\n- Texas TRAIGA (1 Jan 2026) bans intentional AI misuse. Disparate impact alone is not discrimination, most disclosure duties fall on government, and the AG enforces alone with a 60-day cure\n- Illinois HB 3773 applies an effect standard to employer AI and bans zip-code proxies. The Video Interview Act requires notice, explanation and consent; BIPA gives individuals a private right of action\n- The frontier trio (California SB 53, New York RAISE, Illinois SB 315) share the 10^26-operation and $500M-revenue thresholds. Incident deadlines are 15 days in California and 72 hours in New York and Illinois; only Illinois requires annual third-party audits, from 2028\n- NYC Local Law 144 requires an independent bias audit within the year before use, a public summary and 10 business days' notice. The audit reports impact ratios by sex and race/ethnicity, with no pass/fail threshold\n- California's stack: AB 2013 training-data summaries, SB 942 provenance (operative 2 Aug 2026), the CPPA ADMT rules (compliance 1 Jan 2027) and SB 243 for companion chatbots (private right of action)\n- Federal: the TAKE IT DOWN Act requires 48-hour removal, with FTC enforcement from 19 May 2026. EO 14365 cannot preempt state law by itself, and no federal moratorium has been enacted\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] US AI Laws — Federal and State\n\n# US AI Laws, Federal and State — Key Provisions\n\n> **Status:** No comprehensive federal AI statute and no federal preemption of state AI laws, as of 23 September 2026. State and city laws fill the gap, each aimed at a use (hiring, consequential decisions), a technology (frontier models, generative content, chatbots, biometrics) or a harm (intimate deepfakes). **Effective:** staggered, from 2020 (Illinois video interviews) to 2028 (Illinois frontier-model audits, California capture devices). **Regulators:** mainly state attorneys general (AGs); also NYC's Department of Consumer and Worker Protection (DCWP), the California Privacy Protection Agency (CPPA), Utah's Division of Consumer Protection, a new office in New York's Department of Financial Services (DFS), the Illinois Emergency Management Agency and Office of Homeland Security (IEMA-OHS) and, federally, the FTC.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n- **Why it is on the exam.** BOK v2.1 (effective 2 Feb 2026) Domain II now names \"federal and state AI laws that apply to private sector organizations\". The older v2.0.1 blueprint excluded state laws as too piecemeal, so earlier study material often skips them.\n- **What lives here and what lives elsewhere.** This note covers AI-specific statutes and regulations. General laws applied to AI (FTC Act s.5, ECOA and Regulation B, FCRA, Title VII, the Fair Housing Act) are in [[Sectoral and Civil Laws]].\n- **The US shape.** There is no horizontal, EU-style AI Act. The laws fall into four families:\n  1. **Decision laws:** notice, explanation and review rules for AI that shapes hiring, lending, housing and similar decisions (Colorado SB 26-189, the CPPA ADMT rules, NYC Local Law 144, Illinois HB 3773).\n  2. **Frontier-model safety laws:** published safety frameworks, transparency reports and incident reporting for the largest developers (California SB 53, New York RAISE Act, Illinois SB 315).\n  3. **Transparency and content laws:** telling people they are dealing with AI, labelling synthetic media, summarising training data (Utah, California SB 942 and AB 2013, the chatbot laws).\n  4. **Conduct bans:** named intentional misuses (Texas TRAIGA, the federal TAKE IT DOWN Act).\n- **Federal posture, 2025-2026.** The federal government has moved to restrain state regulation (EO 14365 and the DOJ AI Litigation Task Force) but Congress has not passed a moratorium or preemption statute. The Senate voted 99-1 to strip a proposed moratorium on state AI laws from the 2025 budget reconciliation bill. State laws stay enforceable unless a court strikes them down or Congress acts.\n\n---\n\n## 2. At-a-Glance Comparison\n\n| Law | Scope | Core duties | Enforcer | Penalties | Effective |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Colorado SB 26-189** (ADMT Act) | Developers and deployers of ADMT that materially influences a consequential decision in 7 domains | Developer documentation; deployer notice; 30-day post-adverse explanation; data correction; human review; 3-year records | AG only, through the Colorado Consumer Protection Act; no private right of action | Deceptive trade practice; 60-day cure (cure clause sunsets 1 Jan 2030) | Decisions made on or after 1 Jan 2027 |\n| **Colorado HB 26-1263** (conversational AI) | Operators of public-facing chatbots | AI disclosure; age estimation; safeguards for minors; self-harm protocol; no licensed-professional claims | Annual reports go to the AG; the Act adds no penalty clause of its own | None stated in the Act | Duties 1 Jan 2027; reports from 1 Jul 2027 |\n| **Texas TRAIGA** (HB 149) | Anyone doing business in Texas, serving Texans, or developing or deploying AI there | Intent-based bans (manipulation, discrimination, rights, sexual content); government-only disclosure, social-scoring and biometric rules; health-care AI disclosure | AG only; no private right of action; 60-day cure | $10k-12k curable; $80k-200k uncurable; $2k-40k per day continuing; agency sanctions up to $100k | 1 Jan 2026 |\n| **Utah AI Policy Act** and SB 226 | Suppliers using generative AI with consumers; licensed or state-certified professionals | Disclose if clearly asked; disclose up front in high-risk interactions; no \"the AI did it\" defence | Division of Consumer Protection (AG acts as counsel) | Up to $2,500 per violation; up to $5,000 for breaching an order | 1 May 2024; amendments 7 May 2025; Policy Act sunset 1 Jul 2027 |\n| **California SB 53** (TFAIA) | Frontier developers (models above 10^26 operations); \"large\" means over $500M revenue | Large: publish and follow a frontier AI framework; 3-monthly internal-use risk summaries to Cal OES. All: transparency report at deployment; incidents within 15 days (24h if imminent danger) | AG only | Up to $1M per violation (large frontier developers) | 1 Jan 2026 |\n| **California AB 2013** | Developers of public generative AI released since 1 Jan 2022 | Post training-data documentation on the developer's website | The Act has no penalty clause | None stated in the Act | Due 1 Jan 2026, then before each release or substantial modification |\n| **California AI Transparency Act** (SB 942 as amended by AB 853) | Covered providers: generative AI with over 1M monthly users, publicly accessible in California | Free AI detection tool; latent provenance disclosure in image, video and audio; offer a visible-label option; revoke non-compliant licences within 96h | AG, city attorneys, county counsel | $5,000 per violation; each day a separate violation | 2 Aug 2026; large platforms 1 Jan 2027; capture devices 1 Jan 2028 |\n| **CPPA regulations** (ADMT, risk assessments, cybersecurity audits) | CCPA-covered businesses | ADMT for significant decisions: pre-use notice, opt-out (with exceptions), access; risk assessments; annual cybersecurity audits | CPPA | CCPA penalty provisions | Regulations 1 Jan 2026; ADMT 1 Jan 2027; first filings 1 Apr 2028 |\n| **California SB 243** (companion chatbots) | Operators of companion chatbots | AI disclosure; self-harm protocol; for known minors, AI notice, 3-hourly break reminders, sexual-content blocks; annual report to the Office of Suicide Prevention from 1 Jul 2027 | **Private right of action** | Greater of actual damages or $1,000 per violation, plus fees | 1 Jan 2026 |\n| **NYC Local Law 144** | Employers and employment agencies using AEDTs on NYC candidates and employees | Independent bias audit no more than 1 year before use; public summary; 10 business days' notice; data details within 30 days of a request | DCWP; Corporation Counsel in court | Up to $500 first; $500-1,500 each later; each day and each missed notice counts separately | 1 Jan 2023; enforced from 5 Jul 2023 |\n| **New York RAISE Act** (as re-enacted 2026) | Frontier developers; \"large\" means over $500M revenue | Large: framework and disclosure statement with the DFS office. All: transparency report at deployment; incidents within 72h (24h if imminent) | AG; DFS office | Up to $1M first, $3M later; $1,000 per day for disclosure failures | 1 Jan 2027 |\n| **Illinois HB 3773** (Human Rights Act 2-102(L)) | Employers | No AI with a discriminatory *effect*; no zip codes as proxies; notice of AI use | Department of Human Rights (IDHR), under the Human Rights Act | Human Rights Act remedies | 1 Jan 2026 |\n| **Illinois AI Video Interview Act** | Employers using AI on video interviews for Illinois-based jobs | Notice, explanation, consent; limited sharing; delete within 30 days of a request; demographic report if AI alone picks in-person interviews | The Act has no enforcement section | None stated in the Act | 1 Jan 2020; reporting duty 1 Jan 2022 |\n| **Illinois BIPA** | Private entities handling biometric identifiers | Public retention and destruction policy; informed written release before collection; no profiting; disclosure limits; reasonable security | **Private right of action** | $1,000 negligent or $5,000 intentional or reckless (or actual damages if higher), plus fees | 2008; amended 2 Aug 2024 |\n| **Illinois SB 315** (AI Safety Measures Act) | Frontier developers; \"large\" means over $500M revenue | Disclosure statement (2027); framework and **annual independent third-party audit** (2028); incidents within 72h to IEMA-OHS and the AG | AG exclusively; IEMA-OHS for filings | Up to $1M first, $3M later; $1,000 per day for disclosure failures | 1 Jan 2027 |\n| **TAKE IT DOWN Act** (Pub. L. 119-12) | Individuals (criminal offence); covered platforms that host user content | Crime to knowingly publish nonconsensual intimate images, including AI \"digital forgeries\"; platforms run a notice-and-removal process and remove within 48h | DOJ (criminal); FTC (platforms, including nonprofits) | Up to 2 years' prison (3 if a minor); FTC Act penalties for platforms | Criminal 19 May 2025; platforms 19 May 2026 |\n| **EO 14365** | Federal agencies | DOJ litigation task force; Commerce review of state laws; BEAD funding conditions; FCC and FTC actions; legislative proposal | No direct duties on companies | None | Signed 11 Dec 2025 |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Colorado\n\n### 3.1 SB 24-205: the AI Act that never took effect\n\n- Signed 17 May 2024 with a 1 Feb 2026 start date; SB 25B-004 (signed 28 Aug 2025) pushed the start to 30 Jun 2026.\n- Its model was risk management: \"high-risk AI systems\", a duty of reasonable care to avoid algorithmic discrimination, deployer risk programmes, impact assessments, AG notice of discovered discrimination, and a general AI-interaction disclosure.\n- xAI sued in federal court to block it, and on 24 Apr 2026 DOJ moved to intervene, arguing that requiring companies to prevent unintended disparate impact breaches equal protection.\n- **SB 26-189 (signed 14 May 2026) repealed and re-enacted part 17 of Colorado's consumer protection article**, so none of SB 24-205's duties ever became enforceable.\n- **Exam trap:** questions about Colorado impact assessments or a \"duty of reasonable care\" describe a law that never applied. Read the stem's anchor: an item may say \"as originally enacted\".\n\n### 3.2 SB 26-189: automated decision-making technology in consequential decisions\n\n**Key definitions**\n\n| Term | Meaning (paraphrased from 6-1-1701) |\n|---|---|\n| **ADMT** | Technology that processes personal data and uses computation to generate outputs (predictions, recommendations, classifications, rankings, scores) used to make, guide or assist a decision about an individual. Excludes plain infrastructure tools (firewalls, spreadsheets without ML, spell-check), tools used only to summarise, draft or route information for human review, and public chat tools that are not intended for consequential decisions and whose acceptable-use policy forbids that use |\n| **Covered ADMT** | ADMT used to *materially influence* a consequential decision |\n| **Materially influence** | The output is a non-de minimis factor in the decision **and** affects its outcome (by constraining, ranking, scoring, recommending or classifying). Incidental, trivial or clerical uses do not count |\n| **Consequential decision** | Access to, eligibility for, selection for, compensation for, or pricing and terms of, a covered domain: **education, employment, residential real estate, financial or lending services, insurance, health care, essential government services and public benefits**. Routine, low-stakes steps, advertising, search and content moderation are excluded |\n| **Consumer** | Colorado residents, plus employees, Colorado-resident job applicants, and anyone evaluated for an opportunity in Colorado by a business operating there |\n| **Meaningful human review** | A reviewer with authority to approve, change or override the decision who is trained, considers the evidence, does not simply default to the output, and understands the output's intended use, limits, inputs and principal factors |\n\n**Duties**\n\n| Who | Duty | Timing |\n|---|---|---|\n| Developer | Give deployers documentation: intended and known harmful uses, categories of training data, known limitations, instructions for use, monitoring and human review | From 1 Jan 2027 |\n| Developer | Notify deployers of material updates within a reasonable time (release notes allowed); keep records for at least 3 years | Ongoing |\n| Deployer | Clear and conspicuous notice **before** using covered ADMT; a prominent public notice at points of interaction satisfies this | Before use |\n| Deployer | After an **adverse outcome**, give a plain-language description of the decision and the ADMT's role, a way to get more detail (tool name, version, developer, types and sources of personal data), and an explanation of rights | **Within 30 days** of the decision |\n| Deployer | On request after an adverse outcome: a way to correct factually incorrect or materially inaccurate personal data (not opinions, scores or predictions) and meaningful human review and reconsideration, to the extent commercially reasonable | On request |\n| Deployer | Keep compliance records for at least 3 years after each decision | Ongoing |\n\n**Enforcement and liability**\n\n- The AG enforces exclusively under the Colorado Consumer Protection Act; a violation is a deceptive trade practice. **No new private right of action**, but existing anti-discrimination and consumer remedies are preserved.\n- **Cure:** where a cure is possible, the AG must send a notice of violation and wait **60 days**. No cure period is owed for knowing or repeated violations. The cure subsection is **repealed on 1 Jan 2030**.\n- Discrimination liability under state anti-discrimination law is split between developer and deployer by relative fault, with no joint and several liability; a developer is liable only for uses it intended, documented, marketed or contracted for.\n- Contract clauses that indemnify a party against its own discriminatory use of ADMT are void.\n- The AG must adopt rules on the post-adverse-outcome disclosures and consumer rights by 1 Jan 2027.\n\n**Carve-outs and alignments**\n\n- **HIPAA covered entities and business associates:** outside the core duties **except for employment decisions**; they must still give patients a general notice of advanced technologies and specific disclosures where ADMT decides eligibility for financial assistance.\n- **Insurers** subject to Colorado's insurance-algorithm statute (10-3-1104.9) are treated as compliant for the practice of insurance, but not for their own hiring.\n- **FDA-regulated** medical devices and pharmaceutical or device R&D are outside the core duties.\n- **Creditors:** an ECOA/Regulation B (and FCRA) adverse-action notice satisfies Colorado's notice duty for the same decision if it also covers Colorado's content.\n- **Schools under FERPA** can use their existing FERPA notice and records-correction channels.\n\n**What SB 26-189 dropped compared with SB 24-205:** impact assessments, mandated risk-management programmes, the duty of reasonable care, AG notification of discovered discrimination, and the general AI-interaction disclosure.\n\n### 3.3 HB 26-1263: conversational AI services\n\n- Signed 29 May 2026; duties apply from 1 Jan 2027. It covers **operators** that make a conversational AI service available to the public. Customer-service and shopping bots, business productivity tools, narrow single-topic tools, video-game and theme-park characters, and HIPAA-regulated uses are excluded.\n- **All users:** a clear and conspicuous AI disclosure at the start of the first interaction each day, at least every 3 hours in a continuous session (or shown persistently), and whenever a user asks.\n- **Age:** operators must estimate age using commercially reasonable or generally accepted methods and may not wilfully ignore clear evidence that a user is a minor.\n- **Known minors:** AI disclosure (persistent on screen, intermittent audio, or at the start and every 3 hours); no points or rewards at unpredictable intervals meant to drive engagement; measures against sexual content and against responses simulating emotional dependence (claiming to be human or sentient, romantic companionship, adult-minor romantic role-play); privacy tools, including control over whether the minor's data trains the service, and tools for parents.\n- **Self-harm protocol:** referral to a crisis service (not law enforcement) and escalation steps for repeated or severe signals.\n- **No professional impersonation:** outputs may not be presented as coming from, endorsed by or equivalent to licensed health, legal or mental-health professionals or dietitians.\n- **Annual report to the AG** from 1 Jul 2027 (crisis referrals and protocols, with no user identifiers); the AG publishes the data.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Texas: Responsible AI Governance Act (TRAIGA, HB 149)\n\n- Signed 22 Jun 2025; **effective 1 Jan 2026**; Business and Commerce Code Title 11, Subtitle D (chapters 551-554). It applies to anyone who does business in Texas, makes products or services used by Texans, or develops or deploys AI in Texas, and it **preempts local AI ordinances**.\n- It defines an \"AI system\" in broadly OECD/EU-style terms: a machine-based system that infers from inputs how to generate outputs that can influence physical or virtual environments.\n\n| Section | Applies to | Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| 552.051 | **Government agencies**; health-care providers | Tell consumers they are interacting with AI, before or at the time of interaction, even if it seems obvious; clear, plain language, no dark patterns. Health-care providers disclose AI used in a service or treatment by the date it is first provided |\n| 552.052 | Any person | No AI developed or deployed in a way that **intentionally aims** to incite self-harm, harm to others or crime |\n| 552.053 | Government only | No social scoring that leads to unjustified or unrelated detrimental treatment |\n| 552.054 | Government only | No AI that uniquely identifies people from biometric data or scraped images without consent where that infringes their rights |\n| 552.055 | Any person | No AI with the **sole intent** of infringing constitutional rights |\n| 552.056 | Any person | No AI developed or deployed **with intent to unlawfully discriminate** against a protected class; **disparate impact alone does not show intent**. Carve-outs for regulated insurers and federally insured financial institutions |\n| 552.057 | Any person | No AI with the sole intent of producing child sexual abuse material or unlawful sexual deepfakes; no chat AI that simulates sexual conduct while impersonating a minor |\n\n**Enforcement**\n\n- **The AG has exclusive authority; there is no private right of action.** Consumers complain through an AG online portal (due by 1 Sep 2026). The AG can issue civil investigative demands asking for the system's purpose, training data, inputs, outputs, performance metrics, limitations and post-deployment safeguards.\n- **Notice and 60-day cure:** no suit before day 60, and none at all if the person cures, documents the cure and updates its policies in a written statement.\n- **Penalties:** $10,000-12,000 per curable violation (or breach of a cure statement); $80,000-200,000 per uncurable violation; $2,000-40,000 per day for a continuing violation; plus injunctions and fees. Once a violation is established and the AG recommends it, a licensing agency may add sanctions, including licence action and up to $100,000.\n- **Defences:** a rebuttable presumption of reasonable care; no liability if someone else misused the system, or if the defendant found the violation through feedback, **adversarial or red-team testing**, state agency guidelines, or an internal review while **substantially complying with the NIST AI RMF Generative AI Profile** or another recognised AI risk framework (see [[NIST AI 600-1 — Structured Reference]]). No penalties for a system that has not been deployed.\n- **Regulatory sandbox:** run by the Department of Information Resources (DIR); approved participants can test for up to **36 months** with certain laws waived, but never the chapter 552 prohibitions. A 7-member **Texas AI Council** advises.\n- **Biometric privacy amendment:** a public image does not imply consent unless the person posted it; training AI on biometric identifiers is exempt unless the system is used to identify specific people.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Utah\n\n| Law | What it did | Effective |\n|---|---|---|\n| **SB 149 (2024), Utah AI Policy Act** | GenAI used in consumer-protection-regulated activity must disclose it is AI **if asked**; regulated occupations must **prominently disclose** all GenAI use up front (orally at the start of a conversation, by electronic message before a written exchange); \"the AI did it\" is no defence; created the **Office of AI Policy**, the **AI Learning Laboratory** and **regulatory mitigation agreements** | 1 May 2024 (original sunset 1 May 2025) |\n| **SB 226 (2025), AI Consumer Protection Amendments** | Narrowed disclosure: suppliers disclose only on a **clear and unambiguous** request; licensed or state-certified professionals disclose up front only in **high-risk AI interactions** (collecting sensitive health, financial or biometric data, or giving personalised financial, legal, medical or mental-health advice); safe harbour for AI that discloses clearly at the outset and throughout | 7 May 2025 |\n| **SB 332 (2025)** | Extended the AI Policy Act's repeal date to **1 Jul 2027** | 7 May 2025 |\n| **HB 452 (2025)** | Rules for **mental-health chatbots**: disclosures, advertising limits, limits on selling or sharing users' health information | 7 May 2025 |\n| **HB 320 (2026)** | Reworked the Office of AI Policy and learning lab; added **joint interpretation agreements** alongside regulatory mitigation agreements | 6 May 2026 |\n\n- **Enforcement:** the Division of Consumer Protection, with the AG as its counsel. Fines up to **$2,500 per violation**; up to **$5,000** per breach of an administrative or court order.\n\n---\n\n## 6. California\n\n### 6.1 SB 53: Transparency in Frontier Artificial Intelligence Act (TFAIA)\n\n- Signed 29 Sep 2025 (Chapter 138); in force 1 Jan 2026 under the state's default rule for regular-session statutes.\n- **Frontier model:** a foundation model trained with more than **10^26** integer or floating-point operations, counting fine-tuning, reinforcement learning and other material modifications. **Large frontier developer:** a frontier developer that, with affiliates, had more than **$500 million** in annual gross revenue in the preceding calendar year.\n- **Catastrophic risk:** a foreseeable, material risk of more than 50 deaths or serious injuries, or more than $1 billion in damage, from one incident in which a model gives expert help with CBRN weapons, carries out a cyberattack or serious crime without meaningful human oversight, or evades its developer's control.\n\n| Duty | Who | Timing |\n|---|---|---|\n| Write, follow and publish a **frontier AI framework** (standards used, risk thresholds, mitigations, third-party assessment, cybersecurity for weights, incident response, governance) | Large frontier developers | Review at least yearly; publish material changes within 30 days |\n| Publish a **transparency report** for each new or substantially modified frontier model | All frontier developers (large ones add risk-assessment summaries) | Before or when deploying |\n| Send Cal OES summaries of **internal-use** catastrophic-risk assessments | Large frontier developers | Every 3 months or another reasonable schedule |\n| Report **critical safety incidents** to Cal OES | All frontier developers | **Within 15 days**; within **24 hours** to an appropriate authority if imminent risk of death or serious injury |\n| No materially false or misleading statements about catastrophic risk | All frontier developers | Ongoing |\n| Whistleblower protections and, for large developers, an anonymous internal reporting channel | Frontier developers | Ongoing |\n\n- **Enforcement:** civil penalties of up to **$1 million per violation**, sought only by the AG, against large frontier developers that fail to publish or send documents, make false statements, fail to report incidents or fail to follow their own framework. **No third-party audit is required.**\n- **Other:** CalCompute (a public computing consortium, subject to appropriation); preemption of local frontier-risk rules adopted from 1 Jan 2025; compliance through designated equivalent federal standards; Cal OES annual anonymised incident reports from 1 Jan 2027.\n\n### 6.2 AB 2013: Generative AI Training Data Transparency\n\n- Signed 28 Sep 2024 (Chapter 817). A **developer** (anyone who designs, codes, produces or substantially modifies a generative AI system or service for public use in California) must post documentation on its website **by 1 Jan 2026 and before each later release or substantial modification**, covering systems released on or after **1 Jan 2022**.\n- **Contents:** dataset sources or owners; how the data serves the system's purpose; number and types of data points; whether the data includes copyrighted, trademarked or patented material or is wholly public domain; whether it was purchased or licensed; whether it includes personal information or aggregate consumer information; cleaning and processing; collection period; first-use dates; use of synthetic data.\n- **Exempt:** systems used only for security and integrity, aircraft operation, or national security, military or defence purposes made available only to a federal entity.\n- The Act contains no penalty clause of its own.\n\n### 6.3 SB 942: California AI Transparency Act, as amended by AB 853\n\n- Signed 19 Sep 2024 (Chapter 291). **AB 853 (Chapter 674, signed 13 Oct 2025) moved the operative date from 1 Jan 2026 to 2 Aug 2026.**\n- **Covered provider:** creates or produces a generative AI system with **more than 1,000,000 monthly visitors or users** that is publicly accessible in California.\n- **Duties:** offer a **free AI detection tool** (with upload or URL input and API access); embed a **latent disclosure** (provider name, system name and version, creation or edit time, unique identifier) in AI-generated **image, video or audio**; and **offer users the option** of a visible, manifest disclosure. Text output is not covered.\n- **Licensees:** if a licensee modifies the system so it can no longer disclose, the provider must revoke the licence **within 96 hours** of discovering this, and the licensee must stop using it.\n- **AB 853 additions:** from **1 Jan 2027**, large online platforms (over 2,000,000 unique monthly users) must detect and surface provenance data and must not knowingly strip it, and GenAI hosting platforms may not knowingly offer systems that lack the disclosures; from **1 Jan 2028**, capture-device makers must let users embed a latent disclosure in photos, video and audio.\n- **Penalties:** **$5,000 per violation**, each day a separate violation, in actions by the AG, a city attorney or county counsel.\n\n### 6.4 CPPA regulations: ADMT, risk assessments and cybersecurity audits\n\n- Approved by the Office of Administrative Law on 22 Sep 2025 (announced 23 Sep 2025); **effective 1 Jan 2026**.\n- **ADMT:** technology that processes personal information and uses computation to **replace or substantially replace human decision-making**. A tool is outside the definition if a human reviewer knows how to interpret the output, reviews it with other relevant information, and has authority to make or change the decision.\n- **Significant decision:** provision or denial of financial or lending services, housing, education enrolment or opportunities, employment or independent-contracting opportunities or compensation, or health-care services. Advertising is not on the list.\n\n| Obligation | Rule | Date |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Pre-use notice** | Specific purpose (not generic wording), the opt-out and access rights, and how the ADMT works | Before processing |\n| **Opt-out** | Required, **unless** the business offers an appeal to a human reviewer with authority to overturn the decision, or (for admission, hiring and work-allocation uses) the ADMT is used solely to assess ability or allocate work and does not unlawfully discriminate; at least two opt-out methods | **Compliance by 1 Jan 2027** for ADMT already in use |\n| **Access** | Plain-language purpose, the logic of the ADMT, and how its output was used in the outcome | On request |\n| **Risk assessment** | Required before high-risk processing: selling or sharing data, sensitive data, ADMT for significant decisions, some automated inferences, training ADMT or facial or emotion recognition. Review at least every 3 years; update within 45 days of a material change | From 1 Jan 2026; pre-2026 processing assessed by 31 Dec 2027 |\n| **Risk-assessment submission** | Summary information and a signed attestation to the CPPA | **1 Apr 2028** for 2026-2027 assessments, then each 1 Apr |\n| **Cybersecurity audit** | Annual independent audit for businesses whose processing presents significant security risk | First report due **1 Apr 2028** (2026 revenue over $100M), **1 Apr 2029** ($50-100M), **1 Apr 2030** (under $50M) |\n\n### 6.5 SB 243: companion chatbots\n\n- Signed 13 Oct 2025 (Chapter 677); in force 1 Jan 2026. A **companion chatbot** gives adaptive, human-like responses and can meet a user's social needs; customer-service bots, limited video-game characters and voice assistants that do not sustain a relationship are excluded.\n- **Duties:** a clear notice that the chatbot is AI where a reasonable person could think it is human; a protocol to prevent suicide and self-harm content that refers at-risk users to crisis services, published on the operator's website; for users known to be minors, an AI disclosure, a default reminder **at least every 3 hours** to take a break (and that the chatbot is not human), and reasonable measures to block sexually explicit visuals; a notice that companion chatbots may not suit some minors.\n- **Annual report** to the Office of Suicide Prevention from **1 Jul 2027** (crisis referrals and protocols, no personal data).\n- **Private right of action:** injunctive relief, the greater of actual damages or **$1,000 per violation**, and attorney fees. This is the main contrast with the AG-only chatbot and frontier laws.\n- On 10 Sep 2026 the Governor also signed **SB 1119** (further child-safety protections for companion chatbots, including parental controls and independent safety audits) and **SB 867** (companion-chatbot toys).\n\n---\n\n## 7. New York\n\n### 7.1 NYC Local Law 144: automated employment decision tools (AEDTs)\n\n- Enacted 11 Dec 2021; **in force 1 Jan 2023**; DCWP has enforced it and its rules since **5 Jul 2023** (NYC Admin. Code 20-870 to 20-874; 6 RCNY 5-300 et seq.).\n- **AEDT:** a computational process from machine learning, statistical modelling, data analytics or AI that issues a simplified output (score, classification, recommendation) used to substantially assist or replace discretionary hiring or promotion decisions. Under the rules, \"substantially assist or replace\" means relying solely on the output, weighting it more than any other criterion, or using it to overrule other factors, including human judgment.\n- **Bias audit:** an impartial evaluation by an **independent auditor** (not involved in using or developing the tool, with no employment relationship or financial interest), conducted **no more than one year before use**. It must calculate **selection rates** (or scoring rates) and **impact ratios** for sex, race/ethnicity and **intersectional** categories, and report how many people fell in unknown categories. The auditor may exclude a category that is under 2% of the data. Historical data is required, with test data allowed if historical data is insufficient.\n- **Impact ratio:** a category's selection rate divided by the rate of the most-selected category (or scoring rate divided by the highest-scoring category's rate). The law sets **no pass/fail threshold**; the four-fifths rule of thumb is not part of it.\n- **Publication:** the date and summary of the latest audit and the tool's distribution date go on the employment page of the website before use, and stay up for at least 6 months after last use.\n- **Notice:** to candidates and employees **who reside in NYC**, at least **10 business days** before use (website, job posting, or mail or email), stating that an AEDT will be used and which qualifications and characteristics it assesses, and explaining how to request an alternative selection process or accommodation. The rules make clear the employer is not required to provide an alternative. Data type, source and retention information must be provided within **30 days** of a written request if not posted.\n- **Penalties:** up to $500 for a first violation (and others the same day), then $500-1,500 each; every day of non-compliant use and every missed notice is a separate violation. The law creates no private right of action but preserves existing civil actions and the city Human Rights Commission's powers.\n- **Enforcement record:** a 2 Dec 2025 audit by the NY State Comptroller found DCWP's enforcement ineffective: DCWP found 1 issue across 32 companies where the auditors found at least 17 potential violations, and it received only 2 complaints between July 2023 and June 2025.\n\n### 7.2 RAISE Act (Responsible AI Safety and Education Act)\n\n- Signed 19 Dec 2025 (S6953-B/A6453-B). A chapter amendment, **S8828 (Chapter 96, signed 27 Mar 2026)**, repealed that text and re-enacted General Business Law article 44-B in a form closely modelled on California's SB 53. **Effective 1 Jan 2027.**\n- **Same thresholds as SB 53:** frontier model above **10^26** operations; large frontier developer above **$500M** revenue.\n- **Large frontier developers:** publish a frontier AI framework, and file a disclosure statement with a new **office within the Department of Financial Services**, renewed every two years or on material change, paying a share of the office's costs.\n- **All frontier developers:** publish a transparency report before or when deploying a new or substantially modified frontier model (large developers add risk-assessment summaries); report critical safety incidents to the DFS office **within 72 hours** of determining (or reasonably believing) one occurred, or within **24 hours** to an appropriate authority if there is imminent risk of death or serious injury.\n- **Enforcement:** AG civil actions for up to **$1M for a first violation and $3M for later ones**; **$1,000 per day** for disclosure-statement failures; **no private right of action** and no cure period. The office may designate equivalent federal standards as a compliance route. Unlike SB 53 and SB 315, the re-enacted text has no whistleblower section.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Illinois\n\n### 8.1 HB 3773: AI in employment (Human Rights Act 2-102(L))\n\n- Public Act 103-0804; **effective 1 Jan 2026**. It is a civil rights violation for an employer:\n  1. to use AI that **has the effect** of subjecting employees to discrimination on a protected basis in recruitment, hiring, promotion, renewal, training or apprenticeship selection, discharge, discipline, tenure or terms of employment;\n  2. to use **zip codes as a proxy** for protected classes; or\n  3. to fail to **notify** employees that it uses AI for these purposes.\n- \"AI\" follows the OECD-style definition and **expressly includes generative AI**. The Department of Human Rights must adopt rules on when, how and how quickly notice is given.\n- **Contrast:** this is an **effect** (disparate impact) standard, the opposite of Texas's intent requirement.\n\n### 8.2 Artificial Intelligence Video Interview Act (820 ILCS 42)\n\n- **Effective 1 Jan 2020** (Public Act 101-0260). Before asking for video interviews for Illinois-based positions, an employer using AI analysis must **notify** applicants that AI may be used, **explain** how it works and what general characteristics it evaluates, and obtain **consent**; applicants who do not consent may not be evaluated by AI.\n- Videos may be shared only with people whose expertise is needed to evaluate the applicant, and must be **deleted within 30 days** of an applicant's request (including backups held by others).\n- **From 1 Jan 2022** (Public Act 102-0047): an employer that relies **solely** on AI to decide who gets an in-person interview must report applicants' race and ethnicity data to the Department of Commerce and Economic Opportunity (DCEO) each year by 31 Dec; DCEO reports on any racial bias by 1 Jul.\n- The Act has no penalty or enforcement section.\n\n### 8.3 Biometric Information Privacy Act (BIPA, 740 ILCS 14) as applied to AI\n\n- Covers **biometric identifiers** (retina or iris scans, fingerprints, voiceprints, scans of hand or face geometry) and information derived from them. Facial-recognition, face-geometry and voice-cloning systems that capture these data are squarely in scope.\n- **Duties:** a public written policy with a retention schedule, destroying data when the purpose is met or within **3 years** of the person's last interaction, whichever comes first; written notice of the purpose and duration plus a **written release before collection**; **no selling, leasing or otherwise profiting**; disclosure only with consent or legal compulsion; reasonable industry-standard security.\n- **Private right of action:** liquidated damages of **$1,000 per negligent** or **$5,000 per intentional or reckless** violation (or actual damages if greater), plus fees and injunctions.\n- **2024 amendment (Public Act 103-0769, effective 2 Aug 2024):** repeated collection of the same identifier from the same person by the same method is **one violation with at most one recovery**; an electronic signature counts as a written release.\n\n### 8.4 SB 315: Artificial Intelligence Safety Measures Act\n\n- Public Act 104-0538, signed 6 Jul 2026, **effective 1 Jan 2027**. Same **10^26** and **$500M** thresholds as California and New York.\n- **From 1 Jan 2027:** large frontier developers need a current **disclosure statement** filed with IEMA-OHS (renewed annually) to develop, deploy or operate frontier models in Illinois; $1,000 per day for failures.\n- **From 1 Jan 2028:** large frontier developers publish and follow a **frontier AI framework** and must **annually retain an independent third party to audit compliance** (from 1 Jan 2028 or 90 days after first qualifying, whichever is later), publishing a summary within 30 days of receiving the report. **Neither SB 53 nor the RAISE Act requires this.**\n- **Incidents:** report to IEMA-OHS and the AG **within 72 hours** of learning facts that establish a reasonable belief an incident occurred; **24 hours** to an appropriate authority if there is imminent danger.\n- **Enforcement:** AG only, up to **$1M first / $3M later**; **no private right of action**; whistleblower protections for covered employees; regulation of frontier models is reserved to the state (no home-rule ordinances).\n\n---\n\n## 9. Federal AI-Specific Measures\n\n### 9.1 TAKE IT DOWN Act (Pub. L. 119-12, S. 146)\n\n- Signed **19 May 2025**. Its name stands for Tools to Address Known Exploitation by Immobilizing Technological Deepfakes on Websites and Networks.\n- **Criminal offence (47 U.S.C. 223(h)):** knowingly publishing, through an interactive computer service, a nonconsensual intimate visual depiction, authentic or a **\"digital forgery\"** made with software, machine learning or AI that a reasonable person could not tell from a real one. For adults, the depiction must not have been voluntarily exposed or be a matter of public concern, and publication must be intended to cause harm or cause it. Up to **2 years' prison, or 3 years where the victim is a minor**; threats are punishable too. **Consent to creating an image is not consent to publishing it.**\n- **Platform duties (section 3):** a **covered platform** is a public website or app that primarily hosts user-generated content, or regularly publishes nonconsensual intimate images. **Excluded:** broadband providers, email, and sites of mainly preselected content. By **19 May 2026** each had to set up a notice-and-removal process and post a plain-language notice of it. On a valid request it must remove the image **within 48 hours** and make reasonable efforts to remove **known identical copies**. Good-faith removals are protected from liability.\n- **Enforcement:** a failure to comply is treated as a violation of an FTC rule on unfair or deceptive practices, so FTC Act penalties apply. The **FTC can also pursue nonprofits**, which the FTC Act normally does not reach. The FTC has been enforcing since **19 May 2026**.\n\n### 9.2 Executive Order 14365 and the DOJ AI Litigation Task Force\n\n- **EO 14365, \"Ensuring a National Policy Framework for Artificial Intelligence\"** (signed 11 Dec 2025; 90 FR 58499). It sets a policy of a \"minimally burdensome national policy framework for AI\" and directs:\n  - a **DOJ AI Litigation Task Force** within 30 days, whose sole job is to challenge state AI laws (interstate commerce, preemption by existing federal regulations, other unlawfulness);\n  - a **Commerce evaluation** of \"onerous\" state AI laws within 90 days, including laws requiring altered truthful outputs or compelled disclosures that may breach the First Amendment;\n  - **funding levers:** Broadband Equity Access and Deployment (BEAD) non-deployment funds withheld from states with onerous AI laws, to the extent federal law allows, and a review of discretionary grants;\n  - an **FCC proceeding** on a federal AI reporting and disclosure standard that would preempt conflicting state rules, and an **FTC policy statement** on when state laws requiring altered truthful outputs are preempted by the FTC Act;\n  - a **legislative recommendation** for a preemptive federal framework that would not preempt state child-safety, data-centre infrastructure or state-procurement laws.\n- **An executive order cannot preempt state law by itself.** EO 14365 works through lawsuits, funding conditions, agency proceedings and a request to Congress, and it must be implemented \"consistent with applicable law\". Until a court rules or Congress (or an agency acting within its statutory power) acts, state AI laws remain in force.\n- **The Task Force:** created by the Attorney General's memorandum of **9 Jan 2026**. The Attorney General (or a designee) chairs it and the Associate Attorney General is vice chair, with the Deputy AG's office, the Solicitor General's office and the Civil Division. In April 2026 DOJ moved to intervene in xAI's challenge to Colorado SB 24-205.\n\n### 9.3 No federal moratorium or preemption statute\n\n- On **1 Jul 2025** the Senate adopted the Blackburn amendment (S.Amdt. 2814, roll-call vote 363) **99-1** to strike the AI moratorium section from H.R. 1, the budget reconciliation bill.\n- As of September 2026 **no federal statute bars or preempts state AI laws**; EO 14365 itself asks Congress to create one. Private-sector organisations must comply with the state laws that apply to them.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Recurring Patterns\n\n### 10.1 AG-only enforcement with cure periods\n\n| Law | Who enforces | Cure before suit | Private right of action |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Colorado SB 26-189 | AG exclusively | 60 days after notice, if curable; not for knowing or repeated violations; cure clause repealed 1 Jan 2030 | No |\n| Texas TRAIGA | AG exclusively | 60 days after written notice; a cure plus written statement bars suit | No |\n| California SB 53 | AG only | None in the statute | No |\n| New York RAISE | AG | None in the statute | No |\n| Illinois SB 315 | AG exclusively | None in the statute | No |\n| California SB 942 | AG, city attorneys, county counsel | None | No |\n| NYC LL 144 | DCWP / Corporation Counsel | None | No (existing claims preserved) |\n| Utah | Division of Consumer Protection | None in the disclosure chapter | No |\n\nCure periods are a political compromise: they give businesses time to fix good-faith errors in new, untested duties. They usually lapse for knowing or repeated violations, and Colorado's has a sunset date.\n\n### 10.2 Intent versus effect\n\n- **Texas (intent):** TRAIGA bans AI built or used *with intent* to discriminate unlawfully, and says disparate impact alone is not enough.\n- **Illinois (effect):** HB 3773 bans employer AI that *has the effect* of discriminating, plus zip-code proxies.\n- **Colorado (neither, by design):** SB 26-189 regulates process (notice, explanation, correction, review) and leaves discrimination liability to existing anti-discrimination law, split between developer and deployer by fault.\n- **NYC:** measures disparate impact through impact ratios but sets no threshold, so disclosure without a pass/fail test.\n- Federal disparate-impact theories under Title VII and the Fair Housing Act are covered in [[Sectoral and Civil Laws]].\n\n### 10.3 The frontier-model trio\n\n| Feature | California SB 53 | New York RAISE | Illinois SB 315 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Thresholds | Over 10^26 operations; large means over $500M | Same | Same |\n| Framework | Large developers; yearly review | Large developers | Large developers, from 2028 |\n| Transparency report | Every frontier developer, at deployment | Every frontier developer, at deployment | Every frontier developer, at deployment |\n| Incident deadline | **15 days** (24h if imminent) to Cal OES | **72h** (24h) to DFS office | **72h** (24h) to IEMA-OHS and AG |\n| Third-party audit | Not required | Not required | **Annual, from 2028** |\n| Regulator filings | Internal-use risk summaries every 3 months | Disclosure statement every 2 years | Annual disclosure statement from 2027 |\n| Penalties | Up to $1M per violation | $1M first / $3M later | $1M first / $3M later |\n| Effective | 1 Jan 2026 | 1 Jan 2027 | 1 Jan 2027 |\n\nAll three enforce through the AG, create no private right of action, give no cure period, and allow compliance through designated equivalent federal standards. California and Illinois add whistleblower protections; New York's re-enacted text does not. Compare the EU AI Act's presumption of systemic risk for GPAI models above **10^25** FLOP: the US laws set their bar ten times higher.\n\n### 10.4 Disclosure-and-explanation versus risk-management models\n\n| Model | What it requires | Examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Disclosure and explanation** | Tell people AI is used; explain adverse decisions; let them correct data, appeal or opt out | Colorado SB 26-189, CPPA ADMT rules, NYC LL 144 notices, Illinois HB 3773 notice, Utah, the chatbot laws |\n| **Risk management** | Assess, document and mitigate risk before and during use; frameworks, audits, incident reporting | Colorado SB 24-205 (never in force), SB 53, RAISE, SB 315, CPPA risk assessments and cybersecurity audits |\n| **Conduct bans** | Prohibit specific intentional misuses | TRAIGA, TAKE IT DOWN Act |\n| **Provenance and training transparency** | Label synthetic content; publish training-data summaries | SB 942 / AB 853, AB 2013 |\n\nThe 2026 trend moved **decision laws toward disclosure** (Colorado's rewrite dropped impact assessments) and **frontier laws toward risk management** (the three frontier statutes).\n\n### 10.5 Who can sue?\n\n- **Individuals can sue:** Illinois **BIPA** ($1,000 / $5,000 per violation) and California **SB 243** (greater of actual damages or $1,000 per violation).\n- **Regulator-only:** Colorado SB 26-189, Texas TRAIGA, SB 53, RAISE, SB 315, SB 942, NYC LL 144, Utah, and the TAKE IT DOWN platform duties (FTC).\n\n---\n\n## 11. Key Dates\n\n| Date | Event |\n|---|---|\n| 1 Jan 2020 | Illinois AI Video Interview Act in force (demographic reporting from 1 Jan 2022) |\n| 1 Jan 2023 / 5 Jul 2023 | NYC Local Law 144 in force / DCWP enforcement begins |\n| 1 May 2024 | Utah AI Policy Act in force |\n| 2 Aug 2024 | BIPA amendment: one recovery per person per collection method |\n| 7 May 2025 | Utah SB 226, SB 332 and HB 452 in force; Policy Act sunset moved to 1 Jul 2027 |\n| 19 May 2025 | TAKE IT DOWN Act signed; criminal provisions apply |\n| 1 Jul 2025 | Senate strips the AI moratorium from H.R. 1, 99-1 |\n| 11 Dec 2025 | EO 14365 signed |\n| 1 Jan 2026 | TRAIGA, SB 53, SB 243, Illinois HB 3773 and CPPA regulations in force; AB 2013 documentation due |\n| 9 Jan 2026 | DOJ AI Litigation Task Force created |\n| 14 May 2026 | Colorado SB 26-189 signed; SB 24-205 repealed before taking effect |\n| 19 May 2026 | TAKE IT DOWN platform duties apply; FTC enforcement begins |\n| 2 Aug 2026 | California AI Transparency Act (SB 942) operative |\n| 1 Jan 2027 | Colorado SB 26-189 and HB 26-1263 duties; RAISE Act; Illinois SB 315; CPPA ADMT compliance; SB 942 platform duties |\n| 1 Jul 2027 | Utah AI Policy Act sunset (unless extended); first chatbot reports (California and Colorado) |\n| 1 Jan 2028 | Illinois SB 315 frameworks and annual audits; SB 942 capture-device duties |\n| 1 Apr 2028 | First CPPA risk-assessment attestations; first cybersecurity audits (revenue over $100M) |\n| 1 Jan 2030 | Colorado SB 26-189 cure clause repealed |\n\n---\n\n## 12. Comparison with the EU AI Act\n\n| Dimension | EU AI Act | US approach |\n|---|---|---|\n| Structure | One horizontal regulation with risk tiers | Patchwork of state, city and targeted federal laws |\n| Prohibited practices | Art. 5 list (social scoring, manipulation and others) | Narrow intent-based bans (TRAIGA; social scoring only for Texas government) |\n| High-risk / consequential decisions | Conformity assessment, risk management, data governance, human oversight | Mostly notice, explanation, correction and appeal (Colorado, CPPA); bias audits for hiring (NYC) |\n| Frontier / GPAI | Systemic-risk presumption above 10^25 FLOP; AI Office oversight | 10^26 operations plus $500M revenue; state AG enforcement |\n| Transparency | Art. 50 disclosure and machine-readable marking | Utah and chatbot disclosures; SB 942 provenance; AB 2013 training data |\n| Penalties | Up to EUR 35M or 7% of global turnover | Mostly per-violation civil penalties ($500 to $1M-$3M) |\n| Private enforcement | Mainly regulators | Mostly AG-only; BIPA and SB 243 allow private suits |\n\nSee [[The EU AI Act]] and [[EU AI Act 2024 — Key Provisions]].\n\n---\n\n## Exam Angles\n\n- **Colorado trap:** SB 24-205 never took effect. The live law is SB 26-189: notice, a 30-day post-adverse explanation, correction and human review, with no impact assessments or duty of care, AG-only enforcement and a 60-day cure until 2030. Operative for decisions from 1 Jan 2027.\n- **Intent vs effect:** Texas TRAIGA requires intent to discriminate (disparate impact alone is not enough); Illinois HB 3773 catches AI with a discriminatory effect and zip-code proxies.\n- **Who must disclose under TRAIGA?** Government agencies (and health-care providers for treatment), not private businesses in general. The private-sector rules are intent-based bans.\n- **TRAIGA defences:** red-team or adversarial testing, feedback, or an internal review while substantially complying with the NIST AI RMF Generative AI Profile; 60-day cure; three penalty tiers.\n- **Frontier trio:** all use 10^26 operations and $500M revenue. The incident deadline is 15 days in California and 72 hours in New York and Illinois, with 24 hours everywhere for imminent danger. Only Illinois requires annual third-party audits (from 2028).\n- **NYC LL 144 mechanics:** independent bias audit no more than a year before use, public summary, 10 business days' notice to NYC residents, impact ratios by sex, race/ethnicity and intersections, and no pass/fail threshold. The four-fifths rule is not in the law.\n- **California stack:** AB 2013 (training-data summary), SB 942 (1M users; free detection tool; latent label required, visible label optional; $5,000 per day; operative 2 Aug 2026 as amended), CPPA ADMT rules (pre-use notice, opt-out unless a human appeal is offered, access; compliance 1 Jan 2027; filings from 1 Apr 2028).\n- **Private rights of action:** BIPA and SB 243 only. Everything else here is regulator-enforced.\n- **Illinois employment pair:** the Video Interview Act (notice, explanation, consent; 30-day deletion; demographic reports if AI alone decides) versus HB 3773 (effect standard plus notice).\n- **Federal picture:** TAKE IT DOWN (48-hour removal; FTC enforcement from 19 May 2026, including nonprofits; AI digital forgeries are criminal). EO 14365 cannot preempt by itself, and no federal moratorium has been enacted.\n\n## Key Terms\n\n- [[Colorado ADMT Act (SB 26-189)]]\n- [[Texas Responsible AI Governance Act (TRAIGA)]]\n- [[Utah AI Policy Act]]\n- [[California TFAIA (SB 53)]]\n- [[Large Frontier Developer]]\n- [[California AI Transparency Act (SB 942)]]\n- [[CCPA ADMT Regulations]]\n- [[NYC Local Law 144]]\n- [[Bias Audit (NYC LL 144)]]\n- [[RAISE Act]]\n- [[Illinois HB 3773]]\n- [[TAKE IT DOWN Act]]\n\n## Sources\n\n- Colorado SB 26-189 bill page and session law (Ch. 131, 2026): https://leg.colorado.gov/bills/sb26-189 ; https://leg.colorado.gov/laws/session-laws/SB26-189/131/download\n- Colorado HB 26-1263 bill page and session law (Ch. 208, 2026): https://leg.colorado.gov/bills/hb26-1263 ; https://leg.colorado.gov/laws/session-laws/HB26-1263/208/download\n- Colorado SB 24-205 and SB 25B-004: https://leg.colorado.gov/bills/sb24-205 ; https://leg.colorado.gov/bills/sb25b-004\n- DOJ intervention in xAI v. Colorado (24 Apr 2026): https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-intervenes-xai-lawsuit-challenging-colorados-algorithmic-discrimination\n- Texas HB 149 enrolled text and history: https://capitol.texas.gov/tlodocs/89R/billtext/pdf/HB00149F.pdf ; https://capitol.texas.gov/BillLookup/History.aspx?LegSess=89R&Bill=HB149\n- Utah SB 149 (2024), SB 226, SB 332, HB 452 (2025), HB 320 (2026): https://le.utah.gov/~2024/bills/sbillenr/SB0149.pdf ; https://le.utah.gov/Session/2025/bills/enrolled/SB0226.pdf ; https://le.utah.gov/Session/2025/bills/enrolled/SB0332.pdf ; https://le.utah.gov/Session/2025/bills/enrolled/HB0452.pdf ; https://le.utah.gov/Session/2026/bills/enrolled/HB0320.pdf\n- California SB 53: https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billTextClient.xhtml?bill_id=202520260SB53\n- California AB 2013: https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billTextClient.xhtml?bill_id=202320240AB2013\n- California SB 942 and AB 853: https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billTextClient.xhtml?bill_id=202320240SB942 ; https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billTextClient.xhtml?bill_id=202520260AB853\n- CPPA regulations (announcement, rulemaking page, approved text): https://cppa.ca.gov/announcements/2025/20250923.html ; https://cppa.ca.gov/regulations/ccpa_updates.html ; https://cppa.ca.gov/regulations/pdf/ccpa_updates_cyber_risk_admt_appr_text.pdf\n- California SB 243: https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billTextClient.xhtml?bill_id=202520260SB243\n- Governor of California, 10 Sep 2026 signings: https://www.gov.ca.gov/2026/09/10/governor-newsom-signs-the-strongest-child-safety-chatbot-and-social-media-laws-in-the-nation/\n- NYC Local Law 144 (Int. 1894-A) and DCWP rules: https://legistar.council.nyc.gov/LegislationDetail.aspx?ID=4344524&GUID=B051915D-A9AC-451E-81F8-6596032FA3F9 ; https://rules.cityofnewyork.us/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/DCWP-NOA-for-Use-of-Automated-Employment-Decisionmaking-Tools-2.pdf ; https://www.nyc.gov/site/dca/about/automated-employment-decision-tools.page\n- NYS Comptroller audit of LL 144 enforcement: https://www.osc.ny.gov/state-agencies/audits/2025/12/02/enforcement-local-law-144-automated-employment-decision-tools\n- New York RAISE Act: https://www.governor.ny.gov/news/governor-hochul-signs-nation-leading-legislation-require-ai-frameworks-ai-frontier-models ; https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/bills/2025/S8828\n- Illinois Public Acts 103-0804, 101-0260, 102-0047, 103-0769, 104-0538: https://www.ilga.gov/Documents/Legislation/PublicActs/103/PDF/103-0804.pdf ; https://www.ilga.gov/Documents/Legislation/PublicActs/101/PDF/101-0260.pdf ; https://www.ilga.gov/Documents/Legislation/PublicActs/102/PDF/102-0047.pdf ; https://www.ilga.gov/Documents/Legislation/PublicActs/103/PDF/103-0769.pdf ; https://www.ilga.gov/Documents/Legislation/PublicActs/104/PDF/104-0538.pdf\n- Illinois compiled statutes (AIVIA, BIPA) and SB 315 status: https://www.ilga.gov/Legislation/ILCS/Articles?ActID=4015&ChapterID=68 ; https://www.ilga.gov/Legislation/ILCS/Articles?ActID=3004&ChapterID=57 ; https://www.ilga.gov/Legislation/BillStatus?DocTypeID=SB&DocNum=315&GAID=18&SessionID=114\n- TAKE IT DOWN Act, Pub. L. 119-12: https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/PLAW-119publ12/html/PLAW-119publ12.htm ; FTC enforcement: https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2026/05/ftc-begins-enforcing-take-it-down-act\n- EO 14365 (90 FR 58499): https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-16/pdf/2025-23092.pdf\n- DOJ AI Litigation Task Force memorandum (9 Jan 2026): https://www.justice.gov/ag/media/1422986/dl?inline\n- Senate roll-call vote 363 (1 Jul 2025): https://www.senate.gov/legislative/LIS/roll_call_votes/vote1191/vote_119_1_00363.htm\n\n## Status as of September 2026\n\nThis note reflects the law as checked on 23 September 2026, and several items are moving:\n\n- **Colorado:** the AG must adopt SB 26-189 rules by 1 Jan 2027; their final content may refine the post-adverse-outcome disclosures and the meaning of \"materially influence\".\n- **California:** the Governor has until 30 Sep 2026 to act on bills passed in the 2026 session, so further AI laws may be signed or vetoed after this note. SB 1119 and SB 867 were signed on 10 Sep 2026; their details are not covered here.\n- **Federal:** EO 14365's follow-through (the Commerce list of state laws, BEAD conditions, FCC and FTC actions) and any DOJ suits can change the practical risk of enforcing a state law, but none of them preempts state law by itself. Congress could still enact a preemption statute.\n- **Illinois:** HB 3773 directs the Department of Human Rights to set the timing and means of AI-use notices by rule; check for final rules before relying on a specific notice format.\n- **Future dates** (2027-2030) are as enacted and could be amended before they arrive. When an exam item turns on a date, check whether it is anchored to the original or the amended text.", "ch7": "## At a Glance\n\nThis knowledge area tours the voluntary standards and frameworks that turn principles into practice: the OECD AI Principles (the intergovernmental values baseline, with its classification framework, AI-system definition and incident definitions), NIST's AI RMF with its Govern-Map-Measure-Manage cycle and the Playbook's per-subcategory suggested actions, the Generative AI Profile, and the core ISO standards BOK v2.1 names - 22989 (terminology), 42001 (certifiable AI management system) and 42005 (AI system impact assessment). None are law, but regulators, auditors and contracts increasingly treat them as the de facto measure of due care.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nII.D carries 3 to 5 questions (down from 4 to 6 in v2.0.1). It is instrument matching: a function is described and you name the framework. BOK v2.1 lists exactly three core ISO standards (22989, 42001, 42005), dropped NIST ARIA, and reaches into the OECD's working tools as well as its principles.\n\n### OECD: the values baseline\n\nThe **OECD Recommendation on Artificial Intelligence** (OECD/LEGAL/0449), adopted **22 May 2019**, was the first intergovernmental AI standard. The OECD Council revised its **AI system definition on 8 November 2023** and the Recommendation more broadly on **3 May 2024**. It is not binding: adherents (47 as of September 2026, the EU among them) make a political commitment. Five values-based principles: inclusive growth, sustainable development and well-being; respect for the rule of law, human rights and democratic values, including fairness and privacy; transparency and explainability; robustness, security and safety; and accountability. Five recommendations for policy makers cover R&D investment, an inclusive AI ecosystem, interoperable governance, human capacity, and international co-operation.\n\nThe 2023 definition matters because the EU AI Act's Article 3(1) closely tracks it: a machine-based system that, for explicit or implicit objectives, infers from the input it receives how to generate outputs such as predictions, content, recommendations or decisions that can influence physical or virtual environments. The five edits were \"explicit or implicit\" objectives, inference from input, \"content\" added to outputs, \"physical\" replacing \"real\", and adaptiveness after deployment.\n\nThe OECD's tools go past the principles. The **Framework for the Classification of AI Systems** (February 2022) has five dimensions: People and Planet, Economic Context, Data and Input, AI Model, and Task and Output; a trap worth knowing is that **action autonomy** is a Task and Output criterion, not an AI Model one. The **incident and hazard definitions** (May 2024) separate an AI incident (realised harm to health, critical infrastructure, rights or property) from an AI hazard (an event that could plausibly lead to one, with near misses counting), with serious incidents, disasters and serious hazards as narrower tiers. The **AI Incidents and Hazards Monitor (AIM)** tracks publicly reported events, and the **Hiroshima AI Process Reporting Framework** (OECD-hosted since February 2025) lets organizations report against the G7 Code of Conduct in a common format.\n\n### NIST: the risk process\n\nThe **NIST AI Risk Management Framework 1.0** (January 2023) is voluntary, rights-preserving and sector-agnostic. It frames AI risk as contextual (the same system is high-risk in one deployment and low-risk in another), emergent, opaque and societal in scale. Four core functions:\n\n- **GOVERN** - the cross-cutting foundation: policies, roles and accountabilities, risk tolerance and appetite, and a risk-aware culture with escalation channels.\n- **MAP** - context and risk identification: intended purpose and foreseeable misuse, system design and data, stakeholders and affected populations, and prioritised risks including epistemic and aleatoric uncertainty.\n- **MEASURE** - ongoing evaluation: metrics, testing (unit, red team, stress), monitoring and logging, and documented reporting.\n- **MANAGE** - response: risk treatment (accept, transfer, avoid, mitigate), incident response and recovery, integration with cybersecurity, privacy and enterprise risk, and retirement planning.\n\nSeven trustworthiness characteristics run through it: valid and reliable; safe; secure and resilient; accountable and transparent; explainable and interpretable; privacy-enhanced; and fair with harmful bias managed. The **AI RMF Playbook** supplies suggested actions, documentation prompts and references for all 72 subcategories, and the **Generative AI Profile (NIST AI 600-1)** tailors the RMF to generative AI. NIST ARIA, an evaluation programme with model, red-team and field testing, was a II.D indicator in v2.0.1 but is background only under v2.1.\n\n### ISO: the core three\n\n**ISO/IEC 22989:2022** is the terminology standard: 110 or more defined concepts covering the AI lifecycle, ML paradigms, system roles (AI provider, manufacturer, customer, subject) and trustworthiness properties. It defines, and does not prescribe. **ISO/IEC 42001:2023** is the world's first certifiable AI management system standard, built on the Annex SL structure (context, leadership, planning, support, operation, performance evaluation, improvement) and the Plan-Do-Check-Act cycle, modelled on ISO 27001 and ISO 9001. Impact assessment is a requirement, not an option: clause 6.1.4 defines the process, clause 8.4 performs it, and Annex A objective A.5 adds controls. **ISO/IEC 42005:2025** is the guidance on how to do that assessment: a process (clause 5) and a record (clause 6) covering the system, its data, its deployment environment, interested parties, and both benefits and harms, kept under review and reassessed when intended use, the system or the legal context changes. It is guidance, not certifiable, and it feeds but does not replace a GDPR Article 35 DPIA or an EU AI Act Article 27 FRIA.\n\nTwo distractors: **ISO/IEC 42006:2025** sets requirements for the bodies that certify against 42001 (it regulates the certifiers, not the certified), and **ISO/IEC 23894:2023** is AI risk-management guidance extending ISO 31000. The split to remember: 23894 asks about risk to the organization; 42005 asks about impacts on people.\n\n### How they fit\n\nRead the stack as a progression: the OECD supplies values, the NIST AI RMF supplies a risk process, the Playbook supplies how-to actions, ISO/IEC 22989 supplies the vocabulary, ISO/IEC 42001 supplies the certifiable management system that holds it all, and ISO/IEC 42005 supplies the impact assessment of a specific system. A question asking which instrument to use for a given job is asking you to place it on that ladder.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\n3 to 5 questions under BOK v2.1. Match the instrument to its function: values to the OECD; risk process to the NIST AI RMF; how-to actions to the Playbook; vocabulary to ISO 22989; a certifiable management system to ISO 42001; impact assessment of a specific system to ISO 42005. Know the four RMF functions and the seven trustworthiness characteristics cold. Remember NIST ARIA was removed in v2.1, that the core ISO list is a closed set of three, and that 42005 is guidance rather than certifiable. The OECD's tools are fair game: the five classification dimensions, incident versus hazard, and which 2023 definition edit did what.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- OECD AI Principles (2019, revised 2023 and 2024): the first intergovernmental AI standard, non-binding, 47 adherents; the 2023 AI-system definition and the 2022 classification framework sit alongside\n- NIST AI RMF 1.0 (January 2023): voluntary and non-certifiable; Govern then Map, Measure, Manage; seven trustworthiness characteristics\n- NIST AI RMF Playbook: suggested actions, documentation prompts and references for all 72 subcategories, named in the BOK\n- ISO/IEC 42001: the certifiable AI management system (AIMS), Annex SL structure, PDCA, the audit and certification target\n- ISO/IEC 42005: guidance (not certifiable) for assessing a specific AI system's impacts on people and society, complementing 42001's impact-assessment requirement\n- ISO/IEC 22989 defines the shared vocabulary; 23894 (AI risk management) is background only in v2.1, as is NIST ARIA\n- OECD tools beyond the principles: the five-dimension classification framework, incident versus hazard definitions, and the Hiroshima reporting framework\n- Distinguish 42005 from 42006: 42005 is impact-assessment guidance; 42006 sets requirements for the bodies that certify against 42001\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://legalinstruments.oecd.org/en/instruments/OECD-LEGAL-0449\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OECD Recommendation on AI (OECD/LEGAL/0449)</a> · the five principles, the 2023 definition update and the May 2024 revision\n- <a href=\"https://oecd.ai/en/ai-principles\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OECD.AI: AI principles and tools</a> · 47 adherents, the classification framework and the incident and hazard definitions\n- <a href=\"https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI Risk Management Framework</a> · the Govern-Map-Measure-Manage cycle and the seven trustworthiness characteristics\n- <a href=\"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF Playbook</a> · suggested actions for all 72 subcategories, which the BOK names directly\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989 (IEC webstore, free)</a> · the AI vocabulary standard, free from the IEC webstore\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/90574\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 42001 (IEC webstore)</a> · the first certifiable AI management system standard\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/107659\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 42005 (IEC webstore)</a> · AI system impact assessment guidance, one of the BOK's core three\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] AI Standards and Frameworks\n\n## Performance Indicators\n\n*BOK v2.1 (effective 2 February 2026), II.D, paraphrased. Blueprint weight: 3–5 exam questions, down from 4–6 in v2.0.1.*\n\n- Know the **OECD's** trustworthy-AI principles, together with its related framework, policy work and recommended practices\n- Know the **NIST AI Risk Management Framework** and its **Playbook**, down to the core functions, categories and subcategories\n- Know the **core ISO AI standards**, which v2.1 lists as exactly three: **ISO/IEC 22989, 42001 and 42005**\n\n> **What changed from v2.0.1:** the NIST ARIA indicator was dropped (§3 is now background only), and ISO/IEC 42005 joined 22989 and 42001 as a core ISO standard. ISO/IEC 23894 is not named in either version, though 42005's Annex B maps to it (§4).\n\n---\n\n---\n\n> [!source] AI Standards and Frameworks\n\n## 4. ISO Standards\n\n> **The core three (BOK v2.1):** **ISO/IEC 22989** (the vocabulary), **ISO/IEC 42001** (the certifiable management system) and **ISO/IEC 42005** (impact assessment guidance). The blueprint's wording, \"i.e., 22989, 42001 and 42005\", is a closed list. Everything else below is supporting context.\n\n### Framework Comparison: Key ISO AI Standards\n\n| Standard | Type | Purpose | Certifiable? | Named in BOK v2.1? |\n|----------|------|---------|:---:|:---:|\n| **ISO/IEC 22989:2022** | Terminology | Defines core AI concepts and vocabulary | ✗ | ✓ core |\n| **ISO/IEC 42001:2023** | Management system (requirements) | Certifiable AI management system; requires AI system impact assessment | ✓ | ✓ core |\n| **ISO/IEC 42005:2025** | Guidance | How to perform and document AI system impact assessments | ✗ | ✓ core |\n| **ISO/IEC 42006:2025** | Requirements for certification bodies | Competence and process rules for bodies that certify against 42001 | n/a (applies to certifiers) | ✗ |\n| **ISO/IEC 23894:2023** | Risk management guidance | AI-specific risk management (extends ISO 31000) | ✗ | ✗ (42005 Annex B maps to it) |\n| **ISO/IEC 38507** | Governance | Strategic guidance for boards and executives on AI oversight | ✗ | ✗ |\n| **ISO/IEC TR 24028** | Technical report | Trustworthiness concepts and mechanisms | ✗ | ✗ |\n| **ISO/IEC 23053** | Framework | Framework for AI systems that use machine learning | ✗ | ✗ |\n| **ISO/IEC 5338** | Lifecycle | AI system lifecycle processes | ✗ | ✗ |\n\n### ISO/IEC 22989 — Concepts and Terminology\n\nFoundational vocabulary standard. **Defines, but does not prescribe.** Ensures consistent, interoperable terms across stakeholders.\n\n**Key Definitions:**\n\n| Term | Definition (Summarized) |\n|------|------------------------|\n| **AI** | Capability of a system to perform tasks requiring human intelligence |\n| **AI System** | Engineered system generating outputs (predictions, recommendations, decisions) |\n| **Machine Learning** | Methods allowing systems to improve performance based on data |\n| **Autonomy** | Ability to operate without ongoing human intervention |\n| **Transparency** | Degree to which internal workings and decision-making can be understood |\n| **Explainability** | Degree to which a human can understand and interpret AI output/behavior |\n| **Trustworthiness** | Ability to operate as intended while minimizing risk and promoting confidence |\n\n**AI Capabilities:** Perception, Reasoning, Learning, Interaction, Decision-making.\n\n**Lifecycle Stages (conceptual):** Business requirements → Data acquisition → Model development → Integration/testing → Deployment → Monitoring/maintenance → Retirement.\n\n> **Exam note:** 22989 provides the **lexicon**; 42001 provides the **operational governance**. They are \"hand-in-glove.\"\n\n### ISO/IEC 42001 — AI Management Systems\n\nPublished **December 2023** (first edition). As of September 2026 this is still the current edition: the IEC webstore lists no amendment or revision. **World's first certifiable management system** for AI. Modeled after ISO 27001 (ISMS) and ISO 9001 (QMS). Scalable — fits startups to multinationals.\n\n> **Impact assessment is a requirement, not an option.** Clause **6.1.4** (planning) has the organization define an AI system impact assessment process, and clause **8.4** (operation) has it perform those assessments at planned intervals or when significant changes are proposed. Normative Annex A objective **A.5**, \"Assessing impacts of AI systems\", adds controls on the process (A.5.2), its documentation (A.5.3), impacts on individuals or groups (A.5.4) and societal impacts (A.5.5). ISO/IEC 42005 is the companion guidance on *how*.\n\n**High-Level Structure (Annex SL):**\n\n| Clause | Focus |\n|--------|-------|\n| **Context of the organization** | Internal/external factors; applicable laws and standards |\n| **Leadership and commitment** | Top management tone, resources, ownership |\n| **Planning** | Risk assessment, objectives, trustworthiness goals |\n| **Support** | Resources, competence, awareness, documentation |\n| **Operation** | Design, acquisition, development, deployment processes |\n| **Performance evaluation** | Monitoring, audit, management review |\n| **Improvement** | Corrective actions, continual improvement |\n\n**Trustworthiness Dimensions (required considerations):**\n- Transparency and explainability\n- Fairness and non-discrimination\n- Robustness and resilience\n- Accountability and human oversight\n- Privacy and data governance\n\n**Implementation Approach:**\n1. **Readiness assessment** (gap analysis) — inventory AI systems, policies, roles, documentation\n2. **Integrate with existing systems** — ISO 27001, ISO 9001, ISO 27701 (shared risk registers, incident response, audit functions)\n3. **Develop AI-specific policies** — lifecycle management, ethical principles, bias detection/mitigation, monitoring, stakeholder engagement\n4. **Training and awareness** — technical staff understand governance obligations; business users escalate AI risks; leadership models ethical culture\n\n> **Beyond Compliance:** Certification is an indicator of **maturity**, not the end goal. Embedding 42001 as a living governance discipline builds more adaptable, resilient, trusted AI systems.\n\n### ISO/IEC 23894 — AI Risk Management\n\nComplements 42001 with a dedicated AI risk management framework. Aligns with **ISO 31000** (Enterprise Risk Management). Focus: risk management **of** AI (not using AI for risk management).\n\n> **Blueprint status:** not named in BOK v2.1 II.D, which lists only 22989, 42001 and 42005. It remains useful background, and ISO/IEC 42005's informative Annex B explains how impact findings feed a 23894-style risk process. The split to remember: **23894 asks about risk to the organization; 42005 asks about impacts on people.**\n\n### ISO/IEC 38507 — Governance Implications of AI\n\nPart of ISO 38500 series. Strategic guidance for **board members and executives** on AI oversight — roles, risk appetite, ethical alignment, accountability, transparency.\n\n### ISO/IEC TR 24028 — Trustworthiness in AI\n\nTechnical report (not certifiable). Introduces AI trustworthiness concepts and mechanisms (transparency, reliability, resilience, privacy, fairness).\n\n### ISO/IEC 42005 — AI System Impact Assessment (core standard)\n\nPublished **28 May 2025** (first edition, 39 pages) by ISO/IEC JTC 1/SC 42. **Guidance, not certifiable**, for any organization that **develops, provides or uses** AI, of any size. It covers how and when to assess the impacts an AI system and its foreseeable applications can have on **individuals, groups and societies**, what to document, and how to integrate the work with AI risk management and an AI management system. Full note: [[ISO 42005 — AI System Impact Assessment]].\n\n| Part | What it covers |\n|---|---|\n| **Clause 5: the process** | Documenting the process; integrating with other management processes; timing; scope; allocating responsibilities; thresholds for sensitive uses, restricted uses and impact scales; performing; analysing; recording and reporting; approval; monitoring and review |\n| **Clause 6: the record** | Scope; system information (description, capabilities, purpose, intended and unintended uses); data and data quality; algorithms and models; deployment environment (geography, languages, constraints); interested parties; actual and reasonably foreseeable benefits and harms, including failures and misuse; measures |\n| **Annexes (informative)** | **A** use with 42001 · **B** use with 23894 · **C** harms and benefits taxonomy · **D** aligning with other assessments · **E** example template |\n\n- **Lifecycle, not one-off.** Assess at design and before deployment, keep the assessment under review, and reassess when intended use, the system (data, model) or the legal and policy context changes significantly.\n- **Two-sided.** Benefits and harms both belong in the record, with measures for each.\n- **Relationship to law.** It can feed a GDPR Art. 35 DPIA or an EU AI Act Art. 27 FRIA but replaces neither. It is not a harmonised standard, so it gives no presumption of conformity under the AI Act.\n\n### ISO/IEC 42006:2025 — Certification Bodies for AI Management Systems\n\nPublished **7 July 2025**. Sets requirements, additional to **ISO/IEC 17021-1**, for bodies that audit and certify AI management systems against ISO/IEC 42001, so accreditation bodies can judge their competence and consistency. It regulates the **certifiers**, not the certified organization. Not named in the BOK, but a handy distractor: 42001 = the AIMS you certify; 42005 = impact assessment guidance; 42006 = rules for whoever issues the 42001 certificate.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n> [!source] AI Standards and Frameworks\n\n## 5. Other AI-Related Standards and Frameworks\n\n### Global Framework Comparison\n\n| Framework | Origin | Binding? | Core Focus |\n|-----------|--------|:---:|------------|\n| **EU HLEG Ethics Guidelines** | EU (2019) | ✗ | 7 requirements for trustworthy AI; influenced EU AI Act |\n| **G7 Hiroshima Process** | G7 (2023) | ✗ | Code of Conduct for advanced AI; risk lifecycle, transparency, content provenance |\n| **IEEE 7000 Series** | IEEE | ✗ | Ethically aligned design; transparency, privacy, bias, well-being standards |\n| **UNESCO AI Ethics Recommendation** | UN (2021) | ✗ | First global standard; human rights-centered; 194 member states |\n| **Singapore Model AI Framework** | IMDA (2024) | ✗ | Model-based, sector-neutral; accountability, testing, content provenance |\n| **WEF AI Governance Toolkit** | WEF | ✗ | Cross-sector governance; Presidio Recommendations; Gen AI toolkit |\n| **OECD Classification Framework** | OECD (2022) | ✗ | Five dimensions: People & Planet, Economic Context, Data & Input, AI Model, Task & Output (see §1) |\n\n### EU HLEG Ethics Guidelines (2019)\n\nPrecursor to the EU AI Act. **Seven requirements** for trustworthy AI:\n1. Human agency and oversight\n2. Technical robustness and safety\n3. Privacy and data governance\n4. Transparency\n5. Diversity, non-discrimination, and fairness\n6. Societal and environmental well-being\n7. Accountability\n\n**Four ethical principles:** Respect for human autonomy, Prevention of harm, Fairness, Explicability.\n\n### G7 Hiroshima Process (2023)\n\nResponse to generative AI evolution. **Guiding Principles and Code of Conduct** for advanced AI:\n- Identify, evaluate, mitigate risks across lifecycle\n- Publicly report capabilities, limitations, appropriate/inappropriate uses\n- Responsible information sharing and incident reporting\n- Implement robust security controls\n- Content authentication and provenance mechanisms\n- Appropriate data input measures for personal data and IP\n\n**Reporting:** since February 2025 the **OECD** hosts the voluntary **Hiroshima AI Process Reporting Framework**, where organizations report against the Code of Conduct in a common format (see §1).\n\n### IEEE 7000 Series — Ethically Aligned Design\n\n| Standard | Focus |\n|----------|-------|\n| **IEEE 7000** | Model process for ethical concerns in system design |\n| **IEEE 7001** | Transparency in autonomous systems |\n| **IEEE 7002** | Data privacy in AI systems |\n| **IEEE 7003** | Algorithmic bias considerations |\n| **IEEE 7010** | Well-being metrics for autonomous systems |\n\n> Ethics is treated as an **integral design consideration** — incorporated into requirements analysis, system modeling, and validation.\n\n### UNESCO Recommendation on Ethics of AI (2021)\n\nFirst **global standard** on AI ethics (194 member states). **Not binding**, but promotes:\n- Ethical impact assessments\n- Ethical governance and stewardship\n- Data policy, environmental sustainability\n- Gender equality, cultural diversity protection\n- Algorithmic transparency, health and social well-being\n\n> Particularly influential in the **global south** due to cultural inclusion and human rights alignment.\n\n### Singapore Model AI Governance Framework (2024)\n\nLeader in model-based, sector-neutral governance. Emphasizes: accountability, data quality, trusted development/deployment, incident reporting, testing/assurance, security, content provenance, safety/alignment of R&D, AI for public good.\n\n### ISO/IEC JTC 1/SC 42 — Standards Pipeline\n\nAI system impact assessment has moved from the roadmap to a published standard (**ISO/IEC 42005**, May 2025), and requirements for bodies certifying AI management systems followed (**ISO/IEC 42006**, July 2025). SC 42 continues to develop further AI standards.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n> [!source] AI Standards and Frameworks\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n1. **NIST AI RMF is built around four core functions: Govern, Map, Measure, and Manage.** Govern establishes accountability/oversight. Map identifies system context and risks. Measure evaluates performance and risk posture. Manage includes mitigation and incident response. Understanding each function and its supporting categories is essential.\n\n2. **ISO/IEC 22989 provides a standard AI governance lexicon.** Defines key terms (AI, ML, autonomy, transparency, explainability, trustworthiness). No operational guidance — that's found in ISO/IEC 42001.\n\n3. **ISO/IEC 42001 is the first certifiable AI management system standard.** Modeled after ISO 27001. Clauses: leadership, planning, risk management, performance evaluation, continual improvement. Certification demonstrates due diligence and regulatory alignment.\n\n4. **The OECD AI Principles establish an ethical foundation for international AI governance.** Adopted 22 May 2019, definition revised 8 Nov 2023, text revised 3 May 2024; 47 adherents including the EU. Five principles: inclusive growth, sustainable development and well-being; respect for the rule of law, human rights and democratic values, including fairness and privacy; transparency and explainability; robustness, security and safety; accountability. Non-binding. II.D also reaches the OECD's tools: the classification framework (five dimensions), incident vs hazard definitions, and the Hiroshima reporting framework.\n\n5. **NIST ARIA is background only.** It was dropped from the v2.1 blueprint. Recognize it as NIST's evaluation program (model testing, red teaming, field testing) and move on.\n\n6. **IEEE, UNESCO, G7, and EU HLEG have each contributed significant AI frameworks.** IEEE 7000 = ethically aligned design. UNESCO = human rights-centered AI. G7 Hiroshima = Code of Conduct, now with an OECD-run reporting framework. EU HLEG = 7 requirements that shaped the EU AI Act. All non-binding but shape regulatory priorities globally.\n\n7. **ISO/IEC 42005 completes the core three.** Guidance (not certifiable) for any organization that develops, provides or uses AI on assessing benefits and harms to individuals, groups and society across the lifecycle. 42001 requires the assessment (6.1.4, 8.4, Annex A.5); 42005 explains how; 23894 covers risk to the organization; 42006 covers the certifiers.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n> [!source] Framework Recall Cards — AIGP Study Kit\n\n## ISO/IEC standards\n\n*Know which does what — the distinction is the question.*\n\n### ISO/IEC 22989 vs 42001 — the distinction that gets tested\n\n**22989** — concepts and **terminology**. A shared lexicon (bias, drift, transparency, autonomy). Prescribes nothing about how to build or manage AI. It defines an AI system broadly and treats symbolic/knowledge-based (rule) approaches as AI. **Exam framing:** the study guide's line that AI involves **perception, reasoning, or learning**, distinguishing it from simple automation and fixed business rules, is best sourced to the EU AI Act (Recital 12 and the Commission's Feb 2025 definition guidelines) rather than to 22989. Use the distinction on the exam, but don't cite 22989 for it at work.\n**42001** — the **AI management system** standard. **Certifiable**, structured like ISO 27001 (Annex SL), so it integrates with existing ISMS/QMS. Leadership, planning, support, operation, evaluation, improvement.\n\n**Tell:** 'independent certification' or 'auditable management system' → 42001. 'Inconsistent vocabulary across teams/contracts' → 22989.\n\n### The three core ISO AI standards the BoK names — and the others\n\nThe Body of Knowledge names exactly three as core: **22989** (concepts and terminology), **42001** (certifiable AI management system), and **42005** (**AI system impact assessment**). If a question asks for guidance on conducting an impact assessment, the answer is **42005**.\n\nAlso worth recognizing: **23894** (AI risk management guidance), **5338** (AI lifecycle processes), **24028** (trustworthiness), **27001** (information security, which 42001 is built to sit alongside).\n\n> [!warning] **Correction from the official BoK.** Earlier versions of this deck led with 23894. The BoK v2.1 names **22989, 42001 and 42005** as the core trio — 42005 is the one to know cold.", "ref_oecd": "## At a Glance\n\nThe OECD Recommendation on Artificial Intelligence (OECD/LEGAL/0449), adopted 22 May 2019, was the first intergovernmental AI standard. The OECD Council revised its AI system definition on 8 November 2023 and the wider text on 3 May 2024. It sets five values-based principles and five recommendations for policy makers, and OECD.AI lists 47 adherents, the EU among them. Around it sit the OECD's working tools: the Framework for the Classification of AI Systems (2022), the incident and hazard definitions behind the AI Incidents and Hazards Monitor (2024), and the Hiroshima AI Process Reporting Framework (2025).\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nBOK v2.1 II.D covers the OECD's principles, framework, policies and recommended practices, so study the tools as well as the list. Know the five principles by their 2024 headings, the five edits of the 2023 definition, the five classification dimensions, incident vs hazard (near misses are hazards), and that the OECD runs the voluntary Hiroshima reporting framework. The Recommendation is non-binding; the May 2024 revision added information integrity, misuse, safe override and decommissioning, and environmental sustainability, and moved traceability into Accountability.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Five principles: inclusive growth, sustainable development and well-being; respect for the rule of law, human rights and democratic values, including fairness and privacy; transparency and explainability; robustness, security and safety; accountability\n- Five recommendations: invest in AI R&D; foster an inclusive AI-enabling ecosystem; shape an enabling interoperable governance and policy environment; build human capacity and prepare for labour market transformation; international co-operation\n- Dates: adopted 22 May 2019; AI system definition revised 8 Nov 2023; broader revision 3 May 2024. A Recommendation is a non-binding political commitment\n- 2023 definition: explicit or implicit objectives, inference from input, 'content' as an output, physical or virtual environments, adaptiveness after deployment; the EU AI Act's Art. 3(1) closely tracks it\n- Classification framework (Feb 2022): People & Planet, Economic Context, Data & Input, AI Model, Task & Output\n- Incident = realized harm; hazard = plausible future harm (near misses included); serious incidents and AI disasters are narrower tiers; AIM tracks news-reported events\n- Hiroshima AI Process Reporting Framework: OECD-hosted, voluntary reporting against the G7 Code of Conduct since February 2025\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] AI Standards and Frameworks\n\n## 1. OECD AI Principles and Related OECD Tools\n\nThe **OECD Recommendation on Artificial Intelligence** (OECD/LEGAL/0449) was adopted on **22 May 2019** as the first intergovernmental standard on AI. The OECD Council revised its **AI system definition on 8 November 2023** and revised the Recommendation more broadly on **3 May 2024**. As a Recommendation it is **not legally binding**: adherents make a political commitment to implement it. OECD.AI lists **47 adherents**, the EU among them (September 2026). At the June 2019 Osaka Summit, G20 leaders welcomed G20 AI Principles drawn from it. Full detail: [[OECD AI Principles — Structured Reference]].\n\n> **Why II.D says \"principles, framework, policies and recommended practices\":** the exam can reach past the five principles into the OECD's working tools below: the AI system definition, the classification framework, the incident and hazard definitions, and the Hiroshima reporting framework.\n\n### Five Values-Based Principles (2024 headings)\n\n| # | Principle | Core Focus |\n|---|-----------|------------|\n| 1.1 | **Inclusive growth, sustainable development and well-being** | Beneficial outcomes for people and the planet; augmenting human capabilities; reducing inequalities; environmental sustainability (explicit since 2024) |\n| 1.2 | **Respect for the rule of law, human rights and democratic values, including fairness and privacy** | Non-discrimination, dignity, autonomy, privacy and data protection, labour rights; addressing AI-amplified mis- and disinformation; safeguards against misuse. *2019 heading: \"Human-centred values and fairness\"* |\n| 1.3 | **Transparency and explainability** | Meaningful information on capabilities and limitations; awareness of AI interactions; plain-language information on factors and logic; information that lets adversely affected people challenge outputs |\n| 1.4 | **Robustness, security and safety** | Function appropriately under normal use, foreseeable use or misuse, and adverse conditions; mechanisms to override, repair or decommission safely; bolster information integrity |\n| 1.5 | **Accountability** | Role-based accountability; traceability of datasets, processes and decisions; systematic risk management and responsible business conduct across the lifecycle |\n\n### Five Recommendations for Policy Makers\n\n1. Investing in AI research and development\n2. Fostering an inclusive AI-enabling ecosystem\n3. Shaping an enabling interoperable governance and policy environment for AI\n4. Building human capacity and preparing for labour market transformation\n5. International co-operation for trustworthy AI\n\n### AI System Definition Update (8 November 2023)\n\nCurrent text: *\"a machine-based system that, for explicit or implicit objectives, infers, from the input it receives, how to generate outputs such as predictions, content, recommendations, or decisions that can influence physical or virtual environments.\"* A second sentence adds that systems vary in autonomy and adaptiveness after deployment.\n\n| Edit | Why |\n|---|---|\n| Objectives may be **explicit or implicit** (replacing \"a given set of human-defined objectives\") | Goals can be learned, e.g., through reinforcement learning from human feedback |\n| The system **infers from the input it receives** | Stresses the role of input, which may come from humans or machines |\n| **\"Content\"** added to the outputs | Makes clear generative AI is covered |\n| **\"Physical\"** replaces \"real\" | Clarity and alignment with other international processes |\n| **Adaptiveness after deployment** | Some systems keep evolving after design and deployment |\n\nThe EU AI Act's Art. 3(1) definition closely tracks this wording, and Recital 12 asks for close alignment with the work of international organisations. An explanatory memorandum (OECD AI Papers No. 8) followed in March 2024.\n\n### May 2024 Revision\n\n- New language on **information integrity**: mis- and disinformation amplified by AI, with respect for freedom of expression\n- Coverage of **uses outside the intended purpose, intentional misuse and unintentional misuse**\n- Clearer **transparency and responsible-disclosure** information for AI actors\n- **Safety:** systems that risk undue harm or behave undesirably can be overridden, repaired and/or decommissioned safely\n- **Responsible business conduct** across the lifecycle, including co-operation with suppliers and users\n- **Interoperable governance** across jurisdictions\n- An explicit reference to **environmental sustainability**\n- Some headings expanded, and the **traceability and risk-management text moved into Accountability** (1.5). There are still five principles\n\n### Framework for the Classification of AI Systems (February 2022)\n\nPublished as OECD Digital Economy Papers No. 323, this is a tool for policy makers, regulators and legislators to characterize an AI system in its context and link its features to policy concerns.\n\n| Dimension | Sample criteria |\n|---|---|\n| **People & Planet** | Users and their competence; impacted stakeholders; optionality and redress; human rights; well-being and environment; displacement potential |\n| **Economic Context** | Industrial sector; business function and model; criticality; breadth of deployment; technical maturity |\n| **Data & Input** | Detection and collection; provenance; dynamic nature; rights and identifiability; structure and format; scale; quality and appropriateness |\n| **AI Model** | Model type (symbolic, statistical, hybrid); generative or discriminative; how it is built and whether it evolves in the field; deterministic or probabilistic use; transparency and explainability |\n| **Task & Output** | Tasks (e.g., recognition, forecasting); composite systems; **action autonomy**; core application area; evaluation methods |\n\n- **Uses:** a common understanding of AI, registries and inventories, sector-specific frameworks, risk assessment (including a common approach to incident reporting) and risk management.\n- **\"In the lab\" vs \"in the field\":** before deployment, the Data & Input, AI Model and Task & Output dimensions apply (ex ante risk management); after deployment, all five apply (ex post).\n- **Trap:** action autonomy is a **Task & Output** criterion, not an AI Model one.\n\n### Hiroshima AI Process Reporting Framework (OECD, February 2025)\n\n- Implements the G7 **Hiroshima Process International Code of Conduct for Organisations Developing Advanced AI Systems**, which came out of Japan's 2023 G7 presidency.\n- **Hosted by the OECD**, which publishes the reports on OECD.AI. Participation is **voluntary** and the format is common, so reports are comparable.\n- First round (2025): **25** organizations reported, analysed in the OECD report *How are AI developers managing risks?*\n- **Version 2.0** opens it to a wider range of organizations involved in developing, deploying and providing advanced AI systems. Submissions received by **30 September 2026** feed the next analysis.\n\n### AI Incidents and Hazards (OECD definitions, May 2024)\n\n| Term | Meaning (OECD AI Papers No. 16) |\n|---|---|\n| **AI incident** | An event, circumstance or series of events where an AI system's development, use or malfunction directly or indirectly leads to harm: to health; to the management and operation of critical infrastructure; to human rights or legally protected rights (fundamental, labour, IP); or to property, communities or the environment |\n| **Serious AI incident** | The same, with serious harm, e.g., death, or serious and irreversible disruption of critical infrastructure |\n| **AI disaster** | A serious AI incident that disrupts the functioning of a community or society and may test or exceed its capacity to cope with its own resources |\n| **AI hazard** | An event that *could plausibly lead* to an AI incident. **Near misses count as hazards** |\n| **Serious AI hazard** | An event that could plausibly lead to a serious AI incident or AI disaster |\n\n- The **AI Incidents and Hazards Monitor (AIM)** on OECD.AI tracks incidents and hazards reported in the news, classifying articles automatically against these definitions. It captures only publicly reported events.\n- In February 2025 the OECD published a **common reporting framework for AI incidents** (OECD AI Papers No. 34) so reporting can be consistent across jurisdictions.\n\n> **Exam note:** The principles are the values; the OECD tools are how they get applied. Expect scenario questions on which classification dimension a criterion belongs to, whether an event is an incident or a hazard, and which 2023 definition edit does what.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n> [!source] OECD AI Principles — Structured Reference\n\n# OECD AI Principles — Structured Reference\n\n> **Full Title:** Recommendation of the Council on Artificial Intelligence\n> **Legal Instrument:** OECD/LEGAL/0449\n> **Status:** Non-binding recommendation (political commitment)\n> **Originally Adopted:** 22 May 2019\n> **Revised:** 8 November 2023 (AI system definition); 3 May 2024 (substantive updates)\n> **Adherents:** 47, the European Union among them (OECD.AI, September 2026)\n> **Blueprint:** BOK v2.1 II.D asks for the OECD's principles, framework, policies and recommended practices for trustworthy AI, so this note also covers the OECD's working tools: the classification framework, the definition update, incident and hazard definitions, and the Hiroshima reporting framework\n\n---\n\n## Overview\n\nThe **OECD Recommendation on Artificial Intelligence** is the **first intergovernmental standard on AI**. Adopted by the OECD Council at Ministerial level in May 2019, it was revised in 2023 and 2024 to reflect technological developments — including generative AI — and to strengthen implementation guidance.\n\nThe Recommendation has **two complementary sections**:\n1. **Five value-based principles** for responsible stewardship of trustworthy AI (applicable to all stakeholders)\n2. **Five national policy recommendations** for governments and international co-operation\n\n> The G20 Leaders welcomed the **G20 AI Principles** at the June 2019 Osaka Summit — these were drawn directly from the OECD Recommendation, giving the principles global reach beyond OECD membership.\n\n---\n\n## Key Definitions\n\n### AI System\n> *\"An AI system is a machine-based system that, for explicit or implicit objectives, infers, from the input it receives, how to generate outputs such as predictions, content, recommendations, or decisions that can influence physical or virtual environments. Different AI systems vary in their levels of autonomy and adaptiveness after deployment.\"*\n\n**2023 update clarified:** objectives may be explicit or implicit; inputs may come from humans or machines; outputs include \"content\" (capturing generative AI); \"physical\" replaced \"real\" for clarity; systems can continue to evolve after deployment.\n\n### AI System Lifecycle\nPhases — often iterative, not necessarily sequential:\n1. Plan and design\n2. Collect and process data\n3. Build model(s) and/or adapt existing model(s) to specific tasks\n4. Test, evaluate, verify and validate\n5. Make available for use / deploy\n6. Operate and monitor\n7. Retire / decommission\n\n### AI Actors\nOrganisations and individuals who play an active role in the AI system lifecycle, including those who **deploy or operate** AI.\n\n### AI Knowledge\nSkills and resources — data, code, algorithms, models, research, know-how, training programmes, governance, processes, best practices — required to understand and participate in the AI system lifecycle.\n\n### Stakeholders\nAll organisations and individuals involved in, or affected by, AI systems, directly or indirectly. AI actors are a subset of stakeholders.\n\n---\n\n## Section 1: The Five Principles for Trustworthy AI\n\n> The principles are **complementary** and should be considered as a **whole**.\n\n### 1.1 — Inclusive Growth, Sustainable Development and Well-Being\n\n> *\"Stakeholders should proactively engage in responsible stewardship of trustworthy AI in pursuit of beneficial outcomes for people and the planet.\"*\n\n| Element | Description |\n|---------|-------------|\n| **Human augmentation** | Augmenting human capabilities and enhancing creativity |\n| **Inclusion** | Advancing inclusion of underrepresented populations |\n| **Inequality reduction** | Reducing economic, social, gender, and other inequalities |\n| **Environmental sustainability** | Protecting natural environments *(explicit reference added in 2024 revision)* |\n| **Overall aim** | Invigorating inclusive growth, well-being, and sustainable development |\n\n### 1.2 — Respect for the Rule of Law, Human Rights and Democratic Values, Including Fairness and Privacy\n\n> *\"AI actors should respect the rule of law, human rights, democratic and human-centred values throughout the AI system lifecycle.\"*\n\n| Element | Description |\n|---------|-------------|\n| **Foundational values** | Non-discrimination and equality, freedom, dignity, autonomy of individuals, privacy and data protection, diversity, fairness, social justice, internationally recognised labour rights |\n| **Information integrity** | Addressing misinformation and disinformation amplified by AI, while respecting freedom of expression and other protected rights *(2024 addition)* |\n| **Safeguards and mechanisms** | Capacity for human agency and oversight; addressing risks from uses outside of intended purpose, intentional misuse, or unintentional misuse *(2024 addition)* |\n\n### 1.3 — Transparency and Explainability\n\n> *\"AI Actors should commit to transparency and responsible disclosure regarding AI systems.\"*\n\n**Four disclosure obligations** (provide meaningful information, appropriate to context, consistent with state of the art):\n\n| # | Obligation |\n|---|-----------|\n| **i** | Foster a **general understanding** of AI systems, including their capabilities and limitations |\n| **ii** | Make stakeholders **aware of their interactions** with AI systems, including in the workplace |\n| **iii** | Where feasible and useful, provide **plain and easy-to-understand information** on data sources/input, factors, processes and/or logic that led to the prediction, content, recommendation or decision — enabling those affected to understand the output |\n| **iv** | Provide information enabling those **adversely affected** by an AI system to **challenge its output** |\n\n### 1.4 — Robustness, Security and Safety\n\n> *\"AI systems should be robust, secure and safe throughout their entire lifecycle.\"*\n\n| Sub-principle | Description |\n|---------------|-------------|\n| **a) Normal, foreseeable, and adverse conditions** | AI systems should function appropriately and not pose unreasonable safety and/or security risks under normal use, foreseeable use or misuse, or other adverse conditions |\n| **b) Override, repair, decommission** | Mechanisms should be in place to ensure that if AI systems risk causing undue harm or exhibit undesired behaviour, they can be overridden, repaired, and/or decommissioned safely as needed *(2024 strengthening)* |\n| **c) Information integrity** | Where technically feasible, mechanisms should bolster information integrity while ensuring respect for freedom of expression *(2024 addition)* |\n\n### 1.5 — Accountability\n\n> *\"AI actors should be accountable for the proper functioning of AI systems and for the respect of the above principles.\"*\n\n| Sub-principle | Description |\n|---------------|-------------|\n| **a) Role-based accountability** | AI actors should be accountable based on their roles, the context, and consistent with the state of the art |\n| **b) Traceability** | Ensure traceability of datasets, processes, and decisions made during the AI system lifecycle — to enable analysis of outputs and responses to inquiry *(moved from Transparency in 2024 revision)* |\n| **c) Systematic risk management** | Apply a systematic risk management approach to each phase of the AI system lifecycle on an **ongoing basis**; adopt responsible business conduct via co-operation between AI actors, suppliers of AI knowledge/resources, AI system users, and other stakeholders *(2024 elaboration)* |\n| **Risk scope** | Risks include harmful bias, human rights (safety, security, privacy), labour rights, and intellectual property rights |\n\n---\n\n## Section 2: National Policies and International Co-operation\n\n> Consistent with the five principles. Special attention to **small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs)**.\n\n### 2.1 — Investing in AI Research and Development\n\n| Element | Description |\n|---------|-------------|\n| **Public & private investment** | Long-term public investment and encouragement of private investment in R&D and open science |\n| **Interdisciplinary focus** | Spur innovation in trustworthy AI focused on challenging technical issues AND AI-related social, legal, ethical implications and policy issues |\n| **Open-source tools & datasets** | Public and private investment in open-source tools and open datasets that are representative and respect privacy and data protection — to support R&D free of harmful bias and improve interoperability |\n\n### 2.2 — Fostering an Inclusive AI-Enabling Ecosystem\n\n| Element | Description |\n|---------|-------------|\n| **Digital ecosystem** | Foster development of, and access to, an inclusive, dynamic, sustainable, and interoperable digital ecosystem for trustworthy AI |\n| **Infrastructure components** | Data, AI technologies, computational and connectivity infrastructure, mechanisms for sharing AI knowledge |\n| **Data trusts** | Promote mechanisms such as data trusts to support safe, fair, legal, and ethical sharing of data |\n\n### 2.3 — Shaping an Enabling Interoperable Governance and Policy Environment for AI\n\n| Element | Description |\n|---------|-------------|\n| **Agile policy** | Promote an agile policy environment supporting the transition from R&D to deployment and operation |\n| **Experimentation** | Use experimentation (e.g., regulatory sandboxes) to provide controlled environments for testing and scaling AI systems |\n| **Outcome-based approaches** | Adopt approaches providing flexibility in achieving governance objectives |\n| **Interoperability** | Co-operate within and across jurisdictions to promote interoperable governance and policy environments *(2024 emphasis)* |\n| **Regulatory review** | Review and adapt policy and regulatory frameworks and assessment mechanisms to encourage innovation and competition for trustworthy AI |\n\n### 2.4 — Building Human Capacity and Preparing for Labour Market Transformation\n\n| Element | Description |\n|---------|-------------|\n| **Workforce preparation** | Work with stakeholders to prepare for transformation of the world of work and society; empower people to effectively use and interact with AI systems |\n| **Skills** | Equip people with necessary skills across the breadth of AI applications |\n| **Fair transition** | Ensure a fair transition for workers — training programmes along working life, support for those displaced (including social protection), access to new labour market opportunities |\n| **Responsible use at work** | Promote responsible use of AI at work — enhance worker safety, quality of jobs and public services, foster entrepreneurship and productivity, ensure benefits are broadly and fairly shared |\n\n### 2.5 — International Co-operation for Trustworthy AI\n\n| Element | Description |\n|---------|-------------|\n| **Active co-operation** | Governments (including developing countries) and stakeholders should actively co-operate to advance these principles and progress responsible stewardship |\n| **Knowledge sharing** | Work together in the OECD and other global/regional fora to foster sharing of AI knowledge |\n| **Multi-stakeholder initiatives** | Encourage international, cross-sectoral, and open multi-stakeholder initiatives to garner long-term expertise on AI |\n| **Global technical standards** | Promote development of multi-stakeholder, consensus-driven global technical standards for interoperable and trustworthy AI |\n| **Measurement** | Encourage development and government use of internationally comparable indicators to measure AI R&D and deployment, and to assess progress in implementation |\n\n---\n\n## 2024 Revision: Key Updates\n\n| Area | What Changed |\n|------|-------------|\n| **Information integrity** | New emphasis on addressing misinformation and disinformation, safeguarding information integrity in the context of generative AI |\n| **Misuse** | Explicit coverage of uses outside intended purpose, intentional misuse, and unintentional misuse |\n| **Transparency disclosure** | Clarified the specific information AI actors should provide about AI systems |\n| **Safety mechanisms** | Strengthened language: if AI systems risk undue harm or exhibit undesired behaviour, they must be overridable, repairable, and/or decommissionable safely |\n| **Responsible business conduct** | Emphasised throughout the AI system lifecycle, with co-operation among all AI actors, suppliers, users, and stakeholders |\n| **Interoperable governance** | Underscored need for jurisdictions to work together on interoperable governance, given the increase in AI policy initiatives worldwide |\n| **Environmental sustainability** | Explicit reference added — reflecting growing importance since 2019 |\n| **Traceability → Accountability** | Traceability and risk management text moved from elsewhere to the Accountability principle as the most appropriate home |\n| **Headings** | Some principle and recommendation headings were expanded for clarity. Principle 1.2, \"Human-centred values and fairness\" in 2019, became \"Respect for the rule of law, human rights and democratic values, including fairness and privacy\". There are still five principles |\n\n---\n\n## AI System Definition Update (8 November 2023)\n\n**Process.** The Digital Policy Committee approved a draft revised definition in a joint session with the Working Party on AI Governance (AIGO) on 16 October 2023, and the OECD Council adopted it on **8 November 2023**. An explanatory memorandum followed in March 2024 (OECD Artificial Intelligence Papers No. 8).\n\n| Element | 2019 definition (in substance) | November 2023 definition |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Objectives** | \"a given set of human-defined objectives\" | **Explicit or implicit** objectives |\n| **How outputs arise** | Not described | The system **infers, from the input it receives**, how to generate outputs; input may come from humans or machines |\n| **Outputs** | Predictions, recommendations or decisions | Predictions, **content**, recommendations or decisions |\n| **Environments** | Real or virtual | **Physical** or virtual |\n| **Autonomy and change** | Varying levels of autonomy | Varying autonomy **and adaptiveness after deployment** |\n\n**Why each edit was made**\n\n- **Explicit or implicit objectives:** goals may be programmed directly or learned, for example through reinforcement learning from human feedback.\n- **Inference from input:** underscores the role of input in how the system reaches its outputs.\n- **Content:** makes clear that generative AI systems are covered.\n- **Physical for real:** clarity and alignment with other international processes. Virtual environments are real too, so \"real\" was misleading.\n- **Adaptiveness after deployment:** some systems, such as recommender systems that adapt to user preferences, keep evolving once in use.\n\n**Alignment with the EU AI Act.** The update was timed to support alignment with policy processes in the EU and Japan. The AI Act's Art. 3(1) definition closely tracks the OECD wording, and Recital 12 calls for the notion of an AI system to be closely aligned with the work of international organisations.\n\n> **Exam trap:** each edit has one job. \"Content\" = generative AI; \"explicit or implicit\" = learned goals; \"physical\" = wording clarity, not a scope change; \"adaptiveness\" = post-deployment learning.\n\n---\n\n## OECD Framework for the Classification of AI Systems (February 2022)\n\nPublished as **OECD Digital Economy Papers No. 323**, the framework is a user-friendly tool for policy makers, regulators, legislators and others to characterize AI systems in specific contexts and to link their technical features to the policy concerns in the AI Principles. It grew out of the work of the OECD.AI Network of Experts (ONE AI).\n\n### The five dimensions and their criteria\n\n| Dimension | What it describes | Criteria |\n|---|---|---|\n| **People & Planet** | Who interacts with or is affected by the system, and how it bears on human rights, well-being and the environment | Users and their competence; impacted stakeholders; optionality and redress (can users opt out or challenge outputs?); human rights and democratic values; well-being, society and environment; displacement potential |\n| **Economic Context** | The sectoral and economic setting of an applied system | Industrial sector; business function and model (for-profit, non-profit, public service); criticality; breadth of deployment; technical maturity |\n| **Data & Input** | The data and expert input the model uses to represent its environment | Detection and collection (human, automated, both); provenance; dynamic or static; rights (proprietary, public, personal) and identifiability; structure and format; scale; quality and appropriateness |\n| **AI Model** | The computational representation of the environment | Information availability; model type (symbolic, statistical, hybrid); rights (open source or proprietary); generative or discriminative; single or multiple models; how it is built (rules, data, supervised or reinforcement learning); evolution in the field; central or federated learning; deterministic or probabilistic use; transparency and explainability |\n| **Task & Output** | What the system does and the actions that follow | Tasks (recognition, event detection, forecasting and so on); composite systems; **action autonomy**; core application area (e.g., computer vision, human language technologies, robotics); evaluation methods |\n\n### How it is used\n\n- **Promote a common understanding of AI:** identify the features that matter most for policy.\n- **Inform registries or inventories** of algorithms and automated decision systems.\n- **Support sector-specific frameworks**, for example in healthcare or finance.\n- **Support risk assessment**, including a common framework for reporting AI incidents.\n- **Support risk management:** mitigation, compliance and enforcement along the lifecycle.\n\n### \"In the lab\" vs \"in the field\"\n\n- **In the lab** (conception and development, before deployment): the Data & Input, AI Model and Task & Output dimensions apply. Relevant to ex ante risk management.\n- **In the field** (use and evolution after deployment): all five dimensions apply. Relevant to ex post risk management.\n- The dimensions can also be mapped to lifecycle stages to identify the AI actors responsible at each point, which supports accountability.\n\n> **Exam traps:** action autonomy sits under **Task & Output**, not AI Model. Opt-out and redress sit under **People & Planet**. Sector, criticality and scale sit under **Economic Context**.\n\n---\n\n## Hiroshima AI Process (HAIP) Reporting Framework\n\n| Item | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| **What** | A common, voluntary reporting framework through which organizations report how they align with the G7 **Hiroshima Process International Code of Conduct for Organisations Developing Advanced AI Systems** |\n| **Origin** | The Code came out of Japan's 2023 G7 presidency and was advanced under Italy's 2024 presidency |\n| **Run by** | The **OECD**, which hosts the framework and publishes reports on OECD.AI |\n| **Launched** | February 2025 |\n| **First round** | 25 organizations reported; the OECD analysed them in *How are AI developers managing risks?* |\n| **Version 2.0** | Opens reporting to a wider range of organizations involved in developing, deploying and providing advanced AI systems |\n| **Next cut-off** | Submissions received by 30 September 2026 feed the next analytical review |\n| **Legal effect** | None. It is a transparency tool, unlike the EU's GPAI Code of Practice, which providers use to demonstrate AI Act compliance |\n\n> **Exam trap:** the G7 wrote the Code; the **OECD runs the reporting**. It is voluntary and aimed at comparability, not certification or enforcement.\n\n---\n\n## AI Incidents and Hazards\n\nThe OECD's definitions (*Defining AI incidents and related terms*, OECD AI Papers No. 16, May 2024) separate realized harm from potential harm:\n\n| Term | Definition (paraphrased) |\n|---|---|\n| **AI incident** | An event, circumstance or series of events where the development, use or malfunction of one or more AI systems directly or indirectly leads to harm |\n| **Serious AI incident** | As above, but the harm is serious: death or serious harm to health; serious and irreversible disruption of critical infrastructure; serious violation of rights; serious harm to property, communities or the environment |\n| **AI disaster** | A serious AI incident that disrupts the functioning of a community or society and may test or exceed its capacity to cope with its own resources |\n| **AI hazard** | An event, circumstance or series of events that **could plausibly lead** to an AI incident |\n| **Serious AI hazard** | An event that could plausibly lead to a serious AI incident or an AI disaster |\n\n**The four harm types** (for incidents and hazards alike):\n1. Injury or harm to the health of a person or groups of people\n2. Disruption of the management and operation of critical infrastructure\n3. Violations of human rights, or breaches of legal obligations that protect fundamental, labour and intellectual property rights\n4. Harm to property, communities or the environment. Reputational and financial harm to organizations fall here, as harm to property\n\n**Key clarifications**\n- **Near misses are AI hazards.** No harm occurred, but it plausibly could have.\n- AI-related risks count as hazards to the extent they could lead to incidents.\n- An event can include interactions between AI systems, including agentic AI systems.\n- The tiers nest: AI disasters are a subset of serious incidents, which are a subset of incidents. Serious hazards are a subset of hazards.\n- The serious-incident definition was drafted to align with the definition proposed in the EU AI Act context.\n\n**The tools**\n- **AI Incidents and Hazards Monitor (AIM)** on OECD.AI tracks incidents and hazards reported in the news, classifying articles automatically against these definitions. It sees only publicly reported events, so it is a subset of what actually happens.\n- **Common reporting framework for AI incidents** (OECD AI Papers No. 34, February 2025): a shared set of reporting criteria so that jurisdictions can report consistently while keeping their own rules.\n\n> **Exam pattern:** Did harm happen? **Incident.** Could it plausibly have happened (including a caught error)? **Hazard.** Was the harm serious? **Serious incident.** Did it overwhelm a community's ability to cope? **AI disaster.**\n\n---\n\n## Implementation Mechanisms\n\n### OECD.AI Policy Observatory\nLaunched February 2020. An inclusive hub for public policy on AI that aims to help countries encourage, nurture, and monitor responsible development of trustworthy AI. Features:\n- **Live database** of AI strategies, policies, and initiatives (countries and stakeholders can share and update)\n- **Interactive comparison** of key elements across jurisdictions\n- **AI metrics, measurements, policies, and good practices** — continuously updated\n- **Practical guidance for implementation** — iterated based on Observatory findings\n\n### ONE AI (OECD Network of Experts on AI)\n- Informal group of AI experts from government, business, academia, and civil society\n- Provides AI-specific policy expertise and advice to the OECD\n- Space for in-depth discussions about shared AI policy opportunities and challenges\n\n### Working Party on AI Governance (AIGO)\n- Created 2022 under the Digital Policy Committee (DPC)\n- Supports implementation of the Recommendation\n- Develops practical guidance for implementation\n- Provides a forum and online hub for exchanging information on AI policy\n- Fosters multi-stakeholder and interdisciplinary dialogue through ONE AI Expert Groups\n\n### Reporting Cycle\n- DPC instructed to report to Council on implementation, dissemination, and continued relevance\n- **Next report:** No later than five years following the 2024 revision, and at least every ten years thereafter\n\n---\n\n## Comparison with Other Major Frameworks\n\n### OECD AI Principles vs. NIST AI RMF\n\n| Dimension | OECD AI Principles | NIST AI RMF |\n|-----------|--------------------|-------------|\n| **Nature** | Intergovernmental policy recommendation (non-binding) | Voluntary technical framework |\n| **Scope** | 47 adherents (OECD members, non-members and the EU) | Primarily U.S. but adopted globally |\n| **Audience** | Governments + all AI actors (developers, deployers, operators) | AI developers, deployers, evaluators, users |\n| **Structure** | 5 principles + 5 policy recommendations | 4 core functions: Govern, Map, Measure, Manage |\n| **Approach** | Values-based, high-level principles | Process-based, operational risk management |\n| **Accountability** | Role-based; traceability + systematic risk management | Govern function establishes accountability structures; RACI model |\n| **Transparency** | Four disclosure obligations (general understanding → challenge outputs) | Map function documents context; Measure evaluates transparency |\n| **Robustness/Safety** | Override/repair/decommission mechanisms | Manage function: risk treatment, incident response, system retirement |\n| **Update cycle** | Revised 2023, 2024 (political process) | Continuously updated (Playbook, profiles) |\n| **Implementation tools** | OECD.AI Observatory, classification framework, AI Incidents and Hazards Monitor, HAIP Reporting Framework | AI RMF Playbook, profiles, evaluation programs such as ARIA (not in BOK v2.1) |\n\n> **Complementary relationship:** The OECD Principles provide the **normative foundation** (the \"why\"), while the NIST AI RMF provides the **operational methodology** (the \"how\"). Organisations often map OECD principles to NIST AI RMF subcategories for comprehensive governance.\n\n### OECD AI Principles vs. ISO/IEC 42001\n\n| Dimension | OECD AI Principles | ISO/IEC 42001 |\n|-----------|--------------------|---------------|\n| **Nature** | Intergovernmental recommendation | International certifiable standard |\n| **Binding?** | No (political commitment) | Yes, if organisation seeks certification |\n| **Structure** | 5 principles + 5 policy recommendations | Annex SL management system (Context → Leadership → Planning → Support → Operation → Performance → Improvement) |\n| **Focus** | What governments and AI actors *should do* | How organisations *should manage* AI systems |\n| **Trustworthiness** | Five principles define trustworthiness dimensions | Trustworthiness dimensions required as considerations: transparency, fairness, robustness, accountability, privacy |\n| **Alignment** | Foundational values | Operational governance system |\n| **Certification** | N/A | Certifiable by accredited bodies |\n| **Implementation** | Via national policy, OECD.AI guidance | Via documented AI management system, internal audit, management review |\n\n> **Layered approach:** The OECD Principles set the **policy direction** → ISO 42001 provides the **management system** to operationalise those principles → NIST AI RMF provides the **risk management processes** to identify, assess, and treat AI risks on an ongoing basis.\n\n### OECD Influence on Other Frameworks\n\n| Framework | OECD Influence |\n|-----------|---------------|\n| **G20 AI Principles** (2019) | **Directly drawn** from the OECD Recommendation |\n| **EU AI Act** | Its Art. 3(1) AI system definition closely tracks the OECD's November 2023 wording, and Recital 12 calls for alignment with the work of international organisations; the OECD's serious-incident definition was in turn drafted to align with the AI Act context |\n| **G7 Hiroshima Process** (2023) | Code of Conduct for advanced AI builds on OECD principles — risk lifecycle, transparency, content provenance |\n| **UNESCO AI Ethics Recommendation** (2021) | Overlapping human rights and human-centred values, though UNESCO goes further on cultural diversity |\n| **Council of Europe AI Treaty** | References OECD as foundational intergovernmental standard |\n\n---\n\n## Key Principles at a Glance\n\n| # | Principle | One-Line Summary |\n|---|-----------|-----------------|\n| 1 | **Inclusive Growth, Sustainable Development, Well-Being** | AI should benefit people and the planet |\n| 2 | **Human Rights, Democratic Values, Fairness, Privacy** | AI must respect the rule of law, human dignity, and fundamental rights |\n| 3 | **Transparency and Explainability** | AI actors must disclose meaningful information and enable challenges to outputs |\n| 4 | **Robustness, Security and Safety** | AI must be safe throughout its lifecycle, with override and decommissioning mechanisms |\n| 5 | **Accountability** | AI actors are accountable; traceability and systematic risk management are required |\n\n---\n\n## Exam Relevance (AIGP)\n\n- **Domain II, Subdomain II.D (BOK v2.1):** the OECD's principles, framework, policies and recommended practices for trustworthy AI. Study the tools, not just the list of principles.\n- The OECD Principles are **not legally binding** but are highly influential: the G20 AI Principles were drawn from them, and the EU AI Act's AI system definition closely tracks the OECD's 2023 wording.\n- Know the **five principles** by their 2024 headings. Principle 1.2 is now \"respect for the rule of law, human rights and democratic values, including fairness and privacy\", not \"human-centred values and fairness\".\n- **Two revisions, two dates.** 8 Nov 2023 changed only the AI system definition; 3 May 2024 added information integrity, misuse, safe override and decommissioning, responsible business conduct, interoperable governance and environmental sustainability, and moved traceability into Accountability.\n- **Classification framework:** five dimensions (People & Planet, Economic Context, Data & Input, AI Model, Task & Output). Expect \"which dimension holds this criterion?\" questions. Action autonomy is Task & Output.\n- **Incident vs hazard:** harm happened vs harm could plausibly happen. Near misses are hazards; AI disasters are the most severe tier of incidents.\n- **HAIP Reporting Framework:** the G7's Code of Conduct, with voluntary reporting run by the OECD since February 2025.\n- Expect comparison questions: OECD = policy direction and values; NIST AI RMF = operational risk management; ISO/IEC 42001 = certifiable management system; ISO/IEC 42005 = impact assessment guidance.\n\n---\n\n## Key Terms\n\n- [[OECD AI Principles]]\n- [[OECD Framework for the Classification of AI Systems]]\n- [[OECD AI System Definition (2023 Update)]]\n- [[AI Incident vs. AI Hazard (OECD Definitions)]]\n- [[OECD AI Incidents and Hazards Monitor (AIM)]]\n- [[Hiroshima AI Process Reporting Framework]]\n\n---\n\n## Related Notes\n\n- [[AI Standards and Frameworks]] — Chapter-level coverage with comparison tables\n- [[NIST AI RMF — Structured Reference]] — Deep dive on the NIST AI Risk Management Framework\n- [[ISO 42005 — AI System Impact Assessment]] — Impact assessment standard (compare incident monitoring with pre-deployment assessment)\n- [[AIGP Body of Knowledge — Exam Blueprint]] — Domain II blueprint\n- [[The EU AI Act]] — EU regulation whose AI system definition tracks the OECD's\n\n---\n\n## Sources\n\n- https://legalinstruments.oecd.org/en/instruments/OECD-LEGAL-0449 (instrument text via the OECD print service: adoption 22 May 2019; revisions 8 Nov 2023 and 3 May 2024; definition; principles; what each revision changed)\n- https://oecd.ai/en/ai-principles (47 adherents; G20 AI Principles; May 2024 update)\n- https://oecd.ai/en/dashboards/ai-principles/P6 (2019 heading \"Human-centred values and fairness\")\n- https://oecd.ai/en/wonk/ai-system-definition-update (29 Nov 2023 post explaining the five definition edits)\n- https://oecd.ai/en/ai-publications/explanatory-memorandum-on-the-updated-oecd-definition-of-an-ai-system (OECD AI Papers No. 8, March 2024)\n- https://oecd.ai/en/classification · https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2022/02/oecd-framework-for-the-classification-of-ai-systems_336a8b57/cb6d9eca-en.pdf (Digital Economy Papers No. 323)\n- https://oecd.ai/en/transparency/overview (HAIP Reporting Framework)\n- https://oecd.ai/en/incidents · https://oecd.ai/en/incidents-methodology (AIM)\n- https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2024/05/defining-ai-incidents-and-related-terms_88d089ec/d1a8d965-en.pdf (OECD AI Papers No. 16)\n- https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/towards-a-common-reporting-framework-for-ai-incidents_f326d4ac-en.html (OECD AI Papers No. 34, Feb 2025)\n- https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/3/ · https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/12/ (EU AI Act definition and alignment)\n\n---\n\n> [!source] Framework Recall Cards — AIGP Study Kit\n\n## OECD AI Principles\n\n*The ethical substrate under nearly everything else.*\n\n### The five value-based principles, and why they matter for the exam\n\n1. Inclusive growth, sustainable development and wellbeing\n2. Respect for the rule of law, human rights and democratic values, including fairness and privacy (2024 wording; the 2019 text said 'human-centred values and fairness')\n3. Transparency and explainability\n4. Robustness, security and safety\n5. Accountability\n\n**Non-binding**, adopted by 40+ countries, and the acknowledged influence on the EU AI Act and G7 positions. If a question asks which framework provided the *ethical foundation* that shaped later regulation, this is it.", "ref_nist_rmf": "## At a Glance\n\nNIST's AI Risk Management Framework 1.0 (January 2023) — the US government's voluntary, sector-agnostic playbook for managing AI risk. Two parts: framing AI risk and trustworthiness (seven characteristics), and the Core — four functions: Govern (culture, roles, policies — cross-cutting), Map (context, purpose, risk identification), Measure (assess and track risks with metrics), Manage (prioritize, respond, monitor). A companion Playbook gives per-subcategory actions.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nThe four functions and seven trustworthiness characteristics are heavily tested. Trustworthy AI is: valid & reliable; safe; secure & resilient; accountable & transparent; explainable & interpretable; privacy-enhanced; fair with harmful bias managed. Govern is the cross-cutting foundation feeding the other three.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Voluntary, rights-preserving, sector-agnostic; directed by Congress and built through open consultation\n- Core functions: GOVERN (cross-cutting culture, accountability, policies) → MAP (context and risks) → MEASURE (analyze, assess, track) → MANAGE (prioritize and respond)\n- Seven trustworthiness characteristics: valid & reliable, safe, secure & resilient, accountable & transparent, explainable & interpretable, privacy-enhanced, fair with harmful bias managed\n- Risk framing: AI risks are contextual — the same system is high-risk in one deployment and low-risk in another\n- Companion resources: NIST AI RMF Playbook (actionable subcategory guidance), Generative AI Profile (AI 600-1), crosswalks to ISO and other frameworks\n- Profiles tailor the RMF to specific settings, technologies, or sectors — the GAI Profile is the flagship example\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] AI Standards and Frameworks\n\n## 2. NIST AI Risk Management Framework (AI RMF)\n\nPublished **January 2023**. Voluntary, flexible framework for managing AI-specific risks throughout the lifecycle. Accompanied by an **AI RMF Playbook** with practical implementation guidance.\n\n### Key Goals\n\n- Enable **trustworthy and responsible AI** through structured risk management\n- Provide a **flexible, voluntary framework** across sectors and maturity levels\n- Foster **collaboration** among developers, deployers, evaluators, and users\n- Support **innovation** while identifying and mitigating harm\n\n### What Makes AI Risk Unique?\n\n| Characteristic | Implication |\n|----------------|-------------|\n| **Context-dependent** | Same AI system may be high-risk in one use case, low-risk in another |\n| **Emergent behavior** | Outcomes unintended or unforeseen at design time |\n| **Opacity** | Opaque/adaptive algorithms produce unpredictable results |\n| **Societal scale** | Harms affect individuals, groups, or society; outputs scale to millions instantly |\n| **Data-driven bias** | Risks arise from biased data, proxies, or deployment contexts |\n\n### The Four Core Functions\n\n```\n ┌──────────┐\n │ GOVERN │ ← Foundational: culture, policies, accountability\n └────┬─────┘\n ┌────────────────┼────────────────┐\n ┌────┴────┐ ┌─────┴──────┐ ┌───┴──────┐\n │ MAP │ ──→ │ MEASURE │ ──→ │ MANAGE │\n └─────────┘ └────────────┘ └──────────┘\n Understand Assess risk & Respond to\n context trustworthiness identified risks\n```\n\n#### GOVERN — Building the Foundation\n\nEstablishes organizational context, culture, and structures. **Most relevant for AI governance professionals.**\n\n| Category | Key Subcategories |\n|----------|-------------------|\n| **1. Policies, Processes, Procedures** | Documented risk management policies; AI-specific considerations (fairness, explainability, robustness, privacy) |\n| **2. Roles and Responsibilities** | Clear roles/accountabilities across lifecycle; oversight bodies (ethics committees, internal audit); escalation mechanisms |\n| **3. Risk Tolerance and Appetite** | Documented risk appetite/tolerance statements; stakeholder input; periodic reviews as tech/regs evolve |\n| **4. Organizational Culture & Communication** | Leaders model risk-informed decisions; employees raise concerns without retaliation; training reinforces ethical AI use |\n\n> **RACI Model:** Responsible, Accountable, Consulted, Informed — used to assign AI governance responsibilities (see [[Organizational Readiness]]).\n\n> **AI Risk Culture vs. Security Culture:** Both emphasize vigilance, but AI risk culture also requires continuous questioning of assumptions, attention to context, and inclusion of ethical/societal considerations.\n\n#### MAP — Understanding Context\n\nIdentifies the AI system's characteristics and operating context. Iterative across the lifecycle.\n\n| Category | Key Subcategories |\n|----------|-------------------|\n| **1. Context & Intended Purpose** | Documented uses, goals, limitations; foreseeable misuses; socio-technical context; traceable documentation |\n| **2. System Design & Functionality** | Data sources, preprocessing, model features; human-AI interaction design; performance metrics and boundaries |\n| **3. Stakeholders & Affected Populations** | Primary users and decision-makers; impacted individuals/communities; stakeholder engagement documentation |\n| **4. Risk Identification & Prioritization** | Known/foreseeable risks documented; prioritized by likelihood and severity; sources of uncertainty |\n\n> **Epistemic vs. Aleatoric Uncertainty:**\n> - **Epistemic** — unknowns from limited knowledge (e.g., insufficient training data)\n> - **Aleatoric** — inherent randomness (e.g., noise in sensor data)\n> Map should identify both.\n\n> **Performance ≠ Trustworthiness:** High accuracy can still mask harmful stereotypes or erode human autonomy. Mapping requires qualitative context, not just quantitative performance.\n\n#### MEASURE — Assessing Risk and Trustworthiness\n\n**Ongoing** evaluation using quantitative and qualitative methods — not a one-time task.\n\n| Category | Key Subcategories |\n|----------|-------------------|\n| **1. Risk Metrics & Indicators** | Established criteria for each risk; metrics tailored to context/stakeholders; updated as understanding evolves |\n| **2. Evaluation Methods** | Testing (unit, red teaming, blue teaming, stress testing); qualitative methods (expert panels, user studies); third-party audits |\n| **3. System Performance Monitoring** | Continuous monitoring/logging; anomaly/error detection; feedback loops |\n| **4. Risk Documentation & Reporting** | Results documented/archived; stakeholder-appropriate reports; inform future updates |\n\n> **Model Cards & System Cards:** Structured, accessible formats for documenting AI system performance, limitations, and risks (especially for LLMs).\n\n#### MANAGE — Taking Action on AI Risk\n\nOperationalizes insight from Govern/Map/Measure into concrete mitigation and improvement.\n\n| Category | Key Subcategories |\n|----------|-------------------|\n| **1. Risk Treatment & Mitigation** | Controls based on risk assessment; technical (algorithmic constraints) + organizational (policy enforcement); all treatment decisions approved/documented |\n| **2. Incident Response & Recovery** | AI-specific incident playbooks; align with cybersecurity IR; escalation tied to risk thresholds; after-action reviews with learning loops |\n| **3. Risk Management Integration** | AI risk integrated with cybersecurity, privacy, ERM; cross-functional coordination; procurement/vendor AI risk protocols |\n| **4. Risk Evolution & System Retirement** | Periodic fitness reviews; end-of-life planning; decommissioning includes documentation and post-use impact assessment |\n\n> **Risk Treatment Options:** Risk acceptance, risk transfer, risk avoidance, risk mitigation — all must be approved by management and documented.\n\n> **AI Incident Examples:** Self-driving car veering off path, AI assistant giving dangerous advice, bias audit revealing disparate treatment.\n\n### AI RMF vs. NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF)\n\n| Element | AI RMF | CSF |\n|---------|--------|-----|\n| **Focus domain** | AI system lifecycle and socio-technical risks | Cybersecurity controls and resilience |\n| **Types of risk** | Bias, opacity, misuse, autonomy, drift, societal harm | CIA triad (Confidentiality, Integrity, Availability) |\n| **Uncertainty** | Explicit handling of epistemic/aleatoric uncertainty | Generally assumes known or measurable threats |\n| **Explainability & fairness** | Core theme | Not addressed |\n| **System context** | Central to risk mapping | Less context-sensitive, control-based |\n| **Ethical dimensions** | Integral to the framework | Not in scope |\n\n| When to Use | Framework |\n|-------------|-----------|\n| Preventing malware in IT systems | **CSF** |\n| Evaluating bias in HR screening algorithm | **AI RMF** |\n| Securing cloud infrastructure for AI workloads | **CSF** (with AI overlays) |\n| Governing ethics/impacts of autonomous vehicles | **AI RMF** (with crosswalks to CSF for security) |\n\n> **Fundamental Shift:** CSF treats risk as something to defend against with known controls. AI RMF treats risk as **emergent, systemic, and value-laden** — requiring social, organizational, and technical strategies.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n> [!source] NIST AI RMF — Structured Reference\n\n# NIST AI RMF 1.0 — Structured Reference\n\n> **NIST AI 100-1 | Artificial Intelligence Risk Management Framework 1.0**\n> Published: **January 2023** | Voluntary, non-sector-specific, use-case agnostic.\n> Next formal review expected: **no later than 2028** (living document with semi-annual Playbook updates).\n\n---\n\n## Framework Overview\n\nThe AI RMF is divided into **two parts**:\n\n| Part | Content |\n|------|---------|\n| **Part 1: Foundational Information** | Framing risk, audience, AI risks & trustworthiness, effectiveness |\n| **Part 2: Core and Profiles** | The four functions (Govern, Map, Measure, Manage), categories & subcategories, and AI RMF Profiles |\n\n### Definition of AI System (per AI RMF)\n\n> An engineered or machine-based system that can, for a given set of objectives, generate outputs such as predictions, recommendations, or decisions influencing real or virtual environments. AI systems are designed to operate with varying levels of autonomy. *(Adapted from: OECD Recommendation on AI:2019; ISO/IEC 22989:2022)*\n\n### Core Principle: Risk = Composite Measure\n\n**Risk** = probability of an event occurring × magnitude of its consequences. Impacts can be **positive, negative, or both**. The Framework addresses minimizing negative impacts AND identifying opportunities to maximize positive impacts.\n\n---\n\n## Characteristics of Trustworthy AI\n\n> **Valid & Reliable** is the necessary foundation. **Accountable & Transparent** is cross-cutting and relates to all other characteristics.\n\n| # | Characteristic | Core Definition |\n|---|---------------|-----------------|\n| 1 | **Valid and Reliable** | Confirmation that requirements for intended use are fulfilled; accuracy + robustness + reliability over time |\n| 2 | **Safe** | Does not endanger human life, health, property, or environment under defined conditions |\n| 3 | **Secure and Resilient** | Withstands adverse events; maintains confidentiality, integrity, availability; degrades gracefully when necessary |\n| 4 | **Accountable and Transparent** | Information about AI system & outputs available to those interacting with it; accountability presupposes transparency |\n| 5 | **Explainable and Interpretable** | Explainability = *how* the system works; Interpretability = *why* a decision was made and its meaning in context |\n| 6 | **Privacy-Enhanced** | Safeguards human autonomy, identity, dignity; norms for anonymity, confidentiality, control |\n| 7 | **Fair — with Harmful Bias Managed** | Equality + equity; addresses three bias categories: systemic, computational/statistical, and human-cognitive |\n\n> **Key Insight:** Tradeoffs between characteristics are inevitable. Balancing them requires contextual assessment. Trustworthiness is only as strong as its weakest characteristic.\n\n### The Three Categories of AI Bias (NIST SP 1270)\n\n| Bias Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **Systemic** | Present in datasets, organizational norms, practices, processes, and broader societal structures |\n| **Computational & Statistical** | Systematic errors from non-representative samples or algorithmic processes |\n| **Human-Cognitive** | How individuals/groups perceive AI information, make decisions, fill in missing information |\n\n---\n\n## The AI Lifecycle and Key Dimensions\n\nThe AI RMF adopts the OECD framework for AI lifecycle classification, modified to emphasize **TEVV** (Test, Evaluation, Verification, Validation) processes.\n\n### Four Key Dimensions (inner circles):\n\n| Dimension | Focus |\n|-----------|-------|\n| **Application Context** | The problem space, intended use case, operational environment |\n| **Data and Input** | Training data, preprocessing, data quality, provenance |\n| **AI Model** | Model architecture, training, selection, calibration |\n| **Task and Output** | What the system produces, how outputs are used |\n\n### Lifecycle Stages (outer circle):\n\n1. **Plan and Design** — Concept, objectives, requirements, data planning\n2. **Data Collection and Processing** — Gathering, cleaning, labeling, documenting\n3. **Model Building and Interpretation** — Training, selection, calibration, testing\n4. **System Deployment** — Piloting, integration, compliance, user experience\n5. **Operation and Monitoring** — Ongoing assessment, incident tracking, updates\n\n> **TEVV is performed throughout all stages** and is separated as a best practice: model builders/users are distinct from those verifying and validating.\n\n### AI Actors (per OECD definition)\n\nThose who play an active role in the AI system lifecycle, including:\n- AI Design, Development, Deployment actors\n- Operation & Monitoring actors\n- TEVV specialists (distinct from builders)\n- Human Factors professionals\n- Domain Experts, Impact Assessors\n- Procurement, Governance & Oversight\n- Third-party entities, End Users, Affected Individuals/Communities\n- The General Public\n\n---\n\n## The Four Core Functions\n\n```\n ┌──────────┐\n │ GOVERN │ ← Cross-cutting: informs & is infused\n └────┬─────┘ throughout all other functions\n ┌────────────────┼────────────────┐\n ┌────┴────┐ ┌─────┴──────┐ ┌───┴──────┐\n │ MAP │ ──→ │ MEASURE │ ──→ │ MANAGE │\n └─────────┘ └────────────┘ └──────────┘\n Establish Analyze, assess, Allocate resources,\n context to benchmark, and respond to, recover\n frame risks monitor AI risk from, and communicate\n about risks\n```\n\n> After instituting GOVERN, most users start with MAP → MEASURE → MANAGE. The process is iterative with cross-referencing between functions. Functions may be performed in any order.\n\n---\n\n## Function 1: GOVERN\n\n> *Cultivates a culture of risk management. Outlines processes, documents, and organizational schemes. Cross-cutting — infused throughout MAP, MEASURE, and MANAGE.*\n\n### GOVERN 1: Policies, Processes, Procedures, and Practices\n\n*Organizational policies, processes, procedures, and practices across the organization related to mapping, measuring, and managing AI risks are in place, transparent, and implemented effectively.*\n\n| Subcategory | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| **GOVERN 1.1** | Legal and regulatory requirements involving AI are understood, managed, and documented |\n| **GOVERN 1.2** | Characteristics of trustworthy AI are integrated into organizational policies, processes, procedures, and practices |\n| **GOVERN 1.3** | Processes, procedures, and practices are in place to determine the needed level of risk management activities based on the organization's risk tolerance |\n| **GOVERN 1.4** | The risk management process and its outcomes are established through transparent policies, procedures, and other controls based on organizational risk priorities |\n| **GOVERN 1.5** | Ongoing monitoring and periodic review of the risk management process and its outcomes are planned and organizational roles and responsibilities clearly defined, including determining the frequency of periodic review |\n| **GOVERN 1.6** | Mechanisms are in place to inventory AI systems and are resourced according to organizational risk priorities |\n| **GOVERN 1.7** | Processes and procedures are in place for decommissioning and phasing out AI systems safely and in a manner that does not increase risks or decrease the organization's trustworthiness |\n\n### GOVERN 2: Accountability Structures\n\n*Accountability structures are in place so that the appropriate teams and individuals are empowered, responsible, and trained for mapping, measuring, and managing AI risks.*\n\n| Subcategory | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| **GOVERN 2.1** | Roles and responsibilities and lines of communication related to mapping, measuring, and managing AI risks are documented and are clear to individuals and teams throughout the organization |\n| **GOVERN 2.2** | The organization's personnel and partners receive AI risk management training to enable them to perform their duties and responsibilities consistent with related policies, procedures, and agreements |\n| **GOVERN 2.3** | Executive leadership of the organization takes responsibility for decisions about risks associated with AI system development and deployment |\n\n### GOVERN 3: Workforce Diversity, Equity, Inclusion, and Accessibility\n\n*Workforce diversity, equity, inclusion, and accessibility processes are prioritized in the mapping, measuring, and managing of AI risks throughout the lifecycle.*\n\n| Subcategory | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| **GOVERN 3.1** | Decision-making related to mapping, measuring, and managing AI risks throughout the lifecycle is informed by a diverse team (e.g., diversity of demographics, disciplines, experience, expertise, and backgrounds) |\n| **GOVERN 3.2** | Policies and procedures are in place to define and differentiate roles and responsibilities for human-AI configurations and oversight of AI systems |\n\n### GOVERN 4: Organizational Culture & Communication\n\n*Organizational teams are committed to a culture that considers and communicates AI risk.*\n\n| Subcategory | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| **GOVERN 4.1** | Organizational policies and practices are in place to foster a critical thinking and safety-first mindset in the design, development, deployment, and uses of AI systems to minimize potential negative impacts |\n| **GOVERN 4.2** | Organizational teams document the risks and potential impacts of the AI technology they design, develop, deploy, evaluate, and use, and they communicate about the impacts more broadly |\n| **GOVERN 4.3** | Organizational practices are in place to enable AI testing, identification of incidents, and information sharing |\n\n### GOVERN 5: Stakeholder Engagement\n\n*Processes are in place for robust engagement with relevant AI actors.*\n\n| Subcategory | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| **GOVERN 5.1** | Organizational policies and practices are in place to collect, consider, prioritize, and integrate feedback from those external to the team that developed or deployed the AI system regarding the potential individual and societal impacts related to AI risks |\n| **GOVERN 5.2** | Mechanisms are established to enable the team that developed or deployed AI systems to regularly incorporate adjudicated feedback from relevant AI actors into system design and implementation |\n\n### GOVERN 6: Third-Party & Supply Chain\n\n*Policies and procedures are in place to address AI risks and benefits arising from third-party software and data and other supply chain issues.*\n\n| Subcategory | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| **GOVERN 6.1** | Policies and procedures are in place that address AI risks associated with third-party entities, including risks of infringement of a third-party's intellectual property or other rights |\n| **GOVERN 6.2** | Contingency processes are in place to handle failures or incidents in third-party data or AI systems deemed to be high-risk |\n\n---\n\n## Function 2: MAP\n\n> *Establishes the context to frame risks related to an AI system. The basis for MEASURE and MANAGE. Enhanced by diverse internal teams and engagement with external AI actors.*\n\n### MAP 1: Context Establishment\n\n*Context is established and understood.*\n\n| Subcategory | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| **MAP 1.1** | Intended purposes, potentially beneficial uses, context-specific laws, norms and expectations, and prospective settings in which the AI system will be deployed are understood and documented. Considerations include: specific users and their expectations; potential positive and negative impacts to individuals, communities, organizations, society, and the planet; assumptions and related limitations; TEVV and system metrics |\n| **MAP 1.2** | Interdisciplinary AI actors, competencies, skills, and capacities for establishing context reflect demographic diversity and broad domain and user experience expertise, and their participation is documented |\n| **MAP 1.3** | The organization's mission and relevant goals for AI technology are understood and documented |\n| **MAP 1.4** | The business value or context of business use has been clearly defined or — in the case of assessing existing AI systems — re-evaluated |\n| **MAP 1.5** | Organizational risk tolerances are determined and documented |\n| **MAP 1.6** | System requirements (e.g., \"the system shall respect the privacy of its users\") are elicited from and understood by relevant AI actors. Design decisions take socio-technical implications into account to address AI risks |\n\n### MAP 2: AI System Categorization\n\n*Categorization of the AI system is performed.*\n\n| Subcategory | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| **MAP 2.1** | The specific tasks and methods used to implement the tasks that the AI system will support are defined (e.g., classifiers, generative models, recommenders) |\n| **MAP 2.2** | Information about the AI system's knowledge limits and how system output may be utilized and overseen by humans is documented. Documentation provides sufficient information to assist relevant AI actors when making decisions and taking subsequent actions |\n| **MAP 2.3** | Scientific integrity and TEVV considerations are identified and documented, including those related to experimental design, data collection and selection (e.g., availability, representativeness, suitability), system trustworthiness, and construct validation |\n\n### MAP 3: Capabilities, Usage, Goals, and Cost-Benefit\n\n*AI capabilities, targeted usage, goals, and expected benefits and costs compared with appropriate benchmarks are understood.*\n\n| Subcategory | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| **MAP 3.1** | Potential benefits of intended AI system functionality and performance are examined and documented |\n| **MAP 3.2** | Potential costs, including non-monetary costs, which result from expected or realized AI errors or system functionality and trustworthiness — as connected to organizational risk tolerance — are examined and documented |\n| **MAP 3.3** | Targeted application scope is specified and documented based on the system's capability, established context, and AI system categorization |\n| **MAP 3.4** | Processes for operator and practitioner proficiency with AI system performance and trustworthiness — and relevant technical standards and certifications — are defined, assessed, and documented |\n| **MAP 3.5** | Processes for human oversight are defined, assessed, and documented in accordance with organizational policies from the GOVERN function |\n\n### MAP 4: Component Risk & Benefit Mapping\n\n*Risks and benefits are mapped for all components of the AI system including third-party software and data.*\n\n| Subcategory | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| **MAP 4.1** | Approaches for mapping AI technology and legal risks of its components — including the use of third-party data or software — are in place, followed, and documented, as are risks of infringement of a third party's intellectual property or other rights |\n| **MAP 4.2** | Internal risk controls for components of the AI system, including third-party AI technologies, are identified and documented |\n\n### MAP 5: Impact Characterization\n\n*Impacts to individuals, groups, communities, organizations, and society are characterized.*\n\n| Subcategory | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| **MAP 5.1** | Likelihood and magnitude of each identified impact (both potentially beneficial and harmful) based on expected use, past uses of AI systems in similar contexts, public incident reports, feedback from those external to the team that developed or deployed the AI system, or other data are identified and documented |\n| **MAP 5.2** | Practices and personnel for supporting regular engagement with relevant AI actors and integrating feedback about positive, negative, and unanticipated impacts are in place and documented |\n\n---\n\n## Function 3: MEASURE\n\n> *Employs quantitative, qualitative, or mixed-method tools, techniques, and methodologies to analyze, assess, benchmark, and monitor AI risk and related impacts. Uses knowledge from MAP and informs MANAGE.*\n\n### MEASURE 1: Methods and Metrics\n\n*Appropriate methods and metrics are identified and applied.*\n\n| Subcategory | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| **MEASURE 1.1** | Approaches and metrics for measurement of AI risks enumerated during the MAP function are selected for implementation starting with the most significant AI risks. The risks or trustworthiness characteristics that will not — or cannot — be measured are properly documented |\n| **MEASURE 1.2** | Appropriateness of AI metrics and effectiveness of existing controls are regularly assessed and updated, including reports of errors and potential impacts on affected communities |\n| **MEASURE 1.3** | Internal experts who did not serve as front-line developers for the system and/or independent assessors are involved in regular assessments and updates. Domain experts, users, AI actors external to the team that developed or deployed the AI system, and affected communities are consulted in support of assessments as necessary per organizational risk tolerance |\n\n### MEASURE 2: Trustworthy Characteristics Evaluation\n\n*AI systems are evaluated for trustworthy characteristics.*\n\n| Subcategory | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| **MEASURE 2.1** | Test sets, metrics, and details about the tools used during TEVV are documented |\n| **MEASURE 2.2** | Evaluations involving human subjects meet applicable requirements (including human subject protection) and are representative of the relevant population |\n| **MEASURE 2.3** | AI system performance or assurance criteria are measured qualitatively or quantitatively and demonstrated for conditions similar to deployment setting(s). Measures are documented |\n| **MEASURE 2.4** | The functionality and behavior of the AI system and its components — as identified in the MAP function — are monitored when in production |\n| **MEASURE 2.5** | The AI system to be deployed is demonstrated to be **valid and reliable**. Limitations of generalizability beyond the conditions under which the technology was developed are documented |\n| **MEASURE 2.6** | The AI system is evaluated regularly for **safety** risks — as identified in the MAP function. The AI system to be deployed is demonstrated to be safe, its residual negative risk does not exceed the risk tolerance, and it can fail safely, particularly if made to operate beyond its knowledge limits. Safety metrics reflect system reliability and robustness, real-time monitoring, and response times for AI system failures |\n| **MEASURE 2.7** | AI system **security and resilience** — as identified in the MAP function — are evaluated and documented |\n| **MEASURE 2.8** | Risks associated with **transparency and accountability** — as identified in the MAP function — are examined and documented |\n| **MEASURE 2.9** | The AI **model is explained, validated, and documented**, and AI system output is interpreted within its context — as identified in the MAP function — to inform responsible use and governance |\n| **MEASURE 2.10** | **Privacy** risk of the AI system — as identified in the MAP function — is examined and documented |\n| **MEASURE 2.11** | **Fairness and bias** — as identified in the MAP function — are evaluated and results are documented |\n| **MEASURE 2.12** | **Environmental impact and sustainability** of AI model training and management activities — as identified in the MAP function — are assessed and documented |\n| **MEASURE 2.13** | Effectiveness of the employed TEVV metrics and processes in the MEASURE function are evaluated and documented |\n\n### MEASURE 3: Risk Tracking\n\n*Mechanisms for tracking identified AI risks over time are in place.*\n\n| Subcategory | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| **MEASURE 3.1** | Approaches, personnel, and documentation are in place to regularly identify and track existing, unanticipated, and emergent AI risks based on factors such as intended and actual performance in deployed contexts |\n| **MEASURE 3.2** | Risk tracking approaches are considered for settings where AI risks are difficult to assess using currently available measurement techniques or where metrics are not yet available |\n| **MEASURE 3.3** | Feedback processes for end users and impacted communities to report problems and appeal system outcomes are established and integrated into AI system evaluation metrics |\n\n### MEASURE 4: Measurement Efficacy Feedback\n\n*Feedback about efficacy of measurement is gathered and assessed.*\n\n| Subcategory | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| **MEASURE 4.1** | Measurement approaches for identifying AI risks are connected to deployment context(s) and informed through consultation with domain experts and other end users. Approaches are documented |\n| **MEASURE 4.2** | Measurement results regarding AI system trustworthiness in deployment context(s) and across the AI lifecycle are informed by input from domain experts and relevant AI actors to validate whether the system is performing consistently as intended. Results are documented |\n| **MEASURE 4.3** | Measurable performance improvements or declines based on consultations with relevant AI actors, including affected communities, and field data about context-relevant risks and trustworthiness characteristics are identified and documented |\n\n---\n\n## Function 4: MANAGE\n\n> *Allocates risk resources to mapped and measured risks on a regular basis and as defined by the GOVERN function. Risk treatment comprises plans to respond to, recover from, and communicate about incidents or events.*\n\n### MANAGE 1: Risk Prioritization and Response\n\n*AI risks based on assessments and other analytical output from the MAP and MEASURE functions are prioritized, responded to, and managed.*\n\n| Subcategory | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| **MANAGE 1.1** | A determination is made as to whether the AI system achieves its intended purposes and stated objectives and whether its development or deployment should proceed |\n| **MANAGE 1.2** | Treatment of documented AI risks is prioritized based on impact, likelihood, and available resources or methods |\n| **MANAGE 1.3** | Responses to the AI risks deemed high priority, as identified by the MAP function, are developed, planned, and documented. Risk response options can include **mitigating, transferring, avoiding, or accepting** |\n| **MANAGE 1.4** | Negative residual risks (defined as the sum of all unmitigated risks) to both downstream acquirers of AI systems and end users are documented |\n\n### MANAGE 2: Benefit Maximization & Harm Minimization\n\n*Strategies to maximize AI benefits and minimize negative impacts are planned, prepared, implemented, documented, and informed by input from relevant AI actors.*\n\n| Subcategory | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| **MANAGE 2.1** | Resources required to manage AI risks are taken into account — along with viable non-AI alternative systems, approaches, or methods — to reduce the magnitude or likelihood of potential impacts |\n| **MANAGE 2.2** | Mechanisms are in place and applied to sustain the value of deployed AI systems |\n| **MANAGE 2.3** | Procedures are followed to respond to and recover from a previously unknown risk when it is identified |\n| **MANAGE 2.4** | Mechanisms are in place and applied, and responsibilities are assigned and understood, to supersede, disengage, or deactivate AI systems that demonstrate performance or outcomes inconsistent with intended use |\n\n### MANAGE 3: Third-Party Risk Management\n\n*AI risks and benefits from third-party entities are managed.*\n\n| Subcategory | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| **MANAGE 3.1** | AI risks and benefits from third-party resources are regularly monitored, and risk controls are applied and documented |\n| **MANAGE 3.2** | Pre-trained models which are used for development are monitored as part of AI system regular monitoring and maintenance |\n\n### MANAGE 4: Risk Treatment Documentation & Monitoring\n\n*Risk treatments, including response and recovery, and communication plans for the identified and measured AI risks are documented and monitored regularly.*\n\n| Subcategory | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| **MANAGE 4.1** | Post-deployment AI system monitoring plans are implemented, including mechanisms for capturing and evaluating input from users and other relevant AI actors, appeal and override, decommissioning, incident response, recovery, and change management |\n| **MANAGE 4.2** | Measurable activities for continual improvements are integrated into AI system updates and include regular engagement with interested parties, including relevant AI actors |\n| **MANAGE 4.3** | Incidents and errors are communicated to relevant AI actors, including affected communities. Processes for tracking, responding to, and recovering from incidents and errors are followed and documented |\n\n---\n\n## Function Summary Matrix\n\n| Function | Core Question | Key Activities | Ensuing Decision |\n|----------|--------------|----------------|------------------|\n| **GOVERN** | What structure enables risk management? | Set policies, accountability, culture, third-party oversight | Organizational readiness |\n| **MAP** | What is the risk landscape? | Establish context, categorize system, characterize impacts | Go/no-go decision |\n| **MEASURE** | How well is the system performing? | TEVV across all trustworthiness characteristics | Identify gaps & emergent risks |\n| **MANAGE** | How do we respond to risks? | Prioritize, mitigate, monitor, communicate, improve | Continuous risk treatment |\n\n---\n\n## AI RMF Profiles\n\nThe AI RMF includes a profiling mechanism for tailoring the framework:\n\n| Profile Type | Description |\n|--------------|-------------|\n| **Use-Case Profile** | Implementation of functions, categories, and subcategories for a specific setting or application (e.g., AI RMF hiring profile, AI RMF fair housing profile) |\n| **Temporal Profile** | Describes current state or desired target state of AI risk management activities |\n| **Current Profile** | How AI risks are currently being managed (current outcomes) |\n| **Target Profile** | Outcomes needed to achieve desired risk management goals |\n| **Cross-Sectoral Profile** | Covers risks of models or applications used across use cases or sectors (e.g., large language models, cloud-based services) |\n\n> **Gap Analysis:** Comparing Current vs. Target Profiles reveals gaps. Action plans address these gaps. Prioritization is driven by user needs and risk management processes.\n\n---\n\n## Comparison: AI RMF vs. NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF)\n\n| Element | AI RMF | NIST CSF |\n|---------|--------|----------|\n| **Focus domain** | AI system lifecycle and socio-technical risks | Cybersecurity controls and resilience |\n| **Core functions** | GOVERN, MAP, MEASURE, MANAGE | IDENTIFY, PROTECT, DETECT, RESPOND, RECOVER |\n| **Types of risk** | Bias, opacity, misuse, autonomy, drift, societal harm | CIA triad (Confidentiality, Integrity, Availability) |\n| **Uncertainty handling** | Explicit handling of epistemic & aleatoric uncertainty | Generally assumes known or measurable threats |\n| **Explainability & fairness** | Core theme, built into trustworthiness characteristics | Not addressed |\n| **System context** | Central to risk mapping (MAP function) | Less context-sensitive, control-based |\n| **Ethical dimensions** | Integral to the framework | Not in scope |\n| **Human factors** | Human-AI interaction, socio-technical dynamics | Limited to security awareness/training |\n| **Bias management** | Three bias categories (systemic, computational, human-cognitive) | Not addressed |\n| **Third-party AI** | Specific supply chain categories (GOVERN 6, MAP 4, MANAGE 3) | Supply chain risk management (ID.SC) |\n\n### When to Use Which Framework\n\n| Scenario | Framework |\n|----------|-----------|\n| Preventing malware in IT systems | **CSF** |\n| Evaluating bias in HR screening algorithm | **AI RMF** |\n| Securing cloud infrastructure for AI workloads | **CSF** (with AI overlays) |\n| Governing ethics/impacts of autonomous vehicles | **AI RMF** (with crosswalks to CSF for security) |\n| Managing privacy risks in AI training data | **AI RMF** + **NIST Privacy Framework** |\n\n> **Fundamental Shift:** CSF treats risk as something to defend against with known controls. AI RMF treats risk as **emergent, systemic, and value-laden** — requiring social, organizational, and technical strategies simultaneously.\n\n---\n\n## How AI Risks Differ from Traditional Software Risks\n\n| AI-Specific Risk | Why It's Unique |\n|------------------|-----------------|\n| **Data misrepresentation** | Training data may not represent context/intended use; ground truth may not exist |\n| **Data dependency at scale** | Increased volume and complexity of training data |\n| **Training drift** | Intentional or unintentional changes during training fundamentally alter performance |\n| **Data staleness** | Datasets become detached from original/intended context or outdated |\n| **Scale & complexity** | Billions/trillions of decision points in traditional software |\n| **Pre-trained model uncertainty** | Statistical uncertainty, bias management, validity, reproducibility issues |\n| **Emergent failure modes** | Harder to predict for large-scale pre-trained models |\n| **Privacy from aggregation** | Enhanced data aggregation capability creates new privacy risks |\n| **Maintenance frequency** | More frequent maintenance due to data/model/concept drift |\n| **Opacity & reproducibility** | Increased opacity; underdeveloped testing standards |\n| **Testing difficulty** | Hard to determine what to test; no same controls as traditional code |\n| **Environmental costs** | Computational costs for development; impact on environment and planet |\n| **Side effects** | Inability to predict or detect side effects beyond statistical measures |\n\n---\n\n## AI Risk Management Challenges (Section 1.2)\n\n| Challenge Area | Key Considerations |\n|----------------|-------------------|\n| **Risk Measurement** | Third-party complications; tracking emergent risks; lack of consensus on metrics; risk varies across lifecycle stages; lab vs. real-world divergences; inscrutability; human baseline comparison difficulties |\n| **Risk Tolerance** | Highly contextual and application-specific; influenced by legal/regulatory requirements; varies by organization; changes over time |\n| **Risk Prioritization** | Not all risks equal; highest-risk contexts demand most urgent prioritization; unacceptable risk levels = cease development/deployment; human-facing vs. non-human-facing systems differ |\n| **Organizational Integration** | AI risks should not be isolated; integrate with enterprise risk management (ERM); align with cybersecurity and privacy frameworks; requires senior leadership commitment and cultural change |\n\n---\n\n## Practical Implementation Guidance\n\n### Key Implementation Principles\n\n1. **Start with GOVERN** — build the organizational foundation before mapping specific systems\n2. **Iterate continuously** — risk management is not one-and-done; cross-reference between functions\n3. **Involve diverse teams** — demographic, disciplinary, experiential diversity throughout the lifecycle\n4. **Document everything** — transparency is foundational to accountability\n5. **Use progressive depth** — not every subcategory applies to every system; prioritize by risk level\n6. **Engage externally** — include feedback from end users, affected communities, domain experts\n7. **Align with existing frameworks** — integrate with CSF, Privacy Framework, RMF, SSDF where relevant\n\n### The AI RMF Playbook\n\n> **Online companion resource** available at: https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework\n\n- Provides **suggested tactical actions** to achieve AI RMF outcomes\n- Voluntary — organizations can use suggestions according to their needs\n- Users can create **tailored guidance** and contribute suggestions\n- Updated **frequently** (semi-annual review cadence)\n- Part of the **NIST Trustworthy and Responsible AI Resource Center**\n\n### Effectiveness Evaluation\n\nOrganizations are encouraged to periodically evaluate whether the AI RMF has improved their ability to manage AI risks. Expected benefits include:\n\n- Enhanced processes for governing, mapping, measuring, and managing AI risk\n- Improved awareness of trustworthiness characteristics tradeoffs\n- Explicit go/no-go decision processes\n- Better information sharing within and across organizations\n- Greater contextual knowledge for downstream risk awareness\n- Strengthened engagement with interested parties\n- Augmented TEVV capacity\n\n### Attributes of the AI RMF (Appendix D)\n\n| # | Attribute |\n|---|-----------|\n| 1 | Risk-based, resource-efficient, pro-innovation, voluntary |\n| 2 | Consensus-driven, developed through open transparent process |\n| 3 | Clear, plain language understandable by broad audience |\n| 4 | Common language and understanding to manage AI risks |\n| 5 | Easily usable; fits with other risk management approaches |\n| 6 | Useful to wide range of perspectives, sectors, technology domains |\n| 7 | Outcome-focused and non-prescriptive |\n| 8 | Fosters awareness of existing standards, guidelines, best practices |\n| 9 | Law- and regulation-agnostic |\n| 10 | Living document — readily updated as technology and understanding evolve |\n\n---\n\n## Relationship to Other NIST Frameworks\n\n| Framework | Relationship to AI RMF |\n|-----------|----------------------|\n| **NIST CSF** (Cybersecurity) | AI RMF references CSF for security/resilience; CSF can't address bias, fairness, explainability |\n| **NIST Privacy Framework** | AI RMF references for privacy-enhanced characteristic; privacy risks from AI data aggregation |\n| **NIST RMF** (Risk Management) | AI RMF aligns with broader enterprise risk management approach |\n| **NIST SSDF** (Secure Software Development) | Referenced for secure development practices |\n| **NIST ARIA** (Assessing Risks and Impacts of AI) | Complements AI RMF; operationalizes the assessment portion through layered testing (model, red team, field) |\n\n> See [[NIST ARIA — Structured Reference]] for detailed ARIA information.\n\n---\n\n## Key Definitions\n\n| Term | Definition |\n|------|------------|\n| **AI System** | Engineered or machine-based system generating outputs such as predictions, recommendations, or decisions influencing real or virtual environments, operating with varying levels of autonomy |\n| **AI Actor** | Those who play an active role in the AI system lifecycle, including organizations and individuals that deploy or operate AI (OECD definition) |\n| **Risk** | Composite measure of an event's probability of occurring and the magnitude/degree of consequences |\n| **Risk Management** | Coordinated activities to direct and control an organization with regard to risk (ISO 31000:2018) |\n| **Risk Tolerance** | Organization's or AI actor's readiness to bear risk to achieve objectives (ISO GUIDE 73) |\n| **Residual Risk** | Risk remaining after risk treatment (ISO GUIDE 73) |\n| **TEVV** | Test, Evaluation, Verification, and Validation — performed throughout the AI lifecycle |\n| **Trustworthiness** | Ability to operate as intended while minimizing risk and promoting confidence; a social concept that ranges across a spectrum |\n| **Responsible AI** | AI practices emphasizing human centricity, social responsibility, and sustainability |\n| **Explainability** | Representation of the mechanisms underlying AI systems' operation (the *how*) |\n| **Interpretability** | Meaning of AI systems' output in the context of their designed functional purposes (the *why*) |\n| **Transparency** | Extent to which information about an AI system and its outputs is available (the *what happened*) |\n\n---\n\n## Exam Connection (AIGP Domain II)\n\n> The AIGP exam tests on NIST AI RMF per **Domain II, Subdomain II.D**: *\"Understand the Main Industry Standards and Tools That Apply to AI.\"*\n\n**What to know for the exam:**\n\n1. ✅ The **four core functions** — Govern, Map, Measure, Manage — and what each does\n2. ✅ That **Govern is cross-cutting** and infused throughout the other three\n3. ✅ The **seven trustworthiness characteristics** and that Valid & Reliable is the foundation\n4. ✅ The **three bias categories** (systemic, computational/statistical, human-cognitive)\n5. ✅ **How AI RMF differs from CSF** — AI RMF handles bias/fairness/ethics/context; CSF handles CIA triad\n6. ✅ The **AI RMF Playbook** as the companion implementation guide\n7. ✅ That the framework is **voluntary, non-sector-specific, use-case agnostic**\n8. ✅ **AI RMF Profiles** (use-case, temporal, current, target, cross-sectoral)\n9. ✅ **Risk response options**: mitigate, transfer, avoid, accept\n10. ✅ Role of **TEVV** throughout the lifecycle\n\n> See [[AI Standards and Frameworks]] for the area-level study notes and [[AIGP Body of Knowledge — Exam Blueprint]] for exam structure.\n\n---\n\n> [!source] Framework Recall Cards — AIGP Study Kit\n\n## NIST AI Risk Management Framework\n\n*Voluntary, US, non-certifiable. Appears constantly.*\n\n### The AI RMF's structure — what the BoK actually asks for\n\nThe BoK asks for the framework **and Playbook**, including **core functions, categories and subcategories**. The structure: **4 functions → 19 categories → 72 subcategories**. AI RMF 1.0 was released January 2023.\n\nThe **Playbook** is a voluntary companion offering **suggested actions, references and related guidance** for achieving the RMF's outcomes at subcategory level. Both the RMF and the Playbook are **voluntary** — there is no certification against either, which is the contrast with ISO/IEC 42001.\n\n### The four AI RMF functions, and what each one does\n\nGOVERN — cross-cutting. Culture, accountability structures, policies, roles, risk tolerance. The other three operate inside it.\nMAP — context and framing. Purpose, stakeholders, intended use, risks identified before building.\nMEASURE — analyze and quantify. Metrics, benchmarks, TEVV, tracking against tolerance.\nMANAGE — act on risk. Prioritize, treat, respond to incidents, recover, communicate.\n\n**Exam tell:** if the scenario is about *deciding what to do* about a known risk (retrain, roll back, accept), it's Manage. If it's about *establishing who decides*, it's Govern.\n\n### Risk treatment options — and the one people forget\n\n**Mitigate, avoid, transfer, accept.** Risk **acceptance is legitimate** — provided the decision is made by the authorized role and documented. Exam distractors often imply accepting risk is always wrong. It isn't; accepting it *silently* is.\n\n### What ARIA is, and how it relates to the AI RMF\n\nNIST's **Assessing Risks and Impacts of AI** program — a research initiative providing methods, metrics and testbeds to operationalize the RMF. It **supplements, it does not replace or supersede** the RMF. That's the trap.\n\n**Scope note:** BoK v2.1 removed ARIA from the outline, so it is unlikely to be tested; recognise the name and move on.", "ref_playbook": "## At a Glance\n\nNIST's online companion to AI RMF 1.0 (NIST AI 100-1). For each of the Core's 72 subcategories, spread over 19 categories in Govern, Map, Measure and Manage, it gives voluntary suggested actions, transparency and documentation prompts, AI transparency resources and references, and tags the AI actors and topics involved. The RMF states the outcome to reach; the Playbook suggests how to reach it.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nExpect the Framework-versus-Playbook split, the Playbook's voluntary, not-a-checklist, no-certification status, Govern as the cross-cutting function, and scenarios that ask which function or subcategory an activity belongs to, such as MAP 1.1 context-setting, MEASURE 2.x trustworthiness testing, or MANAGE 4.x post-deployment monitoring and incident communication.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Companion to AI RMF 1.0: one entry per subcategory, with About, Suggested Actions, Transparency and Documentation, and References sections\n- Voluntary: organizations borrow as many or as few suggestions as fit; it is neither a checklist nor an ordered set of steps, and there is no certification scheme\n- Govern is cross-cutting and infused throughout Map, Measure and Manage; after Govern, most users start with Map and continue to Measure or Manage\n- MEASURE 2 (13 subcategories) lines up with the trustworthiness characteristics, from 2.5 valid and reliable to 2.11 fairness and bias\n- AI Actor tags follow RMF Appendix A: TEVV on every MEASURE subcategory; AI Deployment and Operation and Monitoring on every MANAGE subcategory\n- Living resource: feedback to AIframework@nist.gov and updates about twice a year; as of 2026 NIST says the next update follows the AI RMF 1.0 revision\n- Published as web pages plus PDF, CSV, Excel and JSON downloads from airc.nist.gov\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] NIST AI RMF Playbook — Structured Reference\n\n# NIST AI RMF Playbook — Structured Reference\n\n> **Status:** Voluntary companion to AI RMF 1.0 (NIST AI 100-1); no certification scheme. **Maintained by:** NIST, in the Trustworthy and Responsible AI Resource Center (airc.nist.gov). **Updates:** about twice a year; NIST says the next update follows the AI RMF 1.0 revision under way in 2026. **Data:** playbook.json retrieved 2026-09-23, 72 subcategory entries. **BOK:** v2.1 II.D.\n\n## 1. Overview\n\nThe **NIST AI RMF Playbook** is NIST's online companion to **NIST AI 100-1, the AI Risk Management Framework (AI RMF 1.0, January 2023)**. The Framework's Core states *outcomes*: four functions (GOVERN, MAP, MEASURE, MANAGE) divided into 19 categories and 72 subcategories (Tables 1–4 of AI RMF 1.0). The Playbook supplies the *how*. NIST says it \"provides suggested actions for achieving the outcomes laid out in the AI Risk Management Framework (AI RMF) Core\", with suggestions aligned to each subcategory. The RMF points readers there too: for each function it says the related practices \"are described in the NIST AI RMF Playbook\". Both belong to NIST's Trustworthy and Responsible AI Resource Center (AIRC, airc.nist.gov).\n\n| | AI RMF 1.0 (NIST AI 100-1) | AI RMF Playbook |\n|---|---|---|\n| What it is | The framework: Part 1 foundational information, Part 2 the Core and Profiles | Online companion, one entry per Core subcategory |\n| What it gives you | Outcome statements (functions, categories, subcategories), trustworthiness characteristics, profiles | Suggested actions, transparency and documentation prompts, AI transparency resources, references, plus AI Actor and Topic tags |\n| Status | Voluntary, rights-preserving, non-sector-specific, use-case agnostic | Voluntary; \"neither a checklist nor set of steps to be followed in its entirety\" |\n| Updates | Living document; formal community review expected no later than 2028; being revised as of 2026 | Updates \"approximately twice per year\"; next update after the AI RMF is revised |\n| Format | Versioned publication (PDF) | Web pages by function, plus PDF, CSV, Excel and JSON downloads |\n\n**Status points a question writer can use**\n\n- **Voluntary.** \"Playbook suggestions are voluntary.\" NIST says organizations can borrow \"as many – or as few – suggestions as apply to their industry use case or interests\". The same applies to the Framework: \"The AI RMF and the Playbook are intended for voluntary use.\"\n- **Not a checklist or a sequence.** The Playbook FAQ says users are not expected to implement every suggestion or to work through them as an ordered series of steps. AI RMF 1.0 says the same of the Core: its actions \"do not constitute a checklist, nor are they necessarily an ordered set of steps\".\n- **No certification.** Neither the RMF nor the Playbook sets up a certification or conformity-assessment scheme. (The \"certifications\" in MAP 3.4 are proficiency standards for system operators, not certification of the organization.)\n- **Living resource.** Comments go to AIframework@nist.gov. AI RMF 1.0 says they are \"reviewed and integrated on a semi-annual basis\", and the Playbook page says updates are released about twice a year.\n- **As of September 2026.** NIST says \"The AI RMF 1.0 is being revised as part of the White House AI Action Plan\" and \"The Playbook will be updated after the AI RMF is revised.\" NIST's March 2026 AI update lists the Action Plan task to \"revise the NIST AI Risk Management Framework to eliminate references to misinformation, Diversity, Equity, and Inclusion, and climate change\". In the current Playbook, the entries closest to those themes are GOVERN 3 (workforce diversity, equity, inclusion and accessibility), MAP 1.2 (demographically diverse, interdisciplinary teams) and MEASURE 2.12 (environmental impact); no entry mentions misinformation. For the exam (BOK v2.1), learn AI RMF 1.0 and the Playbook as published.\n\nFor the Framework itself (trustworthiness characteristics, lifecycle, profiles, the full tables) see [[NIST AI RMF — Structured Reference]]. This note adds what the Playbook says to *do* for each subcategory.\n\n## 2. Anatomy of a Playbook entry\n\nEach subcategory has one entry. The first four parts below are the section headings on the AIRC web pages; AI Actors and Topic are tags carried in the JSON export.\n\n| Part | JSON field | What it holds | In this note |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| About | `section_about` | Background on the outcome and why it matters | Left out (long prose) |\n| Suggested Actions | `section_actions` | Voluntary steps toward the outcome | First 4–6 top-level bullets |\n| Transparency and Documentation: \"Organizations can document the following\" | `section_doc` | Questions to answer when documenting the outcome | 2–3 per subcategory |\n| Transparency and Documentation: \"AI Transparency Resources\" | `section_doc` | Documentation frameworks and checklists | Summarized below |\n| References | `section_ref` | Research, guidance and regulatory sources | Left out |\n| AI Actors (tag) | `AI Actors` | Appendix A actor groups most involved | Kept |\n| Topic (tag) | `Topic` | Subject tags such as Third-party or AI Incidents | Kept |\n\nMost often listed as AI Transparency Resources (number of entries): GAO-21-519SP: AI Accountability Framework for Federal Agencies & Other Entities (63); Intel.gov: AI Ethics Framework for Intelligence Community - 2020 (39); WEF Companion to the Model AI Governance Framework- 2020 (20); WEF Model AI Governance Framework Assessment 2020 (17).\n\n**How this note was built.**\n\n- Generated by `build_playbook_note.py` from playbook.json (72 entries; SHA-256 `aecbee3d3c88…`, retrieved 2026-09-23). Rerun it when NIST updates the Playbook.\n- Subcategory statements are verbatim from the Playbook. Category statements are verbatim from AI RMF 1.0 Tables 1–4.\n- Suggested actions: the first 4–6 top-level bullets in Playbook order, with at most 5 sub-bullets each. HTML and markdown artifacts are removed; NIST's wording, including its occasional typos, is otherwise unchanged. The count shows how many the Playbook lists.\n- Documentation prompts: the first 2–3 distinct questions in Playbook order, trimmed of commentary after the last question mark.\n- AI Actors and Topics are the Playbook's tags. \"all seven\" stands for the seven trustworthiness-characteristic topic tags.\n\n**Data notes (generated).**\n\n- No AI Actor tags in playbook.json for: MAP 1.1, MAP 1.2, MAP 1.3, MAP 1.4, MAP 1.5, MAP 1.6, MAP 2.1, MAP 2.2.\n- Tagged as an actor but not an AI RMF actor group, so shown under Topics instead: 'Fairness and Bias' (GOVERN 4.3).\n- Duplicate actor tag removed: MAP 3.2 (AI Design).\n- A suggested action phrased as a question (really a documentation prompt) was skipped in: MANAGE 3.1.\n- Statement wording differs in substance from AI RMF 1.0, so the RMF text is shown underneath: GOVERN 1.2, GOVERN 1.3, GOVERN 5.2, MANAGE 1.2, MEASURE 1.2, MEASURE 2.6.\n\n## 3. Functions, categories and subcategories\n\n| Function | Categories | Subcategories | Most-tagged AI Actors |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| GOVERN | 6 | 19 | Governance and Oversight (13), Operation and Monitoring (8) |\n| MAP | 5 | 18 | Operation and Monitoring (7), AI Design (6) |\n| MEASURE | 4 | 22 | TEVV (22), AI Deployment (16) |\n| MANAGE | 4 | 13 | AI Deployment (13), Operation and Monitoring (13) |\n| **Total** | **19** | **72** | |\n\n| Function | Category | Focus | Subcategories | Count |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| GOVERN | GOVERN 1 | Policies, processes, procedures and practices | GOVERN 1.1–1.7 | 7 |\n| GOVERN | GOVERN 2 | Accountability structures and training | GOVERN 2.1–2.3 | 3 |\n| GOVERN | GOVERN 3 | Workforce diversity, equity, inclusion and accessibility | GOVERN 3.1–3.2 | 2 |\n| GOVERN | GOVERN 4 | Risk-aware culture and communication | GOVERN 4.1–4.3 | 3 |\n| GOVERN | GOVERN 5 | Engagement with relevant AI actors | GOVERN 5.1–5.2 | 2 |\n| GOVERN | GOVERN 6 | Third-party software, data and supply chain | GOVERN 6.1–6.2 | 2 |\n| MAP | MAP 1 | Context established and understood | MAP 1.1–1.6 | 6 |\n| MAP | MAP 2 | System categorization | MAP 2.1–2.3 | 3 |\n| MAP | MAP 3 | Capabilities, usage, benefits and costs | MAP 3.1–3.5 | 5 |\n| MAP | MAP 4 | Risks and benefits of all components, incl. third-party | MAP 4.1–4.2 | 2 |\n| MAP | MAP 5 | Impacts characterized | MAP 5.1–5.2 | 2 |\n| MEASURE | MEASURE 1 | Methods and metrics | MEASURE 1.1–1.3 | 3 |\n| MEASURE | MEASURE 2 | Evaluation for trustworthy characteristics | MEASURE 2.1–2.13 | 13 |\n| MEASURE | MEASURE 3 | Tracking risks over time | MEASURE 3.1–3.3 | 3 |\n| MEASURE | MEASURE 4 | Feedback on measurement efficacy | MEASURE 4.1–4.3 | 3 |\n| MANAGE | MANAGE 1 | Prioritize and respond to assessed risks | MANAGE 1.1–1.4 | 4 |\n| MANAGE | MANAGE 2 | Maximize benefits, minimize negative impacts | MANAGE 2.1–2.4 | 4 |\n| MANAGE | MANAGE 3 | Third-party risks and benefits | MANAGE 3.1–3.2 | 2 |\n| MANAGE | MANAGE 4 | Risk treatment, monitoring and communication plans | MANAGE 4.1–4.3 | 3 |\n\n## 4. AI Actors in the Playbook\n\nAI RMF 1.0 adopts the OECD definition of AI actors: \"those who play an active role in the AI system lifecycle, including organizations and individuals that deploy or operate AI\". Appendix A describes their tasks. The Playbook tags each subcategory with the groups most involved; counts below are subcategories tagged.\n\n| AI Actor (Playbook tag) | Role (AI RMF Appendix A, paraphrased) | GOVERN | MAP | MEASURE | MANAGE | Total |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Operation and Monitoring | Operate the system and regularly assess its outputs and impacts | 8 | 7 | 15 | 13 | 43 |\n| AI Deployment | Put the system into use: piloting, legacy compatibility, regulatory compliance, organizational change, user experience | 4 | 4 | 16 | 13 | 37 |\n| TEVV | Test, evaluate, verify and validate across the lifecycle; kept apart from model builders as a best practice | 2 | 2 | 22 | 1 | 27 |\n| AI Impact Assessment | Assess accountability, harmful bias, impacts, product safety, liability and security | 2 | 4 | 11 | 6 | 23 |\n| Domain Experts | Sector or application expertise that guides design and helps interpret outputs | – | 4 | 12 | 2 | 18 |\n| Governance and Oversight | Management, fiduciary and legal authority: organizational management, senior leadership, the board | 13 | 1 | – | 3 | 17 |\n| End-Users | Individuals or groups using the system for specific purposes | – | 4 | 8 | 3 | 15 |\n| AI Development | Build, select, calibrate, train and test models | 2 | 6 | 4 | 1 | 13 |\n| AI Design | Set the concept, objectives, assumptions and requirements; plan and collect data | 5 | 6 | – | 1 | 12 |\n| Affected Individuals and Communities | Anyone directly or indirectly affected, whether or not they interact with the system | 1 | 2 | 6 | 3 | 12 |\n| Human Factors | Human-centred design, end-user involvement, context of use, UX evaluation | – | 4 | 1 | 2 | 7 |\n| Third-party entities | External providers, developers, vendors and evaluators of data, models or systems | 2 | 2 | – | 2 | 6 |\n| Procurement | Acquire AI models, products or services from third parties | 1 | 1 | – | – | 2 |\n\n## 5. Topic finder\n\nWhere each Playbook topic tag appears. Use it to answer \"which subcategory covers X?\"\n\n| Topic tag | Entries | Subcategories |\n|---|---|---|\n| TEVV | 21 | MAP 1.1, 2.3 · MEASURE 1.1–1.2, 2.1, 2.3–2.13, 3.1, 3.3, 4.1–4.3 |\n| Governance | 19 | all GOVERN |\n| Impact Assessment | 16 | GOVERN 4.2–4.3, 5.1–5.2 · MAP 1.1, 1.6, 2.2–2.3, 3.2, 5.1–5.2 · MEASURE 1.2–1.3, 2.3, 3.3 · MANAGE 4.2 |\n| Trustworthy Characteristics | 13 | GOVERN 1.2 · MAP 1.1, 3.2 · MEASURE 1.1, 2.5–2.11, 4.2–4.3 |\n| Participation | 10 | GOVERN 5.1–5.2 · MAP 5.1–5.2 · MEASURE 1.3, 3.3, 4.1–4.3 · MANAGE 4.1 |\n| Fairness and Bias | 8 | GOVERN 1.2, 4.3 · MAP 1.1, 3.2 · MEASURE 1.1, 2.11, 4.2–4.3 |\n| Monitoring | 8 | GOVERN 1.5 · MEASURE 2.4, 3.1–3.2 · MANAGE 3.2, 4.1–4.3 |\n| Validity and Reliability | 8 | GOVERN 1.2 · MAP 1.1, 3.2 · MEASURE 1.1, 2.1, 2.5, 4.2–4.3 |\n| Accountability and Transparency | 7 | GOVERN 1.2 · MAP 1.1, 3.2 · MEASURE 1.1, 2.8, 4.2–4.3 |\n| Context of Use | 7 | MAP 1.1, 1.4, 3.3 · MEASURE 1.2–1.3, 2.6, 4.1 |\n| Documentation | 7 | GOVERN 1.4, 1.6 · MAP 1.6, 2.2, 3.1, 3.3 · MEASURE 2.1 |\n| Explainability and Interpretability | 7 | GOVERN 1.2 · MAP 1.1, 3.2 · MEASURE 1.1, 2.9, 4.2–4.3 |\n| Privacy | 7 | GOVERN 1.2 · MAP 1.1, 3.2 · MEASURE 1.1, 2.10, 4.2–4.3 |\n| Risk Tolerance | 7 | GOVERN 1.3, 2.3 · MAP 1.5, 4.1 · MANAGE 1.2–1.3, 2.1 |\n| Safety | 7 | GOVERN 1.2 · MAP 1.1, 3.2 · MEASURE 1.1, 2.6, 4.2–4.3 |\n| Secure and Resilient | 7 | GOVERN 1.2 · MAP 1.1, 3.2 · MEASURE 1.1, 2.7, 4.2–4.3 |\n| Socio-technical systems | 6 | MAP 1.1–1.3, 1.6, 2.1, 3.1 |\n| Risky Emergent Behavior | 5 | MAP 4.1 · MEASURE 1.1, 2.7 · MANAGE 2.4, 4.1 |\n| Third-party | 5 | GOVERN 6.1–6.2 · MAP 4.1–4.2 · MANAGE 3.1 |\n| Continual Improvement | 4 | GOVERN 1.5 · MEASURE 3.1–3.2 · MANAGE 4.2 |\n| Data | 4 | GOVERN 1.6 · MAP 2.3 · MEASURE 2.2, 2.5 |\n| Legal and Regulatory | 4 | GOVERN 1.1, 6.1 · MAP 4.1 · MANAGE 1.3 |\n| Risk Culture | 4 | GOVERN 2.1, 4.1–4.3 |\n| Risk Response | 4 | MANAGE 1.4, 2.3–2.4, 4.1 |\n| Supply Chain | 4 | GOVERN 6.1–6.2 · MAP 4.1 · MANAGE 3.1 |\n| AI Actor Training | 3 | GOVERN 1.1, 2.2, 3.2 |\n| AI Deployment | 3 | MANAGE 1.1, 2.2, 4.1 |\n| AI Incidents | 3 | GOVERN 4.3 · MANAGE 4.1, 4.3 |\n| Adversarial | 3 | GOVERN 4.1 · MEASURE 2.7 · MANAGE 4.1 |\n| Drift | 3 | GOVERN 4.3 · MEASURE 2.4 · MANAGE 2.2 |\n| Human oversight | 3 | GOVERN 3.2 · MAP 2.2, 3.5 |\n| Pre-trained models | 3 | MAP 4.1–4.2 · MANAGE 3.2 |\n| Risk Assessment | 3 | MEASURE 1.1 · MANAGE 1.1, 4.2 |\n| Risk Management | 3 | GOVERN 1.4, 1.6, 6.2 |\n| Societal Values | 3 | MAP 1.1, 1.3 · MANAGE 2.2 |\n| Decommission | 2 | GOVERN 1.7 · MANAGE 2.4 |\n| Diversity | 2 | GOVERN 3.1 · MAP 1.2 |\n| Human-AI teaming | 2 | GOVERN 3.2 · MAP 3.4 |\n| Interdisciplinarity | 2 | GOVERN 3.1 · MAP 1.2 |\n| Limitations | 2 | MAP 2.2–2.3 |\n| Contestability | 1 | MEASURE 3.3 |\n| Effectiveness | 1 | MEASURE 2.13 |\n| Environmental Impact | 1 | MEASURE 2.12 |\n| Human Subjects Protection | 1 | MEASURE 2.2 |\n| Procurement | 1 | GOVERN 6.1 |\n| Trade-offs | 1 | MANAGE 2.1 |\n\n## 6. GOVERN (6 categories, 19 subcategories)\n\nCross-cutting. GOVERN builds the culture, policies, accountability structures and processes for AI risk management. AI RMF 1.0 calls it \"a cross-cutting function to inform and be infused throughout the other three functions\". It applies to all stages of an organization's AI risk management, not to one system, and it keeps running as knowledge, culture and expectations change.\n\n### GOVERN 1 — Policies, processes, procedures and practices\n\n*AI RMF 1.0 category statement:* Policies, processes, procedures, and practices across the organization related to the mapping, measuring, and managing of AI risks are in place, transparent, and implemented effectively.\n\n#### GOVERN 1.1\n\n> Legal and regulatory requirements involving AI are understood, managed, and documented.\n\n*AI Actors:* Governance and Oversight · *Topics:* Legal and Regulatory, Governance, AI Actor Training\n\n**Suggested actions** (3 of 3):\n- Maintain awareness of the applicable legal and regulatory considerations and requirements specific to industry, sector, and business purpose, as well as the application context of the deployed AI system.\n- Align risk management efforts with applicable legal standards.\n- Maintain policies for training (and re-training) organizational staff about necessary legal or regulatory considerations that may impact AI-related design, development and deployment activities.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- To what extent has the entity defined and documented the regulatory environment—including minimum requirements in laws and regulations?\n- Has the system been reviewed for its compliance to applicable laws, regulations, standards, and guidance?\n\n#### GOVERN 1.2\n\n> The characteristics of trustworthy AI are integrated into organizational policies, processes, and procedures.\n>\n> *AI RMF 1.0 wording:* The characteristics of trustworthy AI are integrated into organizational policies, processes, procedures, and practices.\n\n*AI Actors:* Governance and Oversight · *Topics:* Trustworthy Characteristics (all seven), Governance\n\n**Suggested actions** (first 6 of 15): *Organizational AI risk management policies should be designed to:*\n- Define key terms and concepts related to AI systems and the scope of their purposes and intended uses.\n- Connect AI governance to existing organizational governance and risk controls.\n- Align to broader data governance policies and practices, particularly the use of sensitive or otherwise risky data.\n- Detail standards for experimental design, data quality, and model training.\n- Outline and document risk mapping and measurement processes and standards.\n- Detail model testing and validation processes.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- To what extent do these policies foster public trust and confidence in the use of the AI system?\n- What policies has the entity developed to ensure the use of the AI system is consistent with its stated values and principles?\n- What policies and documentation has the entity developed to encourage the use of its AI system as intended?\n\n#### GOVERN 1.3\n\n> Processes and procedures are in place to determine the needed level of risk management activities based on the organization's risk tolerance.\n>\n> *AI RMF 1.0 wording:* Processes, procedures, and practices are in place to determine the needed level of risk management activities based on the organization’s risk tolerance.\n\n*AI Actors:* Governance and Oversight · *Topics:* Risk Tolerance, Governance\n\n**Suggested actions** (5 of 5):\n- Establish policies to define mechanisms for measuring or understanding an AI system’s potential impacts, e.g., via regular impact assessments at key stages in the AI lifecycle, connected to system impacts and frequency of system updates.\n- Establish policies to define mechanisms for measuring or understanding the likelihood of an AI system’s impacts and their magnitude at key stages in the AI lifecycle.\n- Establish policies that define assessment scales for measuring potential AI system impact. Scales may be qualitative, such as red-amber-green (RAG), or may entail simulations or econometric approaches.\n- Establish policies for assigning an overall risk measurement approach for an AI system, or its important components, e.g., via multiplication or combination of a mapped risk’s impact and likelihood (risk ≈ impact x likelihood).\n- Establish policies to assign systems to uniform risk scales that are valid across the organization’s AI portfolio (e.g. documentation templates), and acknowledge risk tolerance and risk levels may change over the lifecycle of an AI system.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- How do system performance metrics inform risk tolerance decisions?\n- What policies has the entity developed to ensure the use of the AI system is consistent with organizational risk tolerance?\n- How do the entity’s data security and privacy assessments inform risk tolerance decisions?\n\n#### GOVERN 1.4\n\n> The risk management process and its outcomes are established through transparent policies, procedures, and other controls based on organizational risk priorities.\n\n*AI Actors:* Governance and Oversight · *Topics:* Risk Management, Governance, Documentation\n\n**Suggested actions** (first 6 of 7):\n- Establish and regularly review documentation policies that, among others, address information related to:\n    - AI actors contact informations\n    - Business justification\n    - Scope and usages\n    - Expected and potential risks and impacts\n    - Assumptions and limitations\n    - *…and 8 more*\n- Verify documentation policies for AI systems are standardized across the organization and remain current.\n- Establish policies for a model documentation inventory system and regularly review its completeness, usability, and efficacy.\n- Establish mechanisms to regularly review the efficacy of risk management processes.\n- Identify AI actors responsible for evaluating efficacy of risk management processes and approaches, and for course-correction based on results.\n- Establish policies and processes regarding public disclosure of the use of AI and risk management material such as impact assessments, audits, model documentation and validation and testing results.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- To what extent has the entity clarified the roles, responsibilities, and delegated authorities to relevant stakeholders?\n- What are the roles, responsibilities, and delegation of authorities of personnel involved in the design, development, deployment, assessment and monitoring of the AI system?\n\n#### GOVERN 1.5\n\n> Ongoing monitoring and periodic review of the risk management process and its outcomes are planned, organizational roles and responsibilities are clearly defined, including determining the frequency of periodic review.\n\n*AI Actors:* Governance and Oversight, Operation and Monitoring · *Topics:* Monitoring, Governance, Continual Improvement\n\n**Suggested actions** (first 6 of 7):\n- Establish policies to allocate appropriate resources and capacity for assessing impacts of AI systems on individuals, communities and society.\n- Establish policies and procedures for monitoring and addressing AI system performance and trustworthiness, including bias and security problems, across the lifecycle of the system.\n- Establish policies for AI system incident response, or confirm that existing incident response policies apply to AI systems.\n- Establish policies to define organizational functions and personnel responsible for AI system monitoring and incident response activities.\n- Establish mechanisms to enable the sharing of feedback from impacted individuals or communities about negative impacts from AI systems.\n- Establish mechanisms to provide recourse for impacted individuals or communities to contest problematic AI system outcomes.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- To what extent does the system/entity consistently measure progress towards stated goals and objectives?\n- Did your organization implement a risk management system to address risks involved in deploying the identified AI solution (e.g. personnel risk or changes to commercial objectives)?\n- Did your organization address usability problems and test whether user interfaces served their intended purposes?\n\n#### GOVERN 1.6\n\n> Mechanisms are in place to inventory AI systems and are resourced according to organizational risk priorities.\n\n*AI Actors:* Governance and Oversight · *Topics:* Risk Management, Governance, Data, Documentation\n\n**Suggested actions** (4 of 4):\n- Establish policies that define the creation and maintenance of AI system inventories.\n- Establish policies that define a specific individual or team that is responsible for maintaining the inventory.\n- Establish policies that define which models or systems are inventoried, with preference to inventorying all models or systems, or minimally, to high risk models or systems, or systems deployed in high-stakes settings.\n- Establish policies that define model or system attributes to be inventoried, e.g, documentation, links to source code, incident response plans, data dictionaries, AI actor contact information.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- Who is responsible for documenting and maintaining the AI system inventory details?\n- What processes exist for data generation, acquisition/collection, ingestion, staging/storage, transformations, security, maintenance, and dissemination?\n- Given the purpose of this AI, what is an appropriate interval for checking whether it is still accurate, unbiased, explainable, etc.? What are the checks for this model?\n\n#### GOVERN 1.7\n\n> Processes and procedures are in place for decommissioning and phasing out of AI systems safely and in a manner that does not increase risks or decrease the organization’s trustworthiness.\n\n*AI Actors:* AI Deployment, Operation and Monitoring · *Topics:* Decommission, Governance\n\n**Suggested actions** (4 of 4):\n- Establish policies for decommissioning AI systems. Such policies typically address:\n    - User and community concerns, and reputational risks.\n    - Business continuity and financial risks.\n    - Up and downstream system dependencies.\n    - Regulatory requirements (e.g., data retention).\n    - Potential future legal, regulatory, security or forensic investigations.\n    - *…and 1 more*\n- Establish policies that delineate where and for how long decommissioned systems, models and related artifacts are stored.\n- Establish practices to track accountability and consider how decommission and other adaptations or changes in system deployment contribute to downstream impacts for individuals, groups and communities.\n- Establish policies that address ancillary data or artifacts that must be preserved for fulsome understanding or execution of the decommissioned AI system, e.g., predictions, explanations, intermediate input feature representations, usernames and passwords, etc.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- What processes exist for data generation, acquisition/collection, ingestion, staging/storage, transformations, security, maintenance, and dissemination?\n- To what extent do these policies foster public trust and confidence in the use of the AI system?\n\n### GOVERN 2 — Accountability structures and training\n\n*AI RMF 1.0 category statement:* Accountability structures are in place so that the appropriate teams and individuals are empowered, responsible, and trained for mapping, measuring, and managing AI risks.\n\n#### GOVERN 2.1\n\n> Roles and responsibilities and lines of communication related to mapping, measuring, and managing AI risks are documented and are clear to individuals and teams throughout the organization.\n\n*AI Actors:* Governance and Oversight · *Topics:* Governance, Risk Culture\n\n**Suggested actions** (6 of 6):\n- Establish policies that define the AI risk management roles and responsibilities for positions directly and indirectly related to AI systems, including, but not limited to\n    - Boards of directors or advisory committees\n    - Senior management\n    - AI audit functions\n    - Product management\n    - Project management\n    - *…and 7 more*\n- Establish policies that promote regular communication among AI actors participating in AI risk management efforts.\n- Establish policies that separate management of AI system development functions from AI system testing functions, to enable independent course-correction of AI systems.\n- Establish policies to identify, increase the transparency of, and prevent conflicts of interest in AI risk management efforts.\n- Establish policies to counteract confirmation bias and market incentives that may hinder AI risk management efforts.\n- Establish policies that incentivize AI actors to collaborate with existing legal, oversight, compliance, or enterprise risk functions in their AI risk management activities.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- To what extent has the entity clarified the roles, responsibilities, and delegated authorities to relevant stakeholders?\n- Who is ultimately responsible for the decisions of the AI and is this person aware of the intended uses and limitations of the analytic?\n- Are the responsibilities of the personnel involved in the various AI governance processes clearly defined?\n\n#### GOVERN 2.2\n\n> The organization’s personnel and partners receive AI risk management training to enable them to perform their duties and responsibilities consistent with related policies, procedures, and agreements.\n\n*AI Actors:* Governance and Oversight · *Topics:* Governance, AI Actor Training\n\n**Suggested actions** (6 of 6):\n- Establish policies for personnel addressing ongoing education about:\n    - Applicable laws and regulations for AI systems.\n    - Potential negative impacts that may arise from AI systems.\n    - Organizational AI policies.\n    - Trustworthy AI characteristics.\n- Ensure that trainings are suitable across AI actor sub-groups - for AI actors carrying out technical tasks (e.g., developers, operators, etc.) as compared to AI actors in oversight roles (e.g., legal, compliance, audit, etc.).\n- Ensure that trainings comprehensively address technical and socio-technical aspects of AI risk management.\n- Verify that organizational AI policies include mechanisms for internal AI personnel to acknowledge and commit to their roles and responsibilities.\n- Verify that organizational policies address change management and include mechanisms to communicate and acknowledge substantial AI system changes.\n- Define paths along internal and external chains of accountability to escalate risk concerns.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- Are the relevant staff dealing with AI systems properly trained to interpret AI model output and decisions as well as to detect and manage bias in data?\n- How does the entity determine the necessary skills and experience needed to design, develop, deploy, assess, and monitor the AI system?\n\n#### GOVERN 2.3\n\n> Executive leadership of the organization takes responsibility for decisions about risks associated with AI system development and deployment.\n\n*AI Actors:* Governance and Oversight · *Topics:* Governance, Risk Tolerance\n\n**Suggested actions** (2 of 2):\n- Organizational management can:\n    - Declare risk tolerances for developing or using AI systems.\n    - Support AI risk management efforts, and play an active role in such efforts.\n    - Integrate a risk and harm prevention mindset throughout the AI lifecycle as part of organizational culture\n    - Support competent risk management executives.\n    - Delegate the power, resources, and authorization to perform risk management to each appropriate level throughout the management chain.\n- Organizations can establish board committees for AI risk management and oversight functions and integrate those functions within the organization’s broader enterprise risk management approaches.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- Did your organization’s board and/or senior management sponsor, support and participate in your organization’s AI governance?\n- What are the roles, responsibilities, and delegation of authorities of personnel involved in the design, development, deployment, assessment and monitoring of the AI system?\n\n### GOVERN 3 — Workforce diversity, equity, inclusion and accessibility\n\n*AI RMF 1.0 category statement:* Workforce diversity, equity, inclusion, and accessibility processes are prioritized in the mapping, measuring, and managing of AI risks throughout the lifecycle.\n\n#### GOVERN 3.1\n\n> Decision-makings related to mapping, measuring, and managing AI risks throughout the lifecycle is informed by a diverse team (e.g., diversity of demographics, disciplines, experience, expertise, and backgrounds).\n\n*AI Actors:* Governance and Oversight, AI Design · *Topics:* Diversity, Interdisciplinarity, Governance\n\n**Suggested actions** (5 of 5): *Organizational management can:*\n- Define policies and hiring practices at the outset that promote interdisciplinary roles, competencies, skills, and capacity for AI efforts.\n- Define policies and hiring practices that lead to demographic and domain expertise diversity; empower staff with necessary resources and support, and facilitate the contribution of staff feedback and concerns without fear of reprisal.\n- Establish policies that facilitate inclusivity and the integration of new insights into existing practice.\n- Seek external expertise to supplement organizational diversity, equity, inclusion, and accessibility where internal expertise is lacking.\n- Establish policies that incentivize AI actors to collaborate with existing nondiscrimination, accessibility and accommodation, and human resource functions, employee resource group (ERGs), and diversity, equity, inclusion, and accessibility (DEIA) initiatives.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- Are the relevant staff dealing with AI systems properly trained to interpret AI model output and decisions as well as to detect and manage bias in data?\n- To what extent are the established procedures effective in mitigating bias, inequity, and other concerns resulting from the system?\n\n#### GOVERN 3.2\n\n> Policies and procedures are in place to define and differentiate roles and responsibilities for human-AI configurations and oversight of AI systems.\n\n*AI Actors:* AI Design · *Topics:* Human-AI teaming, Human oversight, Governance, AI Actor Training\n\n**Suggested actions** (first 6 of 8):\n- Establish policies and procedures that define and differentiate the various human roles and responsibilities when using, interacting with, or monitoring AI systems.\n- Establish procedures for capturing and tracking risk information related to human-AI configurations and associated outcomes.\n- Establish policies for the development of proficiency standards for AI actors carrying out system operation tasks and system oversight tasks.\n- Establish specified risk management training protocols for AI actors carrying out system operation tasks and system oversight tasks.\n- Establish policies and procedures regarding AI actor roles, and responsibilities for human oversight of deployed systems.\n- Establish policies and procedures defining human-AI configurations (configurations where AI systems are explicitly designated and treated as team members in primarily human teams) in relation to organizational risk tolerances, and associated documentation.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- What type of information is accessible on the design, operations, and limitations of the AI system to external stakeholders, including end users, consumers, regulators, and individuals impacted by use of the AI system?\n- To what extent has the entity documented the appropriate level of human involvement in AI-augmented decision-making?\n\n### GOVERN 4 — Risk-aware culture and communication\n\n*AI RMF 1.0 category statement:* Organizational teams are committed to a culture that considers and communicates AI risk.\n\n#### GOVERN 4.1\n\n> Organizational policies, and practices are in place to foster a critical thinking and safety-first mindset in the design, development, deployment, and uses of AI systems to minimize negative impacts.\n\n*AI Actors:* AI Design, AI Development, AI Deployment, Operation and Monitoring · *Topics:* Risk Culture, Governance, Adversarial\n\n**Suggested actions** (5 of 5):\n- Establish policies that require inclusion of oversight functions (legal, compliance, risk management) from the outset of the system design process.\n- Establish policies that promote effective challenge of AI system design, implementation, and deployment decisions, via mechanisms such as the three lines of defense, model audits, or red-teaming – to minimize workplace risks such as groupthink.\n- Establish policies that incentivize safety-first mindset and general critical thinking and review at an organizational and procedural level.\n- Establish whistleblower protections for insiders who report on perceived serious problems with AI systems.\n- Establish policies to integrate a harm and risk prevention mindset throughout the AI lifecycle.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- To what extent has the entity documented the AI system’s development, testing methodology, metrics, and performance outcomes?\n- Are organizational information sharing practices widely followed and transparent, such that related past failed designs can be avoided?\n- Are training manuals and other resources for carrying out incident response documented and available?\n\n#### GOVERN 4.2\n\n> Organizational teams document the risks and potential impacts of the AI technology they design, develop, deploy, evaluate and use, and communicate about the impacts more broadly.\n\n*AI Actors:* AI Design, AI Development, AI Deployment, Operation and Monitoring · *Topics:* Risk Culture, Governance, Impact Assessment\n\n**Suggested actions** (4 of 4):\n- Establish impact assessment policies and processes for AI systems used by the organization.\n- Align organizational impact assessment activities with relevant regulatory or legal requirements.\n- Verify that impact assessment activities are appropriate to evaluate the potential negative impact of a system and how quickly a system changes, and that assessments are applied on a regular basis.\n- Utilize impact assessments to inform broader evaluations of AI system risk.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- How has the entity identified and mitigated potential impacts of bias in the data, including inequitable or discriminatory outcomes?\n- How has the entity documented the AI system’s data provenance, including sources, origins, transformations, augmentations, labels, dependencies, constraints, and metadata?\n- To what extent has the entity clearly defined technical specifications and requirements for the AI system?\n\n#### GOVERN 4.3\n\n> Organizational practices are in place to enable AI testing, identification of incidents, and information sharing.\n\n*AI Actors:* TEVV, Operation and Monitoring, Governance and Oversight · *Topics:* Risk Culture, Governance, AI Incidents, Impact Assessment, Drift, Fairness and Bias\n\n**Suggested actions** (5 of 5):\n- Establish policies and procedures to facilitate and equip AI system testing.\n- Establish organizational commitment to identifying AI system limitations and sharing of insights about limitations within appropriate AI actor groups.\n- Establish policies for reporting and documenting incident response.\n- Establish policies and processes regarding public disclosure of incidents and information sharing.\n- Establish guidelines for incident handling related to AI system risks and performance.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- Did your organization address usability problems and test whether user interfaces served their intended purposes?\n- Did your organization implement a risk management system to address risks involved in deploying the identified AI solution (e.g. personnel risk or changes to commercial objectives)?\n- To what extent can users or parties affected by the outputs of the AI system test the AI system and provide feedback?\n\n### GOVERN 5 — Engagement with relevant AI actors\n\n*AI RMF 1.0 category statement:* Processes are in place for robust engagement with relevant AI actors.\n\n#### GOVERN 5.1\n\n> Organizational policies and practices are in place to collect, consider, prioritize, and integrate feedback from those external to the team that developed or deployed the AI system regarding the potential individual and societal impacts related to AI risks.\n\n*AI Actors:* AI Design, Governance and Oversight, AI Impact Assessment, Affected Individuals and Communities · *Topics:* Participation, Governance, Impact Assessment\n\n**Suggested actions** (3 of 3):\n- Establish AI risk management policies that explicitly address mechanisms for collecting, evaluating, and incorporating stakeholder and user feedback that could include:\n    - Recourse mechanisms for faulty AI system outputs.\n    - Bug bounties.\n    - Human-centered design.\n    - User-interaction and experience research.\n    - Participatory stakeholder engagement with individuals and communities that may experience negative impacts.\n- Verify that stakeholder feedback is considered and addressed, including environmental concerns, and across the entire population of intended users, including historically excluded populations, people with disabilities, older people, and those with limited access to the internet and other basic technologies.\n- Clarify the organization’s principles as they apply to AI systems – considering those which have been proposed publicly – to inform external stakeholders of the organization’s values. Consider publishing or adopting AI principles.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- What type of information is accessible on the design, operations, and limitations of the AI system to external stakeholders, including end users, consumers, regulators, and individuals impacted by use of the AI system?\n- To what extent has the entity clarified the roles, responsibilities, and delegated authorities to relevant stakeholders?\n\n#### GOVERN 5.2\n\n> Mechanisms are established to enable AI actors to regularly incorporate adjudicated feedback from relevant AI actors into system design and implementation.\n>\n> *AI RMF 1.0 wording:* Mechanisms are established to enable the team that developed or deployed AI systems to regularly incorporate adjudicated feedback from relevant AI actors into system design and implementation.\n\n*AI Actors:* AI Impact Assessment, Governance and Oversight, Operation and Monitoring · *Topics:* Participation, Governance, Impact Assessment\n\n**Suggested actions** (first 4 of 5):\n- Explicitly acknowledge that AI systems, and the use of AI, present inherent costs and risks along with potential benefits.\n- Define reasonable risk tolerances for AI systems informed by laws, regulation, best practices, or industry standards.\n- Establish policies that ensure all relevant AI actors are provided with meaningful opportunities to provide feedback on system design and implementation.\n- Establish policies that define how to assign AI systems to established risk tolerance levels by combining system impact assessments with the likelihood that an impact occurs. Such assessment often entails some combination of:\n    - Econometric evaluations of impacts and impact likelihoods to assess AI system risk.\n    - Red-amber-green (RAG) scales for impact severity and likelihood to assess AI system risk.\n    - Establishment of policies for allocating risk management resources along established risk tolerance levels, with higher-risk systems receiving more risk management resources and oversight.\n    - Establishment of policies for approval, conditional approval, and disapproval of the design, implementation, and deployment of AI systems.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- Who is ultimately responsible for the decisions of the AI and is this person aware of the intended uses and limitations of the analytic?\n- Who will be responsible for maintaining, re-verifying, monitoring, and updating this AI once deployed?\n- Who is accountable for the ethical considerations during all stages of the AI lifecycle?\n\n### GOVERN 6 — Third-party software, data and supply chain\n\n*AI RMF 1.0 category statement:* Policies and procedures are in place to address AI risks and benefits arising from third-party software and data and other supply chain issues.\n\n#### GOVERN 6.1\n\n> Policies and procedures are in place that address AI risks associated with third-party entities, including risks of infringement of a third party’s intellectual property or other rights.\n\n*AI Actors:* Third-party entities, Operation and Monitoring, Procurement · *Topics:* Third-party, Legal and Regulatory, Procurement, Supply Chain, Governance\n\n**Suggested actions** (4 of 4):\n- Collaboratively establish policies that address third-party AI systems and data.\n- Establish policies related to:\n    - Transparency into third-party system functions, including knowledge about training data, training and inference algorithms, and assumptions and limitations.\n    - Thorough testing of third-party AI systems. (See MEASURE for more detail)\n    - Requirements for clear and complete instructions for third-party system usage.\n- Evaluate policies for third-party technology.\n- Establish policies that address supply chain, full product lifecycle and associated processes, including legal, ethical, and other issues concerning procurement and use of third-party software or hardware systems and data.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- Did you establish mechanisms that facilitate the AI system’s auditability (e.g. traceability of the development process, the sourcing of training data and the logging of the AI system’s processes, outcomes, positive and negative impact)?\n- If a third party created the AI, how will you ensure a level of explainability or interpretability?\n- Did you ensure that the AI system can be audited by independent third parties?\n\n#### GOVERN 6.2\n\n> Contingency processes are in place to handle failures or incidents in third-party data or AI systems deemed to be high-risk.\n\n*AI Actors:* AI Deployment, TEVV, Operation and Monitoring, Third-party entities · *Topics:* Third-party, Governance, Risk Management, Supply Chain\n\n**Suggested actions** (2 of 2):\n- Establish policies for handling third-party system failures to include consideration of redundancy mechanisms for vital third-party AI systems.\n- Verify that incident response plans address third-party AI systems.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- To what extent does the plan specifically address risks associated with acquisition, procurement of packaged software from vendors, cybersecurity controls, computational infrastructure, data, data science, deployment mechanics, and system failure?\n- Did you establish a process for third parties (e.g. suppliers, end users, subjects, distributors/vendors or workers) to report potential vulnerabilities, risks or biases in the AI system?\n\n## 7. MAP (5 categories, 18 subcategories)\n\nMAP establishes the context to frame risks for a specific AI system: purpose, users, setting, risk tolerance, categorization, benefits and costs, component and third-party risks, and impacts. Its outcomes are the basis for MEASURE and MANAGE and should give enough context for an initial go/no-go decision on whether to design, develop or deploy the system.\n\n### MAP 1 — Context established and understood\n\n*AI RMF 1.0 category statement:* Context is established and understood.\n\n#### MAP 1.1\n\n> Intended purpose, potentially beneficial uses, context-specific laws, norms and expectations, and prospective settings in which the AI system will be deployed are understood and documented. Considerations include: specific set or types of users along with their expectations; potential positive and negative impacts of system uses to individuals, communities, organizations, society, and the planet; assumptions and related limitations about AI system purposes; uses and risks across the development or product AI lifecycle; TEVV and system metrics.\n\n*AI Actors:* none tagged in playbook.json · *Topics:* Socio-technical systems, Societal Values, Context of Use, Impact Assessment, TEVV, Trustworthy Characteristics (all seven)\n\n**Suggested actions** (first 6 of 13):\n- Maintain awareness of industry, technical, and applicable legal standards.\n- Examine trustworthiness of AI system design and consider, non-AI solutions\n- Consider intended AI system design tasks along with unanticipated purposes in collaboration with human factors and socio-technical domain experts.\n- Define and document the task, purpose, minimum functionality, and benefits of the AI system to inform considerations about whether the utility of the project or its lack of.\n- Identify whether there are non-AI or non-technology alternatives that will lead to more trustworthy outcomes.\n- Examine how changes in system performance affect downstream events such as decision-making (e.g: changes in an AI model objective function create what types of impacts in how many candidates do/do not get a job interview).\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- To what extent is the output of each component appropriate for the operational context?\n- Which AI actors are responsible for the decisions of the AI and is this person aware of the intended uses and limitations of the analytic?\n- Which AI actors are responsible for maintaining, re-verifying, monitoring, and updating this AI once deployed?\n\n#### MAP 1.2\n\n> Inter-disciplinary AI actors, competencies, skills and capacities for establishing context reflect demographic diversity and broad domain and user experience expertise, and their participation is documented. Opportunities for interdisciplinary collaboration are prioritized.\n\n*AI Actors:* none tagged in playbook.json · *Topics:* Diversity, Interdisciplinarity, Socio-technical systems\n\n**Suggested actions** (2 of 2):\n- Establish interdisciplinary teams to reflect a wide range of skills, competencies, and capabilities for AI efforts. Verify that team membership includes demographic diversity, broad domain expertise, and lived experiences. Document team composition.\n- Create and empower interdisciplinary expert teams to capture, learn, and engage the interdependencies of deployed AI systems and related terminologies and concepts from disciplines outside of AI practice such as law, sociology, psychology, anthropology, public policy, systems design, and engineering.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- To what extent do the teams responsible for developing and maintaining the AI system reflect diverse opinions, backgrounds, experiences, and perspectives?\n- Did the entity document the demographics of those involved in the design and development of the AI system to capture and communicate potential biases inherent to the development process, according to forum participants?\n\n#### MAP 1.3\n\n> The organization’s mission and relevant goals for the AI technology are understood and documented.\n\n*AI Actors:* none tagged in playbook.json · *Topics:* Socio-technical systems, Societal Values\n\n**Suggested actions** (5 of 5):\n- Build transparent practices into AI system development processes.\n- Review the documented system purpose from a socio-technical perspective and in consideration of societal values.\n- Determine possible misalignment between societal values and stated organizational principles and code of ethics.\n- Flag latent incentives that may contribute to negative impacts.\n- Evaluate AI system purpose in consideration of potential risks, societal values, and stated organizational principles.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- How does the AI system help the entity meet its goals and objectives?\n- How do the technical specifications and requirements align with the AI system’s goals and objectives?\n- To what extent is the output appropriate for the operational context?\n\n#### MAP 1.4\n\n> The business value or context of business use has been clearly defined or – in the case of assessing existing AI systems – re-evaluated.\n\n*AI Actors:* none tagged in playbook.json · *Topics:* Context of Use\n\n**Suggested actions** (3 of 3):\n- Document business value or context of business use\n- Reconcile documented concerns about the system’s purpose within the business context of use compared to the organization’s stated values, mission statements, social responsibility commitments, and AI principles.\n- Reconsider the design, implementation strategy, or deployment of AI systems with potential impacts that do not reflect institutional values.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- What goals and objectives does the entity expect to achieve by designing, developing, and/or deploying the AI system?\n- To what extent are the system outputs consistent with the entity’s values and principles to foster public trust and equity?\n- To what extent are the metrics consistent with system goals, objectives, and constraints, including ethical and compliance considerations?\n\n#### MAP 1.5\n\n> Organizational risk tolerances are determined and documented.\n\n*AI Actors:* none tagged in playbook.json · *Topics:* Risk Tolerance\n\n**Suggested actions** (first 5 of 6):\n- Utilize existing regulations and guidelines for risk criteria, tolerance and response established by organizational, domain, discipline, sector, or professional requirements.\n- Establish risk tolerance levels for AI systems and allocate the appropriate oversight resources to each level.\n- Establish risk criteria in consideration of different sources of risk, (e.g., financial, operational, safety and wellbeing, business, reputational, and model risks) and different levels of risk (e.g., from negligible to critical).\n- Identify maximum allowable risk tolerance above which the system will not be deployed, or will need to be prematurely decommissioned, within the contextual or application setting.\n- Articulate and analyze tradeoffs across trustworthiness characteristics as relevant to proposed context of use. When tradeoffs arise, document them and plan for traceable actions (e.g.: impact mitigation, removal of system from development or use) to inform management decisions.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- Which existing regulations and guidelines apply, and the entity has followed, in the development of system risk tolerances?\n- What criteria and assumptions has the entity utilized when developing system risk tolerances?\n- How has the entity identified maximum allowable risk tolerance?\n\n#### MAP 1.6\n\n> System requirements (e.g., “the system shall respect the privacy of its users”) are elicited from and understood by relevant AI actors. Design decisions take socio-technical implications into account to address AI risks.\n\n*AI Actors:* none tagged in playbook.json · *Topics:* Socio-technical systems, Impact Assessment, Documentation\n\n**Suggested actions** (first 5 of 8):\n- Proactively incorporate trustworthy characteristics into system requirements.\n- Establish mechanisms for regular communication and feedback between relevant AI actors and internal or external stakeholders related to system design or deployment decisions.\n- Develop and standardize practices to assess potential impacts at all stages of the AI lifecycle, and in collaboration with interdisciplinary experts, actors external to the team that developed or deployed the AI system, and potentially impacted communities.\n- Include potentially impacted groups, communities and external entities (e.g. civil society organizations, research institutes, local community groups, and trade associations) in the formulation of priorities, definitions and outcomes during impact assessment activities.\n- Conduct qualitative interviews with end user(s) to regularly evaluate expectations and design plans related to Human-AI configurations and tasks.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- What type of information is accessible on the design, operations, and limitations of the AI system to external stakeholders, including end users, consumers, regulators, and individuals impacted by use of the AI system?\n- To what extent is this information sufficient and appropriate to promote transparency?\n\n### MAP 2 — System categorization\n\n*AI RMF 1.0 category statement:* Categorization of the AI system is performed.\n\n#### MAP 2.1\n\n> The specific task, and methods used to implement the task, that the AI system will support is defined (e.g., classifiers, generative models, recommenders).\n\n*AI Actors:* none tagged in playbook.json · *Topics:* Socio-technical systems\n\n**Suggested actions** (1 of 1):\n- Define and document AI system’s existing and potential learning task(s) along with known assumptions and limitations.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- To what extent has the entity clearly defined technical specifications and requirements for the AI system?\n- To what extent has the entity documented the AI system’s development, testing methodology, metrics, and performance outcomes?\n- How do the technical specifications and requirements align with the AI system’s goals and objectives?\n\n#### MAP 2.2\n\n> Information about the AI system’s knowledge limits and how system output may be utilized and overseen by humans is documented. Documentation provides sufficient information to assist relevant AI actors when making informed decisions and taking subsequent actions.\n\n*AI Actors:* none tagged in playbook.json · *Topics:* Limitations, Human oversight, Impact Assessment, Documentation\n\n**Suggested actions** (6 of 6):\n- Document settings, environments and conditions that are outside the AI system’s intended use.\n- Design for end user workflows and toolsets, concept of operations, and explainability and interpretability criteria in conjunction with end user(s) and associated qualitative feedback.\n- Plan and test human-AI configurations under close to real-world conditions and document results.\n- Follow stakeholder feedback processes to determine whether a system achieved its documented purpose within a given use context, and whether end users can correctly comprehend system outputs or results.\n- Document dependencies on upstream data and other AI systems, including if the specified system is an upstream dependency for another AI system or other data.\n- Document connections the AI system or data will have to external networks (including the internet), financial markets, and critical infrastructure that have potential for negative externalities. Identify and document negative impacts as part of considering the broader risk thresholds and subsequent go/no-go deployment as well as post-deployment decommissioning decisions.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- Does the AI system provide sufficient information to assist the personnel to make an informed decision and take actions accordingly?\n- What type of information is accessible on the design, operations, and limitations of the AI system to external stakeholders, including end users, consumers, regulators, and individuals impacted by use of the AI system?\n\n#### MAP 2.3\n\n> Scientific integrity and TEVV considerations are identified and documented, including those related to experimental design, data collection and selection (e.g., availability, representativeness, suitability), system trustworthiness, and construct validation.\n\n*AI Actors:* AI Development, TEVV, Domain Experts · *Topics:* TEVV, Data, Impact Assessment, Limitations\n\n**Suggested actions** (first 5 of 16):\n- Identify and document experiment design and statistical techniques that are valid for testing complex socio-technical systems like AI, which involve human factors, emergent properties, and dynamic context(s) of use.\n- Develop and apply TEVV protocols for models, system and its subcomponents, deployment, and operation.\n- Demonstrate and document that AI system performance and validation metrics are interpretable and unambiguous for downstream decision making tasks, and take socio-technical factors such as context of use into consideration.\n- Identify and document assumptions, techniques, and metrics used for testing and evaluation throughout the AI lifecycle including experimental design techniques for data collection, selection, and management practices in accordance with data governance policies established in GOVERN.\n- Identify testing modules that can be incorporated throughout the AI lifecycle, and verify that processes enable corroboration by independent evaluators.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- Are there any known errors, sources of noise, or redundancies in the data?\n- Over what time-frame was the data collected?\n- What is the variable selection and evaluation process?\n\n### MAP 3 — Capabilities, usage, benefits and costs\n\n*AI RMF 1.0 category statement:* AI capabilities, targeted usage, goals, and expected benefits and costs compared with appropriate benchmarks are understood.\n\n#### MAP 3.1\n\n> Potential benefits of intended AI system functionality and performance are examined and documented.\n\n*AI Actors:* AI Development, AI Deployment, AI Impact Assessment · *Topics:* Socio-technical systems, Documentation\n\n**Suggested actions** (6 of 6):\n- Utilize participatory approaches and engage with system end users to understand and document AI systems’ potential benefits, efficacy and interpretability of AI task output.\n- Maintain awareness and documentation of the individuals, groups, or communities who make up the system’s internal and external stakeholders.\n- Verify that appropriate skills and practices are available in-house for carrying out participatory activities such as eliciting, capturing, and synthesizing user, operator and external feedback, and translating it for AI design and development functions.\n- Establish mechanisms for regular communication and feedback between relevant AI actors and internal or external stakeholders related to system design or deployment decisions.\n- Consider performance to human baseline metrics or other standard benchmarks.\n- Incorporate feedback from end users, and potentially impacted individuals and communities about perceived system benefits.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- Have the benefits of the AI system been communicated to end users?\n- Have the appropriate training material and disclaimers about how to adequately use the AI system been provided to end users?\n- Has your organization implemented a risk management system to address risks involved in deploying the identified AI system (e.g. personnel risk or changes to commercial objectives)?\n\n#### MAP 3.2\n\n> Potential costs, including non-monetary costs, which result from expected or realized AI errors or system functionality and trustworthiness - as connected to organizational risk tolerance - are examined and documented.\n\n*AI Actors:* AI Design, AI Development, Operation and Monitoring, AI Impact Assessment · *Topics:* Impact Assessment, Trustworthy Characteristics (all seven)\n\n**Suggested actions** (2 of 2):\n- Perform context analysis to map potential negative impacts arising from not integrating trustworthiness characteristics. When negative impacts are not direct or obvious, AI actors can engage with stakeholders external to the team that developed or deployed the AI system, and potentially impacted communities, to examine and document:\n    - Who could be harmed?\n    - What could be harmed?\n    - When could harm arise?\n    - How could harm arise?\n- Identify and implement procedures for regularly evaluating the qualitative and quantitative costs of internal and external AI system failures. Develop actions to prevent, detect, and/or correct potential risks and related impacts. Regularly evaluate failure costs to inform go/no-go deployment decisions throughout the AI system lifecycle.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- To what extent does the system/entity consistently measure progress towards stated goals and objectives?\n- To what extent can users or parties affected by the outputs of the AI system test the AI system and provide feedback?\n- Have you documented and explained that machine errors may differ from human errors?\n\n#### MAP 3.3\n\n> Targeted application scope is specified and documented based on the system’s capability, established context, and AI system categorization.\n\n*AI Actors:* AI Design, AI Development, Human Factors · *Topics:* Context of Use, Documentation\n\n**Suggested actions** (2 of 2):\n- Consider narrowing contexts for system deployment, including factors related to:\n    - How outcomes may directly or indirectly affect users, groups, communities and the environment.\n    - Length of time the system is deployed in between re-trainings.\n    - Geographical regions in which the system operates.\n    - Dynamics related to community standards or likelihood of system misuse or abuses (either purposeful or unanticipated).\n    - How AI system features and capabilities can be utilized within other applications, or in place of other existing processes.\n- Engage AI actors from legal and procurement functions when specifying target application scope.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- To what extent has the entity clearly defined technical specifications and requirements for the AI system?\n- How do the technical specifications and requirements align with the AI system’s goals and objectives?\n\n#### MAP 3.4\n\n> Processes for operator and practitioner proficiency with AI system performance and trustworthiness – and relevant technical standards and certifications – are defined, assessed and documented.\n\n*AI Actors:* AI Design, AI Development, Human Factors, End-Users, Domain Experts, Operation and Monitoring · *Topics:* Human-AI teaming\n\n**Suggested actions** (first 4 of 10):\n- Identify and declare AI system features and capabilities that may affect downstream AI actors’ decision-making in deployment and operational settings for example how system features and capabilities may activate known risks in various human-AI configurations, such as selective adherence.\n- Identify skills and proficiency requirements for operators, practitioners and other domain experts that interact with AI systems,Develop AI system operational documentation for AI actors in deployed and operational environments, including information about known risks, mitigation criteria, and trustworthy characteristics enumerated in Map-1.\n- Define and develop training materials for proposed end users, practitioners and operators about AI system use and known limitations.\n- Define and develop certification procedures for operating AI systems within defined contexts of use, and information about what exceeds operational boundaries.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- What policies has the entity developed to ensure the use of the AI system is consistent with its stated values and principles?\n- How will the accountable human(s) address changes in accuracy and precision due to either an adversary’s attempts to disrupt the AI or unrelated changes in operational/business environment, which may impact the accuracy of the AI?\n\n#### MAP 3.5\n\n> Processes for human oversight are defined, assessed, and documented in accordance with organizational policies from GOVERN function.\n\n*AI Actors:* Human Factors, End-Users, Domain Experts, Operation and Monitoring, AI Design · *Topics:* Human oversight\n\n**Suggested actions** (6 of 6):\n- Identify and document AI systems’ features and capabilities that require human oversight, in relation to operational and societal contexts, trustworthy characteristics, and risks identified in MAP-1.\n- Establish practices for AI systems’ oversight in accordance with policies developed in GOVERN-1.\n- Define and develop training materials for relevant AI Actors about AI system performance, context of use, known limitations and negative impacts, and suggested warning labels.\n- Include relevant AI Actors in AI system prototyping and testing activities. Conduct testing activities under scenarios similar to deployment conditions.\n- Evaluate AI system oversight practices for validity and reliability. When oversight practices undergo extensive updates or adaptations, retest, evaluate results, and course correct as necessary.\n- Verify that model documents contain interpretable descriptions of system mechanisms, enabling oversight personnel to make informed, risk-based decisions about system risks.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- What are the roles, responsibilities, and delegation of authorities of personnel involved in the design, development, deployment, assessment and monitoring of the AI system?\n- How does the entity assess whether personnel have the necessary skills, training, resources, and domain knowledge to fulfill their assigned responsibilities?\n\n### MAP 4 — Risks and benefits of all components, incl. third-party\n\n*AI RMF 1.0 category statement:* Risks and benefits are mapped for all components of the AI system including third-party software and data.\n\n#### MAP 4.1\n\n> Approaches for mapping AI technology and legal risks of its components – including the use of third-party data or software – are in place, followed, and documented, as are risks of infringement of a third-party’s intellectual property or other rights.\n\n*AI Actors:* Third-party entities, Procurement, Operation and Monitoring, Governance and Oversight · *Topics:* Legal and Regulatory, Third-party, Pre-trained models, Supply Chain, Risk Tolerance, Risky Emergent Behavior\n\n**Suggested actions** (4 of 4):\n- Review audit reports, testing results, product roadmaps, warranties, terms of service, end user license agreements, contracts, and other documentation related to third-party entities to assist in value assessment and risk management activities.\n- Review third-party software release schedules and software change management plans (hotfixes, patches, updates, forward- and backward- compatibility guarantees) for irregularities that may contribute to AI system risks.\n- Inventory third-party material (hardware, open-source software, foundation models, open source data, proprietary software, proprietary data, etc.) required for system implementation and maintenance.\n- Review redundancies related to third-party technology and personnel to assess potential risks due to lack of adequate support.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- Did you establish a process for third parties (e.g. suppliers, end users, subjects, distributors/vendors or workers) to report potential vulnerabilities, risks or biases in the AI system?\n- If your organization obtained datasets from a third party, did your organization assess and manage the risks of using such datasets?\n- How will the results be independently verified?\n\n#### MAP 4.2\n\n> Internal risk controls for components of the AI system including third-party AI technologies are identified and documented.\n\n*AI Actors:* AI Deployment, TEVV, Operation and Monitoring, Third-party entities · *Topics:* Third-party, Pre-trained models\n\n**Suggested actions** (4 of 4):\n- Track third-parties preventing or hampering risk-mapping as indications of increased risk.\n- Supply resources such as model documentation templates and software safelists to assist in third-party technology inventory and approval activities.\n- Review third-party material (including data and models) for risks related to bias, data privacy, and security vulnerabilities.\n- Apply traditional technology risk controls – such as procurement, security, and data privacy controls – to all acquired third-party technologies.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- Can the AI system be audited by independent third parties?\n- To what extent do these policies foster public trust and confidence in the use of the AI system?\n- Are mechanisms established to facilitate the AI system’s auditability (e.g. traceability of the development process, the sourcing of training data and the logging of the AI system’s processes, outcomes, positive and negative impact)?\n\n### MAP 5 — Impacts characterized\n\n*AI RMF 1.0 category statement:* Impacts to individuals, groups, communities, organizations, and society are characterized.\n\n#### MAP 5.1\n\n> Likelihood and magnitude of each identified impact (both potentially beneficial and harmful) based on expected use, past uses of AI systems in similar contexts, public incident reports, feedback from those external to the team that developed or deployed the AI system, or other data are identified and documented.\n\n*AI Actors:* AI Design, AI Development, AI Deployment, AI Impact Assessment, Operation and Monitoring, Affected Individuals and Communities, End-Users · *Topics:* Participation, Impact Assessment\n\n**Suggested actions** (4 of 4):\n- Establish assessment scales for measuring AI systems’ impact. Scales may be qualitative, such as red-amber-green (RAG), or may entail simulations or econometric approaches. Document and apply scales uniformly across the organization’s AI portfolio.\n- Apply TEVV regularly at key stages in the AI lifecycle, connected to system impacts and frequency of system updates.\n- Identify and document likelihood and magnitude of system benefits and negative impacts in relation to trustworthiness characteristics.\n- Establish processes for red teaming to identify and connect system limitations to AI lifecycle stage(s) and potential downstream impacts\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- Which population(s) does the AI system impact?\n- What assessments has the entity conducted on trustworthiness characteristics for example data security and privacy impacts associated with the AI system?\n- Can the AI system be tested by independent third parties?\n\n#### MAP 5.2\n\n> Practices and personnel for supporting regular engagement with relevant AI actors and integrating feedback about positive, negative, and unanticipated impacts are in place and documented.\n\n*AI Actors:* AI Design, Human Factors, AI Deployment, AI Impact Assessment, Operation and Monitoring, Domain Experts, Affected Individuals and Communities, End-Users · *Topics:* Participation, Impact Assessment\n\n**Suggested actions** (first 5 of 7):\n- Establish and document stakeholder engagement processes at the earliest stages of system formulation to identify potential impacts from the AI system on individuals, groups, communities, organizations, and society.\n- Employ methods such as value sensitive design (VSD) to identify misalignments between organizational and societal values, and system implementation and impact.\n- Identify approaches to engage, capture, and incorporate input from system end users and other key stakeholders to assist with continuous monitoring for potential impacts and emergent risks.\n- Incorporate quantitative, qualitative, and mixed methods in the assessment and documentation of potential impacts to individuals, groups, communities, organizations, and society.\n- Identify a team (internal or external) that is independent of AI design and development functions to assess AI system benefits, positive and negative impacts and their likelihood and magnitude.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- If the AI system relates to people, does it unfairly advantage or disadvantage a particular social group? In what ways? How was this managed?\n- If the AI system relates to other ethically protected groups, have appropriate obligations been met?\n\n## 8. MEASURE (4 categories, 22 subcategories)\n\nMEASURE uses quantitative, qualitative or mixed methods to analyze, assess, benchmark and monitor the risks identified in MAP. Systems are tested before deployment and regularly in operation, trustworthiness characteristics are evaluated, and the results feed MANAGE.\n\n### MEASURE 1 — Methods and metrics\n\n*AI RMF 1.0 category statement:* Appropriate methods and metrics are identified and applied.\n\n#### MEASURE 1.1\n\n> Approaches and metrics for measurement of AI risks enumerated during the Map function are selected for implementation starting with the most significant AI risks. The risks or trustworthiness characteristics that will not – or cannot – be measured are properly documented.\n\n*AI Actors:* AI Development, TEVV, Domain Experts · *Topics:* Trustworthy Characteristics (all seven), Risk Assessment, Risky Emergent Behavior, TEVV\n\n**Suggested actions** (first 6 of 12):\n- Establish approaches for detecting, tracking and measuring known risks, errors, incidents or negative impacts.\n- Identify testing procedures and metrics to demonstrate whether or not the system is fit for purpose and functioning as claimed.\n- Identify testing procedures and metrics to demonstrate AI system trustworthiness\n- Define acceptable limits for system performance (e.g. distribution of errors), and include course correction suggestions if/when the system performs beyond acceptable limits.\n- Define metrics for, and regularly assess, AI actor competency for effective system operation,\n- Identify transparency metrics to assess whether stakeholders have access to necessary information about system design, development, deployment, use, and evaluation.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- How will the appropriate performance metrics, such as accuracy, of the AI be monitored after the AI is deployed?\n- What corrective actions has the entity taken to enhance the quality, accuracy, reliability, and representativeness of the data?\n- Are there recommended data splits or evaluation measures?\n\n#### MEASURE 1.2\n\n> Appropriateness of AI metrics and effectiveness of existing controls is regularly assessed and updated including reports of errors and impacts on affected communities.\n>\n> *AI RMF 1.0 wording:* Appropriateness of AI metrics and effectiveness of existing controls are regularly assessed and updated, including reports of errors and potential impacts on affected communities.\n\n*AI Actors:* TEVV, AI Impact Assessment, AI Development, AI Deployment, Affected Individuals and Communities · *Topics:* Impact Assessment, TEVV, Context of Use\n\n**Suggested actions** (first 6 of 8):\n- Assess external validity of all measurements (e.g., the degree to which measurements taken in one context can generalize to other contexts).\n- Assess effectiveness of existing metrics and controls on a regular basis throughout the AI system lifecycle.\n- Document reports of errors, incidents and negative impacts and assess sufficiency and efficacy of existing metrics for repairs, and upgrades\n- Develop new metrics when existing metrics are insufficient or ineffective for implementing repairs and upgrades.\n- Develop and utilize metrics to monitor, characterize and track external inputs, including any third-party tools.\n- Determine frequency and scope for sharing metrics and related information with stakeholders and impacted communities.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- What metrics has the entity developed to measure performance of the AI system?\n- To what extent do the metrics provide accurate and useful measure of performance?\n- What corrective actions has the entity taken to enhance the quality, accuracy, reliability, and representativeness of the data?\n\n#### MEASURE 1.3\n\n> Internal experts who did not serve as front-line developers for the system and/or independent assessors are involved in regular assessments and updates. Domain experts, users, AI actors external to the team that developed or deployed the AI system, and affected communities are consulted in support of assessments as necessary per organizational risk tolerance.\n\n*AI Actors:* TEVV, AI Impact Assessment, AI Development, AI Deployment, Affected Individuals and Communities, Domain Experts, End-Users, Operation and Monitoring · *Topics:* Participation, Impact Assessment, Context of Use\n\n**Suggested actions** (first 6 of 8):\n- Evaluate TEVV processes regarding incentives to identify risks and impacts.\n- Utilize separate testing teams established in the Govern function (2.1 and 4.1) to enable independent decisions and course-correction for AI systems. Track processes and measure and document change in performance.\n- Plan and evaluate AI system prototypes with end user populations early and continuously in the AI lifecycle. Document test outcomes and course correct.\n- Assess independence and stature of TEVV and oversight AI actors, to ensure they have the required levels of independence and resources to perform assurance, compliance, and feedback tasks effectively\n- Evaluate interdisciplinary and demographically diverse internal team established in Map 1.2\n- Evaluate effectiveness of external stakeholder feedback mechanisms, specifically related to processes for eliciting, evaluating and integrating input from diverse groups.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- What are the roles, responsibilities, and delegation of authorities of personnel involved in the design, development, deployment, assessment and monitoring of the AI system?\n- How easily accessible and current is the information available to external stakeholders?\n- To what extent does the entity communicate its AI strategic goals and objectives to the community of stakeholders?\n\n### MEASURE 2 — Evaluation for trustworthy characteristics\n\n*AI RMF 1.0 category statement:* AI systems are evaluated for trustworthy characteristics.\n\n#### MEASURE 2.1\n\n> Test sets, metrics, and details about the tools used during test, evaluation, validation, and verification (TEVV) are documented.\n\n*AI Actors:* TEVV · *Topics:* TEVV, Documentation, Validity and Reliability\n\n**Suggested actions** (3 of 3):\n- Leverage existing industry best practices for transparency and documentation of all possible aspects of measurements. Examples include: data sheet for data sets, model cards\n- Regularly assess the effectiveness of tools used to document measurement approaches, test sets, metrics, processes and materials used\n- Update the tools as needed\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- Given the purpose of this AI, what is an appropriate interval for checking whether it is still accurate, unbiased, explainable, etc.? What are the checks for this model?\n- To what extent has the entity documented the AI system’s development, testing methodology, metrics, and performance outcomes?\n\n#### MEASURE 2.2\n\n> Evaluations involving human subjects meet applicable requirements (including human subject protection) and are representative of the relevant population.\n\n*AI Actors:* TEVV, Human Factors, AI Development · *Topics:* Data, Human Subjects Protection\n\n**Suggested actions** (first 6 of 8):\n- Follow human subjects research requirements as established by organizational and disciplinary requirements, including informed consent and compensation, during dataset collection activities.\n- Analyze differences between intended and actual population of users or data subjects, including likelihood for errors, incidents or negative impacts.\n- Utilize disaggregated evaluation methods (e.g. by race, age, gender, ethnicity, ability, region) to improve AI system performance when deployed in real world settings.\n- Establish thresholds and alert procedures for dataset representativeness within the context of use.\n- Construct datasets in close collaboration with experts with knowledge of the context of use.\n- Follow intellectual property and privacy rights related to datasets and their use, including for the subjects represented in the data.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- Given the purpose of this AI, what is an appropriate interval for checking whether it is still accurate, unbiased, explainable, etc.? What are the checks for this model?\n- How has the entity identified and mitigated potential impacts of bias in the data, including inequitable or discriminatory outcomes?\n\n#### MEASURE 2.3\n\n> AI system performance or assurance criteria are measured qualitatively or quantitatively and demonstrated for conditions similar to deployment setting(s). Measures are documented.\n\n*AI Actors:* TEVV, AI Deployment · *Topics:* TEVV, Impact Assessment\n\n**Suggested actions** (first 6 of 9):\n- Conduct regular and sustained engagement with potentially impacted communities\n- Maintain a demographically diverse and multidisciplinary and collaborative internal team\n- Regularly test and evaluate systems in non-optimized conditions, and in collaboration with AI actors in user interaction and user experience (UI/UX) roles.\n- Evaluate feedback from stakeholder engagement activities, in collaboration with human factors and socio-technical experts.\n- Collaborate with socio-technical, human factors, and UI/UX experts to identify notable characteristics in context of use that can be translated into system testing scenarios.\n- Measure AI systems prior to deployment in conditions similar to expected scenarios.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- What experiments were initially run on this dataset? To what extent have experiments on the AI system been documented?\n- To what extent does the system/entity consistently measure progress towards stated goals and objectives?\n- How will the appropriate performance metrics, such as accuracy, of the AI be monitored after the AI is deployed? How much distributional shift or model drift from baseline performance is acceptable?\n\n#### MEASURE 2.4\n\n> The functionality and behavior of the AI system and its components – as identified in the MAP function – are monitored when in production.\n\n*AI Actors:* AI Deployment, TEVV · *Topics:* TEVV, Monitoring, Drift\n\n**Suggested actions** (first 5 of 7):\n- Monitor and document how metrics and performance indicators observed in production differ from the same metrics collected during pre-deployment testing. When differences are observed, consider error propagation and feedback loop risks.\n- Utilize hypothesis testing or human domain expertise to measure monitored distribution differences in new input or output data relative to test environments\n- Monitor for anomalies using approaches such as control limits, confidence intervals, integrity constraints and ML algorithms. When anomalies are observed, consider error propagation and feedback loop risks.\n- Verify alerts are in place for when distributions in new input data or generated predictions observed in production differ from pre-deployment test outcomes, or when anomalies are detected.\n- Assess the accuracy and quality of generated outputs against new collected ground-truth information as it becomes available.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- To what extent is the output of each component appropriate for the operational context?\n- What justifications, if any, has the entity provided for the assumptions, boundaries, and limitations of the AI system?\n- How will the appropriate performance metrics, such as accuracy, of the AI be monitored after the AI is deployed?\n\n#### MEASURE 2.5\n\n> The AI system to be deployed is demonstrated to be valid and reliable. Limitations of the generalizability beyond the conditions under which the technology was developed are documented.\n\n*AI Actors:* TEVV, Domain Experts · *Topics:* TEVV, Validity and Reliability, Trustworthy Characteristics, Data\n\n**Suggested actions** (first 6 of 15):\n- Define the operating conditions and socio-technical context under which the AI system will be validated.\n- Define and document processes to establish the system’s operational conditions and limits.\n- Establish or identify, and document approaches to measure forms of validity, including:\n    - construct validity (the test is measuring the concept it claims to measure)\n    - internal validity (relationship being tested is not influenced by other factors or variables)\n    - external validity (results are generalizable beyond the training condition)\n    - the use of experimental design principles and statistical analyses and modeling.\n- Assess and document system variance. Standard approaches include confidence intervals, standard deviation, standard error, bootstrapping, or cross-validation.\n- Establish or identify, and document robustness measures.\n- Establish or identify, and document reliability measures.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- What testing, if any, has the entity conducted on theAI system to identify errors and limitations (i.e.adversarial or stress testing)?\n- Given the purpose of this AI, what is an appropriate interval for checking whether it is still accurate, unbiased, explainable, etc.? What are the checks for this model?\n\n#### MEASURE 2.6\n\n> AI system is evaluated regularly for safety risks – as identified in the MAP function. The AI system to be deployed is demonstrated to be safe, its residual negative risk does not exceed the risk tolerance, and can fail safely, particularly if made to operate beyond its knowledge limits. Safety metrics implicate system reliability and robustness, real-time monitoring, and response times for AI system failures.\n>\n> *AI RMF 1.0 wording:* The AI system is evaluated regularly for safety risks – as identified in the MAP function. The AI system to be deployed is demonstrated to be safe, its residual negative risk does not exceed the risk tolerance, and it can fail safely, particularly if made to operate beyond its knowledge limits. Safety metrics reflect system reliability and robustness, real-time monitoring, and response times for AI system failures.\n\n*AI Actors:* TEVV, Domain Experts, Operation and Monitoring, AI Impact Assessment, AI Deployment · *Topics:* TEVV, Safety, Trustworthy Characteristics, Context of Use\n\n**Suggested actions** (first 4 of 7):\n- Thoroughly measure system performance in development and deployment contexts, and under stress conditions.\n    - Employ test data assessments and simulations before proceeding to production testing. Track multiple performance quality and error metrics.\n    - Stress-test system performance under likely scenarios (e.g., concept drift, high load) and beyond known limitations, in consultation with domain experts.\n    - Test the system under conditions similar to those related to past known incidents or near-misses and measure system performance and safety characteristics\n    - Apply chaos engineering approaches to test systems in extreme conditions and gauge unexpected responses.\n    - Document the range of conditions under which the system has been tested and demonstrated to fail safely.\n- Measure and monitor system performance in real-time to enable rapid response when AI system incidents are detected.\n- Collect pertinent safety statistics (e.g., out-of-range performance, incident response times, system down time, injuries, etc.) in anticipation of potential information sharing with impacted communities or as required by AI system oversight personnel.\n- Align measurement to the goal of continuous improvement. Seek to increase the range of conditions under which the system is able to fail safely through system modifications in response to in-production testing and events.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- What testing, if any, has the entity conducted on the AI system to identify errors and limitations (i.e.adversarial or stress testing)?\n- To what extent has the entity documented the AI system’s development, testing methodology, metrics, and performance outcomes?\n\n#### MEASURE 2.7\n\n> AI system security and resilience – as identified in the MAP function – are evaluated and documented.\n\n*AI Actors:* TEVV, Domain Experts, Operation and Monitoring, AI Impact Assessment, AI Deployment · *Topics:* TEVV, Secure and Resilient, Trustworthy Characteristics, Adversarial, Risky Emergent Behavior\n\n**Suggested actions** (first 6 of 10):\n- Establish and track AI system security tests and metrics (e.g., red-teaming activities, frequency and rate of anomalous events, system down-time, incident response times, time-to-bypass, etc.).\n- Use red-team exercises to actively test the system under adversarial or stress conditions, measure system response, assess failure modes or determine if system can return to normal function after an unexpected adverse event.\n- Document red-team exercise results as part of continuous improvement efforts, including the range of security test conditions and results.\n- Use red-teaming exercises to evaluate potential mismatches between claimed and actual system performance.\n- Use countermeasures (e.g, authentication, throttling, differential privacy, robust ML approaches) to increase the range of security conditions under which the system is able to return to normal function.\n- Modify system security procedures and countermeasures to increase robustness and resilience to attacks in response to testing and events experienced in production.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- To what extent does the plan specifically address risks associated with acquisition, procurement of packaged software from vendors, cybersecurity controls, computational infrastructure, data, data science, deployment mechanics, and system failure?\n- What assessments has the entity conducted on data security and privacy impacts associated with the AI system?\n\n#### MEASURE 2.8\n\n> Risks associated with transparency and accountability – as identified in the MAP function – are examined and documented.\n\n*AI Actors:* TEVV, Domain Experts, Operation and Monitoring, AI Impact Assessment, AI Deployment · *Topics:* TEVV, Accountability and Transparency, Trustworthy Characteristics\n\n**Suggested actions** (first 4 of 6):\n- Instrument the system for measurement and tracking, e.g., by maintaining histories, audit logs and other information that can be used by AI actors to review and evaluate possible sources of error, bias, or vulnerability.\n- Calibrate controls for users in close collaboration with experts in user interaction and user experience (UI/UX), human computer interaction (HCI), and/or human-AI teaming.\n- Test provided explanations for calibration with different audiences including operators, end users, decision makers and decision subjects (individuals for whom decisions are being made), and to enable recourse for consequential system decisions that affect end users or subjects.\n- Measure and document human oversight of AI systems:\n    - Document the degree of oversight that is provided by specified AI actors regarding AI system output.\n    - Maintain statistics about downstream actions by end users and operators such as system overrides.\n    - Maintain statistics about and document reported errors or complaints, time to respond, and response types.\n    - Maintain and report statistics about adjudication activities.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- To what extent has the entity clarified the roles, responsibilities, and delegated authorities to relevant stakeholders?\n- What are the roles, responsibilities, and delegation of authorities of personnel involved in the design, development, deployment, assessment and monitoring of the AI system?\n- Who is accountable for the ethical considerations during all stages of the AI lifecycle?\n\n#### MEASURE 2.9\n\n> The AI model is explained, validated, and documented, and AI system output is interpreted within its context – as identified in the MAP function – and to inform responsible use and governance.\n\n*AI Actors:* TEVV, Domain Experts, Operation and Monitoring, AI Impact Assessment, AI Deployment, End-Users · *Topics:* TEVV, Explainability and Interpretability, Trustworthy Characteristics\n\n**Suggested actions** (first 4 of 11):\n- Verify systems are developed to produce explainable models, post-hoc explanations and audit logs.\n- When possible or available, utilize approaches that are inherently explainable, such as traditional and penalized generalized linear models, decision trees, nearest-neighbor and prototype-based approaches, rule-based models, generalized additive models, explainable boosting machines and neural additive models.\n- Test explanation methods and resulting explanations prior to deployment to gain feedback from relevant AI actors, end users, and potentially impacted individuals or groups about whether explanations are accurate, clear, and understandable.\n- Document AI model details including model type (e.g., convolutional neural network, reinforcement learning, decision tree, random forest, etc.) data features, training algorithms, proposed uses, decision thresholds, training data, evaluation data, and ethical considerations.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- Given the purpose of the AI, what level of explainability or interpretability is required for how the AI made its determination?\n- Given the purpose of this AI, what is an appropriate interval for checking whether it is still accurate, unbiased, explainable, etc.? What are the checks for this model?\n\n#### MEASURE 2.10\n\n> Privacy risk of the AI system – as identified in the MAP function – is examined and documented.\n\n*AI Actors:* TEVV, Domain Experts, Operation and Monitoring, AI Impact Assessment, AI Deployment, End-Users · *Topics:* TEVV, Privacy, Trustworthy Characteristics\n\n**Suggested actions** (first 6 of 8):\n- Specify privacy-related values, frameworks, and attributes that are applicable in the context of use through direct engagement with end users and potentially impacted groups and communities.\n- Document collection, use, management, and disclosure of personally sensitive information in datasets, in accordance with privacy and data governance policies\n- Quantify privacy-level data aspects such as the ability to identify individuals or groups (e.g. k-anonymity metrics, l-diversity, t-closeness).\n- Establish and document protocols (authorization, duration, type) and access controls for training sets or production data containing personally sensitive information, in accordance with privacy and data governance policies.\n- Monitor internal queries to production data for detecting patterns that isolate personal records.\n- Monitor PSI disclosures and inference of sensitive or legally protected attributes\n    - Assess the risk of manipulation from overly customized content. Evaluate information presented to representative users at various points along axes of difference between individuals (e.g. individuals of different ages, genders, races, political affiliation, etc.).\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- Did your organization implement accountability-based practices in data management and protection (e.g. the PDPA and OECD Privacy Principles)?\n- What assessments has the entity conducted on data security and privacy impacts associated with the AI system?\n\n#### MEASURE 2.11\n\n> Fairness and bias – as identified in the MAP function – are evaluated and results are documented.\n\n*AI Actors:* TEVV, Domain Experts, Operation and Monitoring, AI Impact Assessment, AI Deployment, End-Users, Affected Individuals and Communities · *Topics:* TEVV, Fairness and Bias, Trustworthy Characteristics\n\n**Suggested actions** (first 4 of 24):\n- Conduct fairness assessments to manage computational and statistical forms of bias which include the following steps:\n    - Identify types of harms, including allocational, representational, quality of service, stereotyping, or erasure\n    - Identify across, within, and intersecting groups that might be harmed\n    - Quantify harms using both a general fairness metric, if appropriate (e.g. demographic parity, equalized odds, equal opportunity, statistical hypothesis tests), and custom, context-specific metrics developed in collaboration with affected communities\n    - Analyze quantified harms for contextually significant differences across groups, within groups, and among intersecting groups\n    - Refine identification of within-group and intersectional group disparities.\n    - *…and 3 more*\n- Understand and consider sources of bias in training and TEVV data:\n    - Differences in distributions of outcomes across and within groups, including intersecting groups.\n    - Completeness, representativeness and balance of data sources.\n    - Identify input data features that may serve as proxies for demographic group membership (i.e., credit score, ZIP code) or otherwise give rise to emergent bias within AI systems.\n    - Forms of systemic bias in images, text (or word embeddings), audio or other complex or unstructured data.\n- Leverage impact assessments to identify and classify system impacts and harms to end users, other individuals, and groups with input from potentially impacted communities.\n- Identify the classes of individuals, groups, or environmental ecosystems which might be impacted through direct engagement with potentially impacted communities.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- To what extent are the established procedures effective in mitigating bias, inequity, and other concerns resulting from the system?\n- If it relates to people, does it unfairly advantage or disadvantage a particular social group? In what ways? How was this mitigated?\n\n#### MEASURE 2.12\n\n> Environmental impact and sustainability of AI model training and management activities – as identified in the MAP function – are assessed and documented.\n\n*AI Actors:* TEVV, Domain Experts, Operation and Monitoring, AI Impact Assessment, AI Deployment · *Topics:* TEVV, Environmental Impact\n\n**Suggested actions** (6 of 6):\n- Include environmental impact indicators in AI system design and development plans, including reducing consumption and improving efficiencies.\n- Identify and implement key indicators of AI system energy and water consumption and efficiency, and/or GHG emissions.\n- Establish measurable baselines for sustainable AI system operation in accordance with organizational policies, regulatory compliance, legal frameworks, and environmental protection and sustainability norms.\n- Assess tradeoffs between AI system performance and sustainable operations in accordance with organizational principles and policies, regulatory compliance, legal frameworks, and environmental protection and sustainability norms.\n- Identify and establish acceptable resource consumption and efficiency, and GHG emissions levels, along with actions to be taken if indicators rise above acceptable levels.\n- Estimate AI system emissions levels throughout the AI lifecycle via carbon calculators or similar process.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- Are greenhouse gas emissions, and energy and water consumption and efficiency tracked within the organization?\n- Are deployed AI systems evaluated for potential upstream and downstream environmental impacts (e.g., increased consumption, increased emissions, etc.)?\n- Could deployed AI systems cause environmental incidents, e.g., air or water pollution incidents, toxic spills, fires or explosions?\n\n#### MEASURE 2.13\n\n> Effectiveness of the employed TEVV metrics and processes in the MEASURE function are evaluated and documented.\n\n*AI Actors:* TEVV, AI Deployment, Operation and Monitoring · *Topics:* TEVV, Effectiveness\n\n**Suggested actions** (4 of 4):\n- Review selected system metrics and associated TEVV processes to determine if they are able to sustain system improvements, including the identification and removal of errors.\n- Regularly evaluate system metrics for utility, and consider descriptive approaches in place of overly complex methods.\n- Review selected system metrics for acceptability within the end user and impacted community of interest.\n- Assess effectiveness of metrics for identifying and measuring risks.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- To what extent does the system/entity consistently measure progress towards stated goals and objectives?\n- Given the purpose of this AI, what is an appropriate interval for checking whether it is still accurate, unbiased, explainable, etc.? What are the checks for this model?\n- What corrective actions has the entity taken to enhance the quality, accuracy, reliability, and representativeness of the data?\n\n### MEASURE 3 — Tracking risks over time\n\n*AI RMF 1.0 category statement:* Mechanisms for tracking identified AI risks over time are in place.\n\n#### MEASURE 3.1\n\n> Approaches, personnel, and documentation are in place to regularly identify and track existing, unanticipated, and emergent AI risks based on factors such as intended and actual performance in deployed contexts.\n\n*AI Actors:* TEVV, AI Impact Assessment, Operation and Monitoring · *Topics:* TEVV, Monitoring, Continual Improvement\n\n**Suggested actions** (6 of 6):\n- Compare AI system risks with:\n    - simpler or traditional models\n    - human baseline performance\n    - other manual performance benchmarks\n- Compare end user and community feedback about deployed AI systems to internal measures of system performance.\n- Assess effectiveness of metrics for identifying and measuring emergent risks.\n- Measure error response times and track response quality.\n- Elicit and track feedback from AI actors in user support roles about the type of metrics, explanations and other system information required for fulsome resolution of system issues. Consider:\n    - Instances where explanations are insufficient for investigating possible error sources or identifying responses.\n    - System metrics, including system logs and explanations, for identifying and diagnosing sources of system error.\n- Elicit and track feedback from AI actors in incident response and support roles about the adequacy of staffing and resources to perform their duties in an effective and timely manner.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- Did your organization implement a risk management system to address risks involved in deploying the identified AI solution (e.g. personnel risk or changes to commercial objectives)?\n- To what extent can users or parties affected by the outputs of the AI system test the AI system and provide feedback?\n- What metrics has the entity developed to measure performance of the AI system, including error logging?\n\n#### MEASURE 3.2\n\n> Risk tracking approaches are considered for settings where AI risks are difficult to assess using currently available measurement techniques or where metrics are not yet available.\n\n*AI Actors:* TEVV, Domain Experts, AI Impact Assessment, Operation and Monitoring · *Topics:* Monitoring, Continual Improvement\n\n**Suggested actions** (3 of 3):\n- Establish processes for tracking emergent risks that may not be measurable with current approaches. Some processes may include:\n    - Recourse mechanisms for faulty AI system outputs.\n    - Bug bounties.\n    - Human-centered design approaches.\n    - User-interaction and experience research.\n    - Participatory stakeholder engagement with affected or potentially impacted individuals and communities.\n- Identify AI actors responsible for tracking emergent risks and inventory methods.\n- Determine and document the rate of occurrence and severity level for complex or difficult-to-measure risks when:\n    - Prioritizing new measurement approaches for deployment tasks.\n    - Allocating AI system risk management resources.\n    - Evaluating AI system improvements.\n    - Making go/no-go decisions for subsequent system iterations.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- Who is ultimately responsible for the decisions of the AI and is this person aware of the intended uses and limitations of the analytic?\n- Who will be responsible for maintaining, re-verifying, monitoring, and updating this AI once deployed?\n- To what extent does the entity communicate its AI strategic goals and objectives to the community of stakeholders?\n\n#### MEASURE 3.3\n\n> Feedback processes for end users and impacted communities to report problems and appeal system outcomes are established and integrated into AI system evaluation metrics.\n\n*AI Actors:* TEVV, AI Deployment, Operation and Monitoring, End-Users, Affected Individuals and Communities · *Topics:* Participation, Contestability, TEVV, Impact Assessment\n\n**Suggested actions** (6 of 6):\n- Measure efficacy of end user and operator error reporting processes.\n- Categorize and analyze type and rate of end user appeal requests and results.\n- Measure feedback activity participation rates and awareness of feedback activity availability.\n- Utilize feedback to analyze measurement approaches and determine subsequent courses of action.\n- Evaluate measurement approaches to determine efficacy for enhancing organizational understanding of real world impacts.\n- Analyze end user and community feedback in close collaboration with domain experts.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- To what extent can users or parties affected by the outputs of the AI system test the AI system and provide feedback?\n- Did your organization address usability problems and test whether user interfaces served their intended purposes?\n- How easily accessible and current is the information available to external stakeholders?\n\n### MEASURE 4 — Feedback on measurement efficacy\n\n*AI RMF 1.0 category statement:* Feedback about efficacy of measurement is gathered and assessed.\n\n#### MEASURE 4.1\n\n> Measurement approaches for identifying AI risks are connected to deployment context(s) and informed through consultation with domain experts and other end users. Approaches are documented.\n\n*AI Actors:* TEVV, AI Deployment, Operation and Monitoring, End-Users, Affected Individuals and Communities · *Topics:* TEVV, Participation, Context of Use\n\n**Suggested actions** (first 4 of 6):\n- Support mechanisms for capturing feedback from system end users (including domain experts, operators, and practitioners). Successful approaches are:\n    - conducted in settings where end users are able to openly share their doubts and insights about AI system output, and in connection to their specific context of use (including setting and task-specific lines of inquiry)\n    - developed and implemented by human-factors and socio-technical domain experts and researchers\n    - designed to ensure control of interviewer and end user subjectivity and biases\n- Identify and document approaches\n    - for evaluating and integrating elicited feedback from system end users\n    - in collaboration with human-factors and socio-technical domain experts,\n    - to actively inform a process of continual improvement.\n- Evaluate feedback from end users alongside evaluated feedback from impacted communities (MEASURE 3.3).\n- Utilize end user feedback to investigate how selected metrics and measurement approaches interact with organizational and operational contexts.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- Did your organization address usability problems and test whether user interfaces served their intended purposes?\n- How will user and peer engagement be integrated into the model development process and periodic performance review once deployed?\n- To what extent can users or parties affected by the outputs of the AI system test the AI system and provide feedback?\n\n#### MEASURE 4.2\n\n> Measurement results regarding AI system trustworthiness in deployment context(s) and across AI lifecycle are informed by input from domain experts and other relevant AI actors to validate whether the system is performing consistently as intended. Results are documented.\n\n*AI Actors:* TEVV, AI Deployment, Domain Experts, Operation and Monitoring, End-Users · *Topics:* TEVV, Participation, Trustworthy Characteristics (all seven)\n\n**Suggested actions** (6 of 6):\n- Integrate feedback from end users, operators, and affected individuals and communities from Map function as inputs to assess AI system trustworthiness characteristics. Ensure both positive and negative feedback is being assessed.\n- Evaluate feedback in connection with AI system trustworthiness characteristics from Measure 2.5 to 2.11.\n- Evaluate feedback regarding end user satisfaction with, and confidence in, AI system performance including whether output is considered valid and reliable, and explainable and interpretable.\n- Identify mechanisms to confirm/support AI system output (e.g. recommendations), and end user perspectives about that output.\n- Measure frequency of AI systems’ override decisions, evaluate and document results, and feed insights back into continual improvement processes.\n- Consult AI actors in impact assessment, human factors and socio-technical tasks to assist with analysis and interpretation of results.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- To what extent does the system/entity consistently measure progress towards stated goals and objectives?\n- What policies has the entity developed to ensure the use of the AI system is consistent with its stated values and principles?\n- To what extent are the model outputs consistent with the entity’s values and principles to foster public trust and equity?\n\n#### MEASURE 4.3\n\n> Measurable performance improvements or declines based on consultations with relevant AI actors including affected communities, and field data about context-relevant risks and trustworthiness characteristics, are identified and documented.\n\n*AI Actors:* TEVV, AI Deployment, Operation and Monitoring, End-Users, Affected Individuals and Communities · *Topics:* TEVV, Participation, Trustworthy Characteristics (all seven)\n\n**Suggested actions** (6 of 6):\n- Develop baseline quantitative measures for trustworthy characteristics.\n- Delimit and characterize baseline operation values and states.\n- Utilize qualitative approaches to augment and complement quantitative baseline measures, in close coordination with impact assessment, human factors and socio-technical AI actors.\n- Monitor and assess measurements as part of continual improvement to identify potential system adjustments or modifications\n- Perform and document sensitivity analysis to characterize actual and expected variance in performance after applying system or procedural updates.\n- Document decisions related to the sensitivity analysis and record expected influence on system performance and identified risks.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- To what extent are the model outputs consistent with the entity’s values and principles to foster public trust and equity?\n- How were sensitive variables (e.g., demographic and socioeconomic categories) that may be subject to regulatory compliance specifically selected or not selected for modeling purposes?\n\n## 9. MANAGE (4 categories, 13 subcategories)\n\nMANAGE allocates risk resources to mapped and measured risks, as GOVERN defines. It covers the go/no-go determination, risk treatment and response, third-party risks, post-deployment monitoring, and plans to respond to, recover from and communicate about incidents.\n\n### MANAGE 1 — Prioritize and respond to assessed risks\n\n*AI RMF 1.0 category statement:* AI risks based on assessments and other analytical output from the MAP and MEASURE functions are prioritized, responded to, and managed.\n\n#### MANAGE 1.1\n\n> A determination is made as to whether the AI system achieves its intended purpose and stated objectives and whether its development or deployment should proceed.\n\n*AI Actors:* AI Deployment, Operation and Monitoring, AI Impact Assessment · *Topics:* AI Deployment, Risk Assessment\n\n**Suggested actions** (5 of 5):\n- Consider trustworthiness characteristics when evaluating AI systems’ negative risks and benefits.\n- Utilize TEVV outputs from map and measure functions when considering risk treatment.\n- Regularly track and monitor negative risks and benefits throughout the AI system lifecycle including in post-deployment monitoring.\n- Regularly assess and document system performance relative to trustworthiness characteristics and tradeoffs between negative risks and opportunities.\n- Evaluate tradeoffs in connection with real-world use cases and impacts and as enumerated in Map function outcomes.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- How do the technical specifications and requirements align with the AI system’s goals and objectives?\n- To what extent are the metrics consistent with system goals, objectives, and constraints, including ethical and compliance considerations?\n- What goals and objectives does the entity expect to achieve by designing, developing, and/or deploying the AI system?\n\n#### MANAGE 1.2\n\n> Treatment of documented AI risks is prioritized based on impact, likelihood, or available resources or methods.\n>\n> *AI RMF 1.0 wording:* Treatment of documented AI risks is prioritized based on impact, likelihood, and available resources or methods.\n\n*AI Actors:* AI Deployment, Operation and Monitoring, AI Impact Assessment · *Topics:* Risk Tolerance\n\n**Suggested actions** (3 of 3):\n- Assign risk management resources relative to established risk tolerance. AI systems with lower risk tolerances receive greater oversight, mitigation and management resources.\n- Document AI risk tolerance determination practices and resource decisions.\n- Regularly review risk tolerances and re-calibrate, as needed, in accordance with information from AI system monitoring and assessment.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- Did your organization implement a risk management system to address risks involved in deploying the identified AI solution (e.g. personnel risk or changes to commercial objectives)?\n- What assessments has the entity conducted on data security and privacy impacts associated with the AI system?\n- Does your organization have an existing governance structure that can be leveraged to oversee the organization’s use of AI?\n\n#### MANAGE 1.3\n\n> Responses to the AI risks deemed high priority as identified by the Map function, are developed, planned, and documented. Risk response options can include mitigating, transferring, avoiding, or accepting.\n\n*AI Actors:* AI Deployment, Operation and Monitoring, AI Impact Assessment · *Topics:* Legal and Regulatory, Risk Tolerance\n\n**Suggested actions** (5 of 5):\n- Observe regulatory and established organizational, sector, discipline, or professional standards and requirements for applying risk tolerances within the organization.\n- Document procedures for acting on AI system risks related to trustworthiness characteristics.\n- Prioritize risks involving physical safety, legal liabilities, regulatory compliance, and negative impacts on individuals, groups, or society.\n- Identify risk response plans and resources and organizational teams for carrying out response functions.\n- Store risk management and system documentation in an organized, secure repository that is accessible by relevant AI Actors and appropriate personnel.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- Has the system been reviewed to ensure the AI system complies with relevant laws, regulations, standards, and guidance?\n- To what extent has the entity defined and documented the regulatory environment—including minimum requirements in laws and regulations?\n\n#### MANAGE 1.4\n\n> Negative residual risks (defined as the sum of all unmitigated risks) to both downstream acquirers of AI systems and end users are documented.\n\n*AI Actors:* AI Deployment, Operation and Monitoring, AI Impact Assessment · *Topics:* Risk Response\n\n**Suggested actions** (3 of 3):\n- Document residual risks within risk response plans, denoting risks that have been accepted, transferred, or subject to minimal mitigation.\n- Establish procedures for disclosing residual risks to relevant downstream AI actors.\n- Inform relevant downstream AI actors of requirements for safe operation, known limitations, and suggested warning labels as identified in MAP 3.4.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- What are the roles, responsibilities, and delegation of authorities of personnel involved in the design, development, deployment, assessment and monitoring of the AI system?\n- Who will be responsible for maintaining, re-verifying, monitoring, and updating this AI once deployed?\n- How will updates/revisions be documented and communicated? How often and by whom?\n\n### MANAGE 2 — Maximize benefits, minimize negative impacts\n\n*AI RMF 1.0 category statement:* Strategies to maximize AI benefits and minimize negative impacts are planned, prepared, implemented, documented, and informed by input from relevant AI actors.\n\n#### MANAGE 2.1\n\n> Resources required to manage AI risks are taken into account, along with viable non-AI alternative systems, approaches, or methods – to reduce the magnitude or likelihood of potential impacts.\n\n*AI Actors:* AI Deployment, Operation and Monitoring, AI Impact Assessment, Governance and Oversight · *Topics:* Risk Tolerance, Trade-offs\n\n**Suggested actions** (first 4 of 6):\n- Plan and implement risk management practices in accordance with established organizational risk tolerances.\n- Verify risk management teams are resourced to carry out functions, including\n    - Establishing processes for considering methods that are not automated; semi-automated; or other procedural alternatives for AI functions.\n    - Enhance AI system transparency mechanisms for AI teams.\n    - Enable exploration of AI system limitations by AI teams.\n    - Identify, assess, and catalog past failed designs and negative impacts or outcomes to avoid known failure modes.\n- Identify resource allocation approaches for managing risks in systems:\n    - deemed high-risk,\n    - that self-update (adaptive, online, reinforcement self-supervised learning or similar),\n    - trained without access to ground truth (unsupervised, semi-supervised, learning or similar),\n    - with high uncertainty or where risk management is insufficient.\n- Regularly seek and integrate external expertise and perspectives to supplement organizational diversity (e.g. demographic, disciplinary), equity, inclusion, and accessibility where internal capacity is lacking.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- Are mechanisms in place to evaluate whether internal teams are empowered and resourced to effectively carry out risk management functions?\n- How will user and other forms of stakeholder engagement be integrated into risk management processes?\n\n#### MANAGE 2.2\n\n> Mechanisms are in place and applied to sustain the value of deployed AI systems.\n\n*AI Actors:* AI Deployment, Operation and Monitoring, AI Impact Assessment, Governance and Oversight · *Topics:* AI Deployment, Drift, Societal Values\n\n**Suggested actions** (first 4 of 5):\n- Establish risk controls considering trustworthiness characteristics, including:\n    - Data management, quality, and privacy (e.g. minimization, rectification or deletion requests) controls as part of organizational data governance policies.\n    - Machine learning and end-point security countermeasures (e.g., robust models, differential privacy, authentication, throttling).\n    - Business rules that augment, limit or restrict AI system outputs within certain contexts\n    - Utilizing domain expertise related to deployment context for continuous improvement and TEVV across the AI lifecycle.\n    - Development and regular tracking of human-AI teaming configurations.\n    - *…and 4 more*\n- Establish mechanisms to capture feedback from system end users and potentially impacted groups while system is in deployment.\n- Establish mechanisms to capture feedback from system end users and potentially impacted groups about how changes in system deployment (e.g., introducing new technology, decommissioning algorithms and models, adapting system, model or algorithm) may create negative impacts that are not visible along the AI lifecycle.\n- Review insurance policies, warranties, or contracts for legal or oversight requirements for risk transfer procedures.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- To what extent can users or parties affected by the outputs of the AI system test the AI system and provide feedback?\n- Could the AI system expose people to harm or negative impacts? What was done to mitigate or reduce the potential for harm?\n\n#### MANAGE 2.3\n\n> Procedures are followed to respond to and recover from a previously unknown risk when it is identified.\n\n*AI Actors:* AI Deployment, Operation and Monitoring · *Topics:* Risk Response\n\n**Suggested actions** (first 6 of 7):\n- Protocols, resources, and metrics are in place for continual monitoring of AI systems’ performance, trustworthiness, and alignment with contextual norms and values\n- Establish and regularly review treatment and response plans for incidents, negative impacts, or outcomes.\n- Establish and maintain procedures to regularly monitor system components for drift, decontextualization, or other AI system behavior factors,\n- Establish and maintain procedures for capturing feedback about negative impacts.\n- Verify contingency processes to handle any negative impacts associated with mission-critical AI systems, and to deactivate systems.\n- Enable preventive and post-hoc exploration of AI system limitations by relevant AI actor groups.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- Who will be responsible for maintaining, re-verifying, monitoring, and updating this AI once deployed?\n- Are the responsibilities of the personnel involved in the various AI governance processes clearly defined?\n- What processes exist for data generation, acquisition/collection, ingestion, staging/storage, transformations, security, maintenance, and dissemination?\n\n#### MANAGE 2.4\n\n> Mechanisms are in place and applied, responsibilities are assigned and understood to supersede, disengage, or deactivate AI systems that demonstrate performance or outcomes inconsistent with intended use.\n\n*AI Actors:* AI Deployment, Operation and Monitoring, Governance and Oversight · *Topics:* Risk Response, Decommission, Risky Emergent Behavior\n\n**Suggested actions** (first 6 of 8):\n- Regularly review established procedures for AI system bypass actions, including plans for redundant or backup systems to ensure continuity of operational and/or business functionality.\n- Regularly review Identify system incident thresholds for activating bypass or deactivation responses.\n- Apply change management processes to understand the upstream and downstream consequences of bypassing or deactivating an AI system or AI system components.\n- Apply protocols, resources and metrics for decisions to supersede, bypass or deactivate AI systems or AI system components.\n- Preserve materials for forensic, regulatory, and legal review.\n- Conduct internal root cause analysis and process reviews of bypass or deactivation events.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- What are the roles, responsibilities, and delegation of authorities of personnel involved in the design, development, deployment, assessment and monitoring of the AI system?\n- Did your organization implement a risk management system to address risks involved in deploying the identified AI solution (e.g. personnel risk or changes to commercial objectives)?\n\n### MANAGE 3 — Third-party risks and benefits\n\n*AI RMF 1.0 category statement:* AI risks and benefits from third-party entities are managed.\n\n#### MANAGE 3.1\n\n> AI risks and benefits from third-party resources are regularly monitored, and risk controls are applied and documented.\n\n*AI Actors:* Third-party entities, Operation and Monitoring, AI Deployment · *Topics:* Third-party, Supply Chain\n\n**Suggested actions** (first 6 of 9):\n- Apply organizational risk tolerance to third-party AI systems.\n- Apply and document organizational risk management plans and practices to third-party AI technology, personnel, or other resources.\n- Identify and maintain documentation for third-party AI systems and components.\n- Establish testing, evaluation, validation and verification processes for third-party AI systems which address the needs for transparency without exposing proprietary algorithms.\n- Establish processes to identify beneficial use and risk indicators in third-party systems or components, such as inconsistent software release schedule, sparse documentation, and incomplete software change management (e.g., lack of forward or backward compatibility).\n- Organizations can establish processes for third parties to report known and potential vulnerabilities, risks or biases in supplied resources.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- If a third party created the AI system or some of its components, how will you ensure a level of explainability or interpretability? Is there documentation?\n- If your organization obtained datasets from a third party, did your organization assess and manage the risks of using such datasets?\n\n#### MANAGE 3.2\n\n> Pre-trained models which are used for development are monitored as part of AI system regular monitoring and maintenance.\n\n*AI Actors:* Third-party entities, Operation and Monitoring, AI Deployment · *Topics:* Pre-trained models, Monitoring\n\n**Suggested actions** (5 of 5):\n- Identify pre-trained models within AI system inventory for risk tracking.\n- Establish processes to independently and continually monitor performance and trustworthiness of pre-trained models, and as part of third-party risk tracking.\n- Monitor performance and trustworthiness of AI system components connected to pre-trained models, and as part of third-party risk tracking.\n- Identify, document and remediate risks arising from AI system components and pre-trained models per organizational risk management procedures, and as part of third-party risk tracking.\n- Decommission AI system components and pre-trained models which exceed risk tolerances, and as part of third-party risk tracking.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- How has the entity documented the AI system’s data provenance, including sources, origins, transformations, augmentations, labels, dependencies, constraints, and metadata?\n- Does this dataset collection/processing procedure achieve the motivation for creating the dataset stated in the first section of this datasheet?\n\n### MANAGE 4 — Risk treatment, monitoring and communication plans\n\n*AI RMF 1.0 category statement:* Risk treatments, including response and recovery, and communication plans for the identified and measured AI risks are documented and monitored regularly.\n\n#### MANAGE 4.1\n\n> Post-deployment AI system monitoring plans are implemented, including mechanisms for capturing and evaluating input from users and other relevant AI actors, appeal and override, decommissioning, incident response, recovery, and change management.\n\n*AI Actors:* AI Deployment, Operation and Monitoring, End-Users, Human Factors, Domain Experts, Affected Individuals and Communities · *Topics:* Monitoring, Participation, AI Deployment, AI Incidents, Risk Response, Adversarial, Risky Emergent Behavior\n\n**Suggested actions** (first 6 of 9):\n- Establish and maintain procedures to monitor AI system performance for risks and negative and positive impacts associated with trustworthiness characteristics.\n- Perform post-deployment TEVV tasks to evaluate AI system validity and reliability, bias and fairness, privacy, and security and resilience.\n- Evaluate AI system trustworthiness in conditions similar to deployment context of use, and prior to deployment.\n- Establish and implement red-teaming exercises at a prescribed cadence, and evaluate their efficacy.\n- Establish procedures for tracking dataset modifications such as data deletion or rectification requests.\n- Establish mechanisms for regular communication and feedback between relevant AI actors and internal or external stakeholders to capture information about system performance, trustworthiness and impact.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- To what extent has the entity documented the post-deployment AI system’s testing methodology, metrics, and performance outcomes?\n- How easily accessible and current is the information available to external stakeholders?\n\n#### MANAGE 4.2\n\n> Measurable activities for continual improvements are integrated into AI system updates and include regular engagement with interested parties, including relevant AI actors.\n\n*AI Actors:* TEVV, AI Design, AI Development, AI Deployment, Operation and Monitoring, End-Users, Affected Individuals and Communities · *Topics:* Monitoring, Impact Assessment, Risk Assessment, Continual Improvement\n\n**Suggested actions** (5 of 5):\n- Integrate trustworthiness characteristics into protocols and metrics used for continual improvement.\n- Establish processes for evaluating and integrating feedback into AI system improvements.\n- Assess and evaluate alignment of proposed improvements with relevant regulatory and legal frameworks\n- Assess and evaluate alignment of proposed improvements connected to the values and norms within the context of use.\n- Document the basis for decisions made relative to tradeoffs between trustworthy characteristics, system risks, and system opportunities\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- How will user and other forms of stakeholder engagement be integrated into the model development process and regular performance review once deployed?\n- To what extent can users or parties affected by the outputs of the AI system test the AI system and provide feedback?\n- To what extent has the entity defined and documented the regulatory environment—including minimum requirements in laws and regulations?\n\n#### MANAGE 4.3\n\n> Incidents and errors are communicated to relevant AI actors including affected communities. Processes for tracking, responding to, and recovering from incidents and errors are followed and documented.\n\n*AI Actors:* AI Deployment, Operation and Monitoring, End-Users, Human Factors, Domain Experts, Affected Individuals and Communities · *Topics:* AI Incidents, Monitoring\n\n**Suggested actions** (5 of 5):\n- Establish procedures to regularly share information about errors, incidents and negative impacts with relevant stakeholders, operators, practitioners and users, and impacted parties.\n- Maintain a database of reported errors, near-misses, incidents and negative impacts including date reported, number of reports, assessment of impact and severity, and responses.\n- Maintain a database of system changes, reason for change, and details of how the change was made, tested and deployed.\n- Maintain version history information and metadata to enable continuous improvement processes.\n- Verify that relevant AI actors responsible for identifying complex or emergent risks are properly resourced and empowered.\n\n**Documentation prompts:**\n- What corrective actions has the entity taken to enhance the quality, accuracy, reliability, and representativeness of the data?\n- To what extent does the entity communicate its AI strategic goals and objectives to the community of stakeholders? How easily accessible and current is the information available to external stakeholders?\n\n## Exam Angles\n\n- **Framework vs Playbook.** AI RMF 1.0 defines *what*: the outcome for each category and subcategory. The Playbook gives *how*: suggested actions, documentation prompts and references for each subcategory. \"Where do I find concrete steps for MEASURE 2.11?\" is the Playbook. \"What outcome does MEASURE 2.11 require?\" is the RMF Core.\n- **Status traps.** Voluntary, not a checklist, not an ordered sequence, no certification. Options saying an organization must implement every suggested action, must work through subcategories in order, or can be certified against the Playbook are wrong.\n- **GOVERN is cross-cutting.** It informs and is infused throughout the other three functions and applies to all stages of the organization's risk management. MAP, MEASURE and MANAGE apply to specific systems and lifecycle stages. Once governance is in place, most users start with MAP and continue to MEASURE or MANAGE, iterating as needed.\n- **Placing a scenario.** Organization-wide policy, roles, training, culture and third-party policy point to GOVERN. Purpose, users, setting, risk tolerance, categorization, benefits and costs, component risks and impacts point to MAP. Metrics, TEVV and evaluation of trustworthiness characteristics point to MEASURE. Go/no-go decisions, risk treatment, third-party monitoring, post-deployment monitoring and incident communication point to MANAGE.\n- **MEASURE 2 mirrors the trustworthiness characteristics.** 2.5 valid and reliable, 2.6 safe, 2.7 secure and resilient, 2.8 transparency and accountability, 2.9 explainable and interpretable, 2.10 privacy, 2.11 fairness and bias. 2.12 adds environmental impact and 2.13 checks TEVV effectiveness. With 13 subcategories it is the largest category.\n- **Look-alike pairs.** MEASURE 2.4 (production behaviour monitored, e.g. drift alerts against pre-deployment tests) vs MANAGE 4.1 (the post-deployment monitoring plan: user input, appeal and override, decommissioning, incident response, recovery, change management). GOVERN 6.1 (third-party policies) vs MAP 4.1 (mapping component, legal and IP risks) vs MANAGE 3.1–3.2 (monitoring third-party resources and pre-trained models). GOVERN 1.7 (decommissioning policies) vs MANAGE 2.4 (mechanisms to supersede, disengage or deactivate).\n- **AI actors.** The Playbook tags each subcategory with the Appendix A actor groups most involved. TEVV is tagged on every MEASURE subcategory. AI Deployment and Operation and Monitoring are tagged on every MANAGE subcategory. Governance and Oversight dominates GOVERN. Affected individuals and communities count as AI actors even if they never use the system.\n- **Risk response vocabulary.** MANAGE 1.3: responses to high-priority risks can include mitigating, transferring, avoiding or accepting. MANAGE 1.4: residual risk is \"the sum of all unmitigated risks\", documented for downstream acquirers and end users.\n- **Wording and currency.** Where the Playbook words a statement differently from the RMF tables (the RMF text is shown under those statements in sections 6–9), use the RMF text. AI RMF 1.0 is under revision as of 2026 and the Playbook will follow; answer from the versions currently published.\n\n## Key Terms\n\n- [[Suggested Actions (AI RMF Playbook)]]\n- [[Transparency and Documentation Prompts (AI RMF Playbook)]]\n- [[AI Actors (NIST AI RMF)]]\n- [[AI RMF Subcategory]]\n- [[NIST AI RMF Playbook]]\n\n## Sources\n\n- https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/\n- https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/faq/\n- https://airc.nist.gov/docs/playbook.json\n- https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework/nist-ai-rmf-playbook\n- https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\n- https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework\n- https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework/ai-risk-management-framework-faqs\n- https://www.nist.gov/system/files/documents/2026/03/27/03_AI%20Update-March.pdf", "ref_600": "## At a Glance\n\nNIST AI 600-1, the Generative AI Profile (July 2024) — the AI RMF's companion for generative AI, developed under Executive Order 14110. It names 12 risks unique to or amplified by GAI (confabulation, harmful bias and homogenization, information integrity, data privacy, CBRN information, dangerous/violent content, environmental impacts, human-AI configuration, information security, IP, obscene content, value-chain integration) and maps ~200 suggested actions onto the RMF's Govern/Map/Measure/Manage subcategories.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nKnow what a 'profile' is (the RMF tailored to a context), the EO 14110 origin, and the flagship risks — confabulation (the formal term for hallucination), information integrity, harmful bias/homogenization, value-chain risk, and the four cross-cutting considerations: governance, content provenance, pre-deployment testing, incident disclosure.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- A cross-sectoral AI RMF profile for generative AI — same four functions, GAI-specific risks and actions\n- Mandated by Executive Order 14110 on Safe, Secure, and Trustworthy AI (Oct 2023); published July 2024\n- 12 GAI risks including: confabulation, harmful bias & homogenization, information integrity, data privacy, information security, CBRN information capabilities, IP, value-chain integration\n- Four primary considerations: governance, content provenance (tracking origin/history of content), pre-deployment testing, incident disclosure\n- Suggested actions are organized by RMF subcategory — the profile is a to-do overlay, not a new framework\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] NIST AI 600-1 — Structured Reference\n\n# NIST AI 600-1 — Structured Reference\n\n> **Full Title:** Artificial Intelligence Risk Management Framework: Generative Artificial Intelligence Profile\n> **Published:** July 2024\n> **NIST AI 600-1**\n> **DOI:** `10.6028/NIST.AI.600-1`\n> **Contact:** `ai-inquiries@nist.gov`\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\nNIST AI 600-1 is a **cross-sectoral profile** and companion resource for the [[NIST AI RMF — Structured Reference|AI RMF 1.0]] focused on **Generative AI (GAI)**. Developed pursuant to President Biden's Executive Order (EO) 14110 on Safe, Secure, and Trustworthy AI.\n\n> [!summary] What Is an AI RMF Profile?\n> A profile is an implementation of AI RMF functions, categories, and subcategories for a **specific setting, application, or technology** — based on the requirements, risk tolerance, and resources of the Framework user. Cross-sectoral profiles cover risks common across sectors (e.g., LLMs, cloud services, acquisition).\n\n### Document Structure\n\n| Section | Content |\n|---------|---------|\n| **Section 1** | Introduction — relationship to AI RMF, EO 14110, GAI PWG |\n| **Section 2** | 12 GAI-specific risks defined and described |\n| **Section 3** | Suggested actions organized by AI RMF subcategory (Govern, Map, Measure, Manage) |\n| **Appendix A** | Four primary GAI considerations: Governance, Pre-Deployment Testing, Content Provenance, Incident Disclosure |\n| **Appendix B** | References |\n\n### Four Primary GAI Considerations (GAI PWG Focus)\n\n1. **Governance** — Organizational structures, policies, third-party oversight\n2. **Content Provenance** — Tracking origin and history of digital content\n3. **Pre-Deployment Testing** — TEVV before release\n4. **Incident Disclosure** — Reporting, documenting, and sharing incident information\n\n---\n\n## 2. The 12 GAI Risks\n\nThese are risks **unique to or exacerbated by** generative AI. Each is mapped to relevant AI RMF Trustworthy AI Characteristics.\n\n> [!danger] Risk Categorization (Optional Framework)\n> Organizations may further categorize these as:\n> 1. **Technical / Model risks** (malfunction): Confabulation; Dangerous Recommendations; Data Privacy; Value Chain; Harmful Bias; Homogenization\n> 2. **Misuse by humans** (malicious): CBRN; Data Privacy; Human-AI Configuration; Obscene Content; Information Integrity; Information Security\n> 3. **Ecosystem / societal** (systemic): Data Privacy; Environmental; Intellectual Property\n\n### Risk 1: CBRN Information or Capabilities\n\n| Attribute | Detail |\n|-----------|--------|\n| **Definition** | Eased access to or synthesis of nefarious information/design capabilities related to chemical, biological, radiological, or nuclear weapons |\n| **Trustworthy AI Characteristics** | Safe, Explainable and Interpretable |\n| **Current evidence** | LLMs currently provide minimal assistance beyond search engines; physical synthesis still requires expertise and infrastructure |\n| **Future concern** | Biological design tools (BDTs) may augment capabilities beyond text-based LLMs |\n\n### Risk 2: Confabulation\n\n| Attribute | Detail |\n|-----------|--------|\n| **Definition** | Production of confidently stated but erroneous or false content (colloquially \"hallucinations\" or \"fabrications\") |\n| **Trustworthy AI Characteristics** | Fair with Harmful Bias Managed, Safe, Valid and Reliable, Explainable and Interpretable |\n| **Mechanism** | Natural result of statistical prediction — models generate outputs approximating training data distribution |\n| **Key concern** | Confabulated citations/logic may further mislead users into inappropriate trust |\n| **Domains at risk** | Healthcare (misdiagnosis), legal (fake case citations), consequential decision-making |\n\n> [!warning] Terminology Note\n> Some commenters note that \"hallucination\" and \"fabrication\" anthropomorphize GAI, which itself is a risk (see Human-AI Configuration).\n\n### Risk 3: Dangerous, Violent, or Hateful Content\n\n| Attribute | Detail |\n|-----------|--------|\n| **Definition** | Eased production of and access to violent, inciting, radicalizing, or threatening content; difficulty controlling public exposure to hateful/denigrating/stereotyping content |\n| **Trustworthy AI Characteristics** | Safe, Secure and Resilient |\n| **Includes** | Self-harm recommendations, criminal/illegal activity instructions, jailbreaking to circumvent output controls |\n| **Mental health concern** | Users may disclose mental health issues to chatbots; negative reactions to unhelpful responses in distress |\n\n### Risk 4: Data Privacy\n\n| Attribute | Detail |\n|-----------|--------|\n| **Definition** | Leakage and unauthorized use, disclosure, or de-anonymization of biometric, health, location, PII, or sensitive data |\n| **Trustworthy AI Characteristics** | Accountable and Transparent, Privacy Enhanced, Safe, Secure and Resilient |\n| **Key mechanisms** | Training data memorization, inference of PII from disparate sources, lack of transparency about training data |\n| **Downstream impact** | Wrong inferences of PII can lead to representational or allocative harms |\n\n### Risk 5: Environmental Impacts\n\n| Attribute | Detail |\n|-----------|--------|\n| **Definition** | High compute resource utilization in training/operating GAI models, related outcomes adversely impacting ecosystems |\n| **Trustworthy AI Characteristics** | Accountable and Transparent, Safe |\n| **Scale** | Training a single transformer LLM ≈ carbon emissions of 300 round-trip flights (SF↔NY) |\n| **Inference** | Generative tasks more energy/carbon-intensive than discriminative tasks |\n| **No agreed method** | Currently no standardized method to estimate environmental impacts from GAI |\n\n### Risk 6: Harmful Bias and Homogenization\n\n| Attribute | Detail |\n|-----------|--------|\n| **Definition** | Amplification of historical/societal/systemic biases; performance disparities between sub-groups or languages; undesired output homogeneity |\n| **Trustworthy AI Characteristics** | Fair with Harmful Bias Managed, Valid and Reliable |\n| **Examples** | Text-to-image models underrepresenting women/racial minorities in professional roles |\n| **Homogenization** | Skewed, overly uniform output distributions; foundation models as \"bottlenecks\" / single points of failure |\n| **Model collapse** | Over-reliance on synthetic data → data points disappearing from output distribution |\n\n### Risk 7: Human-AI Configuration\n\n| Attribute | Detail |\n|-----------|--------|\n| **Definition** | Arrangements of or interactions between human and AI system resulting in inappropriate anthropomorphization, algorithmic aversion, automation bias, over-reliance, or emotional entanglement |\n| **Trustworthy AI Characteristics** | Accountable and Transparent, Explainable and Interpretable, Fair with Harmful Bias Managed, Privacy Enhanced, Safe, Valid and Reliable |\n| **Algorithmic aversion** | Human experts unnecessarily avoiding GAI's beneficial uses |\n| **Automation bias** | Excessive deference to automated systems; exacerbates confabulation and bias risks |\n\n### Risk 8: Information Integrity\n\n| Attribute | Detail |\n|-----------|--------|\n| **Definition** | Lowered barriers to generate and support exchange/consumption of content that may not distinguish fact from opinion/fiction, or could be leveraged for large-scale dis-/misinformation campaigns |\n| **Trustworthy AI Characteristics** | Accountable and Transparent, Safe, Valid and Reliable, Interpretable and Explainable |\n| **Misinformation** | Unintentional production/dissemination of false content (often from confabulations) |\n| **Disinformation** | Deliberate production/dissemination at scale; highly realistic deepfakes |\n| **Real-world impact** | Synthetic Pentagon blast image went viral, briefly dropped stock market |\n\n### Risk 9: Information Security\n\n| Attribute | Detail |\n|-----------|--------|\n| **Definition** | Lowered barriers for offensive cyber capabilities; expanded attack surface (prompt injection, data poisoning) |\n| **Trustworthy AI Characteristics** | Privacy Enhanced, Safe, Secure and Resilient, Valid and Reliable |\n| **Offensive** | LLMs discovering vulnerabilities, writing exploit code, GAI-powered security co-pilots |\n| **Defensive gaps** | Direct prompt injection, indirect prompt injection (exploiting LLM-integrated apps), data poisoning |\n| **Disclosure risk** | Querying closed production models can elicit previously undisclosed information |\n\n### Risk 10: Intellectual Property\n\n| Attribute | Detail |\n|-----------|--------|\n| **Definition** | Eased production/replication of copyrighted, trademarked, or licensed content without authorization; exposure of trade secrets; plagiarism |\n| **Trustworthy AI Characteristics** | Accountable and Transparent, Fair with Harmful Bias Managed, Privacy Enhanced |\n| **Legal status** | Relationship of GAI to copyright actively debated in legal fora; training data memorization may infringe copyright |\n| **Likeness concern** | Use/emulation of personal identity, likeness, or voice without permission |\n\n### Risk 11: Obscene, Degrading, and/or Abusive Content\n\n| Attribute | Detail |\n|-----------|--------|\n| **Definition** | Eased production of and access to illegal non-consensual intimate imagery (NCII) of adults, and/or child sexual abuse material (CSAM) |\n| **Trustworthy AI Characteristics** | Fair with Harmful Bias Managed, Safe, Privacy Enhanced |\n| **Scale** | Moved from niche internet forums to mainstream, automated, scaled online businesses |\n| **Training data risk** | Commonly used GAI training datasets found to contain known CSAM images |\n| **Disproportionate impact** | NCII disproportionately impacts women and sexual minorities |\n\n### Risk 12: Value Chain and Component Integration\n\n| Attribute | Detail |\n|-----------|--------|\n| **Definition** | Non-transparent or untraceable integration of upstream third-party components; improper supplier vetting; diminished accountability for downstream users |\n| **Trustworthy AI Characteristics** | Accountable and Transparent, Explainable and Interpretable, Fair, Privacy Enhanced, Safe, Secure and Resilient, Valid and Reliable |\n| **Exacerbated for GAI** | Training data too large for humans to vet; extensive reuse of limited foundation models; integration into many devices/services |\n| **Benchmark instability** | Label errors in test datasets affect benchmark stability used during model selection |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Risk Dimensions\n\nGAI risks vary along multiple dimensions:\n\n| Dimension | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **AI Lifecycle Stage** | Design, development, deployment, operation, decommissioning |\n| **Scope** | Model/system level → Application/implementation level → Ecosystem level |\n| **Source** | GAI model itself (inputs/outputs), human behavior, human-AI interactions |\n| **Time Scale** | Abrupt (immediate emotional harm) → Extended (long-term disinformation erosion of trust) |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Suggested Actions — Govern Function\n\nThe suggested actions are organized by AI RMF subcategories and tagged with Action IDs. Below are the key Govern subcategories covered:\n\n### Govern 1.1 — Legal & Regulatory Requirements\n\n| Action ID | Key Action |\n|-----------|------------|\n| GV-1.1-001 | Align GAI development/use with applicable laws (data privacy, copyright, IP) |\n\n### Govern 1.2 — Trustworthy AI Characteristics in Policies\n\n| Action ID | Key Action |\n|-----------|------------|\n| GV-1.2-001 | Establish transparency policies for training data origin/history |\n| GV-1.2-002 | Evaluate risk-relevant capabilities and robustness of safety measures pre-deployment and ongoing |\n\n### Govern 1.3 — Risk Tolerance Determination\n\n| Action ID | Key Action |\n|-----------|------------|\n| GV-1.3-001 | Consider GAI-specific factors when defining risk tiers (information integrity, psychological impacts, malicious use, etc.) |\n| GV-1.3-002 | Establish minimum thresholds for performance/assurance as part of deployment \"go/no-go\" policies |\n| GV-1.3-003 | Establish test plan and response policy for CBRN/offensive cyber capabilities before developing highly capable models |\n| GV-1.3-004 | Obtain input from stakeholder communities to identify unacceptable use |\n| GV-1.3-005 | Maintain updated hierarchy of identified and expected GAI risks |\n| GV-1.3-006 | Reevaluate risk tolerances for unacceptable negative risk and broad GAI risks |\n| GV-1.3-007 | Devise a plan to halt development or deployment of systems posing unacceptable negative risk |\n\n### Govern 1.4 — Transparent Risk Management\n\n| Action ID | Key Action |\n|-----------|------------|\n| GV-1.4-001 | Prevent GAI systems from generating CSAM, NCII, or illegal content |\n| GV-1.4-002 | Establish transparent acceptable use policies addressing illegal uses |\n\n### Govern 1.6 — AI System Inventory\n\n| Action ID | Key Action |\n|-----------|------------|\n| GV-1.6-001 | Enumerate organizational GAI systems for inventory |\n| GV-1.6-003 | Include in inventory: data provenance, known issues, human oversight roles, IP considerations, underlying foundation models |\n\n### Govern 3.2 — Human-AI Configuration Roles\n\n| Action ID | Key Action |\n|-----------|------------|\n| GV-3.2-001 | Bolster oversight with independent evaluations proportional to identified risks |\n| GV-3.2-003 | Define acceptable use policies for GAI interfaces, modalities, and human-AI configurations |\n| GV-3.2-005 | Engage in threat modeling to anticipate potential risks from GAI systems |\n\n### Govern 4.1 — Safety-First Mindset\n\n| Action ID | Key Action |\n|-----------|------------|\n| GV-4.1-001 | Establish continual improvement processes for GAI risk measurement |\n| GV-4.1-002 | Establish risk measurement in context of use with standardized measurement protocols and structured public feedback |\n\n### Govern 6.1 — Third-Party Risk Management\n\n| Action ID | Key Action |\n|-----------|------------|\n| GV-6.1-001 | Categorize GAI content types with associated third-party rights |\n| GV-6.1-004 | Draft SLAs specifying content ownership, usage rights, security, provenance |\n| GV-6.1-005 | Implement use-case-based supplier risk assessment framework |\n| GV-6.1-009 | Update due diligence processes for GAI acquisition/procurement |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Suggested Actions — Map Function\n\n### Map 1.1 — Intended Purpose & Context\n\n| Action ID | Key Action |\n|-----------|------------|\n| MP-1.1-001 | Identify intended purposes considering internal vs. external use, fine-tuning, data sources (RAG) |\n| MP-1.1-002 | Document expected/acceptable context of use with socio-cultural and domain experts |\n| MP-1.1-003 | Document risk measurement plans addressing cognitive biases, known incidents, foreseeable misuse |\n| MP-1.1-004 | Identify and document foreseeable illegal uses surpassing risk tolerances |\n\n### Map 1.2 — Interdisciplinary Teams\n\n| Action ID | Key Action |\n|-----------|------------|\n| MP-1.2-001 | Establish interdisciplinary teams reflecting diverse capabilities, demographics, domain expertise |\n| MP-1.2-002 | Verify data/benchmarks and feedback exercise participants are representative of user populations |\n\n### Map 4.1 — Legal & Third-Party Risk Mapping\n\n| Action ID | Key Action |\n|-----------|------------|\n| MP-4.1-001 | Conduct periodic monitoring of AI-generated content for privacy risks |\n| MP-4.1-005 | Establish policies for data collection, retention, and minimum quality |\n| MP-4.1-008 | Re-evaluate risks when adapting GAI models to new domains |\n\n### Map 5.1 — Impact Likelihood & Magnitude\n\n| Action ID | Key Action |\n|-----------|------------|\n| MP-5.1-001 | Apply TEVV practices for content provenance |\n| MP-5.1-002 | Identify and rank potential content provenance harms (misinformation, deepfakes, NCII) |\n| MP-5.1-003 | Consider disclosing use of GAI to end users in relevant contexts |\n| MP-5.1-005 | Conduct adversarial role-playing, GAI red-teaming, or chaos testing |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Suggested Actions — Measure Function\n\n### Measure 1.1 — Risk Measurement Approaches\n\n| Action ID | Key Action |\n|-----------|------------|\n| MS-1.1-001 | Employ methods to trace origin and modifications of digital content |\n| MS-1.1-003 | Disaggregate evaluation metrics by demographic factors |\n| MS-1.1-004 | Develop suite of metrics for structured public feedback exercises |\n| MS-1.1-005 | Evaluate novel methods for measuring GAI-related risks (content provenance, CBRN, cyber) |\n| MS-1.1-006 | Implement continuous monitoring to identify whether GAI outputs are equitable across sub-populations |\n| MS-1.1-009 | Track and document risks that cannot be measured quantitatively |\n\n### Measure 2.5 — Validity & Reliability\n\n| Action ID | Key Action |\n|-----------|------------|\n| MS-2.5-001 | Avoid extrapolating GAI performance from narrow, non-systematic assessments |\n| MS-2.5-003 | Review and verify sources/citations in GAI outputs during pre-deployment and ongoing monitoring |\n| MS-2.5-004 | Track instances of anthropomorphization in GAI system interfaces |\n| MS-2.5-006 | Regularly review security and safety guardrails, especially in novel circumstances |\n\n### Measure 2.6 — Safety\n\n| Action ID | Key Action |\n|-----------|------------|\n| MS-2.6-001 | Assess adverse health/wellbeing impacts for value chain actors exposed to explicit/violent content |\n| MS-2.6-002 | Assess harmful bias, IP infringement, data privacy violations, CBRN in training data |\n| MS-2.6-006 | Verify systems properly handle queries that may give rise to malicious/illegal usage |\n| MS-2.6-007 | Regularly evaluate GAI system vulnerabilities to circumvention of safety measures |\n\n### Measure 2.7 — Security & Resilience\n\n| Action ID | Key Action |\n|-----------|------------|\n| MS-2.7-001 | Apply established security measures: backdoors, data breaches, model theft, prompt injection |\n| MS-2.7-007 | Perform AI red-teaming to assess resilience against attacks on other systems, GAI attacks, ML attacks |\n| MS-2.7-008 | Verify fine-tuning does not compromise safety and security controls |\n\n### Measure 2.11 — Fairness & Bias\n\n| Action ID | Key Action |\n|-----------|------------|\n| MS-2.11-001 | Apply use-case appropriate benchmarks to quantify systemic bias, stereotyping, denigration |\n| MS-2.11-002 | Conduct fairness assessments: measure performance across demographic groups, field testing, AI red-teaming |\n| MS-2.11-004 | Review sources of bias in training and TEVV data: distribution differences, proxies, digital divide |\n| MS-2.11-005 | Assess proportion of synthetic to non-synthetic training data to mitigate model collapse |\n\n### Measure 2.12 — Environmental\n\n| Action ID | Key Action |\n|-----------|------------|\n| MS-2.12-002 | Document anticipated environmental impacts in product design decisions |\n| MS-2.12-003 | Measure or estimate environmental impacts (energy, water) for training, fine-tuning, deployment |\n\n---\n\n## 7. Suggested Actions — Manage Function\n\n### Manage 1.3 — Risk Response\n\n| Action ID | Key Action |\n|-----------|------------|\n| MG-1.3-001 | Document trade-offs and decision processes; consider staged release; mitigate/transfer/avoid risks exceeding tolerance |\n| MG-1.3-002 | Monitor robustness and effectiveness of risk controls via red-teaming, field testing, user feedback |\n\n### Manage 2.2 — Sustaining Deployed System Value\n\n| Action ID | Key Action |\n|-----------|------------|\n| MG-2.2-001 | Compare GAI outputs against organizational risk tolerance, guidelines, and principles |\n| MG-2.2-005 | Engage in due diligence to analyze GAI output for harmful content, CBRN, NCII |\n| MG-2.2-008 | Use structured feedback mechanisms to capture user input about AI-generated content |\n\n### Manage 2.4 — Supersede / Disengage / Deactivate\n\n| Action ID | Key Action |\n|-----------|------------|\n| MG-2.4-001 | Maintain communication plans for deactivation, including for open-source models |\n| MG-2.4-002 | Establish procedures for escalating GAI incidents when deactivation criteria met |\n| MG-2.4-004 | Establish and regularly review criteria warranting deactivation in accordance with risk tolerances |\n\n### Manage 3.1 — Third-Party Monitoring\n\n| Action ID | Key Action |\n|-----------|------------|\n| MG-3.1-001 | Apply organizational risk tolerances to third-party datasets, fine-tuned models, domain adaptations |\n| MG-3.1-002 | Test GAI value chain risks (data poisoning, malware, labor practices, data privacy compliance) |\n| MG-3.1-003 | Re-assess model risks after fine-tuning or RAG implementation for unevaluated applications |\n\n### Manage 3.2 — Pre-Trained Model Monitoring\n\n| Action ID | Key Action |\n|-----------|------------|\n| MG-3.2-001 | Apply XAI techniques for continuous improvement |\n| MG-3.2-005 | Implement content filters to prevent inappropriate/harmful/false/illegal content including CSAM and NCII |\n| MG-3.2-008 | Use human moderation systems aligned with socio-cultural norms where AI performs poorly |\n| MG-3.2-009 | Decommission or retrain pre-trained models performing outside defined limits |\n\n### Manage 4.1 — Post-Deployment Monitoring\n\n| Action ID | Key Action |\n|-----------|------------|\n| MG-4.1-001 | Collaborate with external researchers to maintain awareness of emerging best practices |\n| MG-4.1-002 | Maintain post-deployment monitoring for confabulation, CBRN, and cyber risks |\n| MG-4.1-005 | Share transparency reports detailing steps taken to enhance transparency and accountability |\n\n### Manage 4.3 — Incident Communication\n\n| Action ID | Key Action |\n|-----------|------------|\n| MG-4.3-001 | Conduct after-action assessments for GAI system incidents |\n| MG-4.3-002 | Record and track GAI system reported errors, near-misses, and negative impacts |\n| MG-4.3-003 | Report GAI incidents in compliance with legal/regulatory requirements |\n\n---\n\n## 8. Appendix A — Four Primary GAI Considerations (Detailed)\n\n### A.1 Governance\n\n> [!info] Key Points\n> - **Organizational:** GAI may call for different levels of oversight, additional human review, more documentation\n> - **Broader AI actor set:** Data labelers, content moderators, code reviewers, text generators, summarization, search, chat\n> - **Governance tools:** Acceptable use policies, risk-based controls, accessibility accommodations, whistleblower protections, workforce diversity\n> - **Third-party:** Varying risk controls for foundation models vs. fine-tuned models vs. embedded tools; SBOMs, SLAs, SSAE reports\n\n### A.2 Pre-Deployment Testing\n\n> [!warning] Key Limitations\n> - Current TEVV processes may be inadequate, non-systematic, or mismatched to deployment contexts\n> - Anecdotal testing (video games, human standardized tests) does not guarantee validity or reliability\n> - Benchmarks and in silico techniques may not extrapolate to real-world conditions\n> - Measurement gaps exacerbated by prompt sensitivity and heterogeneity of use contexts\n\n> [!tip] Structured Public Feedback Methods\n> - **Participatory engagement:** Focus groups, small user studies, surveys\n> - **Field testing:** UX, usability, structured randomized experiments\n> - **AI Red-teaming:** Structured exercises to find flaws/vulnerabilities in controlled environments\n\n#### AI Red-Teaming Types\n\n| Type | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| **General Public** | Non-expert users with lived experiences; more effective with large groups |\n| **Expert** | Specialists with domain expertise (medicine, biotech, cybersecurity) |\n| **Combination** | Expert + general public; experts modify/verify prompts from general public |\n| **Human/AI** | GAI-led red-teaming with specialist/non-specialist oversight; more cost-effective |\n\n### A.3 Content Provenance\n\n> [!info] Definition\n> Provenance data tracking records the **origin and history** of digital content, allowing authenticity to be determined. Techniques include:\n> - **Digital watermarking** (overt and covert)\n> - **Metadata recording** (creator, date/time, location, modifications, sources)\n> - **Digital fingerprinting**\n> - **Human authentication**\n\n#### Provenance Tracking Approaches\n\n| Approach | Description |\n|----------|-------------|\n| **Training data provenance** | Track origin of data and metadata for GAI systems |\n| **Documentation** | Document provenance data limitations |\n| **Deployment monitoring** | Monitor system capabilities through rigorous TEVV |\n| **Human evaluation** | Evaluate how humans engage with GAI content and provenance techniques |\n\n### A.4 Incident Disclosure\n\n> [!info] AI Incident Definition\n> \"An event, circumstance, or series of events where the development, use, or malfunction of one or more AI systems directly or indirectly contributes to: injury/harm to health; disruption of critical infrastructure; violations of human rights or applicable law; harm to property, communities, or environment.\"\n\n| Key Challenge | Current State |\n|---------------|---------------|\n| **No formal channels** | No formal mechanisms exist for AI incident reporting |\n| **Ad hoc tracking** | Public databases exist but make ad hoc decisions about what to track |\n| **Documentation need** | Logging, recording, analyzing incidents can facilitate sharing |\n| **Third-party risk** | LLM plugins often have distributed, inconsistent access control |\n\n---\n\n## 9. AI RMF Subcategory Coverage\n\nThe GAI Profile covers selected subcategories across all four AI RMF functions:\n\n| Function | Subcategories Covered | Count of Suggested Actions |\n|----------|-----------------------|---------------------------|\n| **GOVERN** | 1.1, 1.2, 1.3, 1.4, 1.5, 1.6, 1.7, 2.1, 3.2, 4.1, 4.2, 4.3, 5.1, 6.1, 6.2 | ~50+ |\n| **MAP** | 1.1, 1.2, 2.1, 2.2, 2.3, 3.4, 4.1, 5.1, 5.2 | ~30+ |\n| **MEASURE** | 1.1, 1.3, 2.2, 2.3, 2.5, 2.6, 2.7, 2.8, 2.9, 2.10, 2.11, 2.12, 2.13, 3.2, 3.3, 4.2 | ~50+ |\n| **MANAGE** | 1.3, 2.2, 2.3, 2.4, 3.1, 3.2, 4.1, 4.2, 4.3 | ~30+ |\n\n> [!note] Future Updates\n> Not every AI RMF subcategory is included. Future revisions will add additional subcategories, risks, and suggested actions as the GAI space evolves.\n\n---\n\n## 10. AI Actor Tasks\n\nApplicable tasks referenced throughout the Profile:\n\n| Actor Task | Relevance |\n|------------|-----------|\n| **Governance and Oversight** | Most Govern actions |\n| **AI Design** | Threat modeling, safety-first design |\n| **AI Development** | Training data curation, model documentation |\n| **AI Deployment** | Acceptable use, post-deployment monitoring |\n| **AI Impact Assessment** | Mapping risks, evaluating impacts |\n| **TEVV** | Test, evaluation, validation, verification |\n| **Operation and Monitoring** | Ongoing monitoring, incident response |\n| **Procurement** | Third-party diligence, SLAs |\n| **Domain Experts** | Context-specific expertise |\n| **End Users** | Feedback, field testing participation |\n| **Affected Individuals and Communities** | Participatory engagement |\n| **Human Factors** | Human-AI configuration evaluation |\n\n---\n\n## 11. Connections to Other Frameworks\n\n- **[[NIST AI RMF — Structured Reference]]** — AI 600-1 is a cross-sectoral profile of the AI RMF; uses the same Govern/Map/Measure/Manage structure\n- **[[NIST ARIA — Structured Reference]]** — ARIA's initial risk testing draws from AI 600-1's 12 GAI risks; ARIA serves the AI RMF Measure function\n- **[[AI Standards and Frameworks]]** — AI 600-1 represents NIST's profile-based approach to sector/technology-specific AI risk management\n- **[[AIGP Body of Knowledge — Exam Blueprint]]** — Directly relevant to Domain II (AI Development & Lifecycle), Domain III (AI Deployment & Use), and Domain IV (AI Governance & Risk Management)\n\n---\n\n## 12. Key Takeaways for AIGP Exam\n\n> [!tip] Exam Relevance\n> 1. **AI 600-1 is a profile** — implementation of AI RMF for a specific technology (GAI)\n> 2. **12 risks** are the core — know them, their categories, and associated trustworthiness characteristics\n> 3. **Four primary considerations** — Governance, Pre-Deployment Testing, Content Provenance, Incident Disclosure\n> 4. **Action IDs follow pattern** — GV/MP/MS/MG - function.subcategory-sequence\n> 5. **Structured public feedback** — three types: participatory engagement, field testing, AI red-teaming\n> 6. **Confabulation vs. hallucination** — NIST prefers \"confabulation\" (avoids anthropomorphization)\n> 7. **Risk dimensions** — lifecycle stage, scope, source, time scale\n> 8. **Third-party throughout** — Govern 6, Map 4, Manage 3 all address value chain risks", "ref_42001": "## At a Glance\n\nISO/IEC 42001:2023 — the world's first certifiable AI management system standard (AIMS). Built on the Annex SL harmonized structure shared with ISO 27001/9001, it requires organizations to establish, implement, maintain, and continually improve a management system for AI: context, leadership, planning (including AI risk and impact assessment), support, operation, performance evaluation, and improvement — with Annex A controls specific to AI.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nIts identity is the differentiator: the *certifiable management-system* standard (vs. NIST's voluntary framework, ISO 23894's guidance). Know Annex SL structure, PDCA logic, that it integrates with 27001/9001, and that certification is by accredited third-party audit.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- First certifiable AI management system standard — organizations can be audited and certified against it\n- Annex SL high-level structure: context, leadership, planning, support, operation, performance evaluation, improvement — Plan-Do-Check-Act\n- Requires AI-specific risk assessment AND AI impact assessment considering individuals, groups, and society\n- Annex A control set: policies, roles, lifecycle processes, data governance, transparency, human oversight, third-party management\n- Integrates with existing management systems (ISO 27001 security, 9001 quality) — one governance machine, multiple lenses\n- Certification signals assurance to customers, partners, and regulators — increasingly requested in AI procurement\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] ISO 42001 — Structured Reference\n\n# ISO/IEC 42001:2023 — Artificial Intelligence Management System (AIMS)\n\n> **Source:** Benraouane, S.A. (2024). *AI Management System Certification According to the ISO/IEC 42001 Standard: How to Audit, Certify, and Build Responsible AI Systems.* Productivity Press / Routledge.\n\n## Overview\n\n**ISO/IEC 42001:2023** is the **first international certifiable standard for AI governance**. Published December 2023 by ISO and IEC through their Joint Technical Committee (JTC1), it provides a comprehensive framework for organizations to establish, implement, maintain, and continually improve an **Artificial Intelligence Management System (AIMS)**.\n\n- **Type:** Requirements standard (certifiable)\n- **Scope:** Any organization, regardless of size, sector, or AI maturity\n- **Purpose:** Help organizations use AI coherently, responsibly, and with appropriate governance\n- **Key concept:** The standard puts **Responsible AI (RAI)** principles into a certifiable practice framework\n\n### See also\n- [[AIGP Body of Knowledge — Exam Blueprint]]\n- [[AI Standards and Frameworks]]\n- [[NIST AI RMF — Structured Reference]]\n\n---\n\n## ISO 42001 Structure: Annex SL & Harmonized Framework\n\nISO/IEC 42001 follows the **Annex SL** high-level structure — the same architecture used by all modern ISO management system standards. This harmonized structure ensures compatibility and integration with other management systems.\n\n### The Annex SL Framework (Clauses 1–10)\n\n| Clause | Title | Role in AIMS |\n|--------|-------|-------------|\n| 1 | Scope | Overall purpose and applicability |\n| 2 | Normative References | Referenced standards |\n| 3 | Terms and Definitions | Common language (links to ISO/IEC 22989:2022) |\n| **4** | **Context of the Organization** | External/internal factors affecting AIMS |\n| **5** | **Leadership** | Vision, policy, roles, responsibilities |\n| **6** | **Planning** | Risk assessment, risk treatment, impact assessment, objectives |\n| **7** | **Support** | Resources, competence, awareness, communication, documentation |\n| **8** | **Operation** | AI project lifecycle controls and processes |\n| **9** | **Performance Evaluation** | Monitoring, measurement, internal audit, management review |\n| **10** | **Improvement** | Nonconformity, corrective action, continual improvement (PDCA) |\n\n> Clauses 1–3 are informational; Clauses 4–10 contain the auditable requirements.\n\n### The PDCA Cycle\n\nISO 42001 is built on the **Plan-Do-Check-Act** (Deming Cycle):\n\n- **PLAN** (Clauses 4–6): Understand context, set leadership direction, plan risks and objectives\n- **DO** (Clauses 7–8): Provide resources and execute AI project lifecycle processes\n- **CHECK** (Clause 9): Monitor, measure, audit, and review performance\n- **ACT** (Clause 10): Correct nonconformities and drive continual improvement\n\n### Annexes\n\n| Annex | Content |\n|-------|---------|\n| **Annex A** | Normative controls — specific requirements organizations must implement (or justify exclusion) |\n| **Annex B** | Informative guidelines — implementation recommendations from ISO experts |\n| **Annex C** | Informative — links to RAI objectives and ethical principles |\n\n---\n\n## Clause-by-Clause Requirements\n\n### Clause 4: Context of the Organization\n\n**Purpose:** Understand the bigger picture — why AI matters to *your* organization.\n\n**Requirements:**\n- Analyze **external and internal factors** affecting AIMS performance\n- Identify **interested parties** (stakeholders) and their needs/expectations (per ISO/IEC 22989:2022)\n- Define the **scope** of the AIMS — what's in, what's out, and boundaries\n- Document the scope and its applicability (Clause 4.3)\n\n**Three critical dimensions of context:**\n1. **Competitive landscape & stakeholders** — industry analysis (Porter's Five Forces, PESTEL, SWOT), customer trends, AI impact on market segments\n2. **Legal context** — GDPR, EU AI Act, US regulatory landscape (EEOC, CFPB, NYC Local Law 144, White House Executive Order), sector-specific regulations\n3. **Ethical AI / Responsible AI** — five core principles (see below)\n\n**Book's 15-step context analysis process:** Industry analysis → internal capabilities → regulatory framework (GDPR, EU AI Act, US trends) → ethical AI / RAI → stakeholder analysis → metrics\n\n---\n\n### Clause 5: Leadership\n\n**Purpose:** Leadership commitment is the *sine qua non* of AIMS success.\n\n**Three main leadership actions required:**\n1. **Set the vision and priorities** — articulate what AI means for the organization, show engagement\n2. **Develop an AI Policy** — an official, auditable document capturing leadership commitment\n3. **Define roles, responsibilities, and authorities** — who does what in the AIMS\n\n**AI Policy must include:**\n- Statement of scope, purpose, and objectives\n- Guidelines on acceptable/unacceptable AI use\n- How AIMS integrates with other management systems\n- Roles and responsibilities (technical + business)\n- Data and privacy requirements\n- AI compliance approach\n- AI talent management\n- Monitoring, improvement, review, and alignment processes\n\n**Key Roles:**\n- **Technical:** Data scientist, ML engineer, domain expert, AI designer\n- **Business:** Executive leadership, project managers, compliance managers, AI strategist, ethics committee\n\n**Responsible AI (RAI) as leadership imperative:**\n- Lead with RAI principles from the top\n- Set the tone through proactive communication\n- Acknowledge uncertainties to build trust\n\n---\n\n### Clause 6: Planning\n\n**Purpose:** Plan risk management, objectives, and change strategy.\n\n**Key Actions:**\n- **Risk Assessment** (Clause 6.1.2): Identify and evaluate AI risks\n- **Risk Treatment** (Clause 6.1.3): Select controls to mitigate risks\n- **Impact Assessment** (Clause 6.1.4): Assess consequences of AI systems on stakeholders\n- **AI Objectives** (Clause 6.2): Set measurable objectives at all organizational levels\n- **Change Planning** (Clause 6.3): Plan how to implement AIMS changes\n\n**Typology of AI Risks (7 categories):**\n1. **Performance Risk** — model inaccuracy, drift, failure\n2. **Security Risk** — adversarial attacks, data poisoning, model theft\n3. **Enterprise Risk** — misalignment with business processes\n4. **Reputational Risk** — public backlash, brand damage\n5. **Legal & Regulatory Risk** — non-compliance with evolving laws\n6. **AI Scalability Risk** — small risks amplified at scale\n7. **Black Box Risk** — inability to explain model decisions\n\n**Eight Principles of AI Risk Management (aligned with NIST AI RMF):**\n1. Integration into enterprise risk management\n2. Holistic/comprehensive perspective\n3. Customization to organizational context\n4. Inclusiveness of stakeholder views\n5. Agile mindset (able to adapt)\n6. Spell out assumptions\n7. Pay attention to cognitive bias\n8. Learn and improve continuously\n\n**Data Quality Requirements (Annex A, B.7.4):**\nGood-quality data must be:\n- **Sensitive** — anonymized, privacy-protecting\n- **Representative** — free from selection/exclusion/reporting bias\n- **Accurate** — objective, precise, reliable, valid, labeled\n- **Accessible** — open, standardized, secure\n- **Coverage** — geographical and temporal consistency\n\n**Data bias enters during three stages:**\n1. Data collection (sampling bias)\n2. Data preparation (attribute selection that proxies for discrimination)\n3. Problem framing (how you ask the question shapes the answer)\n\n---\n\n### Clause 7: Support\n\n**Purpose:** Provide the infrastructure and people capabilities for AIMS effectiveness.\n\n**Five support requirements:**\n\n1. **Resources** (Clause 7.1) — Tangible and intangible\n - **Tangible:** Computing performance (CPU→GPU), storage capacity, networking infrastructure, security\n - **Intangible:** Knowledge, skills, attitudes (KSA)\n\n2. **Competence** (Clause 7.2) — AI competence model\n - Document the KSA needed for employees involved in AIMS\n - Address shifting job descriptions, legacy issues, and need for interdisciplinarity\n - Three KSA pillars: ability to plan/execute digitally, ability to collect/use quality data, ability to learn and adapt\n\n3. **Awareness** (Clause 7.3) — Employees must be aware of:\n - AI governance structure and leadership\n - AIMS scope and objectives\n - Responsible AI principles\n - AI risks and how they are managed\n - Data usage policies\n - How they contribute to AIMS effectiveness\n - Noncompliance consequences\n\n4. **Communication** (Clause 7.4) — Communication strategy covering:\n - Target audience, frequency, medium, objectives\n - Face-to-face communication for complex AIMS topics\n - Policy champions to drive adoption\n - External communication (PR, stakeholder engagement)\n\n5. **Documented Information** (Clause 7.5) — 13 categories of required documents (see below)\n\n**Required Documentation (for certification):**\n1. Scope of AIMS, boundaries, applicability (Clause 4.3)\n2. AI policy (Clause 5.5)\n3. AI risk and opportunities register (Clause 6.1)\n4. Risk assessment methodology (Clause 6.1.2)\n5. Risk treatment justification — why controls were excluded/chosen (Clause 6.1.3)\n6. Impact assessment process and results (Clause 6.1.4)\n7. AI objectives at all levels and how to achieve them (Clause 6.2)\n8. Competence records of AIMS personnel (Clause 7.2)\n9. Planning and control process documentation\n10. Risk assessment, treatment, and impact assessment results (Clauses 8.2–8.4)\n11. Monitoring and measurement records (Clause 9.1)\n12. Audit program and results (Clause 9.2.2)\n13. Management review results (Clause 9.3)\n\n---\n\n### Clause 8: Operation\n\n**Purpose:** Execute AI projects with appropriate controls throughout the lifecycle.\n\n**Three critical operational steps:**\n1. Define required controls for AIMS functioning\n2. Establish monitoring, control, and corrective action systems\n3. Document planning and control processes\n\n**AI Project Lifecycle (Design → Develop → Deploy):**\n\n| Phase | Process Grouping | Activities | ISO 42001 Controls |\n|-------|-----------------|------------|-------------------|\n| **Design** | PG1 | Problem identification, idea selection, context understanding, literature review, question framing | Document RAI objectives (A.6.1.2); Document trustworthiness processes (A.6.1.3) |\n| **Design** | PG2 | Data collection, data wrangling/labeling | Data quality requirements (B.7.4); Data provenance |\n| **Develop** | PG3 | Model building, model evaluation | Model testing, validation metrics |\n| **Deploy** | PG4 | Model production, monitoring | Ongoing monitoring, KPI tracking |\n\n**Key references for operational controls:**\n- ISO/IEC 5338:2023 (AI system life cycle processes)\n- ISO/IEC 23894:2023 (AI risk management guidance)\n- ISO/IEC 38507:2022 (Governance implications of AI)\n\n---\n\n### Clause 9: Performance Evaluation\n\n**Purpose:** Assess whether the AIMS is working as intended.\n\n**Three required actions:**\n\n1. **Monitoring and Measurement** (Clause 9.1)\n - What to evaluate: full AIMS or specific parts\n - AIMS Governance KPIs: Data quality, Security, Cost/Value, Bias, Accountability, Audit frequency\n - ML Technical KPIs: MSE, MTTR, FCRR, Error analysis, Data/concept drift\n\n2. **Internal Audit** (Clause 9.2)\n - Must achieve: conformity to organizational requirements, conformity to ISO 42001, evidence of effective implementation\n - NIST AI RMF audit framework: **Govern → Map → Measure → Manage**\n - **LLM Audit** (Mökander et al. 2023 three-layer framework):\n 1. Governance audit of technology provider\n 2. Model audit (limitations, capabilities, performance, security)\n 3. Application audit (legal/ethical compliance, impact on users/society)\n - **Algorithmic Audit:** Distinguish between ethical risk assessment (socio-technical) and algorithmic bias assessment (technical inspection of model internals)\n\n3. **Management Review** (Clause 9.3)\n - Review previous audits, changes since last cycle, stakeholder expectation changes, opportunities for improvement\n - Must be documented\n\n---\n\n### Clause 10: Improvement\n\n**Purpose:** Continuously improve the AIMS through structured corrective and preventive action.\n\n**Key Concepts:**\n- **Deviation:** Gap between actual and target performance\n- **Nonconformity:** Non-fulfillment of a required action (e.g., missing AI policy)\n\n**Three responses to nonconformity:**\n1. Accept consequences and correct\n2. **Root-cause analysis** (most important) — understand why it happened\n3. Update AIMS based on findings\n\n**CAPA Framework (Corrective and Preventive Actions):**\n- **Corrective Actions:** Eliminate deviations immediately, then permanently address root causes (human error, technical issue, or process error)\n- **Preventive Actions:** Build trigger/alarm systems to predict and prevent recurrence\n\n**PDCA for Continuous Improvement:**\n- **PLAN:** Define business problem, clarify objectives, set metrics and milestones, identify resources\n- **DO:** Collect data, monitor model performance, list risks, test and experiment\n- **CHECK:** Verify governance (policy, risk, documentation) AND technical behavior (model accuracy, validation)\n- **ACT:** Address issues identified in Check — both governance and technical fixes\n\n---\n\n## Ethical AI & Responsible AI (RAI) Framework\n\n**Five consensus ethical principles for RAI:**\n\n| Principle | Description | ISO 42001 Link |\n|-----------|-------------|----------------|\n| **Do No Harm** | AI must be safe, secure; prevent physical/mental harm | Clause B.5.5 — assess and document potential harm |\n| **Fairness & Non-Discrimination** | Equal treatment; free from unfair bias | Algorithmic fairness, representative data |\n| **Human Oversight (Human-in-the-Loop)** | Humans must remain in control; AI empowers, doesn't control | Governance model for oversight |\n| **Explainability** | Meaningful explanation of AI decisions | ISO/IEC TR 24028:2020 — ex ante / ex post explanation |\n| **Robustness** | Consistent performance regardless of environmental changes | Testing, feedback loops, human-in-the-loop validation |\n\n**Seven EU HLEG Requirements for Trustworthy AI:**\n1. Human agency and oversight\n2. Robustness and safety\n3. Privacy and data governance\n4. Transparency\n5. Diversity, non-discrimination, and fairness\n6. Societal and environmental well-being\n7. Accountability\n\n---\n\n## Comparison to Other ISO Management Standards\n\n### ISO 42001 vs. ISO 9001:2015 (Quality Management)\n\n| Dimension | ISO 42001 | ISO 9001 |\n|-----------|-----------|----------|\n| **Focus** | AI governance, risk, responsibility | Product/service quality, customer satisfaction |\n| **Risk approach** | AI-specific risks (bias, black box, scalability) | General quality risks |\n| **Annex SL** | ✓ Same structure | ✓ Same structure |\n| **Documented information** | Same generic requirements (Clause 7.5) | Same generic requirements |\n| **Integration** | Designed to integrate with 9001 | Can serve as foundation for AIMS build |\n| **Audit** | Requires AI/ML technical + governance competence | Quality management competence |\n\n### ISO 42001 vs. ISO 27001:2022 (Information Security)\n\n| Dimension | ISO 42001 | ISO 27001 |\n|-----------|-----------|----------|\n| **Focus** | AI governance and responsibility | Information security (CIA triad) |\n| **Annex A controls** | AI-specific normative controls | Security controls |\n| **Overlap** | Data quality, security controls, infrastructure | — |\n| **Key distinction** | 42001 addresses AI ethics, fairness, explainability | 27001 does not address AI-specific issues |\n\n### ISO 42001 vs. NIST AI RMF 1.0\n\n| Dimension | ISO 42001 | NIST AI RMF |\n|-----------|-----------|-------------|\n| **Nature** | Certifiable requirements standard | Voluntary guidance framework |\n| **Structure** | Annex SL (clauses 4–10) | Govern → Map → Measure → Manage |\n| **Auditability** | Designed for third-party certification | Designed for self-assessment |\n| **Adoption** | Global (ISO member countries) | US-focused but internationally influential |\n\n---\n\n## Certification Process\n\n### Key Points\n- ISO/IEC 42001 is **certifiable** — organizations can obtain third-party certification\n- Certification demonstrates that the AIMS conforms to the standard's requirements\n- The standard specifies **what** must be done, not **how** — organizations choose implementation methods\n- A new standard under development: **ISO/IEC CD 42006** — *Requirements for bodies providing audit and certification of AIMS*\n\n### Preparing for Certification\n\n**Book's recommended approach:**\n1. **Start with Clause 4 (Context):** Conduct fresh context analysis — don't recycle old documentation\n2. **Mobilize a cross-functional team** — IT, legal, compliance, HR, strategy, customer service\n3. **Set the roadmap** — identify AIMS issues, stakeholders, ethical/value system\n4. **Conduct discovery sessions** — workshops to learn about the context together\n5. **External analysis first** — economic impact, regulatory trends, competitive landscape\n6. **Internal analysis second** — digital transformation capabilities, talent, risks\n\n### What Auditors Look For\n- Evidence of leadership commitment (documented AI policy)\n- Thoroughness of context analysis and its connection to planning\n- Documented risk assessment, treatment, and impact assessment\n- Functioning internal audit program\n- Management review records\n- All 13 categories of documented information\n- Traceability from context → planning → operations → evaluation → improvement\n\n### Internal Audit Program\nMust achieve three goals:\n1. Conformity of AIMS with organizational AI requirements\n2. Conformity with ISO/IEC 42001 requirements\n3. Evidence that AIMS is effectively implemented and maintained\n\n**Audit scope depends on:** business environment, legal context, organizational size, economic sector, previous audit results\n\n---\n\n## Integration with Other Management Systems\n\nISO 42001 is designed as a **harmonized management system standard** using Annex SL. It integrates seamlessly with:\n- **ISO 9001:2015** (Quality) — shared structure, documented information requirements\n- **ISO 27001:2022** (Information Security) — complementary security controls\n- **ISO 14001:2015** (Environmental) — shared Annex SL structure\n- **ISO 45001:2018** (Occupational Health & Safety) — shared management approach\n- **ISO 56002:2019** (Innovation Management) — innovation ecosystem for AI\n- **ISO 31000** (Risk Management) — foundational risk concepts\n- **ISO 37000** (Governance of Organizations) — top-level governance\n\n### Related ISO/IEC AI Standards Ecosystem\n- **ISO/IEC 22989:2022** — AI concepts and terminology (defines roles: provider, developer, user, etc.)\n- **ISO/IEC 23894:2023** — AI guidance on risk management\n- **ISO/IEC 38507:2022** — Governance implications of AI use by organizations\n- **ISO/IEC TR 24028:2020** — Overview of trustworthiness in AI\n- **ISO/IEC TR 24368:2022** — Overview of ethical and societal concerns\n- **ISO/IEC 5338:2023** — AI system life cycle processes\n- **ISO/IEC 42006** *(under development)* — Requirements for AIMS audit and certification bodies\n\n---\n\n## Regulatory Context\n\n### EU\n- **EU AI Act** (approved April 2024): Risk-based approach — Unacceptable (prohibited), High (certification/conformity assessment), Limited (transparency), Minimal (voluntary codes)\n- **GDPR** (2018): Data processing principles directly linked to ISO 42001 Annex A controls (B.4.3, B.7.2–B.7.6)\n\n### US\n- **Executive Order on Safe, Secure, and Trustworthy AI** (Oct 2023): NIST to develop standards; foundation model developers must share safety test results\n- **EEOC** guidelines on algorithmic discrimination under Title VII\n- **NYC Local Law 144**: Prohibits discrimination in Automated Employment Decision Tools (AEDT)\n- **Algorithmic Accountability Act** (proposed, not enacted): Mandatory impact assessments\n- 14 states with enacted/adopted AI legislation as of 2023\n\n### Global\n- **OECD AI Principles**\n- **UNESCO AI Ethics Recommendations**\n- **White House voluntary commitments** (Jul 2023): Amazon, Anthropic, Google, Inflection, Meta, Microsoft, OpenAI — safety, security, trust pillars\n\n---\n\n## Book's Practical Framework Summary\n\nThe book provides implementation guidance beyond the standard text. Key frameworks:\n\n1. **15-step context analysis process** (Ch. 5): Industry → internal capabilities → regulatory → ethical → stakeholders → metrics\n2. **7-type AI risk typology**: Performance, Security, Enterprise, Reputational, Legal/Regulatory, Scalability, Black Box\n3. **8 principles of AI risk management**: Integration, Holistic, Customization, Inclusiveness, Agile, Assumptions, Bias awareness, Learning\n4. **AI project lifecycle controls** (Design PG1→PG2 → Develop PG3 → Deploy PG4)\n5. **PDCA improvement cycle** applied specifically to AI projects\n6. **Kotter's change model** adapted for AIMS implementation (Urgency → Guiding Team → Vision → Buy-in → Empower → Perseverance)\n7. **CAPA framework** for corrective and preventive action\n8. **Three-layer LLM audit framework** (Mökander et al.): Governance → Model → Application\n9. **50 key terms** glossary covering AI, ISO standards, quality, and cognitive bias\n\n---\n\n## Quick Reference: ISO 42001 at a Glance\n\n| Element | Detail |\n|---------|--------|\n| **Full name** | ISO/IEC 42001:2023 Artificial Intelligence Management System |\n| **Published** | December 2023 |\n| **Structure** | Annex SL (harmonized with 9001, 27001, 14001, 45001) |\n| **Type** | Certifiable requirements standard |\n| **Core clauses** | 4 (Context) → 5 (Leadership) → 6 (Planning) → 7 (Support) → 8 (Operation) → 9 (Performance Evaluation) → 10 (Improvement) |\n| **Cycle** | PDCA (Plan-Do-Check-Act) |\n| **Annexes** | A (normative controls), B (informative guidelines), C (RAI links) |\n| **Key differentiator** | First standard to operationalize Responsible AI into a certifiable management system |\n| **Related standards** | 22989, 23894, 38507, 24028, 24368, 5338, 42006 |\n| **Certification body standard** | ISO/IEC CD 42006 (under development) |\n\n---\n\n## Key Exam Takeaways\n\n1. **ISO 42001 is the first international certifiable AI management system standard** — published Dec 2023\n2. **Follows Annex SL** — same high-level structure as ISO 9001, 27001, 14001, 45001\n3. **PDCA cycle** underpins Clauses 4–10: Plan (4-6), Do (7-8), Check (9), Act (10)\n4. **Responsible AI** is the central philosophy: Do No Harm, Fairness, Human Oversight, Explainability, Robustness\n5. **Risk-based approach** with three mandatory activities: Risk Assessment (6.1.2), Risk Treatment (6.1.3), Impact Assessment (6.1.4)\n6. **13 categories of documented information** required for certification\n7. **Complements NIST AI RMF** — ISO 42001 is certifiable, NIST is voluntary guidance\n8. **Integrates with GDPR, EU AI Act, and US regulatory frameworks**\n9. **Annex A** contains normative controls; organizations must implement them or justify exclusion\n10. **New ISO/IEC 42006** standard will govern AIMS audit and certification bodies", "ref_42005": "## At a Glance\n\nISO/IEC 42005:2025 (published 28 May 2025) is the ISO/IEC guidance standard for AI system impact assessment, and one of the three core ISO standards named in BOK v2.1 II.D. Clause 5 sets out how an organization builds the process: documentation, integration with other management processes, timing, scope, responsibilities, thresholds for sensitive and restricted uses, performing, analysing, recording and reporting, approval, and monitoring and review. Clause 6 sets out what each assessment records: system information, data and data quality, algorithms and models, deployment environment, interested parties, actual and reasonably foreseeable benefits and harms (including failures and misuse), and measures. Five informative annexes link it to ISO/IEC 42001 (A) and 23894 (B) and supply a harms and benefits taxonomy (C), alignment with other assessments (D) and a template (E).\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nDistinguish it from its neighbours: 42001 is the certifiable management system that requires impact assessment (6.1.4, 8.4, Annex A objective A.5); 23894 covers risk to the organization; 42006 covers the bodies that certify 42001; 42005 is non-certifiable guidance on assessing impacts on people. Expect scenarios on who may use it (developers, providers and users), what belongs in the record, when to reassess, and why it cannot stand in for a GDPR DPIA or an AI Act Art. 27 FRIA.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Guidance, not certifiable; for any organization that develops, provides or uses AI, of any size\n- Focus: benefits and harms for individuals, groups and societies, across the AI system lifecycle\n- Clause 5 = the organizational process (including thresholds for sensitive and restricted uses and formal approval); Clause 6 = what one assessment records\n- Assess at design and before deployment, keep under review, and reassess on a change of use, a significant system change or a new legal or policy requirement\n- Annexes: A with 42001, B with 23894, C harms and benefits taxonomy, D alignment with other assessments, E example template\n- ISO/IEC 42001 requires AI system impact assessment (clauses 6.1.4 and 8.4; Annex A objective A.5); 42005 explains how to meet that requirement\n- Feeds, but never replaces, a GDPR Art. 35 DPIA or EU AI Act Art. 27 FRIA; not a harmonised standard, so no presumption of conformity\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] ISO 42005 — AI System Impact Assessment\n\n# ISO/IEC 42005:2025 AI System Impact Assessment — Key Provisions\n\n> **Status:** Published International Standard, first edition. Guidance only, so there is nothing to certify against. **Published:** 28 May 2025 (39 pages, paid). **Developed by:** ISO/IEC JTC 1/SC 42, Artificial intelligence. **Blueprint:** BOK v2.1 II.D names it as one of the three core ISO AI standards, with 22989 and 42001. It is also the natural method for the impact-assessment tasks in III.A and IV.B.\n\n> **How this note was built.** The standard is copyrighted and sold (CHF 181 on the IEC webstore). This note paraphrases the official abstract, the clause and annex headings shown in ISO's free preview, and independent summaries. Clause numbers appear only where two sources agree. Nothing is reproduced from the standard's text.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\nISO/IEC 42005 tells an organization how to set up and run a repeatable process for assessing how an AI system, and the ways it can foreseeably be used, may affect people: individuals, groups of individuals and society as a whole. It also describes what a finished assessment record should contain.\n\nThree ideas carry most exam questions:\n\n1. **It is guidance, not a requirements standard.** No one can be certified to 42005. The certifiable standard is ISO/IEC 42001, which *requires* an AI system impact assessment but says little about how to do one. 42005 fills that gap.\n2. **Its lens is people, not the organization.** ISO/IEC 23894 manages risk to the organization's objectives. 42005 looks outward at the benefits and harms for those affected, and its findings then feed the organization's risk process.\n3. **It runs across the lifecycle.** An assessment starts at design, gates deployment, and is revisited when the system, its use or its context changes.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Scope and applicability\n\n| Question | Answer |\n|---|---|\n| **Who can use it?** | Any organization that develops, provides or uses AI systems, whatever its size, type or nature |\n| **Whose impacts?** | Individuals, groups of individuals and societies that the system, or its foreseeable applications, can affect |\n| **What does it cover?** | How and when to perform assessments across the AI system lifecycle, what to document, and how to integrate the work with AI risk management and an AI management system |\n| **Certifiable?** | No. There is no 42005 certificate. Certification exists for 42001 management systems, issued by bodies that meet ISO/IEC 42006 |\n| **Legal force?** | None of its own. It can supply content for legally required assessments (GDPR DPIA, EU AI Act FRIA) but does not replace them |\n| **Sector** | Sector-neutral |\n\n> **Trap:** \"42005 is for AI developers.\" It is for organizations that develop, *provide or use* AI. A company that only deploys a vendor's model is squarely in scope and cannot simply adopt the vendor's view of the impacts.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Where 42005 sits in the ISO/IEC AI family\n\nThe standard's introduction places it in an ecosystem of governance, risk and conformity standards, each of which stresses the need to consider impacts on people.\n\n| Standard | Role | How it relates to 42005 |\n|---|---|---|\n| **ISO/IEC 22989:2022** | Concepts and terminology | Supplies the shared vocabulary (AI system, stakeholder roles, trustworthiness properties) the assessment uses |\n| **ISO/IEC 38507** | Governance implications of AI for governing bodies | A board uses impact findings to check that AI use fits the organization's values and goals |\n| **ISO/IEC 23894:2023** | AI risk management guidance, built on ISO 31000 | Organizational-risk lens. 42005's Annex B explains how impact findings feed that risk process |\n| **ISO/IEC 42001:2023** | Certifiable AI management system (AIMS) | Requires AI system impact assessment (clauses 6.1.4 and 8.4; Annex A objective A.5). 42005's Annex A explains using 42005 inside a 42001 AIMS |\n| **ISO/IEC 42006:2025** | Requirements for bodies that audit and certify an AIMS against 42001 | Indirect. A 42001 audit tests the impact-assessment requirements; 42005 is one way to meet them and is never itself certified |\n\n> **Blueprint note:** BOK v2.1 II.D names 22989, 42001 and 42005 as \"the core ISO AI standards\". ISO/IEC 23894 is not on that list (v2.0.1 named only 22989 and 42001), but 42005's Annex B keeps it in view.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Key concepts\n\n| Concept | Meaning in this context |\n|---|---|\n| **AI system impact assessment** | ISO/IEC 42001 (definition 3.24) describes it as a formal, documented process in which an organization that develops, provides or uses AI identifies, evaluates and addresses impacts on individuals, groups and societies. 42005 is the detailed guidance for that process |\n| **Impact** | Two-sided: benefits *and* harms. The assessment asks for measures that address both |\n| **Actual and reasonably foreseeable impacts** | Not only what the designers intend. Includes how the system could fail and how it could foreseeably be misused |\n| **Intended vs unintended uses** | The record describes the uses the organization plans and the uses it does not plan but that could occur |\n| **Interested parties** | Split into those directly affected (for example, applicants scored by a model) and other relevant parties |\n| **Sensitive and restricted uses** | The organization sets thresholds that flag uses needing closer scrutiny because of their potential effect on people, or uses limited by law or policy. Flagged cases get escalation and formal approval |\n| **Impact scales** | Organization-defined scales for rating how significant an impact is, used with those thresholds |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Building the process (Clause 5)\n\nClause 5 is about the organization's *process*, set up before any single system is assessed. Its elements, in order:\n\n| # | Element | What the organization settles |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Write the process down | A documented procedure and templates (Annex E is an example template) |\n| 2 | Integrate with other management processes | Ties into enterprise risk, compliance, privacy and the AIMS instead of running as a stand-alone exercise |\n| 3 | Timing | Which lifecycle stages require an assessment, and what prompts a reassessment |\n| 4 | Scope | Which system, version, uses and boundaries an assessment covers |\n| 5 | Responsibilities | Who performs, who contributes, who reviews and who approves |\n| 6 | Thresholds | Criteria for sensitive uses, restricted uses and impact scales that decide depth, escalation and sign-off |\n| 7 | Perform | Carry out the assessment |\n| 8 | Analyse | Interpret the results and decide what to do |\n| 9 | Record and report | Keep the record and report results to the right audiences |\n| 10 | Approve | Formal approval of the assessment and its outcome |\n| 11 | Monitor and review | Keep the assessment current while the system operates |\n\n> **Memory hook:** *write it down → plug it in → when → what → who → thresholds → do → analyse → record → approve → review.*\n\n---\n\n## 6. Timing and reassessment\n\n**When to assess.** The sources agree on a lifecycle approach rather than a one-off check:\n\n- **At design**, while the use case is still being shaped and changes are cheap.\n- **Before deployment**, as a gate before release or go-live.\n- **During operation**, through monitoring and periodic review.\n- **On significant change**, whenever the system, its use or its context shifts.\n\n**Reassessment triggers** (the categories on which the sources agree):\n\n| Trigger | Example |\n|---|---|\n| Change of intended use or purpose | A demand-forecasting model starts being used to rate individual workers |\n| Significant change to the system | New data types, retraining, or an updated or replaced model or algorithm |\n| Change in the legal or policy environment | A new law, regulation or internal policy now applies to the use |\n| Monitoring or feedback reveals something new | Impacts appear that the assessment did not anticipate |\n\n> **42001 link:** clause 8.4 of ISO/IEC 42001 calls for AI system impact assessments at planned intervals or when significant changes are proposed. 42005 gives the detail for deciding what counts and how deep to go.\n\n---\n\n## 7. What the assessment records (Clause 6)\n\n| Element | What goes in |\n|---|---|\n| **Scope** | The system, version and uses the assessment covers |\n| **AI system information** | Description; functions and capabilities; purpose; intended uses; unintended uses |\n| **Data information and quality** | Data sources and characteristics; documentation of data quality |\n| **Algorithm and model information** | Algorithms used or developed; models used and how they were developed |\n| **Deployment environment** | Geographical area and languages; complexity and constraints of the setting |\n| **Relevant interested parties** | Directly affected parties; other relevant parties |\n| **Actual and reasonably foreseeable impacts** | Benefits and harms; failures and reasonably foreseeable misuse |\n| **Measures** | Actions that address the harms and benefits identified |\n\n> **Trap:** An impact assessment is not just a list of harms. A record with no benefits, no unintended uses or no foreseeable misuse is incomplete.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Harms and benefits\n\n- Assess positive and negative impacts on individuals, groups and society.\n- Annex C offers a taxonomy of harms and benefits spanning themes such as fairness, transparency, privacy and environmental effects.\n- Consider how the system could fail, and how it could be misused in ways that are reasonably foreseeable.\n- Measures work in both directions: reduce or remove harms, and secure the intended benefits.\n- Judge impacts in context. The same model can have very different impacts in another country, language or population, which is why the deployment environment is part of the record.\n\n---\n\n## 9. The annexes (all informative)\n\n| Annex | Subject | Reach for it when… |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A** | Guidance for use with ISO/IEC 42001 | Meeting 42001's impact-assessment requirements inside an AIMS |\n| **B** | Guidance for use with ISO/IEC 23894 | Feeding impact findings into the organization's AI risk management |\n| **C** | Harms and benefits taxonomy | Brainstorming and categorizing impacts |\n| **D** | Aligning the AI system impact assessment with other assessments | Coordinating with other assessments the organization already runs |\n| **E** | Example of an AI system impact assessment template | Starting a first assessment |\n\n> **Mnemonic:** **A** = AIMS (42001) · **B** = the risk standard (23894) · **C** = catalogue of harms and benefits · **D** = dovetail with other assessments · **E** = example template.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Relationship to ISO/IEC 42001\n\n| | ISO/IEC 42001:2023 | ISO/IEC 42005:2025 |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Type** | Requirements standard, certifiable | Guidance, not certifiable |\n| **Level** | The whole AI management system | Individual AI systems and their impacts |\n| **Impact assessment** | Requires a process (clause 6.1.4, planning) and its performance (clause 8.4, operation). Normative Annex A objective A.5, \"Assessing impacts of AI systems\": A.5.2 process, A.5.3 documentation, A.5.4 impacts on individuals or groups, A.5.5 societal impacts | Explains how to design that process and what each record should contain; Annex A maps the two |\n| **Edition** | Still the first (2023) edition as of September 2026 | First edition, May 2025 |\n\n> **Trap:** A 42001 certificate never \"covers 42005\". The auditor tests 42001's own clauses. Following 42005 is simply a well-regarded way to meet them.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Relationship to ISO/IEC 23894\n\n| | ISO/IEC 23894:2023 | ISO/IEC 42005:2025 |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Question asked** | What could stop the organization meeting its objectives? | How could this system affect people and society, for better or worse? |\n| **Lens** | Risk to the organization | Impacts on individuals, groups and societies |\n| **Lineage** | Extends ISO 31000 with AI-specific guidance | Annex B shows how impact findings feed the risk process |\n| **Output** | Risk treatment decisions | Impact record, measures, approval |\n\n> **Exam pattern:** \"harm to the people affected\" points to a 42005 impact assessment; \"risk to the business\" points to 23894 risk management. Each feeds the other. A harm that barely registers as organizational risk can still be a significant impact on the people who bear it.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Comparison: 42005 vs GDPR DPIA vs EU AI Act FRIA\n\n| | ISO/IEC 42005 impact assessment | GDPR Art. 35 DPIA | EU AI Act Art. 27 FRIA |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Nature** | Voluntary guidance | Legal duty when triggered | Legal duty for in-scope deployers |\n| **Who** | Any organization developing, providing or using AI | The controller, seeking the DPO's advice where one is designated | Deployers that are bodies governed by public law or private entities providing public services, plus deployers of Annex III 5(b) creditworthiness and 5(c) life/health-insurance pricing systems |\n| **Trigger** | The organization's own lifecycle points and thresholds | Processing likely to result in a high risk to people's rights and freedoms (Art. 35(1)); the listed cases in Art. 35(3) include systematic, extensive profiling with legal or similarly significant effects | Before first use of an Art. 6(2) (Annex III) high-risk system; Annex III point 2 (critical infrastructure) is excluded |\n| **Focus** | Benefits and harms to individuals, groups and society | Risks to data subjects from processing personal data | Impact on the fundamental rights of the people and groups affected |\n| **Core content** | System, data, model, deployment environment, interested parties, impacts (including failures and misuse), measures | Description and purposes; necessity and proportionality; risks; measures to address them (Art. 35(7)) | Deployer's processes; period and frequency of use; categories of people affected; specific risks of harm; human oversight; measures if risks materialize, including governance and complaints (Art. 27(1)(a)–(f)) |\n| **Outward step** | None required (internal approval) | Prior consultation with the supervisory authority if high risk remains without mitigation (Art. 36(1)) | Notify the market surveillance authority of the results, using the AI Office template |\n| **Keeping current** | Monitor, review, reassess on triggers | Review at least when the risk changes (Art. 35(11)) | Update when any element changes during use |\n| **Overlap** | Annex D helps align it with other assessments | — | Where a DPIA already meets some FRIA obligations, the FRIA builds on it rather than duplicating it (Art. 27(4)) |\n| **Timing** | Available since May 2025 | Applies today | Applies with the Annex III high-risk regime: 2 Dec 2027 as amended by the 2026 Digital Omnibus (originally 2 Aug 2026) |\n\n> **Rule of thumb:** One well-built 42005 record can feed both the DPIA and the FRIA, but neither law accepts a voluntary standard in place of its own assessment. 42005 is not a harmonised standard, so it gives no presumption of conformity under AI Act Art. 40, which covers harmonised standards published in the Official Journal.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Worked example\n\nA regional bank licenses a vendor's credit-scoring model for EU consumer lending. Assume the AI Act's Annex III deployer obligations are in application.\n\n1. **Scope and thresholds.** Scoring the creditworthiness of individuals crosses the bank's sensitive-use threshold, so the assessment escalates to the AI governance committee for approval.\n2. **System, data and model information.** The bank requests the vendor's documentation to complete these sections. Deploying rather than building does not remove the need to understand them.\n3. **Deployment environment and interested parties.** Markets and languages served; applicants (directly affected); co-borrowers, guarantors and supervisors (other relevant parties).\n4. **Impacts.** Benefits (faster decisions, wider access to credit) and harms (unfair denials, reasons that cannot be explained, errors from stale data), including foreseeable misuse such as reusing scores for marketing.\n5. **Legal overlay.** The same evidence feeds a GDPR Art. 35 DPIA (profiling with significant effects) and an AI Act Art. 27 FRIA (the bank deploys an Annex III 5(b) system). The bank must still complete both, and must notify the FRIA results.\n6. **Reassessment.** Triggered when the vendor retrains or replaces the model, or when the bank extends the score to a new product.\n\n---\n\n## Exam Angles\n\n- **Guidance vs requirements.** 42005 is guidance and cannot be certified. 42001 is the certifiable AIMS standard. 42006 sets rules for the bodies that certify 42001.\n- **People, not the balance sheet.** 42005 assesses impacts on individuals, groups and societies; 23894 manages risk to the organization. Annex B connects them.\n- **Both directions.** Benefits and harms, with measures for each. \"Only negative impacts\" is a wrong answer.\n- **Lifecycle, not one-off.** Design, pre-deployment, operation, and reassessment on significant change. \"Once, just before launch\" is wrong.\n- **Users are in scope.** Organizations that develop, provide *or use* AI can apply it. A deployer cannot simply rely on its vendor.\n- **Annex map.** A = 42001, B = 23894, C = harms and benefits taxonomy, D = aligning with other assessments, E = example template.\n- **42001 hooks.** Clauses 6.1.4 and 8.4 and Annex A objective A.5 require AI system impact assessment; 42005 explains how.\n- **No legal shortcut.** A 42005 record can feed a DPIA or FRIA but never replaces them, and it gives no AI Act presumption of conformity.\n- **Structure.** Clause 5 is the process; Clause 6 is what one assessment records (system, data, model, deployment environment, interested parties, impacts, measures).\n- **Currency.** 42005 was published on 28 May 2025; 42001 is still the 2023 edition.\n\n## Key Terms\n\n- [[ISO/IEC 42005]]\n- [[AI System Impact Assessment (ISO/IEC 42005)]]\n- [[Impact Reassessment Trigger]]\n- [[ISO/IEC 42006]]\n\n## Sources\n\n- https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/107659 (ISO/IEC 42005:2025 abstract, date, pages, price, committee)\n- https://www.iso.org/standard/42005 (official ISO page; refused automated access, so the abstract was checked on the IEC webstore)\n- https://cdn.standards.iteh.ai/samples/iso/iso-iec-42005-2025/08d7e571c9c3429d82aceca0b4746d01/iso-iec-42005-2025.pdf (ISO's free preview: contents, foreword, introduction)\n- https://cms.law/en/aut/legal-updates/iso-iec-42005-2025-a-new-blueprint-for-legal-and-commercial-leaders-navigating-ai-risk-and-governance\n- https://digital.nemko.com/insights/iso-iec-42005-ai-impact-assessment-framework-guide\n- https://digital.nemko.com/standards/iso-iec-42005\n- https://www.scrut.io/post/iso-42005\n- https://www.pillar.security/blog/understanding-iso-42005-ai-impact-assessment\n- https://cyberzoni.com/standards/iso-42005/\n- https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/90574 (ISO/IEC 42001:2023, first edition)\n- https://cdn.standards.iteh.ai/samples/81230/4c1911ebc9a641fcb6ee21aa09c28ad3/ISO-IEC-42001-2023.pdf (42001 preview: definition 3.24, clauses 6.1.4 and 8.4)\n- https://advisera.com/articles/iso-42001-clauses-requirements/\n- https://cyberzoni.com/standards/iso-42001/clause-8/\n- https://isomanaged.com/knowledge-base/iso-standards/iso-42001/iso-42001-annex-a-controls/iso-42001-annex-a-5-assessing-impacts-of-ai-systems\n- https://www.isms.online/iso-42001/annex-a-controls/a-5-assessing-impacts-of-ai-systems/\n- https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/108460 (ISO/IEC 42006:2025)\n- https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/27/ · https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/annex/3/ · https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/40/\n- https://gdpr-info.eu/art-35-gdpr/ · https://gdpr-info.eu/art-36-gdpr/\n- https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/news/ai-omnibus-enters-force", "ref_22989": "## At a Glance\n\nISO/IEC 22989:2022 — the AI terminology standard: 110+ defined concepts covering the AI lifecycle, machine learning paradigms, system roles (AI provider, producer, customer, subject), trustworthiness vocabulary (robustness, reliability, controllability, explainability), and functional concepts from NLP to computer vision. Its job is interoperability: everyone arguing about AI using the same words.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nThe definitions themselves are the test material — many flashcards in this app trace here. Precision matters: robustness (performance under any conditions) vs. reliability (consistent intended behavior), AI system vs. AI model, transparency (organization) vs. explainability (system).\n\n## Key Points\n\n- The shared vocabulary standard: 110+ terms other standards (42001, 23894) and regulations lean on\n- Role taxonomy: AI provider, AI producer, AI customer, AI user, AI subject — who builds, sells, buys, operates, and is affected\n- Trustworthiness vocabulary: robustness, reliability, resilience, controllability, predictability, transparency, explainability, accountability\n- Lifecycle model: inception → design & development → verification & validation → deployment → operation & monitoring → re-evaluation → retirement\n- Learning taxonomy: supervised, unsupervised, semi-supervised, reinforcement, transfer learning — with formal definitions\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] ISO 22989 — Key Definitions\n\n# ISO/IEC 22989 — Key Definitions Glossary\n\n> **Full Title:** ISO/IEC 22989:2022 — Information technology — Artificial intelligence — Artificial intelligence concepts and terminology\n>\n> **Status:** International Standard (published 2022)\n>\n> **Scope:** Establishes terminology for AI and describes concepts in the field of AI. Applicable to all types of organizations.\n\n## Cross-References\n\n- [[AIGP Body of Knowledge — Exam Blueprint]]\n- [[ISO 42001 — Structured Reference]]\n- [[AI Standards and Frameworks]]\n- [[ISO 23894 — AI Risk Management]]\n\n---\n\n## 1. Core AI Terms (Clause 3.1)\n\n| Term | ISO 22989 Definition | Governance Notes |\n|------|---------------------|-----------------|\n| **Artificial Intelligence (AI)** | *\\<Discipline>* Research and development of mechanisms and applications of AI systems. R&D can take place in fields such as computer science, data science, humanities, mathematics, and natural sciences. | The standard defines AI only as a *discipline*, not a technology. The context: \"AI is a technical and scientific field that deals with technical systems that generate outputs such as content, predictions, recommendations, or decisions for a specific, human-defined objective.\" |\n| **AI System** | A technical system that generates results such as content, forecasts, recommendations, or decisions for a specific, human-defined objective. May use various techniques and approaches related to AI to develop a model for representing data, knowledge, processes, etc. Designed to operate with varying degrees of automation. | This is the foundational definition used by **ISO 42001** and **ISO 23894**. An AI system is engineered, goal-directed, and operates at varying levels of automation. |\n| **AI Agent** | Automated entity that perceives and reacts to its environment and takes actions to achieve its goals. | Agent paradigm: sensors → perception → action. Types include reflexive, model-based, goal/utility-based, and learning agents. |\n| **AI Component** | Functional element that constitutes an AI system. | Granularity concept — systems are composed of components that may themselves be AI or non-AI. |\n| **Model** | A physical, mathematical, or other logical representation of a system, an entity, a phenomenon, a process, or data. | Generic definition used as a building block for ML model, trained model, etc. |\n| **Autonomy** | A characteristic of a system that is capable of changing its intended domain of use or its objective without external intervention, control, or supervision. | Highest level on the automation spectrum (Level 6). Distinct from automation (which operates within fixed parameters). |\n| **Automation** | Relating to a process or system that operates under certain conditions without human intervention. | Spectrum: no automation → partial automation → full automation → autonomy. |\n| **Heteronomy** | A characteristic of a system that functions under the constraint of external intervention, measures, or supervision. | The \"controlled\" end of the autonomy spectrum. |\n| **Narrow AI (Weak AI)** | A type of AI system designed to perform specific tasks in order to solve a particular problem. | All current AI systems are narrow AI. Contrast with General AI. |\n| **General AI (GAI)** | A type of AI system that performs a broad spectrum of tasks at a satisfactory level of performance. Note: AGI is often used in a broader sense — systems that can perform all tasks a human is capable of. | Not yet technically feasible. Important for risk horizon scanning and governance. |\n| **Knowledge** | *\\<Artificial Intelligence>* Abstracted information about objects, events, concepts, or rules, their relationships, and properties, organized for purposeful systematic use. Does **not** imply cognitive abilities. | Technical, non-cognitive definition. Part of the data → information → knowledge hierarchy. |\n| **Inference** | A form of reasoning in which conclusions are derived from known premises. In AI, a basic premise is either a fact, a rule, a model, a feature, or raw data. | Refers to both the process and its result. |\n| **Prediction** | Primary output of an AI system given input data or information. Does **not** necessarily mean forecasting something in the future. | May refer to translations, synthetic images, or diagnosis of past events. |\n| **Task** | *\\<Artificial Intelligence>* Action required to achieve a specific goal. Examples: classification, regression, ranking, clustering, dimensionality reduction. | Actions can be physical or cognitive. |\n| **Performance** | Measurable result. Can refer to quantitative or qualitative findings. | Covers management/control of activities, processes, products, systems, or organizations. |\n| **Life Cycle** | The development of a system, product, service, project, or other human-made entity from planning through to decommissioning. | ISO 22989 defines an AI-system-specific life cycle model (see Clause 6). |\n| **Symbolic AI** | AI based on techniques and models that manipulate symbols and structures according to explicitly defined rules. | Provides declarative results. Traditional/expert-system approach. |\n| **Subsymbolic AI** | AI based on techniques and models that use an implicit encoding of information derived from experience or raw data. | Statistical approach (e.g., neural networks). Results come with a probability of error. Modern AI systems are typically **hybrid** (combining both). |\n| **Expert System** | An AI system that accumulates, combines, and integrates knowledge provided by one or more human experts in a specific domain to derive solutions to problems. | Classical symbolic AI approach. |\n| **Soft Computing** | A field of data processing that tolerates and exploits imprecision, uncertainty, and partial truth to make problem-solving more transparent and robust. | Encompasses fuzzy logic, ML, probabilistic reasoning. |\n| **Cognitive Computing** | A category of AI systems that enables more natural interaction between humans and machines. | Linked to ML, speech processing, NLP, computer vision, HMI. |\n| **Continuous Learning** (Ongoing/Lifelong Learning) | Incremental training of an AI system that takes place continuously during the operational phase of the AI system's lifecycle. | Special case of retraining. Model updates occur repeatedly at high frequency. Poses significant verification challenges. |\n| **Planning** | *\\<Artificial Intelligence>* Data processing operations that assemble a workflow from a series of actions to achieve a specific objective. | May also include human-performed actions in AI management standards. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Data Terms (Clause 3.2)\n\n| Term | ISO 22989 Definition | Governance Notes |\n|------|---------------------|-----------------|\n| **Data Set** | Collection of data with a common format. | Used for training, validation, or testing of AI models. |\n| **Data Annotation** | A process in which data is provided with a set of descriptive information without the data itself being altered. | Metadata, annotations, anchor points. |\n| **Data Quality Review** | A process in which data is checked for completeness, systematic deviations (bias), and other factors that impair its usefulness for an AI system. | Critical governance checkpoint. Data quality directly affects AI system trustworthiness. |\n| **Data Augmentation** | The process of creating synthetic samples by modifying or utilizing existing data. | Expands datasets when data is insufficient. |\n| **Data Sampling** | Process of selecting a subset of data samples expected to exhibit similar patterns and trends as the larger dataset. | Ideally, the subset is representative of the entire dataset. |\n| **Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)** | Initial examination of the data to identify its salient features and assess its quality. | Includes identification of missing values, outliers, and representativeness. |\n| **Ground Truth** | Value of the target variable for a specific element of the labeled input data. | Does **not** imply the labeled data consistently matches actual values. |\n| **Input Data** | Data for which an AI system calculates a predicted result or inference. | The live data fed to the system. |\n| **Production Data** | Data collected during the operational phase of an AI system for which a deployed AI system calculates a predicted result or inference. | Distinct from training/test data. May exhibit data drift over time. |\n| **Training Data** | Data used to train a machine learning model. | See 3.3.16 below. |\n| **Test Data (Evaluation Data)** | Data used to evaluate the performance of a final model. Distinct from training data and validation data. | Must be **never shown** to the model during training or tuning. |\n| **Validation Data (Development Data)** | Data used to compare the performance of different candidate models. Used to tune hyperparameters. | Separate from test data and (generally) training data. Cross-validation and bootstrapping are common when data is scarce. |\n| **Sample** | An atomic data element that is processed in batches by a machine learning algorithm or an AI system. | Fundamental unit of ML processing. |\n| **Imputation** | A procedure in which missing data are replaced by estimated or modeled data. | Data quality remediation technique. |\n| **Personal Data (PbD)** | All information that can be used to identify the natural person to whom such information relates, or that is/could be directly or indirectly related to a natural person. | Aligned with privacy regulation concepts. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Machine Learning Terms (Clause 3.3)\n\n| Term | ISO 22989 Definition | Governance Notes |\n|------|---------------------|-----------------|\n| **Machine Learning (ML)** | The process of optimizing model parameters using computer-aided methods so that the behavior of the model corresponds to the data or experience. | Core AI technique. Data-driven approach to model building. |\n| **Machine Learning Algorithm** | Algorithm for determining parameters of a machine learning model from data according to specified criteria. | E.g., linear regression, SVM, decision trees. |\n| **Machine Learning Model** | A mathematical construct that makes an inference or a prediction based on input data or information. Result of training based on a ML algorithm. | Distinct from the algorithm — the model is the *output* of training. |\n| **Parameter (Model Parameter)** | Internal variable of a model that affects how the model calculates its results. | Examples: weights in a neural network, state transition probabilities in a Markov model. |\n| **Hyperparameter** | A feature of a machine learning algorithm that influences its learning process. Selected **prior** to training. | Examples: number of layers, learning rate, kernel function choice, k in k-means. |\n| **Training (Model Training)** | Process for determining or improving the parameters of a machine learning model, based on a ML algorithm, using training data. | The core learning process. |\n| **Trained Model** | Result of model training. | Should be usable by an AI system to make predictions on production data. |\n| **Retraining** | Updating a trained model through training with other training data. | Triggered by data drift, concept drift, or insufficient initial data. Risk: catastrophic forgetting. |\n| **Supervised Machine Learning** | Machine learning in which only labeled data is used during training. | Used for classification, regression, structured prediction. |\n| **Unsupervised Machine Learning** | Machine learning in which only unlabeled data is used during training. | Used for clustering, dimensionality reduction. |\n| **Semi-Supervised Machine Learning** | Machine learning in which both labeled and unlabeled data are used during training. | Hybrid approach — useful when labeling is expensive. |\n| **Reinforcement Learning (RL)** | Learning an optimal sequence of actions to maximize a reward through interaction with an environment. | Trial-and-error learning. Agents receive indirect feedback. |\n| **Transfer Learning** | A set of methods in which data intended for solving one problem is used to apply the knowledge gained from it to another problem. | E.g., using house-number recognition to recognize handwritten digits. |\n| **Decision Tree** | Model in which inference is encoded as paths from the root to a leaf node in a tree structure. | Comparable to flowcharts. Used for classification and regression. |\n| **Support Vector Machine (SVM)** | Machine learning algorithm that finds decision boundaries with maximum margin. | Maximum margin classifier. Uses kernel functions for higher-dimensional mapping. |\n| **Bayesian Network** | Probabilistic model that uses Bayesian inference for probability calculations using a directed acyclic graph. | Useful for causality, incomplete data handling, overfitting mitigation. |\n| **Human-Machine Teaming** | Integration of human interaction and machine intelligence. | Important governance concept — relates to human oversight requirements. |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Neural Network Terms (Clause 3.4)\n\n| Term | ISO 22989 Definition | Governance Notes |\n|------|---------------------|-----------------|\n| **Neural Network (NN)** | *\\<Artificial Intelligence>* A network consisting of one or more layers of neurons connected by weighted links with adjustable weights, which accepts input data and generates an output. | Outstanding example of the connectionist approach. Originally inspired by biological neurons. |\n| **Deep Learning** | *\\<Artificial Intelligence>* An approach to creating rich hierarchical representations through the training of neural networks with many hidden layers. Deep learning is a subset of ML. | Reduces/eliminates need for feature engineering. Can require significant time and computational resources. |\n| **Neuron** | *\\<Artificial Intelligence>* A primitive processing element that accepts one or more input values and generates an output value by combining the input values and applying an activation function. | Basic building block of neural networks. |\n| **Activation Function** | A function applied to the weighted combination of all inputs to a neuron. Typically nonlinear. | Enables learning of complex features. Examples: threshold, sigmoid, polynomial. |\n| **Feedforward Neural Network (FFNN)** | Neural network in which information is directed from the input layer to the output layer in only one direction. | Simplest NN architecture. No connections within the same layer. |\n| **Convolutional Neural Network (CNN)** | Feedforward neural network with convolution in at least one of its layers. | Common applications: image recognition, video captioning, NLP. |\n| **Recurrent Neural Network (RNN)** | A neural network in which the outputs of both the previous layer and the previous processing step are fed into the current layer. | Deals with ordered sequences (audio, video, text). Has state-dependent properties. |\n| **Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM)** | A type of recurrent neural network that processes sequential data with satisfactory performance for both long- and short-term dependencies. | Solves the vanishing gradient problem in RNNs. |\n| **Exploding Gradient** | A phenomenon in backpropagation training in a neural network in which large error gradients accumulate and lead to very large updates of the weights, causing the model to become unstable. | Training instability risk. |\n| **Connectionism (Connectionist Paradigm)** | A form of cognitive modeling that uses a network of interconnected units, which are generally simple computational units. | Theoretical foundation for neural network approaches. |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Trustworthiness Terms (Clause 3.5)\n\n> **Critical for AIGP Exam:** These definitions underpin the trustworthiness characteristics referenced in ISO 42001, ISO 23894, and the EU AI Act.\n\n| Term | ISO 22989 Definition | Governance Notes |\n|------|---------------------|-----------------|\n| **Trustworthiness** | The ability to meet the expectations of interested parties in a verifiable manner. Characteristics include: reliability, availability, resilience, security, privacy, accountability, transparency, integrity, authenticity, quality, and user-friendliness. Applies to services, products, technologies, data, information, and organizations. | **Anchor concept** for AI governance. Must be **verifiable**. |\n| **Accountability** | The state of being accountable (responsible for actions, decisions, and performance). Refers to an assigned responsibility based on regulations, agreements, or delegation. A person or entity is accountable to another using specific means and criteria. | Key for governance frameworks. Distinct from mere responsibility. |\n| **Bias (Systematic Deviation)** | Systematic differential treatment of certain objects, persons, or groups compared to others. Treatment includes perception, observation, representation, prediction, or decision. | ISO definition focuses on *systematic* differential treatment — not random variation. Modified from ISO/IEC TR 24027:2021. |\n| **Explainability** | A property of an AI system that allows important factors influencing the results of the AI system to be expressed in a way that is understandable to humans. Intended to answer \"Why?\" without claiming optimality. | Distinct from transparency. Focuses on human understanding of *specific* outputs. |\n| **Transparency (System)** | A characteristic of a system whereby appropriate information about the system is made available to relevant interested parties. May include: characteristics, performance, limitations, components, procedures, measures, design objectives, data sources, labeling protocols. | System-level openness. Note: inappropriate disclosure may violate security/privacy. |\n| **Transparency (Organization)** | The characteristic of an organization whereby relevant activities and decisions are communicated to relevant interested parties in a comprehensive, accessible, and understandable manner. | Organization-level openness. |\n| **Robustness** | The ability of a system to maintain its performance level under **all** circumstances. | Absolute standard — \"all circumstances\" is aspirational. Contrast with resilience. |\n| **Reliability** | Property of the correspondence between intended behavior and the results. | Whether the system does what it's supposed to do. |\n| **Resilience** | The ability of a system to quickly recover its operational state following an incident. | Focus on *recovery* after failure. Contrast with robustness (maintaining performance). |\n| **Controllability** | A property of an AI system that enables a human or another external agent to intervene in the system's operation. | Key for human oversight requirements. |\n| **Predictability** | A property of an AI system that enables interested parties to make reliable assumptions about the outcome. | Allows stakeholders to anticipate system behavior. |\n| **Verification** | Confirmation by providing objective evidence that specified requirements have been met. Ensures only that a product conforms to its specifications. | \"Did we build the system right?\" |\n| **Validation** | Confirmation by providing objective evidence that the requirements for a specific intended use or a specific intended application have been met. | \"Did we build the right system?\" |\n| **Risk** | The effect of uncertainty on objectives. Deviation can be positive, negative, or both. Characterized by risk drivers, potential events, consequences, and likelihood. | Aligned with ISO 31000:2018 definition. |\n| **Availability** | The property of being accessible and usable when an authorized entity has a need. | Information security triad component. |\n| **Stakeholder (Interested Party)** | An individual, group, or organization that can influence a decision or activity, be affected by it, or feel affected by it. | Broad definition — includes those who merely *feel* affected. |\n\n---\n\n## 6. NLP Terms (Clause 3.6)\n\n| Term | Definition |\n|------|-----------|\n| **Natural Language** | A language that is or was actively used in a community of people and whose rules can be derived from usage. |\n| **Natural Language Processing (NLP)** — *System* | Information processing based on natural language understanding or natural language generation. |\n| **Natural Language Processing (NLP)** — *Discipline* | Discipline concerned with how systems acquire, process, and interpret natural language. |\n| **Natural Language Understanding (NLU)** | Extraction of information from text or speech conveyed in natural language, creating a description of the given text/speech and its representation. |\n| **Natural Language Generation (NLG)** | Task of converting semantic data into natural language. |\n| **Machine Translation (MT)** | Task of automatically translating text or speech from one natural language into another using a computer system. |\n| **Speech Recognition (STT)** | Conversion of a speech signal into a representation of the speech content by a functional unit. |\n| **Speech Synthesis (TTS)** | Generation of artificial speech. |\n| **Sentiment Analysis** | Task to computationally identify and categorize opinions expressed in text, speech, or image to determine a range of sentiments (e.g., positive to negative). |\n| **Named Entity Recognition (NER)** | Task of recognizing and labeling the names of entities and their categories for sequences of words. |\n| **Automatic Summarization** | Task of shortening a portion of natural language content or text while preserving important semantic information. |\n| **Question Answering** | Task of determining the most appropriate answer to a question posed in natural language. |\n\n---\n\n## 7. Computer Vision Terms (Clause 3.7)\n\n| Term | Definition |\n|------|-----------|\n| **Computer Vision** | The ability of a functional unit to capture, process, and interpret image or video data. May include wavelengths beyond visible light (e.g., infrared). |\n| **Image Recognition** | An image classification process that classifies objects, patterns, or concepts in an image. |\n| **Face Recognition** | Automatic pattern recognition that compares stored images of human faces with an actual face image and displays any match with identifying data. |\n\n---\n\n## 8. AI System Life Cycle (Clause 6)\n\nISO 22989 defines the AI system life cycle with the following phases and processes:\n\n| Phase | Key Processes |\n|-------|--------------|\n| **Adoption** | Needs analysis, feasibility study, stakeholder engagement, requirements definition |\n| **Design & Construction** | Data collection/preparation, model design, training, tuning |\n| **Verification & Validation** | Testing against specifications, intended use validation |\n| **Deployment** | Integration into operational environment, user training |\n| **Operation & Monitoring** | Performance monitoring, drift detection, incident management |\n| **Continuous Validation** | Ongoing verification that system meets requirements in production |\n| **Reassessment** | Periodic review of continued fitness for purpose |\n| **Decommissioning** | Planned retirement, data disposition, model archival |\n\n> **Exam Note:** This life cycle is referenced by ISO 42001 (AIMS) and ISO 23894 (Risk Management). Annex C of ISO 23894 maps risk management processes to this life cycle.\n\n---\n\n## 9. AI Ecosystem (Clause 8)\n\nThe ISO 22989 ecosystem model includes:\n\n- **AI Systems** — The deployed technical systems\n- **AI Function** — What the system does (prediction, classification, etc.)\n- **Machine Learning** — The training paradigm\n- **Technology** — Expert systems, logic programming, etc.\n- **Big Data & Data Sources** — Including cloud computing and edge computing\n- **Resource Pools** — ASICs, FPGAs, GPUs, NPUs\n\n---\n\n## 10. Stakeholder Roles (Clause 5.19)\n\n| Role | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| **AI Provider** | Entity that makes an AI system available |\n| **AI Manufacturer** | Entity that builds/assembles the AI system |\n| **AI Customer** | Entity that acquires/uses the AI system |\n| **AI Partner** | Entity that collaborates in AI development or deployment |\n| **AI Subject** | Individual affected by or interacting with an AI system |\n| **Competent Authorities** | Regulatory or oversight bodies |\n\n---\n\n## Key Governance Takeaways\n\n1. **ISO 22989 is the terminological foundation** for all other ISO/IEC AI standards — ISO 42001, ISO 23894, ISO 23894, and others normatively reference it.\n2. **AI is defined as a discipline**, not a technology — the technology is the \"AI system.\"\n3. **Trustworthiness is verifiable** — not aspirational. Characteristics must be evidenced.\n4. **Bias is \"systematic differential treatment\"** — focus is on patterns, not isolated incidents.\n5. **Verification ≠ Validation** — verification checks specs, validation checks fitness for intended use.\n6. **The life cycle model** is the backbone for management system standards (ISO 42001) and risk management (ISO 23894).", "ref_23894": "## At a Glance\n\nISO/IEC 23894:2023 — AI risk management guidance that deliberately mirrors ISO 31000:2018 clause-by-clause, extending each generic risk concept with AI-specific guidance: principles, framework (leadership, integration, design, implementation, evaluation, improvement), and process (identification, analysis, evaluation, treatment, monitoring, recording). Three informative annexes carry the AI payload: objectives (A), risk sources (B), and lifecycle mapping (C).\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nIts relationship to ISO 31000 is the tested fact: extends, does not replace. Distinguish from 42001 (management system you certify) — 23894 is guidance you follow. Annex B's AI risk sources (data quality, lack of explainability, automation…) supply exam-friendly lists.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Guidance standard (not certifiable) extending ISO 31000's generic risk management to AI contexts\n- Structure mirrors 31000: principles → framework → process, each clause annotated with AI-specific extensions\n- Annex A: common AI-related objectives (fairness, safety, privacy, robustness, transparency…)\n- Annex B: AI risk sources — data quality/availability, lack of explainability, degree of automation, hardware/software faults, lifecycle complexity\n- Annex C: maps risk management activities onto the AI lifecycle stages\n- Pairs naturally with ISO/IEC 42001: 42001 builds the management system; 23894 details the risk discipline inside it\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] ISO 23894 — AI Risk Management\n\n# ISO/IEC 23894 — AI Risk Management\n\n> **Full Title:** ISO/IEC 23894:2023 — Information technology — Artificial intelligence — Guidance on risk management\n>\n> **Status:** International Standard, First Edition (2023-02)\n>\n> **Scope:** Guidance on how organizations that develop, produce, deploy, or use products, systems, and services utilizing AI can manage risk specifically related to AI. Customizable to any organization and its context.\n>\n> **⚠️ Note:** This reference is based on the publicly available 12-page SAMPLE. The full standard contains additional detail in the process clauses, Annex A (objectives), Annex B (risk sources), and Annex C (AI system life cycle mapping).\n\n## Cross-References\n\n- [[NIST AI RMF — Structured Reference]]\n- [[ISO 42001 — Structured Reference]]\n- [[AI Risk Management and Assurance]]\n- [[ISO 22989 — Key Definitions]]\n\n---\n\n## Document Architecture\n\nISO 23894 is intentionally structured to **mirror ISO 31000:2018** clause-by-clause, extending each with AI-specific guidance:\n\n| ISO 31000:2018 Clause | ISO 23894 Treatment |\n|------------------------|---------------------|\n| **Clause 4 — Principles** | AI-specific implications for each of the 8 principles |\n| **Clause 5 — Framework** | Extended guidance for leadership, integration, design, implementation, evaluation, improvement |\n| **Clause 6 — Process** | AI-specialized risk assessment, treatment, monitoring, recording |\n| **Annex A** (informative) | Common AI-related objectives |\n| **Annex B** (informative) | AI risk sources |\n| **Annex C** (informative) | Mapping between risk management processes and AI system life cycle |\n\n> **Key Relationship:** ISO 23894 extends ISO 31000 — it does **not** replace it. \"Whenever this document extends the guidance given in ISO 31000:2018, an appropriate reference to the clauses of ISO 31000:2018 is made followed by AI-specific guidance.\"\n\n---\n\n## Normative References\n\n| Standard | Role |\n|----------|------|\n| **ISO 31000:2018** | Risk management — Guidelines (the parent framework) |\n| **ISO Guide 73:2009** | Risk management — Vocabulary |\n| **ISO/IEC 22989:2022** | AI concepts and terminology (definitions for \"AI system,\" etc.) |\n\n---\n\n## Part 1: Principles of AI Risk Management (Clause 4)\n\nISO 23894 takes the 8 principles from ISO 31000:2018 and provides AI-specific implications:\n\n| # | Principle | AI-Specific Guidance |\n|---|-----------|---------------------|\n| **a** | **Integrated** — Risk management is an integral part of all organizational activities. | No additional guidance beyond ISO 31000. |\n| **b** | **Structured & Comprehensive** — Structured approach contributes to consistent and comparable results. | No additional guidance beyond ISO 31000. |\n| **c** | **Customized** — Framework and process are customized and proportionate to the organization's external and internal context. | No additional guidance beyond ISO 31000. |\n| **d** | **Inclusive** — Appropriate and timely involvement of stakeholders. | **Significant AI expansion:** Organizations should seek dialog with diverse internal and external groups due to potentially far-reaching AI impacts. Stakeholder involvement is critical for: (1) identifying risks in data collection, processing, and use; (2) addressing transparency and explainability challenges across diverse AI technologies; (3) defining fairness criteria and identifying bias in automated decision-making. |\n| **e** | **Dynamic** — Risks can emerge, change, or disappear as context changes. Risk management anticipates, detects, acknowledges, and responds. | **Critical for AI:** AI systems are inherently dynamic due to continuous learning, refining, evaluating, validating. Customer expectations change quickly. Legal/regulatory requirements are frequently updated. Organizations should integrate with quality, environmental, safety, healthcare, legal, and corporate responsibility management systems. |\n| **f** | **Best Available Information** — Inputs based on historical/current information and future expectations. Takes into account limitations and uncertainties. | **AI expansion:** Historical information can be limited for emerging AI. Future expectations can change quickly. Organizations engaged in AI development should track information about further uses of their AI systems. Users should maintain records throughout the entire lifetime. Internal use of AI systems should be considered. Tracking customer use may be limited by IP, contractual, or market restrictions. |\n| **g** | **Human & Cultural Factors** — Human behaviour and culture significantly influence all aspects of risk management. | **AI expansion:** Organizations should monitor how AI systems interact with pre-existing societal patterns that can lead to impacts on equitable outcomes, privacy, freedom of expression, fairness, safety, security, employment, the environment, and human rights broadly. |\n| **h** | **Continual Improvement** — Risk management is continually improved through learning and experience. | **AI expansion:** Organizations should monitor the AI ecosystem for performance successes, shortcomings, and lessons learned. Maintain awareness of new AI research findings and techniques as opportunities for improvement. |\n\n### Key Insight: The \"Dynamic\" Principle is Uniquely Important for AI\n\nAI systems can **change themselves** (continuous learning, adaptive optimization). This means:\n- Risk profiles are not static — they evolve with the system\n- Verification at deployment is insufficient — continuous monitoring is required\n- Regulatory change is accelerating in the AI space\n\n---\n\n## Part 2: Framework (Clause 5)\n\n### 5.1 General\nThe framework assists organizations in integrating risk management into significant activities and functions. **ISO/IEC 38507** (AI governance) describes additional governance considerations and can be used in combination with this document.\n\n### 5.2 Leadership and Commitment\n- Top management should consider how **policies and statements related to AI risks** are communicated to stakeholders.\n- Demonstrating leadership is critical for ensuring stakeholder confidence that AI is being developed and used responsibly.\n- Organizations should consider **issuing statements related to their commitment to AI risk management**.\n- Top management should be aware of **specialized resources** needed to manage AI risk.\n\n### 5.3 Integration\n- Guidance from ISO 31000:2018, 5.3 applies.\n- Risk management should be embedded in organizational governance, not treated as a separate activity.\n\n### 5.4 Design\n\n#### 5.4.1 Understanding the Organization and Its Context\n\nISO 23894 provides an extended table (Table 2) mapping ISO 31000 external context factors to AI-specific considerations:\n\n| ISO 31000 Generic Factor | AI-Specific Expansion |\n|--------------------------|----------------------|\n| Social, cultural, political, legal, regulatory, financial, technological, economic, environmental factors | **Relevant legal requirements specifically relating to AI.** Guidelines on ethical use and design of AI from government, regulators, standards bodies, civil society, academia, and industry. Domain-specific AI guidelines and frameworks. |\n| Key drivers and trends | **Technology trends and advancements in various areas of AI.** Societal and political implications of AI deployment, including social science guidance. |\n| External stakeholder relationships, perceptions, values, needs, expectations | Stakeholder perceptions affected by **lack of transparency (opaqueness) of AI systems or biased AI systems.** Expectations on availability of AI-based solutions and means by which AI models are made available. |\n| Contractual relationships and commitments | How **continuous learning AI** affects ability to meet contractual obligations and guarantees. Ownership and usage rights of **test and training data** when provided by third parties. |\n| Complexity of networks and dependencies | Use of AI can **increase the complexity** of networks and dependencies. |\n\n#### 5.4.2–5.4.5\n- Articulating risk management commitment\n- Assigning organizational roles, authorities, responsibilities, and accountabilities\n- Allocating resources\n- Establishing communication and consultation\n\n### 5.5 Implementation\nRisk management framework implementation — guidance from ISO 31000:2018, 5.5 applies.\n\n### 5.6 Evaluation\nFramework effectiveness evaluation — guidance from ISO 31000:2018, 5.6 applies.\n\n### 5.7 Improvement\n- **Adapting** the framework as the organization and its AI context changes\n- **Continually improving** suitability, adequacy, and effectiveness\n\n---\n\n## Part 3: Risk Management Process (Clause 6)\n\n### 6.1 General\nThe process involves the systematic application of policies, procedures, and practices to:\n- Communicating and consulting\n- Establishing the context\n- Assessing, treating, monitoring, reviewing, recording, and reporting risk\n\n### 6.2 Communication and Consultation\n- Organizations should communicate and consult with stakeholders throughout the risk management process.\n- AI-specific consideration: diverse stakeholders may include those who do not directly use the AI system but are affected by it (see \"Inclusive\" principle).\n\n### 6.3 Scope, Context, and Criteria\n\n#### 6.3.1 General\nEstablishing the scope, context, and criteria for AI risk management.\n\n#### 6.3.2 Defining the Scope\n- Define what AI systems/activities are covered\n- Consider the full AI system life cycle\n\n#### 6.3.3 External and Internal Context\nSee framework Table 2 above for detailed AI-specific external context factors.\n\n#### 6.3.4 Defining Risk Criteria\n- Define the amount and type of risk the organization is willing to take (risk appetite)\n- Criteria for evaluating risk significance\n\n### 6.4 Risk Assessment\n\n#### 6.4.1 General\nRisk assessment is the overall process of risk identification, risk analysis, and risk evaluation.\n\n#### 6.4.2 Risk Identification\n- Identify risks that could affect AI-related objectives\n- Consider risks arising from:\n - AI system design and development\n - Data quality and provenance\n - Model behavior and performance\n - Deployment context and use cases\n - Stakeholder impacts\n - Regulatory and legal environment\n\n#### 6.4.3 Risk Analysis\n- Analyze the nature and characteristics of identified risks\n- Consider likelihood and consequences\n- Account for AI-specific factors like model uncertainty, data drift, adversarial inputs\n\n#### 6.4.4 Risk Evaluation\n- Compare risk analysis results with established risk criteria\n- Determine which risks need treatment and prioritization\n\n### 6.5 Risk Treatment\n\n#### 6.5.1 General\n- Select and implement options to address risk\n\n#### 6.5.2 Selection of Risk Treatment Options\nOptions may include:\n- Avoiding the risk\n- Taking/increasing risk to pursue an opportunity\n- Removing the risk source\n- Changing the likelihood\n- Changing the consequences\n- Sharing the risk\n- Retaining the risk by informed decision\n\n#### 6.5.3 Preparing and Implementing Risk Treatment Plans\n- Document treatment plans\n- Implement plan elements\n- Monitor effectiveness\n\n### 6.6 Monitoring and Review\n- Ongoing monitoring of risk and effectiveness of treatments\n- Periodic review to ensure continued appropriateness\n\n### 6.7 Recording and Reporting\n- Document risk management activities and results\n- Report to appropriate stakeholders\n- Provide information for decision-making and continual improvement\n\n---\n\n## How ISO 23894 Differs from NIST AI RMF\n\n| Dimension | ISO 23894 | NIST AI RMF |\n|-----------|-----------|-------------|\n| **Parent Framework** | ISO 31000:2018 (generic risk management) | Standalone AI-specific framework |\n| **Structure** | Mirrors ISO 31000 clause structure (Principles → Framework → Process) | Four functions: Govern → Map → Measure → Manage |\n| **Normative Status** | International standard for certification/auditing | Voluntary framework (U.S. federal guidance) |\n| **Risk Definition** | Deviation from objectives (positive or negative) | Primarily negative outcomes (harms) |\n| **Integration Focus** | Designed to integrate with ISO management systems (ISO 42001, ISO 27001, ISO 9001) | Designed to integrate with organizational risk management generally |\n| **Geographic Focus** | Global (ISO/IEC) | United States (NIST), though internationally influential |\n| **Auditability** | Designed for third-party certification under ISO 42001 | Self-assessment and external evaluation (no formal certification) |\n| **Life Cycle Coverage** | Maps to ISO 22989 AI life cycle (Annex C) | Maps to AI lifecycle stages (Map function) |\n\n---\n\n## Integration with ISO 42001 (AI Management System)\n\nISO 23894 is designed to complement ISO 42001:\n\n1. **ISO 42001 Clause 6.1** (Actions to address risks and opportunities) directly references risk management processes — ISO 23894 provides the \"how.\"\n2. **ISO 42001 Clause 8** (Operation) requires risk assessment for AI systems — ISO 23894 provides the methodology.\n3. **ISO 42001 Annex A** controls can be informed by risk assessment outputs from ISO 23894.\n4. Both standards share the **ISO 22989 terminology** foundation.\n5. Both follow the **ISO management system harmonized structure (HS)** — Plan-Do-Check-Act cycle.\n\n### Integration Model\n\n```\nISO 31000:2018 (Risk Management — Principles and Guidelines)\n ↓ extends with AI-specific guidance\nISO/IEC 23894:2023 (AI Risk Management)\n ↓ informs risk processes within\nISO/IEC 42001:2023 (AI Management System — Requirements)\n ↓ uses terminology from\nISO/IEC 22989:2022 (AI Concepts and Terminology)\n```\n\n---\n\n## Key Governance Takeaways\n\n1. **ISO 23894 does not stand alone** — it is an AI-specific extension of ISO 31000 and must be read in conjunction with it.\n2. **The \"Dynamic\" principle is the most AI-distinctive** — AI systems learn and change in production, making static risk assessments insufficient.\n3. **Stakeholder inclusiveness is broadened** — organizations must consider those indirectly affected by AI, not just direct users.\n4. **Continuous learning AI** creates unique contractual and verification challenges.\n5. **ISO 23894 is designed for integration** with ISO 42001 AIMS — risk management is not a separate activity but embedded in AI governance.\n6. **Unlike NIST AI RMF**, ISO 23894 considers risk as both opportunity and threat (aligned with ISO 31000's broader definition).\n7. **The sample document is limited** — the full standard includes Annex A (AI-related objectives), Annex B (risk sources, e.g., data quality, model behavior, deployment context), and Annex C (life cycle mapping table).", "ch8": "## At a Glance\n\nThis knowledge area governs design: documenting the AI lifecycle from the first use-case conversation, grounding systems in business context and defined success criteria, running impact assessments before resources are committed, making design choices that embed governance (architecture, human oversight points, explainability), identifying design-phase risks, and handling the special constraints of proprietary and third-party models.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nSequencing questions are common — impact assessment before build, success criteria before model selection. Know the documentation artifacts (model cards, datasheets, AIIAs) and what each captures, and expect scenarios about design choices that trade accuracy against explainability or privacy.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Document from day one: use case, intended users, success criteria, assumptions, exclusions — the lifecycle record starts at conception\n- Model cards document a model's intended use, training data, metrics, and limitations; datasheets do the same for datasets\n- AI impact assessments (AIIAs) evaluate social, ethical, legal, and operational impacts before and during development\n- Design-phase risk work is the cheapest risk work: changing an architecture costs less than remediating a deployed system\n- Proprietary/third-party models constrain governance: limited visibility into training data demands compensating contractual and testing controls\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] AI System Design\n\n## Performance Indicators\n\n- **Document the designing and building process** (for example, to establish compliance and manage risks).\n- **Document the training and testing process** (for example, to validate results, establish compliance and manage risks).\n- **Define the business context and use case** of the AI system.\n- **Understand the context of the AI use case** (for example, business objectives, performance requirements, data availability, ethical considerations and workforce readiness).\n- **Perform or review an impact assessment** on the AI system.\n- **Apply the policies, procedures, best practices and ethical considerations** to designing and building the AI system (for example, purpose of AI, requirements gathering, architecture and model selection, human oversight, data analysis, metric and threshold evaluation, stakeholder engagement and feedback and operational controls).\n- **Identify and manage the internal and external risks and contributing factors** related to designing and building the AI model and system (for example, using probability/severity harms matrix, using a risk mitigation hierarchy, stakeholder mapping, use case evaluation, benchmarking, pre-deployment pilots and testing).\n- **Identify and understand the risks and opportunities unique to a company deploying its own proprietary AI model** (for example, increased obligations and higher potential liability).\n\n---\n\n> **Note on \"Applicable Laws\":** BOK v2.0.1 listed \"Identify laws that apply to the AI model\" under III.A (and IV.B). **BOK v2.1 removed both indicators** — legal applicability is now tested through Domain II (II.A–II.C). This area focuses on the design process itself.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n> [!source] AI System Design\n\n## 4. AI System Design Considerations\n\nDesigning an AI system is a multidimensional process integrating ethical reasoning, regulatory constraints, stakeholder engagement, and operational foresight.\n\n### 4.1 Defining the Purpose and Framing the Problem\n\nDesign begins with intentionality and clarity. When success is defined in advance, designers have a clearer idea of how to get there.\n\n**Key questions:**\n- What business or operational problem does the system aim to solve?\n- Is AI the appropriate tool for this problem?\n- What are the intended and unintended outcomes?\n\n> The design phase is the **final opportunity to question everything** before the system is built. AI systems often fail because the business problem was misframed or embedded assumptions were not validated.\n\n### 4.2 Architecture and Model Selection\n\nOrganizations must consider not just the design of the AI model but the **full architecture**: how components interact, how data flows, and how results are delivered. Mature organizations often develop both a **logical architecture** and a **physical architecture**.\n\n#### Designing for Modularity and Traceability\n\nModular architectures support scalability, troubleshooting, model updates, and explainability:\n\n| Practice | Benefit |\n|----------|---------|\n| Separate data ingestion, model inference, and output rendering | Independent testing of each component |\n| Log internal system decisions and state transitions | Debugging and auditability |\n| Document all module- and system-level interfaces | Integration clarity |\n| Track versions of data pipelines, models, and dependencies | Reproducibility and rollback |\n| Assign ownership and change-approval responsibilities | Accountability |\n\n#### Choosing the Right Model Type\n\nThe AI model should be fit to effectively address the business problem's complexity, interpretability needs, and data environment.\n\n**Considerations:**\n- High-stakes decisions may demand simpler, explainable models\n- Resource-constrained environments may favor lightweight or cloud-based models\n- Proprietary vs. open-source choices may affect transparency\n\n> **Avoiding \"Model Envy\":** The latest deep learning model may not be the right fit. Simpler models can outperform complex ones when data is structured, features are well-defined, and explainability is crucial.\n\n**Model Selection Tradeoffs:**\n\n| Tradeoff | Tension |\n|----------|---------|\n| Accuracy vs. interpretability | Higher accuracy often means lower explainability |\n| Speed vs. cost | Faster inference may cost more |\n| Fairness vs. personalization | More personalization can introduce bias |\n| Data volume vs. privacy | More data improves performance but increases privacy risk |\n| Data volume vs. security | More data increases attack surface |\n| Risk minimization vs. innovation | Conservative choices may limit capabilities |\n\n### 4.3 Human Oversight and Feedback Loops\n\nAI systems in socio-technical environments require human intervention.\n\n#### Designing Human-in-the-Loop Processes\n\nDetermine the role humans play and at what stage(s) in the workflow:\n\n| Role | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| **Approver** | Worker must sign off on AI output before action commences |\n| **Reviewer** | Worker reviews audit system output periodically |\n| **Escalation point** | System or affected party flags uncertain cases for human review |\n\n#### Designing for Usability and Understanding\n\nHuman oversight is only effective if they understand what the system is doing:\n- Use clear language, not black box scores\n- Provide clear, contextual explanations\n- Allow easy overrides with rationale logging\n\n#### Feedback Integration\n\nSeek feedback from users via:\n- Online surveys\n- Focus groups\n- Explicit feedback (\"This decision was incorrect\")\n- Implicit signals (user overrides, workflow abandonment)\n- Formal review cycles with business stakeholders\n\n> Feedback should trigger retraining, bug fixes, or policy review — not just be logged and ignored.\n\n### 4.4 Metrics, Thresholds, and Controls\n\nDesign decisions determine how success and failure can be measured.\n\n#### Metric Selection\n\nDifferent problems require different metrics. Poorly chosen metrics can lead to unintended behavior or the organization being unaware of significant developments.\n\n> **Further reading:** *How to Measure Anything in Cybersecurity Risk*, 2nd Edition; *Operational Risk: Measurement and Modelling*; *Key Performance Indicators: Developing, Implementing, and Using Winning KPIs*, 4th Edition.\n\n#### Threshold Setting and Adjustment\n\nThresholds must be aligned with risk tolerance, regulatory requirements, and ethical norms:\n- Calibrate thresholds based on business impact and harm potential\n- Require formal approval for threshold changes\n- Document rationale for chosen settings\n\n#### Operational Controls\n\nImplement AI-specific controls as part of responsible AI use:\n\n| Control | Purpose |\n|---------|---------|\n| Alerting for unusual model behavior | Anomaly detection |\n| Failsafe defaults | Deny by default |\n| **Shadow mode** deployment | Test before production |\n| \"Kill switch\" | Disable faulty models |\n\n> **Controls in the EU AI Act:** High-risk AI systems must include human oversight mechanisms, logging, and post-deployment monitoring — all of which depend on decisions made during the design phase.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n> [!source] AI System Design\n\n## 5. Identifying and Managing Design Risks\n\nAI system design introduces unique risks — some inherited from traditional software development, others stemming from the probabilistic and adaptive nature of machine learning. These risks can manifest **internally** (design flaws, poor data quality, integration failures) or **externally** (misuse, bias, systemic harm).\n\n### 5.1 Framing AI Design Risk\n\nUnlike deterministic systems, AI systems introduce **nondeterminism**, **opacity**, and **emergent behavior**, complicating risk identification:\n\n| AI-Specific Risk | Description |\n|------------------|-------------|\n| Unpredictable behavior | Unexpected outputs on unseen data conditions |\n| Difficulty tracing decisions | Cannot trace outputs to specific inputs or logic |\n| Bias embedded in training data | Particularly by proxy variables |\n| Drift in model behavior | Degradation over time |\n| Overreliance on automation | Without proper oversight |\n\n#### Distinguishing Between Internal and External Risk Sources\n\n**Internal risks** (within the organization, in its control):\n\n| Risk | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| Faulty assumptions | Misframed problems |\n| Inadequate documentation | Gaps in records |\n| Inadequate training data | Poor quality or insufficient data |\n| Poor integration | System interface failures |\n| Incomplete testing | Gaps in validation |\n| Model performance gaps | Below-threshold performance |\n| Lack of explainability/transparency | Opaque decision-making |\n| Inadequate monitoring | No drift or misuse detection |\n\n**External risks** (outside the organization, largely out of its control):\n\n| Risk | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| Adversarial use and cyberattacks | Malicious exploitation |\n| Legal or regulatory changes | New compliance obligations |\n| Supply chain risks and breaches | Third-party vulnerabilities |\n| Cultural backlash or ethical misalignment | Public rejection |\n| User misuse or automation complacency | Improper use by operators |\n\n> **Internal ≠ Controllable:** Even though the organization creates internal risks, they are not always entirely within its control — especially when using third-party components or pretrained models.\n\n### 5.2 Systematic Risk Identification Techniques\n\n#### Risk Matrix (Probability/Severity)\n\nOne of the most widely used tools in risk management — prioritizes risks based on probability of occurrence and potential impact/harm.\n\n**Typical Risk Matrix:**\n\n| Severity / Probability | Low Probability | Medium Probability | High Probability |\n|------------------------|-----------------|---------------------|-------------------|\n| **Low severity** | Monitor | Low priority | Medium priority |\n| **Medium severity** | Low priority | Medium priority | High priority |\n| **High severity** | Medium priority | High priority | Critical |\n\n> A medical imaging AI model with low probability of misclassifying rare conditions but high severity when it does may require layered oversight even if errors are rare.\n\n#### Risk Mitigation Hierarchy\n\nA hierarchy-based approach prioritizes interventions from most to least effective:\n\n| Tier | Strategy | Example |\n|------|----------|---------|\n| 1 | **Eliminate the risk** | Don't use AI in high-risk contexts |\n| 2 | **Substitute with lower-risk design** | Use simpler, interpretable models |\n| 3 | **Engineer safeguards** | Confidence thresholds, HITL controls |\n| 4 | **Warn and train users** | Interpretability dashboards |\n| 5 | **Accept with monitoring** | Audit logs, post-market review |\n\n> **Tradeoffs in the Real World:** Elimination or substitution are ideal concepts but often impractical due to business demands. Risk mitigation in AI frequently involves strategic layering of controls. Risk treatment is a component of risk management — discussed at length in CISM and CRISC certification materials.\n\n#### Stakeholder Mapping and Risk Perception\n\nDifferent stakeholders perceive risk differently. Mapping roles, incentives, and exposure helps identify overlooked risk vectors.\n\n| Stakeholder | Risk Concerns |\n|-------------|---------------|\n| **Users** | Error, confusion, overreliance, hallucination |\n| **Subjects** | Unfair decisions, privacy harm |\n| **Operators** | Workload mismatch, liability, costs |\n| **Regulators** | Non-compliance, lack of transparency |\n| **Public** | Reputational or ethical concerns |\n\n### 5.3 Use Case Evaluation and Scenario Planning\n\nThe specific use case and environment significantly influence the types and severity of design risks.\n\n#### Use Case Classification and Risk Profiling\n\nThe frequency and rigor of risk assessments should be proportionate to the level of risk.\n\n**Factors to consider:**\n- Decision impact (low, medium, high)\n- User exposure (internal vs. external)\n- Affected rights or opportunities\n- Automation level (advisory vs. autonomous)\n- Regulation (e.g., EU AI Act, privacy laws)\n- Contextual factors (cultural norms, societal scrutiny, prior incidents)\n\n> An AI used for Netflix recommendations may warrant lighter-touch risk controls than one used for unemployment eligibility decisions.\n\n#### Scenario Planning and Adverse Events\n\nTechniques:\n- **Red teaming** and adversarial testing\n- **\"Premortem\" exercises** — envisioning failure and tracing causes\n- Misuse and attack role-play with diverse personas\n\n#### Benchmarking and Comparative Risk Analysis\n\nCompare planned AI systems to existing benchmarks or similar implementations:\n- Academic benchmarks: ImageNet, GLUE\n- Peer organizations using similar AI systems\n\n> Benchmarking helps inform design and can also help defend decisions during audits or litigation.\n\n### 5.4 Testing, Piloting, and Validation\n\nAI system testing is not a single event — it is a **design tool** conducted in stages throughout the development cycle.\n\n| Testing Stage | Description |\n|---------------|-------------|\n| **Unit testing** | Individual components tested in isolation |\n| **Pre-deployment pilots** | Limited release to \"friendly\" users for readiness testing; define success/failure criteria; include diverse users; plan post-pilot review |\n| **Shadow mode testing** | Integrate into existing processes to capture and analyze outputs (similar to parallel testing in IT DR planning); obtain live performance data; verify integrations |\n| **Stress testing and red teaming** | Out-of-distribution data; adversarial inputs; simulated outages; drift simulations; threat modeling (common in cybersecurity) |\n\n### 5.5 Risk Governance Integration\n\nDesign-phase risk management must connect to the organization's broader risk and compliance infrastructure.\n\n#### Alignment with Enterprise Risk Frameworks\n\nTrack AI design risks in the same systems and formats used for other operational risks:\n- Single risk register\n- Common scoring rubrics\n- Assignment of risk ownership\n- Escalation paths\n\n#### Documenting Residual Risk and Justifications\n\nNot all risks can be eliminated. **Residual risk** — the \"leftover\" risk after mitigation — should be documented. If the magnitude of residual risk is great enough, it should be treated as yet another risk, analyzed, and a decision made on what to do about it.\n\n> **Don't Hide Residual Risk:** Regulators and stakeholders are often more concerned with undisclosed risks than with the existence of risk itself. Transparency builds trust.\n\n#### Preparing for Post-deployment Risk Monitoring\n\nRisk assessments are equally necessary throughout the AI system's service life. Design risk controls must extend beyond deployment:\n- Drift detection\n- Retraining thresholds\n- Monitoring dashboards\n- Incident response workflows\n- Feedback loops from users or regulators\n\n---\n\n---\n\n> [!source] AI System Design\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n1. **Documenting the AI lifecycle ensures traceability, accountability, compliance, and audit readiness.** From system design to testing, comprehensive documentation provides the foundation for governance, compliance, and future maintenance. It supports explainability, allows teams to replicate decisions, and enables post-incident analysis.\n\n2. **Defining the business context and use case(s) aligns AI development with real-world needs and constraints.** AI systems must solve clearly and formally defined problems, serve specific user groups, and operate within set performance boundaries.\n\n3. **Impact assessments are not optional for high-risk AI systems — they are a legal and ethical necessity.** Required under frameworks like the EU AI Act and Canada's DADM, impact assessments identify potential risks, harms, fairness concerns, and gaps in oversight.\n\n4. **Design decisions must balance technical performance with ethical, human-centered, operational considerations.** Choosing a model architecture, defining metrics, and establishing thresholds often requires tradeoffs informed by explainability, fairness, robustness, and usability.\n\n5. **Organizations deploying proprietary models assume greater liability and governance responsibilities.** Unlike off-the-shelf tools, proprietary AI systems place full lifecycle accountability on the development organization — including legal exposure, regulatory classification as a provider, and the burden of maintaining documentation, oversight, and audit readiness internally.\n\n---", "t_lifecycle": "## At a Glance\n\nThe documentation spine of AI governance: recording the system from conception through retirement so every later question — audit, incident, regulator, court — has an answer. The artifact set: model cards (model purpose, data, metrics, limits), datasheets for datasets, system cards for full deployments, plus training/testing records, decision logs, and version history.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nArtifact-matching is common: model card vs. datasheet vs. system card. The principle behind every records question: contemporaneous documentation is evidence of governance; retroactive documentation is evidence of scrambling.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Model cards: intended use, training data summary, evaluation metrics, known limitations, ethical considerations — per model\n- Datasheets for datasets: motivation, composition, collection process, preprocessing, recommended uses — per dataset\n- System cards document the assembled system: components, data flows, oversight points, deployment context\n- Lifecycle documentation is explicitly expected by NIST AI RMF, ISO/IEC 42001, and the EU AI Act's Annex IV technical documentation\n- Version everything: model weights, data snapshots, code, and configuration — reproducibility is the audit baseline\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] AI System Design\n\n## 1. Documenting the Lifecycle\n\nDocumenting the AI system's lifecycle is essential for ensuring compliance, managing risks, promoting accountability, maintaining transparency, and ensuring operational continuity. Each stage of development introduces potential risks, governance obligations, and opportunities to embed trustworthy AI practices.\n\n### 1.1 Documenting Requirements\n\nBefore design can begin, formally identify and capture the **requirements** of the proposed system. Management determines whether the system operates correctly by confirming whether it successfully fulfills each requirement.\n\nOrganizations with a mature system development lifecycle (SDLC) process will have established practices for documenting, reviewing, and baselining a requirements specification.\n\n**Essential practices in requirements management:**\n\n| Practice | Details |\n|----------|---------|\n| **Verifiability** | Each requirement must be verifiable, traceable, unambiguous, and measurable |\n| **Categorization** | Security, privacy, UX, regulatory, operational, and established standards (OS, protocols, tools) |\n| **Functional requirements** | Describe the AI system's functions |\n| **Technical (non-functional) requirements** | Performance, scalability, security, privacy, usability, resilience |\n| **Regulatory requirements** | Functions and characteristics required by specific regulations |\n| **Security requirements** | Functions that protect the AI system and associated data |\n| **Source tracking** | Record source, owner, priority, and rationale for each requirement |\n| **Data governance** | Consent, lawful basis, PII handling, lineage, retention, deletion |\n| **Evaluation metrics** | Define metrics with thresholds (accuracy, calibration, latency, cost per inference) |\n| **Criticality weighting** | Include the criticality or weighting of each requirement |\n| **Approval** | Final set of requirements should be approved by management |\n\n> **Tip:** Stakeholders with organizational influence may dominate the requirements-setting process. Counterbalance by explicitly incorporating feedback from marginalized or impacted groups. Document who provided each requirement, along with its criticality.\n\n### 1.2 Documenting the Designing and Building Process\n\nDocumentation for an AI system has two dimensions:\n1. A detailed description of the **design and build process** (documented once, periodically revised)\n2. A detailed description of the **design of the AI system** (documented per system)\n\n#### Documentation in the Design Phase\n\nDesign documentation creates a business record of intent, key decisions, and helps align system features with business and regulatory goals. In SDLC/AI governance circles, this is known as a \"design baseline\" or \"design artifact.\"\n\n**Key elements to capture in design documentation:**\n\n| Element | Description |\n|---------|-------------|\n| **System purpose and scope** | Primary functions, decision-making roles, context of use |\n| **Stakeholders and roles** | Who will be affected by or interact with the system; who builds, operates, and maintains it |\n| **Ethical and regulatory considerations** | Alignment with fairness, transparency, accountability, privacy, robustness, compliance |\n| **System boundaries and interfaces** | What the system will/will not do, interfaces with other systems, human oversight expectations |\n| **Data and model design** | Data sources, quality criteria, labeling strategies, model selection rationale, target performance, explainability constraints |\n| **Risk and lifecycle management** | Foreseeable risks, misuse cases, safety constraints, fallback mechanisms, monitoring, retraining, retirement plans |\n\n**Minimum Viable Documentation for AI Design:**\n- Problem statement (as defined by the business, not developers)\n- Governance and compliance context\n- Intended users and use cases\n- Assumptions and constraints\n- System inputs and outputs\n- Training data to be used\n- Initial risk analysis\n- High-level model or architectural design\n\n#### Documentation in the Development Phase\n\nThe development phase transitions requirements and design into code and hardware.\n\n**Artifacts to develop:**\n\n| Artifact | Description |\n|----------|-------------|\n| **Model cards and system cards** | Essential AI-specific artifacts detailing characteristics, intended use cases, limitations, ethical considerations, performance |\n| **Architecture diagrams** | High-level and detailed visuals of system components and interactions, with version control |\n| **Code and model repositories** | Proper security, source control, branches, change history, contributor records |\n| **Tooling and dependencies** | Software libraries, cloud services, frameworks — critical for debugging, patching, future maintenance |\n| **Security considerations** | Sandboxing, secure enclaves, vulnerability scanning; artifacts in official business record repositories |\n\n### 1.3 Documenting the Training and Testing Process\n\nOnce architecture is finalized and approved, model training, validation, and testing begin — critical stages for refining performance, detecting bias, and validating outputs.\n\n#### Training Documentation\n\n**Training documentation should include:**\n\n| Element | Details |\n|---------|---------|\n| **Training data sources and characteristics** | All data sources, exclusions, embellishments, filtering, populations represented; data should be archived and immutable |\n| **Labeling process and quality assurance** | How labels were assigned and verified (for supervised learning) |\n| **Model training configuration** | Hyperparameters, batch size, epoch count, optimization strategy, evaluation metrics and results |\n| **Model performance benchmarks** | Baseline performance, ongoing metrics, information supporting selected thresholds |\n| **Bias detection and mitigation** | Fairness assessments, de-biasing techniques, tradeoffs |\n| **Testing outcomes** | Robustness, generalization, privacy/security test results |\n\n> **Regulatory Expectations for Training Logs:** Under the EU AI Act and other frameworks, training documentation should support: comprehensive documentation sufficient to reproduce the design and development effort, explanation of design rationale, long-term auditability, and evidence of risk mitigation.\n\n#### Testing and Validation Documentation\n\nTesting reveals a model's reliability and safety. Testing documentation should include:\n\n| Element | Details |\n|---------|---------|\n| **Testing methodology** | Types of tests run (unit, integration, performance, adversarial, etc.); NIST ARIA guidance (see [[AI Standards and Frameworks]]) |\n| **Test data description** | Whether test data was withheld from training set; whether it represents real-world conditions |\n| **Test results** | Accuracy, false positives/negatives, precision/recall, other metrics |\n| **Stress testing and edge case analysis** | Boundary conditions, adversarial examples, inputs/outputs outside expected norms |\n| **Failure modes and responses** | Known weaknesses, system behavior upon failure |\n| **User testing and feedback** | How usability and human-AI interaction were evaluated |\n\n> **Testing for Trust:** Trustworthy AI requires more than high accuracy. Testing should answer: Does the system fail gracefully? Can outputs be explained or justified? Are error patterns acceptable under ethical and legal standards?\n\n### 1.4 Maintaining Traceability and Audit Readiness\n\nThorough documentation enables organizations to trace AI behaviors back to their origin — crucial for audits, public inquiries, or litigation.\n\n#### Version Control and Change Management\n\n- Maintain linked **version control** across code, data, and documentation\n- Require peer review and formal approval for key changes\n- Ensure requirements are linked to design elements, test plans, and test results\n- Capture not just decisions but the rationales and outcomes\n- Tag releases with associated risk assessments or approvals\n\n#### Explainability and Documentation Artifacts\n\nArtifacts to create and preserve:\n- Annotated decision flows or diagrams\n- **Feature importance** summaries\n- Case-level justifications or reversals\n- Human-in-the-loop (HITL) escalation paths and results\n- Model and system cards\n\n#### Integration with Broader Governance Frameworks\n\nAI system documentation should connect to broader organizational governance systems:\n- Risk management\n- Model inventories\n- Incident response\n- Ethical review board proceedings\n\nAligning documentation with internal controls helps make governance more repeatable, auditable, and defensible.\n\n---", "t_usecase": "## At a Glance\n\nWhere governance starts: defining the business problem, the intended use, the affected populations, and measurable success criteria before any model is built. Use-case assessment screens proposals for feasibility, value, and risk; scope discipline keeps a system from silently expanding beyond what was assessed and approved.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nSequencing questions: use-case definition and assessment come before data collection, model selection, or development. Scope-creep scenarios (a fraud model quietly reused for hiring) test the rule that new uses require new assessment.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Define first: problem statement, intended users and subjects, success metrics, constraints, exclusions — written down\n- Use-case assessment weighs value, feasibility, data availability, and risk before resources commit\n- Business context drives proportionality: the same technique needs different governance in marketing vs. medical triage\n- Scope discipline: any materially new use of an existing system re-enters assessment — approvals attach to use cases, not models\n- Clear success criteria enable everything downstream: validation targets, monitoring thresholds, and retirement triggers\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] AI System Design\n\n## 2. Business Context and Use Cases\n\nAn AI system's effectiveness depends on both its technical performance and whether it effectively serves its intended business purpose. Defining the business context and specific use case is a foundational step — must be formally completed **before** finalizing requirements, design, and development.\n\n### 2.1 Defining the Business Context\n\n**Business context** defines the organizational, strategic, and operational environment in which an AI system will be used. A **business case** is a statement of the expected benefits to the organization from developing an AI system.\n\n#### Understanding Organizational Objectives\n\nEvery AI project should begin with clearly articulated business objectives.\n\n**Key considerations:**\n- The problem that the AI system is intended to solve\n- Expected success metrics from a business perspective\n- How the system will support broader organizational goals\n\n#### Organizational Readiness and Governance\n\nBefore starting development, assess ability to support and operate the AI system:\n\n| Readiness Factor | Considerations |\n|------------------|----------------|\n| **Leadership** | Leadership support and ongoing budget commitment |\n| **Data governance** | Data governance maturity |\n| **Security/privacy** | Cybersecurity and privacy maturity |\n| **Risk culture** | Risk tolerance and ethical culture |\n| **Expertise** | Availability of cross-functional expertise (legal, IT, data science, operations, cybersecurity, privacy) |\n\n> **AI Project Risk Scoring:** Some organizations assign risk tiers to AI projects during planning. A high-impact, customer-facing model may require stronger documentation, pre-deployment review, and executive sponsorship than a low-risk internal tool. Risk tiers defined in the EU AI Act (covered in [[Global AI Regulatory Landscape]]) are a good starting point.\n\n#### Stakeholder Identification and Engagement\n\nUnderstanding who will interact with, benefit from, or be affected by an AI system is essential.\n\n**Stakeholders generally include:**\n- Business sponsors (usually senior executives)\n- System developers and data scientists\n- Legal and compliance teams\n- Cybersecurity and privacy teams\n- Data governance\n- Regulators and external auditors\n- End users and customers\n- Individuals indirectly impacted by decisions (e.g., rejected applicants)\n\n### 2.2 Articulating the AI Use Case\n\nA well-defined **use case** translates the business context into a high-level specification for an AI application. It describes how the system will operate, what data it will use, and what outcomes it is expected to deliver.\n\n#### Defining the Scope and Boundaries\n\nClear definitions help avoid scope creep, mismatched expectations, and unmanageable risk.\n\n| Scope Element | Description |\n|---------------|-------------|\n| **Intended function** | Classification, prediction, assistance, recommendation |\n| **Intended users and environments** | Who and where |\n| **Assumptions and constraints** | Data sources, infrastructure, regulatory constraints |\n| **Decision authority** | Whether the system acts autonomously or with a human in the loop |\n| **Expected lifetime, workload, frequency** | System lifespan and usage patterns |\n\n#### Defining Success Metrics\n\nThe lack of clear metrics introduces the risk that an organization will optimize for the wrong outcomes. Metrics should be aligned with business goals, realistic given available data, and sensitive to unintended consequences.\n\n| Use Case | Example Metrics |\n|----------|----------------|\n| **Fraud detection** | Reduction in undetected fraud, false positive rate |\n| **Chatbot efficiency** | Resolution time, escalation rate, customer satisfaction |\n| **Hiring tools** | Quality of hire, time to fill, bias across protected classes |\n\n> **Metric Pitfalls:** Relying too heavily on precision can lead to an increase in false negatives. Optimizing for efficiency might reduce transparency. Always weigh tradeoffs.\n\n### 2.3 Assessing Data Availability and Suitability\n\nThe failure of many AI projects is not due to flawed models or designs but rather to poor data availability or misalignment between data and business objective. Data suitability should be finalized **before** development efforts begin.\n\n#### Inventorying Available Data\n\nOrganizations must assess what data they already have, where it resides, and how it was collected. Data must be evaluated for completeness, accuracy, consistency, and label availability (for supervised learning).\n\n> **Data Governance Is a Key Prerequisite:** Organizations lacking mature data governance policies, practices, and tools will be at a decided disadvantage, as they will have only partial awareness of their data inventory and management processes.\n\n#### Legal and Ethical Constraints on Data Use\n\nEven if suitable training data exists, there may be legal or ethical reasons it cannot be used:\n- Data collected for one purpose reused by the AI system without subject consent\n- Inclusion of sensitive attributes (race, health, religious affiliation)\n- Inferences about individuals that may violate privacy expectations\n\n> **Business Impact of GDPR Article 5(1)(b):** The \"purpose limitation\" in the GDPR can prevent organizations from using historical data for training, unless a new lawful basis for processing can be established.\n\n#### Data Readiness for Deployment\n\nOrganizations need to assess whether the data ecosystem can support ongoing data needs:\n- Whether the data pipeline is stable and scalable\n- Whether the model will be fed new, updated data over time\n- Whether real-time inference can be supported, if required\n- Whether monitoring is in place to detect data drift or degradation\n- Whether continuous data ingestion complies with privacy, security, and access-control requirements\n\n### 2.4 Ethical and Operational Considerations\n\nA use case that is feasible and valuable may still raise ethical or operational red flags.\n\n#### Risk of Harm and Ethical Tradeoffs\n\nCommon areas of concern:\n- Use of AI in criminal justice, housing, hiring, education, or credit\n- Reinforcement of historical bias\n- Opacity in high-stakes decisions\n- Algorithmic manipulation of behavior (e.g., addiction loops in social media)\n\n#### Human Oversight and Decision Rights\n\nEvery AI system that makes business decisions should be designed as part of a human-centric decision-making process.\n\n**Consider:**\n- What decisions can AI make autonomously?\n- When must humans be involved in decisions?\n- How are overrides or appeals handled?\n- Is the human oversight role well-defined and resourced?\n- Are there sufficient business records to defend decisions?\n\n**RACI Matrix for AI Oversight:**\n\n| Role | Assigned To |\n|------|-------------|\n| **Responsible** | Data science lead |\n| **Accountable** | C-level product owner |\n| **Consulted** | Legal and ethics committee, privacy officers, security officers, risk managers |\n| **Informed** | Frontline managers |\n\n#### Workforce Readiness and Culture\n\nAI systems change workflows, roles, job requirements, and organizational culture. Consider:\n- Whether impacted teams have been consulted and their feedback heard\n- Whether reskilling or new hiring is needed\n- Whether job aids, support systems, and escalation paths are in place\n- Whether regular employee engagement indicates changes in morale\n\n#### Alignment with Organizational Values and Strategy\n\nEven technically excellent and legally sound AI systems may not align with brand identity, reputation, or public commitments:\n- Does the system contradict public statements or corporate objectives?\n- Will it erode trust with customers or partners?\n- Could it introduce reputational risk disproportionate to its value?\n- Will staff see the project as consistent with company values, or could it trigger internal pushback?\n\n---", "t_impact": "## At a Glance\n\nThe look-before-you-leap instruments: AI impact assessments (AIIAs) evaluating effects on individuals, groups, and society; algorithmic impact assessments for public-sector contexts; DPIAs where personal data creates GDPR duties; and the EU AI Act's fundamental rights impact assessment for certain high-risk deployers. All share a shape: describe the system, identify affected parties, assess severity and likelihood, mitigate, document, revisit.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nTrigger questions (when is a DPIA vs. FRIA required?) and content questions (what does an impact assessment contain?). The governing instinct: assess before deployment, treat assessments as living documents, and let findings actually change the design.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- AIIA: broad evaluation of social, ethical, legal, and operational impacts — best practice for any consequential AI\n- DPIA: legally required under GDPR Art 35 for likely-high-risk personal-data processing; AI profiling is a textbook trigger\n- FRIA: EU AI Act requirement for public-body and certain essential-service deployers of high-risk systems before first use\n- Common anatomy: system description → stakeholders → risks (severity × likelihood) → mitigations → residual risk decision → review cycle\n- Impact assessments are living documents — material changes to system, data, or context reopen them\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] AI System Design\n\n## 3. Impact Assessments\n\nImpact assessments are a cornerstone of responsible AI governance. Whether required by regulation or driven by internal policy, impact assessments help organizations anticipate, measure, and mitigate the potential consequences of deploying AI systems **prior to deployment**.\n\n### 3.1 Understanding AI Impact Assessments\n\nThe primary goal of an **AI impact assessment (AIIA)** is to identify and manage potential harms or unintended consequences before deployment — discrimination, lack of transparency, safety failures, excessive surveillance, or harm to fundamental rights.\n\nWhen documented as a repeatable process, impact assessments:\n- Provide a consistent method for risk identification\n- Encourage transparency and accountability\n- Serve as documentation for internal governance and regulatory compliance\n- Help improve stakeholder trust and public acceptance\n\n#### Legal and Regulatory Drivers\n\n| Framework | Requirement |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **EU AI Act** | Article 6 requires \"conformity assessments\" and documentation for high-risk AI systems |\n| **Canada's DADM** | Article 6.1 requires Algorithmic Impact Assessments (AIA) for AI decision-making systems |\n| **California CPRA (and similar)** | Grants regulators authority to require risk assessments for automated decision-making |\n| **OECD and G7 principles** | Encourage impact assessments as a best practice |\n\n> **Not All Risk Assessments Are Equal:** Cybersecurity, data protection, and AI impact assessments may overlap but each focuses on different domains of risk. Aligning them can streamline governance and reduce redundancy.\n\n#### When and How Often to Conduct Assessments\n\nAI impact assessments should be performed:\n- During the design phase **prior to deployment**\n- Whenever **significant changes** are made to the model or its data\n- In response to **incidents**\n- High-risk systems should be assessed **regularly**\n\n### 3.2 Preparing for an Impact Assessment\n\n#### Define the Assessment Scope\n\nThe scope of an assessment ensures it stays focused and aligns with the AI system's intended use:\n\n- System description and functionality\n- Business context\n- Use cases (for AI systems with more than one)\n- Users and affected populations\n- Geographic and legal jurisdiction(s)\n- Level of autonomy and human oversight in supported business processes\n- Assumptions, constraints, and exclusions\n\n#### Identify Stakeholders\n\nAssessment participants may include:\n- Project sponsors and product owners\n- Developers and data scientists\n- Legal, security, compliance, and privacy officers\n- Domain experts (ethics, DEI, accessibility)\n- Representatives of impacted groups, if feasible\n- External stakeholders (advisors, consultants, vendors, members of the public)\n\n#### Inventory Required Information\n\nBefore an impact assessment can proceed, assemble:\n- Organizational documentation (policies, processes, code of ethics)\n- System documentation (design, functionality, requirements, intended use)\n- Applicable laws, regulations, and other legal obligations\n- Stakeholders (users, operators, affected parties)\n- Data flows, sources, provenance, and types\n- Model performance metrics and known limitations\n- User experience details\n- Previous incidents or public concerns in similar domains\n\n### 3.3 Conducting an Impact Assessment\n\nThe impact assessment process involves identifying potential harms, evaluating risks, documenting mitigations, and capturing residual concerns.\n\n#### Identify Potential Impacts\n\nMap where and how the AI system might create risk or harm:\n\n| Area of Impact | Examples |\n|----------------|----------|\n| **Privacy and surveillance** | Unauthorized data collection, tracking |\n| **IP/data theft** | Theft of intellectual property or privacy data |\n| **Discrimination and bias** | Unfair treatment of protected groups |\n| **Freedom of expression/association** | Censorship, chilling effects |\n| **Autonomy and human dignity** | Reduction of human agency |\n| **Psychological harm** | Manipulation, addiction loops |\n| **Access to rights/opportunities** | Denial of services, credit, employment |\n| **Environmental impact** | Energy consumption, resource use |\n\n#### Evaluate Risk Likelihood and Severity\n\nFor each impact identified, assess:\n\n| Factor | Question |\n|--------|----------|\n| **Likelihood** | Probability of the impact |\n| **Severity** | Level of harm to individuals or groups |\n| **Scope** | Number of people affected |\n| **Reversibility** | Whether harm can be undone |\n\nScoring tools, such as **risk matrices or heat maps**, can help prioritize identified risks.\n\n#### Analyze Discrimination and Fairness Risks\n\nAI systems can discriminate even when protected attributes (race, gender) are excluded from training data. Proxy variables or historical biases embedded in training data can still produce discriminatory outputs.\n\n**Assessment considerations:**\n- Whether the system disproportionately affects protected groups\n- Whether fairness metrics are analyzed\n- Whether proxy variables or historical biases were embedded in training data\n\n> **Key insight:** Even neutral input data may produce discriminatory outputs if AI systems are trained on biased historical patterns. Assess **outcomes**, not just features.\n\n#### Assess Explainability and Transparency\n\nIf stakeholders can't understand how or why an AI system makes specific decisions, the organization may suffer trust and accountability concerns.\n\n**Consider:**\n- Whether end users can understand how the system works\n- Whether decisions can be explained to affected individuals\n- Whether documentation is available to regulators or auditors\n\n#### Review Human Oversight Mechanisms\n\nDetermine whether the system provides for effective human intervention:\n\n| Question | Assessment |\n|----------|------------|\n| Are humans \"in the loop\" or \"out of the loop\"? | Level of human involvement |\n| Can decisions be reviewed, appealed, or reversed? | Recourse mechanisms |\n| Are personnel trained and empowered to override outputs? | Authority and capability |\n| Are decision overrides guided by policy and documented? | Process formalization |\n\n> **Oversight Isn't Just a Checkbox:** A human reviewer must have time to intervene, the authority to override, and visibility into how decisions were made. Oversight must be a standard business process, not an incident-driven activity.\n\n#### Document Mitigation Strategies\n\nWhen an AIIA identifies risks, each should be documented, analyzed, and accompanied by a mitigation plan.\n\n**Examples of risk mitigation:**\n- Removal of sensitive features or proxy variables\n- Applying fairness-aware algorithms, adversarial debiasing, or explainability techniques\n- Addition of user consent or opt-out mechanisms\n- Regular audits or bias testing\n- Escalation of high-risk decisions to human reviewers\n\n> **Risk acceptance:** Like mainstream risk management, some risks may be accepted. All instances of risk acceptance should be documented, including the party that approved the risk. **Residual risks** should also be documented, along with any accepted tradeoffs.\n\n### 3.4 Reviewing and Operationalizing the Assessment\n\nAn impact assessment is valuable only if its findings are reviewed, acted upon, and integrated into broader AI and risk governance.\n\n#### Internal Review and Signoff\n\nAssessments should be reviewed by individuals or committees with governance authority:\n- Legal and regulatory compliance check\n- Risk acceptance or escalation process\n- Ethics review (if applicable)\n- Executive or board-level endorsement for high-risk systems\n\n> AI system risk tiering should be extended to AIIAs, considering how frequently and rigorously they are performed, as well as the level of management required to review and approve them.\n\n#### Integration with Development and Deployment\n\nFindings from the impact assessment must feed into development and deployment processes:\n- Changes to model features or training data\n- Adjustments to user interfaces for better explainability\n- Revisions of deployment timelines or rollout strategies\n- Additions of new monitoring or alerting mechanisms\n\n#### External Transparency and Accountability\n\nDepending on regulatory context and contractual obligations, organizations may be required to publish results or summaries:\n\n| Method | Example |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Public summaries** | Canada's Algorithmic Impact Assessments |\n| **Submission to regulatory bodies** | Under the EU AI Act |\n| **Stakeholder briefings** | Community consultations |\n\n> **What Not to Publish:** Organizations should refrain from publishing proprietary model architectures, protected data, or excessive details in the AIIA that could be exploited by others.\n\n#### Continuous Reassessment\n\nAIAs should be revisited when:\n- AI models are retrained or redeployed\n- New data sources are added\n- Data filtering techniques are changed\n- Use cases or environments change\n- New risks emerge in the field\n- New regulations are enacted\n\n---", "ch9": "## At a Glance\n\nThis knowledge area covers the data and the training: establishing data governance for AI (lawful authority, quality, representativeness, stewardship, security), tracking lineage and provenance so every model output can be traced to its inputs, and the full AI testing taxonomy — unit, integration, validation, performance, security, bias/fairness, explainability, human-in-the-loop — plus the testing risks that remain even when everything passes.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nThe most technical knowledge area and it shows on the exam: expect questions on overfitting vs. underfitting, representativeness failures, drift, train/validation/test splits, and which test type catches which failure. 'It passed testing' is never the right final answer — testing risks and residual risk management follow.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Data governance for AI: confirm lawful rights to use data (including secondary use), quality dimensions, fit-for-purpose checks, stewardship roles\n- Representativeness: training data must reflect the deployment population — its absence is the root of most bias findings\n- Lineage tracks data's journey through transformations; provenance records its origin — both are prerequisites for audit and incident response\n- Testing taxonomy: unit → integration → validation → performance → security (poisoning, inversion, adversarial) → bias/fairness → explainability → HITL/scenario\n- Overfitting memorizes noise (great train, poor real-world); underfitting misses the signal entirely; both are data-sufficiency red flags\n- Testing risks persist post-pass: coverage gaps, benchmark gaming, distribution shift between test and production\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Data Governance and Model Training\n\n## Performance Indicators\n\n- Establish and follow the **requirements for data governance** (assess and document lawful rights to collect and use data; assess data quality, quantity, integrity, and fit-for-purpose).\n- Establish and document **data lineage and provenance**.\n- Plan and perform **training and testing of the AI model and system** (unit, integration, validation, performance, security, bias, and interpretability).\n- Identify and manage **issues and risks during training and testing** of the AI model and system.\n- **Document the training and testing process** (to validate results, establish compliance, and manage risks).\n\n---\n\n---\n\n> [!source] Data Governance and Model Training\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n1. **Data governance is a foundational enabler for trustworthy AI.** While not always a strict legal requirement, data governance ensures data is lawful, reliable, and appropriately managed. Poor or absent governance leads to biased outcomes, regulatory exposure, and loss of stakeholder trust. Effective governance includes lawful data use, role assignment, data quality monitoring, and alignment with business purpose.\n\n2. **Data provenance and lineage must be documented to support accountability, traceability, and reproducibility.** Provenance explains origin and collection conditions; lineage tracks how data is transmitted and modified across systems. Both are essential for auditability and understanding model behavior, particularly for regulatory reviews or real-world performance issues.\n\n3. **AI testing is broader than traditional software testing and must address fairness, robustness, and interpretability.** AI testing encompasses unit, integration, validation, and performance testing, plus ethical dimensions (bias, explainability, security). Standard metrics are insufficient — testing must account for edge cases, subgroup disparities, and evolving risks.\n\n4. **Training and testing introduce unique risks that must be proactively identified and mitigated.** AI systems can fail silently if risks during training and testing are ignored: label noise, data drift, test leakage, inappropriate metrics. Organizations should apply structured review processes, approval gates, validation tools, and continuous monitoring.\n\n5. **A risk-aware testing culture requires organizational maturity and interdisciplinary collaboration.** AI testing and risk management require buy-in from governance teams, legal counsel, domain experts, and business leaders. Formal ownership, clear review gates, and robust documentation ensure AI systems are effective and accountable.\n\n---", "t_datagov": "## At a Glance\n\nManaging data as a governed asset through the AI lifecycle: confirming legal authority to use each source (including secondary-use analysis), measuring quality along defined dimensions, ensuring fitness for the specific model purpose, assigning stewardship roles, and securing data through training pipelines that traditional controls don't reach.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nScenarios test the order (rights and quality checks before training) and the dimensions (accuracy, completeness, consistency, timeliness, validity). 'We had the data' never implies 'we could use it' — provenance and purpose analysis is the tested reflex.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Lawful authority first: rights to collect, rights to repurpose for training, third-party data terms, and documented provenance\n- Quality dimensions: accuracy, completeness, consistency, timeliness, validity, uniqueness — measured, not assumed\n- Fit for purpose: quality is relative to the use — clinical data quality bars differ from ad-targeting bars\n- Stewardship: named owners and stewards per dataset with defined accountability — data without an owner is a finding\n- Security across the pipeline: raw stores, feature stores, training environments, and model artifacts all inherit the data's sensitivity\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Data Governance and Model Training\n\n## 1. Data Governance\n\n> **Core principle:** AI systems are only as reliable and resilient as the data on which they are trained. Data governance is a critical enabler — even a prerequisite — of responsible AI development.\n\n### 1.1 Defining Data Governance for AI\n\n**Data governance** — the framework of managing enterprise data through policies, processes, tools, roles, and responsibilities to ensure data is accurate, usable, secure, and compliant with applicable regulations.\n\nIn AI development, governance ensures training, testing, and operational datasets are:\n- Technically suitable\n- Legally and ethically sound\n- Used appropriately\n\n**Key components of data governance:**\n\n| Component | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **Data classification** | Assigning risk levels to datasets, with handling and governance requirements for each level |\n| **Data flow diagrams (DFDs)** | Visual representations of data flow within and between systems and entities, developed by data/system architects |\n| **Input controls** | Ensuring data flowing into a system is approved by management and formatted correctly |\n| **Output controls** | Ensuring data flowing out of a system is approved by management and formatted correctly |\n| **Data loss prevention (DLP)** | Software that monitors data movement, alerting or blocking movements that violate policy |\n\n### 1.2 Legal Authority and Lawful Use of Data\n\nThe first step in AI data governance: confirming a **defendable legal right** to use the data.\n\n#### Confirming Lawful Rights to Use Data\n\nOrganizations must formally verify authorization to collect, process, store, and analyze data. Rights may be based on:\n\n| Basis | Description |\n|-------|-------------|\n| **Direct user consent** | Checkbox acceptance in an app |\n| **Implicit user consent** | Included in terms of use (insufficient in some jurisdictions, including EU under GDPR) |\n| **Contractual provisions** | Data-sharing agreements |\n| **Privacy policy / notice declarations** | Declared privacy practices |\n| **Regulatory allowances** | e.g., HIPAA-compliant health data sharing, often requiring deidentification and **Business Associate Agreements (BAAs)** |\n\n> **Essential:** Formal legal counsel review with a written assertion is considered essential before using data to train AI.\n\n#### Documentation Requirements\n\nData governance policies should include:\n- Data source registration\n- Consent records (if applicable)\n- Contract references\n- Privacy policy / notice of privacy practices references\n- Jurisdiction-specific legal justifications\n\n#### Secondary Use Risks\n\nOrganizations repurposing data beyond its original collection purpose risk **function creep** — leading to violations of privacy policies, contracts, or regulations. Review processes and impact assessments should identify high-risk secondary uses.\n\n#### When Using Third-party AI Systems\n\nWhen training a third-party AI system with personal information, organizations should impose contractual terms including:\n- Training data may not be used for any other system, customer, or purpose\n- **Right to audit** the third-party organization and related systems\n- Audits and cybersecurity assessments by qualified third parties\n- Right to terminate the agreement without cause\n- Vendor must agree to destroy data upon request and at termination\n\n### 1.3 Assessing Data Quality and Integrity\n\nLawfully acquired data can still undermine AI if it lacks quality or integrity.\n\n#### Dimensions of Data Quality\n\n| Dimension | Definition |\n|-----------|------------|\n| **Accuracy** | Are values correct and free from error? |\n| **Completeness** | Are all required fields populated? |\n| **Integrity** | Are relationships across datasets correctly maintained? |\n| **Timeliness** | Is the data up to date? |\n| **Consistency** | Do values follow expected formats and rules? |\n\n### 1.4 Fit for Purpose\n\n> An AI model can be technically excellent but still fail if it does not align with its intended use.\n\nFitness for purpose includes: compliance with regulations, integration with workflows, and suitability for the operational environment.\n\n#### Detecting and Preventing Data Drift\n\n**Data drift** — when new output data deviates significantly from original distributions. Monitoring protocols should be mandated as part of AI model lifecycle governance, especially for models that update or learn over time.\n\n### 1.5 Ensuring Sufficient and Representative Data\n\n| Concept | Definition |\n|---------|------------|\n| **Data sufficiency** | Having enough volume of data to train a model effectively |\n| **Representativeness** | Ensuring data accurately captures the relevant diversity of the deployment environment |\n\n#### Avoiding Underfitting and Overfitting\n\n| Problem | Cause | Result |\n|---------|-------|--------|\n| **Underfitting** | Insufficient volume of training data | Model unable to generalize effectively |\n| **Overfitting** | Too much narrow or repetitive data | Model learns irrelevant patterns |\n\n> Governance helps find the right balance by defining sufficiency thresholds and reuse limits.\n\n#### Representing Real-world Populations\n\nTo avoid bias, AI governance frameworks should incorporate fairness checks:\n- Demographic audits\n- Data distribution checks (e.g., geographical distribution)\n- Detection of outliers\n\n### 1.6 Fit-for-purpose Evaluation\n\nGovernance reviews should evaluate whether the dataset supports the AI model's objective. Using data intended for one purpose for a different purpose should be approached with caution and subject to review and approval.\n\n> **Key insight:** AI Training ≠ General Use. A dataset for language translation may not be suitable for training a medical advice chatbot — even technically similar data can diverge in ethical implications and legal exposure.\n\n### 1.7 Data Stewardship and Roles\n\n| Role | Responsibility |\n|------|---------------|\n| **Data stewards** | Operational custodians ensuring data quality, documentation, and issue resolution; ensure governance policies are consistently applied throughout the data lifecycle |\n| **Data owners** | Accountable for strategic use of data; approve use cases, certify lawful use, and sign off on risk assessments; identified by domain or dataset |\n\n### 1.8 Security, Privacy, and Ethical Use\n\n#### Privacy Risk Assessments\n\nData governance programs should mandate **Privacy Impact Assessments (PIAs)** for all AI systems processing personal data. These evaluate risks of compliance, re-identification, inference, and discriminatory outcomes.\n\n#### Security Controls\n\n| Layer | Controls |\n|-------|----------|\n| **Data layer** | Encryption, access management, audit logging for all AI-related datasets (including training data) |\n| **Infrastructure layer** | Comprehensive controls to protect facilities, networks, systems, and DBMS from attack and misuse |\n\n#### Ethical Considerations\n\nAn ethical review addresses issues beyond policies, requirements, and regulations. While a particular use of data may be allowed and legal, it may still be unwise. Examples of ethical concerns:\n\n- Training data embedding racial, gender, or socioeconomic bias via proxy variables\n- Data collected under exploitative or disingenuous conditions\n- Data that invades privacy, even if technically anonymized or pseudonymized\n\n### 1.9 Organizational Maturity and Implementation\n\nData governance is a **lifecycle process**, not a one-time compliance task. Requires leadership support, institutional buy-in, and may precipitate cultural change.\n\n#### Maturity Model\n\n| Level | Characteristics |\n|-------|----------------|\n| **Ad hoc** | Minimal documentation or oversight |\n| **Emerging** | Some formal roles and basic policies |\n| **Defined** | Clear responsibilities and standardized procedures |\n| **Managed** | Monitoring, measurement, and risk-based decision-making |\n| **Optimized** | Continuous monitoring, automation, metrics, and risk-adjusted decision-making |\n\n### 1.10 Common Pitfalls and Failure Modes\n\n| Pitfall | Description |\n|---------|-------------|\n| **Treating governance as compliance** | Focusing solely on ticking boxes rather than enabling trustworthy data use |\n| **Overcentralization** | Excessively centralized structures creating bottlenecks and disintermediation |\n| **Tool overload** | Excessive governance tools overwhelming teams; simplicity and training are key |\n| **Shadow copies of training data** | Extracting production data for ad hoc offline filtering without recording the procedure — governance will be blind to this use |\n\n### 1.11 Benefits of Governance in AI Development\n\n- **Improved model performance** — clean, representative data leads to better outcomes\n- **Faster audits and investigations** — good documentation accelerates regulatory response\n- **Cross-team alignment** — clear roles reduce conflict and rework\n- **Reputational advantage** — demonstrated governance maturity builds stakeholder trust\n\n> Standards such as **ISO/IEC 38505-1** provide data governance frameworks that organizations can adopt.\n\n---", "t_lineage": "## At a Glance\n\nTraceability for data: provenance records where data originated (source, collection method, legal terms); lineage tracks its journey — every transformation, merge, filter, and feature-engineering step — until it reaches a model. Together they answer the audit and incident question: 'this model behaves badly — what fed it?'\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nDefinition-split questions (provenance = origin; lineage = journey) and application questions: lineage is what lets you trace a biased output back to a corrupted source, honor a deletion request, or prove training data was lawfully sourced.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Provenance: origin, collection method, date, legal basis/terms per source — the data's birth certificate\n- Lineage: the transformation chain from source through cleaning, joins, and features to training sets — the data's biography\n- Enables: audits, incident root-cause tracing, deletion-request compliance, reproducibility, and defensible provenance claims\n- Capture it automatically where possible — pipeline tooling and metadata catalogs beat retrospective archaeology\n- EU AI Act data-governance duties for high-risk systems effectively require lineage: origin, characteristics, and preparation must be documented\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Data Governance and Model Training\n\n## 2. Data Lineage and Provenance\n\n> To build trustworthy, responsible, and ethical AI, organizations need to understand where data comes from, how it moves, and how it changes.\n\n### 2.1 Defining Data Lineage and Data Provenance\n\n| Concept | Focus | Tracks |\n|---------|-------|--------|\n| **Data lineage** | Forward in time (\"how and where\") | How data flows through pipelines: ingestion → storage → transformation → consumption → maintenance → disposal. Includes: generation/origin, storage location, transformation processes, models/systems consuming it, modifications/corrections/removals, retention operations, disposal |\n| **Data provenance** | Backward to origin | Origin and original characteristics: where obtained, who created/collected it, under what conditions, intended purpose |\n\n> **Key distinction:** Lineage tracks flow and transformations; provenance identifies origin and context. Lineage ≠ provenance.\n\nTogether, lineage and provenance support:\n- Identifying sources of errors and bias\n- Reproducing outcomes (for explainable AI models)\n- Demonstrating compliance with regulations and legal obligations\n\n### 2.2 The Role of Lineage and Provenance in AI\n\n| Area | Importance |\n|------|------------|\n| **Explainability and trust** | When stakeholders question AI outputs, developers must trace input data; without lineage/provenance, explanations become speculative |\n| **Data security and integrity** | Lack of lineage can conceal data tampering, poisoning, and unauthorized modifications |\n| **Compliance and auditability** | Regulatory frameworks (e.g., EU AI Act) require traceability for both source data and its transformations |\n| **Reproducibility** | Clear understanding of data's journey and origin supports debugging, retraining, and litigation |\n\n### 2.3 Capturing Data Provenance\n\nEffective provenance tracking begins with **metadata** collection:\n\n- Source entity (owner, organization, system)\n- Collection method and date\n- Version information\n- Data licensing (terms of use)\n- Access rights\n- Intended use or original context\n- Lineage\n- Jurisdictional considerations (where collected, applicable regulations)\n- Context (intended use, assumptions, limitations)\n\n> **Third-party data:** Governance policies should require provenance declarations from data sources, proof of lawful collection and consent, and documentation of modifications/aggregations performed by the source.\n\n**Standards for provenance tracking:** W3C PROV, ISO/IEC 5181 (Artificial Intelligence — Data and AI system provenance, still in development).\n\n### 2.4 Tracking Data Lineage\n\nLineage records should include:\n- Data source and format\n- Storage location\n- Version history\n- Transformation scripts or ETL processes\n- Data pipeline paths\n- Systems and models using datasets\n- Data retention and disposal processes\n\n| Direction | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **Backward lineage** | Traces each data point back to its origin (sometimes confused with provenance) |\n| **Forward lineage** | Tracks how data elements flow through systems to influence downstream processes |\n\n**Tools:** Modern data observability platforms (Monte Carlo, Collibra, OpenLineage) offer visualization tools for graphical lineage representation, valuable when multiple teams interact with shared datasets.\n\n### 2.5 Common Failure Modes\n\n| Failure Mode | Description |\n|--------------|-------------|\n| **Data and organizational silos** | Datasets transferred between departments without clear ownership or standards; lineage and provenance become unclear |\n| **Informal data use** | **Shadow AI** development — data scientists implement models and manipulate datasets outside sanctioned pipelines |\n| **Manual and fragmented processes** | Documentation scattered across multiple tools or manual processes, often overlooked |\n\n### 2.6 Building a Provenance and Lineage Framework\n\n#### Policy Requirements\n\nPolicies should define:\n- Mandatory metadata fields for new datasets\n- When and how provenance must be verified and approved\n- Ownership responsibilities for provenance and lineage documentation\n- Requirements for automated capture and audit trails\n- Required granularity of tracking (dataset-level, record-level, or feature-level)\n\n#### Tech Stack Components\n\n| Component | Examples |\n|-----------|----------|\n| Metadata repositories | Data catalogs |\n| Data lineage visualizers | Graphical pipeline views |\n| ETL tools | Integrated tracking and logging |\n| Version control systems | For datasets and transformation scripts |\n| Security controls | Encryption, access management to protect lineage/provenance records |\n\n**MLOps integration:** Tools like MLflow, DVC (Data Version Control), or Amazon SageMaker enable traceability from raw data through training to deployment. **Model cards** can include references to lineage and provenance data for stakeholder transparency.\n\n### 2.7 Use Cases and Industry Examples\n\n| Industry | Requirements |\n|----------|-------------|\n| **Financial services** | Clear lineage for credit scoring, fraud detection, and algorithmic trading data |\n| **Healthcare** | HIPAA compliance requires all health-related AI to trace data back to validated sources |\n| **Public sector** | Auditability of data sources ensures democratic accountability (criminal justice, welfare automation) |\n\n### 2.8 Benefits of Strong Traceability\n\n- Reduced debugging time and faster model issue resolution\n- Greater confidence in model outputs for stakeholders\n- Improved audit response and regulatory posture\n- Higher data quality with fewer data-related issues\n\n### 2.9 Traceability Challenges and Tradeoffs\n\n| Challenge | Consideration |\n|-----------|---------------|\n| **Performance overhead** | Automated lineage tracking can add latency to data pipelines and increase storage requirements |\n| **Third-party limitations** | Third-party or open-source models may not provide full provenance/lineage (intellectual property concerns) |\n| **Infrastructure investment** | Organizations must determine level of investment for metadata management, auditing, and visualization |\n\n---", "t_testing": "## At a Glance\n\nThe verification battery for AI: unit and component tests, integration tests (models inside real systems), validation against requirements on held-out data, performance testing (accuracy, latency, load), security testing (poisoning, inversion, adversarial inputs), plus regression testing after every change. And the meta-lesson: passing tests reduces risk but never eliminates it — coverage gaps, benchmark gaming, and distribution shift survive green dashboards.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nMatch the failure to the test type that catches it, keep train/validation/test splits straight, and remember integration testing exists because a fair model inside a badly designed system still produces unfair outcomes.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Ladder: unit → integration → validation → performance → security → fairness → explainability → human-in-the-loop/scenario → UAT\n- Holdout discipline: train, validation, and test sets stay separate — leakage between them invalidates every metric\n- Security testing targets ML-specific attacks: data poisoning, model inversion, membership inference, adversarial examples, prompt injection\n- Regression testing after updates: improvements must not silently degrade accuracy, fairness, or safety elsewhere\n- Test coverage means scenarios, edge cases, and demographic slices — overall accuracy can hide subgroup failure\n- 'It passed testing' starts the risk conversation, doesn't end it: residual risks are identified, documented, and monitored\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Data Governance and Model Training\n\n## 3. AI System Testing\n\n> Unlike traditional software testing (verifying expected outputs for given inputs), AI system testing encompasses statistical performance, fairness, security, explainability, and integration within larger systems.\n\n### 3.1 Understanding AI Testing Objectives\n\nAI testing differs from traditional application testing because it involves **stochastic** behavior, data dependency, and shifting performance boundaries. AI system testing must address:\n\n- Variability in model output\n- Performance on unseen or edge cases\n- Tradeoffs between performance and interpretability\n- Compliance with evolving regulatory standards\n\n#### Testing Throughout the AI Lifecycle\n\n| Phase | Focus |\n|-------|-------|\n| **Training** | Monitor convergence and behavior |\n| **Validation** | Verify outputs and decisions |\n| **Pre-deployment** | Stress-test against adversarial or unexpected inputs |\n| **Post-deployment** | Detect data drift or performance decay |\n\n### 3.2 Unit and Component Testing\n\nValidates functionality of individual components: data processing logic and model functions.\n\n| Area | What to Test |\n|------|-------------|\n| **Input data transformations** | Normalization, encoding, imputation — correctness and consistency |\n| **Model wrappers, scoring functions, evaluation logic** | Synthetic inputs for edge cases (null values, extreme values, malformed data) |\n\n> Unit tests can be integrated into **Continuous Integration (CI)** tooling using frameworks like PyTest or unittest in Python.\n\n### 3.3 Integration Testing\n\nValidates interactions between components: data ingestion, model inference, storage, and downstream systems.\n\n- Scrub input data to ensure conformity to expected formats, values, and character sets\n- Verify APIs and interfaces function under load\n- Identify type mismatches (e.g., numeric vs. string age values)\n- Confirm model predictions are correctly handled by applications, dashboards, or decision systems\n\n> **Trust but verify:** Integration tests should simulate full-system, end-to-end workflows including failure conditions (dropped records, unexpected input, unavailable services).\n\n### 3.4 Validation Testing\n\nConfirms whether the AI system performs as intended in **real-world applications** — goes beyond technical correctness to compare outputs against business expectations, ethical requirements, and compliance standards.\n\n> Validation testing confirms whether the AI system solves the stated business problem(s). Success criteria should be defined before testing begins.\n\n**Key metrics:** Accuracy, precision, recall, F1 score.\n\n### 3.5 Performance Testing\n\nEnsures the AI system meets speed, scalability, and availability targets.\n\n| Method | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| **Inference latency** | Time to return a prediction from an input; real-time systems require low-latency inference |\n| **Throughput and scalability** | How many inputs the system can handle per unit time; tools: LoadRunner, JMeter, Locust |\n| **Resource utilization** | CPU, GPU, memory, storage, and network requirements; average load and peak load tests |\n\n> **Test for the worst, not the average:** A model averaging 50 ms inference time but spiking to 2 s under certain loads can still degrade user experience.\n\n### 3.6 Security Testing\n\nIn addition to conventional vulnerabilities, AI models face unique threats.\n\n#### AI-Specific Testing\n\n| Test Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **Adversarial testing** | Inject inputs crafted to cause incorrect predictions (e.g., subtle noise causing misclassification) |\n| **Data poisoning and backdoors** | Assess training data integrity to detect malicious inputs influencing model behavior |\n| **Model extraction and inversion** | Test whether attackers can reconstruct training data or reverse-engineer model behavior via repeated querying |\n\n#### Comprehensive Security Testing\n\n- Vulnerability scans (application, system, network levels)\n- Penetration tests (application, system, network levels)\n- Supply chain security testing for third-party models and dependencies\n- Physical penetration tests for on-premises systems\n- Social engineering testing for workforce resilience\n\n### 3.7 Bias and Fairness Testing\n\n| Test Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **Performance parity** | Measure how performance metrics (accuracy, false positive rate) vary across demographic groups; identify unjustified disparities from proxy bias |\n| **Counterfactual fairness** | Evaluate if changing a protected attribute (gender, religious affiliation, political affiliation) inappropriately changes model output |\n\n**Tools:** AIF360, Fairlearn, What-If Tool (TensorFlow).\n\n> **Legal exposure:** In many jurisdictions, biased algorithms are not only unethical but also illegal. Fairness testing helps avoid regulatory fines and public backlash.\n\n### 3.8 Interpretability and Explainability Testing\n\nDetermines whether AI model behavior can be meaningfully understood and explained.\n\n| Method | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| **Model-agnostic methods** | Tools like SHAP or LIME explain individual predictions, identifying spurious correlations or data artifacts |\n| **Global interpretability** | Summarize what the model generally values — feature importance analysis, decision trees, or coefficient inspection (linear models) |\n| **Simulatability** | Whether a human can replicate or understand model predictions; simpler models (logistic regression, rule-based systems) favored in high-risk use cases |\n\n### 3.9 Human-in-the-loop and Scenario Testing\n\n| Test Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **End-to-end user flows** | Evaluate system behavior interacting with real users; surface unexpected results due to UX design, miscommunication, or edge cases |\n| **Interactive testing and feedback loops** | For recommendation/decision-support systems — user response may influence future behavior; account for long-term dynamics and feedback effects |\n\n### 3.10 Documentation and Traceability\n\nAll testing should be documented (procedures, plans, results) to support transparency, auditability, and reproducibility.\n\n#### Test Plan Should Define\n\n- Objectives\n- Scope and responsibilities\n- Test scenarios\n- Metrics and thresholds\n- Pass/fail criteria\n\n#### Test Results Should Include\n\n- Test results\n- Issues encountered\n- Remediation steps identified\n- After-action review\n- References to datasets, code versions, and environments used\n\n> **Model cards and fact sheets** summarize model behavior, training/testing datasets, performance, intended use cases, and known limitations.\n\n### 3.11 Organizational Practices for AI Testing\n\n- **Clearly designate testing responsibilities** — in regulated sectors, may involve third-party validation or separate risk teams\n- **Establish formal review gates** — testing evidence evaluated before model proceeds to deployment\n- **Foster a testing culture** — testing as a key quality and risk control mechanism; incentives for issue discovery and transparency\n\n### 3.12 Challenges and Tradeoffs\n\n| Challenge | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **No definitive \"correct\" answer** | In some use cases, validation is inherently subjective and fuzzy |\n| **Conflicting fairness definitions** | Equal opportunity vs. equal outcomes — organizations must decide which principles to prioritize |\n| **Explainability vs. performance** | High-performing models (deep neural networks) may be less explainable, complicating integrity and accuracy testing |\n| **Probabilistic nature** | Real-world complexity makes it hard to design tests addressing specific concerns |\n\n---\n\n---\n\n> [!source] Data Governance and Model Training\n\n## 4. Identifying and Managing Testing Risks\n\n> Testing is not just about verifying functionality — it's about identifying risks that emerge during training and validation. AI models' behavior is **learned, not explicitly programmed**, making testing more probabilistic and less deterministic.\n\n### 4.1 Why \"It Passed Testing\" Isn't Enough\n\nAn AI model may perform well on a validation set but poorly in the real world due to:\n- Dataset shifts\n- New edge cases\n- Emerging adversarial techniques\n\n> AI system testing must extend beyond accuracy scores to consider emerging and broadening risk dimensions.\n\n### 4.2 Types of Testing Risks in AI\n\n#### Data-Related Risks\n\n| Risk | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| **Biased training data** | Uneven representation leads to unfair or inaccurate outcomes |\n| **Label noise** | Inconsistent or incorrect labels reduce model reliability |\n| **Outliers and anomalies** | Extreme values or rare cases distort training or cause unstable behavior |\n| **Imbalanced datasets** | One class/group underrepresented, leading to skewed predictions |\n| **Concept drift** | Training data becomes outdated or misaligned with deployment conditions |\n\n#### Model-Related Risks\n\n| Risk | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| **Overfitting** | Model learns noise in training set rather than general patterns |\n| **Underfitting** | Model too simple to capture real-world complexities |\n| **Lack of robustness** | Poor performance on edge cases or minor input variations |\n| **Poor calibration** | Confidence scores don't match actual probabilities (e.g., \"90% confident\" but only 60% correct) |\n\n#### Evaluation Risks\n\n| Risk | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| **Inappropriate metrics** | Wrong metrics selected make test results hard to interpret accurately |\n| **Test set leakage** | Overlap between training and test data leads to inflated performance |\n| **Insufficient coverage** | Test data may not include critical subgroups or production use cases |\n\n#### Deployment and Integration Risks\n\n| Risk | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| **Environment mismatch** | Training assumptions don't hold in production (data, OS, software differences) |\n| **Shadow AI use** | Unvetted models deployed without formal testing, documentation, or approval |\n| **Feedback loops** | Model predictions influence future data collection, reinforcing biases |\n| **Security and access differences** | Production environments introduce new attack surfaces (e.g., exposed APIs) not present in testing |\n\n### 4.3 Testing Risk Identification Techniques\n\n| Technique | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)** | Uncovers data distribution issues, imbalances, missing values, and potential outliers — often the first step before training begins |\n| **Fairness and bias audit** | Examines performance across subgroups (gender, age, race); differential performance may reveal systemic bias |\n| **Error analysis** | Manual reviews of misclassified output reveal systematic patterns of failure — beneficial for high-impact applications (healthcare, criminal justice) |\n\n### 4.4 Risk Mitigation Strategies\n\n#### Improving Data Quality and Diversity\n\n| Technique | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **Data augmentation** | Generate additional training data to fill gaps |\n| **Resampling** | Balance class distributions using upsampling or downsampling |\n| **Synthetic data** | Use carefully constructed artificial data to improve representativeness (ensure it cannot be reidentified as real data subjects) |\n\n#### Regularization Techniques (for Overfitting)\n\n| Technique | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **L1 regularization** | Adds penalty based on absolute value of model parameters, simplifying the model |\n| **L2 regularization** | Adds penalty based on square of model parameters, promoting stability |\n| **Dropout** | Similar technique used in neural networks |\n\n#### Ensemble Approaches\n\n- **Ensemble models** or **model stacking** can mitigate weaknesses of any single approach and improve overall performance and resilience.\n\n> **Accuracy isn't always enough:** In a medical diagnostic system, 95% accuracy may sound impressive, but if disease prevalence is only 5%, the model may be classifying almost every case as \"healthy.\" Precision and recall matter.\n\n### 4.5 Managing Risk Throughout the Training Lifecycle\n\n| Phase | Activities |\n|-------|------------|\n| **Early Training** | Monitor convergence behavior; detect vanishing gradients or poor learning rates; validate model architecture decisions |\n| **Mid-training Monitoring** | Validate loss curves; track performance metrics on validation sets; watch for instability or sudden metric spikes |\n| **Late-stage Tuning** | Final hyperparameter optimization; large-scale validation sets; scenario-based or adversarial tests |\n| **Post-training Testing** | Real-world simulations where possible; evaluate explainability and counterfactual behavior; audit logs and documentation |\n\n> **Regression testing:** Whenever there is any change in operating parameters or training data, AI systems should be regression tested to verify behavior within acceptable bounds.\n\n### 4.6 Testing Tools and Infrastructure\n\n| Category | Tools |\n|----------|-------|\n| **MLOps integrations** | MLflow, SageMaker, Vertex AI — manage training, testing, and deployment with auditability |\n| **Testing platforms** | Great Expectations (GX) — data validation; Checklist — testing across task dimensions; TensorFlow Model Analysis (TFMA) — performance evaluation |\n| **Production monitoring** | Arize, Fiddler — post-deployment testing comparing real-world results to expected outcomes |\n\n> Post-deployment monitoring is an extension of testing; it identifies new risks and feeds them back into the model improvement cycle.\n\n### 4.7 Common Testing Pitfalls\n\n| Pitfall | Description |\n|---------|-------------|\n| **Overreliance on test accuracy** | Accuracy alone masks false positives, false negatives, and unfair subgroup treatment |\n| **Lack of real-world scenarios** | Synthetic tests alone are insufficient; edge cases and environmental variability must be included |\n| **Siloed responsibility** | Data scientists testing their own models without outside review — bias and blind spots go unchecked |\n| **Metric misalignment with business goals** | Model optimizes for technical metrics (F1 score) but fails to deliver business value |\n| **Neglecting explainability** | High performance is meaningless if stakeholders cannot understand or trust model behavior |\n\n### 4.8 Case Studies\n\n| Case | Issue | Resolution |\n|------|-------|------------|\n| **Hiring algorithm bias** | Model favored masculine-coded language via biased word embeddings | Retrained with debiased embeddings; updated evaluation metrics |\n| **Autonomous vehicle simulation** | Poor performance in tunnels (scenario missing from test set) | Built synthetic tunnel-driving simulator; improved edge case handling |\n| **Financial services outlier** | Fraud detection triggered false alarms on large legitimate transactions | High-volume data underrepresented in training; updated with stratified sampling |\n\n---", "t_bias": "## At a Glance\n\nThe fairness discipline: where bias enters (unrepresentative or historically skewed data, proxy features, labeling choices, feedback loops), how it's measured (demographic parity, equalized odds, equal opportunity, predictive parity, disparate-impact ratios), the hard truth that fairness metrics mathematically conflict, and the mitigation toolbox across pre-, in-, and post-processing.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nKnow the metric names and that they can't all be satisfied at once — the governance answer is choosing metrics appropriate to context, documenting trade-offs, and testing across subgroups. 'Remove the protected attribute' is the classic wrong answer.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Bias sources: unrepresentative training data, historical discrimination encoded in labels, proxy variables, feedback loops, measurement bias\n- Metric menu: demographic parity (equal positive rates), equalized odds (equal error rates), equal opportunity (equal true-positive rates), predictive parity, disparate-impact ratio\n- Impossibility results: several fairness metrics cannot hold simultaneously except in degenerate cases — trade-offs are inherent\n- Mitigations: pre-processing (rebalance, reweight), in-processing (constraints during training), post-processing (threshold adjustment by group)\n- NIST SP 1270's three bias categories: systemic, computational/statistical, and human-cognitive — bias is not only in the data\n- Fairness work needs sensitive attributes for testing — governed access, not deletion, is the mature practice\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Data Governance and Model Training\n\n### 3.7 Bias and Fairness Testing\n\n| Test Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **Performance parity** | Measure how performance metrics (accuracy, false positive rate) vary across demographic groups; identify unjustified disparities from proxy bias |\n| **Counterfactual fairness** | Evaluate if changing a protected attribute (gender, religious affiliation, political affiliation) inappropriately changes model output |\n\n**Tools:** AIF360, Fairlearn, What-If Tool (TensorFlow).\n\n> **Legal exposure:** In many jurisdictions, biased algorithms are not only unethical but also illegal. Fairness testing helps avoid regulatory fines and public backlash.\n\n---\n\n> [!source] Sectoral and Civil Laws\n\n#### Sources of Algorithmic Bias\n\n| Source | Example |\n|--------|---------|\n| Biased training data | Historical decisions reflecting systemic discrimination |\n| Proxy variables | Postal codes correlating with race |\n| Opaque models | Black-box models masking bias |\n| Feedback loops | Reinforcing existing inequality |\n| Sampling bias | Under/overrepresentation of certain groups |\n\n---\n\n> [!source] Privacy and Data Protection Law\n\n### Hidden Bias in AI Training Sets\n\nMany datasets contain biased historical decisions even if sensitive attributes were excluded. Mitigation techniques:\n- **Fairness-aware learning**\n- **Counterfactual evaluation**\n- **Sensitive attribute testing**\n\n---\n\n> [!source] NIST AI RMF — Structured Reference\n\n### The Three Categories of AI Bias (NIST SP 1270)\n\n| Bias Type | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **Systemic** | Present in datasets, organizational norms, practices, processes, and broader societal structures |\n| **Computational & Statistical** | Systematic errors from non-representative samples or algorithmic processes |\n| **Human-Cognitive** | How individuals/groups perceive AI information, make decisions, fill in missing information |\n\n---", "t_train": "## At a Glance\n\nGetting the learning right: training data must be sufficient in volume and representative of the deployment population; overfitting (memorizing noise) and underfitting (missing signal) are the twin failure modes, managed through validation splits and regularization; labeling and ground truth quality bound everything; and the training lifecycle — including fine-tuning and retraining — carries its own risks to manage.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nOverfitting vs. underfitting is a guaranteed concept: overfit = excellent training scores, poor real-world performance. Representative-data scenarios (a US-trained model deployed in Asia) test the deployment-population principle.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Overfitting: model learns training noise — high train accuracy, poor generalization; regularization, more data, and early stopping counter it\n- Underfitting: model too simple to capture real patterns — poor everywhere; more capacity or features counter it\n- Representativeness beats raw volume: data must mirror the population and conditions of actual deployment\n- Ground truth quality caps model quality: labeling standards, inter-annotator agreement, and audit of labels matter\n- Fine-tuning adapts pretrained models with domain data — inheriting the base model's biases and licensing constraints\n- Manage risk across the training lifecycle: dataset versioning, experiment tracking, and documented retraining triggers\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Data Governance and Model Training\n\n### 1.5 Ensuring Sufficient and Representative Data\n\n| Concept | Definition |\n|---------|------------|\n| **Data sufficiency** | Having enough volume of data to train a model effectively |\n| **Representativeness** | Ensuring data accurately captures the relevant diversity of the deployment environment |\n\n#### Avoiding Underfitting and Overfitting\n\n| Problem | Cause | Result |\n|---------|-------|--------|\n| **Underfitting** | Insufficient volume of training data | Model unable to generalize effectively |\n| **Overfitting** | Too much narrow or repetitive data | Model learns irrelevant patterns |\n\n> Governance helps find the right balance by defining sufficiency thresholds and reuse limits.\n\n#### Representing Real-world Populations\n\nTo avoid bias, AI governance frameworks should incorporate fairness checks:\n- Demographic audits\n- Data distribution checks (e.g., geographical distribution)\n- Detection of outliers\n\n---\n\n> [!source] Data Governance and Model Training\n\n#### Regularization Techniques (for Overfitting)\n\n| Technique | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **L1 regularization** | Adds penalty based on absolute value of model parameters, simplifying the model |\n| **L2 regularization** | Adds penalty based on square of model parameters, promoting stability |\n| **Dropout** | Similar technique used in neural networks |\n\n---\n\n> [!source] Data Governance and Model Training\n\n### 4.5 Managing Risk Throughout the Training Lifecycle\n\n| Phase | Activities |\n|-------|------------|\n| **Early Training** | Monitor convergence behavior; detect vanishing gradients or poor learning rates; validate model architecture decisions |\n| **Mid-training Monitoring** | Validate loss curves; track performance metrics on validation sets; watch for instability or sudden metric spikes |\n| **Late-stage Tuning** | Final hyperparameter optimization; large-scale validation sets; scenario-based or adversarial tests |\n| **Post-training Testing** | Real-world simulations where possible; evaluate explainability and counterfactual behavior; audit logs and documentation |\n\n> **Regression testing:** Whenever there is any change in operating parameters or training data, AI systems should be regression tested to verify behavior within acceptable bounds.\n\n---\n\n> [!source] AI System Design\n\n### 1.3 Documenting the Training and Testing Process\n\nOnce architecture is finalized and approved, model training, validation, and testing begin — critical stages for refining performance, detecting bias, and validating outputs.\n\n#### Training Documentation\n\n**Training documentation should include:**\n\n| Element | Details |\n|---------|---------|\n| **Training data sources and characteristics** | All data sources, exclusions, embellishments, filtering, populations represented; data should be archived and immutable |\n| **Labeling process and quality assurance** | How labels were assigned and verified (for supervised learning) |\n| **Model training configuration** | Hyperparameters, batch size, epoch count, optimization strategy, evaluation metrics and results |\n| **Model performance benchmarks** | Baseline performance, ongoing metrics, information supporting selected thresholds |\n| **Bias detection and mitigation** | Fairness assessments, de-biasing techniques, tradeoffs |\n| **Testing outcomes** | Robustness, generalization, privacy/security test results |\n\n> **Regulatory Expectations for Training Logs:** Under the EU AI Act and other frameworks, training documentation should support: comprehensive documentation sufficient to reproduce the design and development effort, explanation of design rationale, long-term auditability, and evidence of risk mitigation.\n\n#### Testing and Validation Documentation\n\nTesting reveals a model's reliability and safety. Testing documentation should include:\n\n| Element | Details |\n|---------|---------|\n| **Testing methodology** | Types of tests run (unit, integration, performance, adversarial, etc.); NIST ARIA guidance (see [[AI Standards and Frameworks]]) |\n| **Test data description** | Whether test data was withheld from training set; whether it represents real-world conditions |\n| **Test results** | Accuracy, false positives/negatives, precision/recall, other metrics |\n| **Stress testing and edge case analysis** | Boundary conditions, adversarial examples, inputs/outputs outside expected norms |\n| **Failure modes and responses** | Known weaknesses, system behavior upon failure |\n| **User testing and feedback** | How usability and human-AI interaction were evaluated |\n\n> **Testing for Trust:** Trustworthy AI requires more than high accuracy. Testing should answer: Does the system fail gracefully? Can outputs be explained or justified? Are error patterns acceptable under ethical and legal standards?", "ch10": "## At a Glance\n\nBOK III.C — governing release, monitoring, and maintenance: pre-release readiness (model cards complete, conformity requirements met, stress and adversarial testing passed), controlled release strategies (canary, phased, shadow), and the continuous monitoring and maintenance regime that watches performance, drift, fairness, and security once real users and real data arrive.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nThe heaviest Domain III subdomain at 8–10 questions. Deployment-strategy vocabulary is tested directly (canary vs. phased vs. shadow), and monitoring scenarios are a staple: accuracy degrades, drift is detected — the right answer investigates root causes and follows the escalation path, never 'keep running as-is.'\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Pre-release readiness: model cards, conformity requirements, formal risk/impact reviews signed off before go-live\n- Release strategies manage blast radius: canary (small % first), phased rollout (expanding cohorts), shadow mode (parallel, non-authoritative), A/B\n- Data drift = input distribution shifts; concept drift = input→output relationship shifts; monitoring must catch both\n- Maintenance runs on a schedule: retraining windows, dependency patching, periodic performance/reliability/safety assessments\n- Public disclosures belong here too: technical documentation, deployment instructions, post-market monitoring evidence\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Deployment and Monitoring\n\n## Performance Indicators\n\n- **Assess readiness and prepare for release into production** (e.g., creating the model card and satisfying conformity requirements)\n- **Understand the differences in AI deployment options** (e.g., cloud vs. on-premise vs. edge; using the AI model as-is or with fine-tuning, RAG, **agentic architectures**, or other techniques — see [[Agentic AI Governance]])\n- **Apply policies, procedures, best practices, and ethical considerations** to the deployment of an AI system (e.g., data governance, risk management, issue management, user training)\n- **Conduct continuous monitoring** of the AI model and system and establish a regular schedule for maintenance, updates, and retraining\n\n---\n\n---\n\n> [!source] Deployment and Monitoring\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n1. **Model readiness requires more than accuracy — it requires documentation, governance, and conformity.** Before deployment, AI systems must meet both technical and governance criteria, including model cards, risk assessments, and documentation of limitations and intended use. Under the EU AI Act, formal conformity assessments are required for high-risk systems.\n\n2. **Deployment must align with organizational policies, legal obligations, and operational safeguards.** Deployment is the intersection of technical execution with legal and ethical responsibilities. Organizations must employ version control, enforce environment separation, and ensure data governance policies are followed. Deployments should be auditable, reversible, and compliant with cross-border data regulations and consent requirements.\n\n3. **Risk management in production includes monitoring for drift, fairness degradation, and emerging threats.** Once deployed, AI systems are exposed to real-world data that can deviate from training. Continuous monitoring helps detect input drift, bias, hallucinations, and attacks — supported by alerting tools and defined incident response procedures.\n\n4. **Retraining and maintenance must be scheduled based on system volatility and risk exposure.** AI models are not static; retraining frequency depends on how rapidly the operating environment changes and the criticality of the model's outputs. High-risk/high-volume systems may require monthly retraining with human validation.\n\n5. **Monitoring is both a technical function and a governance responsibility.** Effective monitoring includes logging, alerting, fairness analysis, human oversight, incidents, and regulations. Outputs feed into compliance efforts, risk registers, and responsible AI programs. Monitoring provides critical evidence for audits, transparency reporting, and ethical assurance.\n\n---", "t_readiness": "## At a Glance\n\nThe gate before production: verifying a system is production-worthy across documentation (model cards complete), legal conformity (assessments done, licenses clean), operational resilience (stress testing, failover, latency under load), adversarial robustness (red-teaming), and formal governance signoff (impact and risk reviews, go/no-go decision with named approvers).\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nChecklist-style scenarios: what must be true before deployment? Strong answers combine technical readiness (stress and adversarial testing) with governance readiness (documented approvals, conformity, rollback plan) — either alone is incomplete.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Readiness evaluation spans safety, accountability, and suitability for the deployment context — not just accuracy\n- Stress testing: expected and edge-case workloads, latency/throughput benchmarks, resource limits, failover behavior\n- Red-teaming: deliberate adversarial attempts to break the system — prompts, inputs, and misuse scenarios — before attackers and users do\n- Legal/regulatory gate: conformity assessments, data provenance and licensing verification, insurance-driven requirements\n- Formal reviews with named approvers and documented go/no-go criteria — deployment is a governed decision with an audit trail\n- A rollback plan is part of readiness: know how to retreat before you advance\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Deployment and Monitoring\n\n## 1. Pre-release Readiness\n\nPreparing an AI system for production deployment requires structured documentation, regulatory conformity, performance benchmarking, architectural decisions, and operational planning. Organizations transition from experimentation to execution, ensuring the model is ***production-worthy, well-governed, and responsibly and ethically designed***.\n\n> **Note:** It is assumed that organizations have mature IT service management practices, including *release management*. This area focuses on AI-specific considerations as an overlay to standard release management.\n\n### 1.1 Assessing AI System Readiness for Production\n\nBefore an AI system can be promoted into production, organizations should undertake a readiness evaluation covering safety, accountability, and suitability for its intended context.\n\n**[[Model Cards]]** — structured documentation about the AI model's intended use, training data, evaluation metrics, known limitations, and ethical considerations. Model cards encourage development teams to think critically about assumptions, limitations, and real-world performance. They align with frameworks such as NIST AI RMF and ISO/IEC 42001.\n\n#### Legal and Regulatory Obligations (Pre-Release)\n\n| Obligation | Description |\n|------------|-------------|\n| **Conformity assessments** | Required under the EU AI Act for high-risk systems; includes documented quality controls, risk assessments, and human oversight provisions |\n| **Third-party audits or internal validation** | Internal governance boards may require internal or external validation before approving production use |\n| **Data provenance and licensing compliance** | All training and fine-tuning datasets must be lawfully sourced, appropriately licensed, and compliant with privacy regulations (e.g., GDPR) |\n| **Insurance-imposed requirements** | Cyber insurance policies may require change management, code reviews, gate reviews, and periodic risk assessments |\n\n> **Exam note:** AI governance professionals must be familiar with regional and sector-specific requirements, especially for systems that interact with the public, make consequential decisions, or use personal data.\n\n### 1.2 Operational Readiness and Risk Evaluation\n\nModels should undergo **stress testing** under expected and edge case workloads:\n\n- **Latency and throughput benchmarks** (especially for real-time use cases)\n- **Resource (memory, compute) usage** under load\n- **Failover behavior** during outages or degraded performance\n- **Evaluation against adversarial and malicious prompts**\n\n#### Formal Reviews Required Before Deployment\n\n- **[[AI Impact Assessments (AIIAs)]]** — Evaluate social, ethical, and operational impacts (covered in [[AI Impact Assessments and Auditing|AI System Design]])\n- **[[Risk Assessments]]** — Articulate potential harm events (e.g., discriminatory outcomes, misinformation propagation) and define corresponding mitigation strategies (covered in [[AI Risk Management|AI Standards and Frameworks]] and [[AI Impact Assessments and Auditing|AI System Design]])\n\n### 1.3 AI Model Deployment Considerations\n\nDeployment model decisions influence performance, latency, control, and regulatory posture.\n\n| Deployment Mode | Pros | Cons |\n|-----------------|------|------|\n| **Cloud** | Scalable, cost-effective, easy to integrate with APIs | Less control over data locality, possible regulatory concerns |\n| **On-premises** | High control, good for sensitive data, meets strict compliance | Costly infrastructure, lower scalability |\n| **Edge** | Ultra-low latency, no dependency on network connection, user privacy | Limited compute power, model size constraints |\n| **Hybrid** | Balances performance and compliance (edge/on-prem inference + cloud training/updates) | Added architectural complexity, higher integration overhead |\n\n- **Containerization** (Kubernetes, Docker) enables consistency across environments, auto-scaling, and deployment orchestration in multi-cloud or hybrid environments.\n- The deployment model should be decided in the **design phase** of the AI system project.\n\n### 1.4 AI Model Optimization for Production Fit\n\nOrganizations must decide whether to deploy the model in its original state, fine-tune it, or augment it.\n\n| Approach | Description | Use Case Example |\n|----------|-------------|------------------|\n| **As-is** | Use base model without modification | General-purpose chatbot or summarization tool |\n| **Fine-tuned** | Adapt model to domain-specific data | Legal document classifier for contract terms |\n| **Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG)** | Use external knowledge base during inference | Search assistant that cites internal policy documents |\n\n- **Fine-tuning** enhances domain performance but may increase maintenance costs and retraining requirements.\n- **RAG** reduces hallucinations and improves factual grounding, especially in enterprise settings.\n- **Quantization** (reducing precision from 32-bit to 8-bit) and **distillation** (training a smaller \"student\" model) reduce size and latency, sometimes at the cost of modest accuracy reductions.\n\n### 1.5 Human-in-the-Loop and Release Protocols\n\n#### Human Oversight Models\n\n| Model | Description | Example |\n|-------|-------------|---------|\n| **Human-in-the-loop (HITL)** | Human must approve or confirm some or all model decisions | Loan applications, medical diagnoses |\n| **Human-on-the-loop (HOTL)** | Human monitors outputs and can intervene if needed | Content moderation systems |\n| **Human-in-command** | Strategic decisions remain in human hands | Incident response, system deactivation, escalation protocols |\n\n#### Essential Release Practices\n\n- Release procedure, verification procedure, and **rollback procedure** submitted through **change management** for review and approval\n- **Staged rollouts** with feature flags\n- **Canary deployments** for performance testing\n- Monitoring for KPIs and leading risk indicators\n- Clear communication channels for status reporting and issue escalation\n\n### 1.6 Final Gate Reviews and Stakeholder Signoff\n\nBefore release, the system passes through final gate reviews conducted by a multidisciplinary governance team. These reviews ensure:\n\n- All documentation (model cards, design artifacts, audit logs, licensing records, test reports) is complete\n- Risk mitigations are implemented, or deferrals are approved\n- Security hardening and incident response procedures are validated\n- Operational readiness and monitoring plans are in place\n- Regulatory and policy compliance is verified\n- Version control and traceability of models, data, and configurations are maintained\n- All users and operations personnel are trained\n\n#### Example Gate Review Roles\n\n| Role | Responsibility |\n|------|----------------|\n| **Legal** | Data use, licensing, and contracts |\n| **Engineering** | Technical validation and scalability |\n| **Business** | Value alignment and readiness for customers |\n| **Cybersecurity** | Vulnerabilities and risks addressed |\n| **Privacy** | Data use review |\n| **Governance** | Fairness, accountability, and transparency |\n\n---", "t_deployment": "## At a Glance\n\nHow AI reaches users deliberately: strategy choices that manage blast radius — canary releases (small percentage first), phased rollouts (expanding cohorts with checkpoints), shadow mode (running non-authoritatively alongside the incumbent), A/B testing, blue-green switches — plus environment decisions (cloud, on-prem, edge) and the change-management work that readies people and processes.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nVocabulary questions on the strategy names, and scenario questions choosing a strategy for a risk profile: high-stakes system → shadow mode or small canary with defined success gates before expansion.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Canary: release to a small user percentage, monitor, expand — early detection with limited impact\n- Phased rollout: staged expansion across users/regions/use cases with go/no-go checkpoints per phase\n- Shadow mode: model runs in parallel without affecting decisions — validates real-world behavior at zero user risk\n- A/B deployment compares variants on live traffic; blue-green keeps an instant-rollback environment warm\n- Deployment includes people: user training, workflow changes, communication, and support readiness (change management)\n- Rollback criteria defined in advance: what metric excursion or incident triggers retreat, decided before launch\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Deployment and Monitoring\n\n## 2. Deployment Considerations\n\nDeployment is not only a technical milestone but also a **governance transition** — shifting responsibility from the development team to operational stakeholders.\n\n### 2.1 Enforcing Deployment Policies and Procedures\n\nKey components of an effective deployment policy:\n\n- **Approval gates:** Deployment contingent on prior signoffs (compliance, legal, security)\n- **Strict release procedures:** Developers should not have admin access to production; hand off packaged AI system to operations personnel\n- **Environment controls:** Mandatory isolation between development, staging, and production environments\n- **Rollback procedures:** Each deployment plan includes verification procedures, rollback steps, escalation contacts, and fail-safe procedures\n\n#### Deployment Must Be Traceable, Repeatable, and Reversible\n\n- **Version control** (Git, CI/CD pipelines, automated logging) ensures every change is logged, tracked, auditable, and reversible\n- **Model registries** track versions, training data lineage, metadata, deployment status, ownership, and contact info — supporting reproducibility and governance\n\n### 2.2 Data Governance in the Deployment Phase\n\nAI models are deployed alongside application pipelines or data feeds feeding them real-time data. Key activities:\n\n| Activity | Description |\n|----------|-------------|\n| **Data minimization** | Limit data collection to only what is necessary |\n| **Purpose limitation** | Use data only for formally defined business purposes |\n| **Retention policies** | Define storage duration for datasets, particularly user/behavioral data |\n| **Access controls** | Fine-grained access permissions for real-time data inputs |\n| **Data quality monitoring** | Validate inputs and detect anomalies to prevent cascading errors |\n\n> **Legal triggers:** Deployment frequently triggers cross-border data flow obligations (GDPR, LGPD), consent, and notice requirements.\n\n### 2.3 Risk Management During Deployment\n\nDeployment introduces new risks beyond model performance:\n\n| Risk Category | Examples |\n|---------------|----------|\n| **Infrastructure risks** | Network failures, cloud outages, resource exhaustion |\n| **Security risks** | Prompt injection, model inversion, data exfiltration, denial-of-service (DoS) |\n| **Compliance and legal risks** | Privacy violations, IP infringement, sector-specific regulation breaches |\n| **Reputational risks** | Flawed outputs, ethical failures damaging user trust |\n\n#### Post-Deployment Monitoring Techniques\n\n- **Drift detection** — Alerting when inputs or predictions deviate from training data or historical trends\n- **Bias detection** — Surfacing inequities emerging from real-world interactions\n- **Misuse detection** — Monitoring for unauthorized or unintended uses\n- **Explainability monitoring** — Ensuring generated explanations remain accurate, consistent, and understandable\n\n> Also needed: vulnerability scanning, penetration testing, audit log ingestion/alerting, and threat intelligence analysis.\n\n### 2.4 Issue Management and Incident Response\n\nOrganizations should implement a structured issue management process aligned with ITSM frameworks. AI-specific elements:\n\n- **Triage categories:** Classify issues by severity (service interruption, ethical violation, user harm)\n- **Ownership and escalation paths:** Define responsible teams (data, model, UX) and escalation processes\n- **Tracking tools:** JIRA, ServiceNow, or custom dashboards for model-related tickets\n\n#### AI-Specific Incident Response\n\n- Predefined incident categories (ethical, legal, security)\n- Pre-written response procedures and playbooks\n- Prompt notification to cyber insurance carrier for covered events\n- Stakeholder notification and escalation trees\n- Postmortem procedures including updates to training, documentation, or policy\n\n> **Integration:** AI incident response should be integrated into the organization's overall cybersecurity and privacy incident response frameworks.\n\n#### Anatomy of an AI Incident\n\n1. **Trigger:** User submits a complaint about unfair treatment\n2. **Analysis:** Team reviews model behavior and decision path\n3. **Impact:** System found to misclassify based on dialect\n4. **Communication:** Management and leadership informed and updated periodically\n5. **Action:** Hotfix deployed; long-term retraining scheduled; user notified; incident response record keeping performed\n6. **Governance:** Model card updated, incident logged for audit, post-incident after-action review (AAR) performed\n\n### 2.5 User Training and Enablement\n\nFor internal users (analysts, customer service agents, claims processors):\n\n- **Role-based training** — Tailored instructions for different user groups\n- **Administrative training** — IT and others responsible for managing the AI system and infrastructure\n- **Confidence thresholding** — Teaching users when to trust the system and when to intervene\n- **Documentation and job aids** — Cheat sheets, workflows, escalation guides\n- **Ongoing enablement** — Refreshing training as models evolve and workflows change\n\nFor customer-facing AI systems:\n\n- Disclosure of the presence of AI capabilities\n- Plain-language descriptions of how the AI works\n- Notice-and-consent mechanisms (links to privacy policy / NOPP)\n- Tutorials, FAQs, and customer support integration\n\n### 2.6 Cross-functional Governance Alignment\n\nAI governance depends on cross-functional collaboration:\n\n| Role | Responsibility |\n|------|----------------|\n| **Data science** | Model deployment, monitoring metrics |\n| **IT/DevOps** | Infrastructure, CI/CD, scalability |\n| **Legal** | Compliance with laws and contracts |\n| **Security** | Threat detection and incident response |\n| **Governance** | Alignment with organizational values and policies |\n| **Business units** | Value realization, user adoption |\n\nCoordination mechanisms include standing responsible AI councils, deployment war rooms, governance cross-membership, and shared dashboards.\n\n> **GRC integration:** AI risks should be included in the enterprise risk register, AI controls in audits, and deployment review gates aligned with product governance lifecycle workflows.\n\n---", "t_monitoring": "## At a Glance\n\nProduction vigilance: watching model performance (accuracy proxies, error patterns), data drift (input distributions shifting) and concept drift (input→output relationships shifting), fairness across groups over time, security anomalies, and usage patterns including over-reliance — with alert thresholds, escalation paths, and retraining triggers defined so detection leads to action.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nDrift definitions are precision-tested: data drift vs. concept drift. Scenario pattern: monitoring detects degradation → investigate root cause, assess impact, remediate (possibly retrain), document — 'continue as-is' and 'immediately retrain without diagnosis' both lose.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Data drift: production inputs diverge from training distribution; concept drift: the real-world relationship the model learned has changed\n- Monitor multiple axes: performance, drift, fairness metrics over time, latency/availability, security signals, usage and reliance patterns\n- Ground-truth lag is real: proxies (confidence distributions, agreement rates, downstream outcomes) stand in when labels arrive late\n- Thresholds → alerts → owners → playbooks: monitoring without a response path is dashboard theater\n- Retraining is a governed event: triggered by defined criteria, validated like a new model, regression-tested before release\n- EU AI Act requires post-market monitoring for high-risk systems — this topic is a legal duty, not just hygiene\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Deployment and Monitoring\n\n## 3. Continuous Monitoring\n\nDeployment is not the end — it begins a new operational phase. Continuous monitoring ensures the AI system remains accurate, fair, performant, and secure.\n\n### 3.1 Foundations of AI System Monitoring\n\nAI systems are **probabilistic and data-dependent**, prone to degradation over time. Monitoring ensures:\n\n- Performance remains within defined thresholds\n- Drift is detected early\n- New risks and edge cases are identified\n- Emerging compliance obligations are met\n\n#### Common Monitoring Areas\n\n| Area | What Is Monitored |\n|------|-------------------|\n| **Prediction quality** | Accuracy, precision, recall, confidence |\n| **Data inputs** | Volume, format, anomalies, missing values, prompt injection, model poisoning |\n| **Latency and performance** | Response times under varying load |\n| **Resource consumption** | CPU/GPU, memory, storage, network |\n| **Fairness** | Disparity across subgroups over time |\n| **Drift** | Shifts in data distribution or model output |\n| **Usage metrics** | Adoption rates, abandonment, user behavior |\n\n### 3.2 Technical Monitoring Frameworks\n\nModern monitoring infrastructure includes:\n\n- **Transaction logging** — Capture inputs, outputs, metadata, and errors\n- **Dashboards** — Real-time and historical KPIs and visualizations\n- **Alerts** — Triggered when KPIs exceed predefined thresholds\n- **Audit logs** — Record system and model lifecycle events for forensic analysis and compliance\n\n**Common tools:** Prometheus (metrics), Grafana (dashboards), ELK stack (logs), and AI/ML-specific tools (Evidently, WhyLabs, AWS SageMaker Model Monitor).\n\n**Observability** — understanding *why* an AI model produces a given output or fails. Aims to provide traceability of the prediction path, logging of model versions and configuration, and correlation of outputs with data inputs.\n\n### 3.3 Detecting Drift and Performance Degradation\n\nModel drift occurs when production data differs significantly from training data.\n\n| Drift Type | Description | Example |\n|------------|-------------|---------|\n| **Covariate drift** | Input distribution changes | New customer behaviors |\n| **Label drift** | Changes in distribution of outcomes | Loan defaults rise |\n| **Concept drift** | Relationship between inputs and outputs changes | Fraud tactics evolve |\n\n#### Drift Detection Techniques\n\n- **Statistical tests:** Kolmogorov-Smirnov (K-S), Population Stability Index (PSI)\n- **Prediction monitoring:** Sharp drops in confidence or consistency\n- **Shadow models:** Parallel models trained on recent data for comparison to production models\n\n### 3.4 Fairness and Ethical Monitoring\n\nEven if a model passes fairness checks during development, bias can emerge post-deployment:\n\n- Track prediction patterns across **sensitive attributes**\n- Watch for **subgroup degradation** over time\n- Review **user complaints and appeal outcomes**\n- Monitor AI model transparency\n\n#### Ethical Monitoring Indicators\n\n- Surges in user opt-outs or complaints\n- Increases in flagged content (for content generation systems)\n- Escalation logs involving model overrides or rollbacks\n\n> Organizations should monitor **all aspects of any business process enabled by AI**, not only the AI system itself.\n\n### 3.5 Defining Maintenance Schedules\n\nPeriodic maintenance is essential alongside monitoring:\n\n| Activity | Frequency |\n|----------|-----------|\n| **Regular evaluation** | Quarterly or monthly model quality assessments |\n| **Scheduled retraining** | Weekly to annually, depending on volatility and risk |\n| **Hotfix intervals** | Immediate patches for urgent failures |\n| **Business reviews** | Quarterly reviews of metrics, KPIs, and incidents |\n| **Dependency and compliance updates** | Updating libraries, frameworks, container images; periodic regulatory reassessments |\n\n#### Retraining Tradeoffs\n\n| Consideration | Balance |\n|---------------|---------|\n| Accuracy improvements | vs. Infrastructure costs |\n| Need for freshness | vs. Risk of instability |\n| Legal obligations | vs. Business priorities |\n\n> **Tools:** Automated model pipelines (MLFlow, TensorFlow Extended—TFX) can reduce overhead.\n\n### 3.6 Handling Emerging Risks\n\nLeading indicators of systemic risk that should trigger reviews:\n\n- Rapid increase in edge case failures\n- Repeated human overrides of predictions\n- Decline in user trust or engagement metrics\n- Increase in output toxicity, misinformation, and hallucinations\n- Rapid increase in abuse and misuse cases\n\n> Governance teams should maintain **\"tripwires\"** that trigger model reviews or suspensions. Any occurrence in these areas should be treated as an incident.\n\n### 3.7 Human Oversight in Monitoring\n\nMonitoring responsibilities should be assigned across roles:\n\n| Role | Responsibility |\n|------|----------------|\n| **AI/ML engineering** | Performance, drift, accuracy |\n| **Data governance** | Fairness, data lineage, usage compliance |\n| **Cybersecurity** | Threat intelligence, vulnerabilities, attack attempts |\n| **Operations/IT** | Latency, uptime, resource utilization |\n| **Ethics/risk** | Ethical violations, bias, hallucinations |\n| **Internal audits** | Results and actions taken |\n| **Regulation developments** | New laws, enforcement changes, case law precedents |\n| **Maintenance** | Operations, logs, retraining |\n\nOwnership maps should include escalation trees and support 24/7 coverage for critical systems. Humans must remain \"on the loop\" to validate anomalies, respond to alerts, and exercise judgment.\n\n### 3.8 Monitoring Third Parties\n\nWhen AI systems are operated by third parties (SaaS environments), visibility is reduced. Mitigations include:\n\n- **Specific terms and conditions** in the legal agreement between customer and AI system provider\n- **Periodic audits** of the vendor's operating environment (development, deployment, monitoring, incident response, cybersecurity operations)\n\n> It is assumed that organizations have **third-party risk management programs** in place.\n\n---", "ch13": "## At a Glance\n\nBOK IV.A — evaluating the factors that shape a deployment decision before any assessment or rollout begins: the use-case context (business objectives, required performance, data availability, ethical considerations, workforce readiness), the differences among AI model types (classic vs. generative, proprietary vs. open-source, small vs. large, language vs. multimodal), and the differences among deployment options (cloud vs. on-premises vs. edge; using a model as-is vs. fine-tuning vs. retrieval-augmented generation vs. agentic architectures).\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\n6–8 questions. Expect trade-off scenarios: an organization with strict data-residency needs (→ on-prem/edge), a narrow task with tight latency budgets (→ smaller model), a knowledge-currency problem (→ RAG over retraining). The tested skill is matching the deployment configuration to the context, not naming the fanciest option.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Use-case factors first: business objectives, performance requirements, data availability, ethics, and workforce readiness gate everything downstream\n- Model-type axes: classic vs. generative, proprietary vs. open-source, small vs. large, language vs. multimodal — each axis trades capability against cost, control, and risk\n- Deployment environments: cloud (scale, third-party exposure), on-premises (control, cost), edge (latency, privacy, hard updates)\n- Adaptation spectrum: as-is (fastest, least fit), fine-tuning (domain fit, inherits base-model risk), RAG (current knowledge, injection surface)\n- Every choice here re-scopes the risk assessment — IV.A decisions are inputs to IV.B assessments\n- Agentic architectures (new in BOK v2.1) let the model plan and act through tools — risk scales with autonomy and action-space, so they need least-privilege permissions, approval checkpoints for high-stakes actions, and a kill switch\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Deployment and Monitoring\n\n### 1.3 AI Model Deployment Considerations\n\nDeployment model decisions influence performance, latency, control, and regulatory posture.\n\n| Deployment Mode | Pros | Cons |\n|-----------------|------|------|\n| **Cloud** | Scalable, cost-effective, easy to integrate with APIs | Less control over data locality, possible regulatory concerns |\n| **On-premises** | High control, good for sensitive data, meets strict compliance | Costly infrastructure, lower scalability |\n| **Edge** | Ultra-low latency, no dependency on network connection, user privacy | Limited compute power, model size constraints |\n| **Hybrid** | Balances performance and compliance (edge/on-prem inference + cloud training/updates) | Added architectural complexity, higher integration overhead |\n\n- **Containerization** (Kubernetes, Docker) enables consistency across environments, auto-scaling, and deployment orchestration in multi-cloud or hybrid environments.\n- The deployment model should be decided in the **design phase** of the AI system project.", "t_deployfactors": "## At a Glance\n\nThe context questions that come before any model or vendor choice: what business objective does this deployment serve, what performance does the use case actually require, is the necessary data available and usable, what ethical considerations attach, and is the workforce ready — trained, resourced, and organizationally prepared — to operate it.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nScenario stems often bury the deciding factor: a team with no ML operations experience (workforce readiness), a use case without accessible data (data availability), or objectives nobody quantified (business objectives). The correct answer addresses the missing factor before proceeding.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Business objectives make success measurable — without them, validation thresholds and monitoring targets have no anchor\n- Performance requirements are use-case-relative: triage support and playlist ranking demand different evidence\n- Data availability includes rights and quality, not just existence\n- Ethical considerations at evaluation stage are the cheap ones — declining a bad use case costs nothing downstream\n- Workforce readiness is a deployment risk: capable systems operated by unprepared teams produce incidents", "t_modeltypes": "## At a Glance\n\nThe model-type axes a deployment evaluation weighs: classic (discriminative, task-specific) versus generative models; proprietary versus open-source; small versus large; language-only versus multimodal. Each axis is a trade — capability against cost, vendor support against transparency, breadth against control — and the BOK expects governance professionals to reason about all four.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nTrade-off matching: strict auditability needs favor transparent/classic or open-source models; broad conversational capability favors large generative ones — with confabulation and content risks attached. No axis has a universally right end; context decides.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Classic/discriminative models predict within defined tasks; generative models create content and carry confabulation, IP, and content-safety risks\n- Proprietary: vendor support and frontier capability, but limited inspectability and contractual dependence\n- Open-source: transparency and control, but the deployer inherits security, hosting, and compliance burden — and license terms still bind\n- Small models: cheaper, faster, easier to govern; large models: broader capability, bigger risk surface and cost\n- Multimodality multiplies use cases and failure modes together — each modality adds its own bias and content risks", "t_deployopts": "## At a Glance\n\nHow and where the model runs: environment choices (cloud's managed scale versus on-premises control versus edge's local-inference latency and privacy), and adaptation choices (deploying a model as-is, fine-tuning it on domain data, or wrapping it in retrieval-augmented generation for grounded, current answers). These decisions shape performance, cost, data residency, and the entire monitoring architecture.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nConfiguration-matching scenarios: data-residency mandates point on-prem or edge; a need for current, citable knowledge without retraining points to RAG; domain-specific tone and vocabulary point to fine-tuning. Each choice's risk tail (RAG → indirect prompt injection; fine-tuning → inherited base-model risk) is the follow-up question.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Cloud: scalability and managed infrastructure, with third-party and data-residency exposure to assess\n- On-premises: maximum control and sovereignty at higher operational cost; edge: latency and privacy wins, update and monitoring logistics costs\n- As-is deployment is fastest but least fitted; validate against your population anyway\n- Fine-tuning buys domain fit and inherits the base model's biases, memorized data, and license constraints\n- RAG grounds outputs in retrieved sources — reducing confabulation, enabling citations, and opening the indirect-injection surface\n- Agentic architectures: the model calls tools and takes actions toward goals — weigh the gain against autonomy and action-space risk (see Agentic AI)", "t_agentic": "## At a Glance\n\nAgentic architectures are the newest IV.A deployment option: instead of only producing content, an agent plans and takes actions through tools, often over many steps and alongside other agents. Governance shifts from reviewing outputs to bounding what the agent can do (action space) and how freely it decides (autonomy), keeping humans meaningfully accountable, and being able to stop it. Singapore's IMDA Model AI Governance Framework for Agentic AI (v1.5, May 2026) and OWASP's Top 10 for Agentic Applications 2026 are the main references; the EU AI Act and GDPR apply through their ordinary definitions.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nExpect scenarios: whether a task needs an agent at all (least agency), which control bounds a given risk (least privilege, unique agent identity, sandboxing, system-level approval gates), what to do when oversight decays into rubber-stamping, how to stop a misbehaving agent, and how the AI Act and GDPR Art. 22 attach to an agent's decisions. Traps: prompt instructions offered as sufficient controls, the vendor offered as accountable for the deployer's agent, and dashboard-watching offered as meaningful human involvement.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Two dials of agent risk: action space (tools and permissions) and autonomy (instructions and human involvement); what an agent can touch drives impact, how freely it decides drives likelihood\n- Least agency: not every use case needs an agent, and fixed, repeatable processes are better served by deterministic workflows\n- Bound risk up front: least-privilege, time-bound, non-transferable permissions capped at the authorising human's rights; unique, centrally catalogued agent identities; sandboxes for code execution\n- Human approval checkpoints for high-stakes, irreversible or atypical actions, enforced at system level, with override rates and review times audited for automation bias\n- Test whole workflows (tool calls, policy compliance, robustness), agents together as well as alone, and repeatedly; red-team for goal hijack, tool misuse, memory poisoning and cascading failures\n- Roll out gradually, keep immutable logs, monitor in real time, deny by default when approvals fail, and keep a kill switch that implements the IV.C deactivation policy\n- Law attaches through general rules: AI Act Art. 3(1) covers varying autonomy, the risk tier follows the use, Art. 25(1)(c) can make a deployer a provider, and GDPR Art. 22 needs meaningful rather than token human involvement\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Agentic AI Governance\n\n# Agentic AI Governance — Key Provisions\n\n> **Status:** Governed through general AI and data-protection law (EU AI Act, GDPR), which reaches agents through its ordinary definitions, plus best-practice guidance. **Lead guidance:** Singapore IMDA Model AI Governance Framework for Agentic AI v1.5 (20 May 2026). **Security references:** OWASP Top 10 for Agentic Applications 2026; OWASP Agentic AI – Threats and Mitigations; CSA MAESTRO. **Standards work:** NIST CAISI AI Agent Standards Initiative (launched Feb 2026). **BOK hooks:** the v2.1 introduction (\"generative AI and agentic AI\"), IV.A (\"agentic architectures\"), III.C and IV.C (red teaming, monitoring, deactivation).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n**Why it is on the exam.** BOK v2.1 (effective 2 February 2026) says the program keeps evolving to cover new applications \"such as generative AI and agentic AI\". Performance indicator IV.A now lists \"agentic architectures\" beside fine-tuning and retrieval augmented generation as ways of deploying a model. The existing bank only used \"AI agent\" in the basic taxonomy sense (perceive, decide, act); this note covers the governance of agents as a deployment choice.\n\n**What an agent is.** There is no agreed definition. The IMDA framework picks out the common thread: an agent has some independence in planning, deciding and acting over several steps to reach a goal a user sets. An agentic AI system is one or more such agents, working alone or together. Current enterprise agents mostly use a small, large or multimodal language model as their reasoning engine, although rule-based agents and other kinds exist too.\n\n**Why governance changes.** A generative AI assistant produces content that a person then uses. An agent acts: it updates a database, makes a payment, sends an email or calls another agent. Access to data and the power to change systems are what make agents useful and what make their failures costly. Several interacting agents make outcomes harder to predict, and reviewing every step by hand does not scale. Familiar principles (transparency, accountability, fairness) still apply, but they have to be translated into controls on actions.\n\n| | Generative AI assistant | AI agent |\n|---|---|---|\n| Main output | Content for a human to use | Actions in other systems, plus content |\n| Steps | Usually one prompt, one response | A multi-step plan, executed and revised |\n| Reach | The conversation | Tools, APIs, computers and browsers, other agents |\n| Memory | Mostly the current session | Short-term and long-term memory, possibly shared |\n| Typical failure | Wrong or harmful content | Wrong, unauthorised or irreversible actions |\n\n---\n\n## 2. What Makes Agents Different\n\n### 2.1 Core components (IMDA v1.5)\n\n| # | Component | What it does | Governance angle |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Model | The reasoning and planning engine | Inherits model risks: hallucination, bias, prompt injection |\n| 2 | Instructions | System prompt setting role, capabilities, limits | Prompt-level limits are not deterministic |\n| 3 | Memory | Short- or long-term storage the model can draw on | Persistent memory plus sensitive data raises leakage risk; a poisoning target |\n| 4 | Planning and reasoning | Breaks a task into steps | Plans can misread intent or drift |\n| 5 | Tools | Let the agent act on files, databases, devices, transactions; other agents can be tools | Tools and permissions define the action space |\n| 6 | Protocols | Standard ways to reach tools and agents, e.g. MCP (agent to tool) and A2A (agent to agent) | Untrusted servers and components are a supply-chain risk |\n| 7 | Controls | Access controls, guardrails, human approvals | Limit action space and autonomy |\n| 8 | Logging and monitoring | Record actions, decisions and interactions | Accountability, debugging, incident response |\n\nVersion 1.5 added components 7 and 8 to the list.\n\n### 2.2 Two dials: action space and autonomy\n\n| Dial | Meaning | Set by | How it widens |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Action space** | What the agent *can* do | The tools it may call and the permissions on them | Sandbox only → internal systems → external systems; read → write; a computer-use agent that can click and type anywhere |\n| **Autonomy** | How freely it *decides* what to do | Its instructions and the level of human involvement | Detailed SOP → \"use your judgment\"; fewer approval steps |\n\nRisk grows along both dials. IMDA illustrates this with a coding agent that ranges from one that can list files but needs approval to read them, up to one that browses the web, writes code, runs tests and deploys fixes on its own.\n\n### 2.3 Levels of human involvement\n\nIMDA's four levels of human involvement draw on the Knight First Amendment Institute's paper on levels of autonomy for AI agents. The right-hand column is our rough mapping to the classic oversight labels.\n\n| Level | Human role | Rough label |\n|---|---|---|\n| Agent proposes, human operates | Reviews and approves every action | Human-in-the-loop, every step |\n| Agent and human collaborate | Approves significant steps (writing to a database, paying); can pause or take over at any time | Human-in-the-loop at checkpoints |\n| Agent operates, human approves | Approves only critical steps or failures (deleting a database, paying above a limit) | Exception-based approval |\n| Agent operates, human observes | No approvals; actions may be audited afterwards | Human-on-the-loop |\n\nThe EU High-Level Expert Group's Ethics Guidelines for Trustworthy AI (8 April 2019) give the classic three: **human-in-the-loop** (able to intervene in every decision cycle, which the guidelines note is often neither possible nor desirable), **human-on-the-loop** (intervening in design and monitoring operation) and **human-in-command** (overseeing the system's overall activity and deciding whether, when and how to use it, including overriding it). The same guidelines add a principle that fits agents well: other things being equal, the less oversight a human can exercise, the more testing and the stricter the governance needed.\n\n### 2.4 Multi-agent systems\n\nIMDA describes three simple patterns: **sequential** (each agent's output feeds the next), **supervisor** (one agent coordinates specialists, calling them as tools) and **swarm** (agents work in parallel and hand off to each other). Splitting work lets each agent be given only the tools and permissions its task needs, instead of one agent holding everything. The trade-off is interaction risk, covered next.\n\n---\n\n## 3. How Risk Scales\n\n### 3.1 Familiar risks, new pathways\n\nAgents are software built on language models, so they inherit ordinary software flaws (such as SQL injection) and model risks (hallucination, bias, data leakage, prompt injection). What changes is where those risks surface:\n\n- **Planning:** a plan that misreads the user's intent, cannot achieve the task, or drifts from an earlier plan.\n- **Tools:** calling a tool that does not exist, the wrong tool, or the right tool with the wrong input, or calling tools in a biased way; injected instructions can turn tools into a route for exfiltration.\n- **Protocols:** poorly configured or malicious connectors, such as an untrusted MCP server carrying code that leaks data.\n\n### 3.2 Five kinds of harm (IMDA)\n\n| Harm | Example |\n|---|---|\n| Erroneous actions | Booking an appointment on the wrong date; shipping flawed code |\n| Unauthorised actions | Acting outside permitted scope, e.g. skipping an escalation that policy requires |\n| Biased or unfair actions | Biased vendor selection, grant disbursement or hiring |\n| Data breaches | Exposing or wrongly modifying personal or confidential data |\n| Disruption to connected systems | Deleting a production codebase; flooding an external API with requests |\n\n### 3.3 Systemic and multi-agent risks\n\n- **Speed and volume:** agents act faster than oversight can catch unauthorised actions in real time, and constant approval requests breed automation bias and alert fatigue.\n- **Cascading effects:** one mistake compounds across later steps. IMDA's example is a hallucinated inventory figure that triggers excessive or insufficient reorders downstream.\n- **Shared context:** agents passing memory and intermediate outputs to each other can leak sensitive data to less secure agents.\n- **Agent sprawl:** uncontrolled growth in the number of agents without central management.\n- **Collaborative failures:** miscoordination (agents reading the goal differently), conflict (a refund agent against a revenue-protection agent) and collusion (pricing agents converging on higher prices without being told to).\n- **Emergent behaviour:** outcomes that testing each agent alone cannot predict, which is harder still across organisational boundaries without white-box access.\n\n### 3.4 IMDA risk factors: impact versus likelihood\n\nIMDA treats risk as likelihood times impact and says to weigh it against the benefits of the use case.\n\n| Raises **impact** (severity) | Raises **likelihood** (probability) |\n|---|---|\n| Domain's tolerance for error, and how many critical processes the agent supports | Level of autonomy (own judgment rather than an SOP) |\n| Access to sensitive data, worse with persistent memory | Task complexity |\n| Access to external systems (leakage to, or disruption of, third parties) | Exposure to external or untrusted systems (prompt injection, cyberattack) |\n| Scope of actions: read versus write, few tools versus many or computer use | Agent provided or operated by a third party (less visibility and control) |\n| Reversibility: contracts, sales and external emails are hard to undo | System complexity: multiple agents, feedback loops |\n\nVersion 1.5 added the third-party and system-complexity factors. Threat modelling sharpens the assessment by describing concrete attack paths; IMDA names memory poisoning, tool misuse and privilege compromise as common agent threats and suggests taint tracing to follow untrusted data through complex systems.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Bounding Risk Up Front (IMDA Dimension 1)\n\n### 4.1 Use-case selection\n\n- Not every use case suits an agent; a fixed, repeatable process is often better served by a deterministic workflow. OWASP calls this **least agency**: autonomy deployed where it adds nothing still widens the attack surface.\n- Tiering works well in practice. In IMDA's Dayos case, each IT ticket type was scored on severity, reversibility and whether human review was feasible: low-severity, reversible tickets were automated with periodic audits; moderate ones let the agent diagnose but required an engineer's sign-off; production deployments, security changes and permission changes were kept away from the agent entirely.\n\n### 4.2 Limits by design\n\n- **Tools and systems:** least privilege, meaning only the minimum tools and data the task needs. Functional boundaries (a separate IT helpdesk agent and HR agent) are a natural constraint.\n- **Autonomy:** constrain agents to SOPs rather than letting them define every step.\n- **Area of impact:** build ways to take agents offline, and run high-risk tasks such as code execution in self-contained **sandboxes** with limited network and data access.\n- **Deterministic beats prompt-based:** block a tool with an access control instead of asking the model not to use it. Where a limit is non-deterministic, add monitoring or human review.\n\n### 4.3 Agent identity\n\nIdentity is how organisations already make human actions traceable. IMDA's interim best practice is that each agent's identity be:\n\n1. **Unique** and cryptographically verifiable;\n2. **Accounted for:** tied to a supervising agent, a human user or a department;\n3. **Differentiated by capacity:** recording whether it acts on its own account or for a named user;\n4. **Catalogued and centrally managed:** issued and tracked by one system, which curbs sprawl and lets unused identities be removed.\n\nCurrent identity systems struggle with static scopes, single-human authentication, and agents that spin up sub-agents. Work is under way, including NIST's NCCoE concept paper on software and AI agent identity and authorization (comments closed 2 April 2026).\n\n### 4.4 Authorisation\n\n- Scoped, least-privilege by default, time- or session-bound and non-transferable, with explicit escalation paths for more.\n- **Capped by the authorising human:** a user should not be able to give an agent more access than the user has; delegations are recorded.\n- OWASP adds just-in-time, short-lived credentials, per-action authorisation for privileged steps and tool usage budgets.\n\n### 4.5 Residual risk\n\nSome risk always remains after mitigation. The organisation has to decide explicitly whether it is tolerable.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Meaningful Human Accountability (IMDA Dimension 2)\n\n### 5.1 The agentic value chain\n\nModel developers → platform providers → system providers or app developers (with tooling providers such as MCP servers and APIs) → deployer → end users. One organisation can hold several roles; a firm that builds and runs its own agent is both system provider and deployer. Whatever the chain, the deploying organisation and the people who oversee its agents stay accountable for what the agents do.\n\n### 5.2 Inside the organisation\n\n| Who | Typical responsibilities |\n|---|---|\n| Key decision makers (board, C-suite, department heads) | Goals for agent use; permitted use cases and data-access limits; governance approach and escalation |\n| Product teams | Design and requirements; phased rollouts; testing and monitoring; user education |\n| Cybersecurity teams | Baseline guardrails and secure-by-design templates; red teaming and threat modelling |\n| Users | Responsible use; required training; reporting bugs and issues |\n\nTeams should also build enough understanding of the technology to adjust governance quickly as it changes (IMDA calls this adaptive governance).\n\n### 5.3 Third parties and vendor terms (links to BOK IV.B)\n\n- **Contracts:** set out who owes what, especially security arrangements, performance guarantees and data protection. If gaps remain, re-check whether the deployment still fits the organisation's risk tolerance.\n- **Opacity:** a vendor's agent can be hard to observe. Require disclosures on capabilities and data handling, and ask for and test controls such as scoped API keys, per-agent identity tokens and logging of tool calls and access history. Where these are missing, consider an alternative or in-house build, or narrow the use case (for example, no sensitive data).\n- **Containment:** IMDA's MSD case restricts agentic features inside SaaS tools to their own ecosystem by default, because cross-platform actions may never have been tested.\n- **Supply chain:** OWASP ASI04 covers malicious or tampered third-party models, tools, plug-ins, MCP and A2A components and registries, often loaded at run time.\n\n### 5.4 Human approval checkpoints\n\nIMDA lists four triggers for requiring human approval before an agent acts:\n\n| Trigger | Examples |\n|---|---|\n| High-stakes | Editing sensitive data; final decisions in healthcare or legal matters; actions that create liability |\n| Irreversible | Permanently deleting data; sending communications; making payments |\n| Outlier or atypical | Accessing a system outside the agent's scope; a delivery route twice the median length |\n| User-defined | A user's own limit, such as approval for purchases above an amount |\n\nHow to ask: keep requests short and contextual, state the risk (a risk level or confidence score helps), and match the input to the decision (approve or reject; edit a plan before it runs; a written justification for high-risk approvals). Enforce checkpoints at the system level; IMDA's OpenClaw case study warns that prompt-layer guardrails can be bypassed or forgotten.\n\n### 5.5 Keeping oversight meaningful: automation bias\n\n- **Automation bias** is the tendency to over-trust an automated system, especially one that has been reliable so far. It grows as agents get more capable, and alert fatigue and human-like design make it worse.\n- **Audit the overseers:** track the human override rate (a very low rate may mean rubber-stamping) and review times (very short times may mean automation bias or fatigue), and look for reviewers whose patterns stand out.\n- **Train and equip them:** teach common failure modes, note that a model's displayed chain of thought may not faithfully explain its actions, and make sure reviewers have the domain expertise to judge what they approve.\n- **Back humans up with automation:** alerts on logged events, anomaly detection, agents that monitor other agents, and **deny by default** when the approval system fails or an agent tries an action no policy covers.\n- **Legal echoes:** AI Act Art. 14(4)(b) expects high-risk systems to help overseers stay aware of automation bias; Art. 26(2) makes deployers assign oversight to people with the competence, training and authority to exercise it; the WP29 guidelines on automated decision-making say human involvement must be meaningful, not a token gesture.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Technical Controls and Testing (IMDA Dimension 3)\n\n### 6.1 Choosing controls\n\n- **Structural or rule-based over prompt-layer** for higher-risk actions: restrict the tool itself, or build a required sequence into the workflow, instead of instructing the model.\n- **Model-based safeguards** where fixed rules cannot capture the risk, such as detecting harmful content.\n- **Runtime controls** for what design-time rules miss: rate limits on tool use, input validation before actions run.\n\n| Component | Sample controls |\n|---|---|\n| Planning | Have the agent check its plan against the instructions; summarise its understanding and ask for clarification; log the plan for review |\n| Tools | Strict input formats; least privilege enforced by authentication and authorisation; no write access to sensitive tables unless essential; hand control to the user for passwords and keys |\n| Protocols | Use standard protocols; allow only whitelisted MCP servers; sandbox any code execution |\n| Multi-agent | Structured, typed messages between agents rather than free text; limit shared memory |\n\nIMDA also suggests the MCP layer as a governance chokepoint, since it sits between agents and enterprise systems: filter sensitive data there, log every agent-to-system interaction, and allow only trusted servers.\n\n### 6.2 Pre-deployment testing\n\n- **New test dimensions:** overall task execution; policy compliance, including routing to a human when approval is required; tool calling (right tools, permissions, inputs and order); robustness to errors and edge cases.\n- **Whole workflows,** not just final outputs, because agents take many steps with nobody watching.\n- **Individually and together:** test multi-agent systems as systems, including what happens when one agent is compromised.\n- **Realistic environments** that mirror production, balanced against the risk of giving an untested agent real-world access.\n- **Repeatedly and at scale:** agent behaviour is stochastic, so rare high-impact failures only show up over many runs and varied datasets. Evaluation mixes deterministic checks for structured tool calls with LLM, agent and human review of reasoning.\n\n### 6.3 Red teaming and threat modelling\n\n- **NIST agent hijacking evaluations** (technical blog, 17 January 2025, now listed under CAISI). Agent hijacking is indirect prompt injection: malicious instructions planted in data the agent ingests, causing unintended harmful actions. Lessons: keep improving shared evaluation frameworks; keep evaluations adaptive, because new attacks break systems that resist known ones; look at task-specific success, not just the average (exfiltration matters more than a harmless email); and test repeated attempts, which give more realistic results.\n- **CSA Agentic AI Red Teaming Guide** (28 May 2025; free with registration). Covers permission escalation, hallucination, orchestration flaws, memory manipulation and supply-chain risks, from isolated model behaviour up to full workflows and inter-agent dependencies.\n- **CSA MAESTRO** (Ken Huang, 6 February 2025): Multi-Agent Environment, Security, Threat, Risk, and Outcome. It argues that STRIDE, PASTA and LINDDUN were not built for autonomy, ML-specific attacks or agent-to-agent interaction, and models seven layers:\n\n| Layer | Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Foundation Models |\n| 2 | Data Operations |\n| 3 | Agent Frameworks |\n| 4 | Deployment and Infrastructure |\n| 5 | Evaluation and Observability |\n| 6 | Security and Compliance (a vertical layer that cuts across all others) |\n| 7 | Agent Ecosystem |\n\n### 6.4 OWASP Top 10 for Agentic Applications 2026\n\nReleased 9 December 2025 by the OWASP GenAI Security Project's Agentic Security Initiative (CC BY-SA 4.0). Summaries are ours.\n\n| ID | Name | In short |\n|---|---|---|\n| ASI01 | Agent Goal Hijack | An attacker redirects the agent's goals, task choices or plan, often via indirect prompt injection in content it reads |\n| ASI02 | Tool Misuse and Exploitation | A legitimate tool used unsafely within privileges the agent already has (deleting data, over-calling costly APIs, exfiltration) |\n| ASI03 | Identity and Privilege Abuse | Escalation through delegation chains, inherited credentials or cached sessions; the agentic form of Excessive Agency |\n| ASI04 | Agentic Supply Chain Vulnerabilities | Malicious or tampered third-party models, tools, plug-ins, MCP/A2A components or registries |\n| ASI05 | Unexpected Code Execution (RCE) | Generated or injected code that compromises the host, container or sandbox |\n| ASI06 | Memory & Context Poisoning | Persistent corruption of stored or retrieved context (memory, summaries, RAG stores) that carries across sessions |\n| ASI07 | Insecure Inter-Agent Communication | Messages between agents intercepted, spoofed, replayed or tampered with |\n| ASI08 | Cascading Failures | One fault (hallucination, bad input, corrupted tool, poisoned memory) propagating and amplifying across agents and workflows |\n| ASI09 | Human-Agent Trust Exploitation | Over-trust and human-like fluency used to steer people into harmful approvals |\n| ASI10 | Rogue Agents | An agent diverging from its intended function or scope, even if each action looks legitimate |\n\nClassification rules OWASP spells out, useful for \"which risk is this?\" questions:\n- Misusing privileges the agent already holds is **ASI02**; gaining privileges it should not have is **ASI03**; misuse ending in code execution is **ASI05**.\n- **ASI06** is persistent corruption of stored context; a one-off malicious prompt stays under the LLM Top 10's prompt injection entry. Memory poisoning often leads on to goal hijack.\n- **ASI08** is about propagation. Classify the originating defect (tainted dependency, poisoned memory, spoofed message) under its own entry and use ASI08 when it fans out across agents, sessions or workflows.\n- **ASI09** is human over-reliance; **ASI10** is the agent itself drifting.\n\nCompanion taxonomy: **Agentic AI – Threats and Mitigations** (v1.0, 17 February 2025; v1.1, December 2025, threats T1 to T17), including T1 Memory Poisoning, T2 Tool Misuse, T3 Privilege Compromise, T5 Cascading Hallucination Attacks, T6 Intent Breaking & Goal Manipulation, T8 Repudiation & Untraceability, T10 Overwhelming Human in the Loop, T13 Rogue Agents in Multi-Agent Systems and T17 Supply Chain Compromise.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Monitoring, Logging, Kill Switches and Deactivation (BOK III.C and IV.C)\n\n### 7.1 Gradual rollout\n\nLimit exposure by **users** (trained users first), **tools and protocols** (whitelisted MCP servers first) and **systems** (lower-risk internal systems first). IMDA's GovTech case started with internal developers only, no MCP access and low-risk systems, then built central logging and monitoring and a governed path to whitelisted MCP servers from a containerised sandbox, and red-teamed those guardrails, before widening access.\n\n### 7.2 Continuous monitoring\n\n- **Purposes:** intervene in real time (stop the workflow and escalate), debug incidents step by step, audit at intervals.\n- **What to log:** prioritise high-risk activity such as database updates and financial transactions.\n- **How:** monitor several layers (user-agent interaction, agent-tool calls, model reasoning); set alert thresholds (rule-based, anomaly detection, agents watching agents); define the intervention for each alert type, from scheduled review, to a temporary halt, to termination with a fallback for catastrophic malfunction or compromise; feed into observability tooling (e.g. OpenTelemetry tracing); keep logs **immutable**; feed findings back into evaluation; keep testing for drift.\n\n### 7.3 Change management\n\nDefine triggers for review: technical (model or tool changes), environmental (domain or business shifts), performance (anomalies, degradation) and regulatory. Tier the review by risk: a prompt tweak gets a light review, a model update or **autonomy adjustment** gets a full governance review, and a change touching high-stakes decisions triggers an immediate reassessment.\n\n### 7.4 Kill switches and deactivation\n\n- **OWASP ASI10:** kill switches and credential revocation to disable rogue agents at once; quarantine suspect agents in a sandbox for forensics; readmit them only after fresh attestation, dependency checks and human approval.\n- **OWASP ASI04 and ASI08:** a supply-chain kill switch that revokes specific tools, prompts or connections across every deployment; blast-radius limits such as quotas, progress caps and circuit breakers between planner and executor.\n- **IMDA:** design ways to take agents offline; halt or terminate them, with fallback solutions, when something serious happens.\n- **BOK IV.C:** \"create and implement a policy and controls to deactivate or localize an AI system\" when regulation or performance requires it. For agents the policy should name who may trigger the switch, what gets revoked (credentials, tokens, tool connections), which manual fallback takes over, and what evidence is kept. IMDA's warning about lost tradecraft matters here: staff who never do the task by hand may not be able to run the fallback.\n- **EU AI Act Art. 14(4)(e):** high-risk systems must let overseers interrupt them with a \"stop\" button or similar procedure that brings the system to a halt in a safe state.\n\n### 7.5 Fail-safe design\n\nA fail-safe design falls back to a safe state when something breaks. For agents that means denying the action by default when the approval service is down or no approval policy exists (IMDA), and failing secure in code execution environments (OWASP ASI05). A time-out that lets an agent proceed when nobody answers is fail-open.\n\n---\n\n## 8. End-User Responsibility (IMDA Dimension 4)\n\n| User type | Examples | What the organisation provides |\n|---|---|---|\n| Users who **interact** with agents | Customer service or HR agents; mostly external-facing | **Transparency:** say at the point of interaction that it is an agent; its range of actions and decisions; how user data is used (with consent where needed); a human contact for escalation; the user's own responsibilities, and where appropriate the option to set personal approval limits |\n| Users who **integrate** agents into their work | Coding assistants, enterprise workflows; mostly internal | Transparency **plus education:** suitable and restricted use cases, how to instruct agents, their range of actions, common failure modes (hallucination, loops after errors), refreshers, feedback channels, and keeping core skills so the business can continue if the agent is unavailable |\n\n---\n\n## 9. How Existing Law Attaches\n\n### 9.1 EU AI Act (Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, as amended by the Digital Omnibus, Regulation (EU) 2026/1744)\n\n- **Definition.** Art. 3(1) defines an AI system as a machine-based system \"designed to operate with varying levels of autonomy\" whose outputs \"can influence physical or virtual environments\". Agents fit squarely; no separate agent category is needed.\n- **Risk tier follows the use, not the architecture.** An agent that screens and filters job applications falls under Annex III point 4(a); one that scores creditworthiness under point 5(b). An Annex III system that profiles people is always high-risk (Art. 6(3), final subparagraph).\n- **Roles can shift.** A deployer that changes the intended purpose of a system that was not high-risk, including a general-purpose AI system, so that it becomes high-risk is treated as its provider (Art. 25(1)(c)).\n- **High-risk duties that bite on agents:** effective human oversight, including awareness of automation bias, the ability to override, and a stop function that halts in a safe state (Art. 14(4)(b), (d), (e)); deployers must follow the instructions for use, assign competent and empowered overseers, monitor, suspend use and inform the provider and authority when a risk emerges, and keep logs for at least six months (Art. 26(1), (2), (5), (6)).\n- **Transparency.** Art. 50(1): providers must design AI systems that interact directly with people so those people are told they are dealing with AI, unless it is obvious. Applies from 2 August 2026.\n- **Dates.** As originally enacted, Annex III high-risk obligations were due from 2 August 2026. As amended by the Digital Omnibus, they apply from 2 December 2027, and Annex I (product-embedded) obligations from 2 August 2028.\n\n### 9.2 GDPR\n\n- **Art. 22(1):** a person has the right not to be subject to a decision \"based solely on automated processing\", including profiling, that has legal or similarly significant effects. Exceptions in Art. 22(2): necessary for a contract, authorised by law with safeguards, or explicit consent. Where the contract or consent exception applies, Art. 22(3) requires at least the right to obtain human intervention, to express a view and to contest the decision. Art. 22(4) restricts the use of special-category data.\n- **Agents make decisions.** Recital 71's own examples, the automatic refusal of an online credit application and e-recruiting without human intervention, describe exactly what an autonomous agent does.\n- **Rubber-stamping does not help.** The WP29 guidelines on automated decision-making (WP251rev.01, adopted 3 October 2017, revised 6 February 2018, endorsed by the EDPB on 25 May 2018) say a controller cannot avoid Art. 22 by fabricating human involvement: oversight must be meaningful, carried out by someone with the authority and competence to change the decision, and the degree of involvement should be recorded in the DPIA. An approver with a 99% approval rate and a four-second review time is a warning sign under both GDPR and IMDA.\n- **Chains of agents.** In SCHUFA (C-634/21, 7 December 2023) the CJEU held that an automated credit score is itself an automated decision where the lender gives it a determining role. By analogy, an upstream scoring agent whose output a downstream agent or person simply follows can be caught.\n- **Data minimisation.** In IMDA's Google and Singapore Government sandbox, a social-assistance agent often included extraneous personal details in applications; testing for minimisation belongs in agent evaluation.\n\n### 9.3 Standards and non-binding guidance\n\n- **NIST CAISI AI Agent Standards Initiative** (Center for AI Standards and Innovation; launched February 2026). Three pillars: facilitating industry-led standards, fostering community-led open protocols, and investing in research such as agent authentication and security evaluations. It ran a request for information on AI agent security (closed 9 March 2026) alongside the NCCoE identity concept paper.\n- **IMDA MGF for Agentic AI.** Builds on Singapore's Model AI Governance Framework (2nd edition, 2020). Version 1.0 launched on 22 January 2026 at Davos; v1.5 (20 May 2026) reflects feedback from more than 60 companies. It describes itself as a living document and invites case studies.\n- **OWASP and CSA** outputs (section 6) are free, community-developed security references, not law.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Vocabulary (in our own words)\n\n| Term | Working definition |\n|---|---|\n| Agentic AI | AI that pursues a goal over several steps by planning, deciding and acting through tools with some independence |\n| AI agent | One such goal-directed system; several can make up one agentic system |\n| Autonomy levels | Graded degrees of independence, from \"agent proposes, human operates\" to \"agent operates, human observes\" |\n| Action space | Everything an agent is able to do, set by its tools and permissions |\n| Human-in-the-loop | A human approves before the system acts, at every step or at defined checkpoints |\n| Human-on-the-loop | The system acts; a human monitors and can step in or stop it |\n| Human-in-command | Humans decide whether, when and how the system is used at all, and can override it |\n| Human approval checkpoint | A defined point where the agent must wait for a human decision |\n| Fail-safe | When a part fails, the system defaults to a safe state, e.g. denying the action |\n| Least privilege / least agency | The minimum access, and the minimum autonomy, a task needs |\n| Agent sprawl | Uncontrolled growth of agents with no central inventory or ownership |\n| Kill switch | An emergency control that halts an agent and revokes its access at once |\n| Multi-agent system | Several agents working together in sequential, supervisor or swarm patterns |\n\n---\n\n## Exam Angles\n\n- **Does it need an agent at all?** If the steps are fixed, the best answer is usually a deterministic workflow (least agency). Adding oversight to an unnecessary agent is the distractor.\n- **Action space versus autonomy.** Tools and permissions change what an agent can do; instructions and human involvement change how freely it decides. Expect a question that makes you sort changes into the right dial.\n- **Deterministic beats prompt-based.** When one option enforces a limit at the tool or system layer and another relies on the system prompt, the system-layer control wins, especially for payments, deletions and outgoing messages.\n- **Delegation cannot enlarge access.** An agent's permissions are capped by its authorising human's. Logging, indemnities or time limits do not fix an over-broad grant.\n- **Unique agent identity.** Shared service accounts and borrowed employee credentials are the traps; a unique, owned, centrally registered identity is the answer.\n- **Accountability stays with the deployer.** Answers that shift responsibility to the agent vendor are wrong; the fix for vendor opacity is disclosures, controls, a narrower scope or an alternative.\n- **Rubber-stamp oversight.** Very high approval rates and very short review times point to automation bias. Audit and retrain; don't celebrate or remove the checkpoint. Under GDPR, token review still leaves a decision \"solely automated\".\n- **Fail-safe.** When the approval system is down, deny by default. A time-out that proceeds is fail-open.\n- **Classify the OWASP risk by its vector.** Persistent memory corruption is ASI06; direct goal redirection is ASI01; unsafe use of a granted tool is ASI02; propagation with no attacker is ASI08.\n- **Law attaches through ordinary rules.** Agents are AI systems under Art. 3(1); the risk tier comes from the use; repurposing a general-purpose system for hiring can make the deployer a provider (Art. 25(1)(c)). Anchor dates: Annex III obligations run from 2 December 2027 as amended (2 August 2026 as originally enacted).\n\n## Key Terms\n\n- [[Agentic AI]] — goal-directed AI that plans and acts over multiple steps through tools\n- [[Action Space (Agentic AI)]] — what an agent can do, set by its tools and permissions\n- [[Tool Misuse]] — unsafe use of a legitimately granted tool (OWASP ASI02)\n- [[Agent Goal Hijack]] — redirecting an agent's objectives, often via indirect prompt injection (OWASP ASI01)\n- [[Least-Privilege Agent Permissions]] — minimum, time-bound, non-transferable access capped at the authorising human's rights\n- [[Human Approval Checkpoint]] — a point where a human must approve before the agent proceeds\n- [[Multi-Agent System]] — cooperating agents; narrower scopes but interaction risks\n- [[Agent Kill Switch]] — emergency halt and credential revocation; the operational core of a deactivation policy\n\n## Sources\n\n- https://www.imda.gov.sg/-/media/imda/files/about/emerging-tech-and-research/artificial-intelligence/mgf-for-agentic-ai.pdf\n- https://www.imda.gov.sg/resources/press-releases-factsheets-and-speeches/press-releases/2026/new-model-ai-governance-framework-for-agentic-ai\n- https://genai.owasp.org/resource/owasp-top-10-for-agentic-applications-for-2026/\n- https://genai.owasp.org/resource/agentic-ai-threats-and-mitigations/\n- https://cloudsecurityalliance.org/blog/2025/02/06/agentic-ai-threat-modeling-framework-maestro\n- https://cloudsecurityalliance.org/artifacts/agentic-ai-red-teaming-guide\n- https://www.nist.gov/caisi/ai-agent-standards-initiative\n- https://www.nist.gov/news-events/news/2025/01/technical-blog-strengthening-ai-agent-hijacking-evaluations\n- https://assets.contentstack.io/v3/assets/bltd4dd5b2d705252bc/blt0d33152fd20bc134/AIGP_Cert%20BOK.pdf\n- https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/article-3\n- https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/article-14\n- https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/article-25\n- https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/article-50\n- https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/timeline/timeline-implementation-eu-ai-act\n- https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/PDF/?uri=CELEX:32016R0679\n- https://ec.europa.eu/newsroom/article29/items/612053\n- https://curia.europa.eu/jcms/upload/docs/application/pdf/2023-12/cp230186en.pdf\n- https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/library/ethics-guidelines-trustworthy-ai", "ch11": "## At a Glance\n\nBOK IV.B — the key assessment activities that precede putting a chosen system into use: performing or reviewing an impact assessment on the selected system in its deployment context, evaluating the key terms and risks in the vendor or licensing agreement, and weighing the risks and opportunities unique to deploying a proprietary model — increased obligations and higher potential liability.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\n5–7 questions. Scenarios present an organization about to deploy a third-party, open-source or in-house model and ask what must be assessed first. Strong answers name the specific due-diligence set: an impact assessment on the actual system and use, and license and contract review — not generic 'do a risk assessment.' BOK v2.1 dropped the separate 'identify applicable laws' item; legal questions now sit in Domain II.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Impact assessments re-run against the SELECTED model in its real deployment context — a design-phase assessment of a different model doesn't transfer\n- Legal applicability still matters in practice, but v2.1 tests it through Domain II (II.A–II.C) rather than as an IV.B indicator\n- Vendor and open-source terms allocate risk: data-use rights, warranties, audit rights, update obligations, liability caps, license conditions\n- Proprietary-model deployment carries heightened obligations and liability — you answer for behavior you cannot fully inspect\n- Open-source is not obligation-free: license terms bind, and security/compliance burden shifts to the deployer\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] AI System Design\n\n## 6. Proprietary Model Considerations\n\nAn organization that develops and deploys a proprietary AI model enjoys greater control but also must accept greater responsibility. Unlike third-party AI solutions, proprietary models involve **end-to-end ownership** of requirements, design, training, deployment, and all operational and legal outcomes.\n\n### 6.1 Ownership and Accountability\n\nOrganizations that create proprietary AI models are accountable for the entire model lifecycle:\n\n| Responsibility | Description |\n|----------------|-------------|\n| Ethical alignment and governance | Values-based design |\n| Data sourcing and legality | Lawful basis for all data |\n| Staffing | Design, build, and operate the model |\n| Performance and fairness | Measurement and maintenance |\n| Transparency and documentation | For audits, legal proceedings |\n| Post-deployment behavior | Monitoring and correction |\n| System disposal | End-of-life management |\n\n> **Accountability cannot be outsourced.** Even if an organization uses a third-party AI system, it must still accept a considerable share of accountability — the organization vetted and selected the third party.\n\n#### Legal Liability and Civil Exposure\n\nProprietary models can increase exposure to civil and regulatory liability — there is simply no one else to blame.\n\n**Sources of risk:**\n- Algorithmic bias or discrimination\n- Security vulnerabilities\n- Decisions affecting protected rights (housing, employment, credit)\n- Failure to meet industry-specific legal obligations\n- Deficiencies in explainability or documentation\n\n> **The EU AI Act and Proprietary Models:** Developers of high-risk proprietary models face stricter obligations including conformity assessments, technical documentation, logging and traceability, quality management, human oversight mechanisms, and incident reporting. In many cases, these cannot be delegated.\n\n#### Regulatory Classification as \"Provider\"\n\nUnder frameworks such as the EU AI Act, an organization that develops and deploys its own AI model is typically considered the **\"provider\"** (not just the \"deployer\"). This shifts the burden of compliance upstream.\n\n**Responsibilities as a provider:**\n\n| Responsibility | Description |\n|----------------|-------------|\n| Performing risk classification | Determine risk tier |\n| Conducting impact assessments | Document harms and mitigations |\n| Maintaining documentation | Comprehensive records |\n| Ensuring cybersecurity and robustness | Security controls |\n| Providing disclosures and notices | Transparency obligations |\n\n### 6.2 Data Rights, Quality, and Governance\n\nData is foundational to every proprietary AI model. Organizations must assume full control and legal authority over the data they use.\n\n#### Data Sourcing and Legal Entitlement\n\nUsing internal or scraped data without a proper legal basis can expose the company to significant risk. Organizations should:\n- Ensure data is collected with appropriate consent\n- Ensure usage rights include AI training\n- Document the lawful basis for its use\n\n> **Internal Data ≠ Safe Data:** Just because data is held internally doesn't mean it's legally usable. Repurposing data without proper notice or consent may violate data minimization and purpose limitation principles.\n\n#### Data Quality and Labeling Integrity\n\nLow-quality or biased data can undermine performance and increase risk.\n\n**Data-related controls:**\n- Enforce data validation\n- Document labeling methodology and **inter-rater reliability**\n- Log data transformations and preprocessing steps\n\n#### Data Governance Integration\n\nEssential practices:\n- Defining AI-specific data stewards\n- Tagging training and validation datasets in data catalogs\n- Applying data retention and deletion policies\n- Aligning with broader data ethics frameworks\n\n### 6.3 Model Explainability, Testing, and Monitoring\n\nProprietary models must be explainable to users, defendable to regulators, and maintainable over time.\n\n#### Explainability Obligations\n\nMany regulatory frameworks (GDPR, CPRA, EU AI Act) require that automated decisions affecting individuals be explainable.\n\n**Approaches for explainability:**\n\n| Approach | Description |\n|----------|-------------|\n| Inherently interpretable models | Decision trees, linear models |\n| Post hoc explanations | SHAP, LIME |\n| Counterfactual reasoning | Case-based explanations |\n| User-facing justifications | Plain, friendly, defendable language |\n\n#### Testing and Validation Requirements\n\nUnlike third-party systems, organizations developing proprietary models must rely on internal teams.\n\n**Testing regimes should include:**\n- Bias audits across protected attributes\n- Performance validation on real-world scenarios\n- Adversarial robustness testing (red teaming)\n- Human-in-the-loop failover testing\n\n#### Monitoring and Change Control\n\nProprietary models require ongoing **incident management** and a formal **change management** process. The entire practice of **IT service management** (including change and incident management) is essential.\n\n**Monitoring practices:**\n\n| Practice | Description |\n|----------|-------------|\n| Alert thresholds | Performance degradation |\n| Input/output logging | Traceability |\n| Drift detection | Data drift monitoring |\n| Regular retraining | Model updates |\n\n**Change control practices:**\n- Formal written change requests with stated business reasons\n- Approvals by stakeholders\n- Approved implementation and backout plans\n- **Configuration management** tools to record change details\n\n> **ISO Certification for IT Service Management:** Organizations may wish to pursue **ISO/IEC 20000** certification to attest to their IT service management. Note that ISO/IEC 20000 is not AI-specific but applies generally to IT.\n\n### 6.4 Intellectual Property and Competitive Risk\n\nProprietary AI models may provide strategic advantages but also introduce new risks around **intellectual property (IP)**, secrecy, and potential **reverse engineering** or misuse.\n\n#### IP Ownership and Protectability\n\nOrganizations should maintain an accurate catalog of their intellectual property.\n\n**Steps to consider:**\n- Whether the AI model was developed in-house, or through contractors/consultants\n- Whether the training data is proprietary, licensed, or open-source\n- Whether model weights and code are documented and secured\n- Whether trade secrets are adequately protected\n\n> **Patents vs. Trade Secrets:** AI models are often protected as trade secrets rather than patented, especially when model transparency might expose security or privacy vulnerabilities. This approach limits legal remedies in some cases.\n\n#### Reverse Engineering and Model Theft\n\nProprietary AI models may be vulnerable to reverse engineering by competitors or malicious actors. In-house developed systems are often less resilient than those produced by commercial software vendors.\n\n**Mitigation strategies:**\n\n| Strategy | Description |\n|----------|-------------|\n| Model APIs only | Deploy only APIs, not source code |\n| Robust access control | On all APIs |\n| Authentication, encryption, obfuscation | For model artifacts and API traffic |\n| Monitor for unauthorized access | Abuse and attack detection |\n| Red team and threat model | Often |\n| Audit usage logs | Rate-limit queries |\n| Privacy-preserving techniques | Differential privacy, response randomization, output watermarking |\n\n#### Model Misuse and Access Control\n\nRobust access control is essential:\n- Implement \"need to know\" and \"least privilege\" concepts\n- Apply role-based permissions for development and deployment\n- Log changes to model weights or logic\n- Utilize internal approval workflows for model updates\n- Implement revocation procedures for unused or compromised models\n- Periodically review privileged access to the AI system\n\n### 6.5 Resourcing and Organizational Readiness\n\nProprietary model development is resource-intensive and requires sustained investment. The art of estimating software projects has been a challenge since the 1960s — AI is no different.\n\n#### Technical Infrastructure\n\nCustom AI models typically require more demanding infrastructure than off-the-shelf solutions:\n\n| Need | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| GPU/TPU compute clusters | Specialized hardware |\n| Scalable data pipelines | Data processing at scale |\n| Secure model hosting environments | Protected deployment |\n| Sandboxes | Development and testing isolation |\n| Separate environments | Development vs. testing separation |\n\n#### Talent and Skill Development\n\nInternal development increases reliance on specialized talent (data scientists, ML engineers, model governance professionals). These skills are in short supply and priced accordingly.\n\n**Talent management ideas:**\n- Retention and compensation strategies for key technical staff\n- Cross-training between AI and compliance teams\n- Internal knowledge transfer and documentation\n- Establishment of an AI \"center of excellence\"\n\n> **The Risk of a Single Point of Failure:** When only one or two individuals understand how a proprietary model works, the organization is exposed to continuity and risk management challenges if those individuals leave.\n\n#### Governance and Ethical Review Capacity\n\nOrganizations developing proprietary AI models need additional governance workflows. Given that traditional software development and AI model development share DNA, a mature SDLC may be adaptable rather than requiring a from-scratch build.\n\n**Guardrails:**\n- Implement approval boards for internal models\n- Use expanded ethical review criteria\n- Integrate AI governance with legal, risk, and audit functions\n- Designate \"model owners\" as responsible for ongoing oversight\n\n---", "t_deployimpact": "## At a Glance\n\nThe pre-deployment due diligence the BOK names first in IV.B: performing or reviewing an impact assessment on the actual selected system in its actual deployment context — because impacts are use-and-system-specific and do not transfer automatically from design-phase work or another deployment. (v2.0.1 also listed 'identify applicable laws' here; v2.1 removed it.)\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nThe trap is staleness: a scenario offers an impact assessment done on a different model, an earlier version, or a different use case — the correct answer re-assesses against what is actually being deployed. Applicable-law analysis follows the use, not the technology.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Impact assessments attach to a model-in-context: new model, new version, or new use case → new assessment\n- Legal context still shapes the assessment (hiring, credit, EU users), but v2.1 tests the law itself in Domain II\n- Deployment-stage assessment covers operational, ethical, legal, and reputational risk — wider than the design-phase technical view\n- Outputs feed the go/no-go gate: unresolved high findings block deployment, not paper over it", "t_vendorterms": "## At a Glance\n\nThe contractual and licensing layer of deployment assessment: vendor agreements that allocate data-use rights, warranties, audit rights, update and incident obligations, and liability; open-source licenses whose conditions (attribution, copyleft, use restrictions) bind even without a vendor; and the heightened obligations that come with proprietary models whose training data and behavior cannot be independently verified.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nScenarios test whether terms were actually reviewed: an open-source model adopted without license review, a vendor contract with no bias warranty or audit right, a proprietary model whose claims were taken on faith. The correct answers demand AI-specific terms, not generic procurement paper.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Vendor agreements should secure: data-use limits, performance and bias warranties, model-change notification, incident SLAs, audit rights, exit portability\n- Open-source licenses bind: attribution, copyleft, and AI-specific use restrictions are enforceable conditions, not suggestions\n- Proprietary models mean deploying what you cannot inspect — compensate with contractual assurances and independent testing\n- Sub-tier terms flow through: your vendor's base-model and data licenses become your compliance problem\n- Terms review is a deployment gate, not a legal formality after the fact", "ch12": "## At a Glance\n\nBOK IV.C — governing AI in operation, the largest subdomain in the BOK: applying the operating policies (data governance, risk management, issue management, user training), continuous monitoring and maintenance, periodic assessments (audits, red teaming, threat modeling, security testing), documenting incidents and risks, forecasting secondary and unintended uses and downstream harms, running external communication plans, and executing deactivation and localization policies when regulation or performance demands it.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nThe single heaviest subdomain on the exam at 9–11 questions. Incident lifecycle order, audit independence, records discipline, truthful external communication, and governed retirement are all reliable scenario territory. 'Fix quietly and move on' and 'one-time signoff' are the planted wrong answers.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Risk register discipline: owner, likelihood, impact, treatment, residual risk — the living record auditors ask for first\n- Audits need independence, defined criteria, evidence, and tracked remediation; red teaming and threat modeling recur periodically, not once\n- Incident lifecycle: detect → contain → investigate root cause → remediate → disclose as required → institutionalize lessons\n- Records prove governance happened: decisions, tests, approvals, incidents, versions — retained per schedule (EU AI Act: 10 years for high-risk providers)\n- Forecast secondary/unintended uses and downstream harms — deployment approval attaches to a use case, not to the model in general\n- External communications must be accurate (FTC §5 exposure) and planned in advance; deactivation/localization policies predefine the exit\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Ongoing AI Operations\n\n## Performance Indicators\n\n- **Document incidents, issues, risks and post-market monitoring plans.**\n- **Establish external communication plans.**\n- **Create and implement a policy and controls to deactivate or localize an AI system as necessary** (e.g., due to regulatory requirements or performance issues).\n\n---\n\n---\n\n> [!source] AI Risk Management and Assurance\n\n## Performance Indicators\n\n- Identify and evaluate **key terms and risks in the vendor or licensing agreement**\n- Forecast and reduce **risks of secondary or unintended uses and downstream harms**\n- Conduct periodic activities to assess the AI system's **performance, reliability, and safety** (e.g., audits, red teaming, threat modeling, and security testing)\n- **Manage and document** incidents, issues, and risks\n- Collaborate with **cross-functional stakeholders** to understand why incidents arise (e.g., brittleness, lack of robustness, poor data quality, insufficient testing, model/data drift)\n- Make **public disclosures** to meet transparency obligations (e.g., technical documentation, instructions for use, post-market monitoring plans)\n\n---\n\n---\n\n> [!source] AI Risk Management and Assurance\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n1. **AI risk must be assessed across the entire lifecycle, not just at deployment.** AI systems evolve through retraining, user interactions, and environmental changes. Robust risk management spans design, data collection, mid-stage validation/testing, and post-deployment monitoring/forecasting.\n\n2. **Vendor and open-source AI components may obscure or introduce hidden contractual and technical risks.** Candidates should understand vendor vetting, agreement review/revision, transparency assessment, and alignment of licensing/support terms with risk tolerance and regulatory obligations.\n\n3. **Auditing and testing AI models requires a blend of technical and adversarial methods.** Performance audits, red teaming, threat modeling, threat hunting, and security testing reveal vulnerabilities like bias, prompt injection, model poisoning, data leakage, and system misuse. Know when each is most appropriate.\n\n4. **AI incident response must include documentation, cross-functional review, and feedback into governance processes.** Know the steps: detection → triage → communication → investigation → containment → eradication → recovery → post-incident review → documentation. Align with compliance and stakeholder expectations.\n\n5. **Public disclosure of AI risks and incidents is increasingly required by law.** Frameworks like the EU AI Act and Canada's AIDA require transparency for high-risk systems. Understand disclosure obligations, audience-tailored communications, and supporting documentation artifacts.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n> [!source] Ongoing AI Operations\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n1. **AI incidents, issues, and risks must be documented systematically** to support governance, accountability, and regulatory compliance. Records inform audits, identify patterns, and demonstrate due diligence.\n\n2. **External communication plans must provide accurate, accessible, and timely information** about AI system capabilities, limitations, and incidents. Clear policies, templates, designated spokespeople, and approved channels are essential.\n\n3. **AI systems should be retired through a structured, policy-driven process** that minimizes disruption and preserves essential records. Decommissioning involves more than turning off the model — it includes planning, notification, archiving, and risk mitigation.\n\n4. **Localization may be necessary** to comply with regional regulations, cultural norms, or data residency requirements. AI systems deployed globally may need deactivation, modification, or retraining for specific jurisdictions.\n\n5. **Retirement planning should be integrated into the AI lifecycle from the start.** Designing systems with the end in mind enables smoother transitions and aligns with responsible AI principles.\n\n---", "t_riskmgmt": "## At a Glance\n\nThe risk discipline applied to AI: identify risks across the lifecycle, analyze likelihood and impact, evaluate against defined risk appetite and tolerance, treat (mitigate, transfer, avoid, accept), track in a register with owners, and repeat — because AI risk moves. Forecasting adds horizon-scanning for emerging risks: new attack classes, regulatory shifts, capability jumps.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nVocabulary precision: appetite (willingness) vs. tolerance (acceptable deviation), inherent vs. residual risk. Treatment-selection scenarios reward proportionate, documented choices with named owners — and 'accept' is legitimate when documented and within tolerance.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Cycle: identify → analyze (likelihood × impact) → evaluate vs. appetite → treat → monitor → repeat\n- Treatments: mitigate (controls), transfer (insurance/contracts), avoid (don't do it), accept (documented, within tolerance)\n- Risk register: each risk with owner, scores, treatment, status, and residual risk — the living record auditors ask for first\n- Residual risk is what remains after controls — it must be explicitly accepted by someone with authority\n- AI-specific registers draw from taxonomies like NIST AI RMF categories, ISO/IEC 23894 risk sources, and MITRE ATLAS threat patterns\n- Forecasting: horizon-scan regulation, threats, and capability shifts — yesterday's register doesn't cover tomorrow's model\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] AI Risk Management and Assurance\n\n## 1. AI Risk Management and Forecasting\n\n### AI Risks vs. Traditional Enterprise Risk\n\nAI systems introduce **novel risks** not easily captured by traditional enterprise risk management (ERM). While conventional IT risk frameworks focus on infrastructure availability, data loss, or system failure, AI risks arise from:\n\n- **Probabilistic behavior** — outputs are probabilities, not certainties\n- **Evolving outputs** — models change via retraining, feedback loops, environmental inputs\n- **Third-party model dependencies** — vendor, open-source, or cloud AI services\n- **Societal-level impacts** — discrimination, misinformation, environmental harm\n\n> **Cybersecurity insight:** AI risks exist at or above **Layer 7 (Application Layer)** of the OSI model — they are socio-technical risks related to data use, not infrastructure logic.\n\n> **Assumption:** Organizations using AI are assumed to have mature cybersecurity and ERM practices. AI risk management is an **overlay** to standard practices.\n\n### Proactive Risk Identification and Categorization\n\nEffective AI risk management begins with clear **risk identification**. AI models evolve based on training data, feedback loops, environmental inputs, and retraining schedules — new risks can emerge even **after deployment**.\n\n> **Key principle:** A system secure today may not remain secure tomorrow. Claims of being \"risk-free\" are fleeting at best. Organizations must **proactively and aggressively** seek out risks — there is more **inherent risk** associated with AI than many other emerging technologies.\n\n**AI risk domains:**\n\n| Risk Domain | Examples |\n|-------------|----------|\n| **Data-related risks** | Biases, poor quality, leakage, prohibited cross-border data transfers, illegal/unethical use of personal data |\n| **Model-related risks** | Overfitting, lack of transparency, black box models, prompt injection attacks, model poisoning |\n| **Deployment risks** | Misuse in unintended contexts, automation surprises, performance drift |\n| **Operational risks** | Resource consumption, integration failure, cascading failures |\n| **Ethical and social risks** | Discrimination, loss of agency, manipulation of public opinion |\n| **Regulatory and legal risks** | Non-compliance with emerging AI-specific or general data protection laws |\n\n> **Case study:** A financial institution's third-party fraud detection AI had disproportionately high false-positive rates for foreign-born customers due to training set bias. Result: system recall and public apology.\n\n### Risk Management in AI Supply Chains\n\nMost organizations rely on vendors, open-source models, or cloud AI services. These create **third-party dependencies** that must be proactively managed.\n\n**Essential TPRM (Third-Party Risk Management) practice:** Conduct thorough upfront vendor assessment → use findings to inform contract negotiations.\n\n**Key terms to identify and evaluate in agreements:**\n\n- **Licensing terms** — Open-source components (GPL, AGPL) may impose code disclosure or usage limitations\n- **Data use restrictions** — Data ownership, permitted uses, whether vendor can retain/use customer data for retraining\n- **Model transparency and explainability** — Disclosures on training data sources, model lineage, performance evaluation, audit mechanisms\n- **Indemnity and liability** — Vendors often disclaim responsibility for harmful outputs; negotiate liability clauses for recourse\n- **Cyber liability insurance** — Require vendors to carry insurance, especially smaller vendors\n- **Security and compliance posture** — Certifications (ISO/IEC 27001, ISO/IEC 42001, SOC 2 Type II), secure development practices\n\n> **Case study:** An open-source language model under a non-commercial license was integrated into a for-profit chatbot platform, exposing the company to IP infringement claims.\n\n### Common Contract Clauses to Scrutinize (Red Flags)\n\n| Red Flag Clause | Risk |\n|-----------------|------|\n| Data sent to the model becomes **vendor property** | Loss of data ownership |\n| Data may be **retained for model improvement** | Privacy, confidentiality concerns |\n| Data may be **sent to third parties** to perform services | Uncontrolled data sharing |\n| Output is **not warranted** to be accurate or lawful | No vendor accountability |\n| Customer grants **irrevocable license** to use feedback for future products | Loss of IP rights |\n\n### Forecasting Secondary and Downstream Harms\n\n> **Key concept:** Many AI consequences become apparent only when the system is used at scale or in unanticipated contexts.\n\n**Secondary use** — AI outputs used for purposes different from the original intent, often without institutional awareness/control:\n\n- **Input repurposing** — AI-generated content used as training data elsewhere\n- **System coupling** — Chaining outputs from one model into another system\n- **Malicious adaptation** — Repurposing benign tools for harmful uses (e.g., image upscalers → deepfakes)\n\n**Downstream harms** — consequences felt by individuals, communities, or systems not initially considered:\n\n| Type | Example |\n|------|---------|\n| **Societal** | Discriminatory hiring from algorithmic resume screening |\n| **Environmental** | Massive compute demands → excessive carbon emissions |\n| **Political** | LLMs generating misinformation during elections |\n\n**Tools for mapping downstream harm:**\n- Stakeholder impact mapping\n- Recursive scenario modeling\n- External consultation panels and focus groups\n- Misuse testing and red teaming\n\n> **Principle:** Governance frameworks should extend risk reviews beyond direct users to include stakeholders affected by **second- and third-order effects**.\n\n> **Case study:** An AI model trained for academic essays was later used by students to cheat on exams, triggering plagiarism scandals.\n\n### Risk Forecasting Techniques\n\nForecasting AI risk uses predictive modeling to see how systems behave under various pressures:\n\n| Technique | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **Model drift detection** | **Data drift** (input distribution shift) and **concept drift** (target variable changes) degrade performance, increase bias/error |\n| **Anticipating feedback loops** | Self-reinforcing loops (e.g., recommendation engines, predictive policing) can amplify bias or cause runaway behavior |\n| **Simulation** | Digital twins, synthetic data, simulated user environments anticipate real-world and adversarial behavior |\n\n> **Statistical tools:** KS tests and KL divergence can trigger retraining or alerts when drift thresholds are crossed.\n\n> **Case study:** Predictive policing sent more officers to certain neighborhoods → more arrests → reinforced the area as \"high-risk\" in retrained data.\n\n### Embedding Risk Management into Governance Structures\n\nAI risk management must be integrated into **every phase of the development lifecycle**:\n\n| Phase | Risk Management Activities |\n|-------|---------------------------|\n| **Initiation** | Define use case risk level; consult cross-functional stakeholders |\n| **Design** | Model risk assessments, harm forecasting, identify training data provenance |\n| **Development** | Use explainable, testable algorithms; track lineage |\n| **Testing** | Adversarial testing, red teaming, misuse potential evaluation |\n| **Deployment** | Rollback procedures, incident thresholds |\n| **Post-deployment** | Monitor drift, audit logs, user feedback, incident reports |\n\n> **Practice:** Include AI-related risks in the ERM risk register; use the **same risk management process** for all risks, including AI.\n\n**Risk-related roles:**\n\n| Role | Responsibility |\n|------|---------------|\n| **AI Risk Officer / Chief Risk Officer** | Enterprise-level risk posture |\n| **Business Owners / Senior Executives** | Risk treatment decisions |\n| **System Owners** | Individual models |\n| **Audit Leads** | Testing and review cadence |\n| **DPO / Legal Counsel** | Data protection, IP, AI regulatory compliance |\n\n**Governance tooling:**\n- NIST AI Risk Management Framework (AI RMF)\n- ISO/IEC 42001 (AI Management System Standard)\n- Risk-focused model cards\n- Harm-forecasting templates\n- Automated risk detection in MLOps pipelines\n\n### Documentation and Assurance\n\nDocumentation supports internal risk communication, external audits, incident investigation, model troubleshooting, and regulatory reviews.\n\n**Required documentation artifacts:**\n- AI risk register (dynamic, living business record)\n- Third-party model assessments\n- Data provenance and consent records\n- Data lineage records\n- Impact assessments (Algorithmic Impact Assessment, DPIA)\n- Risk management records (treatment decisions, remediation plans, residual risk justifications)\n- Model and system cards\n\n**Model assurance review points:**\n- Known failure modes\n- How risks are mitigated and residual risks\n- Who is accountable for each risk class\n- How frequently risk controls are tested\n- How failures/harms are investigated, communicated, and corrected\n\n### Regulatory Considerations\n\n| Regulation | Key Risk Management Requirements |\n|------------|--------------------------------|\n| **EU AI Act** | Risk assessments, logs, conformity assessments for high-risk AI systems |\n| **Canada AIDA** | Impact assessments, public disclosure for high-impact AI (not yet enacted) |\n| **U.S. Executive Order on AI (2025)** | Agencies ensure AI systems are \"free from ideological bias or engineered social agendas\" |\n| **China Interim Measures for Generative AI** | Classification and oversight based on risk/category; mandatory labeling of AI-generated content |\n\n> **Consequence:** Organizations that fail to forecast and manage risk may face penalties, injunctions, or civil liability.\n\n---", "t_audit": "## At a Glance\n\nVerification with independence: internal audits (independent of the AI's builders), external audits and certifications (ISO/IEC 42001 for management systems), algorithmic audits targeting bias or specific harms, and regulator-facing conformity evidence. What makes an audit real: defined criteria, qualified independent auditors, evidence-based findings, and tracked remediation.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nIndependence is the tested principle — the team that built the model doesn't audit it. Know the assurance stack (self-assessment < internal audit < external certification) and that audits verify against criteria (a framework, standard, or regulation), not vibes.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Audit anatomy: defined scope and criteria → independent qualified auditors → evidence collection → findings → remediation tracking → closure\n- Independence requirement: organizational separation from the audited system's developers and operators\n- Certification targets: ISO/IEC 42001 (AI management system) is the certifiable standard; NYC LL144 mandates independent bias audits for hiring tools\n- Algorithmic audits probe specific properties: disparate impact, robustness, privacy leakage — with quantitative evidence\n- Audit trails depend on earlier discipline: no logs, no lineage, no documentation → nothing to audit — governance is audit-enablement\n- Findings feed the risk register and corrective-action process; an unremediated finding is a standing risk acceptance\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] AI Risk Management and Assurance\n\n## 2. AI System Audits and Testing\n\n### Performance Audits and Model Validation\n\n**Performance audits** measure how effectively an AI system fulfills its intended purpose across accuracy, fairness, robustness, performance, and generalization. They validate or refute development claims and detect degradation over time.\n\n> **Note:** Continuous monitoring should detect degradation **earlier** than periodic audits would.\n\n**Acceptable performance criteria (established in design phase):**\n\n| Criterion | Example |\n|-----------|---------|\n| **Business alignment** | Fraud detection accuracy |\n| **Context-sensitive** | False-negative tolerance in medical diagnosis |\n| **Stakeholder-informed** | Transparent and explicit opt-in |\n| **Fairness and lack of bias** | Fairness across demographic groups |\n| **Precision and confidence** | Classification accuracy, consistent decision-making |\n| **Robustness and resilience** | Acceptable performance under load and with edge cases |\n\n> **Key insight:** AI models often behave **differently in production** than in controlled test environments. When production systems fail criteria, test the same models in test environments to understand the difference.\n\n> **Case study:** A credit scoring model met accuracy thresholds during development but showed performance disparities for rural applicants after deployment, prompting feature reevaluation and retraining.\n\n### Red Teaming for Adversarial Robustness\n\n**Red teaming** — structured process for simulating adversarial attacks and misuse scenarios against a target system. Essential for identifying overlooked vulnerabilities and ensuring resilience under worst-case conditions.\n\n**Types of red team assessments for AI:**\n\n| Attack Type | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| **Prompt injection / jailbreak testing** | Bypassing safety mechanisms in LLMs |\n| **Evasion attacks** | Modifying inputs to cause misclassification |\n| **Poisoning attacks** | Injecting corrupt data into training/fine-tuning pipelines |\n| **Functionality misuse** | Using tools for unintended/unethical purposes |\n| **Social engineering testing** | Phishing via chatbots |\n\n> **Scope:** Red teaming should evaluate the broader AI ecosystem — supporting infrastructure, APIs, integration layers, data pipelines — not just model parameters.\n\n**Red team composition:**\n- **Domain experts** — understand AI system and common threats\n- **Policy experts** — understand misuse scenarios\n- **External testers** — bring objectivity\n\n> Red teaming targets both the AI systems themselves **and the personnel** who build, manage, and use them.\n\n### Threat Modeling for AI-Specific Attack Surfaces\n\nTraditional threat modeling must be extended for AI-specific attack surfaces: model inversion, training data leakage, adversarial attacks.\n\n**STRIDE adapted for AI:**\n\n| STRIDE Category | AI Threat |\n|-----------------|-----------|\n| **Spoofing** | Prompt injection |\n| **Tampering** | Data poisoning, prompt injection |\n| **Repudiation** | — |\n| **Information Disclosure** | Model theft |\n| **Denial of Service** | — |\n| **Elevation of Privilege** | Unauthorized API chaining |\n\n**Threat modeling methodologies:**\n\n| Methodology | Purpose |\n|-------------|---------|\n| **MITRE ATLAS** | Knowledge base for adversarial threats against AI |\n| **Kill Chain Analysis** | Mapping attacker steps from reconnaissance to exploitation |\n| **Attack Trees** | Visualizing paths to compromise |\n| **TARA** | Threat Analysis and Risk Assessment, adapted for AI |\n| **PASTA** | Process for Attack Simulation and Threat Analysis, extended for AI misuse |\n\n> **Threat hunting** — proactive search for signs of intrusions, intruders, abuse, and indicators of compromise. **Don't wait for incidents** — proactively look for signs of attack.\n\n> **Case study:** A public-facing generative AI model was targeted for training data extraction via repeated sampling with targeted prompts. Threat modeling revealed this vulnerability, prompting throttling and watermarking controls.\n\n### Threat Modeling Across the Lifecycle\n\n| Phase | Focus |\n|-------|-------|\n| **Design phase** | Identify vulnerable features and components |\n| **Pre-deployment phase** | Test implemented defenses |\n| **Post-deployment phase** | Adapt to evolving threats and contexts |\n\n### Security Testing for Confidentiality and Integrity\n\nAudits must extend beyond performance to include data integrity, user privacy, and system confidentiality.\n\n**AI-specific security testing techniques:**\n\n| Technique | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **Penetration testing** | Simulate intrusion attempts into AI systems, pipelines, APIs |\n| **Model extraction testing** | Assess ease of reverse-engineering/copying a model |\n| **Inference attacks** | Determine how easily training data can be inferred from outputs |\n| **Membership inference** | Test whether attacker can guess if specific data was in training set |\n| **Re-identification testing** | Validate anonymization; attempt to re-identify training data |\n| **Adversarial robustness testing** | Examine model behavior under adversarial perturbations/input manipulations |\n\n**CI/CD pipeline safeguards:**\n\n| Safeguard | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| Check-out/check-in monitoring | Track all code/model access |\n| Need-to-know access | Restrict sensitive code access |\n| Code signing and version control | Ensure model artifact integrity |\n| Containerization and sandboxing | Isolate training environments |\n| Policy-based access control | Control retraining triggers |\n| Audit logging | Track data access and model deployment events |\n\n> **Key principle:** Security testing must encompass the **entire AI tech stack** — physical security, networks, OS, DBMS, application servers, storage, network services, etc.\n\n> **Case study:** An e-commerce company discovered a rogue employee deployed an unapproved AI model. Security testing revealed missing integrity checks and weak access controls in CI/CD.\n\n### Regulatory and Compliance Audits\n\nPeriodic audits confirm compliance with applicable laws, agreements, policies, and ethical standards — providing traceability, accountability, and defensibility.\n\n**Key compliance audit objectives:**\n- Verify adherence to AI risk management frameworks (NIST AI RMF, ISO/IEC 42001)\n- Evaluate required controls (bias mitigation, impact assessments)\n- Confirm documentation existence and quality (model cards, data sheets, usage policies)\n- Confirm business records (risk register, treatment decisions, periodic maintenance, opt-out requests)\n- Confirm proper application of opt-out rights\n- Confirm presence and effectiveness of human-in-the-loop safeguards\n\n**Audit requirements by jurisdiction:**\n\n| Regulation | Requirement |\n|------------|-------------|\n| **EU AI Act** | Quality management system, conformity assessments, post-market monitoring for high-risk AI |\n| **Canada AIDA** | Impact assessments, transparency measures (not yet enacted) |\n| **U.S. Federal** | AI governance frameworks via Executive Orders and OMB guidance |\n\n**Audit preparation steps:**\n- Maintain a **single source of truth** for documentation\n- Ensure **audit logs** are immutable and timestamped\n- Practice **dry runs** or internal audit simulations\n- Assign **responsible owners** for each compliance area\n- Coach responsible owners on **proper conduct** with auditors\n\n> **Note:** Some regulations (e.g., EU AI Act) require audits by qualified **external parties** (Notified Bodies), not internal audit staff.\n\n### Real-Time Testing and Continuous Monitoring\n\nPeriodic assessments are essential but **insufficient**. Implement continuous assurance mechanisms:\n\n| Practice | Purpose |\n|----------|---------|\n| **Latency and throughput metrics** | Detect performance issues |\n| **Drift detection** | Track input and output distribution shifts |\n| **Anomaly detection** | Identify rare or unexpected behaviors |\n| **User feedback loops** | Surface safety and ethical issues |\n| **Incident alerts** | Real-time awareness of incidents |\n| **Incident alerting thresholds** | Automated remediation triggers |\n| **Security scans** | Proactively identify new vulnerabilities |\n| **Threat hunting** | Proactively detect abuse and intrusion |\n\n> **Case study:** A healthcare AI monitoring vital signs flagged numerous false positives. Continuous monitoring revealed input drift from a firmware update in a medical device, prompting retraining.\n\n### Integrating Testing into the AI Lifecycle\n\nAudits test not only the AI system but also the **policies, business processes, and tools** used to design, build, test, deploy, and monitor it.\n\n| Phase | Testing/Audit Activities |\n|-------|-------------------------|\n| **Design** | Design reviews, risk assessments, threat modeling, red teaming scenario planning |\n| **Development** | Unit tests for model code, adversarial test sets, repository access controls, feature change approvals |\n| **Pre-deployment** | End-to-end performance audits, regulatory reviews |\n| **Deployment** | Canary testing, rollback safeguards |\n| **Post-deployment** | Continuous monitoring, incident response planning and execution |\n| **Retirement** | Data archival, model decommissioning, disposal, migration (see [[Ongoing AI Operations]]) |\n\n**Gate model for testing:**\n\n| Gate | Criteria |\n|------|----------|\n| **Gate 1** | Risk assessment and design review complete → approve development start |\n| **Gate 2** | Adversarial testing and bias audit passed → promote to production |\n| **Gate 3** | Operational monitoring and rollback plan validated |\n\n### AI Audit Roles and Responsibilities\n\n| Role | Responsibility |\n|------|---------------|\n| **AI Risk Officer** | Oversees assurance strategy across systems |\n| **Model Owner** | System-specific testing results |\n| **Internal Audit Lead** | Coordinates internal and third-party audit activities |\n| **Red Team Coordinator** | Designs and executes adversarial evaluations |\n| **Security Engineer** | Penetration, integrity testing, threat modeling |\n| **Ethics Advisor** | Social harm evaluations |\n\n> **Principle:** Testing efforts must not be siloed within technical teams alone — they must reflect **cross-functional governance**.\n\n### Audit Documentation and Evidence Retention\n\n**Records retention schedule** should specify retention periods for each business record type. Artifacts include:\n\n- Audit charter (defines audit program, roles, responsibilities)\n- Audit reports (findings, severity levels, recommendations)\n- Red teaming logs and adversarial scenarios tested\n- Threat models with attack paths and mitigations\n- Threat modeling records\n- Testing protocols and reproducible test sets\n- Model version histories and deployment metadata\n- Evidence of remediation actions taken\n\n> Storage must meet regulatory, legal, and ethical obligations: access controls, retention schedules, encryption, data minimization. **Consult legal counsel** for retention requirements.\n\n### Audit and Testing Tooling and Automation\n\nManual testing does not scale. Invest in automation:\n\n| Category | Tools/Examples |\n|----------|---------------|\n| **Bias audit frameworks** | Aequitas, Fairlearn |\n| **Red teaming platforms** | Microsoft Azure AI Red Team tooling |\n| **Security scanners** | AI development, testing, and production environments |\n| **Model card/data sheet generators** | Automated documentation |\n| **Monitoring dashboards** | Evidently AI, Arize, WhyLabs |\n\n---", "t_incident": "## At a Glance\n\nWhen AI goes wrong in the world: detection channels (monitoring, user reports, audits, media), containment (rollback, kill switches, scope reduction), root-cause investigation, remediation, and the disclosure matrix — EU AI Act serious-incident reports, personal-data breach notifications, sector regulators, affected individuals — followed by institutionalizing the lessons.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nLifecycle-order questions and disclosure triggers: a high-risk system's serious incident goes to market surveillance authorities; a training-data breach starts GDPR's 72-hour clock. 'Fix quietly and move on' is always the wrong answer.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Lifecycle: detect → contain → investigate root cause → remediate → disclose as required → capture lessons → update controls\n- Detection is multi-channel: automated monitoring, user reports, internal escalation, external researchers, media — design intake for all of them\n- Containment options pre-planned: rollback to prior version, disable features, constrain scope, full shutdown — with authority to invoke defined\n- EU AI Act: providers of high-risk systems report serious incidents to authorities (15 days standard, faster for the gravest cases)\n- Parallel clocks: GDPR breach notification (72 hours to DPA), sector regulators, contractual notice duties — one incident, many obligations\n- Root cause over blame: fixes address systemic causes (data, process, design), and post-incident reviews update the risk register\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] AI Risk Management and Assurance\n\n## 3. Incident Management and Disclosures\n\n### Identifying and Managing AI Incidents\n\n**AI incident** — any unexpected behavior, outcome, or interaction involving an AI system that causes, or has potential to cause, harm, operational disruption, or regulatory concern.\n\n**Examples of AI incidents:**\n- Recommendation system surfacing harmful/illegal content\n- Chatbot providing incorrect medical advice\n- Classification model mislabeling sensitive demographic attributes\n- Autonomous system failing safely under edge case conditions\n- Generative model producing IP-infringing, toxic, or factually incorrect outputs\n- Unexpected performance degradation due to concept drift or environmental changes\n\n> **Best practice:** Enact a **single incident response framework** encompassing IT, cybersecurity, privacy, and AI-related incidents.\n\n**Incident response lifecycle:**\n\n| Step | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| **1. Detection** | Identify anomalous/harmful behavior via monitoring, alerts, user reports |\n| **2. Triage** | Assess severity, scope, potential impact |\n| **3. Communication** | Inform internal and external stakeholders |\n| **4. Investigation** | Determine root cause, including model and data lineage |\n| **5. Containment** | Isolate or disable affected system/component; prevent spread |\n| **6. Eradication** | Corrective actions: model retraining, data updates, policy changes |\n| **7. Recovery** | Restore system to pre-incident state (with changes to prevent recurrence) |\n| **8. After-Action Review (AAR)** | Root cause analysis for effective long-term fixes |\n| **9. Documentation** | Update policies, logs, audit trails |\n\n> **Case study:** A generative AI customer support assistant gave incorrect return instructions due to a prompt leak from a test environment. Triage identified the misrouted input stream. Model was suspended, retrained, and revalidated with updated input filters.\n\n### Not All Incidents Are Failures\n\nSome incidents stem from **misaligned objectives**, not bugs.\n\n> **Example:** A model trained to maximize engagement may inadvertently promote sensationalist or polarizing content. Incident response must consider **intent vs. outcome**.\n\n### Categorizing and Escalating Incidents\n\n**Severity scale:**\n\n| Level | Description |\n|-------|-------------|\n| **Level 1 — Critical** | Widespread harm, regulatory violation, or systemic failure |\n| **Level 2 — Major** | Significant user impact or model breakdown in core function |\n| **Level 3 — Moderate** | Isolated issue with limited exposure |\n| **Level 4 — Minor** | No external impact; handled by internal monitoring |\n\n**Escalation options for higher-severity incidents:**\n- Executive notifications and periodic updates\n- External regulator notification\n- Legal hold of related artifacts\n- Protection of records through attorney-client privilege\n- Model suspension or rollback\n\n> Automated workflows can be configured to trigger escalation rules based on incident metadata.\n\n### Documenting Incidents, Issues, and Risks\n\n> **Essential practice:** Document **all** events, incidents, issues, and risks — enables traceability, regulatory readiness, trend analysis, and proactive mitigation.\n\n**Incident documentation fields:**\n- Date/time of detection\n- Detection source (monitoring system, user report, audit)\n- System, service, or model affected\n- Description of behavior and observed consequences\n- Severity classification\n- Immediate response actions taken (and by whom)\n- Root cause and contributing factors\n- Mitigation measures and verification steps\n- Status and resolution date\n\n> **Standardized taxonomies** (incident type, system component, harm type) facilitate analysis, cross-team understanding, metrics, and reporting.\n\n**Bidirectional risk-incident linking:**\n- When an incident reveals previously unconsidered risks → record in **risk register**\n- Each incident should trace to relevant risks **already existing** in the register when the incident occurred\n\n### Residual Risk After Remediation\n\nRisks are rarely eliminated entirely. When documenting an incident, note:\n- Whether the applied fix **reduces or eliminates** the risk\n- Whether **residual risk** remains within acceptable thresholds\n\n### Collaborating with Stakeholders to Understand Root Causes\n\nUnderstanding AI incidents requires a **multidisciplinary approach**. Root causes may involve technical design, policy choices, and contextual misunderstandings.\n\n**Common root causes of AI incidents:**\n\n| Root Cause | Description |\n|------------|-------------|\n| **Model brittleness** | Inability to handle edge cases or unanticipated inputs |\n| **Lack of robustness** | Small perturbations cause unpredictable outputs |\n| **Insufficient/poor-quality training data** | Garbage in, garbage out |\n| **Misaligned objectives** | Optimizing engagement vs. user safety |\n| **Overreliance on automation / absent human oversight** | Inadequate human-in-the-loop |\n| **Inadequate testing** | Especially under adversarial or real-world conditions |\n| **Model or data drift** | Degradation over time |\n| **Failures in prompt design** | For generative AI systems |\n\n**Post-incident review stakeholders:**\n\n| Stakeholder | Role in Review |\n|-------------|---------------|\n| **Data scientists** | Explain model behavior and training data lineage |\n| **Software engineers** | Investigate system logs and integration layers |\n| **Cybersecurity** | Understand/explain related vulnerabilities and threats |\n| **Privacy** | Evaluate data subject rights failures |\n| **Ethics and legal advisors** | Evaluate regulatory and societal impacts |\n| **Operations staff** | Identify downstream business/customer effects |\n| **End users** (where appropriate) | Provide contextual understanding of harm |\n| **External experts** | Bring objectivity |\n\n> **Case study:** Predictive maintenance model failed to flag a major component failure. Cross-functional review revealed the model was trained only on normal operating conditions, creating a blind spot for rare failure modes.\n\n### Avoiding a Culture of Blame\n\n> The goal of incident analysis is to **learn and improve**, not to identify and punish. Organizations that foster **psychological safety** in postmortem reviews encourage open sharing and process improvement.\n\n### Transparency and Public Disclosures\n\nTransparency is a foundational principle of AI governance — and increasingly a **regulatory requirement**.\n\n**Disclosure triggers (vary by law and context):**\n- High-risk AI system deployment\n- Material performance degradation\n- AI system safety failures\n- Significant data breaches affecting AI inputs/outputs\n- Inaccurate/misleading outputs affecting public health or safety\n- System behavior outside intended use cases\n\n**Regulations requiring transparency:**\n\n| Regulation | Disclosure Requirements |\n|------------|------------------------|\n| **EU AI Act** | Technical documentation, usage instructions, user complaints, performance data, risks/remediation, post-market monitoring plans |\n| **Canada AIDA** | Public disclosure of purpose and function for high-impact AI (not yet enacted) |\n| **California CPRA / U.S. FTC** | Explain automated decision-making in specific contexts |\n| **Brazil LGPD** | Right to explanation and review of AI-driven decisions |\n\n**Disclosure audiences and tailoring:**\n\n| Audience | Content |\n|----------|---------|\n| **Users and customers** | Simple, actionable explanations of what occurred and actions taken |\n| **Regulators** | Technical documentation, timelines, impact assessments, remedial actions |\n| **Partners and vendors** | System-level implications and interface updates |\n| **Internal stakeholders** | Root cause analysis, revised policies, mitigation details, training updates |\n\n**Proactive disclosure preparation:**\n- Public statement and FAQ templates\n- Timeline graphic templates\n- Plain-language model behavior explanations\n- Contact information for inquiries\n\n> **Case study:** A social media company publicly disclosed that its content moderation AI failed to detect a widely disseminated hate speech campaign, triggering reputational damage and increased regulatory scrutiny.\n\n### Organizational Preparedness and Readiness\n\n**Incident response program components:**\n\n| Component | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **AI Incident Response Plan (IRP)** | Standalone or (ideally) integrated with overall IT, cybersecurity, and privacy IRP |\n| **Playbooks** | Common AI-related incident types |\n| **Testing and simulation** | Response scenario exercises |\n| **Integrated response** | Coordination with cybersecurity and legal teams |\n| **Training programs** | Employee training on incident detection and reporting |\n\n> **Simulation exercises** build \"muscle memory\" — a moderator guides responders through a developing incident scenario so they practice procedures before a real incident.\n\n**Simulation scenarios:**\n- Generative model outputs copyrighted material\n- Predictive model fails due to drifted input feature\n- Red teaming reveals security vulnerability in a foundation model\n- Threat hunters find an outside attacker compromised an AI system\n- A regulator requests all logs from a recent incident\n\n> **Cybersecurity insurance:** Policies may stipulate specific terms for incident response, including controls like MFA, detailed audit logging, etc.\n\n---", "t_records": "## At a Glance\n\nThe evidentiary layer of AI governance: business records proving what was decided, tested, approved, monitored, and fixed — kept per retention schedules that balance legal obligations (EU AI Act documentation retention, sector rules, litigation holds) against minimization. If governance happened but nobody documented it, auditors and regulators treat it as not having happened.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nRetention-tension questions (keep for accountability vs. delete for minimization → follow the documented schedule serving both) and EU AI Act specifics: high-risk providers keep technical documentation and logs — 10 years is the anchor number.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Record set: design decisions, data provenance, test results, approvals, monitoring outputs, incidents, model versions, communications\n- Retention schedules are policy: defined periods per record type, driven by regulation, litigation exposure, and business need\n- EU AI Act: high-risk providers retain technical documentation 10 years after market placement; deployers retain logs per obligations\n- Logs must be useful: timestamped, attributable, tamper-resistant, and retrievable — a log nobody can query is not a control\n- Litigation holds override routine deletion the moment litigation is reasonably anticipated\n- Records serve minimization too: documented deletion per schedule is defensible; ad-hoc deletion looks like spoliation\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Ongoing AI Operations\n\n## 1. Managing Business Records\n\nBusiness records tell the story of an organization's operations and are essential for AI governance — ensuring accountability, regulatory compliance, and continuous improvement. This section covers the systematic documentation of incidents, issues, risks, decisions, and post-market monitoring activities.\n\n### 1.1 Incident Documentation and Tracking\n\nIncident records document events that deviate from intended system behavior. **Incident management** is covered fully in [[AI Risk Management and Assurance]].\n\n**Types of incidents to record in detail:**\n\n| Type | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| **Model performance failures** | Degradation in accuracy or reliability |\n| **Ethical/regulatory violations** | Biased outputs, unfair decisions, harmful recommendations |\n| **Security breaches** | Adversarial inputs, model extraction, model poisoning, prompt injection attacks |\n| **System downtime** | AI system service disruptions, particularly from AI components |\n| **Unexpected use cases** | Users applying the AI system in unintended ways |\n\n> **Context matters:** A poorly ranked search result may be a mere annoyance, but a misclassification in a healthcare diagnostic tool could be life-threatening. Classifying an event as an \"incident\" depends on both severity and context.\n\n**Example:** An AI-powered applicant tracking system systematically ranks older applicants lower, jeopardizing compliance with NYC Local Law 144 (requiring annual bias audits). The incident record includes logs from affected queries, training data distribution analysis, internal review findings, and retraining notes.\n\n### 1.2 Issue Reporting and Lifecycle Management\n\n**Issues** reflect undesirable system behavior, user complaints, or technical shortcomings that do not rise to the level of an incident — but still require formal attention and decisions.\n\n**Examples of issues:**\n- Minor UI/UX inconsistencies\n- Sporadic misclassifications\n- Feedback from internal QA testers\n- Suggestions and complaints from user community forums\n\n**Issue lifecycle:**\n\n| Stage | Description |\n|-------|-------------|\n| **Intake** | Submission through designated channels |\n| **Categorization** | Tagging by severity, area, and AI component |\n| **Assignment** | Routing to responsible teams |\n| **Escalation** | Routing to others when SLAs are violated |\n| **Resolution** | Implementation of fixes or improvements |\n| **Closure** | Documenting resolution and notifying stakeholders |\n\n> Mature organizations track issues within CI/CD pipelines and retraining workflows, enabling automated alerts when repeated issues signal model degradation.\n\n### 1.3 Risk Documentation and Mitigation Tracking\n\nAI-related risks must be documented in a **risk register**, ideally within an AI governance platform or GRC tool.\n\n**AI-related risks include:**\n- Model drift\n- Data drift\n- Bias and fairness risks\n- Security vulnerabilities\n- Legal and compliance risks\n- Misuse or dual-use concerns\n\n**Typical risk register record includes:**\n\n| Field | Description |\n|-------|-------------|\n| System/application name | Which AI system the risk applies to |\n| Risk description | Nature of the risk |\n| Associated use cases | Where the risk could manifest |\n| Likelihood and impact scores | Quantitative assessment |\n| Risk treatment decision | Including decision-maker |\n| Mitigation strategy | When applicable |\n| Residual risk post-mitigation | Remaining risk after controls |\n| Risk owner and review schedule | Accountability and cadence |\n\n**Example:** A bank's AI loan application system includes a risk entry for racial bias due to proxy variables in historical data. The record includes fairness audits, post-processing techniques, and periodic review dates.\n\n### 1.4 Post-market Monitoring Plans\n\n**Post-market monitoring** identifies real-world issues that pre-deployment testing may have overlooked.\n\n**Goals:**\n- Validate continued model accuracy, robustness, and fairness\n- Detect performance degradation from data and environmental changes\n- Capture user feedback and behavioral adaptations\n- Support iterative retraining, risk assessments, and governance updates\n\n**Components of a post-market monitoring plan:**\n\n| Component | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **Performance metrics monitoring** | Continuous observation of KPIs (accuracy, F1 score, latency) across user segments |\n| **Bias and fairness audits** | Scheduled reviews using demographic parity, equal opportunity, or counterfactual fairness tests |\n| **Drift detection** | Monitoring input/output distributions using PSI or Kolmogorov–Smirnov tests |\n| **Data flow and transfer reviews** | Periodic reviews of data transfers into/out of the model; **DLP tooling** and **cross-border data transfer** monitoring |\n| **User feedback channels** | Mechanisms integrated into the UI for real-time complaints or suggestions |\n| **Incident and issue integration** | Feeding detected anomalies into incident/issue tracking for resolution |\n\n**Regulatory examples:**\n- **EU AI Act:** Requires high-risk AI systems to implement post-market monitoring with logging, reporting, and human oversight\n- **U.S. Algorithmic Accountability Act** *(proposed):* Calls for impact assessments and continuous evaluation of deployed systems\n\n### 1.5 Governance and Accountability Structures\n\nAccountability for record keeping should be formally documented across all roles.\n\n**Typical roles and responsibilities:**\n\n| Role | Responsibility |\n|------|----------------|\n| **System developers** | Maintain records of system design, training data provenance, data lineage, test plans, results, model versioning |\n| **Product managers** | Ensure user-facing documentation is accurate and up to date |\n| **Compliance officers** | Verify record keeping aligns with legal and regulatory mandates |\n| **Risk owners** | Monitor open risks, treatment decisions, and oversee mitigation plans |\n| **Workflow/process owners** | Maintain records of AI system development reviews and approvals |\n\n#### Retention and Disposal Policies\n\nRecords should be included in the organization's **records retention schedule** (typically 3–7 years, depending on jurisdiction).\n\n**Key policies to define:**\n- **Retention periods:** Based on system type and risk level\n- **Access controls:** Only authorized personnel; most records should be **immutable** to prevent tampering\n- **Archival procedures:** Secure, tamper-evident formats for long-term preservation\n- **Deletion protocols:** Wiping records at end-of-life, commensurate with risk and data sensitivity\n\n### 1.6 Tools and Technologies for Record Management\n\n**Key operational logs:**\n\n| Log Type | Content |\n|----------|---------|\n| **Prediction logs** | Model inputs and outputs for each query |\n| **Error logs** | Failures or exceptions during inference or data processing |\n| **Usage logs** | Frequency and context of system access |\n\n**Emerging AI governance platforms** offering record keeping and compliance features:\n- Model version control and lineage\n- Data provenance and lineage\n- Risk register and compliance integration\n- Post-deployment monitoring dashboards\n- Incident response workflows\n\n> **Example platforms:** Fiddler AI, Arthur.ai, Credo AI, Microsoft Responsible AI Dashboard.\n\n**Blockchain-inspired solutions** (append-only logs, cryptographic hashing) are gaining attention for ensuring immutability, traceability, and **nonrepudiation** in AI audit trails.\n\n### 1.7 Legal and Regulatory Considerations\n\n**Key frameworks driving recordkeeping:**\n\n| Framework | Requirement |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **EU AI Act** | High-risk systems: detailed technical documentation, logs, monitoring records |\n| **Brazil AI Act** *(proposed)* | Document risk classification, impact assessments, operational logs, governance proceedings, incidents, testing, human interventions |\n| **GDPR** | Provide meaningful information about automated decisions; necessitates logging for accountability |\n| **NIST AI RMF** | Documentation at all stages for trustworthiness and auditability |\n| **ISO/IEC 42001** | Logging, incident reporting, risk documentation for AI management systems |\n\n**Jurisdictional variations:**\n- **Canada (PIPEDA):** Accountability for automated decision-making outcomes\n- **Brazil (LGPD):** Intersects with AI auditability in data processing\n- **China:** Algorithmic transparency and regular audits of system behavior\n\n> Organizations should maintain a **regulatory map** cross-referencing system types, jurisdictions, and applicable laws — often via subscription services.\n\n### 1.8 Integration with Broader Organizational Practices\n\nAI records should be embedded into the organization's operational, compliance, and risk management workflows — not isolated.\n\n- Align with enterprise information governance policies, ECM systems, and audit trails\n- **Internal and external audits** should verify completeness, accuracy, access controls, and timely incident response\n- Record keeping supports: **incident after-action reviews**, **RCA reports**, **risk trend analyses**, and **issue recurrence tracking**\n\n---", "t_comms": "## At a Glance\n\nWhat the organization says about its AI: user-facing transparency (you are interacting with AI; this content is synthetic), public transparency reports, regulator communications, and marketing — where every capability claim must be accurate and substantiated or it becomes FTC §5 deception exposure. Crisis communication for AI incidents belongs here too.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nTwo reliable angles: transparency duties (EU AI Act limited-risk disclosures, deepfake labeling) and truthful-claims discipline (AI-washing scenarios). The instinct: say what the system actually does, disclose AI interactions, and prepare incident communications in advance.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- EU AI Act transparency: disclose chatbot interactions, label AI-generated/deepfake content, inform about emotion-recognition or biometric categorization exposure\n- Marketing discipline: AI claims need substantiation before publication — capability inflation is actionable deception\n- Transparency reports: voluntary but increasingly expected — system purposes, safeguards, metrics, incident summaries\n- Regulator communications are records: accurate, consistent, and reviewed — misleading an authority has its own EU AI Act penalty tier\n- Incident communications planned in advance: who speaks, to whom, with what approved facts — improvised crisis messaging compounds harm\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Ongoing AI Operations\n\n## 2. External Communications\n\nExternal communication is a crucial aspect of AI governance. Organizations must communicate with regulators, customers, and stakeholders to maintain trust, comply with regulations, and inform about AI system operations.\n\n### 2.1 Establishing a Communication Governance Structure\n\nCommunications require planning, discipline, and maturity — not ad hoc press releases. A governance structure ensures messages are consistent, authorized, and aligned with compliance and risk policies.\n\n**Key personnel for AI-related external communications:**\n\n| Role | Responsibility |\n|------|----------------|\n| **Public Information Officer (PIO)** | Official representative in public forums |\n| **Social media lead** | Manages social media presence |\n| **AI communications lead** | Owns the AI-specific messaging strategy |\n| **Legal counsel** | Reviews for compliance, IP, liability risk |\n| **Product manager / engineer** | Provides technical explanations |\n\n> Communications to any external party should be **formally approved by a designated leader**. Policies should integrate with enterprise risk management, data governance, privacy, incident response, and regulatory reporting.\n\n### 2.2 Communicating AI System Capabilities and Limitations\n\nStakeholders deserve clear, concise, accessible information about AI systems.\n\n**Disclosures should cover:**\n\n| Topic | Description |\n|-------|-------------|\n| **Purpose and use cases** | What the system is designed to do |\n| **Limitations and known risks** | Accuracy boundaries, potential biases, data dependencies, reliability caveats |\n| **Human involvement** | How and when human oversight is used |\n| **Data sources and model behavior** | General descriptions of training data and decision-making |\n| **Inquiries and data subject requests** | How to get assistance, appeal decisions, correct errors |\n\n**Forms of disclosure:**\n- Product documentation and user manuals\n- Legal agreements and terms of use\n- Privacy notices\n- Web portals or **model cards** summarizing technical characteristics\n- Public FAQs or explainers in plain language\n\n> ⚠️ **Danger of overclaiming:** Overstating capabilities can lead to regulatory scrutiny, loss of consumer trust, or lawsuits. Balance enthusiasm with technical and legal accuracy.\n\n### 2.3 Regulatory and Legal Communication Obligations\n\nSome jurisdictions require proactive or reactive disclosure about AI systems.\n\n| Obligation | Framework |\n|------------|-----------|\n| **Public registration of high-risk systems** | EU AI Act — includes intended purpose, technical specs, risk mitigation measures, post-market monitoring plans |\n| **Breach notification to data subjects** | GDPR (Article 34) — high-risk breaches |\n| **Serious incident reporting** | EU AI Act — informing regulators of serious incidents and corrective actions |\n| **Consumer alerts** | Consumer protection laws — breaches, unauthorized disclosures, product defects, misleading results |\n\n> Mature organizations develop **preapproved communication templates** to streamline time-sensitive disclosures.\n\n### 2.4 Managing Public and Media Relations\n\nPublic perception shapes reputation. Even technically sound systems can lose trust if communications are mishandled.\n\n**Proactive communication strategies:**\n- Press releases announcing new AI deployments\n- Public blog posts explaining design choices\n- Conference presentations or white papers\n- Engaging external advisory panels or ethics boards\n- Webinars and seminars\n\n**Crisis communication plan components:**\n\n| Component | Description |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| **Crisis response team** | Usually led by the PIO; designated group authorized to speak |\n| **Message framing** | Centers transparency, responsibility, and action |\n| **Media readiness** | Prewritten press releases and answers to anticipated questions |\n\n### 2.5 User-facing Communications and Transparency\n\nThe most impactful external audiences are customers and end users.\n\n**Required labeling should indicate:**\n- That a system is AI-driven (e.g., \"AI-generated\")\n- The identity of the deployer\n- Nature of outputs (suggestions vs. decisions)\n- Opt-out or appeal mechanisms\n\n**Regulatory examples:**\n- **EU AI Act:** Mandated labeling of deepfakes and synthetic content\n- **California BOT Act:** Requires bots to self-identify during online interactions for commercial purposes\n\n> Users should have accessible help centers, escalation contacts, and receive timely responses. Record and analyze feedback as part of post-market monitoring.\n\n### 2.6 Partner and Vendor Communications\n\nAI governance extends beyond organizational boundaries to vendors, developers, and third-party integrators.\n\n**Embed communication plans into:**\n\n| Vehicle | Content |\n|---------|---------|\n| **Vendor agreements** | Requirements to report outages, incidents, and performance changes |\n| **Service Level Agreements (SLAs)** | Timelines and scope for updates, disclosures, issue resolution |\n| **Joint communication protocols** | Clarify who speaks on behalf of shared-ownership systems |\n\n**Open-source and collaborative contexts:** Transparent publication of vulnerabilities, roadmaps, and feedback channels.\n\n### 2.7 Communication Planning Across the AI Lifecycle\n\nExternal communications should be mapped across all lifecycle stages:\n\n| Stage | Communication Focus |\n|-------|---------------------|\n| **Design and development** | Research publications, data use notices |\n| **Validation and testing** | Accuracy disclosures, bias audit results |\n| **Deployment** | Purpose statement, user guidance, stakeholder briefings |\n| **Operation** | Post-market updates, incident alerts, FAQs |\n| **Retirement** | Sunset notices, archival disclosures, user offboarding info |\n\n> Communication is the connective tissue between internal governance and public/regulator accountability.\n\n### 2.8 Measuring Communication Effectiveness\n\n**Evaluation criteria:**\n\n| Metric | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| **Clarity** | Whether users and stakeholders understand intended messages |\n| **Reach** | Whether the right audiences are informed |\n| **Responsiveness** | Whether inquiries are handled in a timely manner |\n| **Trust indicators** | Whether public trust improves or erodes over time |\n\n**Measurement methods:** User satisfaction surveys, media sentiment analysis, inquiry volume/resolution time, stakeholder feedback, social media engagement, traffic metrics to AI-specific web pages.\n\n> Conduct **periodic communication audits** to review clarity, consistency, and legal adequacy. Update FAQs, disclosures, and public statements as systems evolve.\n\n---", "t_retire": "## At a Glance\n\nThe governed end of life: retirement triggers (obsolescence, unacceptable risk, regulatory change, business exit), decommissioning plans covering dependency wind-down and successor transition, data disposition per retention and deletion obligations, stakeholder notification, and record preservation that outlives the system itself.\n\n## Why It Matters for the Exam\n\nChecklist scenarios: what does responsible retirement require? Answers that just 'turn it off' miss data obligations, notifications, and records. Deactivation policies — predefined triggers and procedures — are the mature-practice marker.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Retirement triggers: performance decay, unacceptable risk, regulatory shifts, vendor end-of-life, strategic change — predefined in a deactivation policy\n- Decommissioning plan: dependency inventory, successor transition, timeline, rollback-during-retirement contingency\n- Data disposition: delete, return, or archive per contractual and legal obligations — including derived data and the model artifacts themselves\n- Notify stakeholders: users, customers, integrators, and where applicable regulators — silence creates orphaned reliance\n- Records survive the system: retention obligations (e.g., EU AI Act documentation) continue after decommissioning\n- Post-retirement verification: confirm access removal, credential revocation, and monitoring shutdown — zombie systems are unmanaged risk\n\n## Source Notes\n\n> [!source] Ongoing AI Operations\n\n## 3. AI System Retirement\n\nAI systems must eventually be upgraded, replaced, decommissioned, or withdrawn. A well-governed AI lifecycle must include a clear policy and repeatable process for retirement. **Retirement should be a standard part of the SDLC**, not an afterthought.\n\n### 3.1 Understanding the Need for AI System Retirement\n\nRetirement is a critical governance function ensuring AI components do not outlive their usefulness or pose ongoing risk.\n\n**Common triggers for deactivation:**\n\n| Trigger | Description |\n|---------|-------------|\n| **Regulatory changes** | New legal requirements render continued use noncompliant or cost-prohibitive |\n| **Performance degradation** | Model drift, obsolete data, declining accuracy vs. newer systems |\n| **Business reprioritization** | Strategic shifts rendering the system unneeded |\n| **Security or ethical concerns** | Emergence of known vulnerabilities or harmful impacts |\n| **Third-party model withdrawal** | External AI vendor deprecates model or API |\n| **Localization requirements** | Inability to comply with regional obligations (e.g., data sovereignty) |\n\n### 3.2 Building an AI System Retirement Policy\n\nA retirement policy provides formal structure for when and how AI systems are sunset.\n\n**Core elements:**\n\n| Element | Description |\n|---------|-------------|\n| **Criteria for retirement** | Operational, financial, strategic, ethical, and legal thresholds (KPI failure, excessive costs, strategy change, regulatory conflicts, stakeholder demands) |\n| **Approval and escalation process** | Who initiates/approves; escalation when ethical risks or customer harm identified |\n| **Retirement planning requirements** | Stakeholder notification, system backup, data archiving, user transition support |\n| **Documentation and audit trail** | Rationale, timing, methods, stakeholders, proceedings |\n| **After-action review (AAR)** | Lessons-learned analysis to inform future practices |\n\n> **AI Retirement vs. IT Decommissioning:** AI retirement involves more than turning off a system — it may require untraining, ethical review, removal of derivative models, and public communication, especially for high-impact automated decisions.\n\n### 3.3 Planning for Controlled Deactivation\n\nA repeatable, written process to reduce disruption and preserve vital records.\n\n**Steps for controlled deactivation:**\n\n| Step | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| **1. Stakeholder mapping** | Identify all affected parties and their transition needs |\n| **2. Usage analysis** | Map dependencies — where the system is embedded and which downstream tools rely on it |\n| **3. Fallback/transition planning** | Determine replacement model, manual process, or third-party tool |\n| **4. User notification and education** | Clear communication on changes, reasons, and how to ask questions |\n| **5. Final audit snapshot** | Capture final state: inputs, weights, performance metrics, decision logs |\n| **6. Execution of shutdown** | Disable APIs, terminate model endpoints, remove integration points, update security controls |\n| **7. Post-deactivation monitoring** | Monitor system health and user behavior to ensure successful transition |\n\n> **Migration:** When a replacement system takes over, data migration and extensive testing are required. The retired system may enter \"read-only\" or \"reporting\" mode for historical purposes.\n\n**Operational and governance challenges:**\n- **Business continuity:** Ensure critical processes remain operational\n- **Data integrity:** Prevent loss of audit trails or traceability\n- **Access revocation:** Deprovision API keys, credentials, and service accounts\n- **Residual risk management:** Monitor for unintended consequences of shutdown\n\n### 3.4 Localization and Regional Retirement Strategies\n\nModels may be deactivated or **localized** for specific regions due to differing compliance or cultural contexts.\n\n**Localization drivers:**\n\n| Driver | Description |\n|--------|-------------|\n| **Data sovereignty laws** | Mandates requiring training or inference within national borders (e.g., China, Russia) |\n| **Ethical and societal norms** | Regional sensitivities affecting content filtering or recommendations |\n| **Regulatory constraints** | EU AI Act may restrict/ban specific use cases deemed unacceptable or high-risk |\n\n**Approaches:**\n\n| Approach | Description |\n|----------|-------------|\n| **Separate model instances** | Deploy country-specific models trained on localized data |\n| **Federated learning** | Decentralized training to maintain local data residency |\n| **Modular architecture** | Selective enablement of regional features or content |\n\n### 3.5 Data and Model Artifacts: Archiving and Disposal\n\nWhen retiring a system, determine what to retain, archive, or delete based on regulations, legal agreements, and internal policies.\n\n**Information to consider archiving:**\n- Final model versions and training data lineage\n- Performance logs and error reports\n- Incident reports and mitigation records\n- Decision outputs (especially high-impact: healthcare, finance)\n- User and stakeholder communication records\n- Design documents and system artifacts\n- Any information required by regulations\n\n**Information to consider disposing:**\n- Redundant or obsolete training data\n- Temporary model artifacts (checkpoint files)\n- PII not required for compliance\n\n> Use **military-grade data erasure tools** for sensitive data. For cloud-hosted AI, request destruction from the provider, possibly including a **certificate of destruction**. Conduct a **DPIA** before deletion to confirm retention requirements under GDPR, CPRA, etc.\n\n### 3.6 Communication and Transparency in AI Retirement\n\nOrganizations must inform external parties of retirement decisions.\n\n**Communication channels:**\n\n| Channel | Audience |\n|---------|----------|\n| **User notification** | In-product alerts, email campaigns, support updates |\n| **Organization notification** | Official communications to business customers and partners (per legal agreements) |\n| **Public disclosures** | Press releases, social media, website/transparency portal |\n| **Regulatory reporting** | Notification to authorities when required by law |\n| **Internal briefings** | Legal, compliance, support, and sales teams |\n\n**Be prepared for:** User pushback, misunderstanding of reasons, migration questions, regulatory scrutiny. Early involvement of the crisis communications team and PIO mitigates reputational risk.\n\n> ⚠️ **Litigation risk:** Improper or abrupt retirement may expose organizations to legal claims, especially if customers relied on system outputs or discriminatory impacts are discovered after shutdown (another reason for archiving).\n\n**Applicable legal obligations:**\n- **EU AI Act:** Ongoing monitoring and withdrawal of noncompliant high-risk systems\n- **GDPR:** Data minimization; may necessitate erasure of personal data\n- **Consumer protection laws:** May trigger refund/compensation obligations\n- **Contractual terms:** May require notice periods or availability guarantees\n\n### 3.7 Lessons Learned and Organizational Feedback Loops\n\nRetirement should serve as a source of learning and improvement.\n\n**Post-retirement review practices:**\n\n| Practice | Description |\n|----------|-------------|\n| **Root cause analysis** | Why the system needed to be retired |\n| **Impact assessment** | Effect on users, operations, and business reputation |\n| **Post-retirement ethical review** | Ensure retired model is not repurposed in harmful ways |\n| **Policy evaluation** | Whether the retirement process aligned with formal policy |\n| **Knowledge capture** | What to document for future teams |\n\n> Incorporate lessons into training programs for data scientists, engineers, product managers, and governance professionals. This improves institutional maturity and avoids repeat mistakes.\n\n---", "kt0": "## Definition\n\nA comparative testing method where two versions of an AI model run simultaneously to evaluate which performs better — commonly used for deployment validation.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nTwo versions run simultaneously on split live traffic so their real-world performance can be compared statistically before committing to one. For AI deployment it doubles as a governance control: the incumbent keeps serving most users while the challenger proves itself on a controlled slice.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Distinguish from shadow mode — in A/B testing both variants affect real users; in shadow mode the challenger's outputs affect no one.", "kt1": "## Definition\n\nThe state of being accountable (responsible for actions, decisions, and performance). Refers to an assigned responsibility based on regulations, agreements, or delegation. A person or entity is accountable to another using specific means and criteria.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe principle that identifiable humans and organizations answer for AI outcomes — assignment of responsibility, obligation to explain and justify, and exposure to consequences. It is deliberately non-delegable: 'the algorithm decided' is the precise failure mode accountability frameworks exist to prevent.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Accountability = who answers for it; responsibility = who does it — governance questions often hinge on keeping the accountable party human and named.", "kt2": "## Definition\n\nNIST trustworthiness characteristic: information about AI system design decisions and outputs is available to stakeholders, with clear ownership of responsibility.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nOne of NIST AI RMF's seven trustworthiness characteristics: information about the system and its outputs is available to those who need it, and ownership of consequences is clear. NIST pairs the two because transparency without accountability is theater, and accountability without transparency is impossible to exercise.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Recognize the seven-characteristic list on sight: valid & reliable; safe; secure & resilient; accountable & transparent; explainable & interpretable; privacy-enhanced; fair.", "kt3": "## Definition\n\nThe range of actions an agent can actually take, fixed by the tools it can call and the permissions on those tools (sandbox-only, internal or external systems; read or write). The IMDA framework contrasts it with autonomy, which is how freely the agent decides what to do. A wider action space raises potential impact.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nIMDA separates what an agent can do (action space) from how freely it decides (autonomy). Action space grows with each tool and permission: from a sandbox to internal systems to external APIs, from read to write, or to a computer-use agent that can click and type anywhere. It mainly drives the impact side of risk, which is why least privilege and sandboxing are its main controls.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Tools and permissions change action space; instructions and human involvement change autonomy, so expect a question that makes you tell them apart.", "kt4": "## Definition\n\nA function applied to the weighted combination of all inputs to a neuron. Typically nonlinear.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe nonlinear function applied to a neuron's weighted input sum that decides what signal passes onward — the nonlinearity (ReLU, sigmoid, tanh) is what lets stacked layers learn non-linear patterns at all. Governance-level relevance: it's part of why neural networks resist human interpretation.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Know it as neural-network anatomy vocabulary — the exam tests recognition, not calculus.", "kt5": "## Definition\n\nNeeds analysis, feasibility study, stakeholder engagement, requirements definition\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAdoption is the lifecycle stage where an organization decides to take on an AI capability: needs analysis, feasibility study, stakeholder engagement and requirements definition, all before any data is collected or any model is built.\n\nGovernance at adoption is the cheapest governance there is. A use case that is declined, or reshaped, at this stage costs nothing downstream, whereas the same problem discovered after training means retraining, revalidating, re-documenting and possibly recalling a deployed system. That asymmetry is why the BOK sequences the lifecycle as it does and why the use-case assessment gate exists: define the business problem, the intended users and the affected population, the success criteria and the exclusions, and confirm that AI is the appropriate tool before committing resources.\n\nTwo questions at this stage do disproportionate work. **Is AI necessary?** A rule-based solution may be more appropriate, more explainable and less risky for an eligibility decision. **What is the affected population?** Answering that early is what makes representativeness testable later, because it defines the population the training data has to look like.\n\nSuccess criteria set here also become the anchors for everything downstream: validation targets, monitoring thresholds and retirement triggers. Without them, no later stage has a standard to measure against.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Sequencing questions: needs analysis, feasibility and use-case assessment precede any data collection or model work. Success criteria defined at adoption become the validation and monitoring thresholds later, so their absence is a root cause in 'what went wrong' scenarios.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF Playbook, Map function</a> · establishing context, purpose and success criteria\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/5/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 9 (risk management system)</a> · risk management begins before development\n- <a href=\"https://www.iso.org/standard/42001\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 42001 (AI management system)</a> · clause 6 planning and AI system impact assessment", "kt6": "## Definition\n\nAn intentionally crafted input designed to cause an AI model to make incorrect predictions — often imperceptible to humans but catastrophic for model behavior.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAn input crafted to make a model fail — perturbations often imperceptible to humans (a sticker on a stop sign, pixel noise on an image) that flip the model's output. Their existence is why security testing for AI includes adversarial robustness, not just conventional penetration testing.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Adversarial examples attack the model at inference time; data poisoning attacks it at training time — the timing distinction is tested.", "kt7": "## Definition\n\nThe notice a creditor must give after taking adverse action on an application, generally within 30 days of a completed application, including a statement of the specific principal reasons (12 CFR 1002.9). Saying the applicant failed to achieve a qualifying score is insufficient, so AI credit models must support accurate reasons. The rule still applies after the CFPB withdrew its AI adverse action circulars on 12 May 2025.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe official commentary requires the stated reasons to relate to and accurately describe the factors actually considered or scored, and notes that more than four reasons is unlikely to help the applicant. The withdrawn Circulars 2022-03 (complex algorithms) and 2023-03 (use of sample forms) applied that rule to AI, and their withdrawal removed guidance but not the regulation. FCRA adds a separate notice under 15 U.S.C. 1681m when information in a consumer report, including any credit score used, contributes to the decision.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Withdrawn guidance does not withdraw the rule: AI-driven credit denials still need specific, accurate principal reasons.", "kt8": "## Definition\n\nManipulation that redirects an agent's objectives, task choices or plan, typically through indirect prompt injection hidden in content it reads (web pages, emails, documents, tool outputs, other agents' messages). OWASP ASI01. Distinct from memory poisoning (persistent corruption of stored context) and rogue agents (drift without an active attacker).\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAgents cannot reliably tell instructions from content, so text planted in a web page, email, calendar invite or tool output can reset what the agent is trying to do. OWASP ranks it ASI01, and NIST's January 2025 blog defines agent hijacking as indirect prompt injection that causes unintended, harmful actions. Controls: treat all ingested content as untrusted, lock and version system prompts, require human approval for goal-changing or high-impact actions, and monitor for goal drift.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Direct, one-off manipulation of goals is ASI01; corruption that persists in stored memory is ASI06 Memory & Context Poisoning.", "kt9": "## Definition\n\nAn emergency control that immediately stops an agent, or disables a specific tool, prompt or connection, and revokes its credentials when it malfunctions or is compromised. The operational core of a deactivation policy (BOK IV.C); pair it with a fallback process and preserved, tamper-proof logs.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nOWASP recommends kill switches and credential revocation to disable rogue agents instantly, quarantining suspect agents for forensics and requiring fresh attestation and human approval before they return. For supply-chain compromises it suggests revoking specific tools, prompts or connections across every deployment. The switch implements the BOK IV.C deactivation policy; plan the manual fallback too, because staff may have lost the skills to run the process by hand.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] The kill switch is the immediate containment step during an incident; the deactivation or retirement policy is the governed decision and process around it.", "kt10": "## Definition\n\nAI systems made up of one or more agents that pursue a goal over several steps by planning, deciding and acting through tools, with some independence from human direction. Today's agents usually pair a language model with instructions, memory, tools and controls; governance shifts from managing outputs to managing actions.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nBOK v2.1 names agentic AI alongside generative AI in its scope, and IV.A lists agentic architectures as a deployment option next to fine-tuning and RAG. The IMDA framework notes there is no agreed definition but identifies the common thread: independent planning, decision-making and action over multiple steps toward a user's goal. For governance, errors become actions in the world (a payment made, a file deleted, an email sent) rather than text a person may or may not use.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] If the scenario has the system taking actions through tools across several steps, think agentic; if it only produces content for a human to act on, it is generative AI.", "kt11": "## Definition\n\nThe date high-risk AI system requirements take effect, including conformity assessments and CE marking. Full enforcement across all provisions is expected by 2027.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAs originally enacted, this was the AI Act's general date of application, when the Annex III high-risk regime, the Article 50 transparency duties and the Commission's GPAI fining powers were all due to start. The 2026 Digital Omnibus kept 2 August 2026 as the general date but moved the high-risk rules to 2 December 2027 (Annex III) and 2 August 2028 (Annex I), and national sandboxes to 2 August 2027. What still starts on this date: Article 50 transparency, and Commission enforcement of GPAI obligations including Article 101 fines.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Anchor check: 'as enacted', high-risk starts here; 'as amended by the 2026 Omnibus', only transparency and GPAI enforcement do.", "kt12": "## Definition\n\nAs amended by the 2026 Digital Omnibus, the date high-risk requirements start for AI that is a safety component of, or itself, a product under Annex I harmonisation law (Article 6(1)), such as medical devices. As originally enacted, this was 2 August 2027.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nArticle 113(c)(ii), as amended, applies the high-risk rules on 2 August 2028 to systems that are high-risk under Article 6(1): safety components of, or products covered by, Annex I harmonisation law that need third-party conformity assessment. The Omnibus also moved machinery to Annex I Section B, where AI requirements arrive through sectoral delegated acts applying by the same date. It replaces the original Annex I date of 2 August 2027.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Product-embedded AI still comes last: 2 Aug 2027 as enacted, 2 Aug 2028 as amended.", "kt13": "## Definition\n\nAs amended by the 2026 Digital Omnibus, the date the AI Act's high-risk requirements (Chapter III, Sections 1–3) start for Annex III systems such as recruitment, credit-scoring and education tools, including deployer duties and FRIAs. As originally enacted, this was 2 August 2026.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nArticle 113(c)(i), as replaced by Regulation (EU) 2026/1744, starts Chapter III, Sections 1–3 for Article 6(2)/Annex III systems on 2 December 2027. That brings in the whole provider chain (risk management, data governance, documentation, QMS, conformity assessment, registration), deployer duties under Article 26 and FRIAs under Article 27. Systems already on the market before then are caught only after a significant design change, though those intended for public authorities must comply by 2 August 2030.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Annex III (stand-alone) = Dec 2027 and Annex I (in products) = Aug 2028; any answer giving Aug 2026 for high-risk rules is the pre-Omnibus text.", "kt14": "## Definition\n\nThe date prohibited AI practices and AI literacy requirements began to apply — the first substantive obligations under the EU AI Act.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe first substantive AI Act deadline: prohibited practices (social scoring, exploitative manipulation, most real-time remote biometric ID) became illegal, and AI literacy expectations for provider and deployer staff took effect. It came only six months after entry into force — far faster than the rest of the Act.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Two things attach to this date: prohibitions AND AI literacy — questions often test the second, lesser-known half.", "kt15": "## Definition\n\nThe OECD definition adopted by AI RMF 1.0: 'those who play an active role in the AI system lifecycle, including organizations and individuals that deploy or operate AI'. Appendix A groups their tasks (design, development, deployment, operation and monitoring, TEVV, human factors, domain experts, impact assessment, procurement, governance and oversight) and adds third parties, end users, affected communities, other AI actors and the general public.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAI RMF 1.0 borrows the OECD definition, and Appendix A describes each group's tasks. AI Deployment covers piloting, legacy compatibility, regulatory compliance, organizational change and user experience, while TEVV actors test, evaluate, verify and validate throughout the lifecycle and, as a best practice, are kept separate from those who build the models. The Playbook tags each subcategory with the groups most involved, and affected individuals and communities are AI actors even if they never interact with the system.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Match tasks to Appendix A: piloting and managing organizational change is AI Deployment, while examining the system and remediating problems is TEVV.", "kt16": "## Definition\n\nAutomated entity that perceives and reacts to its environment and takes actions to achieve its goals.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe defining loop is **perceive, decide, act**: an agent senses its environment and autonomously takes actions toward goals, rather than only returning a prediction when asked. The distinction from a plain model is not sophistication but agency: a model produces output, an agent changes state in the world (sending mail, moving money, calling APIs, editing files).\n\nThat shift is why agentic AI dominates current governance discussion. Governance controls that were adequate for a model in a human workflow become insufficient when the system has its own tool access, because actions execute without a human between inference and effect. The controls that follow are the ones you would design for any autonomous actor: a defined and least-privilege **action space**, human approval checkpoints for consequential or irreversible steps, logging of every action taken, spending and rate limits, and a kill switch that actually stops the agent rather than just the interface.\n\nBOK v2.1 reflects this in Domain IV.A, which now names **agentic architectures** alongside fine-tuning and retrieval-augmented generation as a deployment option a governance professional must evaluate. Agentic systems also widen the prompt-injection surface, because an agent that reads untrusted content and holds tool credentials can be instructed by that content.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] The words 'takes action' in a scenario are the tell: a system that acts on its environment is an agent, not just a model. When a stem describes an agent, the expected control is almost always a limit on its action space, a human approval checkpoint, or logging, not a model retraining.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/3/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 3 (definitions)</a> · the inference-based AI system definition an agent sits inside\n- <a href=\"https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications</a> · excessive agency and tool misuse in agentic systems\n- <a href=\"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF Playbook</a> · human oversight and action-limit actions", "kt17": "## Definition\n\nThe combined work of checking, and showing to others, whether an AI system deserves trust, using techniques such as impact assessments, bias and compliance audits, conformity assessment, performance testing, formal verification and certification. An audit or a red-team test is one assurance technique; assurance is the overall program that builds justified confidence for regulators, customers and the board.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAs in financial audit or product safety, assurance means evidence that a system meets agreed criteria, communicated to the people who need to rely on it. The UK government's Introduction to AI assurance (DSIT, February 2024) describes it as measuring, evaluating and communicating the trustworthiness of AI systems and lists techniques from risk and impact assessment through audits, conformity assessment, formal verification and performance testing to certification. In the BOK it sits with the periodic assessment activities of III.C and IV.C, such as audits, red teaming, threat modeling and security testing.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] An audit, a conformity assessment or a red-team exercise is one assurance technique; AI assurance is the program that combines them to build justified trust.", "kt18": "## Definition\n\nThe Korean Act's umbrella term for any corporation, organization, individual or government body in the AI business. There are two types: AI developers, which develop and provide AI, and AI-using business operators, which offer products or services built on a developer's AI. Both carry the Act's transparency and high-impact duties.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe Act regulates 'AI business operators' of two kinds: developers that build and supply AI, and AI-using business operators that build products or services on a developer's AI. Unlike the EU's split between provider and deployer, most duties attach to whichever operator provides the product or service. The decree eases this for downstream operators. One that makes no significant functional change is deemed to have carried out the developer's risk-management, explanation and user-protection measures.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Government bodies count as AI business operators too, and the deeming rule never covers human oversight or documentation.", "kt19": "## Definition\n\nFunctional element that constitutes an AI system.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nISO/IEC 22989's building-block term: any functional element that makes up an AI system — a model, a data pipeline, a knowledge base, an inference engine. Governance attaches to the assembled system, but risk analysis has to decompose it into components because each fails differently.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Component ⊂ system: an AI system is components plus interfaces, data flows, and operational context.", "kt20": "## Definition\n\nEntity that acquires/uses the AI system\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nIn the ISO 22989 role taxonomy, the party that acquires or receives an AI product or service — distinct from the provider who supplies it and the user who operates it. Role clarity matters because contractual risk allocation (warranties, audit rights, liability) follows these lines.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] ISO roles (provider/producer/customer/user/subject) are not the EU AI Act roles (provider/deployer/importer/distributor) — keep the two vocabularies separate.", "kt21": "## Definition\n\nPolicy defining how long training data is retained after model development — and addressing that data may effectively persist inside the trained model itself. A common gap when updating privacy policies for AI.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAI breaks classic retention policy in two ways. Training data lingers in derived artifacts (checkpoints, embeddings, caches, feature stores) that deletion schedules never contemplated. And the trained model itself retains data in learned form, so the artifact that produces the risk is not a database row anyone can delete.\n\nA mature policy therefore defines retention separately for each layer: **raw input data** (delete after training converges?), **transformed and feature data** (same rules as raw, or different?), **labelled datasets** (purpose-specific periods), and **model artifacts** (what does a deletion request mean for learned weights?). It also states a position on retraining: whether a deletion request triggers it, and how the organization verifies to a data subject that deletion actually happened. That position has to be honest, because \"we cannot remove data from a trained model\" is a legitimate answer only if it is documented and the residual risk is accepted by someone with authority.\n\nRetention policy also serves minimization, not only accountability. A documented schedule that defines deletion is defensible; ad-hoc deletion looks like spoliation the moment litigation is reasonably anticipated, and litigation holds override routine deletion.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] If a scenario involves deleting user data, ask whether the model trained on it is addressed: that is the gap being tested. Retention rules have to cover derived data and model artifacts, not only the source database.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://gdpr-info.eu/art-5-gdpr/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GDPR, Article 5(1)(e) (storage limitation)</a> · the principle that retention policy implements\n- <a href=\"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2024-12/edpb_opinion_202428_ai-models_en.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EDPB Opinion 28/2024 on AI models and personal data</a> · the DPAs' analysis of deletion, anonymisation and model training\n- <a href=\"https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/artificial-intelligence/guidance-on-ai-and-data-protection/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ICO guidance on AI and data protection</a> · retention and rights in an AI context", "kt22": "## Definition\n\nThe entity that operationalizes AI models in production — responsible for integration, monitoring for risk, and ensuring reliable real-world function.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe AI deployer is the entity that puts an AI system into operational use. Under the EU AI Act that means any organization using an AI system in a professional capacity, as distinct from a private individual using it personally.\n\nDeployer duties are operational rather than build-side, which is the distinction most exam questions turn on. A deployer must use the system in accordance with the provider's instructions, assign human oversight to people with the necessary competence, training and authority, ensure the input data is relevant and sufficiently representative for the intended purpose, monitor operation and report serious incidents, retain logs where required, and in defined cases (public bodies, and private deployers of certain essential-service systems) run a fundamental rights impact assessment before first use.\n\nTwo traps recur. First, **role confusion**: attributing provider duties (conformity assessment, CE marking, Annex IV technical documentation) to a deployer is a standard wrong answer. Second, **role shift**: a deployer that puts its own name or trademark on a high-risk system, substantially modifies it, or changes its intended purpose becomes the provider and inherits the full obligations chain. That single rule converts an apparent deployment question into a conformity question.\n\nThe BOK also treats deployer as a **task label** rather than a legal category, so the same organization can be a deployer for one system and a developer for another.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Deployer duties are operational (use per instructions, oversee, monitor, report, retain logs); provider duties are build-side (design, document, conformity, CE marking). Misattributing them is the classic trap, and rebranding or substantially modifying a high-risk system makes the deployer the provider.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/3/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 3 (definition of deployer)</a> · the legal definition\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/26/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 26 (deployer obligations)</a> · the deployer duty list\n- <a href=\"https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/policies/ai-act-explained\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act explained (European Commission)</a> · role-based obligations in plain language", "kt23": "## Definition\n\nThe entity that builds and trains AI models — responsible for curating datasets, running fairness evaluations, and ensuring ethical design and testing.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe AI developer is the party that designs, builds and trains AI models: data curation and rights checks, architecture and algorithm selection, training and evaluation, fairness and robustness testing, and honest documentation of what the model can and cannot do.\n\nIn value-chain terms the developer's decisions determine most of the risk everyone downstream inherits. Training data sets the fairness and accuracy ceiling; architecture sets the interpretability ceiling; thresholds and objectives set the error profile; and documentation sets whether any of that is visible to the people who will deploy it. That is why developer-side controls are the ones frameworks emphasise: dataset documentation, model cards, bias and robustness test evidence, and reproducible training records.\n\nTwo vocabulary notes matter for the exam. The BOK uses developer as a **task label**, not a legal category, and BOK v2.1 adds **provider** alongside it, because the entity that places a system on the market under its own name carries obligations regardless of who wrote the code. In the EU AI Act the provider is the duty-bearer for high-risk systems: risk management, data governance, technical documentation, conformity assessment, CE marking, registration and post-market monitoring. A small team that fine-tunes someone else's base model can be a provider.\n\nWhen a question asks who should fix biased training data or document model limitations, the developer or provider side answers. The deployer cannot fix what it cannot see.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Developer and provider are the build-side roles, and developer is a task label while provider is the legal duty-bearer. Questions about biased training data, documentation or conformity belong to the developer or provider, not the deployer.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/3/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 3 (definition of provider)</a> · who the provider legally is\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/16/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 16 (provider obligations)</a> · the provider duty list\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · the parallel role taxonomy: provider, producer, customer, user, subject", "kt24": "## Definition\n\nEU AI Act role: entity in the supply chain that makes an AI system available on the EU market without affecting its compliance — must verify CE marking and documentation.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAn EU AI Act supply-chain role: an entity (other than provider or importer) that makes an AI system available on the EU market without changing it. Distributors must verify the system bears CE marking and required documentation, and must not supply systems they have reason to believe are non-compliant.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Distributor = verify-and-pass-through; if it rebrands or substantially modifies the system, it becomes the provider with full provider duties.", "kt25": "## Definition\n\nAny individual or group with a role in AI governance — includes executive leadership, technical teams, legal/compliance, product owners, and end users.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAn AI governance stakeholder is anyone with a stake in an AI system's development or its effects: the board and executives who set risk appetite, the legal, privacy, security, data science and ethics functions who assess it, the business owner who sponsors it, the employees who operate it, the people it scores, the communities it affects, the vendors in its chain, and the regulators with jurisdiction.\n\nStakeholder identification is the first step of both impact assessment and committee design, and its failure mode is quiet. A stakeholder who is never identified is a risk nobody owns and a perspective nobody hears, which is why the frameworks push toward multidisciplinary involvement rather than a single accountable function. The groups internal processes most reliably miss are the external ones: affected communities, civil society, and the frontline staff who will actually operate the system.\n\nIn practice, mapping distinguishes stakeholders by **interest** (how much they care), **influence** (how much they can affect the outcome) and **exposure** (how much the system can affect them). Those three axes determine who needs a seat at review, who needs to be consulted during design, and who needs to be informed or given recourse afterward. The mapping is documentation with a purpose: it is the evidence that the people affected were considered.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Strong exam answers involve stakeholders early and multidisciplinarily. Answers that consult one function, or that never identify the affected population, are engineered to be wrong. External stakeholders are the ones most often missing.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF Playbook, Govern function</a> · stakeholder engagement and accountability actions\n- <a href=\"https://www.iso.org/standard/42001\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 42001 (AI management system)</a> · interested parties and their requirements\n- <a href=\"https://iapp.org/resources/article/key-terms-for-ai-governance/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IAPP Key Terms for AI Governance</a> · stakeholder and role terminology", "kt26": "## Definition\n\nA structured analysis of how an AI system affects individuals, groups, organizations, and society — covers privacy, bias, safety, fairness, and fundamental rights.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe AI-specific look-before-you-leap: a structured evaluation of a system's potential effects on individuals, groups, organizations, and society — broader than a DPIA (not limited to personal data) and increasingly expected by frameworks (ISO 42001 requires one) and laws. Anatomy: describe the system, identify affected parties, rate severity and likelihood, mitigate, decide on residual risk, revisit.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] AIIA covers social/ethical/legal impacts broadly; DPIA is the GDPR-mandated subset for personal-data risk — scope is the difference.", "kt27": "## Definition\n\nEU AI Act role: entity that places an AI system from a third country on the EU market — must verify conformity and ensure CE marking before distribution.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe EU AI Act role for bringing third-country AI into the Union: an EU-established entity placing a foreign provider's system on the EU market. Importers must verify conformity assessment was performed, documentation exists, and CE marking is affixed before the system enters the market.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Importers verify, they don't perform, conformity assessment — 'the importer must conduct the conformity assessment' is a wrong-answer pattern.", "kt28": "## Definition\n\nOECD terms (AI Papers No. 16, May 2024). An AI incident is an event where an AI system's development, use or malfunction directly or indirectly leads to harm to health, critical infrastructure, rights, or property, communities or the environment. An AI hazard is an event that could plausibly lead to an incident; near misses count as hazards. Serious incidents, AI disasters and serious hazards are narrower tiers.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe OECD's May 2024 definitions separate realized from potential harm. An incident is an event where an AI system's development, use or malfunction leads to harm; a hazard is one that could plausibly lead to an incident, and near misses count as hazards. The tiers nest: serious incidents involve serious harm, and AI disasters are serious incidents that overwhelm a community's capacity to cope.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Did harm happen? Incident. Could it plausibly have happened, including an error caught in time? Hazard.", "kt29": "## Definition\n\nA Commission proposal of 28 Sep 2022 (COM(2022) 496) to ease fault-based claims for AI harm through court-ordered disclosure on high-risk AI systems and a rebuttable presumption of causality. Withdrawal was announced in the February 2025 work programme and published in OJ C/2025/5423 on 6 Oct 2025. The revised Product Liability Directive and national tort law fill the gap.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe proposal was the fault-based companion to the no-fault Product Liability Directive, which the Commission proposed the same day and which was adopted in 2024. Work on it was suspended until the AI Act was adopted, the Commission listed it for withdrawal in its February 2025 work programme, and the Parliament's internal market committee called it premature and unnecessary in May 2025. Some of its ideas live on in the revised PLD, which has its own disclosure and presumption rules for no-fault claims.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Any answer that relies on an EU AI Liability Directive presumption is wrong as of 2026: the proposal was withdrawn in October 2025.", "kt30": "## Definition\n\nA set of interrelated organizational elements to establish AI policies, objectives, and processes to achieve those objectives — the core of ISO/IEC 42001.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe ISO/IEC 42001 construct: the interlocking set of policies, objectives, processes, roles, and controls through which an organization governs AI — analogous to an ISMS for security. Built on Annex SL and PDCA, it is the thing an auditor certifies: not any single model, but the organizational machine that manages all of them.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Certification attaches to the management system, not to individual AI models — a certified AIMS doesn't make any given model 'certified safe.'", "kt31": "## Definition\n\nEntity that builds/assembles the AI system\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nIn product-safety framing, the entity that produces an AI-enabled product and answers for its safety — the term matters where AI is a safety component of regulated products (machinery, medical devices, vehicles) and product legislation assigns duties to manufacturers. Under the AI Act, product manufacturers can inherit provider obligations when high-risk AI ships inside their products.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] AI embedded in a regulated product routes through both the product's sectoral law and the AI Act — manufacturers wear both hats.", "kt32": "## Definition\n\nEntity that collaborates in AI development or deployment\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nA third party in the AI value chain that collaborates in developing, supplying, or operating AI capability — cloud platforms, model vendors, data suppliers, integrators. Partner risk is third-party risk: their training data, security posture, and model changes become your exposure without your visibility.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Whatever the partner does, accountability to regulators and affected individuals stays with the organization deploying the system.", "kt33": "## Definition\n\nEntity that makes an AI system available\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe EU AI Act's central duty-bearer: the entity that develops an AI system (or has it developed) and places it on the market or puts it into service under its own name or trademark. Providers of high-risk systems own the heavyweight obligations — risk management, data governance, documentation, conformity assessment, CE marking, post-market monitoring.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Watch the role-shift trap: deployers who rebrand or substantially modify a high-risk system become providers and inherit everything.", "kt34": "## Definition\n\nInformative annex of ISO/IEC 23894 cataloging AI-specific risk sources; Annex A lists common AI-related objectives and Annex C maps risk management processes to the AI system life cycle.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nISO 23894's informative catalog of where AI risk originates: data quality and availability, lack of explainability, degree of automation, system complexity, hardware and software faults, lifecycle and supply-chain factors, and the deployment environment. It functions as a completeness checklist when populating an AI risk register.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Annex B = risk sources, Annex A = objectives, Annex C = lifecycle mapping — the three annexes are easily confused.", "kt35": "## Definition\n\nA numbered outcome statement under a category of the AI RMF Core, e.g. MEASURE 2.11: fairness and bias are evaluated and results documented. AI RMF 1.0 has 72 across 19 categories, and the Playbook gives one entry of suggested actions for each.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe Core has four functions, 19 categories and 72 subcategories, numbered by function, category and subcategory, as in MANAGE 1.3. Each states an outcome: MANAGE 1.3 is that responses to high-priority risks are developed, planned and documented, with options of mitigating, transferring, avoiding or accepting. The Playbook has one entry per subcategory, and use-case profiles implement the functions, categories and subcategories for a specific setting.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] A subcategory is an outcome, not a mandatory control: the RMF says organizations may apply some or all of the categories and subcategories.", "kt36": "## Definition\n\nIndividual affected by or interacting with an AI system\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nISO 22989's term for the person affected by an AI system's operation — the loan applicant scored, the patient triaged, the pedestrian detected — who often never chose or touched the system. Governance frameworks exist substantially to protect this role: impact assessments, transparency, and contestability all point at the AI subject.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Parallel to GDPR's 'data subject' but broader — an AI subject need not have contributed any personal data to be affected.", "kt37": "## Definition\n\nA technical system that generates results such as content, forecasts, recommendations, or decisions for a specific, human-defined objective. May use various techniques and approaches related to AI to develop a model for representing data, knowledge, processes, etc. Designed to operate with varying degrees of automation.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThis is the legally loaded definition, and it decides whether a regulation applies at all. The EU AI Act (Article 3(1)) and the OECD's 2023 revision both define an AI system as a **machine-based system** that, for **explicit or implicit objectives**, **infers** from the input it receives how to generate outputs such as **predictions, content, recommendations or decisions** that can **influence physical or virtual environments**.\n\nFour elements carry the weight:\n\n- **Machine-based**: it runs on hardware, which excludes pure human decision processes.\n- **Explicit or implicit objectives**: the goal need not be coded; a learned objective counts.\n- **Infers**: the operative word. The system derives outputs rather than executing a rule that was written down for each case. This is what separates AI from ordinary rule-following software.\n- **Influence on environments**: the output has to be able to affect something, physically or virtually, which is broad enough to cover a recommendation a human then acts on.\n\nISO/IEC 22989 adds the structural vocabulary around it: an AI system is composed of **AI components** (models, data pipelines, inference engines, knowledge bases), which is why risk analysis decomposes a system into parts even though governance attaches to the assembled whole.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Recite the elements: machine-based, explicit or implicit objectives, infers from input, generates predictions/content/recommendations/decisions, can influence environments. Whether something is an AI system is a threshold question: if it fails this definition, the EU AI Act does not apply to it at all.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/3/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 3(1) (definition of an AI system)</a> · the binding definition\n- <a href=\"https://oecd.ai/en/wonk/definition\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OECD: what is an AI system?</a> · the 2023 revision the EU definition follows\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · AI system, AI model and AI component", "kt38": "## Definition\n\nA formal, documented process for identifying, evaluating and addressing the benefits and harms an AI system and its foreseeable uses can have for individuals, groups and society. ISO/IEC 42001 requires one (clauses 6.1.4 and 8.4); ISO/IEC 42005 explains how to do it. Distinct from the organizational risk assessment of ISO/IEC 23894.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe outward-looking assessment: how could this system, and its foreseeable uses, affect people and society for better or worse? ISO/IEC 42001 makes it mandatory (plan the process in 6.1.4, perform it in 8.4, with Annex A objective A.5), and ISO/IEC 42005 explains how. It records benefits as well as harms, covers failures and reasonably foreseeable misuse, and feeds the organization's risk management rather than replacing it.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Impacts on people means 42005; risk to the organization means 23894; the certifiable requirement to do it sits in 42001.", "kt39": "## Definition\n\nThe person or entity that interacts with AI in real-world scenarios. Often closest to operational impact and first to detect unintended outcomes.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe AI user is the person or entity operating an AI system within its intended purpose: the recruiter reading ranked résumés, the clinician reviewing a risk score, the analyst using a forecast, the customer receiving a recommendation.\n\nUsers are the last line of defence in every oversight design, and the most common point of failure. They need training for **appropriate reliance**: enough trust to gain the benefit, enough scepticism to catch failures, and clear channels to report anomalies. They also need the interface to support them, with reasons and confidence surfaced rather than a bare recommendation, and with disagreement and override easy rather than penalised.\n\nThe characteristic failure at this layer is automation bias, the documented tendency to accept machine output uncritically. It is why \"a human reviews every decision\" is not by itself a control: oversight needs competence, information, authority and time together, and removing any one produces a rubber stamp. Scenarios about over-reliance therefore resolve at the user layer (training, interface friction, oversight workload) rather than at the model, because retraining does not make a human more sceptical.\n\nUsers also hold rights-adjacent duties in some regimes, such as reporting incidents and, under the EU AI Act, using the system per the provider's instructions.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Automation-bias scenarios resolve at the user layer: training, interface design and realistic oversight workload, not model retraining. A human who always accepts the model's output is not meaningful oversight.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/4/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 4 (AI literacy for staff operating AI)</a> · the training expectation that makes the user role effective\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/13/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 13 (instructions for use)</a> · what the provider must tell the deployer and user\n- <a href=\"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF Playbook, Govern function</a> · workforce awareness and human-AI configuration actions", "kt40": "## Definition\n\nEvaluation of AI vendors covering AI-specific risks: model and training-data provenance, labeling quality, explainability, safety filters, and vulnerable dependencies — imposed by procurement policy before contracting.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAI vendor due diligence is the AI-specific extension of third-party assessment: the questions you have to ask about an AI supplier that conventional vendor checks never covered.\n\n| Probe | Why it matters |\n|---|---|\n| Training-data origin and rights | Determines whether the model's outputs are lawfully usable and whether your use inherits a copyright problem |\n| Fairness and robustness test evidence | Whether anyone has measured subgroup performance and behaviour under stress |\n| Explainability support | Whether the vendor can help you meet explanation and contestability duties |\n| Model-change practices | How and when behaviour can change under you |\n| Security of the ML supply chain | Pipeline, model registry and endpoint exposure |\n| Sub-tier dependencies | Who labeled the data, whose base model sits underneath, which open-source components are in play |\n\nThe structural problem is that a vendor's model is a black box inside a black box: you cannot inspect the training data, often cannot see the model version, and may not learn that behaviour changed until users report it. That is why **point-in-time diligence decays fast** and why the assessment has to be paired with contractual notification duties and continuous monitoring. A standardized supplier questionnaire mapped to risk tiers keeps the process repeatable, and flagging AI at procurement intake is what makes sure the questionnaire is actually used.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Answers listing AI-specific probes (data provenance, fairness evidence, model-change notification) beat generic vendor checks (financials, insurance, SOC 2) every time. Diligence alone is insufficient because vendor model updates change behaviour mid-contract, so monitoring and contract terms complete the control.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1), third-party and value-chain risk</a> · GOVERN 6 and dependency mapping\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 600-1, value-chain and component integration risk</a> · the generative-AI form of vendor risk\n- <a href=\"https://www.iso.org/standard/42001\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 42001 (AI management system)</a> · Annex A supplier and third-party controls\n- <a href=\"https://www.cisa.gov/ai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CISA artificial intelligence resources</a> · AI supply-chain guidance", "kt41": "## Definition\n\nSystematic and repeatable errors in an AI system that create unfair outcomes, such as privileging one group over another, often stemming from biased training data.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nSystematic unfairness in algorithmic outputs — sourced from unrepresentative training data, historically biased labels, proxy features, or feedback loops, and manifesting as disparate performance or outcomes across groups. It is a sociotechnical problem: the algorithm faithfully amplifies what the data and design gave it.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Bias can enter at every stage (data, features, labels, deployment) — answers locating it only in 'bad data' are incomplete.", "kt42": "## Definition\n\nISO's harmonized structure for management system standards — provides identical clause structure (4-10) across ISO 42001, 27001, 9001 enabling integration.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nISO's harmonized high-level structure for management system standards — the shared clause skeleton (context, leadership, planning, support, operation, performance evaluation, improvement) that makes ISO 42001 dock cleanly with ISO 27001 and 9001. Organizations with an existing ISMS can extend it to AI rather than building parallel machinery.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Annex SL is why 'integrate the AIMS with existing management systems' is a realistic (and usually correct) answer.", "kt43": "## Definition\n\nThe process of irreversibly transforming personal data so the data subject can no longer be identified — if fully achieved, GDPR no longer applies.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nProcessing personal data so individuals can no longer be identified — by anyone, using reasonably likely means, irreversibly. Genuinely anonymous data exits GDPR scope, which is exactly why the bar is high: quasi-identifier combinations (age + zip + condition) and model-based attacks defeat naive 'we removed the names' claims.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] If re-identification is reasonably possible, it was pseudonymization, not anonymization — and GDPR still applies in full.", "kt44": "## Definition\n\nThe GDPR provision granting data subjects the right not to be subject to solely automated decisions producing legal or similarly significant effects — requires human intervention capability.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe automated-decision article: individuals have the right not to be subject to decisions based solely on automated processing that produce legal or similarly significant effects. Exceptions (contract necessity, law, explicit consent) come with mandatory safeguards — at minimum human intervention, the ability to express a view, and to contest the decision.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] 'Solely automated' is the hinge — genuine human review before the decision takes effect takes you outside Article 22.", "kt45": "## Definition\n\nThe GDPR provision mandating Data Protection Impact Assessments (DPIAs) for processing likely to result in high risk to individuals — frequently triggered by AI systems.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe DPIA mandate: a data protection impact assessment is required where processing is likely to result in high risk to individuals — with systematic and extensive automated evaluation (profiling), large-scale special-category processing, and systematic public monitoring called out expressly. If the DPIA leaves risk high, Article 36 requires consulting the supervisory authority before processing.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] AI profiling with significant effects is the textbook Article 35 trigger — and 'do a DPIA first' is a frequent correct answer.", "kt46": "## Definition\n\nLegal basis for the GPAI Code of Practice, published by the European AI Office. Adherence is a compliance-demonstration aid, NOT conclusive evidence of compliance or a safe harbor.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe provision authorizing codes of practice for GPAI: the AI Office facilitates multi-stakeholder codes that give providers a concrete path to demonstrating compliance with Articles 53 and 55. The 2025 GPAI Code of Practice is its product — a demonstration aid, expressly not a safe harbor.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Article 56 = codes of practice; adherence eases the evidentiary burden but never constitutes conclusive proof of compliance.", "kt47": "## Definition\n\nThe GDPR provision prohibiting processing of special category data (race, religion, health, biometrics, etc.) unless a specific exception applies — critical for AI training data.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe special-categories article: processing data revealing race/ethnicity, political opinions, religion, trade-union membership, genetic data, biometric data for identification, health, or sex life/orientation is prohibited unless one of the 9(2) conditions applies (explicit consent, employment law, vital interests, substantial public interest, etc.). AI multiplies exposure because models infer these categories from ordinary data.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Article 6 lawful basis AND an Article 9 condition are both required for special categories — one without the other fails.", "kt48": "## Definition\n\nA theoretical form of AI capable of understanding, learning, and applying intelligence across any task a human can perform. Does not yet exist publicly.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAGI is hypothetical AI with **human-level competence across essentially all cognitive domains**, able to transfer learning between unrelated tasks the way a person does. It does not exist. Every deployed system today, however broad, is narrow AI, because generality is about transfer across unrelated domains rather than the size of the training set.\n\nThe distinction does real work in governance. It disciplines risk assessment, because claims about a system's capabilities should be bounded by what has been demonstrated rather than what is conceivable. It disciplines marketing, because describing a product as general or near-general intelligence invites consumer-protection exposure. And it disciplines long-term risk debate, which is about systems that do not yet exist and therefore cannot be regulated by capability benchmarks.\n\nFor a governance professional the practical rule is: treat AGI as a taxonomic boundary, not a planning input. When a scenario asks you to reason about a real system, the correct framing is always the narrow, bounded one.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] AGI and ASI are theoretical, so any option that treats an existing product as AGI is wrong by definition. The capability ladder runs narrow (exists) to AGI (theoretical) to ASI (theoretical).\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · the formal capability vocabulary\n- <a href=\"https://oecd.ai/en/wonk/definition\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OECD: what is an AI system?</a> · the functional definition that avoids capability claims\n- <a href=\"https://aiindex.stanford.edu/report/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Stanford AI Index Report</a> · what frontier systems can and cannot currently do", "kt49": "## Definition\n\n*\\<Discipline>* Research and development of mechanisms and applications of AI systems. R&D can take place in fields such as computer science, data science, humanities, mathematics, and natural sciences.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe umbrella discipline: machine-based systems performing tasks associated with human intelligence — learning, reasoning, perception, language. Regulatory definitions narrow it functionally (the EU AI Act/OECD 'infers from inputs to generate outputs' formulation) precisely so 'is this AI?' has a legal answer.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Know both the conceptual definition and the OECD/EU functional one — the latter decides regulatory scope.", "kt50": "## Definition\n\nA theoretical AI that surpasses human intelligence in all domains — creativity, problem-solving, emotional understanding, and decision-making.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nASI is the hypothetical tier beyond AGI: intelligence surpassing the best human performance in essentially every domain. It has no engineering path, no deployed instance, and no agreed definition beyond \"better than us at everything.\"\n\nIts role in a governance syllabus is taxonomic and rhetorical. Taxonomically, it is the far end of the capability ladder, and knowing where the ladder ends is how you avoid misclassifying real systems. Rhetorically, it appears in long-term-risk argument, in frontier-lab safety framing, and occasionally in vendor marketing, where it should be read as a claim requiring substantiation rather than a description.\n\nPractically, the governance instruments you would use for an ASI do not exist, which is why serious long-term-risk work focuses on the precursor capabilities and on the institutional capacity to notice and respond. For exam purposes, ASI is a placement question.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Ladder recall: narrow (exists), AGI (theoretical), ASI (theoretical). Placement questions here are free points, and any option implying a deployed ASI is wrong.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · capability and system vocabulary\n- <a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/ai-safety-institute-overview\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">UK AI Safety Institute overview</a> · how frontier capability is being assessed institutionally", "kt51": "## Definition\n\nA systematic, independent examination of an AI system's design, data, outputs, and governance against established criteria — can be internal (first/second party) or external (third party).\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nSystematic, independent, evidence-based examination against defined criteria — a framework, standard, regulation, or the organization's own policies. For AI: internal audits check the governance program, algorithmic audits probe specific properties (bias, robustness), and external certification audits (ISO 42001) provide third-party assurance.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Independence and defined criteria make an audit an audit — the builders reviewing their own system informally is monitoring, not audit.", "kt52": "## Definition\n\nDecisions made by a system rather than a person, with no meaningful human role in reaching the outcome, such as software approving or refusing a loan from a score. GDPR Art. 22 gives people the right not to be subject to a decision based solely on automated processing, including profiling, that has legal or similarly significant effects, unless it is necessary for a contract, authorised by law, or based on explicit consent.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nWhether a decision is solely automated turns on the quality of human involvement: the Article 29 Working Party's guidelines (WP251rev.01) say a token sign-off does not count, and a reviewer needs the authority and competence to change the outcome and should consider all the relevant data. Where Art. 22 applies, the decision is allowed only under one of the three exceptions, with safeguards, and it may rest on special-category data only with explicit consent or on substantial-public-interest grounds plus suitable measures (Art. 22(4)). GDPR Recital 71 gives automatic refusal of an online credit application and e-recruiting without human intervention as examples.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] A human rubber stamp does not take a decision outside Article 22; meaningful review requires someone with the authority and competence to change the result.", "kt53": "## Definition\n\nTask of shortening a portion of natural language content or text while preserving important semantic information.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe NLP task of condensing text while preserving meaning — extractive (selecting key sentences) or abstractive (generating new phrasing). As an ISO 22989 catalog entry it illustrates task-specific narrow AI; in modern practice it's an LLM capability with hallucination risk attached to the abstractive variant.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] One of the ISO 22989 NLP task definitions — recognize it as terminology rather than a governance concept.", "kt54": "## Definition\n\nRelating to a process or system that operates under certain conditions without human intervention.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAutomation is technology executing tasks with reduced human involvement. The governance question is never automation yes or no; it is **the degree and placement of the remaining human control**. A system can generate a recommendation, a ranked list, a draft decision or an executed action, and each of those places the human at a different point in the chain.\n\nISO/IEC 23894 lists degree of automation as an AI risk source, and the logic is direct: the more the system decides on its own, the fewer the checkpoints at which a human can notice something is wrong, and the more the controls have to be designed in rather than performed by a person. GDPR Article 22 constrains the extreme end, giving individuals the right not to be subject to decisions based solely on automated processing with legal or similarly significant effects. The EU AI Act's human oversight requirement (Article 14) does the same for high-risk systems.\n\nThe practical governance move is to place automation deliberately rather than by default. Decide where in the workflow the human acts (before the decision, supervising it, or after it), what information they need to add value, and what authority they hold to reverse it. \"The system suggests and a human decides\" and \"the system decides and a human is informed\" are very different controls, and only one of them is oversight.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Degree of automation is an ISO/IEC 23894 risk source: more automation, fewer checkpoints, more governance required. When a scenario asks whether a process is compliant, check where the human sits relative to the decision, not whether a human is nominally involved.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://www.iso.org/standard/77304.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 23894 AI risk management</a> · degree of automation as an AI risk source\n- <a href=\"https://gdpr-info.eu/art-22-gdpr/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GDPR Article 22 (automated individual decision-making)</a> · the constraint on solely automated decisions\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/14/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 14 (human oversight)</a> · oversight as a design requirement for high-risk systems", "kt55": "## Definition\n\nA characteristic of a system that is capable of changing its intended domain of use or its objective without external intervention, control, or supervision.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAutonomy carries two meanings in AI governance, and scenarios use both.\n\n**System autonomy** is a system's capacity to operate and pursue objectives without human direction. It is the characteristic that makes AI governance categorically harder than software governance, because decisions execute without a human between model and effect. ISO/IEC 23894 lists degree of automation among its AI risk sources for exactly this reason: the more the system decides on its own, the more governance the use requires, and the more the controls have to be designed in rather than performed by a person.\n\n**Human autonomy** is the value at stake: people's agency against manipulation, over-delegation and the quiet transfer of decisions to systems nobody chose. This is the sense in which autonomy appears in the OECD principles and in responsible AI framing, and it is the reason human-centricity and contestability exist as principles rather than as optional extras.\n\nRead the stem to see which is in play. \"The system acts without review\" is the first; \"users no longer question the recommendation\" is the second, and the control for that one is training plus interface design rather than a technical fix.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] The term carries both meanings: system autonomy as a risk characteristic, human autonomy as a protected value. The control differs, so read the scenario before answering. Degree of automation is an ISO/IEC 23894 risk source.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://www.iso.org/standard/77304.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 23894 AI risk management</a> · degree of automation as an AI risk source\n- <a href=\"https://oecd.ai/en/ai-principles\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OECD AI Principles</a> · human-centred values and autonomy\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · autonomy, controllability and predictability vocabulary", "kt56": "## Definition\n\nThe property of being accessible and usable when an authorized entity has a need.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe security-triad property that systems and data are accessible when needed. For AI it extends to model serving (inference uptime), data pipeline continuity, and graceful degradation — an unavailable safety-critical model is itself a safety incident.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] CIA triad member — availability failures in AI scenarios are security/resilience issues, not performance tuning.", "kt57": "## Definition\n\nProbabilistic model that uses Bayesian inference for probability calculations using a directed acyclic graph.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nA probabilistic graphical model encoding variables and their conditional dependencies as a directed acyclic graph — reasoning under uncertainty with explicit, inspectable probability structure. Governance-relevant because it's a naturally more interpretable model family than deep networks.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Recognize as a classic/symbolic-adjacent, relatively explainable ML technique — a contrast point to black-box deep learning.", "kt58": "## Definition\n\nEvaluating AI system performance against established standards, reference models, or industry baselines to assess relative quality and identify improvement areas.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nEvaluating a model against standardized datasets or tasks to compare performance across models or over time. The governance caveat is benchmark gaming and distribution mismatch: excellent benchmark scores prove little about behavior on your population — which is why ARIA-style realistic evaluation exists.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] 'It scored well on benchmarks' never substitutes for validation on the deployment population.", "kt59": "## Definition\n\nArt. 35 of Korea's AI Basic Act: operators providing high-impact AI must make efforts to assess its impact on fundamental rights in advance, but the assessment itself is not mandatory. Skipping it isn't fined, but public bodies must give priority to assessed products. The decree lists what the assessment covers.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nArt. 35 asks operators providing high-impact AI to try to assess its impact on fundamental rights before offering it. Skipping it brings no fine, and it cannot trigger fact-finding. The incentive is commercial: public bodies must give priority to assessed products, and MSIT may subsidize SMEs' assessments. The decree specifies what the assessment covers and allows it to be done in-house or by a third party; since 21 July 2026 it must also reflect AI-vulnerable groups.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Contrast the EU's FRIA under Art. 27, which is mandatory but applies only to certain deployers.", "kt60": "## Definition\n\nSystematic differential treatment of certain objects, persons, or groups compared to others. Treatment includes perception, observation, representation, prediction, or decision.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe ISO-flavored definition: systematic deviation from a true value or fair outcome — encompassing statistical bias (estimation error), computational bias, and the human/social biases that data absorbs. NIST SP 1270 organizes it in three categories: systemic, computational/statistical, and human-cognitive.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Bias is not only prejudice — statistical deviation counts too; the NIST three-category taxonomy is the tested structure.", "kt61": "## Definition\n\nThe impartial evaluation by an independent auditor that NYC Local Law 144 requires no more than one year before an AEDT is used. It calculates selection or scoring rates and impact ratios by sex, race/ethnicity and intersectional categories. The impact ratio is a category's rate divided by the rate of the highest category. Results must be published, but the law sets no pass/fail threshold.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nUnder DCWP's rules the auditor must be independent of both the employer and the vendor. The audit uses historical data unless there is too little, in which case test data may be used. It reports rates and impact ratios, plus how many people fell into unknown categories, and may leave out categories under 2% of the data. An employer may rely on a vendor's multi-employer audit only in limited circumstances.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] An impact ratio below 0.80 does not by itself violate Local Law 144; the law requires calculation and publication, not a passing score.", "kt62": "## Definition\n\nEvaluating AI system outputs for unfair differences across demographic groups, sensitive attributes, or protected characteristics to detect and mitigate algorithmic bias.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nEmpirically measuring model performance and outcomes across demographic slices — error rates, positive rates, and calibration per group, compared against fairness metrics chosen for the context. It requires access to (or sound inference of) sensitive attributes, which is why governed access beats attribute deletion.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Bias testing compares subgroup performance — overall accuracy alone can hide a 10-point gap between groups.", "kt63": "## Definition\n\nPersonal data resulting from specific technical processing relating to physical, physiological, or behavioral characteristics allowing unique identification — special category under GDPR.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nPersonal data from technical processing of physical, physiological, or behavioral characteristics — faceprints, fingerprints, voiceprints, gait. Under GDPR it becomes special-category when used to uniquely identify someone; under the AI Act, biometric use cases populate both the prohibited list (untargeted scraping, real-time remote ID) and Annex III high-risk categories.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] A photo is not special-category biometric data; a faceprint template used for identification is — the processing purpose flips the category.", "kt64": "## Definition\n\nGDPR requirement to notify the supervisory authority within 72 hours of becoming aware of a personal data breach, and to communicate high-risk breaches to affected data subjects.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nGDPR's clock-driven duty: personal data breaches go to the supervisory authority within 72 hours of awareness (Art 33) and to affected individuals without undue delay when risk is high (Art 34). AI adds novel breach shapes — model inversion or membership inference can constitute disclosure of personal data without any database being touched.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] 72 hours to the authority; individuals only when high risk — and remember parallel AI Act incident-reporting clocks can run simultaneously.", "kt65": "## Definition\n\nA model's tendency to fail catastrophically when encountering inputs slightly outside its training distribution — indicates lack of robustness and generalization.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAI's tendency to fail abruptly and completely when conditions deviate slightly from training experience — the opposite of the graceful degradation engineering aims for. Brittleness is why edge-case and robustness testing exist, and why confident model output in unfamiliar territory is a hazard.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Brittleness = catastrophic failure on small distribution shifts; robustness is its antonym and the design goal.", "kt66": "## Definition\n\nThe organizational environment, objectives, constraints, and stakeholder expectations within which an AI system is designed, developed, and deployed.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe organizational reality an AI system serves: objectives, sector, risk appetite, regulatory exposure, and the workflows it lives inside. Context determines proportionality — identical models warrant different governance in ad targeting versus clinical triage — and grounding systems in defined business context is the first design-phase control.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] ISO 42001's 'context of the organization' clause institutionalizes this — governance is always calibrated to context, never one-size.", "kt67": "## Definition\n\nThe organizational goals that an AI system is intended to serve — deployment decisions must start with objectives rather than model capability alone.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe measurable outcomes an AI initiative exists to achieve — defined before development so success criteria, validation thresholds, and monitoring targets have something to anchor to. Objectives also bound scope: a system approved to reduce fraud losses was not approved to screen employees.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Success criteria defined up front enable everything downstream — their absence is the root cause in many 'what went wrong' scenarios.", "kt68": "## Definition\n\nCalifornia law requiring covered providers (generative AI systems with over 1 million monthly users that are publicly accessible in California) to offer a free AI detection tool, embed latent provenance disclosures in AI-generated image, video and audio, and give users the option of a visible label. As amended by AB 853 it became operative on 2 Aug 2026, adding platform duties in 2027 and capture-device duties in 2028. Penalties are $5,000 per violation per day, enforced by the AG, city attorneys and county counsel.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nSB 942 tackles synthetic-media deception through provenance. Covered providers must make their AI content detectable (a free detection tool) and traceable (latent metadata naming the provider, system version, time and a unique ID), while visible labels are left to user choice. AB 853 delayed the start to 2 Aug 2026 and extended provenance duties to large online platforms (2027) and makers of cameras and phones (2028).\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Latent disclosure is mandatory, the visible (manifest) label is a user option, and text output is not covered.", "kt69": "## Definition\n\nCalifornia's Transparency in Frontier Artificial Intelligence Act, in force 1 Jan 2026. Frontier developers (models trained with more than 10^26 operations) must publish transparency reports and report critical safety incidents to Cal OES within 15 days, or within 24 hours if there is imminent danger. Large frontier developers (over $500M revenue) must also publish and follow a frontier AI framework and send Cal OES summaries of internal-use risk assessments every three months, or on another reasonable schedule. It adds whistleblower protections, and the AG can seek up to $1M per violation.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nSB 53 is a transparency law for the largest model developers, not a licensing regime. It requires published frameworks and transparency reports, confidential internal-use risk summaries and incident reports to the Office of Emergency Services, and whistleblower protections, all backed by AG penalties. It requires no third-party audits. New York's RAISE Act and Illinois SB 315 copied its thresholds and definitions.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] SB 53 incidents go to Cal OES within 15 days (24 hours if imminent), compared with 72 hours in New York and Illinois.", "kt70": "## Definition\n\nCalifornia Privacy Protection Agency rules, effective 1 Jan 2026, for businesses that use automated decision-making technology to make significant decisions about consumers (lending, housing, education, employment, health care). A business must give a pre-use notice, offer an opt-out unless an exception applies (such as a human appeal) and answer access requests about the ADMT's logic and outcome. Compliance is required by 1 Jan 2027. Companion rules require risk assessments and cybersecurity audits.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe CPPA rules turn CCPA rights into AI-specific ones: a specific pre-use notice, an opt-out (unless the business offers a qualifying human appeal or another exception applies) and an explanation of logic and outcome on request. A tool that only supports a human who genuinely reviews the output and can change the decision falls outside the ADMT definition. The same package adds risk assessments and annual cybersecurity audits phased in by revenue.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Know the dates: regulations effective 1 Jan 2026, ADMT compliance 1 Jan 2027, first risk-assessment attestations 1 Apr 2028.", "kt71": "## Definition\n\nThe physical or digital marking indicating conformity with EU health, safety, and environmental requirements — required for high-risk AI systems under the EU AI Act.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe EU's conformity mark: affixing it declares a high-risk AI system has passed conformity assessment against the AI Act's requirements, making it eligible for the EU market. It's the visible end of an evidentiary chain — risk management, data governance, technical documentation, assessment — that authorities can unwind on inspection.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Sequence matters: conformity assessment → CE marking → market placement; importers and distributors verify the mark exists.", "kt72": "## Definition\n\nA privacy principle giving individuals options about how their personal data is collected and used, including the ability to opt out of certain processing.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe privacy principle of giving individuals meaningful options over their data — consent where required, opt-outs where offered, and honored preferences either way. AI strains it because downstream training uses are hard to explain at collection time, and choice given but ignored is worse than no choice at all.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Notice tells; choice empowers — an exam scenario offering buried, illusory, or coerced options is testing whether the choice was meaningful.", "kt73": "## Definition\n\nTraditional AI approaches using rule-based systems, statistical methods, and simpler ML algorithms — contrasted with modern generative AI and LLMs.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe symbolic, rule-based tradition — knowledge encoded as explicit rules and logic (expert systems, search, planning) rather than learned from data. Its governance virtue is transparency: the rules can be read. Its limitation — brittleness outside coded knowledge — is what machine learning displaced.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Symbolic/classic AI = hand-coded rules, explainable; connectionist/ML = learned patterns, opaque — a recurring contrast pair.", "kt74": "## Definition\n\nThe deployment-environment decision: cloud offers scalability and managed infrastructure but raises data-residency and third-party questions; on-premises maximizes control and data sovereignty at higher operational cost; edge deployment puts inference on local devices for latency and privacy benefits with harder update and monitoring logistics.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe environment decision behind every AI deployment: cloud offers elastic scale and managed operations while importing third-party and data-residency exposure; on-premises keeps data and control in-house at real operational cost; edge pushes inference onto local devices for latency and privacy wins, with harder update, monitoring, and security logistics. Regulatory constraints (localization, sectoral rules) often decide before economics do.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Data-residency or sovereignty requirements in a scenario point away from cloud — the environment choice is a compliance decision, not just an infrastructure one.", "kt75": "## Definition\n\nThe voluntary Code of Practice on Transparency of AI-generated Content, final 10 June 2026 under AI Act Article 50(7). Section 1 covers providers' machine-readable marking and detection; Section 2 covers deployers' labelling of deepfakes and AI-generated public-interest text. The Commission and AI Board assessed it as adequate.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nDrawn up by independent experts in two working groups that mirror Article 50 (providers and deployers), it was published in final form on 10 June 2026, ahead of Article 50 applying on 2 August 2026. Signatories can rely on its measures to show compliance with Article 50(2), (4) and (5); non-signatories must prove their own measures adequate to each market surveillance authority. About 190 organisations had signed by the end of July 2026, and the EU offers icons for labels.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Two different codes: the GPAI Code covers Articles 53 and 55 (models); the Marking and Labelling Code covers Article 50 (outputs).", "kt76": "## Definition\n\nA category of AI systems that enables more natural interaction between humans and machines.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nA largely marketing-era label for systems that simulate human thought processes — perception, reasoning, natural language — popularized around IBM Watson. In taxonomy terms it gestures at the same territory as AI broadly; treat it as vocabulary rather than a distinct technology.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Recognize the synonym; don't over-analyze — it's tested as terminology awareness.", "kt77": "## Definition\n\nColorado's 2026 law (signed 14 May 2026) that repealed and replaced the never-effective Colorado AI Act (SB 24-205). For consequential decisions made from 1 Jan 2027 in education, employment, housing, lending, insurance, health care and government services, it requires developer documentation, deployer notice, a plain-language explanation within 30 days of an adverse outcome, data correction and human review. The AG enforces exclusively, with a 60-day cure; there is no private right of action.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nColorado rewrote its AI law before it ever applied. SB 26-189 dropped SB 24-205's risk-management model (impact assessments, a duty of reasonable care) for a disclosure-and-rights model: notice before use, an explanation of adverse outcomes within 30 days, correction of inaccurate data and meaningful human review, with developers supplying documentation to deployers. HIPAA entities (except for hiring), FDA-regulated devices and compliant insurers are largely carved out.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] If an answer mentions Colorado impact assessments or a duty of reasonable care, it describes the repealed SB 24-205, not the law that applies from 2027.", "kt78": "## Definition\n\nRegulatory or oversight bodies\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe bodies each EU member state designates to supervise and enforce the AI Act — notifying authorities (overseeing conformity-assessment bodies) and market surveillance authorities (investigating systems, ordering corrective action, imposing penalties). They anchor national enforcement while the AI Office handles GPAI at Union level.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] National authorities enforce against most systems; the European AI Office supervises GPAI — jurisdiction questions split along that line.", "kt79": "## Definition\n\nA characteristic of AI systems requiring governance: advanced techniques like neural networks create interrelationships between inputs and outputs that are difficult to trace or understand.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nComplexity is the characteristic of AI systems that defeats intuition and simple review: many interacting components, high-dimensional models, and behaviour that emerges from the interactions rather than from any single part.\n\nIt is a risk source in its own right. ISO/IEC 23894's Annex B catalogs AI risk sources, and system complexity and lifecycle complexity both appear there, because more moving parts mean more failure modes, harder root-cause analysis, and more places for a control to be assumed but not present. In an AI system the complexity is layered: the model itself, the data pipeline that feeds it, the integration that calls it, the human workflow around it, and the third-party components inside all of those.\n\nThe governance response is not to demand simplicity, which would mean abandoning the capability. It is to decompose deliberately: document the components and their interfaces, test at the boundaries as well as end to end, and build the observability that makes a failure traceable to a cause. Layered controls (testing, monitoring, oversight) exist precisely because no single control can see the whole system.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Complexity justifies layered controls: testing plus monitoring plus oversight. An option concluding that a system is 'too complex to govern' or 'too complex to explain' is never the right answer; the right answer decomposes the system and adds controls.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://www.iso.org/standard/77304.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 23894 AI risk management</a> · Annex B lists system and lifecycle complexity as AI risk sources\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · contextual risk framing and the Map function", "kt80": "## Definition\n\nThe ability of a functional unit to capture, process, and interpret image or video data. May include wavelengths beyond visible light (e.g., infrared).\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe AI field enabling machines to derive meaning from images and video — classification, detection, segmentation, recognition. Governance flashpoints: facial recognition (biometric identification law), surveillance proportionality, and safety-critical perception in vehicles and medicine.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] When a scenario involves cameras identifying people, jump to biometric/special-category analysis and the AI Act's biometric restrictions.", "kt81": "## Definition\n\nA change in the underlying relationship between input data and expected outputs over time, often due to real-world changes that invalidate the model's learned patterns.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe world changing under the model: the relationship between inputs and outputs shifts (fraud patterns evolve, consumer behavior changes), so a model trained on yesterday's relationship degrades even if input data looks the same. Detection needs outcome monitoring, and the remedy is retraining on current reality.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Concept drift = input→output relationship changes; data drift = input distribution changes — the exam tests the distinction precisely.", "kt82": "## Definition\n\nNIST AI 600-1 GAI risk: a generative model confidently producing false or fabricated content (often called hallucination). Classified among the technical/model risks unique to or exacerbated by GAI.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nNIST AI 600-1's formal term for hallucination: generative models producing confidently stated, plausible, false content — a structural property of next-token prediction, not an occasional bug. Governance responses: grounding (RAG), verification workflows for consequential uses, user training against over-trust, and honest capability communication.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Confabulation/hallucination is the signature GAI risk — listed first among AI 600-1's twelve; 'the model never errs' options are automatically wrong.", "kt83": "## Definition\n\nThe process of verifying that high-risk AI systems meet EU AI Act requirements before market placement — may involve self-assessment or third-party notified bodies.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe EU AI Act's pre-market verification: demonstrating a high-risk system meets Chapter III requirements — via internal control for most Annex III categories, or a notified body for certain cases (notably biometrics). It produces the evidence chain behind the CE mark, and must be repeated after substantial modification.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Most high-risk systems allow provider self-assessment (internal control); third-party notified-body assessment is the exception, not the rule.", "kt84": "## Definition\n\nThe specific standards, regulations, and internal policies an AI system must satisfy before deployment — including technical, legal, and ethical compliance obligations.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe substantive bar a high-risk system must clear: risk management system, data governance and quality, technical documentation (Annex IV), record-keeping/logging, transparency and instructions for use, human oversight, and accuracy/robustness/cybersecurity. Conformity assessment verifies these; CE marking declares them.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Learn the requirement set as a chain — exam questions ask which link is missing in a scenario.", "kt85": "## Definition\n\nA form of cognitive modeling that uses a network of interconnected units, which are generally simple computational units.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe intelligence-from-networks school: cognition emerging from many simple connected units with learned weights — the paradigm behind neural networks and deep learning, standing opposite symbolic AI's explicit rules. Its triumph is capability; its governance cost is opacity.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Connectionist = subsymbolic = neural — three labels for the learned-weights paradigm, contrasted with symbolic/rule-based AI.", "kt86": "## Definition\n\nA privacy principle requiring freely given, specific, informed, and unambiguous indication of the data subject's agreement to processing of their personal data.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nGDPR's most demanding lawful basis: freely given, specific, informed, unambiguous, given by clear affirmative act, withdrawable as easily as given — with the burden of proof on the controller. For AI training it's often the wrong tool (hard to make specific and informed for future model uses), which is why compatibility analysis and other bases get exam attention.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Test each adjective: bundled (not specific), buried (not informed), or forced (not free) consent fails — and consent is one of six bases, not the default.", "kt87": "## Definition\n\nLaws and regulations prohibiting unfair, deceptive, or abusive acts and practices (UDAAP/UDAP) in consumer transactions — applies to AI-driven products and services.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe body of law shielding consumers from unfair, deceptive, or abusive commercial practices — for AI, chiefly the FTC Act §5 and state UDAP statutes. Enforcement themes: AI-washing (overstated capability claims), undisclosed bots, algorithmic pricing manipulation, and dark patterns — with algorithmic disgorgement (model deletion) among the remedies.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] No AI exemption exists: if a human doing it would be deceptive, an algorithm doing it is too.", "kt88": "## Definition\n\nTracking the origin and history of digital content. One of the four primary GAI considerations in NIST AI 600-1, alongside governance, pre-deployment testing, and incident disclosure.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nTracking the origin and history of digital content — who or what created it, and how it's been modified — via techniques like watermarking, cryptographic signing, and metadata standards (C2PA). NIST AI 600-1 elevates it as one of four primary GAI considerations because synthetic media at scale breaks the default assumption that content reflects reality.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Provenance of content (this one) vs. provenance of data (training-data origin) — same word, different governance layers.", "kt89": "## Definition\n\nThe ability of people affected by an AI system's output or decision to challenge it and obtain a review and, where justified, a correction; it needs enough transparency about how the result was reached to argue with. Reflected in OECD AI Principle 1.3 and in the GDPR Art. 22(3) right to contest solely automated decisions; IAPP also calls it redress.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nContestability gives the affected person a practical path to challenge an AI-influenced decision: notice that AI was involved, reasons they can engage with, a channel for raising a challenge, and a human reviewer with the authority to change the result.\n\nTwo legal anchors make it concrete. **GDPR Article 22(3)** requires, for automated decisions made under the contract or explicit-consent exceptions, at least human intervention, the opportunity to express a view, and the right to contest the decision. **OECD Principle 1.3** asks AI actors to provide information enabling those adversely affected by an AI system to challenge its output, which extends contestability beyond the narrow automated-decision case toward a general expectation.\n\nThe distinction that gets tested: **contestability is exercised by the affected person, while human-in-the-loop review is exercised by the operator.** They are not substitutes. An operator reviewing its own recommendation does not give the applicant a route to challenge it, and a complaints channel without a reviewer empowered to reverse the decision is not contestability either. What makes it real is authority to change the outcome.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Contestability belongs to the affected person; human-in-the-loop review belongs to the operator. Both need a human with authority to change the result, but only one gives the subject a route to raise the challenge.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://gdpr-info.eu/art-22-gdpr/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GDPR Article 22 (automated individual decision-making)</a> · human intervention, expressing a view, contesting the decision\n- <a href=\"https://oecd.ai/en/ai-principles\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OECD AI Principles</a> · Principle 1.3 on challengeable outcomes\n- <a href=\"https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/artificial-intelligence/explaining-decisions-made-with-artificial-intelligence/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ICO: explaining decisions made with AI</a> · how contestability works in practice", "kt90": "## Definition\n\nISO 42001 Clause 4: understanding internal and external factors affecting the AI Management System, including stakeholder needs, legal requirements, and organizational objectives.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nISO 42001's opening clause (inherited from Annex SL): before building an AIMS, the organization must determine its internal and external context — its role in the AI value chain, stakeholder expectations, regulatory environment, and climate. Everything downstream (scope, objectives, risk criteria) calibrates to this analysis.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Clause 4 in every Annex SL standard — context analysis precedes and shapes scope, a sequencing fact exams like.", "kt91": "## Definition\n\nOngoing observation of deployed AI system performance, data inputs/outputs, and environmental conditions to detect drift, degradation, or unexpected behavior in real time.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe always-on watch over deployed AI: performance and error patterns, data and concept drift, fairness metrics over time, security anomalies, and usage/reliance patterns — wired to thresholds, alerts, owners, and playbooks so detection produces action. It exists because AI degrades silently: models keep answering confidently as the world moves.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] The EU AI Act makes post-market monitoring mandatory for high-risk systems — monitoring answers are compliance answers, not just hygiene.", "kt92": "## Definition\n\nOngoing verification that system meets requirements in production\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nExtending validation past launch: periodically re-verifying that a deployed model still meets its requirements — accuracy, fairness, robustness — on current data, not the data it was born on. It closes the gap between point-in-time approval and a continuously changing world.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] One-time validation at deployment is the flagged anti-pattern; 'valid then' does not mean 'valid now.'", "kt93": "## Definition\n\nA property of an AI system that enables a human or another external agent to intervene in the system's operation.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nISO 22989 trustworthiness property: the degree to which humans (or external agents) can intervene in a system's functioning — pause, correct, constrain, or shut down. It's the engineering substrate of human oversight: Article 14's override requirements assume controllability was designed in.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Controllability is a system design property; human oversight is the operational practice it enables — build the first to deliver the second.", "kt94": "## Definition\n\nFeedforward neural network with convolution in at least one of its layers.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe CNN is the neural architecture built for grid-like data. Convolutional filters slide across an image learning local patterns in layers (edges, then textures, then parts, then objects), sharing weights across positions so the network needs far fewer parameters than a dense network would for the same task.\n\nIt is the architecture behind computer vision: image classification, object detection, medical imaging, facial recognition. That lineage means CNNs inherit vision's governance flashpoints directly. A face-recognition CNN used to identify people is processing **biometric data for unique identification**, which is a special category under GDPR Article 9, and the same use case sits in both the EU AI Act's prohibited practices (untargeted facial-image scraping, real-time remote biometric identification in public spaces) and its Annex III high-risk categories.\n\nThere is also a security dimension: vision models are the classic target of adversarial examples, where small, often imperceptible perturbations flip a classification. NIST AI 100-2 catalogs this as evasion. For governance, the practical point is that a CNN's accuracy figure describes average behaviour, not behaviour under an input chosen to break it.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Architecture matching: CNN for images and spatial data, RNN or LSTM for sequences, transformer for modern language. When a scenario involves cameras identifying people, move immediately to biometric and special-category analysis.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-2e2023.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 100-2, Adversarial Machine Learning taxonomy</a> · evasion attacks against vision models\n- <a href=\"https://gdpr-info.eu/art-9-gdpr/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GDPR, Article 9 (special categories)</a> · biometric data used for unique identification\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/5/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 5 (prohibited practices)</a> · biometric prohibitions", "kt95": "## Definition\n\nLegal protection for original works of authorship fixed in a tangible medium. AI training on copyrighted content without authorization is subject to ongoing litigation (e.g., Getty v. Stability AI).\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe exclusive-rights regime protecting original works of authorship — colliding with AI at input (training on protected works: fair use in the US, TDM exceptions with opt-outs in the EU), output (no US copyright without human authorship; outputs may infringe memorized works), and obligation (GPAI providers must maintain copyright policies under the AI Act).\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Two separate questions — 'can we train on it?' (contested) and 'is the output protected?' (not without human authorship) — don't let one answer bleed into the other.", "kt96": "## Definition\n\nGPAI Code of Practice chapter applying to ALL GPAI providers: a copyright policy, compliance with text-and-data-mining opt-outs under Art 4(3) CDSM, and measures against infringing outputs.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe GPAI Code of Practice chapter binding all signatory GPAI providers on copyright: maintain a copyright policy, honor machine-readable text-and-data-mining opt-outs under CDSM Article 4(3), and implement measures to prevent infringing output. It operationalizes AI Act Article 53(1)(c).\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Applies to ALL GPAI providers — only the Safety & Security chapter is limited to systemic-risk models.", "kt97": "## Definition\n\nA fairness criterion assessing whether a model's decision would change if a sensitive attribute (gender, race) were altered while keeping all other factors constant.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nA causal fairness criterion: a decision is fair if it would remain the same in a counterfactual world where the individual's protected attribute differed but everything else causally consistent stayed equal. Conceptually elegant, practically demanding — it requires a causal model of how attributes influence features.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Recognize it as an individual-level, causal fairness definition — contrast with group statistical metrics like demographic parity.", "kt98": "## Definition\n\nThe transfer of personal data outside the EU/EEA, requiring appropriate safeguards such as adequacy decisions, standard contractual clauses, or binding corporate rules.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nMoving personal data across jurisdictions — under GDPR Chapter V, lawful only via adequacy decisions, appropriate safeguards (SCCs, BCRs), or narrow derogations, with Schrems II demanding case-by-case assessment of destination-country surveillance law. AI complicates it: training pipelines and cloud inference casually move data in ways architecture diagrams forget.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] For US transfers, think adequacy-via-Data-Privacy-Framework or SCCs plus transfer impact assessment — 'we use a US cloud' is a transfer.", "kt99": "## Definition\n\nMultiple departments (legal, engineering, ethics, product, compliance) sharing responsibility for AI governance decisions to ensure diverse perspectives.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nCross-functional collaboration is the structural requirement that AI governance decisions be made by legal, privacy, security, data science, ethics, risk and business together, because each function sees failure modes the others miss. The BOK states its purpose as efficacy and **diversity of expertise and perspective**.\n\nThe failure it prevents is specific. A governance function centralized under legal produces compliance-shaped answers with no technical depth; centralized under engineering it produces technically sound systems with no regulatory or ethical read; centralized under a business unit it produces whatever ships. No single function holds the full picture, which is why siloed AI decisions are how governance failures start.\n\nThe mechanisms are ordinary: a standing cross-functional governance council that owns the framework and reviews high-risk use cases, use-case reviews triggered by defined conditions (rights-affecting automated decisions, sensitive or biometric data, employment or lending or medical impact, low explainability, rising regulatory scrutiny), shared artifacts that force common vocabulary (model cards, risk assessment forms, impact assessments, ethics checklists), and escalation paths with a real decision right at the top.\n\nCollaboration also needs maintenance rather than a charter. Bridge language gaps with joint workshops and rotational roles, include governance in performance goals so participation is not purely voluntary, and protect the ability to raise concerns without cost, because the people closest to the technology often see the risk first.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Single-function answers (only legal, only IT, only data science) are systematically wrong. Multidisciplinary composition is the tested principle, and the mechanism is a standing cross-functional body with a defined trigger for review and a real escalation path.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF Playbook, Govern function</a> · cross-functional roles, accountability and culture actions\n- <a href=\"https://www.iso.org/standard/42001\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 42001 (AI management system)</a> · clause 5 leadership and organizational roles\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · Govern as the cross-cutting foundation for the other functions", "kt100": "## Definition\n\nThe process of collecting and sourcing data for AI training, including verification of lawful rights, quality assessment, and documentation of origin.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nObtaining data for AI — collection, purchase, licensing, scraping, or partnership — with governance gates at the door: legal rights to each source, terms permitting the intended use, provenance recording, and quality screening. Most training-data legal problems are acquisition problems discovered too late.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Possessing data ≠ having rights to train on it — the acquisition-stage rights check is the tested control.", "kt101": "## Definition\n\nA process in which data is provided with a set of descriptive information without the data itself being altered.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAttaching labels to raw data so supervised learning has ground truth — often via human labelers whose guidelines, consistency, and biases directly shape model behavior. Governance touches: labeling instructions as bias vectors, inter-annotator agreement as quality metric, and labor/sub-tier considerations in outsourced annotation.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Label quality caps model quality — biased or inconsistent annotation is a root cause the exam expects you to trace.", "kt102": "## Definition\n\nThe process of creating synthetic samples by modifying or utilizing existing data.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nExpanding training data by transforming what exists — rotations and crops for images, paraphrases for text, synthetic minority-class examples for imbalance. It combats overfitting and representation gaps, but augmented data inherits and can amplify source biases.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] A legitimate answer to insufficient or imbalanced data — but not a substitute for genuinely representative collection.", "kt103": "## Definition\n\nThe entity that determines the purposes and means of processing personal data — carries primary accountability for GDPR compliance in AI contexts.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nGDPR's accountable role: the entity that determines the purposes and means of processing personal data. Controllers own compliance — lawful basis, transparency, data subject rights, DPIAs, processor contracts — and the label follows facts, not contracts: deciding what a model is trained for makes you a controller regardless of what the paperwork says.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] In AI supply chains, ask who decides 'why' and 'how' — the model vendor processing on instructions is a processor; the customer deciding purposes is the controller.", "kt104": "## Definition\n\nA characteristic of AI requiring governance: most AI systems depend heavily on data quality, volume, and diversity — poor or biased data leads to inaccurate or unfair outcomes.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAI's defining reliance: system behavior is a function of the data it was trained and fed on, making data quality, representativeness, and availability first-order risk factors. Dependencies also chain — upstream source changes silently propagate into model behavior downstream.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] An ISO 23894 risk-source theme: when a scenario's model misbehaves, interrogate the data dependencies before the algorithm.", "kt105": "## Definition\n\nA change in the statistical properties of input data over time, potentially degrading model performance as the production data diverges from training data.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nProduction inputs drifting away from the training distribution — new demographics, changed formats, seasonal shifts — degrading performance even though the world's underlying input-output relationship holds. Statistical distribution monitoring catches it; retraining on current data remedies it.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Data drift = inputs change; concept drift = the relationship changes — pick precisely, the exam pairs them as distractors.", "kt106": "## Definition\n\nThe overall management of data availability, usability, integrity, and security — ensuring data is lawfully collected, accurate, sufficient, and fit for its intended AI use.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nManaging data as a governed asset: lawful authority to use each source, quality dimensions measured, fitness-for-purpose verified, stewardship roles assigned, lineage recorded, and security applied across the pipeline. In AI it is the upstream half of model governance — most model failures are data failures with a delay.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] The EU AI Act makes data governance an explicit high-risk requirement (training/validation/testing data quality and relevance) — it's law, not just practice.", "kt107": "## Definition\n\nThe accuracy, consistency, and trustworthiness of data throughout its lifecycle — critical for AI systems where corrupted or tampered data produces unreliable outputs.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAssurance that data is accurate, complete, and unaltered except through authorized change — spanning accidental corruption and deliberate tampering (data poisoning being the AI-specific attack). Controls: validation checks, access restriction, versioning, checksums, and tamper-evident logging.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Poisoned training data is an integrity failure with model-wide blast radius — integrity controls on training pipelines are security controls.", "kt108": "## Definition\n\nAccidental exposure of confidential, proprietary or personal data to people or systems that should not have it, for example through a misconfigured cloud bucket, a misdirected email or an employee entering records into an external AI tool. When personal data is involved it is a GDPR personal data breach even when no attacker is involved (Art. 4(12)), so the 72-hour notification duty to the supervisory authority can apply (Art. 33).\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nA leak is accidental exposure rather than a deliberate attack: a storage bucket left public, an email to the wrong person, or an employee pasting records into an external AI tool that retains its inputs. Under the GDPR the difference from a hack does not matter, because a personal data breach covers accidental as well as unlawful disclosure or access (Art. 4(12)). The controller must notify the supervisory authority without undue delay and, where feasible, within 72 hours unless the breach is unlikely to result in a risk (Art. 33), and must tell affected people without undue delay when the risk is high (Art. 34).\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Accidental does not mean exempt: a data leak of personal data is a GDPR personal data breach, and the 72-hour clock runs toward the supervisory authority, not toward the individuals.", "kt109": "## Definition\n\nThe tracking of data's lifecycle and processing — where it came from, how it was transformed, where it moved. Essential for traceability and audit readiness.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe recorded journey of data from source through every transformation, join, filter, and feature-engineering step to the training set — the data's biography. It answers the incident question ('what fed this model?'), the audit question ('prove it'), and the rights question ('whose data is in here?').\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Lineage = the journey; provenance = the origin — the definitional split is a reliable test item.", "kt110": "## Definition\n\nA GDPR principle requiring that only personal data necessary for the specific task be collected and processed — critical for AI systems that tend to hoard data.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe GDPR principle that data must be adequate, relevant, and limited to what the purpose requires — applied to AI: fewer features, narrower collection, shorter retention, and the discipline to drop the 35 features that add nothing over the 15 that suffice. Less data means less privacy risk, smaller attack surface, and simpler models.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] The 50-features-but-15-perform-equally scenario has one answer: use 15 — 'more data always helps' options are bait.", "kt111": "## Definition\n\nAn attack that corrupts a model's training data, for example by planting mislabeled or trigger-laden samples in a scraped or third-party dataset, so the trained model learns wrong or hidden behaviour such as a backdoor. It strikes during training, unlike evasion attacks and prompt injection, which manipulate inputs at inference time; the EU AI Act expects high-risk providers to address it where appropriate (Art. 15(5)).\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nPoisoning happens before deployment: attackers tamper with the data a model learns from (scraped web content, crowdsourced labels, third-party datasets, fine-tuning data) so it learns wrong patterns or a backdoor that a trigger unlocks later. Because a poisoned model can pass ordinary tests, defences focus on data provenance and integrity controls, vetting of sources, anomaly detection and targeted testing for triggers. NIST's adversarial machine learning taxonomy (AI 100-2 E2025) treats poisoning and evasion as distinct attack classes, and the EU AI Act lists data poisoning among the AI-specific vulnerabilities in Art. 15(5).\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Poisoning corrupts training data; evasion and prompt injection manipulate inputs at inference time; model inversion and membership inference extract information.", "kt112": "## Definition\n\nA mandatory risk assessment under GDPR Article 35 required when processing is likely to result in high risk to individuals, including when AI processes sensitive data.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nGDPR Article 35's mandatory risk analysis for likely-high-risk processing: systematic description, necessity and proportionality assessment, risk evaluation, and mitigation measures — with supervisory-authority consultation if risk stays high. AI profiling with significant effects is a canonical trigger.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] DPIA is legally required (personal data, GDPR); AIIA is broader best practice — scope and legal force distinguish them.", "kt113": "## Definition\n\nA mandatory role under GDPR for certain organizations — responsible for advising on data protection obligations, monitoring compliance, and serving as contact point for authorities.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nGDPR's mandated independent advisor (Art 37-39) for public authorities and organizations with large-scale systematic monitoring or special-category processing: informs and advises, monitors compliance, advises on DPIAs, and liaises with supervisory authorities — reporting to top management, protected from dismissal for doing the job.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] The DPO advises and monitors — the controller stays accountable; making the DPO 'responsible for compliance' is a wrong-answer pattern.", "kt114": "## Definition\n\nDocumentation of data's origin, collection method, licensing, and conditions of collection — distinct from lineage which tracks movement and transformation.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe origin story of data: source, collection method, date, legal terms, and consent status — recorded at acquisition because it cannot be reconstructed later. Provenance underwrites every downstream claim: lawful training data, honored licenses, defensible deletion, and credible audits.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Provenance = origin; lineage = journey through transformations — paired and tested together.", "kt115": "## Definition\n\nThe degree to which a dataset is fit for the job it will do, judged on dimensions such as correctness, completeness, consistency, timeliness, validity and representativeness; weak data produces weak or biased outputs. For high-risk systems the EU AI Act requires training, validation and testing data to be relevant, sufficiently representative and, to the best extent possible, free of errors and complete for the intended purpose (Art. 10(3)).\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nQuality is always relative to purpose: the same dataset can suit one use and be unfit for another, which is why the BOK pairs quality with quantity, integrity and fitness for purpose (III.B). Representativeness of the people a system will actually affect deserves particular attention, because gaps there turn directly into biased performance. For high-risk systems Art. 10(3) of the EU AI Act makes these legal criteria, adding that data sets must have appropriate statistical properties for the persons or groups on whom the system will be used.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Under-representation of the population a system will serve is a data quality failure under Article 10(3), even if every individual record is accurate.", "kt116": "## Definition\n\nA process in which data is checked for completeness, systematic deviations (bias), and other factors that impair its usefulness for an AI system.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe structured checkpoint assessing data against quality dimensions — accuracy, completeness, consistency, timeliness, validity, uniqueness — before it trains anything. Reviews recur: quality decays as sources evolve, so gates belong at acquisition, pre-training, and periodically in production pipelines.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Sequencing: quality review precedes training — discovering data problems via model misbehavior means the gate was missing.", "kt117": "## Definition\n\nProcess of selecting a subset of data samples expected to exhibit similar patterns and trends as the larger dataset.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nSelecting a subset of data for training or analysis — where representativeness is won or lost: random, stratified, or weighted sampling each shape which populations the model actually learns. Sampling bias (convenience samples, survivorship) is a quiet root cause of downstream unfairness.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] When a model fails on a subgroup, interrogate the sampling frame — stratified sampling is the standard fix for imbalance.", "kt118": "## Definition\n\nCollection of data with a common format.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nA structured collection of data serving a defined role in the ML lifecycle — training, validation, or test. Dataset-level governance artifacts (datasheets, version tags, quality metrics, licenses) make each set auditable; leakage between the three roles invalidates every metric computed on them.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Train/validation/test separation is sacred — 'we tuned on the test set' scenarios are describing invalidated evaluation.", "kt119": "## Definition\n\nA standardized document describing a dataset's composition, collection process, recommended uses, and limitations — supports responsible data governance for AI training.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nDatasheets for datasets: standardized documentation covering motivation, composition, collection process, preprocessing, recommended and discouraged uses — the dataset's spec sheet, by analogy to electronic components. Paired with model cards, they form the documentation backbone of accountable AI.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Datasheet documents a dataset; model card documents a model; system card documents the deployed assembly — match precisely.", "kt120": "## Definition\n\nA formal request by an individual to an organization to access their personal data and information about how it is processed — challenging when AI models have memorized training data.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe exercised right of access: an individual demands their personal data plus the GDPR Article 15 metadata — purposes, categories, recipients, retention, rights, and meaningful information about automated decision-making logic. AI complicates fulfillment: data may live in training sets, feature stores, and arguably in model-encoded form.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Access includes 'meaningful information about the logic' of automated decisions — not the source code, but a real explanation.", "kt121": "## Definition\n\nGDPR-granted rights including access (Art 15), rectification (Art 16), erasure (Art 17), restriction (Art 18), portability (Art 20), and objection (Art 21).\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nGDPR's individual entitlements: access, rectification, erasure, restriction, portability, objection, and Article 22 protections against solely automated decisions. Each has an AI wrinkle — erasure vs. trained models, objection vs. profiling, access vs. inferred data — and honoring them requires knowing where personal data flows through the ML lifecycle.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Rights are not absolute (erasure has exceptions; access can't violate others' rights) — but 'AI makes it impossible' is never a valid refusal.", "kt122": "## Definition\n\nDocumented procedures for safely taking an AI system offline — triggered by regulatory requirements, performance issues, or safety concerns with controlled shutdown steps.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe predefined rulebook for turning AI systems off: triggers (risk thresholds breached, regulation changed, performance decayed, vendor sunset), authority to invoke, decommissioning procedure, data disposition, and stakeholder notice. Its existence converts retirement from emergency improvisation into a governed decision.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Mature-practice marker: the organization decided *in advance* when and how systems die — exam scenarios reward predefined triggers.", "kt123": "## Definition\n\nModel in which inference is encoded as paths from the root to a leaf node in a tree structure.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nA model that splits data through a sequence of readable if-then branches to a prediction — among the most interpretable ML techniques, since the path to any decision can be traced and explained. Ensembles of trees (random forests, gradient boosting) trade that readability back for accuracy.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] The go-to example of interpretable ML — when a scenario demands explainability over raw accuracy, tree-based simplicity is the contrast to deep learning.", "kt124": "## Definition\n\nPlanned retirement, data disposition, model archival\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe operational teardown of an AI system: executing the retirement plan — dependency wind-down, successor cutover, access and credential revocation, data return/deletion per obligations, artifact archival, and monitoring shutdown, with records preserved beyond the system's death. Verification closes it: confirming nothing still silently depends on the corpse.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Records outlive the system — EU AI Act documentation retention continues after decommissioning; 'delete everything at shutdown' is wrong.", "kt125": "## Definition\n\n*\\<Artificial Intelligence>* An approach to creating rich hierarchical representations through the training of neural networks with many hidden layers. Deep learning is a subset of ML.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nDeep learning is machine learning with many-layered neural networks that learn hierarchical representations directly from raw data, removing the hand-engineered features that earlier approaches required. It is what made modern vision, speech and language AI possible, and it is the paradigm that turned opacity into a governance problem: capability arrived at the cost of inspectability.\n\nTwo governance consequences follow from the mechanism. First, because representations are learned rather than specified, the system's behaviour is a property of its training data, so data governance becomes model governance. Second, because the learned weights carry no legible logic, explanation has to be reconstructed after the fact, which is why post-hoc explainability techniques (SHAP, LIME, counterfactuals) exist at all.\n\nThe subset relationships are worth keeping straight: deep learning is a subset of machine learning, which is a subset of AI. \"Deep\" refers to the number of layers in the network, not to the depth of the system's understanding.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Deep learning is a subset of machine learning, which is a subset of AI, and 'deep' means many layers. Distractors often describe deep learning as a separate technology from ML rather than a family within it.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · neural network and deep learning definitions\n- <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.07874\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SHAP: a unified approach to interpreting model predictions</a> · post-hoc explanation of black-box models\n- <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.04938\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LIME: explaining the predictions of any classifier</a> · the other standard post-hoc technique", "kt126": "## Definition\n\nImage, audio or video content generated or manipulated with AI so that it resembles real people, objects, places or events closely enough to pass as authentic; a common tool for impersonation fraud, disinformation and harassment. The EU AI Act defines a deep fake in Art. 3(60) and requires deployers who use AI to generate or manipulate one to disclose that it is artificial (Art. 50(4)).\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nA deepfake is AI-generated or manipulated image, audio or video that resembles real persons, objects, places or events and would falsely appear authentic. The EU AI Act's Article 3(60) definition tracks that wording, which matters because it is the definition that triggers the labelling duty.\n\nThe harm set is broad. Impersonation fraud (voice cloning to authorise a payment), harassment and non-consensual intimate imagery, election manipulation, and the slower damage of eroded trust in authentic recordings, which is a harm to everyone who then disbelieves real evidence. NIST AI 600-1 addresses the category under its **information integrity** risk, and pairs it with content provenance as the countermeasure: tracking where content came from rather than trying to detect fakes after the fact.\n\nThe regulatory duties split by role. Deployers of systems generating synthetic content must disclose it, with a lighter duty where the content is evidently artistic, creative, satirical or fictional (Article 50(4)). AI-generated text on matters of public interest has its own disclosure rule in the same provision, which is easy to miss because it is a subparagraph rather than a separate article. The 2026 Digital Omnibus added new prohibitions covering intimate-imagery generation and child sexual abuse material, effective 2 December 2026.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Under the EU AI Act a deepfake is image, audio or video content; AI-generated text on matters of public interest has its own disclosure rule in Article 50(4). Provenance and watermarking are the preferred controls, not after-the-fact detection.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/50/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 50 (transparency and labelling)</a> · deepfake disclosure and the text subparagraph\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 600-1, Generative AI Profile</a> · information integrity risk and content provenance\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-4.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 100-4 (synthetic content)</a> · provenance, watermarking and detection methods", "kt127": "## Definition\n\nTechniques to remove or obscure personally identifying information from datasets — includes pseudonymization, aggregation, and generalization approaches.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe umbrella for removing or obscuring identifiers from data — spanning weak masking through pseudonymization toward (claimed) anonymization. The operative question is always residual re-identification risk: quasi-identifiers, linkage attacks, and model memorization decide whether deidentified data is actually out of privacy-law scope.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Deidentified ≠ anonymous — GDPR still applies to pseudonymized data; the label matters less than the re-identification risk.", "kt128": "## Definition\n\nIntegration into operational environment, user training\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe lifecycle transition from artifact to operation: releasing a validated system into production through a chosen strategy (canary, phased, shadow, blue-green), with monitoring live from minute one and rollback criteria pre-agreed. Governance-wise it's a formal gate: named approvers, documented go/no-go, and change management for the humans affected.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Deployment is a decision with an audit trail, not an event — scenarios reward staged strategies and pre-defined rollback over big-bang launches.", "kt129": "## Definition\n\nData collection/preparation, model design, training, tuning\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe ISO 22989 lifecycle stage where the system takes shape: architecture selection, data engineering, model building and training — the phase where governance requirements (oversight hooks, explainability, logging) must be built in, because retrofitting them later costs multiples.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Cheapest risk mitigation lives here — 'address it in design' beats 'monitor for it in production' when both are options.", "kt130": "## Definition\n\nA product liability claim where the product's design is inherently unsafe — AI systems with flawed architectures or inadequate safety features may face design defect claims.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe product-liability theory that a product's design itself is unreasonably dangerous — for AI: foreseeable failure modes untested, missing safeguards, or a reasonable alternative design (more testing, human checkpoints, constrained scope) that would have prevented the harm. Strict liability means no negligence proof required.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Three product-liability theories: manufacturing defect (deviation from design), design defect (the design is the problem), failure to warn — map the fact pattern.", "kt131": "## Definition\n\nSystem outputs that are entirely predictable given the same inputs, characteristic of traditional rule-based systems. Contrasts with probabilistic AI outputs.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nDeterministic output means the same input produces the same output every time, which is the property of rule-based software and the property AI abandons. Model output is probabilistic: it depends on learned weights, on sampling settings, and often on the model version, so identical inputs can yield different results and a single observed output is one draw from a distribution.\n\nThe governance consequence is methodological. With deterministic software, testing can enumerate behaviour and a defect is a deviation from specification. With a probabilistic system, you cannot enumerate behaviour, so you substitute statistical evaluation on representative data, accept a residual error rate as a design parameter, and monitor the distribution in production. That is why AI assurance leans on metrics, thresholds and drift detection rather than on pass/fail conformance testing alone.\n\nIt also changes what \"correct\" means in an incident. A model that was wrong on one case may simply be performing as measured; the governance question becomes whether the measured error rate was acceptable for this use, and whether anyone documented that judgement.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Deterministic versus probabilistic output is the first-principles reason AI cannot be governed like ordinary software. If an option assumes same-input-same-output or treats an error as a coding defect, it contradicts this characteristic.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · valid and reliable, and the Measure function's metrics\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · reliability and robustness vocabulary\n- <a href=\"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF Playbook</a> · measurement and monitoring actions", "kt132": "## Definition\n\nA mathematical framework for quantifying privacy loss when analyzing datasets — ensures individual records cannot be inferred from aggregate outputs.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe mathematically rigorous PET: calibrated noise added so any single individual's presence in a dataset changes outputs by at most a provable bound (epsilon), making membership statistically deniable. Used in census statistics and privacy-preserving ML training, it trades accuracy for quantifiable privacy guarantees.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] The differentiator is *provable, quantifiable* protection — when a scenario wants privacy guarantees stronger than masking, differential privacy is the term.", "kt133": "## Definition\n\nEU regulation of 8 July 2026 amending the AI Act, in force 27 July 2026. It moves the high-risk dates to 2 December 2027 (Annex III) and 2 August 2028 (Annex I), softens the AI literacy duty, adds two Article 5 bans from 2 December 2026, creates Article 4a on bias-detection data, widens the AI Office's competence, pushes national sandboxes to 2 August 2027 and extends SME relief to small mid-caps.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nProposed on 19 November 2025 and politically agreed on 7 May 2026, it was signed on 8 July, published on 24 July and in force from 27 July 2026. The final text sets fixed new high-risk dates rather than a standards-linked trigger. Beyond dates, it replaces Article 4 (AI literacy), adds Article 4a and two Article 5 bans, and gives the AI Office exclusive competence over AI built on a provider's own GPAI model and over AI in very large online platforms. It leaves the risk tiers, penalty ceilings and GPAI obligations untouched.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] The BOK predates it, so watch the anchor: 'as enacted' puts Annex III high-risk on 2 Aug 2026, 'as amended' on 2 Dec 2027.", "kt134": "## Definition\n\nFalse or manipulated content that is created or spread deliberately to deceive or cause harm, such as a fabricated video of a candidate released to sway an election. Intent is what separates it from misinformation; NIST AI 600-1 notes that generative AI makes it cheaper to produce at scale and to target at specific groups.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nWhat makes content disinformation is the **intent** of whoever creates or spreads it, not how false or how viral it is. Deliberate deception aimed at influencing belief or behaviour is disinformation; false content spread in honest error is misinformation; the false content itself is the shared object.\n\nGenerative AI changes the economics rather than the concept. It lowers the cost of producing convincing fake articles, images, voices and bot personas, and of targeting them at particular demographics, so a capability that once required a team now requires a prompt. NIST AI 600-1 records this under its information integrity risk, alongside deepfakes and the erosion of shared factual ground.\n\nGovernance for an organization is mostly about its own tools, since few companies run disinformation campaigns. The controls are: content provenance and watermarking on generated output, abuse monitoring on publicly available generative features, usage policies that forbid deceptive use, rate and access limits to raise the cost of automation, and an incident response path for misuse of the organization's own systems. Where the organization is a platform, the additional duties concern amplification, not just generation.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Same false content, different intent: deliberate deception is disinformation, honest error is misinformation. An organization's own controls target misuse of its generative tools and provenance of its output.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 600-1, Generative AI Profile</a> · information integrity risk and suggested actions\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/50/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 50 (transparency and labelling)</a> · disclosure duties on synthetic content\n- <a href=\"https://oecd.ai/en/incidents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OECD AI Incidents and Hazards Monitor</a> · catalogued real-world information-integrity incidents", "kt135": "## Definition\n\nUnintentional discrimination where an AI system's neutral policy disproportionately harms a protected group — still unlawful under civil rights laws even without intent.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nDiscrimination without intent: a facially neutral practice — say, a hiring model's cutoff score — that disproportionately harms a protected class and can't be justified by business necessity. It's the doctrine that catches algorithmic discrimination, since models rarely 'intend' anything but routinely encode proxies.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] No intent required — that is the point; disparate treatment (this term's sibling) is the intentional kind.", "kt136": "## Definition\n\nIntentional discrimination where an AI system explicitly treats individuals differently based on protected characteristics — prohibited under civil rights laws.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nIntentional discrimination: treating individuals differently because of a protected characteristic — in AI, explicitly using race, sex, or age as decision features, or deliberately targeting protected groups. Cleaner to litigate than disparate impact, rarer in algorithmic form, but not extinct: proxy use with knowledge can shade into it.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Treatment = intentional, impact = effect-based — the pairing is one of the exam's favorite definitional splits.", "kt137": "## Definition\n\nThe systematic recording of AI lifecycle activities, decisions, risks, and incidents to establish compliance, traceability, and audit readiness.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe evidence layer of governance: contemporaneous records of decisions, tests, approvals, incidents, and changes, plus the reporting flows that keep oversight bodies informed — internal (committees, boards) and external (regulators, auditors). Its absence converts good governance into unprovable governance.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] 'If it isn't documented, it didn't happen' — in audit and enforcement scenarios, the documented-but-imperfect program beats the perfect-but-undocumented one.", "kt138": "## Definition\n\nA Korea-based agent that a foreign AI business operator with no Korean address must appoint in writing, and report to MSIT, if it meets any one decree threshold. The thresholds are KRW 1 trillion prior-year revenue, KRW 10 billion AI-service revenue, 1 million average daily Korean users, or a prior fine for ignoring an order. The operator is liable for the agent's violations.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThis is Korea's version of the EU's authorized representative, but only size triggers it: a foreign operator with no Korean address must appoint one if it meets any one decree threshold. The representative files Art. 32 safety results, requests high-impact confirmations and supports the Art. 34 measures, including checking that records are current and accurate. Any violation by the representative is treated as the foreign operator's own.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] The thresholds are alternatives, so meeting any one is enough, and failing to appoint a representative is one of only three directly finable violations.", "kt139": "## Definition\n\nNegative consequences that ripple from an AI system's outputs to affect individuals, groups, or systems further along the value chain beyond the immediate deployment context.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nDownstream harm is harm that materialises away from the model that caused it: a biased upstream score propagating through hiring pipelines, a hallucinated fact republished at scale, a foundation-model flaw inherited by a thousand applications built on it. The harm and the control are separated, which is the structural problem.\n\nTwo governance consequences follow. First, **value-chain governance exists for this reason**: the party best placed to prevent the harm (the base-model provider) is furthest from the victim, so the frameworks substitute information transfer for proximity. EU AI Act Article 53 requires GPAI providers to document the model and supply information to downstream providers; NIST AI 600-1 names value-chain and component integration as a distinct risk; ISO/IEC 42001 places third-party controls in Annex A.\n\nSecond, **accountability does not follow the harm downstream**. An organization that deploys a third-party model still answers for the outcome, and under the EU AI Act an entity that rebrands or substantially modifies a high-risk system becomes the provider. The exam-relevant instinct: inherited risk is still your risk, and the control is diligence plus contract plus testing, not a disclaimer.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] GPAI and value-chain questions turn on this: upstream providers owe documentation and downstream information precisely so deployers can manage inherited risk. Outsourcing the model does not outsource the accountability.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/53/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 53 (GPAI provider documentation duties)</a> · the information transfer that makes downstream risk manageable\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/25/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 25 (responsibilities along the AI value chain)</a> · how obligations follow the chain\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 600-1, value-chain and component integration risk</a> · the generative-AI form of inherited risk", "kt140": "## Definition\n\nCJEU judgment of 27 February 2025 on GDPR Article 15(1)(h): 'meaningful information about the logic involved' means a concise, intelligible explanation of the procedure and principles actually applied to the person's data. Handing over a formula is not enough. Claimed trade secrets must be given to the supervisory authority or court to balance.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nA mobile operator refused a €10-a-month contract on the strength of Dun & Bradstreet Austria's credit assessment. The CJEU held that the controller must explain the procedure and principles actually applied, so that the person can see which data were used and how. Neither a complex formula nor a step-by-step technical description is enough, but showing how a change in the data would have changed the result may be. Trade secrets go to the supervisory authority or court for case-by-case balancing, and blanket national exclusions are precluded.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Explain the logic actually applied: neither 'here is our algorithm' nor 'it's a trade secret, so no' is a lawful answer.", "kt141": "## Definition\n\nA phenomenon where AI-driven content recommendation systems reinforce existing beliefs by limiting exposure to diverse perspectives, contributing to polarization.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAn echo chamber is the feedback loop where recommendation algorithms optimise for engagement by serving more of what a user already agrees with, narrowing their exposure and hardening their beliefs. No individual decision in the loop is wrong; the aggregate degrades discourse and autonomy.\n\nIt is the canonical societal-scale AI harm, and its structure explains why it is hard to govern with the usual instruments. There is no biased output to point at, no protected class to test fairness against, and no single decision that a person could contest. The harm is an emergent property of an optimisation objective applied across a population, which means the control has to act on the objective and the system design rather than on individual outputs.\n\nThat is why the governance instruments that fit are the ones aimed at system-level effects: impact assessment that considers societal harm, monitoring of aggregate outcomes (diversity of exposure, distribution of amplification) rather than only per-item accuracy, and transparency about how ranking works. It belongs in the same file as misinformation, manipulation and polarisation: harms that emerge from optimisation at scale.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] File under societal harms alongside misinformation and manipulation. The governance angle is system-level: impact assessment, aggregate monitoring and ranking transparency, not per-output correction.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 600-1, Generative AI Profile</a> · information integrity and homogenization risks\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · contextual and societal risk framing\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/50/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 50 (transparency)</a> · disclosure duties on recommender and generative systems", "kt142": "## Definition\n\nDraft EDPB guidelines, adopted 7 July 2026 for consultation until 30 October 2026, on scraping personal data to train generative AI. Consent is rarely workable; legitimate interest is the usual basis if necessity and balancing are met. Special-category data needs both an Article 6 basis and an Article 9(2) exception.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nBuilding on Opinion 28/2024, the draft applies the GDPR to scraping by private entities for generative-AI training. It stresses purpose limitation, transparency (with the disproportionate-effort route where it applies), minimisation filters, and accuracy checks such as timestamps and validation. For sensitive data it requires an Article 9(2) exception on top of an Article 6 basis, and it suggests GC and Others (C-136/17) may cover incidental collection. It remains a consultation draft until 30 October 2026.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Two locks for sensitive scraped data (Article 6 plus Article 9(2)); 'it was public' is never a blanket exemption.", "kt143": "## Definition\n\nEDPB opinion of 17 December 2024 on AI models under the GDPR. A model is anonymous only if extracting training subjects' data from it, directly or through queries, is insignificantly likely. Legitimate interest follows a three-step test (interest, necessity, balancing). Unlawful training can taint deployment unless the model was properly anonymised.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nIssued under Article 64(2) at the Irish supervisory authority's request, it answers four questions on AI models. On anonymity, the test is two-sided: extraction from the model and personal data surfacing in answers to queries must both be insignificantly likely. On legitimate interest, it applies the classic three steps, stressing reasonable expectations and mitigations beyond legal minimums. On unlawful training, it sets out three scenarios; a later deployer must check the model's lawful origin unless it was genuinely anonymised.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] No hierarchy of legal bases and no automatic anonymity: 'model weights aren't personal data' is the trap answer.", "kt144": "## Definition\n\nAugust 1, 2024 — the AI Act formally entered into force (published in the Official Journal July 12, 2024). Entry into force started the clock but imposed no immediate obligations.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAugust 1, 2024 — twenty days after Official Journal publication (July 12, 2024): the Act became law and its transition clocks started, though no substantive obligations applied yet. 'In force' and 'applicable' are different legal states, and the Act's staircase of applicability dates is measured from this anchor.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] In force ≠ applicable — obligations arrive Feb 2025 (prohibitions), Aug 2025 (GPAI), Aug 2026 (high-risk).", "kt145": "## Definition\n\nMoral principles and values that should guide AI development and deployment — includes fairness, dignity, autonomy, transparency, and prevention of harm.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nEthical considerations are the value questions that remain after legal compliance: should we build it at all, who bears the residual risk, is the benefit distribution just, does the system respect dignity and autonomy. Law sets a floor; ethics asks what the floor should be and whether the floor is high enough for this use.\n\nThe governance move is to make ethics operational rather than aspirational. That means principles written as testable requirements, an ethics review with a defined trigger and a real veto or escalation path, impact assessments that ask who could be harmed and how, and named owners for the residual risks that survive mitigation. Ethics that exists only as a values statement has no effect on any decision.\n\nThe BOK places ethics in three separate places, which is a signal of its weight: as a design consideration in I.C (ethics by design), as an input to the design and build process in III.A, and as an input to deployment in IV.C. In scenario items the practical tell is that **lawful does not mean acceptable**. When two options are both legally defensible, the one that addresses the ethical dimension as well is usually the intended answer.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Legal is not ethical: scenarios include lawful-but-harmful options, and governance answers weigh both. Ethics review needs a trigger, a decision right and documented outcomes, or it is decoration.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://www.unesco.org/en/artificial-intelligence/recommendation-ethics\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">UNESCO Recommendation on the Ethics of AI</a> · the 193-state ethical framework\n- <a href=\"https://oecd.ai/en/ai-principles\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OECD AI Principles</a> · human-centred values and fairness\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · trustworthiness characteristics as the operational form of ethics", "kt146": "## Definition\n\nIntegrating ethical considerations at every stage of AI system development, from problem definition through deployment and monitoring.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nEthics by design builds ethical requirements into a system from conception: fairness constraints in the objective and the data, oversight hooks in the interface, transparency affordances in the output, and escalation paths in the workflow. It follows the pattern of privacy by design and shifts ethics from a review-stage veto to a design-stage requirement.\n\nThe argument for doing it early is economic as much as moral. Changing a model's training data, objective or architecture before it is built costs almost nothing; remediating a deployed system means retraining, revalidating, re-documenting and possibly recalling it. The BOK makes this explicit in III.A: design-phase risk work is the cheapest risk work there is.\n\nIt also needs teeth to be more than a slogan. In practice that means ethical requirements written as testable criteria, a review that happens before build rather than after, an assessment that can change the design (not just note concerns), and records showing what was decided and why. The exam-relevant instinct: answers that embed ethics at design consistently beat answers that audit for ethics afterward, which is the same logic as privacy by design generalised to the wider principle set.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Design-stage answers beat post-hoc review answers, and the justification is cost plus effectiveness: it is cheaper and more effective to change an objective before training than to remediate a deployed system.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF Playbook, Govern function</a> · embedding values and policies before build\n- <a href=\"https://oecd.ai/en/ai-principles\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OECD AI Principles</a> · human-centred values as a design commitment\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/5/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 9 (risk management system)</a> · the statutory version: risk management across the lifecycle", "kt147": "## Definition\n\nRegulation (EU) 2024/1689 — the world's first comprehensive AI law establishing a risk-based regulatory framework. Effective August 2024, general application August 2026.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nRegulation (EU) 2024/1689, the world's first comprehensive horizontal AI law: risk-tiered obligations (prohibited, high, limited, minimal), a GPAI regime, conformity infrastructure (assessments, CE marking, notified bodies), governance bodies (AI Office, Board, national authorities), GDPR-scale penalties and extraterritorial reach. It entered into force on 1 August 2024. The Digital Omnibus on AI (Regulation (EU) 2026/1744) amended it from 27 July 2026, moving high-risk rules to 2 December 2027 (Annex III) and 2 August 2028 (Annex I).\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Still the most-tested instrument: know the tiers, roles and penalties cold, and learn the timeline in both its enacted and amended versions.", "kt148": "## Definition\n\nProhibited AI practices: up to €35M or 7% of global annual turnover (whichever is higher). Non-compliance with high-risk obligations: up to €15M or 3%. Supplying incorrect or misleading information to authorities: up to €7.5M or 1%.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThree fine ceilings, each 'whichever is higher': €35M or 7% of global annual turnover for prohibited-practice violations; €15M or 3% for most other obligations (including high-risk requirements); €7.5M or 1% for supplying incorrect or misleading information to authorities — with SME-proportionate caps.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] 35/7 → prohibitions, 15/3 → obligations, 7.5/1 → misleading info — memorize as pairs; compare with GDPR's 20/4.", "kt149": "## Definition\n\nEU body housed within the European Commission that coordinates supervision of GPAI models with systemic risk, maintains the public database of high-risk AI systems, and issues guidance and best practices.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe Commission-housed enforcement hub for GPAI: supervises general-purpose model providers, coordinates the GPAI Code of Practice, issues guidance, and anchors Union-level consistency while national market-surveillance authorities police everything else. Created by the AI Act, operational since 2024.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Jurisdiction split: AI Office → GPAI models; national authorities → AI systems generally — assigning the wrong enforcer is the distractor pattern.", "kt150": "## Definition\n\nUS Executive Order on Safe, Secure, and Trustworthy AI (October 2023) that directed NIST to develop the Generative AI Profile (NIST AI 600-1) as a companion to the AI RMF. Revoked on 20 January 2025 by EO 14148; the NIST profile it produced remains published.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe October 2023 US Executive Order on Safe, Secure, and Trustworthy AI — the Biden administration's whole-of-government AI directive: safety test reporting for powerful dual-use models (via Defense Production Act authority), NIST guidance mandates (yielding AI 600-1), agency AI governance requirements, and workstreams on privacy, equity, and workers. Revoked on 20 January 2025 by EO 14148; later US federal policy (the 2025 AI Action Plan, EO 14365 on state AI laws) points the other way.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Know it as the origin of NIST's Generative AI Profile — and that it was revoked in January 2025, while the profile itself stays in use.", "kt151": "## Definition\n\nUS executive order of 23 Apr 2025, 'Restoring Equality of Opportunity and Meritocracy', declaring a policy to eliminate disparate-impact liability to the maximum degree possible and directing federal agencies to deprioritise its enforcement. It cannot amend statutes: Title VII's §703(k) disparate-impact standard, the Fair Housing Act theory recognised in Inclusive Communities, and private lawsuits remain.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nSection 4 tells agencies to deprioritise enforcement of statutes and regulations to the extent they include disparate-impact liability, naming Title VII's 42 U.S.C. 2000e-2; section 6 orders a review of pending disparate-impact matters; section 5 starts repeal of disparate-impact provisions in Title VI regulations. Agencies followed with concrete steps: the CFPB removed the effects test from Regulation B (effective 21 July 2026) and HUD proposed deleting its discriminatory-effects rule (January 2026). Testing AI for disparate impact remains prudent because courts, states and private plaintiffs are not bound by the order.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] An executive order changes enforcement priorities, not statutes: Title VII disparate impact still binds employers using AI tools.", "kt152": "## Definition\n\nAn AI system that accumulates, combines, and integrates knowledge provided by one or more human experts in a specific domain to derive solutions to problems.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAn expert system is classic symbolic AI in production form: a **knowledge base** of rules hand-encoded from human experts, plus an **inference engine** that applies them to facts. In narrow, well-bounded domains (medical diagnosis aids, equipment configuration, tax rules) they performed well, and they were transparent by construction, because the rules could be read and argued with.\n\nTheir limitation is brittleness at the edge of coded knowledge. Anything the experts did not encode, the system cannot handle, and rules that interact in unforeseen ways produce surprising conclusions. Machine learning displaced them because learning patterns from data generalises better than enumerating rules by hand.\n\nThey remain the standard contrast case in governance discussions, and a useful one. An expert system is explainable and auditable by inspection, which is why the design guidance in this space still says that for eligibility decisions a rule-based system may be more appropriate than a black-box model. The trade is capability against explainability, and the governance professional is the person who has to make that trade explicit rather than defaulting to the more capable option.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Rule-based, explainable, brittle: expert systems are the contrast case to learned, opaque, generalising ML. Expect them as an answer option when a scenario needs an inherently interpretable decision method.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · symbolic and knowledge-based system vocabulary\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · explainable and interpretable as a trustworthiness characteristic", "kt153": "## Definition\n\nA property of an AI system that allows important factors influencing the results of the AI system to be expressed in a way that is understandable to humans. Intended to answer \"Why?\" without claiming optimality.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nExplainability is the capacity to render a system's outputs understandable to humans, at two levels: **globally** (how the model behaves in general) and **locally** (why this particular decision came out this way). Techniques span inherently interpretable models at one end and post-hoc methods at the other, including feature attribution (SHAP, LIME) and counterfactual explanations of the \"had this been different, the outcome would have changed\" form.\n\nIt underwrites several other obligations rather than standing alone. Oversight is impossible without it, because an overseer who cannot understand the basis of an output cannot add judgement. Contestability depends on it, because a person cannot challenge what they cannot comprehend. Debugging and incident investigation depend on it. And it is legally anchored: GDPR Article 13 to 15 give a right to meaningful information about automated decision-making logic, and the EU AI Act's transparency and instructions-for-use duties assume deployers can explain system behaviour to the people affected.\n\nThe precision distinction to hold: **transparency** is about the organization (that AI is in use, what it does, its limits), while **explainability** is about a specific output. **Interpretability** is the underlying model property that makes explanation possible. Exam items offer all three as interchangeable answers and only one fits the stem.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Transparency equals information available; explainability equals outputs understandable; interpretability equals the model property underneath. Local explanation is about one decision, global explanation is about the model's overall behaviour.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/artificial-intelligence/explaining-decisions-made-with-artificial-intelligence/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ICO: explaining decisions made with AI</a> · what a meaningful explanation must contain\n- <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.07874\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SHAP: a unified approach to interpreting model predictions</a> · feature-attribution explanations\n- <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.04938\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LIME: explaining the predictions of any classifier</a> · local surrogate explanations\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · explainable and interpretable", "kt154": "## Definition\n\nNIST trustworthiness characteristic: AI system's mechanisms and outputs can be understood by humans — explainability (can justify) + interpretability (can trace reasoning).\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe NIST AI RMF trustworthiness characteristic bundling the two comprehension properties: explainable (mechanisms and outputs can be described) and interpretable (outputs are meaningful in context to users). NIST treats them as distinct but paired — a system can generate explanations nobody can actually interpret.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] One of the seven NIST characteristics — and NIST's subtle split (explainable = how, interpretable = meaningful why) appears in careful questions.", "kt155": "## Definition\n\nA phenomenon in backpropagation training in a neural network in which large error gradients accumulate and lead to very large updates of the weights, causing the model to become unstable.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAn exploding gradient is a training pathology in neural networks: during backpropagation the gradients grow uncontrollably as they pass through layers, so parameter updates become enormous and training diverges instead of converging. It is the mirror image of the vanishing-gradient problem, where gradients shrink toward zero and the earliest layers stop learning at all.\n\nThe standard remedies are gradient clipping (capping the update magnitude), careful initialisation, normalization layers, and architectural choices such as gated recurrent units, which were partly a response to gradient instability in sequence models.\n\nFor governance purposes this is vocabulary rather than engineering. It matters because it is an example of a failure mode that is invisible in the model's outputs until it is severe, and because it explains why training runs need monitoring and why reproducibility depends on recording hyperparameters and random seeds. A training run that diverged and was restarted is a documentation event: the version that ships is not the version that was first attempted.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Training-stability vocabulary: recognize the term and its vanishing-gradient sibling. The exam tests recognition, not calculus, so the useful pair to hold is 'exploding = updates blow up, vanishing = early layers stop learning'.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · training and neural network terminology\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · documentation and reproducibility expectations", "kt156": "## Definition\n\nInitial examination of the data to identify its salient features and assess its quality.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe look-at-your-data phase before modeling: distributions, outliers, missingness, correlations, and subgroup composition — where representation gaps and quality problems surface early enough to fix cheaply. Skipping EDA is how datasets' pathologies become models' pathologies.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] EDA belongs early in the data phase — it's where the analyst first *sees* bias and quality risks the governance program must catch.", "kt157": "## Definition\n\nA documented strategy for communicating about AI system incidents, capabilities, limitations, and changes to stakeholders including customers, regulators, and the public.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe prepared playbook for speaking about AI beyond the organization: user transparency notices, regulator communications, incident disclosures, and press response — with speakers, approval chains, and pre-cleared facts defined before a crisis compresses timelines. Improvised AI-incident messaging reliably compounds the incident.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Incident scenarios reward pre-planned communications with designated owners — 'draft a statement after containment' is table stakes, having the plan already is the answer.", "kt158": "## Definition\n\nThe harmonic mean of precision and recall — a balanced metric combining both false positives and false negatives, preferred when class distributions are uneven.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe F1 score is the harmonic mean of precision and recall: a single number balancing false positives against false negatives. Precision answers \"of the items we flagged, how many were right?\" Recall answers \"of the items that were actually positive, how many did we catch?\" F1 is favoured when classes are imbalanced, because plain accuracy flatters a model that predicts the majority class and ignores everything else.\n\nIts governance relevance is that **metric choice encodes a value judgement about which errors matter**, and that judgement belongs to an accountable human rather than to a default. In fraud detection a false positive means a declined transaction and an annoyed customer; in cancer screening it means an unnecessary biopsy. In content moderation a false positive silences legitimate speech and a false negative leaves harm visible. Optimising F1 assumes the two error types cost the same, which is rarely true in consequential settings.\n\nThat is why evaluation should report precision, recall and the threshold-dependent trade-off curve alongside any summary metric, and why subgroup breakdowns matter: a model with a healthy overall F1 can still fail badly for one group.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Precision is of those flagged how many were right; recall is of actual positives how many were caught; F1 balances the two. A single summary metric hides subgroup failure, so expect the correct answer to ask for per-group results.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · measurement and the choice of metrics as a governance decision\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/SpecialPublications/NIST.SP.1270.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST SP 1270, bias in AI</a> · subgroup performance as the bias-detection surface", "kt159": "## Definition\n\nAutomatic pattern recognition that compares stored images of human faces with an actual face image and displays any match with identifying data.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nBiometric identification from facial features — the governance lightning rod: GDPR special-category processing when identifying, EU AI Act prohibitions (untargeted scraping, real-time remote public identification with narrow carve-outs), Annex III high-risk classification otherwise, and demonstrated accuracy disparities across demographics.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Scenario reflex: face recognition → biometric special-category analysis + AI Act tier check (prohibited? high-risk?) + bias evidence.", "kt160": "## Definition\n\nA fallback decided in advance that kicks in if an AI system starts behaving anomalously or unsafely, or operates outside its set limits: for example halting safely, handing over to a backup system, or reverting to a manual process. The EU AI Act lists backup or fail-safe plans among the technical redundancy solutions that support the robustness of high-risk systems (Art. 15(4); Recital 75).\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nA fail-safe plan answers the question of what happens when the system goes wrong before it is released: it defines the triggers (anomalous outputs, out-of-bounds inputs, lost sensor data), the safe state (halt, fall back to rules, hand over to a person or a backup system) and who may invoke it. The EU AI Act treats backup and fail-safe plans as technical redundancy that supports the robustness of high-risk systems (Art. 15(4)), and Recital 75 describes them as mechanisms that let a system safely interrupt its operation. It complements, rather than replaces, human oversight (Art. 14) and a deactivation policy.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Fail-safe plans and technical redundancy are tied to robustness in Article 15(4), not to accuracy, cybersecurity or human oversight.", "kt161": "## Definition\n\nA legal doctrine permitting limited use of copyrighted material without permission for purposes such as criticism, comment, news reporting, teaching, scholarship, or research.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe US copyright doctrine excusing certain unauthorized uses, weighed on four factors: purpose and character (transformativeness), nature of the work, amount taken, and market effect. Whether training generative models on protected works is fair use is the defining open question of AI copyright litigation.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Fair use is a US doctrine and it is *unsettled* for AI training — options claiming it's clearly resolved either way are wrong.", "kt162": "## Definition\n\nHow US courts apply the four fair use factors of 17 U.S.C. §107 to training AI on copyrighted works. Training was held fair use on the records in Bartz v. Anthropic and Kadrey v. Meta (June 2025) but not in Thomson Reuters v. Ross (Feb 2025, a competing non-generative tool). How the data was acquired and market harm, including market dilution, are decisive; no appellate ruling as of Sep 2026.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nFactor one (purpose, including transformativeness) and factor four (market effect) dominate the AI cases. Bartz called training exceedingly transformative but refused to excuse a central library built from pirated books; Kadrey ruled for Meta only because the plaintiffs offered no evidence of market dilution; Ross rejected fair use where the copying served a direct competitor. The Copyright Office's Part 3 report (May 2025) adds that commercial use of vast troves of works to produce competing content, especially through illegal access, goes beyond established fair use.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Fair use is a US defence decided case by case; the EU counterpart is the Article 4 TDM exception with opt-outs, not fair use.", "kt163": "## Definition\n\nA core principle of responsible AI: AI systems must not discriminate against individuals or groups based on race, gender, religion, ethnicity, disability, or socioeconomic status.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe principle that AI treats individuals and groups equitably — operationalized through competing mathematical definitions (parity, equalized odds, calibration) that cannot all hold simultaneously, forcing documented context-specific choices. It spans process (how decisions are made) and outcome (who gets what).\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] The mature answer acknowledges metric trade-offs and picks per context with documentation — 'optimize all fairness metrics' is mathematically impossible.", "kt164": "## Definition\n\nA training approach where models are trained across decentralized devices using local data, without transferring raw data to a central server — enhances privacy.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nTraining without centralizing data: the model travels to the data (devices, hospitals), local updates are computed, and only parameter updates return for aggregation. It's a PET for the collection problem — raw data never leaves — though updates themselves can leak and typically get differential-privacy or secure-aggregation reinforcement.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] When a scenario needs multi-party training without sharing raw data, federated learning is the named technique.", "kt165": "## Definition\n\nA cycle where AI system outputs influence future inputs, potentially amplifying biases or errors over time — requires monitoring and circuit-breaking mechanisms.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nModel outputs shaping the future inputs the model learns from: predictive policing directing patrols that generate arrest data justifying more patrols; recommenders training users whose clicks retrain recommenders. Loops entrench and amplify initial bias, and they hide from static testing — only longitudinal monitoring sees them.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Feedback loops explain how a small initial bias becomes structural — cite them in scenarios where deployment data feeds retraining.", "kt166": "## Definition\n\nNeural network in which information is directed from the input layer to the output layer in only one direction.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe feedforward network is the baseline neural architecture: information flows in one direction, from input through hidden layers to output, with no cycles and no memory of previous inputs. Each layer applies weights and an activation function to the previous layer's output, and training adjusts those weights by backpropagation.\n\nIt is the building block the specialised architectures elaborate on. Add recurrence and you get memory across time steps (RNN, LSTM); add convolution and weight sharing over a grid and you get spatial pattern learning (CNN); add attention and you get the transformer that underpins modern language models. Knowing the baseline is what makes the specialisations legible.\n\nGovernance relevance is indirect but real: an FFNN is a learned, opaque function approximator, so it carries the same opacity problem as any deep model, and its capacity is set by architecture choices (depth, width) that a governance professional should see documented alongside the training data and evaluation results.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Architecture taxonomy anchor: feedforward means no memory, recurrent means sequence memory, convolutional means spatial patterns, transformer means attention. Four one-line pairings cover most architecture-recognition items.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · neural network architecture vocabulary\n- <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.03993\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Model Cards for Model Reporting</a> · what to document about a model's architecture and limits", "kt167": "## Definition\n\nThe process of adapting a pre-trained AI model to a specific task or domain by additional training on targeted data — balances customization with resource efficiency.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAdapting a pretrained model with additional domain-specific training — the economic default for LLM deployment. Governance inheritance is the catch: the fine-tuned model carries the base model's biases, memorized data, and licensing constraints, plus whatever the new data adds — and substantial modification can shift regulatory roles from deployer toward provider.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Fine-tuning inherits base-model risk and can transfer provider obligations — two consequences in one technique.", "kt168": "## Definition\n\nAssessment that data is appropriate and adequate for the specific AI use case — considering quality, quantity, representativeness, and relevance to the intended task.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe proportionality test for data and systems: quality is not absolute but relative to the intended use — clinical decision support demands a different evidentiary bar than playlist ranking. Fit-for-purpose evaluation asks whether this data, this model, these controls suffice for this use and its stakes.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Anti-perfectionism principle with teeth: the question is never 'is the data perfect' but 'is it adequate for this purpose' — proportionality is the tested logic.", "kt169": "## Definition\n\nA large model pretrained on very large, diverse data, usually by self-supervision, that can be adapted through prompting, fine-tuning or RAG to many downstream tasks; LLMs and large image generators are examples. The EU AI Act's legal term for the concept is general-purpose AI model (Art. 3(63)), and IAPP also lists frontier AI as an alternative name.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nA foundation model is pretrained once on broad, varied data and then adapted to many downstream uses by fine-tuning, prompting or retrieval. The term was popularised by Stanford researchers (Bommasani et al., 2021) to capture the shift from task-specific models to a general base that many applications sit on.\n\nIts breadth is the governance problem. Defects and biases in the base model are inherited by every application built on it, and the organizations adapting it typically have little or no visibility into its training data, its data rights, or how it was evaluated. That inheritance is why NIST AI 600-1 names **value-chain and component integration** as a generative-AI risk, and why the controls that matter are the ones that travel with the model: model cards, documentation of training data, stated limitations, and downstream information.\n\nThe EU AI Act regulates the same idea under a different label, **general-purpose AI model** (Article 3(63)). Worth knowing precisely: the final Act text does not use the phrase foundation model at all, so an exam stem saying 'foundation model' is describing the EU's GPAI concept.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] When a stem says foundation model, think of the EU's general-purpose AI model: one broadly capable base model adapted downstream into many applications, with the provider owing documentation, downstream information, a copyright policy and a training-content summary.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/53/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 53 (obligations for providers of general-purpose AI models)</a> · the statutory duties on the provider of a base model\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 600-1, Generative AI Profile</a> · value-chain and component integration risk\n- <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.03993\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Model Cards for Model Reporting</a> · the documentation artifact that travels with a model", "kt170": "## Definition\n\nA type of AI system that performs a broad spectrum of tasks at a satisfactory level of performance. Note: AGI is often used in a broader sense — systems that can perform all tasks a human is capable of.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAmbiguity alert, and a real one on the exam. \"GAI\" is used two ways in the literature. In older capability taxonomy it means **artificial general intelligence**: human-level competence across all domains, theoretical. In NIST's AI 600-1 the acronym means **generative AI**, and the document uses it that way throughout.\n\nSo the expansion is a context question. Capability-ladder discussions (narrow, general, superintelligence) mean the first. NIST risk documents, and most current practitioner writing, mean the second, because generative AI is the thing that actually needs governing today.\n\nThere is a third, weaker usage worth recognising: \"general\" as a marketing adjective for a model that handles many task types. That is breadth, not generality, and it does not graduate a system to AGI. When a scenario uses \"general AI,\" check whether the stem is discussing capability tiers or content generation before you answer.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Expand the acronym from context. In NIST AI 600-1, GAI means generative AI. In a capability-ladder question, general AI means AGI and is theoretical. Answering the wrong expansion produces the wrong answer even when the reasoning is sound.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 600-1, Generative AI Profile</a> · uses GAI for generative AI throughout\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · the formal capability vocabulary", "kt171": "## Definition\n\nEU Regulation 2016/679 — comprehensive data protection framework governing processing of personal data, with significant implications for AI systems processing EU residents' data.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe EU's 2016/679 data protection regime and the gravitational center of global privacy law: principles (Art 5), lawful bases (Art 6), special categories (Art 9), data subject rights (Arts 12-22), accountability infrastructure (DPIAs, DPOs, processor contracts), breach notification, transfers, and 4%-of-turnover fines — all fully applicable to AI built on personal data.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] GDPR and the AI Act run in parallel — one system can owe both; neither displaces the other.", "kt172": "## Definition\n\nEU AI Act category: AI models with significant generality, capable of performing a wide range of tasks. Those posing systemic risk face additional obligations.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe AI Act's term for foundation models: trained on broad data at scale, displaying significant generality, competent across many distinct tasks, and integrable into countless downstream systems. Generality is precisely why they get their own regime — one model's flaw becomes every downstream application's flaw.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] GPAI is defined by generality and downstream integrability — and carries its own obligations tier separate from the system-level risk ladder.", "kt173": "## Definition\n\nAI systems that create new content — text, images, code, audio, video — based on patterns learned from training data. A subset of AI exemplified by LLMs and diffusion models.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nGenerative AI creates new content (text, images, audio, video, code) by learning the distribution of its training data and sampling from it. Large language models and diffusion models are the exemplars. The mechanism is what produces the risk profile: because output is generated rather than retrieved, plausibility and truth are separate properties.\n\nNIST's Generative AI Profile names twelve risks unique to or amplified by GAI. The ones that recur on the exam: **confabulation** (confidently stated false content), **harmful bias and homogenization**, **information integrity** (deepfakes, disinformation, eroded trust in authentic media), **data privacy** (training-data leakage and memorization), **information security**, **intellectual property**, and **value-chain integration** risk. Alongside them sit four cross-cutting considerations: governance, content provenance, pre-deployment testing, and incident disclosure.\n\nGenerative is not the same as general. Generative describes what the system produces; general describes the breadth of its intelligence. Confusing the two is the most common misreading in this area.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Generative describes output (content), general describes capability (AGI). Expect the risk bundle to be tested: confabulation, memorization leakage, synthetic media, IP exposure, and the injection surface.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 600-1, Generative AI Profile</a> · the twelve GAI risks and four cross-cutting considerations\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/50/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 50 (transparency obligations)</a> · labelling AI-generated content and disclosing AI interaction\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-4.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 100-4 (synthetic content)</a> · provenance, watermarking and synthetic-content detection", "kt174": "## Definition\n\nThe Art. 31(2)-(3) duties to indicate that generative AI outputs are AI-generated, and to flag realistic synthetic audio, images or video clearly. The label may be human-recognizable or machine-readable; a machine-readable mark needs at least one text or voice notice as well. Artistic works may use a less intrusive notice.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nArt. 31(2) requires operators to indicate that generative AI outputs are AI-generated. The decree accepts methods humans can recognize or machine-readable ones such as watermarks, but a machine-readable mark must come with at least one text or voice notice. Art. 31(3) adds a stricter, clearly recognizable notice for realistic synthetic sound, images and video, with a less intrusive method allowed for artistic or creative works. The duties can be waived where AI use is obvious or the AI is used only internally.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] A labeling breach isn't fined directly (MSIT investigates and orders a fix, and only an ignored order draws a fine), unlike the separate advance-notice duty in Art. 31(1), which is fined outright.", "kt175": "## Definition\n\nCross-sectoral NIST AI RMF profile for generative AI (July 2024), developed under Executive Order 14110. Defines 12 GAI-specific risks and suggests actions organized by the Govern, Map, Measure, Manage functions.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe AI RMF's generative-AI overlay, mandated by EO 14110 and published July 2024: twelve GAI-specific risks (confabulation, information integrity, harmful bias and homogenization, CBRN capability, value-chain integration…) mapped to suggested actions across Govern/Map/Measure/Manage. A 'profile' means the RMF tailored to a technology — this is the flagship example.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Profile = RMF applied to a context; AI 600-1 = the GAI application; its risk list supplies the exam's generative-AI vocabulary.", "kt176": "## Definition\n\nNIST AI RMF function: establishing organizational culture of risk management — policies, accountability structures, workforce diversity, and third-party oversight.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe AI RMF's cross-cutting foundation: cultivate a risk-management culture — policies, accountable roles, workforce competence, incident processes, third-party oversight — that the other three functions (Map, Measure, Manage) operate inside. NIST draws it as surrounding the rest: governance isn't a phase, it's the medium.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Govern is cross-cutting and continuous, not step one of a sequence — the RMF's most-tested structural fact.", "kt177": "## Definition\n\nGPAI models placed on the market before August 2, 2025 have until August 2, 2027 to reach full compliance with the EU AI Act's GPAI obligations.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nArticle 111(3): GPAI models placed on the market before 2 August 2025 have until 2 August 2027 to comply, while models placed after that date must comply at once. The Commission's enforcement powers, including Article 101 fines, began only on 2 August 2026, so newer models had a year of AI Office collaboration before fines were possible. The Commission's guidelines say legacy providers need not retrain or unlearn models where that is impossible or disproportionate, if they disclose and justify it. The 2026 Digital Omnibus changed none of these dates.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Three GPAI dates, all unchanged by the Omnibus: obligations Aug 2025, fines Aug 2026, legacy models Aug 2027.", "kt178": "## Definition\n\nValue of the target variable for a specific element of the labeled input data.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe reference answers a supervised model learns from and is scored against — labels, verified outcomes, gold standards. Its quality caps everything: label errors become learned errors, and labeler bias becomes model bias, which is why annotation guidelines, inter-annotator agreement, and label audits are governance controls rather than technical trivia.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Trace bias to labels, not just features — 'the historical decisions used as ground truth were themselves biased' is a recurring scenario key.", "kt179": "## Definition\n\nA phenomenon where an AI model generates plausible-sounding but factually incorrect or fabricated information — a significant risk in LLM deployment.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nHallucination is the colloquial term for generative models producing fluent, confident, false content: fabricated citations, invented facts, plausible nonsense. NIST formalises it as **confabulation** in AI 600-1, where it appears first among the twelve generative-AI risks.\n\nIt is structural rather than incidental. A language model optimises for plausible next tokens, not for truth, so there is no mechanism inside the model that guarantees factual grounding. Confidence in the output's tone carries no information about its accuracy, which is what makes hallucination dangerous in professional settings: a fabricated case citation or drug interaction reads exactly like a correct one.\n\nThe governance answers are a set of layered controls rather than a fix. Ground the model in retrieved, citable sources (retrieval-augmented generation) so claims can be checked. Require verification workflows for consequential uses, with a human accountable for the output before it acts on anyone. Train users against over-trust, because calibration is the human half of the control. Communicate capability honestly, so downstream users know the failure mode exists. And measure it: hallucination rate on a representative task set is a monitored metric, not an assumption.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Interchangeable with confabulation, and its existence is why LLM output in consequential workflows needs verification. Any option asserting a well-tested model cannot be confidently wrong is wrong by construction.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 600-1, Generative AI Profile</a> · confabulation as the first of the twelve GAI risks\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/50/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 50 (transparency)</a> · disclosure duties on generated content\n- <a href=\"https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/artificial-intelligence/guidance-on-ai-and-data-protection/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ICO guidance on AI and data protection</a> · accuracy expectations for AI-generated output about people", "kt180": "## Definition\n\nA characteristic of a system that functions under the constraint of external intervention, measures, or supervision.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe opposite of autonomy: being governed by external rules or forces rather than self-direction. In AI ethics it names the risk to *people* — decisions about individuals increasingly made by systems they neither chose nor can contest, eroding self-determination one automated decision at a time.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Autonomy (self-rule) vs. heteronomy (other-rule) — the philosophical pair behind human-centricity and contestability requirements.", "kt181": "## Definition\n\nUnder Korea's AI Basic Act (Art. 2(4)), an AI system that could significantly affect or endanger life, physical safety or fundamental rights and is used in a listed domain. The domains are energy, drinking water, health care, medical devices, nuclear, biometric analysis for policing, hiring or lending-type judgments, transport, public-sector decisions and K-12 student assessment; the decree can add more.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThis is Korea's counterpart to the EU's high-risk category, but it is defined in the statute rather than an annex. A system qualifies only if it sits in a domain listed in Art. 2(4) and could significantly affect life, safety or fundamental rights. Operators must check status in advance and may ask MSIT to confirm, with an answer due within 30 days (extendable once). High-impact status triggers advance notice, the six Art. 34 measures and a best-efforts impact assessment, but no conformity assessment or registration.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Being in a listed domain isn't enough on its own, and student assessment counts only from early childhood through secondary school.", "kt182": "## Definition\n\nCommentators' label for AI systems subject to Korea's Art. 32 safety duties: trained with at least 10^26 FLOPs, built on state-of-the-art technology, and posing wide-ranging, significant risks. Their operators must manage lifecycle risks, monitor and respond to safety incidents, and report the results to MSIT.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThis is not a statutory label but the common name for systems caught by Art. 32. They are systems trained with at least 10^26 FLOPs that also use state-of-the-art technology and pose wide-ranging, significant risks. Their operators must identify, assess and mitigate risks across the lifecycle, run a system to monitor and respond to safety incidents, and submit the results to MSIT. Korea has no separate GPAI tier with documentation or copyright duties.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Korea's bar, 10^26 FLOPs plus two qualitative tests, is higher than the EU's 10^25 FLOP presumption of systemic risk.", "kt183": "## Definition\n\nVoluntary reporting framework launched by the OECD in February 2025, through which organizations report in a common format on how they align with the G7 Hiroshima Process International Code of Conduct for Organisations Developing Advanced AI Systems. Reports are published on OECD.AI; the first round had 25, and version 2.0 widens participation to organizations deploying and providing advanced AI.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe G7's Hiroshima Process produced an International Code of Conduct for Organisations Developing Advanced AI Systems (Japan's 2023 presidency). Since February 2025 the OECD has hosted a voluntary, common-format reporting framework so organizations can show how they align with it. The first round produced 25 reports, and version 2.0 opens participation to organizations that deploy and provide advanced AI.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] The G7 wrote the Code; the OECD runs the reporting; it is voluntary transparency, not certification or legal compliance.", "kt184": "## Definition\n\nA predefined point in an agent's workflow where a human must approve before it proceeds, typically for high-stakes actions, irreversible ones (payments, permanent deletions, outgoing messages), atypical behaviour, or user-set thresholds. Best enforced at system level and audited for rubber-stamping.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nIMDA lists four triggers: high-stakes actions, irreversible actions (deleting data, sending communications, making payments), outlier behaviour, and user-defined limits. Approval requests should be short and state the risk, and the checkpoint should be enforced by the system rather than a prompt. Because approvers drift into rubber-stamping, IMDA recommends auditing override rates and review times.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Irreversibility is the classic trigger: payments, deletions and outgoing messages need a human before the action, not after.", "kt185": "## Definition\n\nThe US rule that copyright protects only works authored in the first instance by a human being. Upheld in Thaler v. Perlmutter (D.C. Cir., 18 Mar 2025; cert. denied 2 Mar 2026). AI-assisted works remain protectable to the extent of the human's expressive contribution, selection or arrangement; prompts alone are not enough (Copyright Office Part 2, Jan 2025).\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe D.C. Circuit read the Copyright Act of 1976 as requiring a human author and did not need to decide whether the Constitution also requires one. It stressed that the rule does not bar protection for work made with the assistance of AI; it only denies authorship to the machine itself. The Copyright Office's Part 2 report draws the same line: human-authored material perceptible in an output, or creative arrangement or modification of outputs, can be protected, while expressive elements determined by the machine cannot.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] No human author means no copyright; a human who selects, arranges or edits AI output owns only that human contribution.", "kt186": "## Definition\n\nA safeguard where a human reviews, overrides, or validates AI decisions in high-stakes scenarios, maintaining accountability in consequential decision-making.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nHuman-in-the-loop is the tightest oversight configuration: a person reviews and approves each consequential decision before it takes effect. It is appropriate for high-stakes, lower-volume decisions where the cost of a wrong decision is high and the review load is small enough to permit real attention: loan denials, medical triage, benefits determinations, content takedowns with legal effect, custody assessments.\n\nIts trade-off is throughput. Per-decision review does not scale, which is precisely why it is reserved for the cases where scaling the error would be unacceptable. It also has a legal function: GDPR Article 22's protection against solely automated decisions is discharged in part by genuine human involvement, so the design of the review is what keeps a process outside the prohibition rather than inside it.\n\nThe exam-relevant nuance is that the loop has to be **real**. A reviewer who sees only a recommendation and clicks approve, who lacks the information or the authority to disagree, or who has seconds per case, is not in the loop; they are providing legal cover for a decision the machine already made. That is automation bias with a witness, and it satisfies no obligation.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] In-the-loop means approving each decision; on-the-loop means supervising with intervention power. Match the level to risk and volume in scenarios, and remember that per-decision review needs information, authority and time to count.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://gdpr-info.eu/art-22-gdpr/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GDPR Article 22 (human intervention safeguard)</a> · why genuine involvement matters legally\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/14/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 14 (human oversight)</a> · oversight design for high-risk systems\n- <a href=\"https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/artificial-intelligence/explaining-decisions-made-with-artificial-intelligence/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ICO: explaining decisions made with AI</a> · what meaningful human involvement requires", "kt187": "## Definition\n\nEU AI Act requirement that high-risk AI systems be designed to allow natural persons to oversee their operation, intervene when necessary, and override automated decisions.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nHuman oversight is the umbrella requirement that humans meaningfully supervise AI: the designed-in capability to understand outputs, monitor operation, interrupt the system and override its decisions, staffed by people who are competent, informed and authorised to act.\n\nThe EU AI Act makes it concrete for high-risk systems. Article 14 requires that such systems be designed so competent humans can effectively oversee them, including the ability to understand the system's outputs, to disregard or override them, and to interrupt the system through a stop button or similar procedure. It also obliges deployers to assign oversight to people with the necessary competence, training and authority. The three levels sit inside this: in-the-loop (per-decision approval), on-the-loop (supervisory monitoring with intervention power), and in-command (setting the bounds and retaining override and deactivation authority).\n\nThe word that does the work is **meaningful**. Oversight requires four things together: **competence** (the overseer understands the system's limits and failure modes), **information** (the interface shows reasons and confidence, not only a recommendation), **authority** (the overseer can reverse the output without career cost), and **time and volume** (the review load permits real attention). Remove any one and the control becomes decorative.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Oversight must be effective, not decorative: competence plus information plus authority plus time. Without all four it is automation bias with a witness. EU AI Act Article 14 is the article to cite for high-risk oversight design.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/14/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 14 (human oversight)</a> · the design requirements, including interruption and override\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/26/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 26 (deployer oversight duties)</a> · assigning competent, authorised overseers\n- <a href=\"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF Playbook, Govern function</a> · human-AI configuration and oversight actions\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · controllability and predictability vocabulary", "kt188": "## Definition\n\nA principle of responsible AI: AI systems should be designed with human well-being and dignity in mind, augmenting rather than replacing human capabilities.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nHuman-centricity is the design principle that AI serves human flourishing: augmenting rather than replacing human judgement, preserving agency and dignity, and measuring success by human outcomes rather than by model metrics. It is the value the OECD principles and the EU's human-centric framing place at the centre.\n\nThe principle cashes out in concrete requirements rather than remaining aspirational. **Human oversight** for consequential decisions (EU AI Act Article 14) is human-centricity written as a design obligation. **Contestability** is human-centricity written as a right. **Impact assessment** is human-centricity written as a process, because it asks who could be harmed rather than only whether the model works. And **accessibility and disclosure** are human-centricity written as interface requirements, so that the people affected can understand what is happening to them.\n\nIts exam function is as a tiebreaker. When answer options trade user wellbeing against an optimisation metric, or trade a person's ability to understand a decision against operational convenience, human-centricity is the principle the correct answer honours. It also supplies the reason the frameworks insist that accountability rests with a human: responsibility cannot be delegated to a system, because a system cannot be accountable to anyone.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] When options trade user wellbeing for optimisation metrics, human-centricity is the principle the correct answer honours. It is the value behind oversight, contestability and impact assessment, so those three controls are usually the human-centricity answer.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://oecd.ai/en/ai-principles\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OECD AI Principles</a> · human-centred values and fairness\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/14/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 14 (human oversight)</a> · human-centricity as a design obligation\n- <a href=\"https://www.unesco.org/en/artificial-intelligence/recommendation-ethics\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">UNESCO Recommendation on the Ethics of AI</a> · dignity, agency and human rights framing\n- <a href=\"https://www.coe.int/en/web/artificial-intelligence/the-framework-convention-on-artificial-intelligence\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Council of Europe Framework Convention on AI</a> · the binding-treaty expression of the same values", "kt189": "## Definition\n\nIntegration of human interaction and machine intelligence.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nDesigning humans and AI as a combined system with complementary strengths — machine scale and consistency, human judgment and context — rather than as a machine with a human fallback. Good teaming engineers the interface: calibrated trust, legible uncertainty, workload balance, and clear handoff points.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Over-reliance and under-reliance are teaming failures — solutions live in interface design and training, not just model accuracy.", "kt190": "## Definition\n\nA feature of a machine learning algorithm that influences its learning process. Selected **prior** to training.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nHyperparameters are the settings fixed before training rather than learned from data: learning rate, number of layers, tree depth, regularization strength, batch size, dropout rate. They are chosen by the practitioner, often through systematic search, and they shape the model's behaviour, capacity and tendency to overfit or underfit.\n\nThey are distinct from **parameters**, which are the weights training itself learns. That two-word distinction is what the exam tests.\n\nGovernance relevance is documentation and reproducibility. Hyperparameter choices, along with data versions, random seeds and code versions, are part of the record that lets an auditor or incident investigator reproduce a model and understand why it behaves as it does. A model registry entry that records only the resulting metrics, without the configuration that produced them, cannot be reproduced, and an unreproducible model cannot be meaningfully audited or remediated.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Hyperparameters are configured before training; parameters are learned during it. If a question asks which setting a practitioner selects, the answer is a hyperparameter.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · training and model terminology\n- <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.03993\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Model Cards for Model Reporting</a> · what to record about how a model was built", "kt191": "## Definition\n\nPublic Act 103-0804, amending the Illinois Human Rights Act from 1 Jan 2026. Employers may not use AI that has the effect of discriminating on a protected basis in recruitment, hiring, promotion, discipline, discharge or terms of employment. They may not use zip codes as a proxy for protected classes, and must notify employees when AI is used for these decisions. This is an effect standard, unlike Texas's intent standard.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nHB 3773 folds AI into existing civil rights law rather than creating a separate AI regime. Discrimination by an AI tool is the employer's civil rights violation even without intent, and the Act names zip codes as a forbidden proxy. Employers must also give notice of AI use, with the details left to rules from the Department of Human Rights. It sits alongside Illinois's AI Video Interview Act and BIPA.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] The test is effect, not intent: an unintended disparate impact from an employer's AI can violate HB 3773.", "kt192": "## Definition\n\nAn image classification process that classifies objects, patterns, or concepts in an image.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe computer-vision task of identifying what an image contains — objects, scenes, faces, text. Exam-relevant as the canonical narrow-AI example and as the entry point to vision governance: when recognition becomes person-identification, biometric and surveillance law activate.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Recognizing objects is minimal-risk; recognizing *people* walks into biometric special-category and AI Act territory.", "kt193": "## Definition\n\nA systematic evaluation of potential harms, benefits, compliance gaps, and ethical issues an AI system may create — required for high-risk systems under the EU AI Act.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe genus of look-before-you-leap instruments: structured evaluation of a system's potential effects before and during deployment — with species including DPIAs (GDPR, personal data), AIIAs (broad AI impacts), FRIAs (EU AI Act fundamental rights), and algorithmic impact assessments (public sector). Shared anatomy: describe, identify stakeholders, assess severity × likelihood, mitigate, document, revisit.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Match the species to the trigger: personal data high-risk → DPIA; high-risk AI deployer (public body) → FRIA; general AI ethics → AIIA.", "kt194": "## Definition\n\nAn event that should prompt an organization to revisit an existing AI system impact assessment: a change in intended use or purpose, a significant change to the system (new data, a retrained or replaced model), or a change in applicable law or policy. ISO/IEC 42001 clause 8.4 also calls for assessments at planned intervals or when significant changes are proposed.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAn impact assessment is a living record. Sources on ISO/IEC 42005 agree that a change of intended use or purpose, a significant change to the system itself (new data types, retraining, a replaced model) or a new legal or policy requirement should prompt a fresh look, alongside routine monitoring and review. ISO/IEC 42001 clause 8.4 adds assessments at planned intervals or when significant changes are proposed.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] In scenario questions, the trigger is the event that changes who is affected or how the system behaves, not administrative events like contract renewals or code moves.", "kt195": "## Definition\n\nA procedure in which missing data are replaced by estimated or modeled data.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nFilling in missing data values — with means, medians, model predictions, or learned estimates — so incomplete records remain usable. Governance angle: imputation invents data, and biased imputation (filling gaps with majority-group patterns) quietly launders missingness into unfairness.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Missing data is rarely random — question what the gaps correlate with before accepting imputed values as neutral.", "kt196": "## Definition\n\nThe process of detecting, containing, assessing, remediating, and documenting events where AI systems deviate from intended behavior or cause harm.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe discipline of handling AI failures in production: multi-channel detection, pre-authorized containment (rollback, kill switch), root-cause investigation, remediation, obligation-driven disclosure (AI Act serious incidents, GDPR breaches, sector regulators), and institutionalized lessons. AI adds novel incident species — bias events, drift failures, prompt-injection compromises — to the classic catalog.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Order is tested: detect → contain → investigate → remediate → disclose → learn; skipping containment or disclosure is the planted error.", "kt197": "## Definition\n\nA form of reasoning in which conclusions are derived from known premises. In AI, a basic premise is either a fact, a rule, a model, a feature, or raw data.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nInference has two meanings in AI governance, and both matter.\n\n**Runtime inference** is the act of a trained model producing outputs from new inputs. It is the production phase, with its own cost, latency and security surface: the serving endpoint is an asset to protect, its queries are a log to retain, and its latency is a monitoring metric. Inference is also where attacks land, since evasion and prompt injection act on inputs at inference time rather than on the training process.\n\n**Inferential processing** is deriving facts that were not explicitly provided. This is where privacy risk concentrates, because a model can infer sensitive attributes from ordinary data (purchase history implying pregnancy, typing patterns implying health status). The regulatory position is that the inference is itself processing of that sensitive attribute, so GDPR Article 9 conditions apply to special-category data the organization never collected, and the EU AI Act treats the derived output as within scope.\n\nRead the stem to see which meaning is in play: 'inference cost' is serving, 'inferred sensitive data' is privacy exposure.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Two meanings, one word. Serving-time inference is a cost and security concern; inferred data is a privacy and special-category concern. Article 9 applies to what the model derives, not only to what the organization collects.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/3/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 3(1) (infers from input)</a> · inference as the defining property of an AI system\n- <a href=\"https://gdpr-info.eu/art-9-gdpr/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GDPR, Article 9 (special categories)</a> · inferred sensitive data is still special-category processing\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-2e2023.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 100-2, Adversarial Machine Learning taxonomy</a> · evasion and inference-time attacks", "kt198": "## Definition\n\nData for which an AI system calculates a predicted result or inference.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe data a system operates on at runtime — distinct from training data, and a distinct risk surface: production inputs drift from training distributions, adversarial inputs attack the model, and deployers under the AI Act must ensure input data is relevant to the system's intended purpose.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Deployer duty under the AI Act: input data relevance — a runtime obligation distinct from the provider's training-data governance.", "kt199": "## Definition\n\nValidating that all components of an AI system work together correctly — data flows, API calls, model inference, and downstream system interactions.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nVerifying the model works inside the assembled system — data pipelines feeding it, APIs serving it, interfaces presenting it, workflows consuming it. A fair model in a system whose UI amplifies bias, or whose pipeline silently reformats inputs, still produces harm — integration is where component-level assurance meets system-level truth.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] The recurring scenario: model tests clean, system behaves badly — integration testing (and system-level fairness evaluation) is the missing control.", "kt200": "## Definition\n\nLegal rights protecting creations of the mind — copyright, patents, trademarks, trade secrets — increasingly challenged by AI training on protected content.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe exclusive-rights family — copyright, patents, trade secrets, trademarks — colliding with AI at every joint: training-data rights, output protectability (human authorship required), model-as-trade-secret, patentability of AI inventions (human inventor required), and confidentiality leakage through prompts.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Both US doctrines converge on humanity: no copyright without human authorship, no patent without human inventor.", "kt201": "## Definition\n\nThe ability to understand the relationships between patterns in data used by an AI system. Related to explainability but focused on internal mechanics.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nInterpretability is the degree to which a human can understand and predict a model's behaviour. It is a property of the model, and it varies by architecture: linear models and decision trees are inherently interpretable, because their logic can be read; deep networks are not, and their behaviour has to be explained after the fact.\n\nNIST pairs interpretability with explainability, and the pairing is precise rather than redundant. **Explainability** is about producing an account of an output. **Interpretability** is about whether that account, or the model itself, makes contextual sense to the person who has to act on it. An explanation that is faithful to the model but unintelligible to a loan officer is explainable but not interpretable, which is why the NIST characteristic is phrased as \"explainable and interpretable\" rather than as either alone.\n\nThe governance consequence is a design choice rather than a fix. Where understanding is a requirement (eligibility decisions, safety-critical settings, anything with a contestability right attached), an inherently interpretable model may be the correct choice even at an accuracy cost, and the trade-off should be documented and approved rather than made silently. Post-hoc explanation of a black box is the fallback, not the default.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Inherently interpretable model versus post-hoc explanation of a black box: that is the design choice the exam poses when stakes demand understanding. Explainable is not the same as interpretable, and NIST pairs them for that reason.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · explainable and interpretable as one characteristic\n- <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.07874\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SHAP: a unified approach to interpreting model predictions</a> · post-hoc interpretation\n- <a href=\"https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/artificial-intelligence/explaining-decisions-made-with-artificial-intelligence/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ICO: explaining decisions made with AI</a> · audience-appropriate explanation", "kt202": "## Definition\n\nAssessing whether AI system decisions can be understood and explained in human terms — critical for high-stakes domains like healthcare and finance.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nInterpretability testing evaluates whether a model's explanations actually work: whether they are **faithful** to real model behaviour, **understandable** to the intended audience, and **useful** for the decision that audience has to make. Explanations that fail these tests are compliance theatre.\n\nThe fidelity point is the one most often missed. A plausible narrative about why a model decided something is not evidence that it decided that way, and post-hoc methods can produce explanations that are stable and convincing while misrepresenting the underlying computation. Testing fidelity means checking the explanation against the model's actual sensitivity to the features it names. The audience point is the second: an accurate explanation expressed in terms the reader cannot use has not discharged the obligation, which is why the test has to involve the humans who will rely on it rather than only technical reviewers. The usefulness point is the third: the explanation must support the action the person is taking, whether that is approving, overriding, or explaining the decision to the person affected.\n\nThis is the operational form of the NIST \"explainable and interpretable\" characteristic: explanations exist and they make contextual sense. Testing both halves is what turns a claim into evidence.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Explanations must be faithful, understandable AND useful. A question offering an accurate explanation nobody comprehends, or a plausible explanation that does not match the model's real behaviour, is testing which of the three is missing.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.04938\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LIME: explaining the predictions of any classifier</a> · fidelity of local explanations\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · explainable and interpretable as a tested characteristic\n- <a href=\"https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/artificial-intelligence/explaining-decisions-made-with-artificial-intelligence/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ICO: explaining decisions made with AI</a> · the audience and purpose test", "kt203": "## Definition\n\nThe generic risk management standard that ISO/IEC 23894 mirrors clause-by-clause and extends with AI-specific guidance — principles (Clause 4), framework (Clause 5), and process (Clause 6). ISO 23894 extends it; it does not replace it.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nISO's generic risk management standard — principles, framework, and process (identify, analyze, evaluate, treat, monitor, record) applicable to any risk in any organization. Its AI relevance is structural: ISO/IEC 23894 mirrors it clause-by-clause, extending each element with AI-specific guidance rather than inventing a new discipline.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] 23894 extends 31000, it does not replace it — the relationship is the tested fact.", "kt204": "## Definition\n\nInternational standard establishing terminology and definitions for AI systems, capabilities, and lifecycle stages — foundational reference for all other ISO AI standards.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe AI vocabulary standard: 110+ formal definitions — system roles (provider, producer, customer, subject), lifecycle stages, ML paradigms, trustworthiness properties (robustness, reliability, controllability) — that other standards and regulations lean on for interoperability. A large share of this app's flashcards trace to it.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] When a question hinges on a precise definition of an AI concept, 22989 is the source being quoted.", "kt205": "## Definition\n\nThe first AI-specific management system standard organizations can certify against (like ISO 27001 for security). Specifies requirements for an AI Management System (AIMS).\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe certifiable AI management system standard (2023): Annex SL structure, PDCA cycle, mandatory AI risk and impact assessments, Annex A control catalog — the ISO 27001 of AI. Organizations certify the management system through accredited third-party audit, signaling assurance to customers and regulators.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] The differentiators: certifiable (vs. NIST's voluntary framework) and management-system-scoped (vs. 23894's risk guidance).", "kt206": "## Definition\n\nInternational standard (first edition, 28 May 2025) giving guidance on AI system impact assessment: how to set up the process (Clause 5), what each assessment records (Clause 6), and how it fits with ISO/IEC 42001 and 23894 (Annexes A and B). Voluntary guidance for any organization that develops, provides or uses AI; not certifiable.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe how-to for AI system impact assessment. Clause 5 sets up the organizational process (documentation, integration, timing, scope, responsibilities, thresholds, performing, analysing, recording, approval, monitoring and review); Clause 6 lists what each assessment records (system, data, model, deployment environment, interested parties, benefits and harms, measures). Five informative annexes link it to 42001 (A) and 23894 (B) and supply a harms and benefits taxonomy (C), alignment with other assessments (D) and a template (E).\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Guidance, not certifiable, and for developers, providers and users alike: any option offering a '42005 certificate' or limiting it to developers is wrong.", "kt207": "## Definition\n\nISO/IEC 42006:2025 (published 7 July 2025): requirements, additional to ISO/IEC 17021-1, for bodies that audit and certify AI management systems against ISO/IEC 42001. It governs the certifiers, not the organizations being certified.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nPublished 7 July 2025, ISO/IEC 42006 supplements ISO/IEC 17021-1 with AI-specific requirements for certification bodies that audit AI management systems against ISO/IEC 42001. Accreditation bodies use it to judge whether a certifier is competent and consistent. It is not named in the AIGP blueprint, but it makes a handy distractor.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] 42001 = what you get certified to; 42005 = impact assessment guidance; 42006 = rules for whoever issues the 42001 certificate.", "kt208": "## Definition\n\nThe systematic process of identifying, tracking, prioritizing, and resolving problems with deployed AI systems — bridging incidents to long-term remediation.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe tracking discipline for problems below incident severity: logged, owned, prioritized, remediated, and trended — bugs, degradations, complaints, near-misses. Its governance value is the pipeline: issue trends are the early-warning system that catches incidents while they're still issues.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Issues are managed continuously; incidents trigger response protocols — the severity threshold between them should be predefined.", "kt209": "## Definition\n\n*\\<Artificial Intelligence>* Abstracted information about objects, events, concepts, or rules, their relationships, and properties, organized for purposeful systematic use. Does **not** imply cognitive abilities.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nIn ISO 22989 usage: the structured information about the world an AI system holds and reasons over — hand-encoded in symbolic systems (knowledge bases, ontologies), learned and implicit in neural ones. The symbolic/learned split in knowledge representation is the deep divide between AI paradigms.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Terminology recognition: explicit knowledge bases belong to symbolic AI; learned implicit knowledge belongs to connectionist ML.", "kt210": "## Definition\n\nPresidential Decree No. 36053 (21 Jan 2026), in force with the Act on 22 Jan 2026. It fills in the Act's thresholds and procedures, including the 10^26 FLOP safety test, the domestic-representative thresholds, labeling methods, the 30-day high-impact confirmation process and five-year document retention.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe Act leaves most of its numbers and procedures to this Presidential Decree, which was issued after public consultation on the subordinate legislation during 2025. It sets the three-part test for the Art. 32 safety duties (10^26 FLOPs, state of the art, wide-ranging risk), the domestic-representative thresholds, the notice and labeling methods and their exemptions, and the 30-day high-impact confirmation process. It also fixes five-year document retention, lists what an impact assessment covers, and creates the deemed-compliance rule for AI-using operators. MSIT notices and guidelines sit below it.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Korea's 10^26 FLOP figure and the KRW 1 trillion representative threshold come from the decree, not the Act.", "kt211": "## Definition\n\nA model-type choice in deployment evaluation: language models process and generate text only, while multimodal models handle combinations of text, images, audio, or video. Multimodality expands use cases and also expands the risk surface — each added modality brings its own failure modes, biases, and content risks.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe modality axis of model choice: language-only models process text; multimodal models add images, audio, or video — expanding use cases and simultaneously multiplying failure modes, since every added modality carries its own biases, content risks, and evaluation burden. Multimodal evaluation is genuinely harder: each input-output combination needs testing.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Multimodality expands the risk surface with the capability — an answer adding modalities without adding evaluation scope is incomplete.", "kt212": "## Definition\n\nUnder California SB 53, New York's RAISE Act and Illinois SB 315: a developer that has trained or begun training a frontier model (more than 10^26 operations) and that, with its affiliates, had more than $500 million in gross revenue in the preceding calendar year. Large frontier developers carry the heaviest duties: a published frontier AI framework, regulator filings and, in Illinois from 2028, annual independent audits.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe two-part test of frontier compute plus revenue puts the heaviest obligations on a handful of well-resourced companies, while other frontier developers carry lighter transparency and incident-reporting duties. Revenue is counted with affiliates for the preceding calendar year. The same definition appears in all three state frontier laws, so it is a reliable anchor across jurisdictions.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Both limbs must be met: more than 10^26 training operations and more than $500 million in prior-year revenue.", "kt213": "## Definition\n\nA type of AI model trained on vast text corpora, capable of understanding and generating human-like text. Examples: GPT-4, Claude, Gemini.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAn LLM is a transformer-based generative model trained on very large text corpora to predict the next token, which yields broad language competence: drafting, summarising, translating, coding, question answering. The same mechanism produces the signature risk bundle, and the exam tests the bundle as much as the definition.\n\n- **Confabulation**: fluent, confident, false output, structural to next-token prediction.\n- **Memorization and leakage**: models can reproduce fragments of training data, which is the empirical basis for the privacy concerns (Carlini et al.'s extraction work).\n- **Prompt injection**: instructions smuggled through inputs, directly or via retrieved content.\n- **Bias and homogenization**: inherited from training data, amplified by scale.\n- **Intellectual property exposure**: training on protected works and unclear output ownership.\n\nRegulatory position: most LLMs are **general-purpose AI models** under the EU AI Act, so their providers owe technical documentation, information for downstream providers, a copyright policy and a public training-content summary. The largest may cross the systemic-risk presumption (cumulative training compute above 10^25 FLOPs), which adds model evaluations, adversarial testing, risk mitigation, incident reporting and cybersecurity duties.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] LLM scenarios test the risk bundle (confabulation, leakage, injection, bias) and the GPAI classification consequence. An option asserting that a well-tested LLM cannot be confidently wrong is wrong by construction.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/53/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 53 (GPAI provider obligations)</a> · the duties most LLM providers carry\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 600-1, Generative AI Profile</a> · the twelve GAI risks\n- <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.08232\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Secret Sharer: unintended memorization in neural networks</a> · evidence for memorization and extraction risk", "kt214": "## Definition\n\nGranting each agent only the minimum tools, data and rights its task needs: scoped, time- or session-bound and non-transferable, with explicit escalation for anything more, and never beyond what the authorising human may do. A core control for bounding agent risk up front, enforced by access controls rather than prompts.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe IMDA framework asks that agent authority be scoped, least-privilege by default, time- or session-bound and non-transferable, with explicit escalation for more, and that a human never delegate more access than they hold; delegations should be recorded. OWASP adds just-in-time, short-lived credentials and per-action authorisation for privileged steps. Enforce these limits through access controls at the tool layer, not instructions the model might ignore.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] An agent's permissions are capped by its authorising human's permissions: delegation can never enlarge access.", "kt215": "## Definition\n\nThe development of a system, product, service, project, or other human-made entity from planning through to decommissioning.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe governed arc of an AI system — ISO 22989's stages run inception → design & development → verification & validation → deployment → operation & monitoring → re-evaluation → retirement. Lifecycle thinking is the spine of every framework: obligations, documentation, and risk work attach stage by stage, cradle to grave.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Governance is lifecycle-long — any answer implying it ends at deployment ('we validated it before launch') is structurally wrong.", "kt216": "## Definition\n\nAI systems that retain information for short periods and use past experiences to improve decisions. Example: autonomous vehicles processing recent traffic data.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nLimited memory AI is the functionality tier that retains recent information to inform decisions: an autonomous vehicle tracking nearby cars and their trajectories, a chatbot holding the thread of a conversation, a recommender reacting to the last few items viewed. It does not build persistent self-knowledge or learn from that history in a lasting way, which is what distinguishes it from the theoretical tiers above.\n\nNearly all deployed learning systems sit here, and that is why it is the governance-relevant tier. Memory introduces two properties that matter. First, **statefulness**: an output depends on what came before, so reproduction, explanation and audit require the history rather than the triggering input alone. Second, **temporal drift**: the context the system conditions on changes as the world changes, which is one of the mechanisms behind concept drift and why monitoring has to watch performance over time rather than only at release.\n\nThe exam uses the ladder for example-matching: reactive machines (Deep Blue, no memory), limited memory (self-driving cars, conversation context), theory of mind and self-aware (both theoretical).\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Ladder placement: reactive (Deep Blue), limited memory (self-driving cars), theory of mind, self-aware (theoretical). Statefulness is the property that complicates audit and explanation at this tier.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · system capability vocabulary\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · monitoring and drift in the Measure and Manage functions", "kt217": "## Definition\n\nProcedures for restricting an AI system's operation to specific geographic regions, jurisdictions, or contexts — often required by data residency or regulatory mandates.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nRules requiring data to be stored or processed within a jurisdiction — motivated by sovereignty, surveillance, and enforcement concerns (Russia, China localization laws; EU data-residency preferences). For AI it constrains architecture: training pipelines, cloud regions, and inference endpoints must respect where data may live.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Localization is the inverse of free transfer — where transfers can't be legitimized, keeping data in-jurisdiction is the compliance path.", "kt218": "## Definition\n\nA type of recurrent neural network that processes sequential data with satisfactory performance for both long- and short-term dependencies.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe LSTM is the gated recurrent architecture that made sequence learning work at length. Input, forget and output gates control what information a network carries forward across time steps, which solves the vanishing-gradient problem that limited plain RNNs to short dependencies.\n\nIt was the state of the art for language, speech and time-series tasks before transformers displaced it for language. For governance purposes it is history with a lesson: architecture progress is what changes the risk profile, and the governance professional has to track capability shifts rather than assume a fixed technology.\n\nThere is also a documentation point. Sequence models carry state across inputs, so a single output depends on what came before it. That makes reproduction and explanation harder than for stateless models, and it is one of the reasons an audit trail for a deployed sequence model needs the input history, not just the input that produced the output under review.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] LSTM is an RNN variant that handles long sequences through gating. Architecture recognition is the test level; transformers superseded it for language, so expect it as a historical option.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · recurrent network terminology\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · documentation and traceability expectations", "kt219": "## Definition\n\nThe process of optimizing model parameters using computer-aided methods so that the behavior of the model corresponds to the data or experience.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nMachine learning is the dominant AI paradigm: systems improve at tasks by deriving patterns from data rather than by following explicit programming. The families are supervised, unsupervised, semi-supervised, reinforcement and self-supervised learning.\n\nThe paradigm shift is the governance shift. When behaviour is specified in code, review means reading the code. When behaviour is learned from data, review means governing the data, the objective and the evaluation, and accepting that the resulting behaviour is a statistical artefact rather than a specification. That is why data governance, lineage and provenance, representativeness and bias testing sit at the centre of every AI governance framework: they are the only points at which the model's behaviour can be influenced before it exists.\n\nKeep the subset relationships straight, because they are tested directly: machine learning is a subset of AI, and deep learning is a subset of machine learning.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] The definitional phrase to recognize is 'learns from data without explicit programming'. ML is a subset of AI, and deep learning is a subset of ML; distractors that invert those relationships are common.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · the formal learning-paradigm definitions\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · data and measurement as the core governance levers\n- <a href=\"https://iapp.org/resources/article/key-terms-for-ai-governance/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IAPP Key Terms for AI Governance</a> · the glossary the BOK leans on", "kt220": "## Definition\n\nAlgorithm for determining parameters of a machine learning model from data according to specified criteria.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe algorithm is the learning procedure: gradient descent on a neural network, recursive partitioning in a decision tree, margin maximisation in an SVM, policy optimisation in reinforcement learning. It consumes training data and produces a trained model.\n\nAlgorithm selection is a governed design choice, not a purely technical one, because families differ systematically along axes a governance professional cares about:\n\n| Axis | Why it matters |\n|---|---|\n| Interpretability | Trees and linear models can be inspected; deep networks cannot |\n| Data hunger | Some families need orders of magnitude more labelled data |\n| Accuracy ceiling | The choice bounds what evaluation can ever achieve |\n| Failure modes | Different families fail differently under distribution shift |\n\nThe BOK's design guidance reflects this: enforce interpretable models unless a performance trade-off is documented and approved, and require justification for complex architectures. Recording which algorithm was chosen and why is part of the design documentation an auditor will ask for.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Algorithm is the recipe; model is the artifact the recipe produces. A question asking why one technique was chosen over another is testing the interpretability-versus-accuracy trade-off, not the mathematics.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · algorithm, model and training definitions\n- <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.07874\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SHAP: a unified approach to interpreting model predictions</a> · post-hoc interpretation of the less interpretable families\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · valid and reliable, explainable and interpretable", "kt221": "## Definition\n\nA mathematical construct that makes an inference or a prediction based on input data or information. Result of training based on a ML algorithm.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe model is the trained artifact: learned parameters plus the architecture they sit in, ready to map new inputs to outputs. It is training's output and inference's engine, the point at which data becomes behaviour.\n\nThat makes it the unit governance actually operates on. Models get **versioned** (so an incident can be traced to a specific artifact), **validated** (so release is a documented decision), **documented** (model cards recording intended use, data, metrics and limitations), **attacked** (inversion, membership inference, extraction, evasion), and **monitored** (drift, fairness, performance). It is also the artifact that regulation reaches: the EU AI Act's technical documentation, logging and post-market monitoring duties attach to systems built around models, and a high-risk provider must be able to say which version is in the field.\n\nA model also embodies its training data. That is why deletion requests, bias findings and copyright claims all end up pointing at the artifact rather than at a database row.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] The model is the governed asset: version it, document it, test it, monitor it. An option that says governance applies to the application code but not to the model artifact misses the object of the whole discipline.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.03993\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Model Cards for Model Reporting</a> · the documentation standard for a model artifact\n- <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.09010\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Datasheets for Datasets</a> · the parallel documentation standard for the data behind it\n- <a href=\"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF Playbook</a> · measurement, tracking and documentation actions", "kt222": "## Definition\n\nTask of automatically translating text or speech from one natural language into another using a computer system.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAutomated translation between human languages — the NLP task whose history traces AI's paradigm shifts: rule-based, statistical, then neural (transformers were invented for it). Governance notes: quality asymmetry across language pairs disadvantages low-resource languages, and consequential-use translation errors carry real harm.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] ISO 22989 task vocabulary — plus a quiet fairness angle: performance disparity across languages is a bias pattern.", "kt223": "## Definition\n\nA rightsholder's express reservation of rights against Article 4 text and data mining. For content made publicly available online, the DSM Directive requires the reservation to be machine-readable, for example in metadata or website terms. GPAI providers must identify and honour these reservations with state-of-the-art technologies under AI Act Art. 53(1)(c).\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nRecital 18 of the DSM Directive says that for online content a reservation is appropriate only by machine-readable means, including metadata and the terms and conditions of a website or service; offline, contracts or unilateral declarations can work. The GPAI Code of Practice commits signatories to respect robots.txt and other appropriate machine-readable protocols, and the Commission consulted from 1 December 2025 to 23 January 2026 on which protocols should count as generally agreed. A reservation has no effect on the Article 3 research exception.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] An opt-out blocks Article 4 mining only; it never blocks Article 3 scientific research by research organisations.", "kt224": "## Definition\n\nA planned cadence for AI system updates, retraining, and maintenance — frequency determined by risk level, data volatility, and regulatory requirements.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe planned cadence of AI upkeep: model retraining windows, data refresh cycles, dependency patching, performance reviews, and documentation updates — turning 'we'll retrain when it breaks' into governed, budgeted operations with owners.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Predefined maintenance beats reactive maintenance in every scenario — models decay on schedule, so upkeep should run on one too.", "kt225": "## Definition\n\nNIST AI RMF function: allocating risk resources to mapped and measured risks — prioritizing, responding to, and recovering from AI incidents, plus ongoing monitoring.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe AI RMF function where risk work becomes action: prioritize mapped-and-measured risks, select and implement responses (mitigate, transfer, avoid, accept), plan incident response and recovery, and monitor treatment effectiveness over time — allocating resources to risks by priority.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Map identifies, Measure analyzes, Manage acts — verbs distinguish the functions; response selection and prioritization signal Manage.", "kt226": "## Definition\n\nA product liability claim where a product deviates from its intended design during production — AI systems with deployment errors or corrupted models.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe product-liability theory for units deviating from their intended design — the flawed copy, not the flawed blueprint. For AI it's the awkward fit among the three theories (software replicates perfectly), which is why design defect and failure-to-warn carry most algorithmic liability weight.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Manufacturing = deviation from design; design = the design itself is dangerous; warning = known risks uncommunicated — classify the fact pattern.", "kt227": "## Definition\n\nNIST AI RMF function: establishing context to frame AI risks — understanding system purpose, categorization, benefits/costs, and mapping risks to impact on individuals and society.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe AI RMF's context-establishment function: characterize the system, its purpose, its stakeholders, its deployment setting, and identify risks in that frame — because AI risk is contextual, the same model being high-risk here and trivial there. Mapping output feeds Measure and Manage.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Map = establish context and identify risks — it's the function that makes 'risk depends on context' operational.", "kt228": "## Definition\n\nEU AI Act inspection powers: require access to technical documentation, perform unannounced inspections and digital audits, mandate corrective actions, suspend or prohibit marketing, and require withdrawal or recall of non-compliant systems.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nWhat EU AI Act enforcers can actually do: demand access to documentation, data, and (for high-risk source verification) even models; conduct inspections including digital audits; order corrective actions; and escalate to suspension, withdrawal, or recall of non-compliant systems. Obstruction has its own penalty exposure.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] The escalation ladder — information demands → corrective orders → market removal — mirrors other EU product law; authorities can compel documentation access.", "kt229": "## Definition\n\nNIST AI RMF function: employing quantitative and qualitative methods to analyze and track AI risks — testing, evaluation, verification, and validation (TEVV).\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe AI RMF's analytic engine: assess, benchmark, and track the risks Map identified — quantitative metrics and qualitative evaluation of trustworthiness characteristics, testing before and during deployment, with results feeding Manage's response decisions. It's where 'we think it's fair' becomes 'we measured, here's the evidence.'\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Measure = assess and track with metrics and TEVV — evaluation vocabulary (test, benchmark, monitor metrics) signals this function.", "kt230": "## Definition\n\nA privacy attack that determines whether a specific individual's record was part of a model's training set. A key AI-specific threat that existing privacy policies typically fail to address.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nA membership inference attack asks the model whether a specific person's record was in its training data. The mechanism is a telltale statistical difference: a model tends to be more confident, or lower-loss, on examples it saw during training than on examples it did not, so an attacker can query it and infer participation.\n\nThe harm is that **participation itself is a disclosure**. Knowing that someone's medical records trained a disease model reveals something about them, even if no record content is recovered. That makes it a privacy harm and a GDPR-relevant one, and it is one of the reasons the EDPB's analysis of AI models treats the anonymity question as empirical rather than formal: a model trained on personal data is not automatically anonymous.\n\nDefenses reduce rather than eliminate the signal: differential privacy during training, regularization and early stopping, limiting the number of training epochs, output perturbation, and rate-limiting or restricting query access to the model. The governance consequences are monitoring for anomalous query patterns, treating the model endpoint as a privacy-relevant asset, and documenting the residual risk. The exam-relevant contrast: membership inference reveals **presence in training data**; model inversion reconstructs **the data itself**.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Membership inference confirms participation; model inversion reconstructs content. Both are privacy attacks on the model rather than on a database, which is why conventional threat models miss them and why the model endpoint is a governed asset.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.08232\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Secret Sharer: unintended memorization in neural networks</a> · the research behind extraction and membership risk\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-2e2023.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 100-2, Adversarial Machine Learning taxonomy</a> · privacy attacks as a structured category\n- <a href=\"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2024-12/edpb_opinion_202428_ai-models_en.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EDPB Opinion 28/2024 on AI models and personal data</a> · anonymity, legitimate interest and model training", "kt231": "## Definition\n\nA risk where an AI system's behavior, benign in testing, leads to unintended harmful consequences when deployed — often due to poorly specified objectives.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nMisalignment is the gap between what a system optimises and what its operators actually value: proxy metrics gamed, specifications exploited, objectives satisfied in letter while violated in spirit. The BOK names it as one of its three harm examples for I.A, which signals how central it is to the domain's framing.\n\nThe mechanism is usually a proxy. A model told to maximise engagement maximises it, which may mean amplifying outrage. A model told to reduce fraud flags more transactions, including legitimate ones. An agent told to complete a task completes it, including by routes nobody intended. The system is not malfunctioning; it is doing exactly what it was asked, which is why the failure is invisible to conventional testing against specification.\n\nGovernance responses work on the objective and the evaluation rather than on the model. Define the outcome you actually want and the guardrails around it, not only the metric. Evaluate against intent with people who understand the domain, rather than only against the metric you specified. Monitor for the side effects the metric ignores, because those are where misalignment shows up first. And document the objective's limits, so a later use does not inherit an optimisation target that never fitted it.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] The optimizing-the-wrong-thing pattern: when a scenario's system achieves its metric while causing harm, misalignment is the named diagnosis. The control is objective design plus evaluation against intent, not more training data.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · Map and Measure on intended purpose versus observed behaviour\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 600-1, Generative AI Profile</a> · value-chain and human-AI configuration risks\n- <a href=\"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF Playbook</a> · documenting purpose, assumptions and limitations", "kt232": "## Definition\n\nFalse or misleading content shared without intent to deceive, such as a user reposting a chatbot's invented statistic in the belief that it is true. The falsehood can be identical to disinformation; the difference is the absence of intent. NIST AI 600-1 flags generative AI as easing its unintentional production and spread.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nMisinformation is the honest-mistake category: false or misleading content spread by people who believe it. The distinction from disinformation is intent, not accuracy. The same false claim can be disinformation at its source and misinformation by the time it reaches the person who repeats it.\n\nGenerative AI makes misinformation easier to produce inadvertently. NIST AI 600-1 notes that confabulation turns a chatbot into a source of plausible falsehoods, which then get copied into documents, summaries and advice. The harm compounds because the output carries the authority of a tool rather than the uncertainty of a rumour.\n\nMitigations target both the source and the reader. On the source side: ground answers in retrieved, citable material, label AI-generated content, and measure fabrication rates rather than assuming them. On the reader side: train users to verify claims before relying on them, and design interfaces that show provenance rather than presenting generated text as settled fact. For an organization's own outputs, accuracy obligations also attach: publishing false information about people engages data-protection accuracy duties, and overstating what a system can do engages consumer-protection deception standards.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] No intent to deceive means misinformation, even when the content originated as someone else's disinformation. The controls are grounding, labelling and user verification, plus accuracy duties on anything published about real people.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 600-1, Generative AI Profile</a> · information integrity and confabulation\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/50/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 50 (transparency and labelling)</a> · disclosure of generated content\n- <a href=\"https://gdpr-info.eu/art-5-gdpr/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GDPR, Article 5(1)(d) (accuracy)</a> · accuracy duties on personal data, including derived output", "kt233": "## Definition\n\nAdversarial Threat Landscape for Artificial-Intelligence Systems — a knowledge base of adversary tactics and techniques specific to AI systems, modeled after MITRE ATT&CK.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe adversarial-ML threat knowledge base — ATT&CK's AI sibling: a matrix of real-world attack tactics and techniques against AI systems (poisoning, evasion, model theft, prompt injection) with case studies. Used to structure AI threat modeling, red-team planning, and security-control mapping.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] ATLAS = adversarial threat catalog for AI security — distinct from risk frameworks (RMF) and management standards (42001).", "kt234": "## Definition\n\nA physical, mathematical, or other logical representation of a system, an entity, a phenomenon, a process, or data.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nA model is the mathematical representation of learned patterns: parameters plus architecture, turning inputs into predictions, classifications, scores or content. It is distinct from the algorithm that produced it (the procedure) and from the system that contains it (the model plus pipelines, interfaces, data flows and operational context).\n\nThe distinction matters because governance attaches at different levels for different purposes. Risk analysis decomposes to the model, because that is where bias, opacity and attack surface live. Accountability attaches to the system and its operator, because that is where harm lands and where a human can be named. Documentation spans both: model cards for the artifact, system cards for the assembled deployment.\n\nIn ISO/IEC 22989 terms, an AI system is composed of AI components, and the model is the central component. Keeping that vocabulary straight is what lets you answer a question about which layer an obligation or a control belongs to.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Model is the learned artifact, not the algorithm and not the system. If a stem asks who is accountable for an outcome, the answer is a human or organization, never the model.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · AI model, AI component and AI system\n- <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.03993\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Model Cards for Model Reporting</a> · model-level documentation", "kt235": "## Definition\n\nA short document released with a trained model that states its intended and out-of-scope uses, how it was evaluated, how it performs across relevant groups and conditions, and its known limitations (format proposed by Mitchell et al., 2019). BOK III.C lists creating the model card as part of release readiness; a system card covers the whole deployed system and a datasheet covers the dataset.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nIntroduced by Mitchell et al. at FAT* 2019, a model card is the model's specification sheet for anyone deciding whether and how to use it: intended and out-of-scope uses, evaluation data and metrics, results broken down by relevant groups and conditions, ethical considerations and caveats. The BOK places creating the model card in release readiness (III.C). It pairs with a datasheet, which documents a dataset, and a system card, which documents how the models and components of a deployed system work together.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Model card = one model's uses, performance and limits; datasheet = the dataset; system card = the whole deployed system.", "kt236": "## Definition\n\nA privacy attack that reconstructs sensitive training data from a model's outputs or parameters. AI-era privacy and security policies must add controls for this threat — traditional policies assume data minimization applies only to storage, not to what a trained model retains.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nA model inversion attack reconstructs training data by querying the model: recovering a recognisable face from a recognition system, or inferring sensitive attributes from a classifier's outputs. It works because the model's behaviour encodes statistical properties of its training examples, and repeated, careful queries can recover more of them than intuition suggests.\n\nIts governance significance is that it turns \"the model is not the data\" from a legal argument into an empirical question. If reconstruction succeeds, personal data is being disclosed by the model, and the organization is processing and exposing data it may believe it no longer holds. That has consequences for deletion requests (the data persists in the artifact), for transparency obligations (the model is a processing operation), and for breach assessment (an extraction event may be a personal data breach without any database being touched).\n\nControls follow the same family as membership inference defenses: differential privacy, limiting what the model exposes through its API (confidence scores, gradients, embeddings), restricting query volume, and monitoring for systematic probing. The exam-relevant contrast is severity and mechanism: inversion reconstructs content, while membership inference only confirms participation.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Inversion reconstructs training data; membership inference merely confirms participation. The severity and mechanism differ, and both mean the model artifact is a privacy surface rather than an inert output.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-2e2023.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 100-2, Adversarial Machine Learning taxonomy</a> · privacy attacks on models\n- <a href=\"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2024-12/edpb_opinion_202428_ai-models_en.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EDPB Opinion 28/2024 on AI models and personal data</a> · whether a trained model is anonymous\n- <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.08232\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Secret Sharer: unintended memorization in neural networks</a> · extraction of training data from models", "kt237": "## Definition\n\nA system in which several agents cooperate, for example in sequential, supervisor or swarm patterns. Splitting work lets each agent be scoped narrowly, but interaction adds risks: cascading errors, miscoordination, conflicting goals, collusion, emergent behaviour, and sensitive data leaking through shared memory.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nCommon patterns are sequential (each agent's output feeds the next), supervisor (one agent calls specialists as tools) and swarm (agents work in parallel and hand off). The design lets each agent carry only the tools it needs, but IMDA flags miscoordination, conflicting objectives, collusion and emergent behaviour, and OWASP's ASI07 and ASI08 cover insecure inter-agent messages and cascading failures. Test the agents together as well as individually.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Splitting one all-powerful agent into narrow agents improves least privilege but adds interaction risk, and the best answer usually names both.", "kt238": "## Definition\n\nTask of recognizing and labeling the names of entities and their categories for sequences of words.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe NLP task of finding and classifying named entities — people, organizations, locations, dates — in text. Governance relevance: NER is how pipelines detect personal data at scale, making it both a privacy-compliance tool (PII discovery) and a privacy risk (systematic identification).\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] ISO 22989 task vocabulary — recognize it as the PII-detection workhorse in data governance tooling.", "kt239": "## Definition\n\nA type of AI system designed to perform specific tasks in order to solve a particular problem.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nNarrow AI is AI built for a specific task or task family: spam filtering, face matching, translation, recommendation, and large language models too. Everything deployed today is narrow AI. The capability ladder's higher rungs (AGI, ASI) are theoretical.\n\nThe term does useful work in two directions. For **risk analysis**, it keeps assessment capability-specific: a system that cannot do X cannot fail at X, and a governance plan should be scoped to what the system actually does rather than to what its category implies. For **vendor claims**, it is a discipline: describing a product as general or near-general intelligence is a capability claim, and unsubstantiated capability claims are consumer-protection exposure under the FTC Act's deception standard and equivalent regimes.\n\nBreadth is not generality. An LLM that answers questions about law, medicine and poetry is still narrow, because generality means transferring competence across unrelated domains the way a person does, not covering many subjects from one large training corpus.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] All existing AI is narrow, and breadth of use does not graduate a system to AGI. Any option that treats a current product as general intelligence is wrong by definition.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://oecd.ai/en/wonk/definition\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OECD: what is an AI system?</a> · the functional definition that sidesteps capability tiers\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · formal capability vocabulary\n- <a href=\"https://www.ftc.gov/industry/technology/artificial-intelligence\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">FTC artificial intelligence resources</a> · unsubstantiated AI capability claims as deception", "kt240": "## Definition\n\nOne or more authorities per member state responsible for market surveillance, handling complaints, investigating breaches, and imposing penalties within their jurisdiction.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nEach member state's designated enforcers: at least one notifying authority (accrediting conformity-assessment bodies) and one market surveillance authority (investigating, correcting, penalizing) — due for designation by August 2025, coordinating through the European AI Board.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] National authorities handle AI systems; the EU-level AI Office handles GPAI — the two-tier enforcement split recurs.", "kt241": "## Definition\n\nA language that is or was actively used in a community of people and whose rules can be derived from usage.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nHuman language as humans actually produce it — ambiguous, contextual, evolving — as distinct from formal languages built for machines. Its messiness is why NLP is hard and why language-model errors are subtle: fluent form conceals unreliable content.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Contrast anchor: natural language (human, ambiguous) vs. programming language (formal, exact) — a definitional distractor pattern.", "kt242": "## Definition\n\nTask of converting semantic data into natural language.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe production side of language AI: generating human-readable text from data, structured inputs, or learned distributions — from template-driven report writing to free-form LLM generation. Its governance shadow is confabulation: fluency without guaranteed truth.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] NLU understands input; NLG produces output — the U/G split inside NLP is tested at recognition level.", "kt243": "## Definition\n\nAI systems that understand, interpret, and generate human language by combining linguistics and machine learning. Example: ChatGPT.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nNLP is the field concerned with understanding, interpreting and generating human language: translation, sentiment analysis, summarisation, question answering, speech transcription, and the large language models that now dominate it. Natural language understanding and natural language generation are its comprehension and production halves.\n\nGovernance relevance is unusually concentrated, because language is where most personal data lives and where most manipulation happens. An NLP system that processes customer messages is processing personal data; one that summarises employee feedback may be processing special-category data; one that generates persuasive text at scale is an information-integrity and consumer-protection concern. Text is also the medium of prompt injection, which makes any NLP system with tool access an agentic security surface.\n\nTwo documentation artifacts travel with NLP systems specifically: the training-content summary that EU AI Act Article 53 requires from general-purpose AI providers, and the transparency disclosure that Article 50 requires when a person is interacting with an AI rather than a human.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] NLP is the umbrella term for language tasks; NLU and NLG are its two halves. When a scenario involves chatbots, summarisation or generated text, expect the transparency disclosure and training-content documentation duties to be in play.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/50/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 50 (transparency obligations)</a> · disclosing AI interaction and labelling generated content\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · NLP task definitions\n- <a href=\"https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications</a> · the language-model attack surface", "kt244": "## Definition\n\nExtraction of information from text or speech conveyed in natural language, creating a description of the given text/speech and its representation.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe comprehension side of NLP: extracting meaning, intent, and structure from human language — intent classification, entity extraction, semantic parsing. It's what lets a system act on 'cancel my subscription' — and misunderstanding at this layer propagates into wrong actions downstream.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Understanding (NLU) in, generation (NLG) out — match the definition to the direction.", "kt245": "## Definition\n\n*\\<Artificial Intelligence>* A network consisting of one or more layers of neurons connected by weighted links with adjustable weights, which accepts input data and generates an output.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nA neural network is a computing architecture loosely inspired by biological neurons: layered nodes connected by weights that training adjusts through backpropagation, capable of approximating very complex functions. It is the foundation of deep learning and, therefore, of the opacity problem, because a set of learned weights explains nothing on inspection.\n\nTwo governance consequences follow from the architecture rather than from any particular model. First, **behaviour is data-derived**, so data quality, representativeness and provenance are the levers that determine fairness and accuracy. Second, **explanation must be reconstructed**, which is why a whole family of post-hoc techniques exists and why explanation quality itself has to be tested rather than assumed.\n\nNeural networks also carry a distinct attack surface. Small, deliberately chosen input perturbations can flip outputs (evasion), and because the network is a continuous function, adversarial examples can often be found reliably rather than by chance. That is why security testing for a neural system has to include adversarial robustness and not only conventional penetration testing.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Inspired-by-biology, interconnected nodes, learned weights: those are the definitional phrases. Distractors confuse neural networks with computer networking, and adversarial robustness is the security property a question about perturbation attacks is reaching for.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · neural network and deep learning definitions\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-2e2023.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 100-2, Adversarial Machine Learning taxonomy</a> · evasion attacks against neural models\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · secure and resilient as a trustworthiness characteristic", "kt246": "## Definition\n\n*\\<Artificial Intelligence>* A primitive processing element that accepts one or more input values and generates an output value by combining the input values and applying an activation function.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe network's atomic unit: receives weighted inputs, sums them, applies an activation function, passes the signal on. Individually trivial, collectively capable — intelligence emerges from scale and connection, which is the connectionist thesis in one component.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Anatomy vocabulary: neuron (unit) → layer (collection) → network (architecture); weights are learned, activations are chosen.", "kt247": "## Definition\n\nNIST AI 100-1 — voluntary framework with four functions (Govern, Map, Measure, Manage) providing organizations a structured approach to AI risk management.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe US voluntary framework (1.0, January 2023) for trustworthy AI: risk framing plus seven trustworthiness characteristics plus the four-function Core — Govern (cross-cutting), Map (context), Measure (assessment), Manage (response). Sector-agnostic, rights-preserving, and the de facto US governance vocabulary.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Four functions and seven characteristics — the two lists to know cold; Govern surrounds, not precedes, the others.", "kt248": "## Definition\n\nCompanion guide to the NIST AI RMF providing practical implementation guidance, use-case examples, and templates for operationalizing the framework's four functions.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe AI RMF's companion how-to: for every subcategory of the four functions, suggested actions, informative references, and documentation guidance — turning the framework's what into implementable how. Online, evolving, and explicitly non-mandatory: organizations pick actions proportionate to their context.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Framework = the RMF itself; Playbook = the per-subcategory action guide — the pairing question is common.", "kt249": "## Definition\n\nLaws prohibiting unfair treatment based on protected characteristics — applies to AI in employment (Title VII, ADA, ADEA), credit (ECOA), housing (FHA), and insurance.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe legal regime prohibiting discrimination on protected characteristics — reaching AI through disparate treatment (intentional) and disparate impact (effect-based) doctrines under Title VII, ECOA, FHA, ADA, ADEA, and EU equality law. Algorithms discriminate at scale and through proxies, which is exactly the pattern impact doctrine was built to catch.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Facially neutral + disproportionate harm + weak business justification = disparate impact — the algorithmic fact pattern to recognize.", "kt250": "## Definition\n\nA privacy principle requiring organizations to inform individuals about what personal data is collected and how it will be used — foundational to AI transparency.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe transparency obligation: telling individuals what data is collected, why, on what basis, shared with whom, kept how long, and what rights they hold — before or at collection (GDPR Arts 13-14). AI strains notice because future model uses resist description at collection time — which doesn't excuse the obligation.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Notice informs, consent authorizes — they're distinct duties; buried or vague AI-training notice is a recurring violation pattern.", "kt251": "## Definition\n\nTwo bans added to EU AI Act Article 5 by the 2026 Digital Omnibus (points (ba) and (bb)), applying from 2 December 2026: AI that generates or manipulates realistic intimate or sexual imagery of an identifiable person without explicit consent, and AI that generates child sexual abuse material. Placing on the market is caught where that output is the intended purpose, or a foreseeable result the system has no adequate safeguards against.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe Commission's May 2026 Article 112 review flagged a gap: generators of non-consensual intimate imagery and of child sexual abuse material were not prohibited. The Omnibus closed it by adding Article 5(1)(ba) and (bb), with scoping rules in Article 5(1a)–(1b). Providers are caught where that output is the intended purpose, or a reasonably foreseeable, reproducible outcome that the system lacks reasonable safeguards to prevent; deployers are caught when they use a system for that purpose.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Eight prohibitions as enacted, ten as amended, and the two new ones start on 2 Dec 2026, not Feb 2025.", "kt252": "## Definition\n\nNew York City's automated employment decision tool (AEDT) law, in force 1 Jan 2023 and enforced by DCWP from 5 Jul 2023. Employers and employment agencies may use an AEDT on candidates or employees who live in NYC only if it had an independent bias audit within the past year, the audit summary is public, and notice was given at least 10 business days before use. Penalties run up to $500 for a first violation and $500-1,500 for each later one.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nLocal Law 144 is a transparency statute: it forces audits, publication and advance notice but does not ban biased tools or set a fairness threshold. It covers tools that substantially assist or replace discretionary hiring or promotion decisions, which DCWP's rules define narrowly. A December 2025 audit by the state Comptroller found the city's enforcement ineffective.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] There are three pre-use conditions: an audit within the past year, a public summary, and 10 business days' notice.", "kt253": "## Definition\n\nOECD.AI tool that tracks AI incidents and hazards reported in the news, automatically classifying articles against the OECD's incident and hazard definitions. Because it relies on public reporting, it captures only a subset of the incidents that actually occur.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAIM is the OECD.AI tracker of AI incidents and hazards, built from global news reporting that is automatically classified against the OECD's definitions. It gives policy makers evidence about where AI harms are appearing, but it sees only publicly reported events. The OECD's February 2025 common reporting framework for AI incidents is meant to support more consistent reporting across jurisdictions.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] AIM is compiled from media reports, so it is not a channel for organizations to file their own risk reports; that role belongs to the Hiroshima reporting framework.", "kt254": "## Definition\n\nThe first intergovernmental standard on AI (2019, updated 2024) — five principles: inclusive growth, human-centered values, transparency, robustness, and accountability.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe 2019 intergovernmental consensus (updated 2024): five values — inclusive growth and well-being; human rights and democratic values including fairness and privacy; transparency and explainability; robustness, security and safety; accountability — plus five policy recommendations. The vocabulary source for 'trustworthy AI' worldwide and the definitional anchor the EU AI Act aligned to.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Five values + five recommendations; first intergovernmental AI standard; the AI-system definition other instruments borrow.", "kt255": "## Definition\n\nDefinition adopted by the OECD Council on 8 November 2023: a machine-based system that, for explicit or implicit objectives, infers from the input it receives how to generate outputs such as predictions, content, recommendations or decisions that can influence physical or virtual environments. Systems vary in autonomy and adaptiveness after deployment. The EU AI Act's Art. 3(1) definition closely tracks it.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAdopted by the OECD Council on 8 November 2023, the update made five edits: objectives may be explicit or implicit; the system infers from its input how to generate outputs; 'content' joins the outputs, covering generative AI; 'physical' replaces 'real'; and systems may adapt after deployment. The EU AI Act's Art. 3(1) definition closely tracks the new wording, and an explanatory memorandum followed in March 2024.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Match each edit to its purpose: content means generative AI, implicit objectives means learned goals, physical is a wording clarification.", "kt256": "## Definition\n\nOECD tool (Digital Economy Papers No. 323, February 2022) for characterizing AI systems from a policy perspective along five dimensions: People & Planet, Economic Context, Data & Input, AI Model, and Task & Output. Used for registries and inventories, sector frameworks, risk assessment, incident reporting and risk management.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nPublished in February 2022 (Digital Economy Papers No. 323), the framework helps policy makers characterize an AI system in context. Its five dimensions each carry criteria: People & Planet (users, stakeholders, optionality, rights, well-being), Economic Context (sector, function, criticality, scale), Data & Input (collection, provenance, rights and identifiability, quality), AI Model (type, how built, how used) and Task & Output (tasks, action autonomy, application area). Before deployment ('in the lab') three dimensions apply; after deployment ('in the field') all five do.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Expect 'which dimension holds this criterion?' questions: action autonomy is Task & Output, opt-out and redress are People & Planet, criticality and scale are Economic Context.", "kt257": "## Definition\n\nA characteristic of AI systems requiring governance: the 'black box' nature of some AI models where it is challenging to understand how inputs are transformed into outputs.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nOpacity is the black-box condition: model internals that neither users nor, in practice, the builders can meaningfully interpret. Learned weights carry no legible logic, so there is no code to read and no rule to point at when a decision is questioned.\n\nIt is the root characteristic behind a cluster of governance requirements. **Explainability** requirements exist because opacity defeats explanation. **Oversight** design has to work around it, which is why interfaces must surface reasons and confidence rather than only a recommendation. **Contestability** rights depend on it, because a person cannot challenge a decision nobody can describe. And **auditability** has to reconstruct behaviour through testing and documentation rather than through inspection.\n\nOpacity is not binary, and the practical move is to distinguish three layers. The model may be opaque (a deep network's internals), the **system** may be opaque (many components and integrations, each understandable but the whole not), and the **organizational process** may be opaque (nobody documented why the model was chosen or what it was tested against). The third is the one a governance professional can always fix, and it is where most real opacity complaints actually originate.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Opacity is the problem; explainability, transparency and contestability are the countermeasures. If a scenario describes a decision nobody can explain, check whether the gap is model opacity or missing documentation, because the fix differs.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · explainable and interpretable as a trustworthiness characteristic\n- <a href=\"https://www.iso.org/standard/77304.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 23894 AI risk management</a> · lack of explainability as an AI risk source\n- <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.07874\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SHAP: a unified approach to interpreting model predictions</a> · post-hoc explanation techniques\n- <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.04938\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LIME: explaining the predictions of any classifier</a> · local explanations of individual outputs", "kt258": "## Definition\n\nLicense terms governing the use of open-source AI models — may include restrictions on commercial use, attribution requirements, or copyleft provisions.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe license terms governing open-source models, code, and datasets — 'open' never means unconditioned: attribution, copyleft, use restrictions (some AI licenses bar specific applications), and no-warranty clauses all bind. AI supply chains stack licenses (base model + fine-tune data + libraries), and violations are IP violations.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Open-source ≠ free-for-anything — license compliance review belongs in AI due diligence like any other rights check.", "kt259": "## Definition\n\nPerformance monitoring, drift detection, incident management\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe ISO 22989 lifecycle stage where the system earns its keep under watch: serving predictions while telemetry tracks performance, drift, fairness, and security — the longest stage of the lifecycle and the one where reality diverges from validation assumptions.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Lifecycle-stage vocabulary: deployment puts it in production; operation & monitoring keeps it honest there.", "kt260": "## Definition\n\nPolicies, procedures, and technical mechanisms governing how an AI system functions in production — including access controls, monitoring thresholds, and intervention protocols.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe running-state safeguards: access management on models and data, change management for updates, logging and audit trails, monitoring thresholds, incident procedures, and backup/recovery — the ISO 42001 Annex A territory that keeps a governed system governed day to day.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Design controls prevent; operational controls sustain — scenarios about production hygiene (access, change, logs) point here.", "kt261": "## Definition\n\nA modeling error where an AI model learns training data too precisely, including noise, and fails to generalize to new data — results in poor real-world performance.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe model that memorized instead of learned: capturing training-set noise and idiosyncrasy, acing training metrics, failing new data. Counters: more/better data, regularization, early stopping, simpler models — and honest validation on held-out data, which is how it's caught.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] High training accuracy + poor real-world performance = overfitting; poor everywhere = underfitting — the signature pattern pair.", "kt262": "## Definition\n\nInternal variable of a model that affects how the model calculates its results.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nWhat training learns: the weights and biases encoding a model's knowledge — billions of them in modern LLMs. Contrast hyperparameters (set by humans before training). Parameter counts drive capability, cost — and regulatory attention, since compute thresholds proxy for parameter-scale capability.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Parameters learned, hyperparameters configured — and 'the model IS its parameters' explains why model theft and deletion questions target the weights.", "kt263": "## Definition\n\nLegal protection for novel, useful, and non-obvious inventions. AI-generated inventions raise questions about inventorship (e.g., DABUS/Thaler v. Vidal).\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe exclusive right for inventions — novel, non-obvious, useful. AI angles: algorithms and models can be patentable subject matter (within abstract-idea limits), but inventorship must be human — AI-generated inventions without a human inventor are unpatentable under current US/EU doctrine (the DABUS line).\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Human inventor required — parallel to copyright's human-author rule; the symmetry makes both memorable.", "kt264": "## Definition\n\nPlan-Do-Check-Act — the continuous improvement model underlying ISO management system standards including ISO 42001's AI Management System.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nPlan-Do-Check-Act: the continuous-improvement loop underneath every ISO management system — plan the AIMS, run it, audit and measure it, correct and improve it, repeat. It's why certification is a cycle of surveillance audits, not a one-time trophy.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] PDCA = the improvement engine inside ISO 42001; management systems iterate by design.", "kt265": "## Definition\n\nMeasurable result. Can refer to quantitative or qualitative findings.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nHow well a system does its job, measured against defined criteria — accuracy family, latency, throughput, robustness — with metric *choice* being the governance act: optimizing precision vs. recall encodes whose errors matter. Performance also decays: drift makes yesterday's metrics fiction.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Performance is multi-dimensional and metric choice is value-laden — 'high accuracy' alone never closes a governance question.", "kt266": "## Definition\n\nAll information that can be used to identify the natural person to whom such information relates, or that is/could be directly or indirectly related to a natural person.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nGDPR's expansive trigger: any information relating to an identified or identifiable natural person — names, IDs, location, online identifiers, and combinations that single someone out. Includes inferences and pseudonymized data; excludes only the truly anonymous. If personal data is processed, the whole regime applies.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Identifiability is the test — indirect identification counts, inferences count, pseudonymized counts; the scope is deliberately wide.", "kt267": "## Definition\n\nAny information that can identify a specific individual, directly or indirectly — name, email, IP address, location data, or online identifiers.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe US-flavored cousin of personal data: information that can identify a specific individual, alone or combined — the operative concept in US breach laws and federal privacy guidance. Narrower in spirit than GDPR's 'personal data,' which sweeps in relatability, not just identifiability.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] PII (US usage) ⊂ personal data (GDPR usage) roughly — cross-jurisdiction questions trade on the width difference.", "kt268": "## Definition\n\n*\\<Artificial Intelligence>* Data processing operations that assemble a workflow from a series of actions to achieve a specific objective.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nDouble duty: the classical-AI capability of computing action sequences toward goals, and the lifecycle/management activity (ISO 42001 clause 6) of setting objectives, assessing risks, and designing the roadmap before building. Exam context usually means the latter — governance starts at planning.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Read context: 'planning' in a lifecycle question = the pre-build governance stage; in an AI-capability list = classical action planning.", "kt269": "## Definition\n\nEU AI Act requirement for ongoing surveillance of high-risk AI systems after deployment — documenting incidents, tracking performance, and reporting to authorities.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe EU AI Act's mandated afterlife-watch: high-risk providers must run documented systems collecting and analyzing performance data from deployed systems, feeding corrective action and serious-incident reporting. Borrowed from medical-device law — approval is conditional on continued good behavior.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Provider duty, mandatory for high-risk, documented plan required — 'we monitor informally' fails the standard.", "kt270": "## Definition\n\nA characteristic of AI requiring governance: AI can be used maliciously or cause unintended negative consequences through surveillance, autonomous weapons, or malfunction.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nPotential for harm is the forward-looking magnitude question in risk assessment: what damage could this system do, to whom, how severely, and how reversibly. Severity multiplied by likelihood yields risk, and the two are assessed separately precisely because they trade off differently across contexts.\n\nThe BOK lists it among the characteristics requiring comprehensive governance, and the reason is that potential harm drives proportionality everywhere else in the framework. The same technique carries different obligations depending on what it can do: a recommender ranking films and a model ranking job applicants use comparable machinery and sit at opposite ends of the control spectrum. Regulation tiers by potential, which is what the EU AI Act's Annex III is: a list of use contexts whose potential for harm was judged high enough to trigger the full obligations chain.\n\nThe judgement rule worth carrying into scenario items: **high potential harm demands controls even at low likelihood**, because severity dominates when consequences are irreversible. A rare outcome that cannot be undone is not a low risk, and an option that dismisses it on probability grounds is usually wrong.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Severity and likelihood are assessed separately, and high potential harm justifies controls even at low likelihood when consequences are irreversible. Annex III is the regulatory version of this judgement.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · contextual risk: same system, different risk by setting\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/annex/3/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Annex III (high-risk use cases)</a> · potential for harm frozen into law\n- <a href=\"https://www.iso.org/standard/77304.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 23894 AI risk management</a> · risk identification, analysis and evaluation", "kt271": "## Definition\n\nThe proportion of positive predictions that are actually correct — measures how many selected items are relevant. High precision = few false positives.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nOf everything the model flagged positive, the fraction actually positive — the metric that punishes false alarms. Traded against recall (catching everything real): fraud teams drowning in false positives need precision; cancer screening that must miss nothing needs recall.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Precision = trustworthy positives; recall = complete positives — pick per the cost of each error type in the scenario.", "kt272": "## Definition\n\nA property of an AI system that enables interested parties to make reliable assumptions about the outcome.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nISO 22989 trustworthiness property: stakeholders can reliably anticipate how the system behaves — consistent outputs for similar inputs, stable behavior across versions, no surprises. Predictability underwrites trust calibration and oversight: humans can only supervise what they can anticipate.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Trustworthiness-vocabulary item: predictability enables oversight — erratic systems defeat even well-designed human review.", "kt273": "## Definition\n\nPrimary output of an AI system given input data or information. Does **not** necessarily mean forecasting something in the future.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe basic act of inference: a model's estimate about something unknown — a class, a value, a next token, a risk score. Legally weightless until acted on: prediction becomes decision when it produces effects, which is where Article 22 and oversight duties attach.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Prediction vs. decision: governance obligations concentrate where predictions drive effects on people.", "kt274": "## Definition\n\nA core principle of responsible AI: organizations must ensure AI systems comply with privacy regulations, track all uses of PII, and provide means for inquiries and corrections.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe individual's interest in controlling information about themselves and being free from unwarranted intrusion — the value data protection law operationalizes. AI pressures it uniquely: inference reveals the uncollected, aggregation defeats context, and models remember what databases forget.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Privacy is the value; data protection is the legal machinery — and AI's inference power is the novel threat vector to name.", "kt275": "## Definition\n\nIntegrating privacy considerations at every stage of system development, from requirements through deployment, rather than bolting privacy on afterward.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nBuilding privacy into systems from conception — proactive not reactive, default-protective, embedded in architecture — codified in GDPR Article 25 (data protection by design and by default). For AI: minimization in pipelines, PETs in training, retention limits in models, and privacy review at design gates.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Article 25 makes it law, not aspiration — and 'by default' means the protective setting ships enabled.", "kt276": "## Definition\n\nNIST trustworthiness characteristic: AI system incorporates safeguards for human autonomy, identity, and dignity through data minimization, anonymization, and consent mechanisms.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe NIST trustworthiness characteristic: the system incorporates norms and techniques safeguarding autonomy and identity — minimization, PETs, de-identification — by design. NIST's framing makes privacy a property of trustworthy AI, not a compliance afterthought.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] One of the seven NIST characteristics — recognize the list membership.", "kt277": "## Definition\n\nOutputs based on probabilities rather than certainties, common in neural networks and ML models. Contrasts with deterministic systems where outputs are fully predictable.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nProbabilistic output is AI's native epistemic mode: the system produces likelihood-weighted estimates rather than certainties. An 87% fraud probability, a sampled token, a confidence-scored class label. The same input may produce different outputs across runs, and a single observed output is one draw from a distribution.\n\nThree governance consequences follow. **Thresholds are policy choices**: deciding that 0.8 is the action threshold is a decision about who bears the cost of false positives versus false negatives, and it belongs to an accountable human rather than to a default. **Error is structural**: a model operating at 97% accuracy has a 3% error rate by design, so the governance question is whether that rate is acceptable for this use and who documented the judgement. **Confidence is not correctness**: a model can be confidently wrong, which is precisely what confabulation is.\n\nThis is why AI assurance leans on statistical evaluation, confidence handling, abstention options and continuous monitoring, where conventional software assurance could rely on enumerating behaviour and testing each path.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Probabilistic does not mean unreliable, but it does mean error is structural. Plan for it with thresholds, oversight and monitoring. Any option assuming same-input-same-output contradicts this characteristic.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · valid and reliable, and measurement as the assurance route\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 600-1, Generative AI Profile</a> · confabulation as the extreme case of confident error\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · reliability and robustness vocabulary", "kt278": "## Definition\n\nA risk assessment tool that quantifies and prioritizes risks by plotting likelihood (probability) against potential impact (severity), typically on a grid.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe classic risk-visualization grid: likelihood on one axis, impact on the other, risks plotted into green/amber/red zones driving treatment priority. Simple, communicable, and honest about its ceiling — qualitative scoring invites false precision and gaming.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Standard risk-prioritization tool: position = likelihood × severity; the red corner gets treated first.", "kt279": "## Definition\n\nLegal responsibility of manufacturers and sellers for defective products that cause harm — applies to AI systems under design defect, manufacturing defect, and failure to warn theories.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nLiability for harm caused by defective products — strict (no fault required) across manufacturing defects, design defects, and failure to warn. The EU's revised Product Liability Directive drags software and AI explicitly into scope, including harm from updates and learned behavior post-sale.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Strict liability = injury + defect suffices; the EU revision explicitly covering AI/software is the current-events fact.", "kt280": "## Definition\n\nThe EU's revised no-fault product liability law, replacing Directive 85/374/EEC. Software, including AI systems, is a product however supplied; free and open-source software supplied outside commercial activity is excluded. It adds court-ordered disclosure, rebuttable presumptions of defect and causation, and compensation for medically recognised psychological harm and loss of non-professional data. Transposition by 9 Dec 2026; applies to products placed on the market after that date.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAdopted on 23 October 2024 and in force since 8 December 2024, the Directive is maximum harmonisation (Article 3). Defectiveness now takes account of a product's ability to keep learning, cybersecurity requirements and the moment it leaves the manufacturer's control (Article 7), and defects caused by software updates, or missing updates needed for safety, within the manufacturer's control cannot be excused as arising after sale (Article 11(2)). Claims expire after 10 years, or 25 years for latent personal injury, and anyone who substantially modifies a product outside the manufacturer's control becomes its manufacturer.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] PLD means no-fault liability, software counts as a product, and presumptions help claimants; the fault-based AI Liability Directive was withdrawn.", "kt281": "## Definition\n\nData collected during the operational phase of an AI system for which a deployed AI system calculates a predicted result or inference.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe live data a deployed system processes — real users, real stakes, real legal obligations, unlike curated training corpora. Two disciplines attach: monitoring it for drift from training assumptions, and governing any reuse of it for retraining (fresh purpose, fresh rights analysis).\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Using production data to retrain is a new processing purpose — the rights/compatibility analysis re-runs; it's not automatically permitted.", "kt282": "## Definition\n\nStructured pre-deployment review that an AI system is production-worthy: safety, accountability, suitability for intended context, regulatory conformity, performance benchmarking, and operational planning.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe pre-launch verdict across every readiness axis: documentation complete, conformity obligations met, stress and adversarial testing passed, monitoring wired, rollback planned, approvals signed. Its output is a governed go/no-go decision with named owners — the audit trail proving deployment was a choice, not a drift.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Readiness is technical AND governance — a scenario passing performance tests but missing signoffs or rollback plans is not ready.", "kt283": "## Definition\n\nEU AI Act category: AI practices banned entirely — includes social scoring, real-time remote biometric identification in public spaces (with narrow exceptions), and subliminal manipulation.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe AI Act's banned list (Article 5, effective February 2025): social scoring by public authorities, exploitation of vulnerabilities causing harm, subliminal manipulation, untargeted facial-image scraping, emotion recognition in workplaces and schools, biometric categorization inferring protected traits, and real-time remote biometric identification in public spaces — with narrow, warrant-like law-enforcement exceptions.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Memorize the list — 'spot the prohibited practice' is a near-guaranteed question, and the law-enforcement carve-outs are the nuance layer.", "kt284": "## Definition\n\nCrafting and iterating on the instructions, context and examples sent to a generative model, including system prompts and few-shot examples, to steer it toward more useful, accurate and safe output. It changes no model weights, unlike fine-tuning, and adds no external knowledge source, unlike retrieval augmented generation.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nCommon techniques are clear role and task instructions, system prompts that set rules and refusal behaviour, few-shot examples, output templates and step-by-step instructions. Because it changes no weights and adds no data source it is usually the lightest-weight way to improve a deployed model's fit, but it is also brittle: outputs can shift with small wording changes or model updates, and prompt-level guardrails can be bypassed by prompt injection. BOK IV.A expects candidates to compare using a model as is with fine-tuning, RAG, agentic designs and other ways of improving fit.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] No weight changes and no external knowledge source means prompt engineering; weight changes mean fine-tuning; documents retrieved at query time mean RAG.", "kt285": "## Definition\n\nAn attack where malicious input manipulates an AI model's behavior by overriding system instructions or injecting harmful content through the user prompt.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nPrompt injection is the language-model-native attack: adversarial instructions smuggled through inputs, hijacking the model's behaviour, exfiltrating data or bypassing safety filters. It has two forms, and the distinction is what the exam tests.\n\n**Direct injection** is a user typing instructions that override the system's own. **Indirect injection** is instructions hidden in content the model ingests: a poisoned webpage, a document, an email, a calendar invite, a retrieved chunk in a RAG pipeline. The indirect variant is the dangerous one, because the user never sees it, the operator may not know the content source is untrusted, and in an agentic system the model holds tool credentials that the injected instruction can direct.\n\nThere is no complete fix, which is itself the governance point. Defenses are layered and partial: treat model input as untrusted data rather than instructions, separate privileged operations from content ingestion, filter and sanitize inputs, constrain what tools an agent can reach and with what privileges, monitor outputs for anomalous actions, and require human confirmation for irreversible steps. OWASP lists prompt injection at the top of its LLM application risks, and MITRE ATLAS catalogs the technique alongside conventional adversarial ML attacks.\n\nThe governance consequence: because mitigation is incomplete, the control is containment and detection, plus an incident playbook that treats a successful injection as a security incident rather than a model quality issue.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Direct injection is user-typed; indirect injection hides in retrieved or ingested content. Indirect is what makes RAG and agent systems risky. Because it cannot be fully prevented, the right controls are least-privilege tool access, human confirmation for irreversible actions, and monitoring.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://genai.owasp.org/llm-top-10/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications</a> · prompt injection as the leading LLM application risk\n- <a href=\"https://atlas.mitre.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MITRE ATLAS</a> · adversarial techniques against AI systems\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-2e2023.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 100-2, Adversarial Machine Learning taxonomy</a> · abuse and evasion categories\n- <a href=\"https://www.cisa.gov/ai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CISA artificial intelligence resources</a> · secure development guidance for AI systems", "kt286": "## Definition\n\nThe heightened duties that come with deploying a proprietary third-party model: increased contractual obligations, reliance on vendor claims that cannot be independently verified, potential for higher liability exposure, and dependence on the vendor for updates, incident support, and continuity.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nWhat changes when the model you deploy is someone else's black box: you carry deployment obligations and liability for behavior you cannot inspect, you depend on vendor claims you cannot independently verify, and your continuity hangs on their update and support commitments. The BOK flags this as its own IV.B assessment item — compensating controls are contractual (warranties, audit rights, SLAs) and empirical (independent testing).\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Deploying proprietary AI heightens rather than reduces your obligations — 'the vendor is responsible' is the planted wrong answer.", "kt287": "## Definition\n\nA core deployment-evaluation choice: proprietary models offer vendor support and cutting-edge capability but limited visibility into training data and terms-of-service constraints; open-source models offer transparency, control, and customization but shift security, hosting, and compliance burden onto the deployer. License terms bind in both cases.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe control-versus-support axis of model selection: proprietary buys vendor accountability, managed updates, and often frontier capability at the price of opacity and contractual dependence; open-source buys transparency, customization, and cost control at the price of inheriting the security, hosting, and compliance burden yourself. The BOK names this axis explicitly as a deployment-evaluation factor.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Neither end is 'safer' in general — auditability needs favor open-source, accountability-through-contract needs favor proprietary; context decides.", "kt288": "## Definition\n\nProcessing personal data so it can no longer be attributed to a specific data subject without additional information kept separately — GDPR encourages as a safeguard.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nReplacing identifiers with pseudonyms while a re-linking key exists somewhere — GDPR names it as a security measure, and pseudonymized data remains fully personal data. Useful risk reduction, routinely mistaken for anonymization, which requires irreversibility.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Pseudonymized = still personal data, GDPR applies; anonymized = out of scope — the single most-tested privacy distinction.", "kt289": "## Definition\n\nTransparency obligation to publish information about AI system capabilities, limitations, training data, evaluation results, and intended use — supports accountability.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nMaking information about AI systems public — transparency reports, incident announcements, registry entries (the EU database for high-risk systems), and legally compelled notices. Timing and accuracy are governed: premature disclosure can mislead, late disclosure compounds harm, false disclosure is its own violation.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Some disclosure is mandatory (EU database registration, breach notices), some strategic (transparency reports) — sort by obligation first.", "kt290": "## Definition\n\nA GDPR principle requiring personal data be collected for specified, explicit, and legitimate purposes and not further processed in an incompatible manner.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nData collected for specified purposes may not be processed incompatibly with them — the GDPR principle AI most reliably strains, since training is rarely the collection purpose. The path forward: compatibility analysis, or a fresh lawful basis with fresh transparency — never silent repurposing.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] 'We had the data anyway, so we trained on it' is the violation pattern — repurposing demands analysis or a new basis.", "kt291": "## Definition\n\nA formalized system documenting processes, procedures, and responsibilities for ensuring AI system quality and compliance throughout the lifecycle.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe documented organizational machine ensuring consistent quality — processes, roles, records, improvement loops (ISO 9001 lineage). The AI Act requires high-risk providers to run one covering design, development, testing, and post-market phases; ISO 42001's AIMS deliberately docks with it.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] QMS is an explicit EU AI Act high-risk provider requirement — not just an ISO nicety.", "kt292": "## Definition\n\nTask of determining the most appropriate answer to a question posed in natural language.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe NLP task of answering natural-language questions — extractive (find the answer in text) or generative (compose it). Modern QA rides LLMs and RAG, inheriting confabulation risk: fluent answers with fabricated content, dangerous exactly in proportion to user trust.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] ISO 22989 task vocabulary — with hallucination as the attached governance risk in generative form.", "kt293": "## Definition\n\nA responsibility assignment framework: Responsible (does the work), Accountable (owns the outcome), Consulted (provides input), Informed (kept updated).\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nRACI is the accountability grid: for each activity it names who is **Responsible** (does the work), **Accountable** (answers for the outcome, exactly one person), **Consulted** (gives input before the decision) and **Informed** (told after it).\n\nIn AI governance it kills the ambiguity that produces most governance failures. \"Someone monitors the model\" is not an assignment; \"the AI Ops lead is accountable for drift monitoring, the risk officer is consulted on thresholds, the product owner is informed monthly\" is. Applied per governance task rather than per department, RACI forces two things org charts hide: exactly one accountable owner for each task, and an explicit list of who must be consulted before the decision is made.\n\nTwo rules carry most of the value. **Exactly one accountable per activity**: diffuse accountability means no accountability, and a task with two accountable owners has none. **Consulted is not optional**: the consulted party is the one whose perspective the framework says must be in the room, so skipping consultation is how a decision gets made without the function that would have caught the problem.\n\nThe matrix should also be documented alongside the role definition it belongs to, with mission, decision rights, escalation procedure and performance metrics, so that a role's authority is as explicit as its duties.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Exactly one Accountable per activity; diffuse accountability is the failure RACI exists to prevent. When a scenario has a governance task with no named owner, the missing element is the accountable party, not the process.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF Playbook, Govern function</a> · GV1 on roles, responsibilities and accountability\n- <a href=\"https://www.iso.org/standard/42001\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 42001 (AI management system)</a> · roles, responsibilities and authorities\n- <a href=\"https://iapp.org/resources/article/key-terms-for-ai-governance/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IAPP Key Terms for AI Governance</a> · governance role terminology", "kt294": "## Definition\n\nNew York's Responsible AI Safety and Education Act, signed December 2025 and re-enacted by a March 2026 chapter amendment modelled on California SB 53; effective 1 Jan 2027. It uses the same 10^26-operation and $500M thresholds. Frontier developers report critical safety incidents to a new office within the Department of Financial Services within 72 hours, and large developers publish frameworks and file disclosure statements. The AG can seek up to $1M for a first violation and $3M for later ones; there is no private right of action.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nNew York's frontier-model law was substantially rewritten before taking effect: the 2026 chapter amendment repealed the December 2025 text and re-enacted it to track California's SB 53 more closely. It keeps New York-specific features: a dedicated oversight office in the Department of Financial Services, disclosure statements with cost assessments, a 72-hour incident deadline and higher penalties for repeat violations.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] RAISE is 72 hours to the DFS office and $1M/$3M; SB 53 is 15 days to Cal OES and $1M.", "kt295": "## Definition\n\nAI systems that respond to specific stimuli without memory or learning capability. They operate on pre-programmed rules. Example: IBM Deep Blue.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nReactive machines are the ground floor of the functionality ladder: systems that respond to current input with no memory of past inputs and no learning from experience. IBM's Deep Blue, evaluating chess positions without recalling earlier games, is the canonical example.\n\nTheir governance profile is the friendliest on the ladder. They are deterministic, so behaviour can be enumerated and tested; auditable, because the evaluation function can be inspected; and bounded, because the system cannot drift or develop behaviour after deployment. They are also limited, because anything requiring context or adaptation is out of reach.\n\nTheir exam value is as the contrast case. When a question asks why AI needs governance that ordinary software does not, the ladder runs reactive (governable like software) to limited memory (stateful, monitorable) to theory of mind and self-aware (theoretical). Placing an example on that ladder is a recurring question format, and Deep Blue is the example to know.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Deep Blue is the canonical reactive machine, and 'no memory' is the defining feature. Example-matching across the ladder is the question format: reactive, limited memory, theory of mind, self-aware.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · system capability vocabulary\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · contextual risk: the same technique carries different risk in different settings", "kt296": "## Definition\n\nAI systems with advanced pattern analysis and prediction capabilities. Most current LLMs fall in this category, capable of passing standardized tests.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nReasoning AI describes systems that chain inference steps, analysing patterns and drawing conclusions across multiple stages rather than emitting a single reactive judgement. The category spans symbolic logic engines at one end and modern language models that work through multi-step problems at the other.\n\nIt sits on the functionality ladder as the tier above limited memory, and it is where the explainability question gets harder rather than easier. A multi-step chain is more inspectable than a single opaque score, which is why step-by-step output is attractive for oversight. But a plausible chain is not necessarily the chain that produced the answer, so a reasoning trace can be an explanation-shaped artifact rather than a faithful one. That is the same fidelity problem interpretability testing exists to catch.\n\nFor governance, the practical consequence is that reasoning output should be treated as evidence requiring verification in consequential uses, not as proof of correctness.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Functionality-ladder vocabulary: multi-step inference above limited memory. If a stem presents a step-by-step rationale as sufficient justification for a consequential decision, the missing element is verification of whether the rationale is faithful.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · reasoning and inference terminology\n- <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.04938\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LIME: explaining the predictions of any classifier</a> · fidelity of explanations to actual model behaviour\n- <a href=\"https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/artificial-intelligence/explaining-decisions-made-with-artificial-intelligence/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ICO: explaining decisions made with AI</a> · what a meaningful explanation requires", "kt297": "## Definition\n\nPeriodic review of continued fitness for purpose\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe scheduled or triggered re-run of governance evaluations — risk assessments, impact assessments, validations — because their conclusions age: models drift, contexts shift, regulations land, incidents teach. ISO 22989's lifecycle includes re-evaluation as a formal stage; mature programs predefine reassessment triggers.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Assessments are living documents — material change (system, data, context, law) reopens them; annual-at-minimum is the default pattern.", "kt298": "## Definition\n\nThe proportion of actual positives correctly identified — measures how many relevant items are selected. High recall = few false negatives.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nOf everything actually positive, the fraction the model caught — the metric that punishes misses. The precision trade-off is governance in miniature: screening for cancer maximizes recall (miss nothing), spam filtering leans precision (flag carefully).\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Recall = completeness of catch; the cost of a miss vs. the cost of a false alarm decides which metric leads.", "kt299": "## Definition\n\nThe obligation for controllers and processors to maintain records of processing activities, including purposes, data categories, recipients, and retention periods.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe evidentiary discipline: contemporaneous, retrievable records of decisions, tests, approvals, incidents, and system events — with the AI Act adding automatic logging requirements for high-risk systems and 10-year documentation retention for providers. Records are how governance survives contact with auditors, regulators, and courts.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] High-risk systems must log automatically; providers keep technical documentation 10 years — the two AI Act record numbers.", "kt300": "## Definition\n\nA neural network in which the outputs of both the previous layer and the previous processing step are fed into the current layer.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAn RNN is the sequence architecture: outputs loop back as inputs, giving the network a memory across time steps. That makes it suited to language, speech and time series, where the order and context of inputs carry meaning.\n\nPlain RNNs suffer from vanishing gradients, which limits how far back the memory usefully reaches; LSTMs and gated variants patched that, and transformers ultimately displaced the family for language by removing recurrence altogether in favour of attention over the whole sequence.\n\nGovernance relevance is twofold. Sequence models are **stateful**, so an output depends on the input history, which complicates reproduction, explanation and audit: you need the sequence, not just the triggering input. And RNNs appear in reinforcement learning as the policy network that gives an agent memory of its trajectory, which is where the autonomy questions start. Architecture-recognition questions pair RNN with sequences, CNN with images and transformer with modern language.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] RNN means sequences and memory; CNN means images; transformer means modern language. Statefulness is the property that makes an RNN's outputs harder to reproduce and explain than a stateless model's.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · recurrent network terminology\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · traceability and documentation expectations", "kt301": "## Definition\n\nAdversarial testing where a team simulates attacks or misuse scenarios to uncover vulnerabilities that standard testing might miss — critical for AI safety evaluation.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nStructured adversarial exercise: dedicated attackers probing a system for failures before real adversaries and users find them — jailbreaks, injections, bias elicitation, unsafe outputs. Now institutionalized: NIST ARIA's middle evaluation level, an EU AI Act expectation for systemic-risk GPAI, and a pre-release readiness staple.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Red teaming = offensive testing by design — required territory for systemic-risk GPAI and the canonical pre-deployment adversarial control.", "kt302": "## Definition\n\nLearning an optimal sequence of actions to maximize a reward through interaction with an environment.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nReinforcement learning is learning by consequence: an agent acts in an environment, receives rewards and penalties, and iteratively improves the policy that maps situations to actions. There are no labelled answers, only feedback, which is what distinguishes it from supervised learning, and there is no structure discovery for its own sake, which is what distinguishes it from unsupervised learning.\n\nIt produced milestone results in games and robotics, and in the form of reinforcement learning from human feedback it is how modern language models are tuned to be useful and to refuse harmful requests. That makes RLHF a governance-relevant training stage rather than a technical detail: the reward model encodes human preferences, so whose preferences were used, and how consistently, becomes part of the fairness and alignment record.\n\nIts characteristic failure is **reward misspecification**: the agent optimises the objective it was given, which may be a proxy for the outcome anyone wanted. An agent confidently pursuing the wrong goal at scale is the archetypal misalignment scenario, and the control is careful objective design plus evaluation against intent rather than against the metric.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Trial-and-error with reward signals: that is RL. It is distinct from supervised (labels) and unsupervised (structure). Reward design is where it goes wrong, so 'the agent optimised the wrong metric' is the misalignment answer.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · the formal reinforcement learning definition\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 600-1, Generative AI Profile</a> · human-AI configuration and value-chain risk\n- <a href=\"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF Playbook</a> · Map and Measure actions for objective and outcome alignment", "kt303": "## Definition\n\nA training technique in which people compare or rank a model's outputs, a reward model is trained on those preferences, and reinforcement learning then tunes the model toward the responses people prefer; a common way chat-style LLMs are steered toward helpful, harmless answers. Also called reinforcement learning from human feedback.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nRLHF usually runs in stages: supervised fine-tuning on answers written by people, collection of human rankings of alternative outputs, training a reward model on those rankings, then reinforcement learning to maximize the reward. InstructGPT (Ouyang et al., 2022) showed that a 1.3-billion-parameter model tuned this way was preferred by human evaluators over the 175-billion-parameter GPT-3. Governance questions include who the raters are, what guidelines they follow, and the risk that the model learns what raters like rather than what is true.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Human rankings of outputs feeding a reward model point to RLHF; human-written example answers alone point to supervised fine-tuning.", "kt304": "## Definition\n\nProperty of the correspondence between intended behavior and the results.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nISO-flavored trustworthiness: the system performs its intended function consistently under stated conditions — the steady-state cousin of robustness (which covers adverse and unexpected conditions). Reliability failures are availability and correctness failures users experience directly.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Reliability = consistent intended behavior (normal conditions); robustness = holds up under stress (abnormal conditions) — ISO splits them.", "kt305": "## Definition\n\nThe ability of a system to quickly recover its operational state following an incident.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe bounce-back property: withstand disruption, degrade gracefully, recover fast — spanning infrastructure failover, model fallbacks, and attack recovery. NIST bundles it with security ('secure and resilient'): a trustworthy system survives both adversaries and accidents.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Resilience is about recovery and graceful degradation — distinct from robustness (resisting failure in the first place).", "kt306": "## Definition\n\nA framework ensuring AI systems are designed, developed, and deployed to respect human rights, promote fairness, and minimize risks while benefiting individuals and society.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe umbrella practice of developing and deploying AI ethically, legally, and safely — the principle set (fairness, accountability, transparency, human-centricity, robustness, privacy) plus the organizational machinery that enforces it. Effectively synonymous with 'trustworthy AI' (NIST) and 'ethical AI' in most usage.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Treat responsible/trustworthy/ethical AI as one concept family — the principles underneath are what get tested.", "kt307": "## Definition\n\nUpdating a trained model through training with other training data.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nUpdating a model with new data to counter drift, add capability, or fix defects — a governed event, not a background process: retraining triggers defined in advance, new versions validated like new models, regression-tested against prior behavior, and documented for the audit trail.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Monitoring detects degradation → investigate root cause → retrain if indicated → validate before release — retraining without diagnosis or validation is the planted error.", "kt308": "## Definition\n\nA technique where an AI model retrieves relevant external data at query time to augment its responses, improving accuracy and reducing hallucinations.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nGrounding generation in retrieval: the system fetches relevant documents from a knowledge store and conditions the LLM's answer on them — reducing (not eliminating) confabulation, enabling citations, and keeping knowledge current without retraining. New risk surface: retrieved content can carry indirect prompt injections.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] RAG = the standard hallucination mitigation — grounding in retrieved sources; but retrieval opens the indirect-injection door.", "kt309": "## Definition\n\nGDPR Article 17: the right of individuals to have their personal data deleted in certain circumstances — raises 'machine unlearning' challenges for AI systems.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nGDPR Article 17's 'right to be forgotten': individuals can demand deletion when data is no longer necessary, consent is withdrawn, or processing was unlawful — with exceptions (legal obligations, public interest, claims). The AI frontier: what erasure means for models trained on the data, where retraining and machine unlearning are the live options.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Not absolute (exceptions exist) — and the model-trained-on-deleted-data question is the AI-specific twist to expect.", "kt310": "## Definition\n\nThe effect of uncertainty on objectives. Deviation can be positive, negative, or both. Characterized by risk drivers, potential events, consequences, and likelihood.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe governing abstraction: effect of uncertainty on objectives, operationalized as likelihood × severity of harm. Every framework tiers by it — the AI Act's ladder, the RMF's mapping, ISO's registers — because governance resources are finite and risk is how they're rationed.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Risk = likelihood × impact; inherent (before controls) vs. residual (after) — the vocabulary every scenario assumes.", "kt311": "## Definition\n\nAI systems capable of performing kinetic physical tasks such as carrying objects, moving, and more advanced tasks like cooking.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nRobotics is AI with a body: systems performing kinetic tasks in physical space, from manufacturing arms and warehouse robots to surgical assistants and autonomous vehicles. Embodiment raises the stakes categorically, because a software error becomes a physical injury and the harm is often irreversible.\n\nThat shifts which legal regimes apply. An AI-enabled physical product routes through both its sectoral product-safety law (machinery, medical devices, vehicles) and the EU AI Act, where a system that is a safety component of a product covered by EU harmonisation legislation is **high-risk by Annex I**, triggering the full obligations chain. Product liability also engages directly: design defects, manufacturing defects and failure to warn, and in the EU the revised Product Liability Directive now treats software, including AI systems, as a product.\n\nGovernance practice follows the safety-engineering tradition rather than the data-science one: hazard analysis, fail-safe and fail-operational design, human override, and monitoring for model drift that manifests as equipment failure rather than as a metric decline.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Physical embodiment means safety-critical analysis plus product law: AI inside a regulated product routes through both sectoral regulation and the AI Act, and Annex I is the high-risk route that captures it.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/6/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 6 and Annex I (high-risk classification via product safety law)</a> · the safety-component route to high-risk\n- <a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/dir/2024/2853/oj\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Revised Product Liability Directive (EU) 2024/2853</a> · software and AI as products, with eased proof\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · robotics and embodied system terminology", "kt312": "## Definition\n\nThe ability of a system to maintain its performance level under **all** circumstances.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nHolding up under the unexpected: maintaining performance on edge cases, distribution shifts, noisy inputs, and adversarial pressure — tested through stress testing, edge-case suites, and adversarial evaluation. OECD and NIST both enshrine it; brittleness is its failure mode.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Robustness = performance under adverse/unusual conditions; reliability = consistency under normal ones — the tested pair.", "kt313": "## Definition\n\nNIST trustworthiness characteristic: AI system does not, under defined conditions, lead to a state in which human life, health, property, or the environment is endangered.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe NIST trustworthiness characteristic: the system does not, under defined conditions, endanger human life, health, property, or environment — engineered through safe design, testing against failure modes, and the ability to halt. Safety is the characteristic where AI governance meets classic product safety.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] One of NIST's seven — and safety-critical scenarios (health, vehicles, infrastructure) elevate it to the controlling consideration.", "kt314": "## Definition\n\nGPAI Code of Practice chapter applying ONLY to systemic-risk GPAI providers: safety and security framework, risk identification and analysis, mitigations, serious-incident reporting, and cybersecurity.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe GPAI Code of Practice chapter binding only systemic-risk model providers: a safety and security framework, state-of-the-art model evaluations, systemic risk identification/analysis/mitigation, serious-incident reporting, and cybersecurity protections for model weights and infrastructure — operationalizing AI Act Article 55.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Only systemic-risk GPAI providers — the Transparency and Copyright chapters bind everyone; this one doesn't.", "kt315": "## Definition\n\nAn atomic data element that is processed in batches by a machine learning algorithm or an AI system.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nA sample is a subset drawn from a population for training or evaluation. It is the statistical unit whose selection determines whether anything learned generalises, which makes sampling method a governance-relevant design decision rather than a technical detail.\n\nThe methods matter because each carries a bias profile. **Random sampling** assumes every member of the population had an equal chance of selection, which fails when the frame itself is incomplete. **Stratified sampling** deliberately represents subgroups, which is the approach fairness work pushes toward. **Convenience sampling** (whatever data was available, often web scrapes or existing customers) is the most common in practice and the most likely to encode who was reachable rather than who is affected.\n\nThe governance question that follows is always the same: does this sample look like the population the system will act on? A model trained on one region, age band or language deployed across another is the canonical representativeness failure, and the flaw traces to sampling before it traces to architecture. When a model underperforms for a subgroup, interrogate the sampling frame first.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] When a model fails for a subgroup, look at the sampling frame before the algorithm: biased selection produces biased models regardless of downstream care. Stratified sampling is the fairness-positive method.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.09010\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Datasheets for Datasets</a> · documenting how a dataset was sampled and why\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/SpecialPublications/NIST.SP.1270.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST SP 1270, bias in AI</a> · systemic bias originating in data collection\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · dataset and training data terminology", "kt316": "## Definition\n\nCJEU judgment of 7 December 2023: a credit bureau's automated score is itself an automated decision under GDPR Article 22 when a lender that receives it draws strongly on it to enter into, perform or end a contract. Upstream score providers can therefore owe Article 22 and Article 15(1)(h) duties.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nA German court referred the case of SCHUFA, whose probability scores lenders relied on. The CJEU held that the score itself can be the Article 22 decision when the lender draws strongly on it, closing a gap in which bureau and lender could each disclaim responsibility. The bureau then needs an Article 22(2) exception and must provide the safeguards and the access information. In AI supply chains, any model output that effectively decides for the customer should be analysed the same way.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] 'Solely automated' can sit upstream: the score-maker, not only the lender, can be the Article 22 decision-maker.", "kt317": "## Definition\n\nThe use of an AI system or its outputs for purposes beyond the original intended use case — creates unanticipated risks requiring governance oversight.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nProcessing data for a purpose other than the one it was collected for — AI training being the canonical modern example. GDPR requires a compatibility analysis (link between purposes, context, data nature, consequences, safeguards) or a fresh lawful basis plus transparency — silent repurposing is the violation.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Data on hand ≠ data available for training — the compatibility-or-new-basis analysis is the required step.", "kt318": "## Definition\n\nNIST trustworthiness characteristic: AI system can withstand adverse events and recover to normal function while maintaining confidentiality, integrity, and availability.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe NIST trustworthiness characteristic pairing protection with recovery: withstand unauthorized access and adversarial attack (secure), and maintain function or degrade gracefully through disruption (resilient) — covering the ML-specific attack surface from poisoning to model theft.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] NIST bundles the pair as one characteristic of the seven — security keeps attackers out, resilience survives what gets through.", "kt319": "## Definition\n\nA core principle of responsible AI: protecting sensitive data (PII, IP, copyrighted content) used by AI systems from unauthorized access, compromise, and theft.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nProtecting AI assets — data, models, pipelines, endpoints — against the classic triad threats plus ML-native attacks: data poisoning, model inversion, membership inference, adversarial examples, prompt injection, model theft. The AI Act makes cybersecurity an explicit high-risk requirement; MITRE ATLAS catalogs the threat landscape.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] AI security = traditional security + the ML attack family — answers covering only conventional controls are incomplete.", "kt320": "## Definition\n\nEvaluating AI system resistance to attacks — includes adversarial example testing, prompt injection testing, model extraction attempts, and API security validation.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nProbing AI systems for exploitable weaknesses — conventional (access, injection, infrastructure) plus ML-specific: can training data be poisoned, can the model be inverted or stolen, do adversarial inputs flip outputs, do prompts jailbreak it. Red teaming is its adversarial, exercise-style form.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Match attack to test: poisoning (training-time), inversion/membership (privacy), adversarial examples (inference-time), injection (LLM) — coverage questions expect the full family.", "kt321": "## Definition\n\nThe highest theoretical level of AI possessing consciousness and self-awareness. Would recognize its own existence. Currently does not exist.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nSelf-aware AI is the functionality ladder's theoretical summit: systems with consciousness and knowledge of their own internal states. There is no engineering path to it and no deployed instance.\n\nIts role in a governance syllabus is as a boundary marker. The ladder runs reactive machines (no memory), limited memory (context retained), theory of mind (models other minds, theoretical) and self-aware (conscious, theoretical). Knowing where the ladder ends is how you avoid misclassifying real systems and how you recognise anthropomorphic framing when it appears in a scenario.\n\nThat anthropomorphism is the practical hazard. Language models produce first-person statements about their own states and can simulate introspection convincingly, but simulated self-description is generated text, not evidence of self-awareness. Governance responses that treat a system's self-report as reliable testimony about its own behaviour are being misled by the same plausibility mechanism that produces confabulation.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Theoretical, like theory-of-mind AI above limited memory. Ladder-placement questions expect 'does not exist', and a system's own first-person statements are not evidence of self-awareness.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · capability tier vocabulary\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 600-1, Generative AI Profile</a> · human-AI configuration and over-trust", "kt322": "## Definition\n\nA training method using a small amount of labeled data combined with a large amount of unlabeled data. The AI learns from labeled examples then predicts labels for the rest.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nSemi-supervised learning trains on a small labelled set plus a large unlabelled one. The labelled data anchors the task, and the unlabelled mass refines the representation the model learns. The economics are the point: labels are expensive and slow to produce, raw data is abundant, so semi-supervision buys accuracy without the full labelling bill.\n\nIt sits between supervised and unsupervised learning in the paradigm taxonomy, and it is the practical reality of most modern AI development: a modest curated set of examples to define the task, then scale on data without labels. Self-supervised learning, which derives its own labels from the structure of the data and underpins modern language models, is the logical endpoint of the same logic.\n\nGovernance relevance is that the unlabelled portion is usually the least documented and least reviewed part of the training set. It determines representation and therefore fairness, but it typically arrives without provenance records. A dataset documentation practice that covers only the curated labelled subset leaves the largest input to the model unexamined.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Small labelled plus large unlabelled equals semi-supervised, positioned between supervised and unsupervised. The governance gap to look for is undocumented provenance on the unlabelled majority.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · the formal paradigm definitions\n- <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.09010\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Datasheets for Datasets</a> · documenting composition and collection for each portion", "kt323": "## Definition\n\nMachine learning in which both labeled and unlabeled data are used during training.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe ISO/IEC 22989 formal name for the same paradigm as semi-supervised learning: machine learning that uses both labelled and unlabelled data during training. It is dual-listed here because standards documents and exam text use the full term, and recognising both phrasings as one paradigm is a small but reliable point.\n\nThe substantive content is identical. A small labelled set defines the task; a large unlabelled set improves the learned representation; the model generalises better than it would from the labelled data alone. The governance implications are the same too: the unlabelled majority is usually the least documented input, so dataset documentation has to cover both portions rather than only the curated one.\n\nKeeping the vocabulary straight matters more than it sounds. Standards-based questions and ISO-anchored scenario items expect you to map the formal term onto the concept you already know, and an option that treats semi-supervised machine learning and semi-supervised learning as different techniques is testing whether you can.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Same paradigm, two phrasings: semi-supervised learning and semi-supervised machine learning are one concept. Do not let a formal-sounding option convince you they differ.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · the formal term as defined in the standard", "kt324": "## Definition\n\nData requiring heightened protection due to potential for harm if misused — includes special category data plus financial, location, and children's data depending on jurisdiction.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nSensitive data is the umbrella for information whose misuse causes elevated harm: GDPR special categories (racial or ethnic origin, political opinions, religious beliefs, trade-union membership, genetic data, biometric data used for identification, health, sex life or orientation), plus financial data, children's data, precise location and other categories under sectoral or state law.\n\nAI's twist is **inference**. A model can derive a special category from ordinary inputs: purchase history implying pregnancy, typing patterns implying a health condition, browsing patterns implying political affiliation. Under GDPR the derived attribute is still special-category processing, so an organization that never collected health data can nonetheless be processing it, and needs an Article 9 condition it may not have.\n\nSensitive attributes also cut both ways in fairness work. They are dangerous as model features, because using them directly discriminates. They are necessary for testing, because you cannot measure disparate impact without knowing who is in which group. The mature practice is governed access: hold the attributes for evaluation under strict controls rather than deleting them and losing the ability to test.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Inference creates sensitive data too. A model deriving health status from shopping behaviour is processing special-category data, so Article 9 applies even though nothing sensitive was collected. Governed access beats attribute deletion for fairness testing.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://gdpr-info.eu/art-9-gdpr/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GDPR, Article 9 (special categories)</a> · the special-category list and conditions\n- <a href=\"https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2024-12/edpb_opinion_202428_ai-models_en.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EDPB Opinion 28/2024 on AI models and personal data</a> · inference, anonymity and special categories in model training\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/SpecialPublications/NIST.SP.1270.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST SP 1270, bias in AI</a> · sensitive attributes in bias measurement", "kt325": "## Definition\n\nTask to computationally identify and categorize opinions expressed in text, speech, or image to determine a range of sentiments (e.g., positive to negative).\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe NLP task of classifying emotional tone — positive/negative/neutral, or finer emotional states — powering brand monitoring, feedback triage, and market analysis. Governance shadow: sentiment inference about individuals edges toward emotion recognition, which the AI Act prohibits in workplaces and schools.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Aggregate sentiment on reviews is minimal-risk; emotion inference about individuals at work or school crosses into prohibited territory.", "kt326": "## Definition\n\nUse of AI tools or systems inside an organization without approval, or outside the view of its governance process, such as staff putting company data into a personal chatbot account; it usually breaks the organization's rules on acceptable AI use, confidentiality or personal data. Managed through discovery, an AI inventory, an acceptable use policy and approved alternatives.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nShadow AI is AI use that the organization has not approved, does not know about, or cannot see: staff reaching for a free chatbot, a browser plug-in, a meeting transcriber, a code assistant with an unvetted licence. It is usually well-meant, adopted because it saves time and the approved path does not exist or is slow.\n\nThe consequences are specific and cumulative. **Data leaves the perimeter**: prompts containing customer records, source code, contract terms or personal data go to a vendor whose terms nobody reviewed. **Processing is unlawful or undocumented**: personal data enters a system with no lawful basis, no record of processing and no data protection assessment. **Outputs feed real decisions** without validation, so a generated summary or analysis becomes an input to something consequential. And **IP and confidentiality exposure** is often the first thing noticed, typically by a client or a regulator rather than internally.\n\nGovernance responses have to be discovery-led, because you cannot manage what you cannot see: network and expense monitoring, staff surveys, an AI inventory, and a clear acceptable-use policy naming what data may enter a prompt. Critically, a blanket ban without an approved alternative reliably pushes use further out of sight, which is worse than the exposure it was meant to prevent. The mature pattern is approved tooling plus training plus an easy route to get something new approved.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] If a scenario describes AI use the governance team never approved or knew about, the concept is shadow AI, and the best first step is discovery and inventory rather than an outright ban. Banning without an approved path increases the problem.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF Playbook, Govern function</a> · policies, acceptable use and inventory actions\n- <a href=\"https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/artificial-intelligence/guidance-on-ai-and-data-protection/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ICO guidance on AI and data protection</a> · accountability and lawful basis for AI processing\n- <a href=\"https://www.iso.org/standard/42001\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 42001 (AI management system)</a> · Annex A controls for AI use and third-party services", "kt327": "## Definition\n\nA deployment-evaluation trade-off: larger models generally offer broader capability but cost more to run, respond slower, and carry a larger risk surface; smaller models are cheaper, faster, and easier to control but may underperform on complex tasks. The governing question is fitness for the specific use case, not maximum capability.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe capability-versus-cost axis: large models bring breadth and emergent capability with heavy inference cost, latency, and a wider risk surface; small models are cheap, fast, controllable — and sufficient for most narrow tasks. Right-sizing is a governance act: over-provisioned capability is unassessed risk.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] 'Use the most capable model' is a distractor — fitness for the specific use case, not maximum capability, is the tested principle.", "kt328": "## Definition\n\nA field of data processing that tolerates and exploits imprecision, uncertainty, and partial truth to make problem-solving more transparent and robust.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe tolerance-for-imprecision family: fuzzy logic, neural networks, evolutionary computation — approaches that trade exactness for tractability on messy real-world problems, contrasted with hard computing's precise algorithms. Mostly historical/taxonomic vocabulary today.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Recognize as the umbrella for approximate methods (fuzzy, neural, evolutionary) — terminology awareness suffices.", "kt329": "## Definition\n\nGDPR Article 9 data types subject to stricter processing prohibitions: race, ethnicity, politics, religion, trade union membership, genetics, biometrics, health, sex life, sexual orientation.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nGDPR Article 9's protected list: racial/ethnic origin, political opinions, religious/philosophical beliefs, trade-union membership, genetic data, biometric data for identification, health, sex life/orientation — processing prohibited absent a 9(2) condition, DPIA-triggering at scale, and inferable by AI from ordinary data.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Article 6 basis AND Article 9 condition both required — and criminal-offense data (Art 10) is its own separate regime, a nuance distractor.", "kt330": "## Definition\n\nConversion of a speech signal into a representation of the speech content by a functional unit.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nSpeech-to-text: converting spoken audio into written language — the input side of voice interfaces. Governance angles: voice is biometric-adjacent (speaker identification is biometric processing), accuracy disparities across accents are a fairness pattern, and always-listening devices raise collection questions.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] STT = speech in, text out; TTS is the reverse — and accent-based accuracy gaps are the fairness hook.", "kt331": "## Definition\n\nGeneration of artificial speech.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nText-to-speech: generating spoken audio from text — now realistic enough that voice cloning enables fraud and deepfake audio, pulling TTS into synthetic-media governance: disclosure duties, consent for voice likeness, and provenance marking.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] TTS = text in, speech out — with voice-cloning abuse as the attached risk to name.", "kt332": "## Definition\n\nA characteristic of AI systems requiring governance: AI processes data and makes decisions far faster than humans and at massive scale, creating both opportunities and risks.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nSpeed and scale is the multiplier characteristic. AI executes decisions in milliseconds across millions of cases, so a single flaw propagates to population scale before any human notices. A biased rule in a manual process harms the cases it touches; the same rule in a deployed model harms everyone it scores in the interval before detection.\n\nThis is the quantitative argument for front-loading AI governance. Because the error has already happened a million times by the time it is observed, the controls that matter are the ones applied before deployment (testing on representative data, adversarial and edge-case testing, impact assessment) and the automated ones applied during operation (drift detection, fairness monitoring, threshold alerts, rollback criteria defined in advance). Post-hoc review cannot keep up with the rate of decisions.\n\nIt also reframes what an incident is. In AI, an incident is usually not a single wrong output but a systematic pattern discovered late, which is why root-cause analysis looks at the data and the objective rather than at the individual case, and why the scope of remediation is often the whole affected population rather than one decision.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Speed and scale explain why AI governance front-loads: by the time a human sees the problem it has already happened a million times. Pre-deployment testing, automated monitoring and predefined rollback criteria are the controls this characteristic justifies.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · Manage function: prioritisation, response and monitoring\n- <a href=\"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF Playbook</a> · monitoring, alerting and response actions\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 600-1, Generative AI Profile</a> · scale effects and information integrity", "kt333": "## Definition\n\nAn individual, group, or organization that can influence a decision or activity, be affected by it, or feel affected by it.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nISO's formal term: any person or organization that can affect, be affected by, or perceive itself affected by a decision or activity — for AI, spanning boards to data subjects to regulators to civil society. Stakeholder determination is an explicit ISO 42001 clause-4 requirement shaping AIMS scope.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] 'Interested party' in ISO text = stakeholder — same concept, standards register.", "kt334": "## Definition\n\nThe process of identifying all parties affected by an AI system, understanding their concerns, interests, and influence — essential for comprehensive risk management.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nStakeholder mapping is the structured exercise of identifying stakeholders and charting their interest, influence and exposure. It feeds three downstream activities: impact assessment (who could be harmed), governance design (who needs a seat), and communications (who needs to know what, and when).\n\nIts tested purpose is risk identification. Different stakeholder groups are affected differently by the same system, so mapping is how a governance professional surfaces harms that are invisible from the sponsor's perspective: the applicants screened out, the employees whose workflow changed, the customers whose data trained the model, the community where the system is deployed. Omission is why systems surprise the people they affect.\n\nThe three axes do distinct work. **Interest** determines engagement depth. **Influence** determines whether they need a decision right or only a voice. **Exposure** determines whether they need protection, recourse, or explicit disclosure, and it is the axis most often skipped, because exposure is a property of the affected population rather than of anyone in the room.\n\nMapping should also be revisited rather than done once: a system's scope, data or deployment context changes, and with them the population it touches.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] During risk identification, stakeholder mapping is what reveals how different groups are differentially affected. The axis most often missing from a wrong answer is exposure: who the system acts on, as opposed to who has influence over it.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF Playbook, Map function</a> · identifying affected parties and context\n- <a href=\"https://www.iso.org/standard/42001\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 42001 (AI management system)</a> · interested parties and their requirements\n- <a href=\"https://oecd.ai/en/ai-principles\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OECD AI Principles</a> · engagement with affected stakeholders", "kt335": "## Definition\n\nPre-release testing under expected and edge-case workloads: latency and throughput benchmarks, memory/compute usage under load, failover behavior during outages, and evaluation against adversarial and malicious prompts.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nPushing the system past comfortable conditions before launch: peak loads, degraded infrastructure, malformed inputs, adversarial prompts, edge-case scenarios — measuring latency, resource ceilings, failover behavior, and failure gracefulness. The readiness gate's empirical core.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Stress testing = operational limits and failure behavior — complementary to accuracy validation, not a substitute.", "kt336": "## Definition\n\nA threat modeling framework: Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Information disclosure, Denial of service, Elevation of privilege — adapted for AI security analysis.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nMicrosoft's threat-modeling mnemonic — Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Information disclosure, Denial of service, Elevation of privilege — applied to AI pipelines: tampering maps to data poisoning, information disclosure to model inversion, DoS to resource-exhaustion attacks on inference.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] STRIDE = threat modeling framework (security analysis structure), not an AI-specific control — recognize and map its categories.", "kt337": "## Definition\n\nHidden supply-chain risk beneath a contracted AI vendor: subcontracted data-labeling teams, models trained on scraped or copyrighted data, and outdated code libraries. A core challenge is how many levels down the TPRM program should probe.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nSub-tier dependencies are the suppliers behind your suppliers: the data labeling firms, annotation vendors, base-model providers, data brokers and open-source components your AI vendor relies on. They are invisible in your contract and present in your risk.\n\nThe failure pattern is well documented. A procurement team contracts with one vendor and does not know that the vendor subcontracts labeling to teams in other jurisdictions, that the model was trained on scraped or copyrighted material, that a code library in the pipeline is unmaintained and vulnerable, or that the vendor's own third parties use AI capability that affects your data. Each of those is a risk your organization answers for, because accountability does not stop at the contracting counterparty.\n\nTPRM's hardest question is how many tiers down to probe, and the honest answer is that it scales with system criticality and with the sensitivity of the data involved. The instruments are flow-down contract clauses (the vendor must impose equivalent obligations on its own suppliers), provenance demands (where did this data come from and under what terms), and an **AI system bill of materials** listing upstream inputs. The EU AI Act reinforces the principle by attaching obligations along the value chain rather than only at the point of sale.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Vendor risk includes the vendor's vendors. Flow-down contract clauses, provenance demands and an AI bill of materials are the tested controls, and the depth of probing scales with system criticality and data sensitivity.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/25/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 25 (responsibilities along the AI value chain)</a> · how obligations follow the chain\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 600-1, value-chain and component integration risk</a> · inherited risk from upstream components\n- <a href=\"https://www.cisa.gov/ai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CISA artificial intelligence resources</a> · software and AI supply-chain guidance", "kt338": "## Definition\n\nAI based on techniques and models that use an implicit encoding of information derived from experience or raw data.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe paradigm where knowledge lives in numbers, not symbols: distributed weights, learned representations, no human-readable rules — neural networks being the flagship. Capability high, inspectability low; the opacity that drives explainability requirements is subsymbolic by nature.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Subsymbolic = connectionist = neural (learned, opaque) vs. symbolic (rules, transparent) — one distinction, several labels.", "kt339": "## Definition\n\nThe Playbook's voluntary, per-subcategory steps for reaching an AI RMF outcome, e.g. MAP 1.1's step to identify whether a non-AI or non-technology alternative would give more trustworthy outcomes. Organizations use as many or as few as fit; they are not a checklist or an ordered sequence.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nEach Playbook entry lists suggested actions for its subcategory, from one (MAP 2.1) to two dozen (MEASURE 2.11). They turn an outcome into practical steps: MEASURE 2.11's include identifying types of harm and quantifying them with fairness metrics, and MANAGE 4.1's include red-teaming at a prescribed cadence. NIST says organizations may use as many or as few as apply, and users are not expected to implement them all or in order.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] An option saying every suggested action must be implemented, or that implementing them earns certification, is wrong.", "kt340": "## Definition\n\nA training method using labeled data where the AI learns from examples with known correct answers. Example: spam filter trained on labeled spam/legitimate messages.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nSupervised learning is the labelled-data paradigm: the model learns a mapping from inputs to outputs from example pairs (spam versus not-spam, price given features, diagnosis given symptoms) and generalises that mapping to new inputs. It is the paradigm behind most deployed business ML: credit scoring, churn prediction, fraud detection, medical triage support.\n\nBecause it learns from labels, supervised learning inherits its labels' properties, and that is the governance core. Labels are produced by humans, by processes, or by historical outcomes, and each route carries bias. A hiring model trained on a decade of past hiring decisions learns that decade's preferences. A credit model trained on repayment data learns who was historically given credit. This is how discrimination enters a system that never mentions a protected attribute: it is encoded in the target the model was taught to predict.\n\nThe practical controls are therefore label-focused: document how labels were produced and by whom, measure inter-annotator agreement, test performance across subgroups, and treat a historically derived target as a fairness risk rather than a ground truth.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Labelled input-output pairs equals supervised learning, and the labels are where historical bias enters. A scenario where a model reproduces past discriminatory outcomes points at the target variable, not the algorithm.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · the formal supervised learning definition\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/SpecialPublications/NIST.SP.1270.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST SP 1270, bias in AI</a> · how historical and measurement bias enter training data\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/10/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 10 (data and data governance)</a> · the statutory duties on training data for high-risk systems", "kt341": "## Definition\n\nMachine learning in which only labeled data is used during training.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe ISO/IEC 22989 formal name for supervised learning: machine learning in which training data carries target labels that the model learns to predict. Dual-listed because standards text and exam items use the full term, and recognising the two phrasings as one paradigm is a reliable small point.\n\nThe substance is identical to supervised learning, including its governance profile. Labels encode the judgement being taught, so a model trained on historical decisions reproduces historical preferences, and the quality of the labelling process bounds the quality of everything downstream. Label provenance, annotator agreement and subgroup performance are the controls that matter.\n\nOne practical reason the formal term is worth holding: ISO-anchored documentation duties talk about training, validation and testing data with specific meanings, and being able to map \"supervised machine learning\" onto the data-governance requirements lets you answer a standards-framed question using the concept you already know.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] One paradigm, two names: supervised learning and supervised machine learning. A distractor suggesting they describe different techniques is testing recognition, not knowledge.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · the formal term as defined\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/SpecialPublications/NIST.SP.1270.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST SP 1270, bias in AI</a> · training-data bias mechanisms", "kt342": "## Definition\n\nMachine learning algorithm that finds decision boundaries with maximum margin.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAn SVM is the classic maximum-margin classifier: it finds the decision boundary that separates classes with the widest possible buffer, using kernel functions to handle structure that a straight line cannot. It was a pre-deep-learning workhorse and remains competitive on smaller, structured datasets.\n\nIts governance interest is as a **traditional ML** option in a design decision. An SVM is far more inspectable than a deep network: the margin and the support vectors that define it can be examined, and with a linear kernel the contribution of each feature is legible. That makes it an attractive choice where explanation is a requirement and the accuracy penalty is acceptable, which is exactly the trade-off the BOK's design guidance describes: enforce interpretable models unless a performance trade-off is documented and approved, and require justification for complex architectures.\n\nExpect it as a distractor or as the correct answer to \"which technique would support explainability requirements\" in a scenario with modest data and stakes that demand understanding.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Recognition level: a classical, margin-based classification technique, and therefore a 'traditional ML' or 'inherently more interpretable' answer option rather than a black-box one.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · classical machine learning technique vocabulary\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · explainable and interpretable as a trustworthiness characteristic", "kt343": "## Definition\n\nAI based on techniques and models that manipulate symbols and structures according to explicitly defined rules.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe rules-and-logic paradigm: knowledge encoded explicitly (ontologies, rules, logic), reasoning by symbol manipulation — expert systems as its production form. Transparent and brittle where learned systems are capable and opaque; the pendulum between the paradigms is AI history in one sentence.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Symbolic = explicit rules, explainable, brittle; its opposite pole is subsymbolic/connectionist learning.", "kt344": "## Definition\n\nArtificially generated records that imitate the patterns and statistical make-up of a real dataset without copying actual records; used where genuine data is scarce, skewed toward some groups, or too risky to use or share. It lowers but does not remove privacy risk, since a generator trained on personal data can reproduce details of real people.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nSynthetic data can come from simulations, rules or generative models trained on real data, and is used to enlarge small datasets, rebalance under-represented groups, test systems, or share data with fewer privacy concerns. It is not automatically anonymous, so re-identification risk still has to be tested. The EU AI Act makes it a first resort for bias work: providers of high-risk systems may process special-category data to detect and correct bias only if synthetic or anonymised data would not do the job (Art. 10(5)(a) as originally enacted; Art. 4a(1)(a) after the 2026 Digital Omnibus).\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Synthetic data mimics real data's statistical patterns; it reduces but does not eliminate privacy risk, and it is the named alternative in the AI Act's bias-detection exception.", "kt345": "## Definition\n\nA transparency document describing an AI system's architecture, training data, intended use, limitations, and ethical considerations — broader scope than a model card.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nDocumentation for the deployed whole: the assembled system's components, data flows, model interactions, oversight points, and operational context — one level up from model cards, because users experience systems, not models, and risk lives in the assembly.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Model card documents the model; system card documents the assembled deployment — pick the right artifact for the scope.", "kt346": "## Definition\n\nFederal law (Pub. L. 119-12, signed 19 May 2025). It makes it a crime to knowingly publish nonconsensual intimate images, including AI digital forgeries. Covered platforms that host user content must run a notice-and-removal process and take reported images down within 48 hours, along with known identical copies. The platform duties have applied since 19 May 2026 and are enforced by the FTC, including against nonprofits.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe Act pairs criminal liability for people who publish nonconsensual intimate images, real or AI-made, with a civil notice-and-takedown duty for platforms. Platforms must remove reported images within 48 hours and make reasonable efforts to remove identical copies. Failures are treated as violations of an FTC rule, and the Act extends that enforcement to nonprofits, which the FTC normally cannot reach.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Remember 48 hours, FTC enforcement from 19 May 2026, and that AI digital forgeries count.", "kt347": "## Definition\n\n*\\<Artificial Intelligence>* Action required to achieve a specific goal. Examples: classification, regression, ranking, clustering, dimensionality reduction.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nISO 22989's unit of AI work: the defined function a system performs — classify, translate, rank, generate. Task definition disciplines everything: narrow AI is task-scoped, evaluation is task-relative, and scope creep is task expansion without re-assessment.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Terminology anchor — capabilities are defined per task, and approvals attach to tasks, not to models in the abstract.", "kt348": "## Definition\n\nRequired documentation for high-risk AI systems demonstrating compliance — includes system design, training methodologies, testing results, and risk management measures.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe AI Act's evidence dossier (Annex IV): system description, development process, data requirements, risk management, testing, performance, oversight design — prepared before market placement, kept current, retained ten years, and producible on regulatory demand. Conformity's paper trail.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Annex IV + 10-year retention + must exist before CE marking — the three facts that get tested.", "kt349": "## Definition\n\nData used to evaluate the performance of a final model. Distinct from training data and validation data.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe held-out final exam: data untouched during training and tuning, used once to estimate real-world performance honestly. Its integrity is sacred — leakage from training, or repeated tuning against it, silently converts 'evaluation' into fiction.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Train fits, validation tunes, test evaluates — and tuning on test data is the invalidation pattern to spot.", "kt350": "## Definition\n\nTexas HB 149, effective 1 Jan 2026. It bans developing or deploying AI with intent to push people toward self-harm or crime, to discriminate unlawfully (disparate impact alone is not enough), to infringe constitutional rights, or to produce child sexual abuse material or sexual deepfakes. AI disclosure, social-scoring and biometric-identification limits apply to government. The AG alone enforces, with a 60-day cure, penalties up to $200,000 per uncurable violation, a NIST-framework defence and a 36-month sandbox.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nTRAIGA is a conduct-ban statute rather than a governance mandate: it prohibits AI built or used with intent to cause specific harms and leaves most transparency duties to government agencies. The AG must give 60 days to cure, and a company is not liable if it found the problem through red-teaming, feedback or an internal review while substantially following the NIST AI RMF Generative AI Profile. It also preempts local AI ordinances.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] TRAIGA requires intent to discriminate and says disparate impact alone is not enough, the opposite of Illinois HB 3773's effect standard.", "kt351": "## Definition\n\nEU copyright exceptions in the DSM Directive (EU) 2019/790 for automated analysis of text and data. Article 3 lets research organisations and cultural heritage institutions mine lawfully accessed works for scientific research, with no opt-out. Article 4 lets anyone mine lawfully accessible works unless the rightsholder has reserved its rights.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nArticle 2(2) of the DSM Directive defines text and data mining as any automated technique that analyses digital text and data to generate information such as patterns, trends and correlations. The two exceptions differ in who benefits and whether rightsholders can refuse: Article 3 cannot be overridden by contract (Article 7(1)) or by an opt-out, while Article 4 yields to an express reservation. The AI Act ties GPAI providers to the Article 4(3) reservation through Article 53(1)(c), and the Munich Regional Court read the exception as covering corpus-building but not memorisation inside a model (GEMA v. OpenAI, 2025).\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Commercial trainer: Article 4 plus respect for opt-outs; research organisation doing scientific research: Article 3, no opt-out.", "kt352": "## Definition\n\nA theoretical advanced AI that could understand and simulate human emotions, beliefs, intentions, and thoughts. Example: HAL-9000 (fictional).\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nTheory-of-mind AI is the hypothetical functionality tier that models other minds: inferring beliefs, intentions and emotional states in order to interact socially. It sits above limited memory and below self-awareness on the ladder, and no deployed system has it.\n\nThe governance trap is that current systems **simulate** the surface of it convincingly. A chatbot that acknowledges frustration, adapts its tone and refers to what you said earlier looks like social understanding, but it is pattern completion over a large corpus of human interaction. Treating that simulation as genuine comprehension is the anthropomorphism hazard: it inflates assessments of what the system understands, which in turn inflates trust.\n\nWhere this matters operationally is in systems designed to influence people, from customer service to mental-health adjacent applications to political persuasion. The absence of real social understanding means the system cannot recognise when its output is harmful to a particular person, so the safeguard has to be designed in (escalation to a human, content limits, detection of vulnerable contexts) rather than relied on as an emergent property.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Theoretical tier: simulated empathy in current chatbots does not qualify. Ladder placement is the question format, and anthropomorphic framing in a stem is usually the thing being tested.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · capability tier vocabulary\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 600-1, Generative AI Profile</a> · human-AI configuration and over-trust risks", "kt353": "## Definition\n\nAn entity that processes personal data on behalf of the data controller — must have a contract specifying scope, safeguards, and responsibilities (GDPR Art 28).\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nGDPR's outsourced hands: an external entity processing personal data on the controller's documented instructions — cloud platforms, analytics vendors, AI-API providers — bound by Article 28 contracts covering security, sub-processors, audit rights, and breach notice. The controller's accountability never transfers with the data.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] AI vendors processing your users' data on your instructions are processors — Article 28 DPA required, accountability retained.", "kt354": "## Definition\n\nGovernance of outsourced AI components — pretrained models, cloud infrastructure, data labeling, algorithmic APIs. For AI it must evolve beyond traditional vendor checklists to cover black-box algorithms, datasets of unknown origin, and unpredictable LLM APIs.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nTPRM is the lifecycle discipline for vendor exposure: due diligence before contracting, contractual risk allocation, continuous monitoring during the relationship, and orderly exit after it. Applied to AI, it needs extending at every stage.\n\n**Due diligence** gains AI-specific probes: training-data provenance and rights, fairness and robustness evidence, explainability support, model-update practices, and named sub-tier dependencies. **Contracts** gain clauses that make the risk manageable: data-use limits, performance and fairness warranties, model-change and incident notification, audit rights, model deactivation rights, and termination and portability terms. **Monitoring** becomes continuous rather than annual, because a vendor model update can change behaviour mid-contract. **Exit** has to address data return or deletion, model artifacts, and any dependency that cannot simply be switched off.\n\nThe BOK frames this under policies, assessments and contracts across procurement, supply chain, human resources and acceptable use, and the governing rule is that **accountability is non-delegable**. Outsourcing the AI does not outsource the regulatory or ethical responsibility, which is why TPRM for AI is a risk-allocation exercise rather than a transfer of liability.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] AI TPRM is classic TPRM plus AI-specific probes: provenance, bias evidence, model-change notice, sub-tier visibility. The extension is what gets tested, and outsourcing never transfers accountability.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · third-party risk in the Govern function\n- <a href=\"https://www.iso.org/standard/42001\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 42001 (AI management system)</a> · Annex A third-party and supplier controls\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/25/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 25 (value-chain responsibilities)</a> · obligations that follow the chain\n- <a href=\"https://iapp.org/resources/article/key-terms-for-ai-governance/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IAPP Key Terms for AI Governance</a> · third-party and vendor terminology", "kt355": "## Definition\n\nAn agent using a tool it is legitimately allowed to use in an unsafe or unintended way, such as deleting valuable data, over-calling a costly API or leaking information, often after prompt injection or an ambiguous instruction. OWASP ASI02 (Tool Misuse and Exploitation); gaining privileges it should not have is ASI03 instead.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nOWASP's ASI02 covers an agent working within its authorised privileges but applying a legitimate tool unsafely, for example deleting records or exfiltrating data after reading a malicious document. It extends the LLM Top 10's Excessive Agency entry to multi-step agent workflows. Mitigations include per-tool least-privilege profiles, sandboxed execution with egress allowlists, a policy gate that checks tool calls before they run, usage budgets and short-lived credentials.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Misusing privileges it already has is ASI02 Tool Misuse; gaining privileges it should not have is ASI03 Identity and Privilege Abuse.", "kt356": "## Definition\n\nConfidential business information providing competitive advantage. AI models and training methodologies may be protected as trade secrets if reasonable secrecy measures are maintained.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nIP protection through secrecy: information deriving value from not being known, guarded by reasonable measures — the default protection for model weights, training recipes, and prompt libraries. Fragile by design: disclosure destroys it, so employee prompts pasted into public AI tools are an IP event, not just a privacy one.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Protection requires actual secrecy measures — and feeding confidential data into external AI tools can forfeit trade-secret status.", "kt357": "## Definition\n\nResult of model training.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nISO 22989's term for training's product: the parameter-laden artifact ready for inference — versioned, evaluated, documented, and deployed as a unit. Distinguishing 'model' (architecture/concept) from 'trained model' (specific learned instance) keeps versioning and validation talk precise.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Each training run yields a distinct trained model — validation and approval attach to the instance, not the architecture.", "kt358": "## Definition\n\nProcess for determining or improving the parameters of a machine learning model, based on a ML algorithm, using training data.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nTraining is the learning process itself: an algorithm iteratively adjusts the model's parameters against data until it captures the target patterns, evaluated against a held-out set to check that it generalises rather than memorises. It is compute-intensive, data-hungry, and consequential, because everything the data contains the model absorbs.\n\nTraining is where the governance controls upstream of the model either pay off or fail. Lawful rights to use the data, quality, representativeness, provenance and privacy all get baked in here; once training completes, none of them can be retrofitted without retraining. That is why the BOK sequences the lifecycle the way it does: data governance and rights checks come before training, not after evaluation.\n\nTraining is also a documentation event. The record that makes a model auditable and reproducible includes the dataset versions, the hyperparameters, the random seeds, the code version and the evaluation results. Training and fine-tuning both need to be versioned, because a fine-tuned derivative is a new artifact with its own risks and its own inherited properties from the base model.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Training means patterns learned from data through iteration, and its inputs (rights, quality, representativeness) are the governed surface. Sequencing questions expect data governance before training, and fine-tuning inherits the base model's properties.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · training, validation and testing definitions\n- <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.03993\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Model Cards for Model Reporting</a> · recording how a model was trained\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/10/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 10 (data and data governance)</a> · the statutory data-governance duties for high-risk systems", "kt359": "## Definition\n\nAn organizational program delivering AI terminology, strategy, and governance education to all stakeholders, tailored to each role's interaction with AI.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nA training and awareness program is the organizational competence layer: role-tailored AI education that turns a written policy into day-to-day behaviour. The BOK requires it to cover AI terminology, strategy and governance for all stakeholders, and since 2 February 2025 the EU AI Act's Article 4 has made AI literacy an expectation for provider and deployer staff.\n\nThe design rule the exam tests is **awareness for everyone, depth by role**. Executives need risk literacy, regulatory exposure and the ability to read a risk register. Developers need governance by design, documentation standards and bias testing. Legal, risk and compliance need regulatory fluency and assessment practice. Frontline users need to know what the system can and cannot do, when to override, and how to report an anomaly. One-size-fits-all training is the wrong answer in every scenario that offers it.\n\nTraining is also a control, not only a culture program. It is the primary countermeasure to automation bias, the documented human tendency to over-trust machine output. It supports contestability, because a user who cannot interpret an output cannot challenge it. And it needs maintenance: a curriculum written before the current obligations existed does not satisfy them, so programs need refresh cycles tied to regulatory and system change.\n\nMeasure effectiveness rather than attendance. Pre and post literacy assessment, the share of staff trained within a stated window, documentation completeness on AI projects, and the volume of risk escalations initiated by trained staff. The last is the most informative, because it measures whether people believe reporting is safe.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Role-tailored beats one-size-fits-all in every training scenario, and AI literacy became a legal expectation under EU AI Act Article 4 from 2 February 2025. If an option treats training as a one-time onboarding event, it contradicts the maintenance requirement.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/4/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 4 (AI literacy)</a> · the legal expectation in force from 2 February 2025\n- <a href=\"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF Playbook, Govern function</a> · workforce competence and awareness actions\n- <a href=\"https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/artificial-intelligence/guidance-on-ai-and-data-protection/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ICO guidance on AI and data protection</a> · training alongside accountability expectations\n- <a href=\"https://www.iso.org/standard/42001\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 42001 (AI management system)</a> · clause 7.2 competence and 7.3 awareness", "kt360": "## Definition\n\nThe phase where AI models learn from data (training) and are evaluated for accuracy, bias, and performance (testing) before deployment.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe paired disciplines of the model lifecycle: learn from training data, then verify against data the model never saw — with the discipline being separation. The phrase names the workflow whose corruption (leakage, test-set tuning) invalidates every quality claim downstream.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] The separation is the point — any scenario blending training and evaluation data is describing invalidated results.", "kt361": "## Definition\n\nData used to train a machine learning model.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nTraining data is the dataset a model learns from, and therefore the single most consequential governance surface in AI. Its properties determine almost everything that follows:\n\n- **Rights** determine legality: whether the organization had lawful authority to collect and to use the data for training, including for secondary use, and whether third-party terms permit it.\n- **Quality** determines accuracy: accuracy, completeness, consistency, timeliness, validity and uniqueness, measured rather than assumed.\n- **Representativeness** determines fairness: whether the data reflects the population the system will act on, which is the root of most bias findings.\n- **Sensitivity** determines privacy exposure: whether special-category or otherwise protected data is present, including data from which such attributes can be inferred.\n- **Documentation** determines auditability: provenance, lineage and composition recorded so that a later question has an answer.\n\nThe BOK states the rule directly for high-risk systems, and the EU AI Act's Article 10 turns it into law: training, validation and testing datasets must meet quality criteria and be examined for bias. When a model misbehaves, the investigation should start here, because most model problems trace to data before they trace to algorithms.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Rights, quality, representativeness, sensitivity, documentation: those five are the training-data governance surface. Most model problems trace to training data, so 'investigate the data' beats 'retrain the model' in a root-cause scenario.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/10/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 10 (data and data governance)</a> · the legal standard for high-risk training data\n- <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.09010\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Datasheets for Datasets</a> · the documentation standard for a dataset\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/SpecialPublications/NIST.SP.1270.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST SP 1270, bias in AI</a> · how bias enters through data\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · training, validation and test data terminology", "kt362": "## Definition\n\nA set of methods in which data intended for solving one problem is used to apply the knowledge gained from it to another problem.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nReusing learned representations: start from a model trained on one task, adapt it to another — the economics behind the foundation-model era (pretrain once, fine-tune everywhere). Governance inheritance rides along: the base model's biases, data provenance, and license terms transfer with the weights.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Transfer learning inherits the base model's risks and restrictions — provenance questions don't reset at fine-tuning.", "kt363": "## Definition\n\nThe characteristic of an organization whereby relevant activities and decisions are communicated to relevant interested parties in a comprehensive, accessible, and understandable manner.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nISO 22989 distinguishes the organizational property: openness about activities and decisions — what AI the organization uses, for what, governed how — expressed through notices, reports, and registries, as distinct from any single system's inspectability.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Organization-level transparency (we disclose our AI use) vs. system-level (this system's workings are inspectable) — ISO splits them deliberately.", "kt364": "## Definition\n\nA characteristic of a system whereby appropriate information about the system is made available to relevant interested parties. May include: characteristics, performance, limitations, components, procedures, measures, design objectives, data sources, labeling protocols.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe system-level property: information about a specific system's capabilities, limitations, data, and logic is available to those who need it — the substrate for informed use, effective oversight, and contestable decisions. Companion to explainability: transparency supplies information, explainability makes outputs comprehensible.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Transparency = information availability; explainability = output comprehensibility — the exam's favorite near-synonym split.", "kt365": "## Definition\n\nThe questions in each Playbook entry's Transparency and Documentation section, under 'Organizations can document the following', e.g. GOVERN 1.6's 'Who is responsible for documenting and maintaining the AI system inventory details?'. Each list is followed by AI Transparency Resources such as GAO-21-519SP.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nUnder 'Transparency and Documentation', each entry lists questions headed 'Organizations can document the following', then 'AI Transparency Resources', most often GAO-21-519SP, GAO's AI Accountability Framework. The questions help an organization record how it approaches the outcome; MAP 1.5, for instance, asks how the entity identified its maximum allowable risk tolerance. Like the rest of the Playbook they are voluntary.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Suggested actions say what to do; documentation prompts ask what the organization can record and show, and both are voluntary.", "kt366": "## Definition\n\nGPAI Code of Practice chapter applying to ALL GPAI providers: model documentation, information to downstream providers, and the Annex XI/XII technical documentation obligations of Articles 53(1)(a)-(b).\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe GPAI Code of Practice chapter binding all signatory providers: model documentation aligned to Annex XI, information packages enabling downstream providers to meet their own obligations (Annex XII), and contact/update practices — operationalizing AI Act Articles 53(1)(a)-(b).\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] All GPAI providers — transparency and copyright chapters are universal; only safety & security is systemic-risk-only.", "kt367": "## Definition\n\nThe ability to meet the expectations of interested parties in a verifiable manner. Characteristics include: reliability, availability, resilience, security, privacy, accountability, transparency, integrity, authenticity, quality, and user-friendliness. Applies to services, products, technologies, data, information, and organizations.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe summary property: an AI system worthy of justified confidence — NIST decomposes it into seven characteristics (valid & reliable, safe, secure & resilient, accountable & transparent, explainable & interpretable, privacy-enhanced, fair), ISO into properties like robustness, reliability, controllability. Trustworthy AI is the goal-state all frameworks describe from different angles.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] 'Trustworthy AI' is NIST's umbrella — when a question lists characteristics, count them against the seven.", "kt368": "## Definition\n\nAI that is built and governed so that people can justifiably rely on it, in line with principles such as safety, fairness and accountability; IAPP treats the term as largely synonymous with ethical AI and responsible AI. The NIST AI RMF's seven characteristics of trustworthy AI are: valid and reliable; safe; secure and resilient; accountable and transparent; explainable and interpretable; privacy-enhanced; and fair with harmful bias managed.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nTrustworthy AI is a goal state rather than a single test, reached by balancing characteristics that can pull against each other. NIST's own examples of the tension are **predictive accuracy versus interpretability** and **interpretability versus privacy**: you can usually improve one by spending the other, so trustworthiness is a documented set of trade-offs rather than a score.\n\nThe characteristics to know cold, because they are tested directly, are NIST's seven: valid and reliable; safe; secure and resilient; accountable and transparent; explainable and interpretable; privacy-enhanced; and fair with harmful bias managed. Two structural notes matter. **Valid and reliable is the necessary base**: a system that does not work as intended cannot be trustworthy in any other respect, so the other characteristics are conditional on it. And **accountable and transparent relates to all of them**, because the accountability mechanism is what makes the rest enforceable rather than aspirational.\n\nThe same substance appears under other labels: the OECD's five principles aim at AI that is innovative and trustworthy and respects human rights and democratic values, and the EU AI Act's requirements operationalize the set for high-risk systems. Recognising the concept under any framework's vocabulary is the skill the exam checks.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Memorize NIST's seven: valid and reliable; safe; secure and resilient; accountable and transparent; explainable and interpretable; privacy-enhanced; fair with harmful bias managed. Valid and reliable is the base, and accountable and transparent is the one that makes the rest enforceable.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF (AI 100-1)</a> · the seven trustworthiness characteristics and their trade-offs\n- <a href=\"https://oecd.ai/en/ai-principles\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OECD AI Principles</a> · the intergovernmental trustworthy-AI framing\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 600-1, Generative AI Profile</a> · trustworthiness characteristics applied to generative AI", "kt369": "## Definition\n\nA modeling error where an AI model is too simple to capture underlying patterns in the data — results in poor performance on both training and new data.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe model that never learned: too simple, too constrained, or too starved to capture real patterns — performing poorly on training data and new data alike. Remedies run opposite to overfitting's: more capacity, richer features, longer training.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Bad everywhere = underfitting; great on training only = overfitting — the diagnostic pair in one line.", "kt370": "## Definition\n\nFTC-enforced standard prohibiting misleading or harmful business practices. AI systems making unsubstantiated claims or hiding AI involvement may violate UDAP.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nUDAP is the consumer-protection workhorse: section 5 of the FTC Act federally, and state mini-FTC acts at state level. It prohibits two things. **Deception** is a material representation or omission likely to mislead a reasonable consumer, which covers overstated AI capability claims (\"AI-powered\" applied to a rules engine), hidden material facts, and undisclosed bots. **Unfairness** is an act or practice that causes or is likely to cause substantial injury that consumers cannot reasonably avoid and that is not outweighed by benefits.\n\nThe doctrine is technology-neutral, which is the point: there is no AI exemption, and AI conduct is tested under the same standards as any other commercial conduct. That makes UDAP the regime most likely to reach a company's AI marketing, its chatbot disclosures, its pricing personalisation and its algorithmic practices, often before any AI-specific law applies.\n\nOne remedy is worth knowing because it is distinctive: the FTC has ordered **algorithmic disgorgement**, requiring a company to delete models and algorithms trained on improperly obtained data. It is the consumer-protection system reaching into the model artifact itself, and it is the clearest illustration of why training-data provenance is a legal question rather than a data-science one.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] UDAP is technology-neutral, so AI claims and AI conduct face the same deception and unfairness standards as everything else. AI-washing, undisclosed bots and algorithmic disgorgement are the three enforcement patterns to recognize.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://www.ftc.gov/industry/technology/artificial-intelligence\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">FTC artificial intelligence resources</a> · enforcement actions and guidance on AI claims and conduct\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI 600-1, Generative AI Profile</a> · information integrity and deceptive-use risks\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/50/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 50 (transparency)</a> · the EU analogue of disclosing AI interaction", "kt371": "## Definition\n\nApplication of an AI system in ways not anticipated by its developers — may produce harmful outcomes due to mismatched capabilities and context.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nUnintended use is a system being used beyond its intended purpose: a triage assistant becoming the de facto diagnostician, a fraud score leaking into hiring decisions, a support chatbot answering questions about regulated advice. The BOK treats it as a governance problem in Domain IV, alongside **downstream harm** and **secondary use**.\n\nThe reason it matters is that **intended purpose anchors everything**. It sets the risk classification, drives the choice of test scenarios and evaluation population, determines the instructions for use, and defines what the provider has assessed. Change the use and every one of those judgements is void, which is why material repurposing should re-enter assessment rather than proceed by default.\n\nThere are two responsibilities to keep separate. **Foreseeable misuse** is the provider's problem: if a reasonable person could anticipate that users would put the system to a particular use, the provider should warn against it, design against it, or document it as a limitation. **Actual unintended use** is the deployer's problem, and under the EU AI Act a deployer that changes a high-risk system's intended purpose can become its provider, inheriting the full obligations chain. Scope discipline, with approvals attached to use cases rather than to models, is the control.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Intended purpose anchors risk class, testing and instructions, so a new use re-opens assessment. Foreseeable misuse is the provider's duty to warn or design against; a deployer that changes intended purpose can become the provider.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/25/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 25 (responsibilities along the value chain)</a> · modifying intended purpose can make a deployer the provider\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/3/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 3 (intended purpose)</a> · the definition that anchors classification\n- <a href=\"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF Playbook</a> · documenting purpose, assumptions and limits of use", "kt372": "## Definition\n\nTesting individual components or functions of an AI system in isolation to verify each part works correctly before integration.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nTesting components in isolation — a preprocessing function, a feature transform, a scoring module — before integration compounds failures. In ML pipelines it covers the deterministic scaffolding around the model; the model itself needs statistical evaluation beyond pass/fail assertions.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Unit tests verify pipeline components; model quality needs validation testing — the ladder starts here but can't end here.", "kt373": "## Definition\n\nA training method using unlabeled data where the AI must detect patterns independently. Example: customer segmentation from a CRM database.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nUnsupervised learning works without labels: it finds structure in raw data. Clustering groups similar records, dimensionality reduction compresses many features into fewer, anomaly detection flags what does not fit the learned pattern.\n\nIts governance edge is subtler than supervised learning's, and more interesting. Because no one specified the target, the structure the model finds is not accountable to a stated purpose. Clusters can encode sensitive groupings nobody asked for, so a customer segmentation model may reconstruct something close to a protected characteristic without a protected attribute being present. Anomaly detection quietly becomes policy, because whatever is defined as anomalous is whatever gets flagged, scrutinised or excluded, and that definition was learned rather than decided.\n\nThe practical controls are the ones the frameworks ask for elsewhere, applied to a system with no labelled truth: document what the model found and why it matters, review cluster interpretations with people who understand the domain and the affected population, and treat any use of an unsupervised output to make decisions about individuals as requiring its own assessment.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] No labels, finds structure: clustering and anomaly detection are the recognition examples. The governance point is that an unsupervised model can reconstruct sensitive groupings without being given the attribute.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · the formal unsupervised learning definition\n- <a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/SpecialPublications/NIST.SP.1270.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST SP 1270, bias in AI</a> · bias arising without a labelled target\n- <a href=\"https://gdpr-info.eu/art-9-gdpr/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GDPR, Article 9 (special categories)</a> · inferred sensitive groupings are still special-category processing", "kt374": "## Definition\n\nMachine learning in which only unlabeled data is used during training.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe ISO/IEC 22989 formal phrasing of the unlabelled paradigm: machine learning that draws patterns from data without target labels. Dual-listed with its short form because standards documents use the full term, and recognising both names as one paradigm is a reliable small point.\n\nThe substance is the same as unsupervised learning. The model discovers structure rather than predicting a specified target, which means the findings are not accountable to a stated purpose: clusters may encode sensitive groupings nobody intended, and anomaly definitions become policy by default.\n\nOne standards-specific nuance worth holding: ISO/IEC 22989 draws the paradigm boundaries formally, distinguishing unsupervised from supervised, semi-supervised, reinforcement and self-supervised learning. A question framed in standards vocabulary expects you to map the formal term onto the concept, so the practical skill is translation rather than new knowledge.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Same paradigm as unsupervised learning. If two options present the short and formal names as different techniques, the item is testing whether you recognise the synonym.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · the formal term as defined", "kt375": "## Definition\n\nA specific application scenario defining what problem the AI system solves, for whom, under what conditions, and with what expected outcomes — foundational to governance.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe defined application: what the system does, for whom, in what context, with what success criteria — the unit governance attaches to. Risk classification, impact assessment, and approval all bind to the use case, which is why undocumented new uses are governance violations, not features.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Approvals attach to use cases, not models — same model, new use case, new assessment.", "kt376": "## Definition\n\nThe process of evaluating a proposed AI application against organizational policies, risk appetite, and ethical standards before development begins.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe screening gate for proposed AI applications: value, feasibility, data availability, risk profile, and regulatory exposure evaluated before commitment — where bad ideas die cheap and good ideas acquire their governance requirements. The first formal artifact in a well-run lifecycle.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Sequencing: assess the use case before collecting data or building — the earliest and cheapest control gate.", "kt377": "## Definition\n\nEducation provided to individuals who interact with AI systems — covering capabilities, limitations, appropriate use, and how to flag unexpected or harmful outputs.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nUser training prepares the humans who operate AI: what the system can and cannot do, how to read its outputs and uncertainty, when to override it, and how to report an anomaly. It is the counterweight to automation bias and the enabler of meaningful oversight, which is why the EU AI Act's Article 4 made AI literacy an expectation for provider and deployer staff.\n\nThe design principles follow from the failure mode. Training should be **role-tailored**, because a recruiter reading ranked candidates, a clinician reviewing a risk score and an analyst using a forecast need different things. It should cover **limits and failure modes** explicitly, because users cannot calibrate trust against a system whose error modes they do not know. It should name the **escalation path**, so a user who spots a problem knows where to take it. And it should be **refreshed**, because systems, data and obligations change.\n\nTraining is necessary but not sufficient, which is the nuance exam items probe. Over-reliance scenarios resolve to training **plus** interface design (making disagreement and override easy, showing confidence and reasons rather than only a recommendation) **plus** oversight staffing that leaves time for real review. Training alone against a frictionless interface loses.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Over-reliance scenarios resolve through user training plus interface design plus realistic oversight workload, not through model accuracy improvements alone. Role-tailored beats one-size-fits-all every time.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/4/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EU AI Act, Article 4 (AI literacy)</a> · the legal expectation from 2 February 2025\n- <a href=\"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF Playbook, Govern function</a> · workforce and training actions\n- <a href=\"https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/artificial-intelligence/explaining-decisions-made-with-artificial-intelligence/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ICO: explaining decisions made with AI</a> · what the human reviewer needs in order to add value", "kt378": "## Definition\n\nUtah's 2024 law (SB 149, effective 1 May 2024) that first required generative AI disclosures and created the Office of AI Policy, the AI Learning Laboratory and regulatory mitigation agreements. As revised by SB 226 (2025), suppliers must disclose AI when a consumer clearly asks, and licensed professionals must disclose up front in high-risk interactions. Using AI is no defence to a consumer-protection violation. Fines reach $2,500 per violation, and the sunset is extended to 1 Jul 2027.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nUtah paired light disclosure rules with an innovation office and a learning lab that can agree regulatory mitigation with participants. The 2025 amendments narrowed disclosure: ordinary suppliers disclose only on a clear and unambiguous request, while licensed or certified professionals disclose first in high-risk interactions involving sensitive health, financial or biometric data or personalised advice. Blaming the AI is never a defence.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Utah means disclose when asked, but disclose first in high-risk interactions by licensed professionals.", "kt379": "## Definition\n\nNIST trustworthiness characteristic: AI system produces accurate (valid) and consistent (reliable) outputs under specified conditions.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nNIST's first-listed trustworthiness characteristic — and the framework calls it a precondition: the system measurably does what it claims (valid) and keeps doing it under expected conditions (reliable). Without validity, the other six characteristics are decorations on a system that doesn't work.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] The foundational NIST characteristic — validity is a necessary condition for trustworthiness; the other six assume it.", "kt380": "## Definition\n\nConfirmation by providing objective evidence that the requirements for a specific intended use or a specific intended application have been met.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nAre we building the right thing, and does it meet requirements? — evaluating the trained model against its specified functional, performance, and fairness requirements on data it hasn't seen, before deployment authorizes it. Paired with verification (built right?) in the V&V discipline.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Validation = meets requirements/fit for intended use; verification = built to spec — the V&V split is precise and tested.", "kt381": "## Definition\n\nData used to compare the performance of different candidate models. Used to tune hyperparameters.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe middle dataset: used during development to tune hyperparameters and compare candidate models — spent data, because decisions were made against it, which is why the untouched test set must render the final verdict.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Three-way split roles: training fits, validation tunes, test judges — validation data can't double as the final exam.", "kt382": "## Definition\n\nContract with third-party AI providers specifying data usage rights, performance guarantees, liability allocation, audit rights, and compliance obligations.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe contract that allocates AI risk between customer and supplier: data-use limits, performance and bias warranties, audit rights, model-change and incident notification, IP and confidentiality terms, liability, and exit portability. For AI vendors it's where governance requirements become enforceable obligations.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] The AI-specific clauses (data use, bias warranties, change notice, audit rights) are what distinguish an adequate AI vendor agreement from a generic one.", "kt383": "## Definition\n\nConfirmation by providing objective evidence that specified requirements have been met. Ensures only that a product conforms to its specifications.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nDid we build it right? — confirming the system conforms to its design specifications: code does what the design says, pipeline transforms match documentation, implementation matches architecture. The engineering half of V&V, logically prior to validation's fitness question.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Verification = conformance to spec; validation = fitness for intended purpose — keep the order and the questions straight.", "kt384": "## Definition\n\nTesting against specifications, intended use validation\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nThe paired assurance discipline — verification (built to specification) plus validation (meets requirements and intended use) — institutionalized as a formal lifecycle stage in ISO 22989 and as TEVV (test, evaluation, verification, validation) throughout NIST's framework vocabulary.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] V&V is an ISO lifecycle stage and the VV in NIST's TEVV — both acronyms decompose to the same two questions.", "kt385": "## Definition\n\nHiding a signal in AI-generated content, or attaching one to its metadata, that people generally cannot perceive but software can detect, so the content can later be identified as machine-made; one provenance technique alongside metadata, fingerprinting and cryptographic signatures. The EU AI Act requires providers of systems generating synthetic audio, image, video or text to mark outputs in a machine-readable, detectable way (Art. 50(2)).\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nWatermarks can sit in the content itself (pixel, audio or token-level patterns) or in attached metadata, and Recital 133 of the EU AI Act lists them with metadata identifications, cryptographic provenance methods, logging and fingerprints as ways to meet the marking duty. They are imperfect, since editing can weaken or strip them, which is why NIST AI 600-1 suggests measuring the reliability of content-authentication methods such as watermarking. In the AI Act the marking duty sits with the provider of the generating system (Art. 50(2)); disclosing deep fakes is a separate duty on deployers (Art. 50(4)).\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Providers mark AI output (Art. 50(2)); deployers disclose deep fakes (Art. 50(4)); swapping the two is the classic trap.", "kt386": "## Definition\n\nAssessment of whether staff have the skills, training, and awareness to effectively use, monitor, and govern AI systems in their operational context.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nWhether the organization's people are prepared for AI — skills to build and govern it, literacy to use it appropriately, and change management for the roles it transforms. A readiness gap is a deployment risk in its own right: capable systems operated by unprepared workforces produce incidents.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Readiness assessments include the humans — technology-ready but workforce-unready is a failing state the exam recognizes.", "kt387": "## Definition\n\nA machine learning technique where an AI model recognizes objects or concepts without having seen any training examples of those categories.\n\n## Deep Dive\n\nZero-shot learning performs a task with no task-specific training examples, relying on generalisation from what the model already learned. Modern language models do this constantly: a novel instruction is followed without any examples of that instruction being trained on. Related terms mark the gradient: few-shot means a handful of examples in the prompt, and one-shot means one.\n\nThe capability is real and the governance implication is uncomfortable: **zero-shot competence is unvalidated competence**. If a system can do a task it was never evaluated on, then deploying it for that task means deploying something nobody has measured. There is no benchmark, no held-out test set, no accuracy figure, and therefore no basis for the risk judgement the lifecycle requires.\n\nThat is why using a general model for a new purpose should re-enter assessment rather than proceed by default, and why the governance answer to \"can it do this?\" is not \"it appears to\" but \"we tested it on our population and here is the result.\" Capability without task-specific validation still needs task-specific testing before consequential use.\n\n## Exam Angle\n\n> [!tip] Zero-shot means no task examples needed. The governance corollary is what gets tested: capability demonstrated without task-specific evaluation still requires validation before consequential use, and a new use re-enters assessment.\n\n## Sources & Further Reading\n\n- <a href=\"https://webstore.iec.ch/en/publication/77839\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ISO/IEC 22989:2022 (free from IEC)</a> · zero-shot, one-shot and few-shot learning definitions\n- <a href=\"https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/playbook/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIST AI RMF Playbook</a> · validation and testing actions before deployment\n- <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.07682\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Emergent Abilities of Large Language Models</a> · capability that appears without task-specific training"};
